d_index": 7, "context": "She was so fond of following the devious course of the opposing parties", "context_len": 13}}}, "progressing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "Now , however , that he had mastered him , he raised his head a little to look ; but the boat was far away beyond his reach , and progressing at such a rate that he could not have overtaken it even had he been alone", "context_len": 47}}}, "explicit": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "But the 'Ladies ' Column ' should have been a little more explicit as to the quantity of flavouring , for , when it came to a matter of eating , the herbs so entirely predominated that the mutton was utterly lost , and , as she had unfortunately cut up the stalks as well as the leaves , the mixture bore a horrible resemblance to chopped hay", "context_len": 68}}}, "pinnacle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Well , you should have seen her ! She was quite set up on a pinnacle and declared that she must write out the story", "context_len": 25}}}, "candor": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "Ben could not doubt the honest candor of his companion , but he shrunk from unbosoming himself , dreading to narrate the unpleasant story of the events which had made both Bern Hayden and his father his uncompromising enemies and had forced him to flee like a criminal from his native village in order to escape being sent to the State Reformatory", "context_len": 62}}}, "mirthfully": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Are there four and twenty black birds in it ? '' she asked mirthfully", "context_len": 15}}}, "unearthed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "It was in vain that Lilian held him in her arms , and tried to soothe him to sleep ; he grunted and whined , and wriggled down on to the floor , where , with a shrill bark , he unearthed a cardboard box full of old gloves , which had been stored away under the bed", "context_len": 58}}}, "chancery": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "They are not likely to make her a ward in chancery as if she had a million", "context_len": 17}}}, "trustee": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` A trustee might answer for that", "context_len": 7}}}, "litigation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "The lawyer wrote that matters would probably be settled in favor of the present landholders , but that , so long as litigation pended , all titles were so clouded as to make any questions of loans untenable", "context_len": 38}}}, "goddess": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` I suppose it 's all settled , old fellow ! You are lucky with the prospect of such a home and such a presiding -- shall I call her the goddess of the hearth ? That room is a perfect gem , and you three people are to the manner born", "context_len": 52}}}, "satiric": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "I am a few years her junior , '' he said in a half satiric tone", "context_len": 16}}}, "rue": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 49, "context": "`` When the Lady Bedrulbudour heard Alaeddin 's words , she said ! to him , `` This is a thing exceeding hard on me to do ; but it is lawful to slay this accursed , so we may be delivered from his uncleanness who hath made me rue thy separation and that of my father", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "How I thought of those who tithed mint and rue , and all manner of herbs , and passed over justice , mercy , and the love of God", "context_len": 29}}}, "evergreens": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "They trudged through the half-melted snow to the church to help with the decorations , for it was fun twisting holly and evergreens , and making garlands of ivy to fasten round the pillars", "context_len": 34}}}, "ante": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 28, "context": "82 , note see FN 231 ) seems to me founded on a misconception , he being misled by the word `` fihi '' ( see my note ante , p", "context_len": 31}, "UNK": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 54, "context": "' '' As far as I can see , Galland was quite right , a staircase ( and not a ladder ) being , in my judgment , meant in each case , and Sir Richard Burton 's translation of sullem min thelathin derejeh as `` a ladder of thirty rungs '' ( see ante p", "context_len": 56}}}, "alleyway": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Out to pasture -- let us go down this main alleyway and I will explain the lay-out to you", "context_len": 20}}}, "evincing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Warren returned with some sewing and without evincing undue curiosity led Marilla to talk of her past , though the child really knew very little about her mother and seemed to have no tender or regretful regard for this Mrs", "context_len": 40}}}, "primary": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "By confrontation of the points of each secondary with those of its corresponding primary , the following four fresh figures are obtained :", "context_len": 23}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "I found that poor Kyneswith had impressed all the primary truths of our holy faith deeply upon his mind , although he wanted much building up , and needed instruction in details ; he seemed deeply impressed by the main facts of the life and teaching of our blessed Lord , particularly His message of peace on earth , good will towards men , contrasting so forcibly with the faith of his own people", "context_len": 74}}}, "chiffoniers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "There were two chiffoniers , and a big closet between the rooms , two pretty willow rockers and some lovely pictures beside various small gifts one could hang up or stand around", "context_len": 32}}}, "rockers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "The handsome house in its brilliant setting of lawns and trees , the wide verandah with its crimson Mount Washington rockers , luxurious hammocks , and low table covered with freshly-cut magazines , the pleasant-faced man who was her nearest of kin , and his graceful wife in a tea-gown of soft summer silk with rich lace about her throat and wrists , her cousins in their dainty muslins , and Russell in his fresh summer suit", "context_len": 77}}}, "albums": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "May brought out her pretty dishes and her card albums", "context_len": 10}}}, "rigorously": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "In the desert of Bethany Home all these emotions had been rigorously repressed", "context_len": 13}}}, "portfolios": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "`` I was thinking of the letter I wrote to you in the summer , and I had to beg everything to write with , and Edith has such a nice portable writing desk , and the girls have portfolios , and they all go to school", "context_len": 47}}}, "shames": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "What a wonderful city it is ! It quite shames a country doctor", "context_len": 13}}}, "huddle": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "The road itself formed the main street of the place , and while the boys were jogging at a very leisurely gait toward the huddle of store buildings , a man in a flannel shirt and with his trousers tucked in his boot tops , jumped across the road , dragging a rattling chain behind him", "context_len": 56}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 52, "context": "These sand-birds feed on the shore till they are chock brimful , and the tide comes and drives them off ; then they fly on to these ledges ; but they are as afraid of getting wet as a cat ; and when the tide comes up around the rock , they huddle together to keep out of the water , till they are all in a bunch , and the rock looks blue with them ; it \u2019 s the greatest chance for a shot ; but , continued he , after a pause , perhaps Mrs", "context_len": 98}}}, "dating": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "Peggy 's quiet talk with Father next day was so entirely between themselves that I shall not repeat it ; but it is often incidents , and not years , which help us to grow up , and somehow afterwards she always found herself dating events from that night in the cave , and all the part 'when I was little ' came before , and the older and more sensible part seemed to follow afterwards", "context_len": 76}}}, "braids": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "It is most serious , this business ! '' And casting her apron over the whip-cord braids of her coarse hair , she started hastily down toward the bridge", "context_len": 29}}}, "soaping": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Dear me ! You would n't catch me soaping and brushing the curl out of it if mine curled , '' declared May", "context_len": 23}}}, "exacted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "On the way Joe exacted from Jennie a faithful promise that she would tell nothing about his plan", "context_len": 18}}}, "dollies": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 53, "context": "`` I 'd have to be as fat as Auntie Belle to do that , '' she laughed , `` and now papa I want Marilla to tell you about a queer Home she was put in where they would n't let the little girls have dolls nor playthings , and they made dollies out of clothes pins and had to hide them", "context_len": 63}}}, "rehearsed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "Ben was obliging , and , finding he could give pleasure , he generally complied with the request of the miners and rehearsed such songs as he knew , so that he was considered a decided acquisition by the little company , and his popularity was unbounded", "context_len": 47}}}, "overstepped": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Neither overstepped the prescribed bounds , and he must have something to love and to cherish", "context_len": 16}}}, "dressmakers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Warren had gone to the dressmakers", "context_len": 6}}}, "wrinkle": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "He wanted to pound and bang and wrinkle up things and ask silly questions", "context_len": 14}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "-- You wo n't mind getting a wrinkle or two from an old woman , will you , Rose ? ''", "context_len": 21}}}, "intonation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Lem me go ! '' he mouthed , with purposely subdued intonation", "context_len": 13}}}, "beading": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "He saw the shining tears beading the curly lashes", "context_len": 9}}}, "salved": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "He salved his , thinking if she had wanted to she might have made some proffer of adoption", "context_len": 18}}}, "legislature": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "You have to apply to the legislature , and you will have your name changed to Richards", "context_len": 17}}}, "clogs": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` I suppose not quite all , '' said Isabel , `` though the boys are to give an Indian dance in costume , and the Dutch dance is in clogs , and oh , you ca n't imagine how funny and clumpy it sounds , but it is real pretty with the aprons and the caps , but the Spanish is beautiful with castanets", "context_len": 65}}}, "clumpy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "`` I suppose not quite all , '' said Isabel , `` though the boys are to give an Indian dance in costume , and the Dutch dance is in clogs , and oh , you ca n't imagine how funny and clumpy it sounds , but it is real pretty with the aprons and the caps , but the Spanish is beautiful with castanets", "context_len": 65}}}, "castanets": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 64, "context": "`` I suppose not quite all , '' said Isabel , `` though the boys are to give an Indian dance in costume , and the Dutch dance is in clogs , and oh , you ca n't imagine how funny and clumpy it sounds , but it is real pretty with the aprons and the caps , but the Spanish is beautiful with castanets", "context_len": 65}}}, "adornments": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "It was all so vivid and Marilla made such pretty gestures with her hands and swayed her head to and fro , that they could fairly see the palace , and the banquet was superb with its lights and flowers and beautiful adornments", "context_len": 43}}}, "changeful": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Richards watched the sweet , changeful face", "context_len": 7}}}, "engrossed": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` I 've been so engrossed -- and this sort of vision has come to my very door as it were , and I have let it in", "context_len": 28}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 10, "context": "The crowd had melted away , Bob Haven was totally engrossed with the magnificent prize he had drawn , and Darry was busily engaged in closing up the records of the sale", "context_len": 32}}}, "guardianship": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` I suspect she has buried herself in some obscure country place under some assumed name , there to remain till she has attained her twenty-fifth year , when my guardianship ceases", "context_len": 32}}}, "firth": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Firth was very stout , but she had a really pretty face and a voice that won you with a certain caressing mellowness", "context_len": 23}}}, "mellowness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Firth was very stout , but she had a really pretty face and a voice that won you with a certain caressing mellowness", "context_len": 23}}}, "tumultuously": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 66, "context": "To-morrow morning , then , my dear , if your Father will be at liberty , I shall give myself the pleasure of calling upon him ; ' and regretfully closing the lid , he tore himself away from the old chest , like a reluctant lover from his mistress , and took his departure , leaving Peggy , nearly wild with excitement , to rush tumultuously into the house with her wonderful piece of news", "context_len": 76}}}, "plaits": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "By four o'clock the school was full of impatient children , dressed in their best , the girls with their heads such marvels of frizzing and curling that you could well imagine their hair had been screwed up in plaits and papers for several days previously , while the boys were shiny with soap and hair-oil", "context_len": 56}}}, "ditto": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Laura Everett , fourteen ; Annie Millar , ditto ; Rosamund Cunliffe , fifteen ; and Phyllis Flower , thirteen", "context_len": 20}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The black fellows ditto", "context_len": 4}}}, "governesses": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 57, "context": "`` Did n't I do it well ? Oh , what a hateful , hateful girl she is ! Now , Rosamund -- Rose -- whatever you call yourself -- you had better just get right out of this window with me as fast as ever you can , or you 'll have Lucy bringing her precious governesses , and her mother , and that sick girl , Jane Denton -- how dare she call herself Jane , my dear mother 's name ? -- as well as the Professor himself , on the scene to hunt for the rats", "context_len": 100}}}, "wagonette": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` I had meant to order a wagonette and take you all for a nice drive , '' said nurse , who was sorry for their disappointment", "context_len": 27}}}, "irresponsive": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Merriman darted forward and took the somewhat irresponsive hand of a tall , pale girl , who replied languidly that her name was Jane Denton", "context_len": 25}}}, "gloved": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "The broad-brimmed hat flaring up in front , the fluttering handkerchief about the neck , the fringed collar , the close fitting coat , cartridge belt at the waist , gloved hands , revolver at the hip , rifle across his thighs , fringed trouser legs , tucked just below the knees into the boots , spurs , keen , alert eyes , body leaning slightly forward but as firm in the saddle , as if the rider were a part of the pony ; these with mail pouches locked and secured in place , were the noticeable points of the man who dashed up at headlong speed his horse setting his legs and coming to a stop within a single bound", "context_len": 122}}}, "hoydenish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "Merriman started the idea of a small school for the special education of special girls , she had spared no expense to have everything done in as thoroughly nice a manner as possible ; and the girls themselves were delighted , and showed their appreciation by behaving in a hoydenish and school-girl fashion", "context_len": 53}}}, "waylay": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "They 'll waylay you in a minute or two", "context_len": 9}}}, "jocular": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "But now they are there , all of them , as merry and jocular as you like , running about the place , racing here and there , and devouring all our best fruit , tramping in and out of the greenhouses and conservatory , and making such a noise just over your study", "context_len": 54}}}, "conservatory": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 43, "context": "But now they are there , all of them , as merry and jocular as you like , running about the place , racing here and there , and devouring all our best fruit , tramping in and out of the greenhouses and conservatory , and making such a noise just over your study", "context_len": 54}}}, "rearranged": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "She took down the dividing curtain between herself and Jane Denton without asking any one 's permission ; and she slept in the bed intended for Jane , and rearranged the drawers , putting them into another part of the room ; and complained about the wardrobe , saying that she would like it put opposite the door instead of in its present position", "context_len": 64}}}, "tearfully": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` I was born here , '' said Lucy almost tearfully", "context_len": 11}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "After that they could not shake her , although Belle hung about her tearfully", "context_len": 14}}}, "anthology": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "The Professor could , therefore , go on with the writing of his great work on Greek anthology ; even if the money for this unique treatise came in slowly , there would be enough to keep the little family from the products of the school", "context_len": 46}}}, "undermined": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 40, "context": "This is not the first nor the second time I have been in a house as big as this , and just as grand and full of visitors , and everything seemingly as right as possible , and the house undermined", "context_len": 41}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "The most ponderous stones are removed , the strongest fastenings are torn asunder , and embankments the most compact and solid are undermined and washed away", "context_len": 26}}}, "bulgy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "She had a large , pale face , somewhat flat , with wide nostrils , a long upper lip , small pale-blue eyes , and a somewhat bulgy forehead", "context_len": 29}}}, "parishioners": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 7, "context": "She was beloved by her husband 's parishioners , and in especial she was loved by Lucy Merriman , who had a sort of fascination in watching her and in wondering at her", "context_len": 33}}}, "attuned": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Ca n't you fancy some one floating , floating down it in a boat , with heart attuned -- -- ''", "context_len": 21}}}, "undeniably": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "You do not know what bitter feelings I had in my heart , for they really were undeniably some of the most attractive children you could possibly find ; and Florence was so witty , and Janet so delicate and refined and sweet in all her ways ! I could not be angry with them , but I did think it fearfully unfair that so many blessings should be poured on their heads and so few given to me , for I was not even specially clever", "context_len": 87}}}, "poky": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "She said they wanted change of air , as they were looking quite cooped up in our poky town", "context_len": 19}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "It \u2019 s a poky old hole", "context_len": 7}}}, "aunty": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` And I do not mean to be morbid about it ; only , at the time , my conscience troubled me , and your poor aunty had a very bad time", "context_len": 32}}}, "shrubberies": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "The garden was very large , and besides the lawn and the winding walks among the shrubberies , which afforded such capital hiding-places when they played hide-and-seek , there was the large kitchen-garden as well", "context_len": 35}}}, "mowed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "The tennis-lawn was not properly mowed for tennis , and anyhow the net was not out , and there seemed to be no croquet-ground anywhere", "context_len": 25}}}, "factotum": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Rosamund Cunliffe walked with Phyllis Flower , Jane Denton with Agnes Sparkes , and Laura Everett with her special friend and factotum , Annie Millar", "context_len": 25}}}, "pokes": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "She is an inveterate relic-hunter ; is enraptured with Bunker Hill and the Old South ; delights in Cornhill , and wherever she can find a crooked old street that reminds her of Washington ; and pokes about all the old cemeteries , until I feel as eerie as Coleridge 's ancient mariner", "context_len": 53}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Burton , `` pokes and breast-pockets", "context_len": 6}}}, "pertinent": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` You have one gift , dear , '' said Rosamund , looking at her kindly ; `` you are an excellent listener , and you can make as pertinent a remark as any one in the school", "context_len": 38}}}, "tittered": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "The girls tittered , for Mademoiselle 's mistakes in English were a by-word all over the school", "context_len": 17}}}, "maidenhair": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "Precious little of the sun did we see , and it was necessary to keep one 's eyes on the path which wound up and down into dark green hollows where beds of maidenhair fern flourished , or up to hilly spots where rock ferns grew in patches on enormous boulders", "context_len": 51}}}, "gipsies": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 16, "context": "Vaughan did not take quite such a serious view of the episode , he thought the gipsies were certainly undesirable additions to the countryside , and sent a hint to the local police , with the result that , when Peggy and Bobby screwed up their courage , and dared each other to go and have another peep at the encampment , they found the tent struck and the wanderers flown , with nothing to mark their passing visit but a few dirty pieces of paper and the ashes of the wood fire , though loud complaints from neighbouring farmers as to missing ducks and geese , and traces of snares found by the keepers in the preserves , showed that they had not gone away empty-handed", "context_len": 127}}}, "vivacious": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 61, "context": "But when the next morning came , and she saw the carriage arrive at the door , and perceived Rosamund 's trunks being put on the roof , she suddenly woke to the fact that the strong influence of her life during the last couple of months had come to a complete end ; that Rosamund , the strong , the vivacious , the daring , the noble , was leaving her", "context_len": 72}}}, "jealousies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Even Lucy forgot her jealousies , and even Rosamund forgot her anger", "context_len": 12}}}, "proportionately": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 65, "context": "A sharp snatch at his line , followed by a long peculiar drag , told him what was at his bait ; and after a little giving and taking , he drew a heavy twining conger eel over the boat 's edge , having no little difficulty in preventing it from tangling his line , for it was quite a yard in length , and proportionately thick", "context_len": 67}}}, "rosamund": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 0, "context": "Rosamund got up softly and locked the door , not a minute too soon , for she had scarcely done so before the handle was turned and the voice of Irene was heard outside crying through the keyhole , `` What changeling is in this room ? Which of you housemaids has dared to lock herself in ? Come out ! I 've got a big spider ready , and '' -- --", "context_len": 73}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 0, "context": "Rosamund suggested to her that she should lock her door at nights , which the poor lady did very willingly", "context_len": 20}}}, "prepossessing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "He was finely formed in person , and very prepossessing in his manners", "context_len": 13}}}, "rustle": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 86, "context": "The children could hear most of what he said , and a very interesting account of the Abbey it proved to be , with a description of the size and extent of the old buildings , and the life led by the ancient monks , which quite delighted Peggy , who revelled in Scott 's novels and historical stories , and which made the past days rise up so clearly before her that she could almost fancy the bell ringing for vespers , and hear the rustle of the gray robes of the friars as they passed silently up the chancel to their stalls in the choir", "context_len": 107}}}, "crape": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 43, "context": "By-and-by the rustle of a very rich silk caused her to turn her attention again to the outside world , and she observed a lady of about forty-five years of age , richly dressed in deep mourning , with a good deal of crape and a widow 's bonnet , walking up the church", "context_len": 54}}}, "curate": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 81, "context": "Peggy , hard at work behind the scenes , put her eye to a hole in the curtain , and surveyed the prospect below , where children big and little were engaged in jumping over the forms , chasing each other round the stove , and generally acting more like monkeys at the Zoo than civilized human beings , while the Rector strove in vain to collect them at the empty end of the room to play games , and the curate , a shy young man fresh from Oxford , looked as if he would have preferred to wrestle with a consignment of heathens from the Cannibal Islands", "context_len": 109}}}, "primly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The elderly governess primly followed , and then the lady in black silk also left her pew", "context_len": 17}}}, "haymaking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "How nice that field looks , with all that grass growing up so green after the haymaking", "context_len": 17}}}, "platitudes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Oh , do n't talk platitudes , '' said Lucy", "context_len": 11}}}, "hobbies": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "Aunt Helen was very long-suffering with respect to hobbies , for she rightly thought that the more a child 's life is filled with interests , the more chance it has of growing up an intelligent and broad-minded individual , and of escaping from that lethargy of boredom which swallows up the lives of many young people who ought to know better how to amuse themselves in God 's beautiful world", "context_len": 71}}}, "curtsy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The girl dropped a somewhat impertinent curtsy and marched on her way", "context_len": 12}}}, "elm": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 52, "context": "Whether Jim was annoyed at being classed as a girl , and was therefore put on his mettle , can not be said for certain , but at any rate his very next ball hit the tree fair and square , and with so much violence that a piece of the rough elm bark was knocked off", "context_len": 57}}}, "willful": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "Ashleigh , a man of great wealth , although their acquaintance had very much dropped into the background , yet still the stories about the beautiful and willful Lady Jane had delighted Rosamund when she was a little girl herself", "context_len": 40}}}, "wildness": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 40, "context": "The contrast between the girl in red and the lady in deep mourning who now appeared puzzled the girl a good deal ; also the extreme calm and graciousness of Lady Jane 's bearing , the absence of all that wildness in the eyes which Rosamund 's own mother had explained so fully", "context_len": 53}}}, "coerced": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Why should I be coerced and told that things were wrong by a man like Mr", "context_len": 16}}}, "changeling": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 41, "context": "Rosamund got up softly and locked the door , not a minute too soon , for she had scarcely done so before the handle was turned and the voice of Irene was heard outside crying through the keyhole , `` What changeling is in this room ? Which of you housemaids has dared to lock herself in ? Come out ! I 've got a big spider ready , and '' -- --", "context_len": 73}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` Well , '' said Rosamund stoutly , `` I will say this , Irene , that you are -- whether changeling or not -- an exceedingly naughty girl", "context_len": 29}}}, "puritanical": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "I was listening at one of the windows , and I heard you tell mother -- dear old puritanical mother -- that you had crept away without leave from the learned professor , and had got into difficulties", "context_len": 38}}}, "adores": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "She is very handsome , I know , and every one adores her , and so does her loving daughter Irene ; but , all the same , I was made in a sort of fashion that I really can not keep indoors", "context_len": 43}}}, "missions": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "Not only was he a shining light in his own parish , but he also went about the country and assisted at revival missions , showing himself everywhere a bright and helpful minister of the Gospel", "context_len": 36}}}, "terrify": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 18, "context": "This was a new experience , and she could do nothing , try as she would , to terrify Rosamund , who laughed when she assured her that she was a changeling , and might perhaps take any form at any time , and might return to her real home with the fairies at any moment ; who laughed still more merrily when she vowed she would upset the boat", "context_len": 70}}}, "changelings": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "At this moment she looked more spirit than girl ; and if Rosamund could have got herself to believe that there were such creatures as changelings , she might almost have given credence to Irene 's own story of herself", "context_len": 40}}}, "pulp": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 79, "context": "As for Peggy , a young gipsy tramp would have looked more respectable , for the brown holland dress , which had started out stiff and clean yesterday morning , was smeared with whinberry juice , black smudges from the kettle , and green stains from the mossy stones in the stream , and clung around her bare legs in damp , clammy folds , while the drenching rain had reduced the poppies in her hat to a scarlet pulp , which dripped down in crimson tears upon her cheek", "context_len": 90}}}, "naughtiness": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 27, "context": "To feel conscious that she was not one scrap afraid of this queer girl , to feel conscious also that the girl herself , notwithstanding her extreme naughtiness , could in the end be managed by her , brought such a rush of interest into her life that she forgot everything else for the moment ; and , besides , Irene was exceedingly strong , and although she was much slimmer and not so tall as Rosamund , she dragged her down the path with a power that it was almost impossible to resist", "context_len": 94}}}, "slimmer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 70, "context": "To feel conscious that she was not one scrap afraid of this queer girl , to feel conscious also that the girl herself , notwithstanding her extreme naughtiness , could in the end be managed by her , brought such a rush of interest into her life that she forgot everything else for the moment ; and , besides , Irene was exceedingly strong , and although she was much slimmer and not so tall as Rosamund , she dragged her down the path with a power that it was almost impossible to resist", "context_len": 94}}}, "primness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` Oh , for primness ! '' began Irene", "context_len": 9}}}, "rational": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "The girls ate and talked , and Irene , perhaps for the first time in her life , became almost rational in her behavior", "context_len": 24}}}, "slugs": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` I cruel ? Well , I like that ! I often and often put slugs and snails and worms , and that sort of thing , out of the path for fear any one should tread on them", "context_len": 39}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The rest of the little slugs struck the log hulls or missed entirely", "context_len": 13}}}, "prig": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Thank goodness , Rosamund is n't a prig ! She 's awfully passionate , and has plenty of strong feelings", "context_len": 20}}}, "harping": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Mother is always harping on them , and bringing them up to me", "context_len": 13}}}, "blanch": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "I have been rather curious to see them , and to give them a good round fright , every one of them , till they blanch to the color of ghosts , and then their green dresses would n't suit them any longer", "context_len": 43}}}, "landau": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Rosamund took the lady 's hand , and they walked up to the house , where an open landau was waiting for them", "context_len": 23}}}, "priggish": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Merriman very well , and some of the girls ; but I ca n't stand that priggish Lucy", "context_len": 18}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "The grave , studious , young pupil-teacher was no favorite with the other boys , who thought him priggish and rather arbitrary ; but at least he was always courteous in his dealings with them , and , indeed , rather prided himself upon his manners", "context_len": 46}}}, "gloats": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` I know it ; but then I can not lead her , and she has got the whole house so thoroughly in her power that she gloats over the position", "context_len": 31}}}, "category": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` And I am quite glad to be everybody 's friend , if only Lucy Merriman may be left out of the category , '' was Rosamund 's answer", "context_len": 29}}}, "extenuating": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "On the first opportunity which presented itself the morning after his arrival at his uncle 's he told her all , extenuating nothing of his own misconduct and weakness in the beginning , and acknowledging that he had almost wilfully suffered himself to be led into disobedience and wrong , and richly deserved all the shame and trouble which had fallen upon him", "context_len": 63}}}, "scriptural": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "The chamber occupied by the king was a long roomy place , containing a single bedstead of carved wood , surmounted by the usual distinctive canopy , from which tapestried hangings depended , and upon which scriptural subjects were woven ; the furniture of the room partook of the usual meagreness of the times", "context_len": 54}}}, "emancipation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "By-and-by your time of emancipation will come , and you will owe allegiance only to God and those whom you love , my dear ; but until that time comes it seems to me scarcely fit or advisable that you should have anything to do with Irene", "context_len": 47}}}, "irreclaimable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Is no one to help a girl who will soon become irreclaimable ? Why , already I have an influence over her", "context_len": 23}}}, "cantankerous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "She had forgotten to be wild or cantankerous", "context_len": 8}}}, "promptitude": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "She had even ceased to notice Lucy ; and as to her lessons , she had gone through the tasks assigned to her with sufficient promptitude and sufficient correctness to win fairly good remarks from the two governesses and from the different teachers who came to visit the little school", "context_len": 50}}}, "correctness": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "She had even ceased to notice Lucy ; and as to her lessons , she had gone through the tasks assigned to her with sufficient promptitude and sufficient correctness to win fairly good remarks from the two governesses and from the different teachers who came to visit the little school", "context_len": 50}}}, "bowery": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "It was , therefore , at this hour a delightfully cool room , and was rendered more so by the bowery shade of green trees", "context_len": 25}}}, "sixes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "We had no governess ; we used to go to a sort of school -- not the Merrimans ' , for they had not started one at the time -- and I used to teach the little children , and things were rather at sixes and sevens", "context_len": 47}}}, "sevens": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "We had no governess ; we used to go to a sort of school -- not the Merrimans ' , for they had not started one at the time -- and I used to teach the little children , and things were rather at sixes and sevens", "context_len": 47}}}, "maud": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "romping": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 10, "context": "She saw now that , while they had still been romping at rounders , Mary must have gone up to the schoolroom , and finding the missing notes in her desk , had carried them at once to the library", "context_len": 40}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "But -- could she ever forget the weary conditions of Sleepy Hollow ? She frequently heard in imagination the clatter of the dishes and the rough romping of the children as they noisily trooped to bed", "context_len": 36}}}, "unsympathetic": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Miss Archer , the English governess , will be as strict and as unsympathetic as ever , and Mademoiselle Omont will teach excellent French , no doubt", "context_len": 27}}}, "certificated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "But I have a little brother and sister both dependent on me altogether for their daily bread , and Lady Jane gives me one hundred and fifty pounds a year , a very large sum for a governess who is not certificated", "context_len": 42}}}, "smothers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "She has no respect for authority , not even her mother ; and although she rushes at her sometimes and smothers her with kisses , she seems to have no real affection for her", "context_len": 34}}}, "distresses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "You can scarcely believe how this subject distresses me and causes that terrible trembling to come on", "context_len": 17}}}, "silliest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` We want a game of Puss-in-the-corner , and the silliest and youngest among us are going to play", "context_len": 19}}}, "clinical": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Merriman always kept a clinical thermometer handy , and on discovering that the young girl 's temperature was considerably over one hundred degrees , she took fright and had her removed to a room in a distant part of the house", "context_len": 41}}}, "administering": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "Just before it was presented , Alexander received a letter from Parmenio , informing him that he had good reason to believe that Philip had been bribed by the Persians to murder him , during his sickness , by administering poison in the name of medicine", "context_len": 46}}}, "eccentricities": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "She could not help liking the girl , notwithstanding her eccentricities and her very bold act of disobedience on the previous Sunday", "context_len": 22}}}, "entwined": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Merriman admitted that it was , and the two girls , their arms entwined , went out into the soft summer night", "context_len": 22}}}, "chemistry": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "She was devoted to chemistry and mathematics , and could already work well in these two branches of science", "context_len": 19}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "She would take up chemistry , of course , and biology , mathematics and physics , French and Latin , geology and botany , and -- well , she would decide later upon the rest of her curriculum", "context_len": 38}}}, "pernicious": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 53, "context": "She had rather taken Laura under her own wing , had intended to make her her special friend , had meant to trot her round and to show her to other friends ; in short , as much as possible to divide her from Rosamund , whom she considered a most dangerous and pernicious influence", "context_len": 55}}}, "glutton": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "She is a little bit of a glutton is my Jane , and she overate herself at tea at the Singletons '", "context_len": 22}}}, "overate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "She is a little bit of a glutton is my Jane , and she overate herself at tea at the Singletons '", "context_len": 22}}}, "buttered": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` Ah , you 're a proud young girl , '' she said , `` you 're one of those who do not know which side their bread is buttered ; but you will some day , the knowledge will come to you , and soon , I 'm thinking , soon", "context_len": 52}}}, "wistaria": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` Gracious ! '' said Irene , `` could n't we both get out of the window , and climb down by the wistaria and the ivy , and reach the ground , and go and hide in the plantation ? We could spend the night there , locked in each other 's arms , so happy -- oh , so happy ! By the way , I saw a little summer-house -- we could spend the night in the summer-house , could n't we ? Could n't we ? ''", "context_len": 91}}}, "elfin": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "She was in the red frock that she usually wore ; her wild hair curled in elf-locks all over her head ; her eyes , bright as stars , shone in the middle of her little elfin face ; her charming lips pouted just for a moment", "context_len": 47}}}, "hungered": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "But for Hayden he might also be there taking part in the practice , enjoying that for which his heart hungered , the friendly companionship of other lads", "context_len": 28}}}, "frump": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Of course he hates me , horrid old frump ! '' said Irene", "context_len": 14}}}, "spitfire": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Oh , what a mean spitfire of a thing you 'd be if you did that ! '' said Irene , her eyes flashing with anger", "context_len": 27}}}, "plait": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Lucy looked prim and neat , as usual , in her white dressing-gown and her hair in a long plait down her back", "context_len": 23}}}, "vinegar": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "For it is written of him that `` he was always as sour as if he had swallowed a pint of vinegar except when he was being shot at , -- and then he was as blithe as a schoolboy out for a holiday ''", "context_len": 45}}}, "receded": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 57, "context": "But on second thought the desire to confide in him passed out of sight , more particularly as there was a noise and confusion -- a sort of stifled confusion -- in the house : people hurrying backwards and forwards , and voices sunk to whispers , which came sometimes to Rosamund 's ears , and sometimes receded in the distance", "context_len": 61}}}, "reciting": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "`` But , Aunt Susan , '' interrupted Lucy , `` do you really approve of a girl who burns the candle at both ends ? It so happens that I was obliged to invade Rosamund 's room last night , and I heard her reciting poetry in two voices , and then I heard her throw her voice into a distant part of the room , so that you might almost imagine that she was a ventriloquist", "context_len": 78}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "The folk gathered about him , believing him to be Fatimeh the Recluse , and he proceeded to do like as she had been used to do , laying hands on those in pain and reciting for this one the Fatiheh [ 645 ] and for that a [ nother ] chapter of the Koran and praying for a third", "context_len": 60}}}, "ventriloquist": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 77, "context": "`` But , Aunt Susan , '' interrupted Lucy , `` do you really approve of a girl who burns the candle at both ends ? It so happens that I was obliged to invade Rosamund 's room last night , and I heard her reciting poetry in two voices , and then I heard her throw her voice into a distant part of the room , so that you might almost imagine that she was a ventriloquist", "context_len": 78}}}, "antitoxin": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "They are going to try that new treatment -- antitoxin", "context_len": 10}}}, "childless": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "My husband and I are a childless couple , and we have plenty of room in our house", "context_len": 18}}}, "whys": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Brett ; `` and now the sooner we drop the subject of whys and wherefores the better", "context_len": 17}}}, "wherefores": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Brett ; `` and now the sooner we drop the subject of whys and wherefores the better", "context_len": 17}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "I have talked all I \u2019 m going to about the whys and wherefores", "context_len": 14}}}, "benediction": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "Even as he lifted his eyes toward the overcast sky , the pure white moon burst through a widening rift and poured its light like a benediction upon the silent world", "context_len": 31}}}, "misdirected": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` It 's nothing to worry about , '' he said , `` only misdirected energy", "context_len": 16}}}, "expelling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "I have told you how naughty I have been , and you can punish me by expelling me from the school", "context_len": 21}}}, "bewilder": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "From the depths of despair he was thus lifted to the heights of hope , but the sudden change seemed to bewilder him", "context_len": 23}}}, "sprite": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "She , a girl of only fifteen years of age , was more or less responsible for the entire life , the entire future , of a brilliant little sprite like Irene Ashleigh", "context_len": 33}}}, "multiply": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` They will multiply inside me , '' said poor Miss Frost", "context_len": 12}}}, "swallowing": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 82, "context": "`` How are we to begin ? Shall we do penance like the old monks ? Do you know , Rosamund '' -- here Irene linked her thin , almost steel-like little hand inside Rosamund 's arm -- '' that I am a most voracious reader ? Father was a great collector of books , and when I am tired of frightening the servants , and terrifying Frosty , and annoying mother , I spend days at a time in his library swallowing down the contents of his books", "context_len": 89}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The incongruity of the wolf 's swallowing whole the grandmother and child does not destroy the child 's enjoyment of the story , while the happy release of both grandmother and little girl forms a suitable close", "context_len": 37}}}, "uncurl": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The rest began to uncurl in the box , and she discovered what had happened", "context_len": 15}}}, "birdie": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 7, "context": "' Oh , yes , you darling birdie ! do you want to say you know all about it ? Bessie , let 's think the canary is a kind of keepsake from Dolly , 'cause you know it seems as if it came by her , and mamma says it is of no use to take the marigolds to town , for they will be quite dead in a few weeks", "context_len": 72}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Never was a birdie coaxed with more pretty names than was this one during the next ten minutes ; and he seemed to like them well , for , after answering with one or two more half-timid `` cheeps , '' he broke into a soft trill , which soon swelled into a clear , sweet song of joy", "context_len": 59}}}, "dumpy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "After a pause she said , `` I did remember that yesterday morning ; and knowing that you 'd be frightfully dumpy -- oh , mummy ! you know you never are cheerful -- I thought I 'd have a spree on my own account", "context_len": 45}}}, "welled": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` Give 'em the 'Conquer or Die ' song now ! '' cried Bean Perkins , and the strains of `` Aut vincere aut mori '' welled out over the gridiron", "context_len": 31}}}, "contrives": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "`` I often wonder beyond words why I am given such a very naughty child -- a child who understands me so very little , who can not sympathize with my sorrows and can not understand my griefs , and who contrives to make others miserable", "context_len": 46}}}, "stings": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "The remedies I have used will soon take the pain out of your stings , and you will be all right again", "context_len": 22}}}, "dudgeon": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "The butler , in high dudgeon , had retired to his own apartment , where he had locked and barred the door in order to prevent any pranks of that imp , as he privately styled Irene", "context_len": 37}}}, "tremblingly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "The other servants were tremblingly attending to their duties ; but all smelled mischief in the air", "context_len": 17}}}, "punishes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` It strikes me , '' she said after a pause , `` that God is punishing me more cruelly than He punishes most people , and I can not understand it", "context_len": 32}}}, "boxful": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` I have not brought any of my things with me , except just what I am wearing , so you will have to provide me until mother sends me a boxful from London", "context_len": 34}}}, "servitude": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "I am very anxious that your time of servitude should be over", "context_len": 12}}}, "scents": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "It is possible that the mention of this latter may be an interpolation by some ignorant copyist , who , seeing two only of the three favourite Oriental scents named , took upon himself to complete the odoriferous trinity , so dear to Arab writers , by the addition of ambergris", "context_len": 51}}}, "emanates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "She says that she has a strong sense of smell , and knows each person by a sort of delicate perfume which emanates from them", "context_len": 25}}}, "throttle": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "The moment the girl saw her , she sprang to the ground , ran to her side , flung her arms round her neck so tightly as almost to throttle her , and kissed her over and over again", "context_len": 39}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "The B-26 was already in the catapult ; her Double Wasp radial motors were roaring at full throttle", "context_len": 18}}}, "voracious": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 44, "context": "`` How are we to begin ? Shall we do penance like the old monks ? Do you know , Rosamund '' -- here Irene linked her thin , almost steel-like little hand inside Rosamund 's arm -- '' that I am a most voracious reader ? Father was a great collector of books , and when I am tired of frightening the servants , and terrifying Frosty , and annoying mother , I spend days at a time in his library swallowing down the contents of his books", "context_len": 89}}}, "initiative": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "They evidently meant , as usual , to take the initiative ; in fact , in the recent reign , had they not done so , there would never have been any fighting at all", "context_len": 35}}}, "emaciation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "She was a very cadaverous-looking person , about forty years of age , thin to emaciation , with small , light , frightened-looking eyes , a long upper lip , and a great many freckles on her face", "context_len": 38}}}, "freckles": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "Just at that moment there came a whoop and a spring , and Hughie , his red face redder than ever , his freckles more marked , his carroty hair sticking up all over his head , and his light-blue eyes wearing a most mischievous expression , entered the little arbor and sat down at one side of Irene", "context_len": 59}}}, "unbar": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` And please unbar the shutters and draw up the blinds , for it is a lovely summer 's day , and Irene wo n't do you any harm", "context_len": 29}}}, "basins": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 51, "context": "The genie carried him into a treasury full of all manner of gold and silver and precious jewels past count or reckoning , price or estimation ; then he brought him into another place , where he saw all the requisites of the table , platters and spoons and ewers and basins and cups , of gold and silver , and thence to the kitchen , where he found cooks , [ 476 ] with their cooking-gear and utensils , all on like wise of gold and silver", "context_len": 88}}}, "newts": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "I ca n't tell you what an effort it has been not to collect worms and toads , and frogs and newts , and wasps and bees , and blue-bottles and spiders", "context_len": 32}}}, "leeches": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 98, "context": "Horrid , affected creatures ! And then to see Carter 's frightened eyes looking at me ! Have n't I seen them in my dreams until I am sick of the sight of them ? And Rosamund wants me to go and see them again ! Why , Carter , poor thing ! would nearly die of fright , and every one of the Leaves would get into their native trees and disappear from view ! Oh , Rosamund is all very well , but she is n't worth that ! I wish I had n't given those leeches back to the chemist", "context_len": 103}}}, "stalk": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "That one on the stalk -- do you see how he is wriggling , poor sweet pet ? -- that one I call Fuzz , and this one at the bottom of the glass is Buzz", "context_len": 36}}}, "tomboy": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 26, "context": "'What mad folly is this ? Really , Lilian , I am astonished that you countenance such wild proceedings ! Peggy I knew was a sad tomboy , but I thought you , at least , were the sensible member of the family , and would try to train the younger ones into more civilized habits", "context_len": 56}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 60, "context": "Her friendships were so keen and her affections so warm , that I think she found the world a very pleasant place to be in , and made it better and pleasanter for other people too ( which is , after all , the grand secret of happiness ) , and the energy and enterprising spirits which had caused the tomboy pranks of her childhood were qualities which , turned to good use , proved of the utmost service to her in after-years", "context_len": 83}}}, "bulletins": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "She had two trained nurses ; bulletins were put up at the door ; no one was allowed to come in", "context_len": 21}}}, "disinfected": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 31, "context": "Is n't it jolly , Jane being quite out of danger ? She is to go away at the end of the week , and next week the house will be disinfected , and then everything will be as it was before", "context_len": 42}}}, "punctilious": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "She was so extra good , so punctilious , and so proper ; she did n't suit me one bit , and I did n't suit her one little bit either", "context_len": 31}}}, "naughtier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "I never was too good -- was I , mother dear ? Perhaps , darling , I 'd have become an awfully naughty Rosamund had it not been for Irene -- poor little wild Irene ; but she was really and truly much naughtier than I ever thought of being , and her example shocked me and pulled me up , and I resolved to try to be good for her sake", "context_len": 72}}}, "systematically": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "I should like to stay with Irene for the next few months at any rate , and if you want me to get on very fast indeed with my music , and to take up my drawing systematically , some of the masters who attend at the Merrimans ' could come on here , could n't they ? I think that could be arranged", "context_len": 64}}}, "frights": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "She told her there was not the slightest danger of anything happening , as nothing would induce Irene to give her any more frights", "context_len": 24}}}, "melodies": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` That is not the way to bring out that note , '' he would say ; and then he would sit down to the piano himself , and ring out great melodies in the most splendid style , until the enthusiastic child almost danced with pleasure", "context_len": 47}}}, "malignancy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Irene backed away , her face crimson , her eyes dancing with all their old malignancy", "context_len": 16}}}, "harnessed": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 2, "context": "To be harnessed in the little pony-carriage , and trot about with the old lady for her daily drive , was all the work to which Nonesuch was used ; and when he found Frankie perched upon him , he was very much displeased , and began a series of antics and prancings which were more becoming some frisky pony than a sober , well-behaved donkey", "context_len": 66}}}, "satirical": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Maud held out a gracious hand to Irene , whose little mouth , satirical enough at first , broke now into a gentle smile , while her eyes became tranquil and even happy", "context_len": 33}}}, "whetted": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "Bertha was nowhere to be seen , and where was `` Cartery dear '' ? That one glimpse she had caught of the terrified woman , who had disappeared like a flash into the house , had whetted Irene 's desire to behold her again", "context_len": 45}}}, "detested": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "This was his reward ; in this time when his heart was sore and heavy with the belief that he was detested of all the world , Jimmy watched and waited for him at the gate , and came limping toward him with a cheery greeting", "context_len": 46}}}, "acme": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "As this was Tige \u2019 s method of manifesting the very acme of happiness , we are bound to suppose , with John , that he knew what was in store for him", "context_len": 33}}}, "darlings": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 42, "context": "But we must ask Him from our hearts ; and can we do so if those hearts are full of unkindness and hard feeling towards those who have injured us ? I know how hard it is for you both , my darlings ; I know by my own feelings how hard it is to forgive Lem and Dolly ; but I can not hope to be forgiven myself for what I have done wrong this day , unless I forgive them the harm they have done to me", "context_len": 89}}}, "mismanaged": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "You and I , who are so much older , ought to try to encourage her ; for , after all , she has been a most sadly mismanaged child , allowed even from her earliest days to see that people were afraid of her , and thus the spirit of cruelty gained a strong hold ; but there is a great deal of good in her nature", "context_len": 68}}}, "disport": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` But I am so happy , '' said Irene , who had been helping some of the little girls to climb up and tumble down cocks of hay , and otherwise disport themselves", "context_len": 34}}}, "singleton": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Singleton", "context_len": 1}}}, "verve": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "There he spoke with such verve and enthusiasm with regard to Rosamund , and the marvelous change she had already wrought in the naughtiest girl in the entire district , that he induced that gentleman to change his mind", "context_len": 39}}}, "naughtiest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "There he spoke with such verve and enthusiasm with regard to Rosamund , and the marvelous change she had already wrought in the naughtiest girl in the entire district , that he induced that gentleman to change his mind", "context_len": 39}}}, "culpable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "It would be culpable to allow such a girl to enter on the world with such a stigma as being expelled from school would mean", "context_len": 25}}}, "stigma": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "Ere he died , being satisfied beyond doubt that there was no hope for him , he made a confession which fully exonerates your father and clears his name of the stigma upon it", "context_len": 34}}}, "tempest": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 23, "context": "But the strain was too much for Peggy , and she plumped down on the dead leaves by the roadside in such a tempest of tears that Father had to climb out of the gig and pick her up to comfort her ; but as he could not get a word of sense , he popped her in the vacant place by his side and drove on , while she clung to his arm , still shaking with sobs , till they reached the Abbey , where he helped her down , such a miserable little tear-stained picture of woe , gulping out the confession of her escapade , that he had not the heart to scold her , though he had a word of warning to say", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Bomba had scarcely taken a dozen steps carrying his heavy burden when the tempest broke", "context_len": 15}}}, "churchwardens": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "One of the churchwardens invited the two young people to come up higher ; but Rosamund requested to be left where they were , and presently the rest of the congregation streamed in", "context_len": 33}}}, "grayness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The quiet grayness and the age of the building impressed her , she knew not why", "context_len": 16}}}, "surplices": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Then the boys in their white surplices excited her wonder ; then she watched the congregation", "context_len": 16}}}, "cherubic": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Merriman , round and cherubic , looking as though no care had ever troubled her ; and there was Lucy , fair almost as the Singletons , with that particularly prim face which aggravated Rosamund , and which would certainly drive Irene to distraction", "context_len": 44}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 103, "context": "No , I do n't want to hunt for any , thank you ! It 's girls ' stuff ! I are n't going to bother with marrying when I grow up ; I mean to be a pirate , and live in a ship with a black flag , and a lot of jolly fellows with pistols and cutlasses , and we 'll overhaul every merchantman we see , and string the sailors up from the yard-arm ! ' and the future buccaneer swung his legs over the rock , and put on a cut-throat expression , strangely at variance with his cherubic cast of countenance", "context_len": 107}}}, "sinned": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 41, "context": "The sad procession passed through the streets , the people flocked into the church , and in the presence of all the `` wise men '' of London , they solemnly committed the frail tabernacle in which the living spirit had sinned and suffered to the parent earth , where the rush and roar of a mighty city should ever peal around it", "context_len": 63}}}, "scandalous": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` See a girl student of Fairview after dark ? Why , the idea is preposterous , my dear sir ! Perfectly scandalous ! '' and Frank gave a fair imitation of an indignant lady teacher", "context_len": 36}}}, "sponsor": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Fanning , this young girl is very dear to me , I have known her since her birth ; I stood sponsor for her when she was a baby", "context_len": 29}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Since Josiah Franklin did not recognize his son \u2019 s unusual abilities , he , the governor , would sponsor him", "context_len": 21}}}, "ejaculating": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "It would wheel along the ground in a straight course and also in a circle , apparently possessed of some power in itself , and the black fellow would jump up and down , talking and ejaculating to it as though it understood him", "context_len": 44}}}, "nagging": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` Wo n't you tell my sister that she is not to hold the reins like that ? She is nagging at the pony 's mouth all the time , and hurting him", "context_len": 33}}}, "shunned": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 26, "context": "Irene -- who never before in all her life had any creature to look up to her , who was looked down upon with terror and shunned by her fellow-creatures , with the exception of Rosamund , who ruled her , although with the weapons of love -- felt an altogether new sensation now as the little creature , not so old as herself , clung to her confidently", "context_len": 69}}}, "coverlet": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "Maggie did not answer , except by gently kissing the poor hurt hand , which lay upon the coverlet ; but it was plainly to be seen that she was a good deal excited ; and Uncle Ruthven , fearing one of her sudden bursts of crying , said the children had stayed long enough , and led them from the room", "context_len": 62}}}, "munch": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 80, "context": "He never let on that he suspected for a moment that he was not entirely alone , but , walking over to a tree stump , where , spread out on a newspaper , was the remains of a lunch , he acted delighted at the discovery , picked up a hunk of bread in one hand , a piece of cheese in the other , and , throwing himself on the green sward at full length , proceeded to munch the eatables , with every semblance of satisfaction", "context_len": 89}}}, "unction": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "The animal was a big half-breed mastiff and shepherd dog that Lem Wacker had introduced to his railroad friends with great unction , one Christmas day", "context_len": 26}}}, "apprenticed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 43, "context": "Since Benjamin liked to read , what would he think of being a printer a trade that deals with pamphlets , books , everything made with words ? The idea appealed to Benjamin , though he balked when he learned he would be apprenticed to his brother until he was twenty", "context_len": 51}}}, "bookbinder": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Perhaps he will be a bookbinder , or perhaps a cabinetmaker", "context_len": 11}}}, "cabinetmaker": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Perhaps he will be a bookbinder , or perhaps a cabinetmaker", "context_len": 11}}}, "ply": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "She began to ply her oars again , and in a minute or two they had landed , the boat had been moored , and the two children went up to the house", "context_len": 33}}}, "expostulations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "She ran out into the cool night air , notwithstanding the expostulations of her mother , and came in late , feeling fagged and wearied", "context_len": 25}}}, "fagged": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` But to-morrow came and the Duchess did not arrive , nor the next day , nor the next , and mother looked rather fagged , and rather sad and disappointed , and at the end of a week or fortnight she ceased to watch anxiously for the sound of wheels in the Square , and said less and less about her dear friend Victoria", "context_len": 65}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Dear Jane 's fagged face began to appeal less to me than it had at first", "context_len": 16}}}, "defenseless": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` There are two years between us ; but then , Agnes is so very small , so petite in every way , and so -- so sweet and so defenseless", "context_len": 31}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 61, "context": "It is horrible , because the soldiers , exasperated to fury by the resistance which they meet with , and by the awful malignity of the passions always excited in the hour of battle , if they succeed , burst suddenly into the precincts of domestic life , and find sometimes thousands of families -- mothers , and children , and defenseless maidens -- at the mercy of passions excited to phrensy", "context_len": 72}}}, "frowzy": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Only she happens to be her sister , and that tiresome , frowzy old Frosty , as you call her , has looked after her since she was a little child , when her mother died", "context_len": 37}}}, "carroty": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Just at that moment there came a whoop and a spring , and Hughie , his red face redder than ever , his freckles more marked , his carroty hair sticking up all over his head , and his light-blue eyes wearing a most mischievous expression , entered the little arbor and sat down at one side of Irene", "context_len": 59}}}, "wickedest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` And what if I had , you wicked boy -- for you are about the wickedest and rudest boy I have ever come across ? Much do I care ! I wanted her to go , and I thought that would be a good way to get rid of her", "context_len": 51}}}, "vise": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "Page 15 held him as in a vice changed to held him as in a vise", "context_len": 16}}}, "ripest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Then I 'll pick out the ripest in the basket for you , '' said Irene , her voice trembling", "context_len": 21}}}, "stationer": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 46, "context": "`` Well , I 'll call you Rosamund , because all the rest of the people here do ; but by-and-by perhaps I shall be behind a counter , and you will come in and ask for stationery -- I want particularly to go into a stationer 's shop -- or any other article you fancy , and I 'll have to say , 'Yes , miss", "context_len": 67}}}, "gamekeeper": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 45, "context": "He found , too , considerable pleasure and interest on his own account at The Follies , for Lady Jane was singularly kind to him , and gave him a pony to ride , and he was permitted the rare indulgence of going with the gamekeeper into the woods to take his first lesson in partridge-shooting ; but this came later on", "context_len": 62}}}, "terrorism": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Accordingly , she made up her mind to administer a lesson , and to make it as stiff a piece of terrorism as she could devise", "context_len": 26}}}, "repugnant": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "There was something to him repugnant about her", "context_len": 8}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "The only method seemed to accompany the emigrant train until it met Brandley returning , or overtook him at the next station ; but , to do that , placed him in a delicate and repugnant position", "context_len": 37}}}, "freckled": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 31, "context": "There was Charley Tuttle , called Chub for short , a roly-poly , round-faced , laughing chap , who was munching peanuts ; Tim Davis , nicknamed Spotty , even more freckled than Ben , thin-legged , sly-faced , and minus the two front teeth of his upper jaw ; Sam Rollins , a big , hulking , low-browed fellow , who lost no opportunity to bully smaller boys , generally known as Hunk ; Berlin Barker , a cold blond , rather good-looking , but proud and distant in his bearing ; and others who did not impress the new boy at all with their personalities", "context_len": 107}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "She looked at the solitary lamp with its grotesquely hideous ornament of red flannel , at Susan 's expressionless , freckled face , at the boys in their copper-toed boots and overalls , at the good-natured , but hopelessly common-place Martha Spriggs , with her thin hair drawn tight into a knob the size of a bullet , and her bare arms akimbo", "context_len": 63}}}, "jasmine": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 0, "context": "Jasmine and her husband had gone to the theatre ; they had expressed regret at leaving me , but I was glad , very glad , to be alone", "context_len": 29}}}, "crushes": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "It is the fashion to be eccentric now , it is poverty that crushes , you must know that", "context_len": 19}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "He is as quick to bite the hand that has fed his hunger as a rattlesnake is to strike the foot that crushes him", "context_len": 24}}}, "fogs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The morning was a foggy one , one of the first bad fogs of the season", "context_len": 16}}}, "armstrong": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Armstrong from her place at the opposite side of the board , `` that you have a large connection with the theatrical managers ? ''", "context_len": 25}}}, "bridling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "Bradshaw , bridling , for though she had been bribed she did not like to confess it", "context_len": 17}}}, "quizzical": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 39, "context": "He came in with that very quiet manner which he always wore , that absolute self-possession which I do not think under any circumstances would desert him , but I read the anxiety in his grey eyes , the quizzical , half-laughing glance was gone altogether , the eyes were very grave and almost stern", "context_len": 55}}}, "randolph": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 0, "context": "Randolph ! do you think I have made a mistake , a great mistake , in coming here ? ''", "context_len": 20}}}, "brougham": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 26, "context": "But what I wanted to say to you now was this : May Miss Wickham come for a drive with me in my son 's own brougham ? I am anxious to have an outing with her , and I see by her face she is desirous to come ; may she ? Say yes , madam ; if you are wise , you will", "context_len": 65}}}, "defect": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "The Sultan accordingly went up to the pavilion [ 535 ] and entering the kiosk , [ 536 ] viewed it right and left and saw no manner defect in its lattices , but found them all perfect ; whereat he was astounded and embracing Alaeddin , fell a-kissing him and saying , `` O my son , what is this extraordinary thing ? In one night thou dost a work wherefrom the jewellers would fail in months ! By Allah , methinketh thou hast not thy fellow [ 537 ] in the world ! '' Quoth Alaeddin , `` God prolong thy life and perpetuate thy continuance ! Thy slave is not worthy of this praise", "context_len": 117}}}, "guineas": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 7, "context": "People do n't want to pay five guineas a week to live in Bloomsbury , and he has a small room ; and it is a great relief to me that he should be here and pay so good a sum for his room", "context_len": 44}}}, "sampson": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Sampson ; she was slightly deaf , and said `` Eh , eh ! '' to each remark of mine", "context_len": 20}}}, "mezzo": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "My voice was a rather sweet mezzo soprano , and I had been well taught", "context_len": 15}}}, "soprano": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "My voice was a rather sweet mezzo soprano , and I had been well taught", "context_len": 15}}}, "clique": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "If I sat with you we should have a regular clique in the place , and by degrees the boarders would go , at least those boarders who were not included in our set", "context_len": 34}}}, "unsuited": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Do n't you think you are peculiarly unsuited to your present life ? ''", "context_len": 14}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "Notwithstanding the scorching heat of an Indian summer , -- in spite , too , of the fact that a number of the men were obliged to march in heavy garments utterly unsuited to the climate ; though death , disease , and a thousand perils lay in front of them , -- not a man of Havelock 's `` Ironsides '' but was impatient to push onward to death or victory", "context_len": 72}}}, "birthright": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 56, "context": "Peggy does not feel the least little bit older than when she climbed the water-wheel , or thrashed Jones minor behind the paling of the cricket-field -- if growing old means losing your ideals , and your keen enthusiasm , and your hopefulness , and a certain appreciation of other people 's worth which is our birthright when we are young", "context_len": 61}}}, "extremes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "But neither Bradley nor Ben was willing to go to such extremes", "context_len": 12}}}, "stammering": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` You think , '' I said , stammering , `` that he comes because -- -- ''", "context_len": 18}}}, "flirtation": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "Fanning , after one or two astonished glances , during which he took in the tout ensemble of the ugly robe , began to enter into a playful bear-like flirtation with Miss Armstrong", "context_len": 33}}}, "sanctum": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 51, "context": "Mary opened the box , but dropped it with a howl of dismay , for in place of the tempting sweetmeats she had expected lay a writhing mass of fat green caterpillars , newly picked from the gooseberry-bushes , a subtle revenge on Bobby 's part for his expulsion from the sanctum", "context_len": 52}}}, "agreements": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Oh , I know nothing about lawyers nor about legal agreements , '' I answered ; `` but , Jane , there are some things I can not put up with , and one of them is -- -- ''", "context_len": 41}}}, "constructions": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "They talk about him here , these hateful people ; they put false constructions on his kindness ; I know he is kind and he is a gentleman , but he does me harm , Jane , even as much harm as that horrible Mr", "context_len": 45}}}, "commercially": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The thing must be met commercially , or I for one give it up", "context_len": 14}}}, "taxes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "What is to meet the rent , my dear ? What is to meet the taxes ? What is to meet the butcher 's , the baker 's , the grocer 's , the fishmonger 's bills if we dismissed our tenants", "context_len": 42}}}, "fishmonger": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 64, "context": "`` I expected Pattens to send a man in , for I had met him once or twice , and he told me that his bill was not paid , and that he did not mean to supply any more meat , and what Pattens said the baker and greengrocer said too , and so did Allthorp the grocer , and so did the fishmonger , Merriman , and so did all the other tradespeople , and if one spoke to me , so did they all", "context_len": 87}}}, "attracts": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "You never can tell what will happen if you do something that attracts their attention", "context_len": 15}}}, "chiffon": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 43, "context": "Phil took Madge , and very charming she looked in a new gown of -- oh , well , what difference does it make what her dress was like , anyhow ? Besides , I do n't know whether it was bombazine or chiffon , and the more I try to describe it the worse I will get tangled , so if you 'll take my word for it , as well as Phil 's , who ought to know , she looked very pretty indeed", "context_len": 86}}}, "turquoise": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 5, "context": "The yellow chiffon and the turquoise blue bows were much in evidence , and I did really feel that I was a martyr when I went downstairs in that dress with its outr\u00e9 trimmings", "context_len": 34}}}, "vulgarity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "It was the irritating air of vulgarity all over the dress which was so hard to bear", "context_len": 17}}}, "unattractive": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Unattractive and uncomely he was , beyond question , but now his unspeakable gratitude to this kind woman so softened and transformed his face that , could they have seen him , those who fancied they knew him well would have been astonished at the change", "context_len": 46}}}, "expansive": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 51, "context": "Altogether the vehicle had a lordly air , easily matching that of the individual sitting alone on the purple cushions -- a man whose features were not very clear at the distance , although the yellowness of his beard , the glitter of his studded shirt-front , and whole consequential , expansive effect recalled to the doctor 's mind an image of the past , less ornate , indeed , and affluent , but of similar aspect", "context_len": 77}}}, "bedizened": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "She had a quantity of grizzly grey hair , which was turned back from her expansive forehead ; a cap of almost every colour in the rainbow bedizened her head , she wore diamond pendants in her ears , and had a flashing diamond brooch fastening the front of her dress", "context_len": 51}}}, "pendants": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "Jack Frost threw photographs of fairyland upon the windows , and hung the roofs with fringes of crystal pendants , while the snowflakes piled themselves over the fences and made a shroud for the trees , and every day Pauline , with this strange peace in her heart , did her housework to the glory of God", "context_len": 57}}}, "octopus": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Fanning 's had a wonderfully clinging effect ; it seemed to encircle my fingers something like an octopus , and she pulled me gently towards the corner she had in view , and presently had pinned me there , seating herself well in front of me , so that there was no possible escape", "context_len": 54}}}, "pertinacity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Albert was her theme , and she stuck to him with the pertinacity which would have done any leader of a debate credit", "context_len": 23}}}, "artists": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "Oh , he has made me roar , the way he talks of young authors and young artists , how they come to him with their wares , and how he beats them down", "context_len": 34}}}, "snares": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 110, "context": "Vaughan did not take quite such a serious view of the episode , he thought the gipsies were certainly undesirable additions to the countryside , and sent a hint to the local police , with the result that , when Peggy and Bobby screwed up their courage , and dared each other to go and have another peep at the encampment , they found the tent struck and the wanderers flown , with nothing to mark their passing visit but a few dirty pieces of paper and the ashes of the wood fire , though loud complaints from neighbouring farmers as to missing ducks and geese , and traces of snares found by the keepers in the preserves , showed that they had not gone away empty-handed", "context_len": 127}}}, "receptacles": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "Frank was in the midst of the tent , surrounded by several pots , pans , pails , dishes and other receptacles , filled with pancake batter", "context_len": 27}}}, "mementoes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The boxes are receptacles for my household gods , dear , those dear mementoes of the past , that I could not quite part with", "context_len": 25}}}, "gourmands": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "They were both gourmands , and criticised adversely the meal to their mutual pleasure", "context_len": 14}}}, "adversely": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "They were both gourmands , and criticised adversely the meal to their mutual pleasure", "context_len": 14}}}, "smirks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Meanwhile Miss Armstrong was all blushes and smirks", "context_len": 8}}}, "singling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Fanning , I also will take the present opportunity of telling you that you and I have nothing in common , that I dislike your singling me out for special conversations of any kind , and that I hope in the future you will clearly understand that I do not wish you to do so", "context_len": 55}}}, "ponder": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 42, "context": "And if I can not answer poor folk like ourselves , how , O my son , shall I dare to seek the King 's daughter of China , who hath none before him and none after him ? Wherefore do thou ponder this matter in thine understanding", "context_len": 48}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "hassock": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Here , this little hassock at your feet will suit me far better", "context_len": 13}}}, "eavesdropping": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` No , '' replied Jane stoutly , `` for you were eavesdropping when you heard them , and there is no reason why I should explain what you had no right to listen to", "context_len": 35}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Eavesdropping was certainly not one of Peggy 's besetting sins , but she could not help overhearing all that was said , and as the pair rode on down the path towards the gate , she picked up her basket and walked in the same direction on her own side of the hedge , so as to get a good look at them if possible", "context_len": 65}}}, "obliges": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "He will come back again ; but sudden and important news obliges him to go to Australia", "context_len": 17}}}, "mortally": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 3, "context": "Job Stanton was mortally afraid of legal proceedings , and after a while he acceded to the major 's proposal , which was himself to accept a mortgage for the sum of five hundred dollars secured upon the place", "context_len": 39}}}, "unclasping": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "I stood clasping and unclasping my hands , and staring at Jane", "context_len": 12}}}, "sententious": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` We never know beforehand where the shoe is going to pinch , '' remarked Jane in a sententious voice", "context_len": 20}}}, "guileful": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 51, "context": "Randolph comes back everything will be right , but during his absence we will have to go through rather a tight place ; and Albert Fanning is the sort of person who might take advantage of us , and what you must do , my dear girl , is to be guileful", "context_len": 52}}}, "turbot": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Fanning sniffed at her soup instead of eating it this evening , and the turbot was not as fresh as it ought to be", "context_len": 24}}}, "refilling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "The next morning after breakfast I went into the drawing-room for my usual task of dusting and arranging the furniture and refilling the vases with fresh flowers , when Mr", "context_len": 30}}}, "sprays": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "The long , slender sprays flung themselves from tree to tree , or ran climbing over the rocks , while the delicate pink blossoms hung , many of them , within the children 's reach", "context_len": 35}}}, "mimosa": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "To my dying day I shall never forget how that mimosa seemed to slip about , and would not get into the best position in the vase in which I was placing it", "context_len": 33}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "Donkeys , dogs , rats , everything indeed in the way of flesh , had been consumed ; even boot leather , the straps of native bedsteads , and mimosa gum did not come amiss to the sorely-tried garrison", "context_len": 39}}}, "specialist": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 19, "context": "I shall watch his case , and when I see the first indication of the services of some skilled specialist being of benefit to him I will tell you", "context_len": 29}}}, "reade": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 0, "context": "Reade called in the course of the morning , and Dr", "context_len": 11}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Reade must be a very clever physician", "context_len": 7}}}, "diagnosis": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Reade to give me a diagnosis of her case", "context_len": 9}}}, "accelerated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The interest I felt in him immediately became accelerated tenfold", "context_len": 10}}}, "worshipping": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Randolph bent forward and talked to mother in that worshipping son-like way with which he generally addressed her", "context_len": 18}}}, "digestion": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Now Reade is a great specialist for diseases of the digestion", "context_len": 11}}}, "digested": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` So she is , poor dear , and I am inventing quite a new sort of soup , which is partly digested beforehand , that I think she will fancy", "context_len": 31}}}, "consumptives": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Well , my dear , consumptives suffer more than most folks from indigestion", "context_len": 14}}}, "anderson": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "Anderson", "context_len": 1}}}, "aortic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The aortic valve is affected", "context_len": 5}}}, "unnecessarily": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "Florence might have answered that he was too much like his father , but she did not care to anger her guardian unnecessarily , and she simply answered , `` It would be quite impossible for me to regard him as I wish to regard the man whom I hope to marry", "context_len": 52}}}, "warding": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "He was always warding off death , or doing all that man could do to ward it off , and in many many cases death was afraid of him , and retired from his prey , vanquished by that knowledge , that genius , that sympathy , that love for humanity , which overflowed the little doctor 's personality", "context_len": 59}}}, "precarious": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 7, "context": "He loved better than simple work the precarious excitement of prospecting -- an occupation which , except in isolated and accidental instances , can not be pursued to any good save with the aid of science and capital", "context_len": 38}}}, "unwillingness": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 20, "context": "It is just like Aunt Patty to do a thing in this way ; and her very secrecy and her unwillingness to confess why she would not have the grove , or what she intended to do with the money , convinced me that she was sacrificing herself for the good of some other person or persons", "context_len": 57}}}, "daunt": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 9, "context": "Now there ai n't a stair in Britain would daunt me ; I 'd be good for climbing the Monument any fine morning , and it 's all owing to Williams ' Pink Pills", "context_len": 34}}}, "dowdy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 48, "context": "Stirling later learned that their recipient had expressed herself perfectly delighted with the delicate , beautiful gift , but , being a true lady , Bart 's mother said nothing about the matter to those who would have been glad to spread a little gossip unfavorable to the dowdy society queen of Pleasantville", "context_len": 53}}}, "unbecoming": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Alack and alas ! I had no dowdy jackets and no unbecoming hats", "context_len": 13}}}, "nicety": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "I knew exactly or nearly to a nicety , and if I did n't my mother did , what the dinners cost which we ate night after night in your dining-room , and what the furniture must have cost , and what the breakfast cost , and the hundred and one things which were necessary to keep up an establishment of that kind , and I said to the mater , 'Look you here , mater , the incomings are so and so , and the outgoings are so and so , and a smash is inevitable", "context_len": 97}}}, "bus": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 47, "context": "Marvin ; de way I knowed whar he was , was dat I knowed it ; I felt he had flopped ober to de left , so I knowed jes \u2019 de p \u2019 int to aim at ; dat \u2019 s de skience ob de hull bus \u2019 ness", "context_len": 50}}}, "winks": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` I believe I should like to curl up and get a few thousand winks myself", "context_len": 16}}}, "bragged": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 53, "context": "I wonder why Baker was so excited when he heard that name ? I wonder why Lem Wacker bid it up ? Is he aware of the mystery surrounding Baker ? Has this package got something to do with it ? Wacker looked as though he had struck a prosperous streak , and bragged recklessly about the lot of money he could get", "context_len": 63}}}, "gimcrack": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` My baby ! '' said nurse , who seemed to think the baby her own special property , -- '' my baby ! Do you think I 'd risk her neck in a gimcrack like that ? There is n't one of them I 'd trust a hand 's breadth with ye , not if ye was to go down on your bended knees", "context_len": 65}}}, "ventilators": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "I 'd be awful glad to tip the messenger handsomely to have someone at Pleasantville , where they transfer the hives , open the ventilators for a spell and tip down into the pans some of the honey syrup", "context_len": 39}}}, "consummate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Fanning was in his element , and was so pleased with his own consummate common sense and his own skill in getting the right things into the right corners , and in showing me what a mind he had for contriving and for making money go as far as possible , that I allowed him to talk to his heart 's content", "context_len": 62}}}, "infliction": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "There was no escaping this infliction , and accordingly into the grounds we went", "context_len": 14}}}, "wed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "proposals": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 76, "context": "The company on board The Revenge was gradually getting less and less ; the Spanish ships , meanwhile , as they received a sufficient evidence of The Revenge's powers of destruction , dropped off , and their places were taken by others ; and thus it happened that ere the morning fifteen ships had been engaged , and all were so little pleased with the entertainment provided that they were far more willing to listen to proposals for an honourable arrangement than to make any more assaults", "context_len": 87}}}, "repulsing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "I wondered if the man cared for me , perhaps he did after his fashion , but as no self-denials on my part had the effect of repulsing him , I would give way to my fancy and dress properly for dinner", "context_len": 42}}}, "buoying": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "There is no use in buoying people up with false hopes", "context_len": 11}}}, "marion": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Marion , behind whose chair Mr", "context_len": 6}}}, "initiate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Fanning 's calibre to initiate", "context_len": 5}}}, "fichu": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 71, "context": "So I put away the new hat and pretty jacket and went down to mother , and I amused mother by showing her the lace I had bought , and I told her all about the Duchess , and mother was much pleased at the thought of seeing her old friend on the following morning , and she and I sat that afternoon in the drawing-room making up the pretty lace fichu , and I resolved that mother should wear it the next day when the Duchess came", "context_len": 88}}}, "duchess": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "She strolled up to the astonished children with the dignity of a duchess", "context_len": 13}}}, "socialistic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` You are so ridiculously proud and so ridiculously socialistic in all your ideas", "context_len": 14}}}, "bagatelle": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "He had many important matters on his mind , matters in which hundreds of thousands were concerned , and two gold bars were a mere bagatelle", "context_len": 26}}}, "inexplicable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` This is very sudden and terrible , '' he said ; `` very inexplicable too", "context_len": 16}}}, "agitate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Do not say anything to agitate her", "context_len": 7}}}, "ponderously": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Jane said the words almost ponderously , and then she threw her hands to her sides and gazed at me with an expression which I can not by any possibility describe", "context_len": 31}}}, "unintentionally": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "Because we deceived our landlord , unintentionally it is true , but nevertheless we deceived him , with promises which we can not fulfil , he can take back the lease of this house if he pleases , and take it back he will , because our paying guests do n't pay , because the whole thing from first to last is a miserable failure", "context_len": 65}}}, "lease": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 51, "context": "`` It 's wonderful , my love , the interest he takes in it , '' she said ; `` I never saw anything like it in the whole course of my life , and for a publisher too ! But his idea is no less than this : When the lease of the next house falls in , we take it too , and break open doors , and have the two houses instead of one", "context_len": 77}}}, "takings": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "Before the third hour of the day all had been done which the simple habits of the time required , and only the sorrowful leave takings remained", "context_len": 27}}}, "ledger": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Here are the takings '' -- she pointed to one side of the ledger -- '' here are the expenses '' -- she pointed to the other -- '' expenses so much , takings so much , look at the balance , Westenra", "context_len": 43}}}, "tradespeople": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 74, "context": "`` I expected Pattens to send a man in , for I had met him once or twice , and he told me that his bill was not paid , and that he did not mean to supply any more meat , and what Pattens said the baker and greengrocer said too , and so did Allthorp the grocer , and so did the fishmonger , Merriman , and so did all the other tradespeople , and if one spoke to me , so did they all", "context_len": 87}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` But I do hope , dear Miss Mullins , '' said mother , turning to her and looking at her fixedly , `` that you pay the tradespeople weekly", "context_len": 30}}}, "overtired": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Have I overtired her ? ''", "context_len": 6}}}, "topple": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 101, "context": "If we had it we could go on , we could go on at least till the end of the season , and there would be an excuse to take your mother to the country , and she might never know , never ; but it wants two months to the end of the season , and the house is full , and every one is in the height of good humour , and yet they are all walking on the brink of a precipice ; the earth is eaten away beneath us , and any moment the whole thing may topple through", "context_len": 103}}}, "dawns": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Even at the very worst day dawns but it is black night at present , that it is , '' said the faithful creature", "context_len": 24}}}, "diary": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 7, "context": "The voluminous pages of Father Cuthbert 's diary for the years of bitter woe and misery which followed can not be fully transcribed ; they would fill a volume themselves , and we must content ourselves with a few extracts , which will probably interest our readers , and carry on the thread of the history to the place where our narrative will again flow free and uninterrupted", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "You can read for yourself from the reproduction of the last page of his diary , written on the 14th December , 1884 , his own estimate of the length of time he could hold out ; and , though he managed to keep back the enemy for another month , yet on the 26th January , 1885 , whilst yet Sir Charles Wilson and the British troops were fighting their way up the river Nile to his relief , Khartoum fell", "context_len": 82}}}, "tasteful": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "There were some vases of flowers , and the plate , and china , the polished glass , the snowy napery , all looked as tasteful , as fresh , as pretty , as heart could desire", "context_len": 37}}}, "podgy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "A little short man , stout and podgy , in a greasy coat , and a greasy waistcoat , and a dirty tie , rose as I entered", "context_len": 28}}}, "justifies": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "You 're the daughter of the lady who owns this house , and I have heard that you own it partly yourself ; and it 's this paper that justifies me , miss , and I ca n't go out", "context_len": 40}}}, "succumbing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` There 's the way of succumbing like , and going into hysterics , and making no end of a scene , and the man stays on all the same , and the neighbours get wind of it , and the ruin 's complete in no time , so to speak", "context_len": 51}}}, "totter": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "The next moment everything in the room seemed to go round , and I was obliged to totter towards a chair", "context_len": 21}}}, "smug": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` What is your name ? '' I heard her say to him as he brought her some vegetables , and he replied in a smug , comfortable voice , `` Robert , ma'am", "context_len": 34}}}, "rigors": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` She had a sharp attack , '' continued Jane , `` rigors and extreme weakness", "context_len": 16}}}, "embarrassments": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "We would be relieved from our embarrassments , mother would receive no shock if I promised to marry Albert Fanning", "context_len": 20}}}, "quaked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "My heart trembled , my spirit quaked", "context_len": 7}}}, "roughing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` She has not had any of the roughing", "context_len": 9}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "I have been used to roughing it in a nice way with some of my rich masters , but I certainly would n't like to put in a winter here with those daylight chinks between the big logs", "context_len": 38}}}, "counterpane": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Vaughan was on the point of following their example when he noticed a curious and most unusual lump underneath his counterpane", "context_len": 21}}}, "balmy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The closeness and oppression of the day before had changed into a most balmy and delicious spring morning ; a sort of foretaste day of early summer", "context_len": 27}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Although the weather now was balmy , there were times when it raged like a hurricane from the Arctic regions", "context_len": 20}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "It was bright and balmy", "context_len": 5}}}, "stodgy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "`` Not bad for a city man 's office , eh ? '' he continued , `` all the books are amusing ; you might like to dip into 'em by-and-by , nothing deep or dull , or stodgy here , all light , frothy , and merry", "context_len": 48}}}, "frothy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 44, "context": "`` Not bad for a city man 's office , eh ? '' he continued , `` all the books are amusing ; you might like to dip into 'em by-and-by , nothing deep or dull , or stodgy here , all light , frothy , and merry", "context_len": 48}}}, "mater": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 5, "context": "I found out from the mater that you were Miss Wickham , and that your father had been a general officer in the army , and you lived in Mayfair , and went into society ; and often and often I went into the Park to catch a glimpse of you , and I got the number of your house , and sometimes I passed it by and looked up at the windows , and once I saw you there ; you were arranging some flowers", "context_len": 86}}}, "blurted": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` It 's me , '' blurted Joel , forgetting grammar and everything else , and pulling away from her , he slipped off the sofa and began a quick pace to the door", "context_len": 34}}}, "repellant": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "He lifted his head once more and looked around , but the disdainful and repellant faces he saw about him checked the words , and he turned despairingly away", "context_len": 29}}}, "outgoings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 86, "context": "I knew exactly or nearly to a nicety , and if I did n't my mother did , what the dinners cost which we ate night after night in your dining-room , and what the furniture must have cost , and what the breakfast cost , and the hundred and one things which were necessary to keep up an establishment of that kind , and I said to the mater , 'Look you here , mater , the incomings are so and so , and the outgoings are so and so , and a smash is inevitable", "context_len": 97}}}, "honester": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 51, "context": "Why should n't he take a wife a peg above him ? It 's done every day , and why should not his wife be happy ? What is there against that house at Highgate , for instance , and what is there against the old woman ? Is there an honester or a better heart than hers ? ''", "context_len": 60}}}, "sloes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "There was one , I never saw blacker eyes than hers ; they were as black as sloes , and I always admired black eyes , because I am fair , you know , and the mater is fair", "context_len": 39}}}, "tiniest": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "You were bending forward , doing something for your mother , putting a shawl about her or something , and you just gave the tiniest bit of a smile , and I saw a gleam of your teeth , and I looked at your grey eyes ; and , upon my word , it was all over with me", "context_len": 59}}}, "courting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "So then we arranged that we 'd come and live in the boarding-house , and I began my courting in hot earnest , and fortune has favoured me , Miss Wickham ; fortune has favoured me , Westenra , and oh ! I love you , God knows how much , and I 'd be a good husband to you , and you should have your own way in everything", "context_len": 70}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 71, "context": "I wouldn \u2019 t ; he \u2019 s a particular old toad , and would rather have it just as it grew ; but if you touch it , he \u2019 ll think you \u2019 ve taken off some where you ought not to , and spilte it ; he \u2019 ll no more thank you for saving him labor on a float-piece , than a feller would thank you for courting a girl for him ; he \u2019 d rather do it himself", "context_len": 84}}}, "rhythm": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "The explosion in the cylinders was unfailing , the mixture of air and gasoline was perfect , and the coils hummed their beautiful rhythm to the well-timed spark", "context_len": 28}}}, "jarring": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "That all the jarring notes of life Seem blending in a psalm , And all the angles of its strife Slow rounding into calm", "context_len": 24}}}, "psalm": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Do n't you remember ? `` Life is what we make it -- an anthem or a dirge , a psalm of hope or a lamentation of despair", "context_len": 28}}}, "harms": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "If nothing harms her , if she gets no shock , how long will my mother live ? ''", "context_len": 19}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "'Therefore , they thought it good you hear a play , And frame your mind to mirth and merriment , Which bars a thousand harms , and lengthens life", "context_len": 29}}}, "weaving": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 9, "context": "Lilian transformed the pulpit into a perfect bower , weaving in holly and laurels till the Rector hoped she would leave room for him inside it ; and Peggy and Bobby helped -- or hindered -- the schoolmistress as she adorned the font with a wonderful arrangement of cotton-wool and frosted leaves", "context_len": 52}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 82, "context": "May it please Your Honor , said Sleuth loftily , I have been requested by the attorney for the defense to tell my story in my own way , and ere I have finished I will demonstrate to your satisfaction and the satisfaction of every person present that every word I speak has a bearing upon the matter and is necessary to explain the reasons which led up to my deduction involving the before-mentioned Timothy Davis in a network of his own weaving , from which I think he will scarcely escape with ease", "context_len": 94}}}, "lifelike": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 69, "context": "Peggy and Bobby as 'the children , ' with crumpled pinafores and smudged faces , were an equal success , for in watching Archie Peggy had forgotten her sudden shyness , and she now threw herself thoroughly into her part , howling most realistically when her nose was scrubbed in the wash-tub , or her hair combed with a fork ; while Bobby stole jam and resisted medicine with lifelike zeal", "context_len": 71}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "Her bright eyes were so sparkling with fun and mischief that they looked as if they would almost dance out of the picture , and the mouth was so smiling and lifelike that it seemed as if the rosy lips must part the next moment with a joyous , ringing laugh", "context_len": 51}}}, "slam": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "In desperation the locals made a furious slam into center , recovering , however , barely the distance lost ; and then , forced to it , Ramsdal fell back to kick", "context_len": 32}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "The Japs will still be blinking the sleep out of their eyes when we slam \u2019 em", "context_len": 17}}}, "extensions": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "stilled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "One woman rose superior to fear -- the Lady Hilda ; aided by her , I stilled the tumult , and we took hasty counsel together", "context_len": 26}}}, "tailors": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 58, "context": "`` `` O my son , '' quoth she , `` God deliver me from this ! What , have I lost my wits like thee ? Put away this thought from thy mind and bethink thee who thou art , O my son , -- the son of a tailor , the poorest and least of the tailors in this city , and I also am thy mother and my folk are exceeding poor ; so how wilt thou dare to demand the Sultan 's daughter , whom her father would not vouchsafe to marry with kings ' sons and Sultans , except they were his peers in puissance and rank and noblesse ; nay , were they one degree less than he , he would not", "context_len": 128}}}, "flaxen": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "Her light flaxen hair and pale , fair complexion gave her a smooth , shining appearance , and somehow Peggy always thought her manners were smooth and shining too , for she had a way of wriggling out of any little difficulty and unpleasantness , so that the blame rested upon other people , and was always ready to take a mean advantage , or play some of those little underhand tricks which schoolgirls know only too well", "context_len": 78}}}, "picturing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` I am picturing the time when the year is out , '' he said slowly , `` when you and I are away together in the country", "context_len": 28}}}, "embody": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "You embody a great deal to me , you make poetry for me", "context_len": 13}}}, "writ": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` I have put down the date , '' he said ; `` I put it down in my note-book ; I am going to keep it always by me ; it is writ in my heart too", "context_len": 38}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 44, "context": "Therewith Alaeddin arose and would have taken leave ; but the Sultan laid hold on him and said to him , `` Whither away , O my son ? The bride-feast is toward and the bride present ; the knot is knotted and the writ written", "context_len": 46}}}, "s\u00e8vres": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "S\u00e8vres china is nothing to you", "context_len": 6}}}, "daintier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "I have seen dainty bits of art sold at Christy 's before now , but there never was anything daintier than you before in the world , and I love you , there ! I have said it", "context_len": 38}}}, "unreturned": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 60, "context": "But he gave me to understand that if for any reason his return was delayed I might act on my own discretion , and tell you what comforts me beyond all possible words , and what may also cheer you , for I can scarcely think , my darling , that the love of a man like that would be unreturned by a girl like you , when once you knew , Westenra , when once you surely knew", "context_len": 79}}}, "pilgrimage": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 12, "context": "Joseph 's tree at Glastonbury , and that he was there on pilgrimage when Alfgar saw him -- saw him , mark you -- at the Danish camp on the borders of Sussex ; and I saw men , I wo n't mention names , who had more than once taken reward to slay the innocent , look as if they would go down on their knees to this holy thorn , which was n't a holy thorn at all , but plucked from some hedge hard at hand", "context_len": 89}}}, "heliotrope": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 59, "context": "The tiny fences were pulled up , and scattered far and wide ; lady-slippers , mignonette , verbenas , and all the other simple flowers which had flourished so well , and given such pride and delight to the little gardeners , were rooted up and trampled into the earth ; and , worse than all , the beloved heliotrope and geranium were torn leaf from leaf and sprig from sprig , while their main stems had been twisted and bent , till no hope remained that even these could be revived", "context_len": 92}}}, "revulsion": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The next instant , however , a revulsion came over me , and I found that I was glad , very glad , that he was alive , that he was in the land of the living , that I had a chance of seeing him from time to time", "context_len": 50}}}, "dividend": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "At that time these stocks were paying a high dividend , and everything seemed to be going well ; but you had not been three months in the boarding-house before the bank in Melbourne which held such a large amount of my money went smash , and I was obliged to go over to secure what was left", "context_len": 58}}}, "despairs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "I longed most passionately to see him , and so I was torn from hour to hour and from minute to minute with longings and doubts and despairs ; but all through everything , I kept my secret untold within my breast", "context_len": 42}}}, "baronetcy": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "In a few years at latest he will be Sir James Severn , for of course he must take the name with the baronetcy , and you will be his pretty wife", "context_len": 32}}}, "flashy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "I would not allow him to give me a flashy or showy ring , as he wanted to do , and I think he would gladly have spent a couple of hundred pounds on my engagement-ring , but I would not have it , not until the whole thing was known , then he might lavish jewellery on me as much as he pleased for all I cared", "context_len": 68}}}, "consummated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "As bon\u00e2 fide as there is a day and a night ; as bon\u00e2 fide as that I am in existence ; but the marriage is not to be consummated until the 1st of June of next year", "context_len": 38}}}, "pedestal": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 9, "context": "It represents the bare cost of a new copper pedestal to replace the one you shot to pieces last night , and it 's a wonder you are not in jail for murder , for had that cannon ball struck a human being -- Enough ! before I take up this outrage with the district attorney in its criminal phase , are you going to settle the damage , or are you not ? ''", "context_len": 75}}}, "silhouetted": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "It was silhouetted in the landscape on a slight slant towards the stately mansion and grounds of Colonel Harrington , in full view at all times of the magnate who had improved its surroundings", "context_len": 34}}}, "radiate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The sky seemed to radiate heat , and the earth seemed to embrace it ; and we poor human beings who were subjected to it scarcely knew what to do with ourselves", "context_len": 32}}}, "efface": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "You may be quite sure that the old mother will efface herself in order to give her son and her dear new daughter every possible opportunity for enjoying life", "context_len": 29}}}, "ennobles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "It is wonderful what a power love has , how it ennobles and purifies and sanctifies , and raises , and Albert 's love was very unselfish -- how utterly unselfish , I was to know before long", "context_len": 38}}}, "purifies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "It is wonderful what a power love has , how it ennobles and purifies and sanctifies , and raises , and Albert 's love was very unselfish -- how utterly unselfish , I was to know before long", "context_len": 38}}}, "sanctifies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "It is wonderful what a power love has , how it ennobles and purifies and sanctifies , and raises , and Albert 's love was very unselfish -- how utterly unselfish , I was to know before long", "context_len": 38}}}, "pined": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Our father has long pined for you , '' he said , in a timid voice ; `` I fear you are too late , and that he will hardly know you", "context_len": 33}}}, "undulate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "He had a way of walking on his toes , so that his tall figure seemed to undulate up and down as he approached you", "context_len": 25}}}, "bided": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "Peter Grimm , notoriously the stingiest man in Pleasantville , who raised the sourest apples in the town and spent most of his time watching the boys and picking up what fruit rolled outside of the fence , bided his time with watchful ferret eyes until a promising-looking package came along", "context_len": 51}}}, "consecutively": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "From the quiet of despair my mind was suddenly roused to a perfect whirl of anxiety , and I could not think consecutively", "context_len": 23}}}, "trepidation": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` Ca n't a fellow take a run around the house without anything being the matter with him ? '' asked Lewis , sharply , but with a little nervous trepidation in his tone and manner ; but the subject was now dropped , and he had more than recovered his composure and was taking an apparently interested part in the renewed discussion over Percy 's loss , when the enraged Seabrooke entered the room", "context_len": 75}}}, "thundery": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` It is very good of you , Westenra ; we shall have a delightful evening ; all that thundery feeling has gone out of the air , everything is crisp and fresh , and you 'll enjoy your drive", "context_len": 40}}}, "unrestrainedly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "I had never heard him speak more freely nor unrestrainedly", "context_len": 10}}}, "shack": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 63, "context": "Men who had prospected for precious metals all summer in the mountains now bundled their pots and pans and blankets back to shelter for the winter ; the long-eared burros , lost in great rolls of bedding , stood about the tipple awaiting the result of their masters ' interviews with the mine boss , concerning work and the occupancy of any `` shack '' that might still be empty", "context_len": 70}}}, "unfeminine": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Jane , with her rugged , middle-aged face , baggy blouse , hob-nailed shoes and man 's hat , was so unfeminine a figure as she plowed and planted her little vega , that some village wag had once referred to her as `` Annie Laurie", "context_len": 46}}}, "vega": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "Jane , with her rugged , middle-aged face , baggy blouse , hob-nailed shoes and man 's hat , was so unfeminine a figure as she plowed and planted her little vega , that some village wag had once referred to her as `` Annie Laurie", "context_len": 46}}}, "sterling": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` Because of its happy absurdity the name long clung to Jane ; but despite such small jests every one respected her sterling traits , -- every one , that is , except Se\u00f1ora Vigil , who lived hard by in a mud house like a bird 's nest , and who cherished a grudge against her neighbor", "context_len": 58}}}, "muddle": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Jane would have waived her right , but the surveyor said that the land office could not `` muddle up '' the records in any such way ; she must take her land", "context_len": 33}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "'She calls it `` taking us just as we are , '' but then we aren't generally in a muddle like this , so it does n't seem quite fair", "context_len": 30}}}, "antedated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "From the Mexican end of town , -- the old `` plaza , '' -- which antedated coal-mines and Americanisms , gleamed the little gold cross of the adobe Church of San Antonio", "context_len": 33}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "of the Cabinet des Fees ( printed in 1793 , though antedated 1788 and 1789 ) do not form the first edition of Chavis and Cazotte 's so-called Sequel , which was in 1793 added , by way of supplement , to the Cabinet des Fees , having been first published in 1788 ( two years after the completion-in thirty-seven volumes-of that great storehouse of supernatural fiction ) under the title of `` Les Veillees Persanes '' or `` Les Veillees du Sultan Schahriar avec la Sultane Scheherazade , histoires incroyables , amusantes et morales , traduites par M", "context_len": 99}}}, "adobe": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 75, "context": "She laid down her work to wipe her eyes , and , observing two of her sons grappling in fraternal war at the house corner , she arose to cuff each one impartially , exclaiming , `` Ea , muchachos ! You fight before my very eyes , eh ? Take that ! and that ! '' Waddling reluctantly back to her sewing , she saw Lola standing in the white-pillared porch of the big adobe house beyond , and a gleam of inspiration crossed the se\u00f1ora 's dark , fat face", "context_len": 92}}}, "cottonwoods": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "One is made to see the green , tall cottonwoods , the straggling mud-houses and pungent goat-corrals of its people , among whom lived the woman who took to her great heart the motherless Lola", "context_len": 35}}}, "tipple": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 41, "context": "Men who had prospected for precious metals all summer in the mountains now bundled their pots and pans and blankets back to shelter for the winter ; the long-eared burros , lost in great rolls of bedding , stood about the tipple awaiting the result of their masters ' interviews with the mine boss , concerning work and the occupancy of any `` shack '' that might still be empty", "context_len": 70}}}, "colliery": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Toward the ca\u00f1on rose the tipple and fans of the Dauntless colliery , banked in slack and slate , and surrounded by paintless mine-houses , while to the right swept the ugly shape of the company 's store", "context_len": 38}}}, "miners": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 11, "context": "And here he was , practising among a few Mexicans and miners , tending their bruises , doling them out quinine , and taking pay of a dollar a month from every man , sick or well , enrolled on the mine books , and frequently getting nothing at all from such as were not therein enrolled", "context_len": 57}}}, "slaty": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Alejandro 's red cap was a mere speck in the ca\u00f1on , and his herd was sprinkled , like bread-crumbs , over the slaty hills", "context_len": 25}}}, "untended": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Three horses were hobbled near by , and a little fire smoked itself out , untended", "context_len": 16}}}, "farer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "The whole thing meant merely the night halt of some farer to the mountains", "context_len": 14}}}, "peaked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "This man wore a broad felt hat , whose peaked crown was bound in a silver cord which glittered gaily above the startled whiteness of his face", "context_len": 27}}}, "distractedly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "He looked distractedly about , and seeing Jane Combs in her field , called to her and came running", "context_len": 19}}}, "sombrero": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 3, "context": "He wore a sombrero , with a fringed hunting shirt , leggings and moccasins , and rode a powerful , bony Indian horse , larger than any animal in the train", "context_len": 31}}}, "mia": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 55, "context": "I remember , as it were but yesterday , the coming of the Se\u00f1ora , Benita said , and the Se\u00f1or calling Benita ! Oh , Benita ! Here is your new mistress ! \u2019 She was but the young thing -- that little Se\u00f1ora -- not much older than you are now , Se\u00f1orita mia , and with the face all bright and the eyes so expressive -- like yours", "context_len": 71}}}, "wordless": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "And as she stood wordless , with dimming eyes , there came from without a sound of mingling voices", "context_len": 19}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "For wordless moments every man in the cockpit gazed at the orderly patchwork of little fields below", "context_len": 17}}}, "dimming": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "And as she stood wordless , with dimming eyes , there came from without a sound of mingling voices", "context_len": 19}}}, "doughy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Behind him rose the round , doughy visage of his wife , blank with awe", "context_len": 15}}}, "irresistibly": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 35, "context": "First Sobrinini \u2019 s voice -- he could not be mistaken in it now -- then other voices joining in a sort of weird chant that chilled the lad \u2019 s blood yet drew him irresistibly toward the point from which the sound was coming", "context_len": 45}}}, "deplored": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` If I 'd only known how to call the poor little soul a lot of coaxing names ! '' deplored Jane , `` Then maybe she 'd have come with me", "context_len": 32}}}, "mats": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 20, "context": "She 'd have been better off sleeping on my good feather bed than what she will on those ragged Mexican mats over to Vigil 's", "context_len": 25}}}, "burros": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 29, "context": "Men who had prospected for precious metals all summer in the mountains now bundled their pots and pans and blankets back to shelter for the winter ; the long-eared burros , lost in great rolls of bedding , stood about the tipple awaiting the result of their masters ' interviews with the mine boss , concerning work and the occupancy of any `` shack '' that might still be empty", "context_len": 70}}}, "alfalfa": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` Then , observing that two burros and several goats , taking advantage of the open gate , were now gorging themselves on her alfalfa , she proceeded to make a stern end of their delight", "context_len": 36}}}, "colts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Early in the morning of the stranger 's burial , Mexicans from up the ca\u00f1on and down the creek arrived in town in ramshackle wagons , attended by dogs and colts", "context_len": 31}}}, "unfriended": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "'Would you like a game of croquet , Margaret ? ' said Muriel , noticing at last that Peggy stood unfriended and alone", "context_len": 23}}}, "poorest": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 53, "context": "`` `` O my son , '' quoth she , `` God deliver me from this ! What , have I lost my wits like thee ? Put away this thought from thy mind and bethink thee who thou art , O my son , -- the son of a tailor , the poorest and least of the tailors in this city , and I also am thy mother and my folk are exceeding poor ; so how wilt thou dare to demand the Sultan 's daughter , whom her father would not vouchsafe to marry with kings ' sons and Sultans , except they were his peers in puissance and rank and noblesse ; nay , were they one degree less than he , he would not", "context_len": 128}}}, "austere": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "For Jane , weighted with the gravity of the occasion , had donned an austere black bonnet such as aged ladies wear , and its effect upon her short locks was incongruous in the extreme", "context_len": 35}}}, "incongruous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "For Jane , weighted with the gravity of the occasion , had donned an austere black bonnet such as aged ladies wear , and its effect upon her short locks was incongruous in the extreme", "context_len": 35}}}, "sunburned": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "No one , however , thought of her as being more queer than usual ; for her sunburned cheeks were wet with tears , and her eyes were deep with tenderness and pity as they fixed themselves upon the small , rigid figure in the shadows of the altar 's dark burden", "context_len": 52}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "Three days later Rosy O \u2019 Grady \u2019 s sunburned crew had lost ten or fifteen pounds apiece , but the roadway of perforated steel was completed", "context_len": 27}}}, "flume": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Upon the following day , as Miss Combs opened her ditch-gate for the tide of mine water which came in a flume across the arroyo , she saw the doctor and Mr", "context_len": 32}}}, "arroyo": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "Upon the following day , as Miss Combs opened her ditch-gate for the tide of mine water which came in a flume across the arroyo , she saw the doctor and Mr", "context_len": 32}}}, "peons": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` Her mother would n't rest in her grave , '' cried Keene , `` if she knew the child was being brought up amongst a tribe of peons ! And me -- I want my child to grow up an American citizen , ma'am ! ''", "context_len": 47}}}, "collapsible": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "That night a boy brought to Jane 's door a queer little collapsible trunk of sun-cured hide , thonged fast with leather loops", "context_len": 23}}}, "saddening": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Keene had sent it because he dreaded its saddening associations", "context_len": 10}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` You 're lucky to come while we 've got a home to welcome you to , '' said Job , his face again saddening", "context_len": 25}}}, "bland": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The other 's face grew very bland", "context_len": 7}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 24, "context": "The tame animal immediately started off at great speed , arching his neck and shaking his head , while the poor Chinaman , his bland smile succeeded by a look of extreme terror , was bounced up and down in the most unceremonious fashion , and would have been thrown off quickly but for the Mexican saddle , which is a securer seat than that used at the East", "context_len": 69}}}, "ven": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "But it is for you to tell her you come to fetch her away ! '' And the se\u00f1ora called out , `` Lolita , ven aca ! ''", "context_len": 29}}}, "unwisely": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "She referred him , with her own full consent , to her father ; and Elfwyn says , not unwisely , that he can not consent until the land is at peace ; that it is currently reported that Thurkill , a Danish earl , is at hand with an immense fleet , and that to marry might both hamper a warrior 's hands and be the means of bringing up children for the sword", "context_len": 75}}}, "dizzily": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 54, "context": "`` Is he gone -- my father ? But no ! he would not leave me behind ! No ! no ! Dejeme ! dejeme ! you do not say the truth ! You shall not touch me ! I will not -- will not go with you ! '' She turned wildly , dizzily , as if about to run she knew not where ; and then flung herself down before Se\u00f1ora Vigil , clasping the Mexican woman 's knees in a frantic , fainting grasp", "context_len": 87}}}, "neutrality": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Jane helplessly regarded the child 's despair , while Se\u00f1ora Vigil maintained an attitude curiously significant of deep compassion and a profound intention of neutrality", "context_len": 25}}}, "refusals": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "Perhaps she would not have gone had she been able to do so , for she broke forth into angry cries and refusals to be moved , which were only stopped by a violent fit of coughing", "context_len": 37}}}, "deploring": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "Even now he was traveling through the green hills , regretting that he had left his child behind him at the instance of a strange woman ! Even now he was doubtless deploring that he should have been moved to consider another 's loneliness before his own", "context_len": 47}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "And Polly took her departure with a conscious sniff , as if deploring the depravity of her neighbours", "context_len": 18}}}, "stealth": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 73, "context": "He had been exasperated to a great degree by Flagg 's endeavor to drug him on the night of the expedition to Rice 's , and that with good reason ; and now his suspicions , nay , more than suspicions aroused that he was trying to make it appear that he , Seabrooke , had wrongfully kept Percy 's money and then pretended that the latter had taken it from him by stealth , enraged him beyond bounds", "context_len": 79}}}, "pyramids": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Before her the great Spanish Peaks heaved their blue pyramids against the desert sky", "context_len": 14}}}, "gorge": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 36, "context": "Could those odd sounding signals which the single warrior sent forward from his perch on the rocks have had anything to do with it ? Did they cause the forbearance of his comrades farther up the gorge ? That such should be the case seemed incredible , but days afterward Alden submitted the question to Shagbark", "context_len": 56}}}, "spume": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "Before the rain began to fall on Lola 's bare head , as it did shortly in sheets , the stream-bed had become a raging torrent , down which froth and spume and uprooted saplings were spinning", "context_len": 37}}}, "uprooted": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "'All the things which we care for most will be done away with , ' grieved Peggy -- 'the dear old tower pulled down , the ruins destroyed , the garden uprooted , and the woods cleared away", "context_len": 38}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "There a forest monarch , uprooted by the gale , lay prostrate", "context_len": 12}}}, "pi\u00f1on": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "There was a porch , too , with snow-white pillars , and an open fireplace , all tiled with adobe , in which might blaze fires of pi\u00f1on wood , full of resin and burning as nothing else can burn save driftwood , sodden with salt and oil and the mystery of old ocean", "context_len": 54}}}, "herder": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The rain had slackened when Bev Gribble , looking from his herder 's hut up on the mesa , saw that his `` bunch '' of cattle had disappeared", "context_len": 29}}}, "cloudburst": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "That there had been a cloudburst over toward the Peaks he was as yet ignorant ; nor did he discover this until he had caught his cow-pony and descended into the ravine", "context_len": 32}}}, "ravine": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 67, "context": "This he did five or six times in succession , to the great amusement and satisfaction of the little ones , who were beginning to hope he would go with them all the way to the house , when with a pert , defiant whisk of his bushy tail , he leaped down the bank , and was lost to sight in the thick trees of the ravine", "context_len": 68}}}, "lucidly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "Price at the post-office said she had heard Sir Somebody Wilkins was a very great artist in London , and had pictures in the Royal Something-or-other , ' explained Bobby lucidly", "context_len": 31}}}, "bronco": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Sus ! Sus ! '' said Bev to his pony , who knew Spanish best , being a bronco from the south", "context_len": 23}}}, "lasso": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 43, "context": "On came the attacking party ; he faced them , and with a coolness that did him credit at such a critical moment he picked out the one that he could most easily capture , and was in the act of hurling the lasso , when , up from the very midst of the hawthorn bushes at the back of the fort , Hal 's voice was heard again", "context_len": 69}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` I approve , '' he said ; `` only look out for that lasso trick", "context_len": 16}}}, "laxly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "A drenched thing it was which huddled at the roadside ; very limp , indeed , and laxly lending itself to the motions of Gribble 's hands as he lifted and shook it", "context_len": 33}}}, "scrolling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "When the coal-camp came in sight on the green skirt of the plains , with the Apishapa scrolling the distance in a velvet ribbon , sunset was already forward , and the smoke of many an evening fire veined the late sky", "context_len": 42}}}, "veined": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "When the coal-camp came in sight on the green skirt of the plains , with the Apishapa scrolling the distance in a velvet ribbon , sunset was already forward , and the smoke of many an evening fire veined the late sky", "context_len": 42}}}, "broncos": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` As this consoling assurance seemed not to lessen Jane 's alarm , he went on cheerfully to say , `` There is n't one in my body has n't been splintered by these broncos ! Tinker 'em up and they 're better than new", "context_len": 45}}}, "doc": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Here 's doc coming lickety-switch ! He 'll tell you the same", "context_len": 12}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` The doc was here a while ago , and left some new liniment that he said would soften up the strained muscles and ligaments", "context_len": 25}}}, "husk": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "He , she knew , viewed this life as merely the stepping-stone to a fuller and richer life beyond , and the body as but the worthless husk of the soul , so with a dreamy feeling that somehow Mr", "context_len": 40}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "When Bessie reached her playfellows , the fire was burning famously ; but they had waited to husk the corn till she should come to have her share in that pleasure", "context_len": 31}}}, "gamboling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "She watched the Vigils playing , the kids gamboling , the magpies squabbling ; but never a lighter look stirred the chill calm of her little , russet-toned features , or the sombre depths of her dark , long eyes", "context_len": 40}}}, "squabbling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "She watched the Vigils playing , the kids gamboling , the magpies squabbling ; but never a lighter look stirred the chill calm of her little , russet-toned features , or the sombre depths of her dark , long eyes", "context_len": 40}}}, "wishful": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 53, "context": "When I am fishing for flounders he will sit by me till I pull up a fish , and then he will swallow it in no time ; sometimes I say , Rover , you can \u2019 t have that ; it is for the house ; \u2019 and he will look so wishful I have to give it to him", "context_len": 61}}}, "untruth": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` Of course he would remember it , my dear ! '' she said , on the instant ; she consoled her conscience by reflecting that there was no untruth in her words", "context_len": 33}}}, "measurably": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Keene had sent never a word or sign to Aguilar , it was measurably certain that he remembered his obligations", "context_len": 20}}}, "pigtails": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "She had a queer little half-civilized look in the neat pigtails which Jane considered appropriate to her age , and which were so tightly braided as fairly to draw up the girl 's eyebrows", "context_len": 34}}}, "braided": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "She had a queer little half-civilized look in the neat pigtails which Jane considered appropriate to her age , and which were so tightly braided as fairly to draw up the girl 's eyebrows", "context_len": 34}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "There were red ribbons also in Lola 's braided hair ; and the girl , although still aware of bitter wrongs , was sensible of being pleased with her raiment", "context_len": 30}}}, "garland": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "To garland that viny strip in Lola 's locks was beyond Jane 's power", "context_len": 14}}}, "thorn": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 11, "context": "However , by leaving scraps of her clothing on every sharp thorn , and getting her hands and legs terribly scratched , she forced her way out at last ; and keeping a wary outlook on the fort , she tried to unloose the knots that bound Jim", "context_len": 48}, "UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 39, "context": "Dear little pilgrims ! there were jewels in their way such as they did not dream of ; but it was only earnest seeking such as theirs which could find them ; for they lay hidden beneath many a thorn and bramble and unsightly weed ; and they were to be found only by the help of this very jewel of prayer which shone so brightly that its light guided the little feet to the dark places where the hidden gems lay", "context_len": 82}}}, "mil": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Lola had seized it and was kissing the big fingers passionately , while she cried , `` Gracias ! mil gracias , se\u00f1or ! You have made me well ! When my papa comes he will bless you ! He will pour gold over you from head to foot ! ''", "context_len": 51}}}, "symptom": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 45, "context": "That Lola should be engaged with mud casas was , indeed , hardly in accord with Jane 's experience of the girl 's dignity ; but that she should be playing ever so foolishly in a slush of clay delighted Jane as being a healthful symptom", "context_len": 46}}}, "bulbous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "It was a square pedestal of adobe , sun-baked hard as stone , upon which sat a queer adobe creature , with a lean body and a great bulbous head", "context_len": 30}}}, "anatomy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "This personage showed the presence in his anatomy of an element of finely chopped straw", "context_len": 15}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "'Why so ? Is there nothing in the world but suffrage , and opium and -- anatomy ? '", "context_len": 19}}}, "knobby": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "From his widely open mouth hung a thirsty mud tongue , and between his knobby knees he held an empty bowl , toward the filling of which his whole expression seemed an invocation", "context_len": 33}}}, "invocation": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "Alaeddin did that which behoveth and befitteth unto kings of obeisance and invocation and said to him , `` O our lord the Sultan , thy Grace 's munificence hath vouchsafed [ 464 ] to accord me the Lady Bedrulbudour thy daughter , albeit I am unworthy of this great favour , for that I am of the lowliest of thy slaves ; wherefore I beseech God that He keep and continue thee", "context_len": 73}}}, "ditches": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 57, "context": "The spot was on the northern borders of the ancient kingdom of Sussex -- the land of the Saxon Ella -- a spot marvelously favoured by nature , occupying the summit of a low hill , which commanded a wide prospect on all sides , while itself almost impregnable when fortified , as it was , by ditches and mounds , dug in the usual Danish fashion , for the Danes owed much of their success to their skill in fortification", "context_len": 81}}}, "irrigate": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 63, "context": "But Fleming showed such a rooted distrust for anything that was intended to 'go off , ' and , indeed , such absolute abhorrence of any further mechanical contrivance in the vicinity of the stables , that Archie had transferred his attentions to the garden , where he was full of a scheme for utilizing the water power of the little stream to irrigate the soil , after the fashion of the Nile in Egypt , in a series of canals between the beds , and had already made the hose-pipe work with capital effect by means of a siphon and an old barrel", "context_len": 104}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "You don \u2019 t mean to say that you \u2019 re from Arizona , and don \u2019 t irrigate ! gasped Random", "context_len": 22}}}, "logic": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "There was no resisting the girl \u2019 s logic", "context_len": 9}}}, "flowery": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Afterward , a dark , adder-like compunction glided through the flowery expanse of her joy in Tesuque , as she wondered if there was not something heathenish in his lordly enshrinement upon a Christian mantelpiece", "context_len": 35}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "Bart started to return with his empty cart the way he had come , but about ten feet from the platform paused for a moment to take in the exceptionally flowery sentiment that was being enunciated by the speaker of the day", "context_len": 42}}}, "enshrinement": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Afterward , a dark , adder-like compunction glided through the flowery expanse of her joy in Tesuque , as she wondered if there was not something heathenish in his lordly enshrinement upon a Christian mantelpiece", "context_len": 35}}}, "misplace": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "She saw the joke of Lola 's advice to her not to misplace her devotions , and one day she repeated the story to the doctor , showing him the rain-god", "context_len": 31}}}, "devotions": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` Quoth the Lady Bedrulbudour , `` Have no manner of care , O my lady Fatimeh ; I will give thee a place in my house , where thou shalt do thy devotions , and none shall ever go in to thee ; nay , here shalt thou serve God better than in thy cavern", "context_len": 56}}}, "ochre": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "Oftener she worked in her roofless pottery by the ditch , where many a queer jug and vase and bowl , gaudy with ochre and Indian red , came into being and passed early to dust again , for want of firing", "context_len": 42}}}, "clench": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Ben threw his head back and took his hands out of his pockets to clench them together hard", "context_len": 19}}}, "disability": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Keene 's news , since this was merely a statement of his financial disability", "context_len": 14}}}, "conjecturally": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Not to have any money to send might conjecturally be distressing to Mr", "context_len": 13}}}, "distressing": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Sure , '' answered Bart with an off-handed alacrity that was distressing to the responsibility burdened personality of the accredited representative of Monroe , Purcell & Abernethy", "context_len": 28}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "'Girls , ' she began , with an air almost of tragedy , ' a very distressing incident has happened to-day -- a circumstance which in all the records of this school has never occurred before", "context_len": 36}}}, "compassed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "A happier child , with more childish ways , might not so fully have compassed Jane 's awakening ; for this had been in proportion to the needs of the one who so forlornly made plea for entrance", "context_len": 38}}}, "blessedness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Having once thrown wide the door of her heart , Jane had begun to understand the blessedness that lies in generosity", "context_len": 21}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "She had taken Jane 's hand and was holding it closely -- that happy hand which for very blessedness and amazement trembled more than her own", "context_len": 26}}}, "valueless": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "It seemed to contain papers of some kind , and so many bidders had bought old deeds , contracts , plans , manuscripts and the like , utterly valueless to them , that the lot hung at twenty-five cents for several minutes", "context_len": 42}}}, "crushingly": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` If you had not behaved like a set of duffers who had all lost their heads , I could have told you that myself , '' said Hal crushingly", "context_len": 30}}}, "hardening": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Such words as these Jane instinctively knew would fall crushingly upon Lola , and leave her in a sorry plight of abject , hardening thought", "context_len": 25}}}, "misinterpretation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Therefore , steeling herself to bear the girl 's misinterpretation , she said , `` Lola , your father would n't want you to see this letter", "context_len": 27}}}, "apprise": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Campbell , '' said Richard Dewey , `` I will thank you to apprise your father of his ward 's marriage , and ask him to arrange for the surrender of her property at the proper time", "context_len": 37}}}, "defining": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "This shall be thy house when thou art in trouble ! '' and thus defining the limits of her hospitality , she made a gesture toward the mud walls on which strings of goat meat were drying in a sanguinary fringe", "context_len": 41}}}, "molten": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "While the latter was growing moment by moment more refulgent , the former caught the wondrous dyes , till the water seemed everywhere like molten gold with ruddy and empurpled reflections where the sea gave a gentle heave", "context_len": 38}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "At the peak rivers were forming , rivers of molten lava , which , even as Bomba looked , began to flow down the sides of the mountain in every direction", "context_len": 31}}}, "ricks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Ricks of green and silver rose all along the Apishapa", "context_len": 10}}}, "affluence": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "When the Sultan entered the palace and viewed its building and ordinance and saw its stones , which were of jade and agate , he was amazed [ 505 ] and his wit was bewildered at that affluence and wealth and magnificence ; so he turned to the Vizier and said to him , `` How sayst thou , O Vizier ? Hast thou in all thy days seen aught like this ? Are there found with the greatest of the kings of the world riches and gold and jewels such as these we see in this palace ? '' `` O my lord the King , '' answered the Vizier , `` this is a thing beyond the competence of a king of the sons of Adam", "context_len": 128}}}, "ranchers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The town was thronged with ranchers , coming in to trade ; the mine had started up for the winter", "context_len": 20}}}, "prospected": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Men who had prospected for precious metals all summer in the mountains now bundled their pots and pans and blankets back to shelter for the winter ; the long-eared burros , lost in great rolls of bedding , stood about the tipple awaiting the result of their masters ' interviews with the mine boss , concerning work and the occupancy of any `` shack '' that might still be empty", "context_len": 70}}}, "metals": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "Men who had prospected for precious metals all summer in the mountains now bundled their pots and pans and blankets back to shelter for the winter ; the long-eared burros , lost in great rolls of bedding , stood about the tipple awaiting the result of their masters ' interviews with the mine boss , concerning work and the occupancy of any `` shack '' that might still be empty", "context_len": 70}}}, "occupancy": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 59, "context": "Men who had prospected for precious metals all summer in the mountains now bundled their pots and pans and blankets back to shelter for the winter ; the long-eared burros , lost in great rolls of bedding , stood about the tipple awaiting the result of their masters ' interviews with the mine boss , concerning work and the occupancy of any `` shack '' that might still be empty", "context_len": 70}}}, "noisier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Now , too , the bell of the red-brick school clamored loudly of mornings ; and dark , taciturn Mexican children , and paler , noisier children from the mining end of town , bubbled out of every door", "context_len": 39}}}, "breadths": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "More than once on her way to school that first day she looked at the breadths of her scarlet cashmere with a gratified eye ; and catching her at this , Ana Vigil had sighed disapprovingly , saying , `` It is too good for every day -- that dress", "context_len": 50}}}, "piously": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "'It be the same with all they that make haste to get rich , ' he added piously", "context_len": 18}}}, "suave": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "She was a plump girl , with eyes like splinters of coal in her suave brown face ; despite the extreme softness of her voice , these glittering splinters rested with no gentle ray on Lola", "context_len": 36}}}, "assumptions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` So long a tongue as yours , Ana , weaves a short web ! '' growled Alejandro , with a masculine distrust of his sister 's friendly assumptions", "context_len": 29}}}, "tranquilly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Lola knows if I speak truth , '' returned Ana , tranquilly", "context_len": 13}}}, "schoolfellows": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "The little tricks and taunts of her schoolfellows tormented her deeply", "context_len": 11}}}, "unseeing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "For when she encased herself in cold silence , and stalked home with lifted head and unseeing eyes , often a little throng of Mexican children would walk behind her , imitating her stately gait and calling mockingly , `` Ea ! ea ! See the madamisela ! See the princess ! She is sister to the king -- that one ! Vah ! vah ! vah ! ''", "context_len": 69}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "And after he had left her , she went with a light heart down the unpaved street that she had lately traversed in unseeing bitterness", "context_len": 25}}}, "ea": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "baldest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Her own garments remained things of the baldest utility , but the village seamstress was kept busy feather-stitching and beribboning articles for Lola 's wear", "context_len": 25}}}, "expending": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "In these things Jane developed a most prodigal pride , freely expending upon them the little patrimony which had been put in the Trinidad bank against her old age", "context_len": 29}}}, "patternless": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Her usual good judgment quite failed her ; and she who , patternless and guideless , slashed brown denim fearlessly into uncouth vestures for herself , now had a pulse of trepidation at laying the tissue-paper model of some childish garment for Lola upon a length of dainty wool", "context_len": 49}}}, "slashed": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 16, "context": "Her usual good judgment quite failed her ; and she who , patternless and guideless , slashed brown denim fearlessly into uncouth vestures for herself , now had a pulse of trepidation at laying the tissue-paper model of some childish garment for Lola upon a length of dainty wool", "context_len": 49}}}, "denim": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Her usual good judgment quite failed her ; and she who , patternless and guideless , slashed brown denim fearlessly into uncouth vestures for herself , now had a pulse of trepidation at laying the tissue-paper model of some childish garment for Lola upon a length of dainty wool", "context_len": 49}}}, "vestures": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Her usual good judgment quite failed her ; and she who , patternless and guideless , slashed brown denim fearlessly into uncouth vestures for herself , now had a pulse of trepidation at laying the tissue-paper model of some childish garment for Lola upon a length of dainty wool", "context_len": 49}}}, "idiom": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "When Lola , however , one of themselves by reason of her color and her fluency in their idiom , displayed an ability to master those remorseless obscurities of spelling and arithmetic which had seemed sufficient to dethrone reason in any but a Saxon mind , then the peon children began to find some personal satisfaction in her achievements", "context_len": 59}}}, "remorseless": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "When Lola , however , one of themselves by reason of her color and her fluency in their idiom , displayed an ability to master those remorseless obscurities of spelling and arithmetic which had seemed sufficient to dethrone reason in any but a Saxon mind , then the peon children began to find some personal satisfaction in her achievements", "context_len": 59}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "Living as they did in an unsettled age , and far more prepared than we should be for such a contingency , yet the sense of the rapid approach of a cruel and remorseless foe struck terror into many hearts", "context_len": 40}}}, "obscurities": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "When Lola , however , one of themselves by reason of her color and her fluency in their idiom , displayed an ability to master those remorseless obscurities of spelling and arithmetic which had seemed sufficient to dethrone reason in any but a Saxon mind , then the peon children began to find some personal satisfaction in her achievements", "context_len": 59}}}, "dethrone": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "When Lola , however , one of themselves by reason of her color and her fluency in their idiom , displayed an ability to master those remorseless obscurities of spelling and arithmetic which had seemed sufficient to dethrone reason in any but a Saxon mind , then the peon children began to find some personal satisfaction in her achievements", "context_len": 59}}}, "peon": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 48, "context": "When Lola , however , one of themselves by reason of her color and her fluency in their idiom , displayed an ability to master those remorseless obscurities of spelling and arithmetic which had seemed sufficient to dethrone reason in any but a Saxon mind , then the peon children began to find some personal satisfaction in her achievements", "context_len": 59}}}, "liveryman": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "In the upper benches of the pavilion were ranged the flower and chivalry of the town -- the families of the mine boss , the liveryman , the lawyer , the schoolmaster and several visiting personages", "context_len": 36}}}, "enliven": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` 'Sta 'ueno ! '' they would whisper , watching Edith May , who upon such occasions was wont to enliven things by bursting into tears , and who commonly brought upon the following day a note from her mother , stating that Edith May must be excused for missing in spelling because she had not been at all well and had misunderstood the word", "context_len": 65}}}, "constraint": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "The next two years also mitigated much of the constraint which had marked Miss Combs 's relations with Lola", "context_len": 19}}}, "fabrics": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "The girl had a sort of barbaric love of brightness and softness ; and one day , as she looked over some fabrics for which Jane , spurred by the approach of the vacation and the fact that Lola was to have a part in the closing exercises of school , had sent to Denver , the girl said suddenly , `` How good my father is to me , tia ! ''", "context_len": 73}}}, "stipend": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Keene a monthly stipend", "context_len": 4}}}, "combated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "But Percy , even in the midst of his own wrath and indignation , generously combated this ; he inclined to the first supposition that Seabrooke had mislaid or lost the note , and he even maintained that it would shortly be found", "context_len": 43}}}, "appertain": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "She had never clearly realized , on her own part , those distinctions in labor which appertain to the sexes ; she had herself always done everything that had to be done , whether it were cooking or plowing", "context_len": 39}}}, "plowing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "For a long distance Fenn tramped on , plowing through the woods , making turns now and then to avoid streams , partly frozen over , leaping them when he could , fording them at other times , for he had on high , water-proof hunting boots , but keeping as nearly as he could in the proper course", "context_len": 59}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "sterile": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "There was now a carpet , while muslin shades softened the glare of the Colorado sun and the view of the sterile hills", "context_len": 23}}}, "greenly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Geraniums bloomed on the window-sills , and some young cottonwoods grew greenly at the door", "context_len": 15}}}, "pensioner": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 47, "context": "He waited for a minute or two , hoping Baker would divulge something further , but he did not do so , and Bart said good night , secured the padlock on the outside , and left the place with a parting cheery direction to his strange pensioner to sleep soundly and rest well", "context_len": 54}}}, "register": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "He took off his helmet and looked solemnly to Heaven , as he called on the Supreme Being to register his vow , and a deep murmur of sympathy arose around , until it found loud utterance in the cry , `` We will conquer with our king or die , '' from a thousand voices , until the glorious enthusiasm spread throughout the camp", "context_len": 65}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` Well ! well ! '' he cried briskly , with a gratified glance at his son after looking over the register , `` all the real hard work is done , the work that always worries me , with my poor eyesight", "context_len": 43}}}, "purposed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "There was a base of sound reason in his purposed action", "context_len": 11}}}, "smoldering": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "The doctor felt that Lola 's mind held a smoldering distrust of Jane , which not even the consciousness of Jane 's love could dispel", "context_len": 25}}}, "formulating": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "There might be a dozen , a score , or half a hundred who were formulating if they had not already formulated a plan to surprise and massacre every one of the whites", "context_len": 33}}}, "tides": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` For hundreds upon hundreds of years the winds and tides have combined to rid themselves of this obstacle to their progress , the winds urging the waves that come rolling in from the vast Atlantic , gathering force as they increase in speed , like one rushing at a leap ; and at last leap they do , upon the great black mass of shale , tons upon tons in weight , seeming as if they would sweep it clear away , and rush on in mad ruin to tumble the fishing luggers together and shatter them like eggs as they lie softly rubbing together in the harbour", "context_len": 109}}}, "benevolence": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` I am right to put an end to Miss Combs 's unwise benevolence , '' thought the doctor , as he tied his horse outside the schoolhouse", "context_len": 28}}}, "looped": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Gaunt Mexican women , holding quiet babies in their looped rebozos , stood about , hardly ever speaking", "context_len": 18}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 54, "context": "Granby made the most extraordinary flowers of red flannel and gilt paper , -- flowers whose likeness never grew in gardens or greenhouses of any known land ; had she not baked sugar cakes which were intended to represent men and women , pigs , horses , and cows ? Were not the branches looped with gay ribbons ? Did they not bear rosy-cheeked apples , an orange for each child , some cheap but much prized toys , and , better than all , several useful and greatly needed articles , which had been the gift of Mrs", "context_len": 99}}}, "countrywomen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Se\u00f1ora Vigil , more lavishly built than the rest of her countrywomen and gayer of port than they , moved from group to group , talking cheerfully", "context_len": 27}}}, "gayer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Se\u00f1ora Vigil , more lavishly built than the rest of her countrywomen and gayer of port than they , moved from group to group , talking cheerfully", "context_len": 27}}}, "jean": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "Jane also awaited the opening of the schoolhouse door , watching the scene with interest and having no conception of herself as an object of note , in her elderly black bonnet and short jean skirt", "context_len": 36}}}, "junctures": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "Little beribboned girls exhibited their skill in dialogue , and read essays and filed through some patriotic drill , to which a forest of tiny flags gave splendid emphasis at impressive junctures", "context_len": 32}}}, "pics": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "' A los pics de V", "context_len": 6}}}, "citron": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "She was fairer than the Mexicans , although not fair in the fashion of Edith May , but with a faint citron hue which , better than pink and white , befitted the extreme darkness of her hair and eyes", "context_len": 40}}}, "monotonously": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "The fussy little alarm clock ticked monotonously on , as if anxious to get its work done", "context_len": 17}}}, "jestingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "`` What are you saying to make my little girl so wide-eyed ? Remember , she has a fierce old guardian -- one that expects every one to 'tend to his own affairs ! '' Jane spoke jestingly , but the doctor knew he was worsted", "context_len": 46}}}, "entries": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 9, "context": "She was notified by the Dauntless Company that two entries , the fourth and fifth east , had entered her property , in which she had never suspected the presence of coal , and that the owners were prepared to negotiate with her suitable terms for the right of working the vein in question", "context_len": 54}}}, "royalties": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "When the matter of royalties was settled and several hundred dollars paid to Jane 's account for coal already taken out , she had a sudden rush of almost tearful joy", "context_len": 31}}}, "determinate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Every month would come to her , while the coal lasted , a determinate sum of money", "context_len": 17}}}, "embellish": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "Then , glowing with enterprise , she proceeded to extend and embellish her property in a way which speedily set the town by the ears , and aroused every one to dark prophecies as to what must happen when her money should all be gone , and nothing left her but to face poverty in the palatial five-room dwelling now growing up around the pine homestead of the past", "context_len": 69}}}, "palatial": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 111, "context": "My deduction of the case is as follows : Eliot has a sister ; this sister is attacked by the vicious dogs of one Fletcher ; Stone rushes to her defense ; he beats off the said dogs and kills one of them ; the before-mentioned Eliot takes his before-mentioned sister home ; he relates to his folks how she was rescued from dire peril and a fearful fate by the before-mentioned Stone ; at once her parents wish to see and thank the said Stone ; Roger is dispatched post haste for the hero of the thrilling and deadly struggle ; said hero is carried off in triumph to the palatial residence of the before-mentioned parents", "context_len": 117}}}, "adobes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Lola liked adobe houses ; and fortunately Enrique Diaz , the blacksmith , had a fine lot of adobes which he had made before frost , and put under cover against a possible extension of his shop , `` to-morrow or some time after a while", "context_len": 46}}}, "tiled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "There was a porch , too , with snow-white pillars , and an open fireplace , all tiled with adobe , in which might blaze fires of pi\u00f1on wood , full of resin and burning as nothing else can burn save driftwood , sodden with salt and oil and the mystery of old ocean", "context_len": 54}}}, "resin": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "There was a porch , too , with snow-white pillars , and an open fireplace , all tiled with adobe , in which might blaze fires of pi\u00f1on wood , full of resin and burning as nothing else can burn save driftwood , sodden with salt and oil and the mystery of old ocean", "context_len": 54}}}, "queries": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "Surely she could not mean to be ill-tempered -- Ana , with a face as broad and placid as a standing pool ? No , no , Ana was too simple to wish to pain any one ! Yet as Jane dwelt upon Ana 's queries , it came slowly to Jane that certain changes in herself might be well", "context_len": 60}}}, "tia": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "Something was surely amiss ; but the girl as yet fully realized only one fact -- that tia , always so steadfast and strong and cheerful , had gone hastily from the room in the agitation of one who struggled with unaccustomed tears", "context_len": 43}}}, "deriving": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` She is Mexican -- yes ! '' they nodded to one another , deriving much simple satisfaction from the circumstance", "context_len": 21}}}, "provocative": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "For was it not provocative of racial pride that one of their compatriots should be able to make tunes -- actual tunes ! -- issue from those keys which responded to their own tentative touches merely with thin shrieks or a dull , rumbling note ?", "context_len": 46}}}, "tentative": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "For was it not provocative of racial pride that one of their compatriots should be able to make tunes -- actual tunes ! -- issue from those keys which responded to their own tentative touches merely with thin shrieks or a dull , rumbling note ?", "context_len": 46}}}, "horsemanship": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 20, "context": "Moreover , he fell to going everywhile [ 539 ] to the chase and the horse course and to practicing horsemanship and archery [ 540 ] before the Sultan , whilst the Lady Bedrulbudour redoubled in love of him , whenassoever she saw him disporting himself a horseback , and thought in herself that God had wrought exceeding graciously by her in that there had befallen her what befell with the Vizier 's son , so He might keep her for her true bridegroom Alaeddin", "context_len": 85}}}, "violin": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 17, "context": "A little colored boy , the sole orphaned remainder of a long line of masters of the violin , alone of the army of negroes who had borne the family name , is left to wait upon the old mistress and Miss Patrice at the `` Great House", "context_len": 48}}}, "tuning": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Already the sound of a violin in process of tuning rang cheerfully through the open", "context_len": 15}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "A selection of small boys and girls sang school glees and Christmas carols , a trifle flat occasionally , but perhaps that was the fault of the piano , which needed tuning", "context_len": 32}}}, "equipages": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "It was to be a great day for Aguilar ! People from Santa Clara and Hastings and Gulnare were arriving in all manner of equipages", "context_len": 25}}}, "mexican": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Mexican vehicles made a solid stockade along the west of the track", "context_len": 12}}}, "stockade": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "The prisoners , stirred to new life by this unexpected chance of escape after all hope had been lost , responded to the call of their chief , hurried out of the stockade and mingled with the frantic , fleeing headhunters", "context_len": 41}}}, "inferiority": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` She is not ashamed to be one , either ! '' cried Alejandro , accepting Ana 's tacit imputation of some inferiority in their race", "context_len": 26}}}, "suavity": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Who said she was ashamed ? '' asked Ana , with suspicious suavity", "context_len": 14}}}, "sulk": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Alejandro turned off in a sulk , and , leaving Ana to her own resources , went toward the place where the ponies and burros were tethered", "context_len": 27}}}, "burro": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "It was comparatively lonely here , and Alejandro began to make friends with a disconsolate burro who was bewailing his fate in a series of lamentable sounds", "context_len": 27}}}, "bewailing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "When he returned to his country , he abode all this time , bewailing that which he had endured of toil and stress , so he might compass the lamp , yet had his travail all been wasted and the morsel had escaped from his hand , after it had reached his mouth ; and he still thought upon all this , bemoaning himself and reviling Alaeddin of the excess of his anger against him ; and whiles he said in himself , `` Since yonder whoreson is dead under the earth , I am content withal and I have hopes of the lamp , that I may yet achieve it , inasmuch as it is still safeguarded", "context_len": 118}}}, "amigo": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` What is the use of wasting breath ? Sus , sus , amigo ! '' The burro began to buck and Alejandro stepped back", "context_len": 25}}}, "furzy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "As he did so he saw approaching him from behind the wagons a man in tattered garments , with a hat dragged over his eyes , and a great mass of furzy yellow beard", "context_len": 34}}}, "behest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Why should he put himself at the behest of this vagabundo who impeached his English ? The man , however , had an eye on him", "context_len": 26}}}, "impeached": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Why should he put himself at the behest of this vagabundo who impeached his English ? The man , however , had an eye on him", "context_len": 26}}}, "impelling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "It was an eye which Alejandro felt to be impelling", "context_len": 10}}}, "conceded": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` They are that , '' conceded Bricktop Molloy , one of the biggest guards who ever supported a center", "context_len": 20}}}, "assertion": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "There had been in Jane 's words a broken , yet oddly definite , assertion of impending poverty", "context_len": 18}}}, "rakishly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Keene 's project , seemed to see him rakishly ascending the pavilion steps , among a wondering throng , and making way to Lola as she sat , happy and honored , with her friends", "context_len": 35}}}, "prevision": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Jane had a sharp prevision of Lola 's face when her father should appear before her , so different from the tender ideal of him which she had cherished , so intent upon himself , so bent upon shattering with his first word to his child all those visions of unselfish kindness and generosity which had made her thoughts of him beautiful", "context_len": 62}}}, "succor": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "In ordinary circumstances Jane could not deny to herself that this course would be the right course for a daughter ; that such an one would do well to succor a father 's failings , to add hope to his despondency and love to the mitigation of his trials", "context_len": 49}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "This abode , in its mere cubhood , had afforded her financial succor", "context_len": 13}}}, "failings": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` The story of the girl 's life is charmingly told , and eventually , her father , a man who , despite some failings , is generous and well-meaning , reappears in the character of a wealthy mine owner , and brings the story to an unlooked for and happy termination", "context_len": 52}}}, "despondency": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 40, "context": "In ordinary circumstances Jane could not deny to herself that this course would be the right course for a daughter ; that such an one would do well to succor a father 's failings , to add hope to his despondency and love to the mitigation of his trials", "context_len": 49}}}, "mitigation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "In ordinary circumstances Jane could not deny to herself that this course would be the right course for a daughter ; that such an one would do well to succor a father 's failings , to add hope to his despondency and love to the mitigation of his trials", "context_len": 49}}}, "irrelevance": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` I 'd have been a better man if I 'd had any luck , '' he said , with apparent irrelevance", "context_len": 22}}}, "controlling": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "Keene , controlling his emotion , said it would , and they parted upon the understanding that they should meet at Lynn two days later , for the transference of the fund", "context_len": 32}}}, "transference": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Keene , controlling his emotion , said it would , and they parted upon the understanding that they should meet at Lynn two days later , for the transference of the fund", "context_len": 32}}}, "mutely": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Then Jane plodded wearily back to the pavilion , and mutely watched the cow-ponies rush and buck around the course", "context_len": 20}}}, "girdled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "The vast improvement in the selection of books and the service of papers , however , induced other companies to desire to have a similar arrangement , till the chief portion of all the English railways came to be girdled by Smith 's bookstalls", "context_len": 44}}}, "inadvisable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "The mine boss came one day to tell Jane that , because of a certain `` roll '' in the east entries , it was deemed inadvisable farther to work these levels", "context_len": 32}}}, "horizons": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "Something was always calling him to go elsewhere , beckoning him on to new horizons , telling him that he belonged to the white people", "context_len": 25}}}, "unformulated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "To be content in the present , to be trustful in the future , was her unformulated creed", "context_len": 18}}}, "pended": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "The lawyer wrote that matters would probably be settled in favor of the present landholders , but that , so long as litigation pended , all titles were so clouded as to make any questions of loans untenable", "context_len": 38}}}, "untenable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "The lawyer wrote that matters would probably be settled in favor of the present landholders , but that , so long as litigation pended , all titles were so clouded as to make any questions of loans untenable", "context_len": 38}}}, "springlike": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "She looked at Lola , and her heart sank at the girl 's air of springlike happiness and hope", "context_len": 19}}}, "penury": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Must these sweet hours be broken upon with a tale of impending penury ?", "context_len": 14}}}, "mutation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "To read of a noble deed brought swift tears to her eyes in these days of mutation , and stirred her to emulative dreams", "context_len": 24}}}, "emulative": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "To read of a noble deed brought swift tears to her eyes in these days of mutation , and stirred her to emulative dreams", "context_len": 24}}}, "undramatic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "She could not yet know how she had been impressed by the simple page of plain , undramatic kindness and generosity which Jane 's life opened daily to her eyes", "context_len": 30}}}, "ambiguous": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "Luckily Bobby 's ill-humours were of a short-lived nature , and after a few minutes of cutting silence , he volunteered the rather ambiguous remark that there were 'lots of things a fellow could do when he grew up , anyhow , ' and was his smiling self again", "context_len": 49}}}, "lassoing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "Over in the Vigil yard Lola saw the smaller Vigil boys lassoing one another with a piece of clothes-line , while , dozing over her sewing , Se\u00f1ora Vigil herself squatted in the doorway", "context_len": 34}}}, "doff": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Diego was two years old -- quite old enough to doff his ragged frock for the `` pantalones '' which his mother was still working upon , after weeks of listless endeavor", "context_len": 32}}}, "fraternal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "She laid down her work to wipe her eyes , and , observing two of her sons grappling in fraternal war at the house corner , she arose to cuff each one impartially , exclaiming , `` Ea , muchachos ! You fight before my very eyes , eh ? Take that ! and that ! '' Waddling reluctantly back to her sewing , she saw Lola standing in the white-pillared porch of the big adobe house beyond , and a gleam of inspiration crossed the se\u00f1ora 's dark , fat face", "context_len": 92}}}, "feelingly": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Well , I do n't know how to begin to thank you , '' said the man , feelingly , when they were in a somewhat secluded place on the main street", "context_len": 33}}}, "caballeros": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "The picture of Ana , standing disdainful among the sorrowing caballeros and waving off their pleas with an imperious hand , was one to bring a smile to lips of deadliest gravity", "context_len": 32}}}, "expanding": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "He rested for a while , for the effort had exhausted him ; rested , while he drew great draughts of air into his lungs , luxuriously expanding the chest that had been so cruelly imprisoned", "context_len": 36}}}, "disdainful": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The picture of Ana , standing disdainful among the sorrowing caballeros and waving off their pleas with an imperious hand , was one to bring a smile to lips of deadliest gravity", "context_len": 32}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Then with a disdainful gesture he replaced the knife in his belt , as though they were foes too insignificant to bother with any further , took up the paddle , and without once looking behind him made for the opposite shore", "context_len": 42}}}, "pleas": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The picture of Ana , standing disdainful among the sorrowing caballeros and waving off their pleas with an imperious hand , was one to bring a smile to lips of deadliest gravity", "context_len": 32}}}, "squat": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "Ana , with her hands on her broad hips , short and thick as a squat brown jug with its handles akimbo , -- Ana , with her great clay-colored face and tiny , glittering eyes , with her thick , pale lips and coarse , black hair , -- surely none but a mother could view in Ana such charms as bedewed Se\u00f1ora Vigil 's eyes only to think of !", "context_len": 72}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "He had the same squat , sturdy figure , the coarse black hair dangling about his bare shoulders , and growing low upon his forehead , the naked chest , the frowsy hunting shirt of deerskin , with leggings and dilapidated moccasins", "context_len": 42}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "He held it , too , in such a funny way , with the tips of his fingers , as if afraid he should squat it to death , and with an expression of anxiety upon his face amounting almost to anguish", "context_len": 42}}}, "bedewed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 62, "context": "Ana , with her hands on her broad hips , short and thick as a squat brown jug with its handles akimbo , -- Ana , with her great clay-colored face and tiny , glittering eyes , with her thick , pale lips and coarse , black hair , -- surely none but a mother could view in Ana such charms as bedewed Se\u00f1ora Vigil 's eyes only to think of !", "context_len": 72}}}, "vaquero": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Ah , yes ! I wept much the day when you were lost , and Bev Gribble , the vaquero , brought you home on his horse", "context_len": 27}}}, "unlikelihood": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "She had spoken of the unlikelihood of another year in Pueblo for Lola , and the girl for the first time began to realize this fact with a sinking of the heart", "context_len": 32}}}, "diatribe": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` The se\u00f1ora 's tongue wagged on in a diatribe of accusation and pity", "context_len": 14}}}, "despoiled": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "Who am I to judge ? Nay , even I try to forget that me she has also despoiled ; that she took a corner of our back yard , and plants corn in it to this day ! I am all for forgiving", "context_len": 44}}}, "velvety": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "She had a burning wish to escape from the soft buzzing of the se\u00f1ora 's words , which , a velvety , sting-infested swarm , whirred around her bee-like , seeking hive and home", "context_len": 34}}}, "whirred": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "She had a burning wish to escape from the soft buzzing of the se\u00f1ora 's words , which , a velvety , sting-infested swarm , whirred around her bee-like , seeking hive and home", "context_len": 34}}}, "hive": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "She had a burning wish to escape from the soft buzzing of the se\u00f1ora 's words , which , a velvety , sting-infested swarm , whirred around her bee-like , seeking hive and home", "context_len": 34}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "In fact , he thought that a hive might be swarming from one side of the river to the other , and looked to see if he could detect the presence of the insects in the moonlight", "context_len": 37}}}, "alkaline": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Summer dust whitened the road , and rose to her tread in alkaline clouds", "context_len": 14}}}, "ugliness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "She was struggling with a thing that was half anguish and half anger , and that lifted upon her a face more and more convincing in its ugliness", "context_len": 28}}}, "delinquency": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "Jane had faltered in the trust which she had assumed , and now , confronted with the embarrassment of facing Lola 's father in a plain confession of her delinquency , she hesitated and was miserable and afraid and reluctant", "context_len": 40}}}, "stoppage": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "It was not the stoppage of her own course , indeed , although this was a misery , but the loss of trust in all humanity which distrust of Jane seemed to the girl to inflict upon her", "context_len": 38}}}, "shortcoming": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Lola 's father could easily repair Jane 's shortcoming , but not without having an explanation of the facts of the case", "context_len": 22}}}, "brooded": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 19, "context": "She remembered reading somewhere that the children of men were often brought up under alien conditions , like ducklings brooded over by a mother hen , but as soon as a chance was given , they flew to their native element and the former things were as though they had not been", "context_len": 52}}}, "crooning": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "In the strange peacefulness which brooded over her she walked home between the pi\u00f1on-sprinkled hills , where doves were crooning and the far bleating of an upland herd echoed among the barren ridges", "context_len": 33}}}, "sunniness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "She reflected quietly upon meeting Jane without a hint of any shadow in her face , but in such sunniness of humor as should gladden and reassure", "context_len": 27}}}, "gladden": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "It was a sight which I thought might gladden their guardian angels to see him and his sister kneeling with clasped hands at their uncle Elfric 's tomb , and when service was over , they made me tell them the old old story about the first Elfric , the brother of my father , and how my father rescued him when the old castle was burnt iii", "context_len": 68}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "As he passed , all the folk cried out to him with one voice , saying , `` God gladden thee ! God increase thee in glory ! God continue thee ! '' And so they brought him home in great procession , what while he showered gold on them", "context_len": 50}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "It will be a great reward for the Author 's toil should this little volume similarly gladden many firesides during the approaching Christmas , and perhaps cause some to thank God for the contrast between the Christmas of 1007 and that of 1874", "context_len": 43}}}, "survives": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 65, "context": "[ Footnote 638 : Keniz , a word which I can not find in any dictionary , but which appears to be the past participle ( in the secondary form for mecnouz , as ketil , slain , for mertoul , ) of keneza , a lost verb of which only the fourth form acneza , he drank from a cup ( kinz ) , survives , and to mean `` something drunk from a cup", "context_len": 76}}}, "clinics": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` He thought of Berlin and Vienna and Paris , and the clinics where he had meant to acquire such skill as , aiding his zeal , should write him among the first physicians of his day", "context_len": 37}}}, "physicians": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` He thought of Berlin and Vienna and Paris , and the clinics where he had meant to acquire such skill as , aiding his zeal , should write him among the first physicians of his day", "context_len": 37}}}, "doling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "And here he was , practising among a few Mexicans and miners , tending their bruises , doling them out quinine , and taking pay of a dollar a month from every man , sick or well , enrolled on the mine books , and frequently getting nothing at all from such as were not therein enrolled", "context_len": 57}}}, "soberness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` I would do little things , '' said the doctor , with whimsical soberness", "context_len": 15}}}, "equipage": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "She did not see the equipage or the man on the purple cushions", "context_len": 13}}}, "brasses": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "It looked a cheerful place , with its polished-oak dressers and shining brasses , and on a winter 's evening , when the shutters were closed and the settle drawn close to the fire , it seemed the cosiest spot in the world ; and Peggy and Bobby would often escape from the sober atmosphere of the dining-room to pull their little stools into the ingle nook , and listen to Nancy 's wonderful tales of ghosts and goblins , which seemed twice as thrilling when the wind was howling like a banshee in the chimney , and rattling the doors till they could fancy that spirit fingers were tapping on the panels , and only waiting a chance to catch them in the dark passages , and", "context_len": 128}}}, "freakish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "An unusual equipage was turning in from the Trinidad road -- an equipage on which leather and varnish shone , and harness brasses flashed , while the dust rolled pompously after it in a freakish fantasy of postilions and outriders", "context_len": 40}}}, "fantasy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "An unusual equipage was turning in from the Trinidad road -- an equipage on which leather and varnish shone , and harness brasses flashed , while the dust rolled pompously after it in a freakish fantasy of postilions and outriders", "context_len": 40}}}, "postilions": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "An unusual equipage was turning in from the Trinidad road -- an equipage on which leather and varnish shone , and harness brasses flashed , while the dust rolled pompously after it in a freakish fantasy of postilions and outriders", "context_len": 40}}}, "matching": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "Altogether the vehicle had a lordly air , easily matching that of the individual sitting alone on the purple cushions -- a man whose features were not very clear at the distance , although the yellowness of his beard , the glitter of his studded shirt-front , and whole consequential , expansive effect recalled to the doctor 's mind an image of the past , less ornate , indeed , and affluent , but of similar aspect", "context_len": 77}}}, "yellowness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Altogether the vehicle had a lordly air , easily matching that of the individual sitting alone on the purple cushions -- a man whose features were not very clear at the distance , although the yellowness of his beard , the glitter of his studded shirt-front , and whole consequential , expansive effect recalled to the doctor 's mind an image of the past , less ornate , indeed , and affluent , but of similar aspect", "context_len": 77}}}, "unpaved": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "And after he had left her , she went with a light heart down the unpaved street that she had lately traversed in unseeing bitterness", "context_len": 25}}}, "cottonwood": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Set emerald-like in the yellow circle of the prairies , the green young cottonwood grove about Jane 's house shone fresh and vivid", "context_len": 23}}}, "scrutinized": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 32, "context": "It had all the features of a regular secret society meeting room , with inner and outer sentinels , a hole cut in the door , through which doubtful visitors could be scrutinized ; and once inside a more or less blood-curdling ritual was gone through with", "context_len": 47}}}, "monologue": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Who could have come ? Whose voice was that which issued from the house in an unbroken monologue , genial , laughing , breathless ?", "context_len": 25}}}, "unalterable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "Then the unalterable joyousness of his glance reassured her , and she rushed forward crying , `` Oh , it 's you ! It 's you ! ''", "context_len": 28}}}, "unwitting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Keene , unwitting and bewildered", "context_len": 5}}}, "ore": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 84, "context": "`` Of course she does n't know all I suffered , what with taking up one worthless claim after another month in and out -- if you mean that ! Why , I actually thought one time of giving up prospecting and settling down to day 's work ! Yes 'm ! It was sure enough that grub-stake you gave me last Fourth of July that brought me my first luck ! I put it right into Pony Gulch and my pick struck free-milling ore the first blow ! Some of the stuff runs ninety dollars to the ton and some higher", "context_len": 102}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "When a little later on he was offered a much better situation on the condition that he gave up Methodism he refused it , preferring , as he says , `` his God to white and yellow ore ''", "context_len": 39}}}, "mortgaging": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` Is that all the satisfaction you are goin ' to give me ? Do n't you ever mean to pay me that money you 've made me pay out on your account , mortgaging my house and risking my home ? ''", "context_len": 43}}}, "palliate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "No need to summon charity for Jane 's shortcomings ! No need to overlook , to palliate , to forgive ! Jane 's fault had been merely too lavish a generosity , too large a love", "context_len": 36}}}, "designing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "Peggy had ambitions towards the trial scene in the 'Merchant of Venice , ' with herself as Portia , having a scarlet dressing-gown which she thought would answer beautifully for the doctor 's robes , and designing Archie for the part of Shylock , while Lilian was to don male attire , and represent the unfortunate merchant", "context_len": 57}}}, "fallacies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "He had disclosed fallacies in himself of which Lola , it seemed , knew nothing", "context_len": 15}}}, "allowances": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 73, "context": "No , father , no ! And now that you have let me find out what I owe her , do you think I sha'n't remember it always with every beat of my heart ? Oh , yes -- although I can never repay her for all she has suffered in keeping me from knowing things which would have hurt me too much when I was little and -- and could not make allowances -- as I can now", "context_len": 79}}}, "uncertainly": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "Scrimmage ? echoed Ben uncertainly , vaguely fancying he had been in a fight with his bitter enemy", "context_len": 18}}}, "actuality": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "There was actuality in the scene", "context_len": 6}}}, "crisply": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "The cottonwoods rustled crisply , Alejandro Vigil was calling to his dog , and the tinkle of his herd stole softly upon her ear", "context_len": 24}}}, "geraniums": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "A great white cat peeped out from behind the geraniums which filled the window ; a greyhound lay upon the doormat , and beneath and about the porch hung several bird-cages , containing half a dozen canaries and two mocking-birds , while a donkey and a tame goat looked , the one over , the other between the bars of the fence which divided their little pasture ground from the neat garden", "context_len": 72}}}, "reassurance": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "The boy \u2019 s words brought some reassurance to his companions , but not enough to banish their fears wholly , and it was with great trepidation that they viewed the dreaded island as the tree drew ever nearer", "context_len": 39}}}, "develops": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` You wait till he develops , you young thing , '' said Mr", "context_len": 14}}}, "excites": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "The tropical brilliancy of the girl , by reason of her red frock and the red ribbons in her hair , excites the jealousy of the little Mexicans and the paler children from the mining end of the town , and in their disapproval they style her `` Infanta", "context_len": 49}}}, "reappears": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` The story of the girl 's life is charmingly told , and eventually , her father , a man who , despite some failings , is generous and well-meaning , reappears in the character of a wealthy mine owner , and brings the story to an unlooked for and happy termination", "context_len": 52}}}, "bridgman": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "BRIDGMAN", "context_len": 1}}}, "borrows": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Aunt Kate , one of the `` right kind , '' is lonesome in her new house without any young people , and borrows Sonny Boy for six months", "context_len": 29}}}, "chants": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Miss Patrice teaches Orphy to sing the chants and anthems in the service of the little church where he was baptized , and with her voice new airs for his violin", "context_len": 31}}}, "anthems": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Miss Patrice teaches Orphy to sing the chants and anthems in the service of the little church where he was baptized , and with her voice new airs for his violin", "context_len": 31}}}, "homeliest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "An irresistibly humorous relation of the haps and mishaps of the homeliest , yet most dependable dog in the world , and a delightful red-haired and freckled child , whose united ages did not exceed seven years", "context_len": 37}}}, "dependable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "An irresistibly humorous relation of the haps and mishaps of the homeliest , yet most dependable dog in the world , and a delightful red-haired and freckled child , whose united ages did not exceed seven years", "context_len": 37}}}, "birch": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 19, "context": "All along the shore of the island the line of cliff was fringed with a mixed growth of white birch , maple , spruce , and red oak , contrasting beautifully with the ragged and perpendicular cliffs which had been spared by Ben as a shelter to the land from the easterly winds , and more than all for the beauty of their appearance", "context_len": 64}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Cornish to strip and birch me", "context_len": 6}}}, "london": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "LONDON :", "context_len": 2}}}, "lambkin": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "But now , your lambkin by your side Takes all your thoughts and all your care ; I may go , and run , and ride , You are careless how , or where", "context_len": 34}}}, "carrots": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Turnips , cabbages , carrots , Ho ! Now try , Ma'am ; I dare say you and I can agree upon a price for this bunch of turnips : they were fresh gathered this morning , I assure you ; and I think if you buy of me once , you will never buy of any body else", "context_len": 60}}}, "mettlesome": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "To Brighton or Worthing , and all with full speed , Which way are you going so mightily fast ? Take care of the reins , for your mettlesome steed Might stumble and lay you too low at the last", "context_len": 40}}}, "sieve": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Like water through a sieve , chuckled Chipper Cooper ; and barely had the words left his lips when Sage began calling a signal which sent Barker into the other wing of the line", "context_len": 34}}}, "sifted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The sifted gravel , I suppose , will be carried in a cart to some gentleman 's garden , to make walks ; and the stones will be used in mending the roads", "context_len": 33}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "All around , the woods were covered with a mantle of white , which had sifted down through the trees , while the firs , spruces , hemlocks and pines , which had heavy foliage that caught the white crystals , were mounds of white", "context_len": 45}}}, "wells": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 117, "context": "There was the much-longed-for guinea-pig from Father , which had been smuggled up in a hamper , and kept with elaborate care in a remote portion of the barn ( a secret which Bobby found the utmost difficulty in preserving ) ; a nightdress case worked by Lilian 's neat fingers ; a cork frame from Bobby , made under Archie 's supervision ; a round pink-and-white wool mat , which Nancy had crocheted at odd times during the evenings ; and a little mug from Joe , with a Welshwoman in a tall hat on one side , and ' A present from Llandrindod ' on the other , bought on a visit to the famous wells which he had paid in company with the church choir", "context_len": 128}}}, "asses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Some boys are very cruel to poor asses", "context_len": 8}}}, "harming": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Oh ! how delightful and charming To take the fresh air in a chaise ; To gallop along without harming : Whip away ! what a dust you do raise !", "context_len": 31}}}, "cask": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "That tells the story , said the captain ; we \u2019 ll give her a few more ; there \u2019 s nothing like an empty cask ; I \u2019 ll find a use for them when we get out there", "context_len": 40}}}, "harrowing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "Many facts therein given have been rejected by the Author as too harrowing in their nature ; and he has preferred to render the contemplation of woe and suffering less painful , by a display of those virtues of patience , resignation , and brave submission to the Divine will , which affliction never fails to bring out in the fold of Christ , whose promise stands ever fast , that the strength of His people shall be equal to their needs", "context_len": 82}}}, "cottager": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "See that cottager , with a load upon her head : her eldest girl is helping her ; and a chubby little boy trudges joyfully by her side", "context_len": 28}}}, "trudges": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "See that cottager , with a load upon her head : her eldest girl is helping her ; and a chubby little boy trudges joyfully by her side", "context_len": 28}}}, "sifting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Afterwards , all the coarse parts of the husk are taken away by means of sifting : this coarse part is called bran ; and the fine white inside is flour , of which bread is made", "context_len": 37}}}, "bran": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "They did not do very much except nibble at bran and lettuce-leaves , it is true , but they were pretty , soft creatures , with long , silky ears , and it was fun sometimes to let them out for a scamper on the granary floor", "context_len": 47}}}, "monograph": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Stanley Hall 's suggestions in his monograph , `` How to Teach Reading , '' where he asks for `` true child-editions , made by testing many children with the work piece-meal and cutting and adapting the material till it really and closely fitted the minds and hearts of the children", "context_len": 51}}}, "orally": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "After a time the story was produced orally by the children", "context_len": 11}}}, "mimeographed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Later the story was either written on the blackboard or mimeographed and put into the pupils ' hands to read", "context_len": 20}}}, "duckling": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 16, "context": "While the mother duck was eating a big bug , an old duck bit the ugly duckling", "context_len": 17}, "NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 21, "context": "Through the hatchway dropped a small , bandy-legged man whose short blonde hair bristled like the fuzz of a newly hatched duckling", "context_len": 22}}}, "ducklings": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 18, "context": "She remembered reading somewhere that the children of men were often brought up under alien conditions , like ducklings brooded over by a mother hen , but as soon as a chance was given , they flew to their native element and the former things were as though they had not been", "context_len": 52}}}, "thor": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "Thor was very proud of Sif 's golden hair", "context_len": 9}}}, "dwarf": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "After that came the giant and dwarf , the former a weedy specimen of unwholesome overgrowth , while the latter looked as if he had been reared upon gin to keep him small", "context_len": 33}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "'Way back there , ye young rascals ! there 's the giant and the dwarf a-comin ' ! '", "context_len": 19}}}, "pigskin": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 45, "context": "He was able to catch punts on the dead run ; when he fell on the ball he got it cleanly , never once permitting it to bound away from him ; and he could kick , too , his sturdy right leg sending the pigskin sailing far through the air", "context_len": 51}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "But there came a change , and when Randall had the pigskin Phil rushed his men up the field to such good advantage that they scored the first touch-down , to the no small dismay of the Wescott team", "context_len": 39}}}, "freyja": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Freyja", "context_len": 1}}}, "vail": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "They put on Freyja 's dress , necklace and vail , and braided his hair", "context_len": 15}}}, "mead": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "dinner salmon mead whole thirsty barrels", "context_len": 6}, "NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 22, "context": "The watch was duly set ; campfires were lighted , and joints of meat suspended over them ; barrels of wine and mead were broached , for all the country around contributed with loving willingness to the support of its defenders ; and when hunger was appeased the patriotic song arose from the various fires , and stirring legends of the glorious days of old , when Danes and Norsemen fled before the English arms , nerved the courage of the men for the morrow 's stern conflict", "context_len": 88}}}, "fleece": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "ago horns fleece king Greece loved playing Helle grass garden catch clouds", "context_len": 12}, "NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 21, "context": "When Jason came to Colchis , he went to the king and said , `` Will you give me the golden fleece ? ''", "context_len": 24}}}, "plow": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "something plow bulls stronger chains noses mouths smoke plant stone flew stall", "context_len": 12}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "The next day Jason had to plow with the brass bulls and plant the dragon 's teeth", "context_len": 17}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "Of course a nearly empty B-26 wouldn \u2019 t plow up wet beach sand like a fortress", "context_len": 17}}}, "anniversaries": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "She was just seventeen , but they did not keep any account of anniversaries at Hickory Farm", "context_len": 17}}}, "elimination": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Certainly not on washing day , when every energy was absorbed in the elimination of impurity from her household linen , and life looked grotesque and hazy through clouds of soapy steam", "context_len": 32}}}, "impurity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Certainly not on washing day , when every energy was absorbed in the elimination of impurity from her household linen , and life looked grotesque and hazy through clouds of soapy steam", "context_len": 32}}}, "tribulations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Old Mrs Goodenough was always sighing for rest , and Deacon Croaker prayed every week to be set free from the trials and tribulations of this present evil world , and brought into everlasting peace", "context_len": 35}}}, "passivity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "An endless passivity seemed a dreary outlook to her active soul , which was sighing to plume its cramped wings , and soar among the endless possibilities of earth : it seemed strange that there should be no wonders to explore in heaven", "context_len": 43}}}, "soar": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 51, "context": "Each moment is so keenly lived that time flies by on eager wings , and though there may be stormy troubles sometimes , as a rule the spirit dwells , like the swallows , in an upper region of joy , which is scarcely dreamt of by those who can not soar so high", "context_len": 54}}}, "patriots": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "A thousand unrecorded patriots helped to make Wellington", "context_len": 8}}}, "trundle": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "So this trouble was at an end , and Maggie felt quite free to enjoy all the new pleasures about her , without fear of the dreaded trundle bed", "context_len": 29}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` Do you have trundle beds ? '' whispered Maggie again", "context_len": 11}}}, "hi": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Hi ! you 'll git it ! ' and he endeavoured to suspend himself from a chair by his braces", "context_len": 20}}}, "wiggle": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "The latter , by a great effort , managed to wiggle along the last few inches , and placed the ball over the final white mark", "context_len": 26}}}, "eel": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "A sharp snatch at his line , followed by a long peculiar drag , told him what was at his bait ; and after a little giving and taking , he drew a heavy twining conger eel over the boat 's edge , having no little difficulty in preventing it from tangling his line , for it was quite a yard in length , and proportionately thick", "context_len": 67}}}, "twiddling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "'Bout all them teachers is good for anyway -- settin ' like ladies twiddling at the leaves of a book , an ' thinkin ' themselves somethin ' fine because they know a few words of Latin , an ' can figure with an x", "context_len": 45}}}, "multiplication": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "He was a little boy who had run away from school to be a pirate , and very often he had been heard to remark that now he really was a pirate he would rather learn the multiplication table", "context_len": 39}}}, "unpresentable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Pauline toiled bravely over the endless array of pinafores which the youthful Hardings managed to make unpresentable in a week", "context_len": 20}}}, "wearers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "'Saves sortin ' , ' had been the brief response , when she had suggested varying the colours in order to cultivate the \u00e6sthetic instinct in the wearers", "context_len": 28}}}, "hifalutin": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "'If your hifalutin ' people mean to say that if I do n't get papering to suit their notions , I will make my boys thieves an ' liars , then it 's well for us the walls is covered with sensible green paint that 'll wash", "context_len": 47}}}, "papering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "'If your hifalutin ' people mean to say that if I do n't get papering to suit their notions , I will make my boys thieves an ' liars , then it 's well for us the walls is covered with sensible green paint that 'll wash", "context_len": 47}}}, "philandering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Her head 's as full of nonsense now as an egg is of meat , an ' she would n't know a broom from a clothes-wringer after she 'd been philandering round a couple of months with people that are never satisfied unless they 're peeking into something they ca n't understand", "context_len": 52}}}, "peeking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "Her head 's as full of nonsense now as an egg is of meat , an ' she would n't know a broom from a clothes-wringer after she 'd been philandering round a couple of months with people that are never satisfied unless they 're peeking into something they ca n't understand", "context_len": 52}}}, "sonorously": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 70, "context": "The soft spring air floated in through the open window , and she heard the birds twitter and the frogs peep : she heard Abraham Lincoln , the old horse that she used to ride to water before she grew big enough to work , whinney over his hay ; and Goliath , the young giant that had come to take his place in the farm work , answer him sonorously : the dog barked lazily as a nighthawk swept by , and in the distant hen-yard she heard a rooster crow", "context_len": 92}}}, "nighthawk": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 78, "context": "The soft spring air floated in through the open window , and she heard the birds twitter and the frogs peep : she heard Abraham Lincoln , the old horse that she used to ride to water before she grew big enough to work , whinney over his hay ; and Goliath , the young giant that had come to take his place in the farm work , answer him sonorously : the dog barked lazily as a nighthawk swept by , and in the distant hen-yard she heard a rooster crow", "context_len": 92}}}, "benison": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Her pity grew , until it rested like a benison upon all her humble friends , for they must remain in Sleepy Hollow , and she was going away", "context_len": 29}}}, "gewgaws": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "'You can have Abraham Lincoln and the spring waggon this afternoon , if you want to go to the village for your gewgaws", "context_len": 23}}}, "catnip": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "'You are a grateful piece , I must say ! ' remarked her step-mother , as she administered some catnip tea to the whining Polly", "context_len": 25}}}, "herring": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 64, "context": "A boy had heard tell that bladders would float a person , and thought he would walk on the water with them ; so he went down to the pond , tied the bladders on to his feet , and waded into the water : they found him , a few hours afterwards , feet up and head down , as dead as a herring ; and that would have been the way with our craft", "context_len": 76}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "Then he added , `` They are gulls -- herring gulls , I think", "context_len": 14}}}, "strut": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "How was this young general going to acquit himself ? Suddenly to his dismay , Wolfe got up , began to strut about the room , drew his sword , struck the table with it , and boasted about what this good sword was to do in Canada", "context_len": 48}}}, "swinish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "Lemuel burst into the room in riotous distress with a bruised knee , the result of his attempt to imitate the Prodigal Son , which had ended in an ignominious head-over-heels tumble into the midst of his swinish friends", "context_len": 39}}}, "odorous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "This caused a delay , for he had to be hurried out to the back stoop and divested of garments as odorous , if not as ragged , as those of his prototype", "context_len": 33}}}, "prototype": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "This caused a delay , for he had to be hurried out to the back stoop and divested of garments as odorous , if not as ragged , as those of his prototype", "context_len": 33}}}, "immersed": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 13, "context": "The stout man seemed to comply unwillingly , and the friends were soon immersed in calculations , quite unaware of the two pairs of watchful eyes close by which had taken in the whole situation", "context_len": 35}}}, "deltas": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The youthful Prodigal appeared again in the doorway , his ready tears had made miniature deltas over his molasses-begrimed countenance , his lower lip hung down in an impotent despair", "context_len": 30}}}, "cramming": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 69, "context": "She looked lovingly at the cows , lazily chewing their cud in the sunshine ; she felt sorry for her step-mother , as she strove to woo slumber to Polly 's wakeful eyes with the same lullaby which had done duty for the whole six ; she even found it in her heart to kiss Lemuel , who , with his ready talent for the unusual , was busily cramming mud paste into the seams of the little trunk which held her worldly all", "context_len": 84}}}, "saratogas": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "'You poor old thing ! You 'll feel as small as I shall among the saratogas and the style", "context_len": 19}}}, "pauline": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 0, "context": "Pauline leaned towards him , and laid both hands upon his arm", "context_len": 12}}}, "massively": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "The snowy table with its silver dishes and graceful centre-piece of hot-house blooms , the crystal sparkling in the rosy glow cast by silken-shaded , massively carved lamps , the perfect , noiseless serving , and the bright conversation which flowed freely , little hindered by the different courses of soup and fish , and game and ices -- conversation about things that were happening in the world which seemed to be growing larger every minute , apt allusions by Mr Davis , lively sallies by Belle , and quotations by Russell from authors who seemed to be household friends , so highly were they held in reverence", "context_len": 108}}}, "hindered": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 44, "context": "The snowy table with its silver dishes and graceful centre-piece of hot-house blooms , the crystal sparkling in the rosy glow cast by silken-shaded , massively carved lamps , the perfect , noiseless serving , and the bright conversation which flowed freely , little hindered by the different courses of soup and fish , and game and ices -- conversation about things that were happening in the world which seemed to be growing larger every minute , apt allusions by Mr Davis , lively sallies by Belle , and quotations by Russell from authors who seemed to be household friends , so highly were they held in reverence", "context_len": 108}}}, "violins": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Afterwards there had been music , Russell at the piano , and Gwendolyn and Belle with their violins , and she had sat upon the sofa by the gracious , new-found friend , who stroked her rough hand gently with her white jewelled fingers , and talked to her softly , in the pauses of the music , of what her mother was like as a girl", "context_len": 67}}}, "inborn": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "An inborn instinct of refinement made this new life immediately congenial", "context_len": 11}}}, "droning": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Her nerves quivered as she listened to Mrs Harding shrilly droning the worn-out lullaby to the sleepless Polly , and Lemuel demanding to have Jack the Giant Killer told to him six times in succession", "context_len": 35}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "An owl began to hoot in the woods beyond the river , a great stag-beetle came droning by , and the bats flew over her head with their shrill little cry , flitting here and there like night swallows", "context_len": 39}}}, "brownest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "'You have been in the brownest of studies", "context_len": 8}}}, "briar": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "' I believe it was a briar tangle , Aunt Rutha , of the worst kind ; but I shall see daylight soon , thank you", "context_len": 26}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "The woods around were full of wild flowers , briar roses , delicate primroses , and the bright red columbine , and even here and there , a late anemone ; the little star-like flower , looking almost as if it had dropped from heaven , and wondered to find itself alone and solitary , so far away from its sister stars", "context_len": 62}}}, "arteries": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "'If good will can take the place of skill , I 'll promise to cut no arteries", "context_len": 17}}}, "adaptability": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "'She has wonderful powers of adaptability , too , ' said Gwendolyn", "context_len": 12}}}, "fallow": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 64, "context": "When I told her we had a three months ' vacation , she never said , `` How delightful ! '' as most girls would , but calmly inquired what I took up in the holidays , and when I groaned at the very thought of taking up anything , she said so seriously , `` But you do n't let your mind lie fallow for three whole months ? '' And then she sighed a little , and added , half to herself , `` Some girls would give all the world for such a chance to read", "context_len": 99}}}, "shrivelling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 52, "context": "'And did you see her , ' said Gwendolyn , 'when I happened to mention that our church was always shut up in the summer because so many people were out of the city ? She just turned those splendid eyes of hers on me until I actually felt my moral stature shrivelling , and asked , `` What about the people in the city ? do n't they have to go on living ? '' '", "context_len": 77}}}, "marvelled": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 57, "context": "`` So the Sultan turned to the Vizier and said to him , `` How sayst thou , O Vizier ? He that can in so short a time avail unto riches like these , is he not worthy to be the Sultan 's son-in-law and to have his daughter to bride ? '' Now the Vizier marvelled at the greatness of these riches yet more than the Sultan , but envy was killing him and waxed on him more and more , when he saw that the Sultan was content with the bride-gift [ 435 ] and the dowry ; withal he could not gainstand the [ manifest ] truth and say to the Sultan , `` He is not worthy ; '' so he cast about", "context_len": 128}}}, "saleswoman": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "She looked in amazement at the elegantly furnished apartments of Madame Louise , and the wonderful structures of feathers and lace and ribbon , which the voluble saleswoman assured them were cheap at thirty dollars , and was lost in a rapturous delight , as , with the calmness of experienced shoppers , her cousins went from one department to another in White 's and Hovey 's , laying in a supply of airy nothings of which she did not even know the use ; always being treated by them with the same delicate consideration : there was nothing forced upon her , only , as they were getting things , she might as well be fitted too", "context_len": 118}}}, "shoppers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 51, "context": "She looked in amazement at the elegantly furnished apartments of Madame Louise , and the wonderful structures of feathers and lace and ribbon , which the voluble saleswoman assured them were cheap at thirty dollars , and was lost in a rapturous delight , as , with the calmness of experienced shoppers , her cousins went from one department to another in White 's and Hovey 's , laying in a supply of airy nothings of which she did not even know the use ; always being treated by them with the same delicate consideration : there was nothing forced upon her , only , as they were getting things , she might as well be fitted too", "context_len": 118}}}, "macaroons": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Lilian put out a very tempting looking selection of rock-buns and ginger-nuts , and Archie produced a tin of real Scotch shortbread and some macaroons , a contribution from his aunt ; so with bread-and-butter , and a pot of the newly-made blackberry jam , there was quite a noble display", "context_len": 51}}}, "frothed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "Then to Huyler 's for ices and macaroons , then up past St Paul 's and the Common , and then home to a lunch of chicken salad and strawberries and frothed chocolate , in the cool dining-room , with its massive leather-covered chairs and potted plants and roses", "context_len": 49}}}, "potted": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "Then to Huyler 's for ices and macaroons , then up past St Paul 's and the Common , and then home to a lunch of chicken salad and strawberries and frothed chocolate , in the cool dining-room , with its massive leather-covered chairs and potted plants and roses", "context_len": 49}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "Why not a plane while we \u2019 re about it ? I \u2019 d rather take a chance of getting shot down by our own fighters than be potted like a sitting duck on the water by Jap Zeros", "context_len": 39}}}, "lifelong": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "'What an inveterate bookworm you are , Paul , ' and Belle looked at the pile of volumes Pauline had brought from the library to study in the long morning hours which the force of a lifelong habit gave her , before the rest of the family were astir", "context_len": 49}}}, "ignoramus": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "'You forget I am an ignoramus , ' she answered quietly", "context_len": 11}}}, "heresy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "'Such a speech from a woman in this age is rank heresy ! '", "context_len": 14}}}, "suffrage": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "'Oh , of course , if you are going in for equal suffrage and anti-opium , and the rest , but I never aspired to the garment of either Lucy Stone or Frances Willard", "context_len": 34}}}, "aspired": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "'Oh , of course , if you are going in for equal suffrage and anti-opium , and the rest , but I never aspired to the garment of either Lucy Stone or Frances Willard", "context_len": 34}}}, "anatomist": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "I do pine to be an anatomist , and Professor Herschel says I have a decided talent for it too", "context_len": 20}}}, "progressive": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "However , papa is not progressive , at least he does not want his daughters to be , although I tell him I might be a professor in Harvard some day , so there is nothing left for me but to fall into the ranks of the majority and do nothing", "context_len": 51}}}, "opium": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "'Why so ? Is there nothing in the world but suffrage , and opium and -- anatomy ? '", "context_len": 19}}}, "philanthropy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "'Oh , dear , yes , there 's philanthropy , but Gwen does that for the family", "context_len": 17}}}, "ameliorate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "In fact she is fairly torn asunder in her efforts to ameliorate the condition of the `` submerged tenth", "context_len": 19}}}, "unrighteousness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "'You dear stupid , of course ! The unseen population in filth , rags and unrighteousness , and the rest of us in lazy self-indulgence , which , perhaps , in God 's sight , is about as bad", "context_len": 39}}}, "societies": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "But a calmer spirit prevailed , and when some of the school societies took up the matter it was agreed that the four had a right to do as they pleased , and that the lad who had made the offensive remark was in the wrong ; and so matters quieted down", "context_len": 52}}}, "theorize": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` I ca n't , '' admitted Bart , `` except to theorize , of course , that someone had enough interest in myself or the company to rush into the burning shed and save the books and close the safe while I was getting my father to safety", "context_len": 49}}}, "vineyard": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "Do n't you know there is work and work ? God says , `` Go work in My vineyard , '' and we good Christians answer , `` Yes , Lord , but let some one else go ahead and take out the stumps", "context_len": 44}}}, "thistles": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "It is pleasanter to drive a team of eight horses over cleared land than to grub out dockweed and thistles all alone in one corner", "context_len": 25}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Ten thousand thistles ! '' yelled Tom as he began pulling off his pajamas", "context_len": 15}}}, "monarchical": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "I had no idea you had monarchical tendencies", "context_len": 8}}}, "summertime": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "It was chilly work getting up in the dark mornings by candle-light , and driving off to school when the sun had scarcely risen ; the four miles of road seemed much longer than they had done in the summertime , and in spite of woollen gloves , the hand which held the reins was apt to be stiff and numb with the cold long before Warford was reached", "context_len": 69}}}, "gie": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Oh , wad some power the giftie gie us , to see oorsels as ithers see us", "context_len": 18}}}, "altitudes": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "'Do you believe in altitudes ? ' It was Richard Everidge , Aunt Rutha 's favourite nephew , who asked the question of Pauline , as they sat on the broad piazza after church waiting for lunch", "context_len": 37}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Even at somewhat lower altitudes they helped keep your head clear and your stomach in place", "context_len": 16}}}, "trilogy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "' I mean that trilogy of exulting triumph over the trammels of circumstance that Mr Dunn gave us this morning", "context_len": 20}}}, "anthem": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "Some of the boys in the crowd `` caught on '' to the situation , and giggled and made significant remarks , but the chairman on the platform covered the colonel 's confusion by announcing the national anthem , and Bart effected his escape", "context_len": 44}}}, "dirge": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Do n't you remember ? `` Life is what we make it -- an anthem or a dirge , a psalm of hope or a lamentation of despair", "context_len": 28}}}, "tandem": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "'There have been so many other things , ' said Belle , 'tennis , you know , and canoe practice and tandem parties", "context_len": 23}}}, "exams": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "'But that is only when Russ and I are not reading up for exams", "context_len": 14}}}, "holden": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "O country the fairest ! Our country the dearest , We press toward thee ! O Sion the golden ! Our eyes are still holden , Thy light till we see", "context_len": 31}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "The Sultan came out to meet him and give him joy and embraced him and kissed him , and there was high festival holden in the kingdom and great rejoicing", "context_len": 30}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` Then the Sultan bade decorate the city and hold festival and rejoicings and commanded the crier to cry in the city that that day was a great festival , wherefore rejoicings should be holden in all the realm during the space of a month , [ to wit , ] thirty days ' time , for the return of the Lady Bedrulbudour his daughter and her husband Alaeddin", "context_len": 69}}}, "mansions": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "There were cement mines to be seen , and old white-faced mansions ; and half-way up the boat passed under a lofty iron bridge across which dashed a railway train", "context_len": 30}}}, "dowered": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Pauline saw a large bay window opening into a tiny conservatory , which loving hands kept dowered with a profusion of blooming plants", "context_len": 23}}}, "buhl": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "The room was large and dainty with delicate draperies , two or three fine pictures , and a beautiful representation in marble of the Angel of Patience , which stood on a buhl table , where the invalid 's eyes could always rest upon it", "context_len": 45}}}, "wrestled": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "Young Hermann 's sword broke against a Danish axe ; he rushed in and got within the swing of the weapon ; both wrestled for the deadly steel , they fell , rolled over and over on the grass ; at length Hermann grasped his opponent 's throat like a vice with his mailed hand , and held till the arms of his foe hung nerveless by the side and the face grew black , when , disengaging his right hand , he found his dagger , and drove it to the victim 's heart", "context_len": 95}}}, "lustrous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Her eyes were lustrous with a radiance not of earth , and a wealth of silver hair fell in soft curling waves about her face ; her mouth , sweet and tender , parted in a smile of welcome as she held out her hands to the girls", "context_len": 48}}}, "thrusts": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 35, "context": "The moment before Harry Paul seemed to have been exerting his full strength to force the boat through the water , but an accession of strength came to him , and with a few fierce thrusts he drove her bows into the edge of the current , which gave it so quick a snatch that it was whirled round , and its occupant nearly lost his footing ; but he was too practised a boatman for that", "context_len": 77}}}, "gaslit": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 53, "context": "`` Would you like a heaven so small , so human , that mortal words could line it out , and mortal wishes be its boundary ? The things we look for are prepared by One whose thoughts are as far above our thoughts as the broad starlit heaven is above this little gaslit earth", "context_len": 55}}}, "identities": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "And do you think that people are to be all massed in heaven , losing their various identities , their differing tastes , their separate natures ? Going from this lower world so full of its adaptations , where colour and form take on a thousand changes , and life and pursuit can be varied almost at will , to a mere dead level of perfect felicity ? To leave earth where no two things are alike , and go to heaven to find no two different ! The Lord 's preparations mean more than that", "context_len": 96}}}, "adaptations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "And do you think that people are to be all massed in heaven , losing their various identities , their differing tastes , their separate natures ? Going from this lower world so full of its adaptations , where colour and form take on a thousand changes , and life and pursuit can be varied almost at will , to a mere dead level of perfect felicity ? To leave earth where no two things are alike , and go to heaven to find no two different ! The Lord 's preparations mean more than that", "context_len": 96}}}, "felicity": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 66, "context": "And do you think that people are to be all massed in heaven , losing their various identities , their differing tastes , their separate natures ? Going from this lower world so full of its adaptations , where colour and form take on a thousand changes , and life and pursuit can be varied almost at will , to a mere dead level of perfect felicity ? To leave earth where no two things are alike , and go to heaven to find no two different ! The Lord 's preparations mean more than that", "context_len": 96}}}, "plashing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "What blessed coolness was in the night air as Bomba drank it in deep draughts ! And what added delight came to him as he felt on his uplifted face the plashing of raindrops !", "context_len": 35}}}, "biology": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 48, "context": "`` I say , you fellows , '' remarked Sid one sunny November afternoon , when all three chums were in the room after lectures , `` do n't you want to take a walk with me ? I 've got to do some observation work in my biology course , and I 'm going to take my camera along and make some pictures", "context_len": 64}}}, "physics": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "She would take up chemistry , of course , and biology , mathematics and physics , French and Latin , geology and botany , and -- well , she would decide later upon the rest of her curriculum", "context_len": 38}}}, "geology": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "She would take up chemistry , of course , and biology , mathematics and physics , French and Latin , geology and botany , and -- well , she would decide later upon the rest of her curriculum", "context_len": 38}}}, "botany": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "She would take up chemistry , of course , and biology , mathematics and physics , French and Latin , geology and botany , and -- well , she would decide later upon the rest of her curriculum", "context_len": 38}}}, "curriculum": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "She would take up chemistry , of course , and biology , mathematics and physics , French and Latin , geology and botany , and -- well , she would decide later upon the rest of her curriculum", "context_len": 38}}}, "diversions": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "Never had a night seemed so long ; the only one who thoroughly enjoyed himself was the puppy , who , delighted to receive so much attention , pursued his diversions until the sun was well in at the window , when at length he snuggled into Peggy 's pillow , and composed himself to sleep , leaving his weary guardians only time for a brief rest before Nancy 's unwelcome tap was heard at the door", "context_len": 77}}}, "reformers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Look at the men who have made their mark in the world -- reformers , inventors , discoverers , all men of a single purpose ; and Paul says , `` This one thing I do", "context_len": 36}}}, "inventors": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Look at the men who have made their mark in the world -- reformers , inventors , discoverers , all men of a single purpose ; and Paul says , `` This one thing I do", "context_len": 36}}}, "discoverers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Look at the men who have made their mark in the world -- reformers , inventors , discoverers , all men of a single purpose ; and Paul says , `` This one thing I do", "context_len": 36}}}, "strives": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "The first person an Apache strives to kill when the chance offers is he who has given him bread and drink", "context_len": 21}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "blanks": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "The inspector asks me to go and see him , take some of our blanks , open a set of books for him , and try and install a system that will bring things around clearer", "context_len": 36}}}, "sanctification": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "-- I preached at Astbury at five , and at seven proclaimed at Congleton Cross Jesus Christ our wisdom and righteousness and sanctification and redemption", "context_len": 25}}}, "snapshot": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "'My dear Paul , do you know we are all waiting ? What have you been doing ? If I could only get a snapshot at you now I should call it `` The Intoxication of Success", "context_len": 37}}}, "rhapsodies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 54, "context": "`` You would make a splendid Jeanne d'Arc , with the light of high and holy purpose in her eyes ; but as this is the last Saturday in the year that we shall have the chance of a ride to Forest Glen and home by moonlight , I move that we postpone our rhapsodies until a more convenient season", "context_len": 60}}}, "hampers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "He was standing on his tip-toes , for the rack was high , and was just taking down one of the small hampers , when there came a sudden crash , followed by a ripping , tearing sound , and before Bart knew what had happened he was deposited in a heap on top of Fenn , who , in turn , was mixed up with Frank and Ned", "context_len": 69}}}, "wooed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Even Gwen has been wooed by the beauty of the morning to accompany us , though I think there are about a dozen meetings on her calendar", "context_len": 27}}}, "calendar": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 32, "context": "It was about five in the afternoon , but it was also the third of July , and that date , like the twenty-fourth of December , was the busiest in the calendar for the little express office", "context_len": 38}}}, "beatitude": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "'Dear Tryphosa , ' she whispered to herself with a smile , 'you little thought , when you gave me that new beatitude , what constant friends the grey angel of Drudgery and I were to be", "context_len": 37}}}, "incompatible": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "'Perhaps , but ease and victory are for ever incompatible", "context_len": 10}}}, "similitude": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` Silver purified seven times , '' and `` gold tried in the fire , '' and `` polished after the similitude of a palace", "context_len": 25}}}, "keynote": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "He puts us in tune with our great keynote Jesus , and then we are like an \u00c6olian harp", "context_len": 19}}}, "huskiness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "Russell and Gwen protested , Aunt Rutha looked at her with sorrowful eyes , and Mr Davis repeated that the very idea was absurd , as he paced up and down with a strange huskiness in his throat", "context_len": 38}}}, "perseverance": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` Some one has said : `` Latin and Greek , and music and art , and travel , are the decorations of life , but industry and perseverance , courage before difficulties , and cheer under straining burdens , self-control and self-denial are the indispensables", "context_len": 46}}}, "indispensables": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "`` Some one has said : `` Latin and Greek , and music and art , and travel , are the decorations of life , but industry and perseverance , courage before difficulties , and cheer under straining burdens , self-control and self-denial are the indispensables", "context_len": 46}}}, "educates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "It is our daily task that mainly educates us , and the humblest woman may live splendidly", "context_len": 17}}}, "handshake": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "As the train slackened speed , Pauline lifted her eyes from the book which Richard Everidge had laid on the seat beside her , after giving her that last strong handshake , to see her father standing in front of the Sleepy Hollow Station", "context_len": 44}}}, "denuded": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "Abraham Lincoln and the spring waggon ! Had the whole beautiful summer been one delicious dream ? Could it be only a week since she had stood entranced in that forest of flame ? Here the leaves hung brown and shrivelled on the denuded branches , stray flakes of snow were in the air , and the early twilight fell chill and dreary", "context_len": 63}}}, "grotesquely": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "She looked at the solitary lamp with its grotesquely hideous ornament of red flannel , at Susan 's expressionless , freckled face , at the boys in their copper-toed boots and overalls , at the good-natured , but hopelessly common-place Martha Spriggs , with her thin hair drawn tight into a knob the size of a bullet , and her bare arms akimbo", "context_len": 63}}}, "idealize": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "'Idealize her real ! ' Would it be possible to idealize anything at Sleepy Hollow ?", "context_len": 16}}}, "behindhand": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "It was churning day , and there was baking to be done , and the mending was behindhand , and the children needed clothes ; besides the numerous 'odd jobs ' which Mrs Harding had deferred , but which she was prompt to require done as soon as she had some one besides Martha to call on", "context_len": 57}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "' I hear you are behindhand with your wool , ' she said , in her straightforward way", "context_len": 18}}}, "deferred": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 35, "context": "It was churning day , and there was baking to be done , and the mending was behindhand , and the children needed clothes ; besides the numerous 'odd jobs ' which Mrs Harding had deferred , but which she was prompt to require done as soon as she had some one besides Martha to call on", "context_len": 57}}}, "fretfully": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "'It beats me , ' said Mrs Harding fretfully , as Pauline hushed Polly to sleep , 'what you do to that child", "context_len": 23}}}, "powders": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "That new doctor 's no more good than his powders are", "context_len": 11}}}, "scintillating": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "Humming-birds , green-backed , lily-breasted , with purple throat and crest , darted hither and thither like living gems , with a hundred firelike reflections scintillating from their little bodies", "context_len": 30}}}, "reposing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 46, "context": "'Ca n't , ' said the boy coolly , ' I 'm goin ' ter tie it to Poll 's balloon , an ' let go of the string , an ' then it 'll go straight to heaven , ' and , with the letter reposing in his cheek , he began to sing vociferously : --", "context_len": 58}}}, "vociferously": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 55, "context": "'Ca n't , ' said the boy coolly , ' I 'm goin ' ter tie it to Poll 's balloon , an ' let go of the string , an ' then it 'll go straight to heaven , ' and , with the letter reposing in his cheek , he began to sing vociferously : --", "context_len": 58}}}, "scalding": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "A cry from Polly made Pauline hurry into the house to find that Martha Spriggs had slipped while passing the child 's couch , and upset a bowl of scalding milk , which she was carrying , right over the little invalid 's foot", "context_len": 44}}}, "bossed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "'Ai n't goin ' ter be bossed by nobody , ' said the boy doggedly , as he reached his room", "context_len": 21}}}, "discontentedly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "'Father is gettin ' so mortal queer , ' said Stephen discontentedly", "context_len": 12}}}, "querulous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "'Pawliney ! ' called her father , in the querulous accents of one whose brain is weakening", "context_len": 17}}}, "forehanded": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "I ai n't partial to payin ' forehanded , but you was awful kind to Mis ' Croaker when her rheumatiz was bad on her , an ' I ai n't one ter forgit a favour", "context_len": 36}}}, "spinner": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Mis ' Croaker will be rale pleased ; she thinks you 're the best spinner in the valley", "context_len": 18}}}, "ben": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` This is an evident mistake ( due to some ignorant copyist or reciter of the story ) of the same kind as that to be found at the commencement of the story of Ghanim ben Eyoub , ( see my Book of the Thousand Nights and One Night , Vol I", "context_len": 52}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "BEN THE LUGGAGE BOY", "context_len": 4}}}, "vigils": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 56, "context": "In the evenings , long after the others were in bed , she paced up and down the kitchen , spinning Deacon Croaker 's wool into smooth , even threads , but her heart ached as she prayed for her boy , and often , when in the still watches of the night Polly kept her vigils with pain , she heard her cry softly : --", "context_len": 67}}}, "consecrated": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 39, "context": "'Just imagine , Paul , ' the letter went on , ' I , of all possible people , a missionary 's wife ! But the fact of the matter is , my precious saint , your splendid , consecrated life made me tingle with shame to my finger tips when I thought of my aimless existence , and when I remembered how you took up your cross and followed your Master to Sleepy Hollow , there seemed to be no reason why I should not follow Him to Africa", "context_len": 90}}}, "guiding": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 22, "context": "It was both amusing and touching to see the watch she kept over this child who was twice her own size , guiding his steps with a motherly sort of care , looking up at him with wistful pity and tenderness , and speaking to him in a soft , coaxing voice such as one would use to an infant", "context_len": 60}}}, "sloth": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "If it will comfort you , I want you to know that you have been the guiding star which has led me out of the sloth of my selfishness into active work for the King", "context_len": 35}}}, "haymow": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 39, "context": "How could he save Aleck ? The fire was plainly at the foot of the stairs , even if they were not already burning , while those stifling clouds of smoke rolled between them and the doors of the haymow , and were now pouring up through every chink and cranny of the floor on which he stood", "context_len": 58}}}, "huskily": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Is it -- is it as bad as that ? '' asked Tom huskily , and he put his arm over Phil 's shoulder , as his chum sat in the old easy chair", "context_len": 35}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Perhaps if you knew all about it you would change your opinion of me , said the boy a trifle huskily , for he was affected by her confidence in him", "context_len": 31}}}, "quaintest": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "'It is the quaintest old house , with low , small rooms , except on the east side , where Captain Lemuel has added two large rooms with the loveliest bay windows , which are always full of flowers and sunshine", "context_len": 41}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "But of all the rooms I think the quaintest was the kitchen", "context_len": 12}}}, "snoozed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "'MY DEAREST PAPA , -- We are stopping now in the quaintest little place , a veritable Sleepy Hollow , like its name , where Rip Van Winkle might have snoozed away for centuries without fear of being disturbed", "context_len": 39}}}, "stepbrothers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "My princess , and her two stepbrothers , Stephen and Lemuel , and Polly , who has been a sufferer from spinal trouble all her life", "context_len": 26}}}, "spinal": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 7, "context": "I have known a child develop a spinal complaint from a far less fall , and I should have thought you were old enough , Margaret , to restrain your brother from such foolish feats , instead of encouraging him", "context_len": 40}}}, "idolize": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "You know country people always keep their best parlours done up in must and green paper ; but the princess says , `` Nothing is too good for Polly and the boys ! '' They just idolize her , and I fancy they have good reason to , for , as Stephen said , in his queer , blunt way , `` she comes as near to an angel as any mortal ever will", "context_len": 74}}}, "dispenses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "She seems to have all the destitute and afflicted under her wing , and dispenses beef-tea and Bible promises with the same liberal hand", "context_len": 24}}}, "perfectness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Do n't you know that `` he who aims for perfectness in a trifle , is trying to do that trifle holily ? '' '", "context_len": 25}}}, "holily": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Do n't you know that `` he who aims for perfectness in a trifle , is trying to do that trifle holily ? '' '", "context_len": 25}}}, "freshman": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 17, "context": "Tom and Sid , of whom more will be told presently , had roomed together during their freshman year at Randall , and Phil 's apartment was not far away", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 23, "context": "In fact , to this day the yarn is handed down in the annals of Randall College as an example of how a freshman , single-handed , played a joke on three sophomores ; for it developed that Gerhart had done the trick alone", "context_len": 44}}}, "splice": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "We 'll splice it", "context_len": 4}}}, "freshmen": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 20, "context": "From various sophomores who came into their room to exchange notes , Tom , Phil and Sid learned that the freshmen had executed a well-organized fire scare by the simple process of burning in each corridor some of the powder extensively used on Fourth of July , or in political parades", "context_len": 51}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` We have not forgotten that we were once freshmen , and that we had many clashes with the second-years", "context_len": 20}}}, "racquets": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "In the center was piled a collection of baseball bats , tennis racquets , boxing gloves , foils , catching gloves , a football , some running trousers , a couple of sweaters , and a nondescript collection of books", "context_len": 40}}}, "sweaters": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "In the center was piled a collection of baseball bats , tennis racquets , boxing gloves , foils , catching gloves , a football , some running trousers , a couple of sweaters , and a nondescript collection of books", "context_len": 40}}}, "nondescript": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "In the center was piled a collection of baseball bats , tennis racquets , boxing gloves , foils , catching gloves , a football , some running trousers , a couple of sweaters , and a nondescript collection of books", "context_len": 40}}}, "roomed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "It was no easy task , but they managed to get it out of the east dormitory , where they had roomed for a year , and then they began the journey across a stretch of grass to the west building", "context_len": 41}}}, "sophomores": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 26, "context": "It was to be held in a hall in Haddonfield , and many were the precautions taken by the committee to prevent any of the hated sophomores from attending , or getting to the place beforehand , lest they might , by some untoward act , `` put it on the blink , '' as Holly Cross used to say", "context_len": 60}}}, "juniors": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 45, "context": "With the opening of the new term , when the freshmen had become more or less dignified sophomores , Phil had proposed that he and his two chums shift to a large room in the west dormitory , where the majority of the sophomores and juniors lived", "context_len": 47}}}, "fiddlesticks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` Artistic fiddlesticks ! '' exclaimed Phil", "context_len": 7}}}, "flounce": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` One could , I suppose , put a sort of drapery -- flounce , I believe , is the proper word -- over it , '' went on Tom", "context_len": 30}}}, "anvil": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` It 's a regular anvil chorus , '' remarked Tom", "context_len": 11}}}, "sagging": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "Farther along the street stood a square , old-fashioned , story-and-a-half house , with a more modern ell and shed adjoining , and a wretched sagging barn , that lurched on its foundations , and was only kept from toppling farther , and possibly falling , by long , crude timber props , set against its side", "context_len": 57}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "I I haven \u2019 t much time , faltered Stone ; but he wonderingly took his place at Roger \u2019 s side and was whirled away , regretfully watched by Jimmy , who hung on the sagging gate and stared after the carriage until it turned the corner under the street-light opposite the post office", "context_len": 55}}}, "inventor": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` It 's like putting a new keel on a ship ! '' cried the inventor of the scheme gaily", "context_len": 20}}}, "shay": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "That 's like the 'one-horse shay ' -- it 'll hold together until it flies apart by spontaneous combustion", "context_len": 19}}}, "spontaneous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "That 's like the 'one-horse shay ' -- it 'll hold together until it flies apart by spontaneous combustion", "context_len": 19}}}, "combustion": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "That 's like the 'one-horse shay ' -- it 'll hold together until it flies apart by spontaneous combustion", "context_len": 19}}}, "insertion": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Yes , I guess with a ruffle or two , a little insertion , and a bit of old lace , it will hide the fractured places , Phil", "context_len": 30}}}, "insert": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Say , why did n't you put new legs on it , insert new springs , and cover it over while you were about it ? '' he asked sarcastically", "context_len": 31}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "Phil , who was holding the cork , endeavored to insert it during the transfer", "context_len": 15}}}, "movers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "By this time the throng had done with punishing poor Fenton , and once more turned its attention to the movers", "context_len": 21}}}, "antiques": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` No ; they 've been to an auction sale of antiques , and this is the bed on which Louis XIV slept the night before he ate the Welsh rarebit , '' declared Ed Kerr , the champion catcher on the 'varsity nine", "context_len": 44}}}, "rarebit": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` No ; they 've been to an auction sale of antiques , and this is the bed on which Louis XIV slept the night before he ate the Welsh rarebit , '' declared Ed Kerr , the champion catcher on the 'varsity nine", "context_len": 44}}}, "catcher": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "`` No ; they 've been to an auction sale of antiques , and this is the bed on which Louis XIV slept the night before he ate the Welsh rarebit , '' declared Ed Kerr , the champion catcher on the 'varsity nine", "context_len": 44}}}, "horseplay": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` Here , let me see if I can jump over it while it is in motion , '' he added , for he was full of `` horseplay , '' and always anxious to try something new", "context_len": 38}}}, "horsehair": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Make him eat the horsehair in the sofa ! ''", "context_len": 11}}}, "sanctuary": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` But if we put a little more speed on we can get to the dormitory , and that will be sanctuary , I guess", "context_len": 25}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 84, "context": "But , though all is well that ends well , Peggy was still so tear-stained and upset that she did not feel equal to facing her school-fellows , however sympathetic they might be , so she escaped with Lilian through the side-door into the street , feeling she would never be really happy or at ease again till she was back in that haven of home where she was always appreciated and understood , and pouring out her troubles to Aunt Helen in the sanctuary of the Rose Parlour", "context_len": 89}}}, "holly": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 11, "context": "Lilian transformed the pulpit into a perfect bower , weaving in holly and laurels till the Rector hoped she would leave room for him inside it ; and Peggy and Bobby helped -- or hindered -- the schoolmistress as she adorned the font with a wonderful arrangement of cotton-wool and frosted leaves", "context_len": 52}}}, "ark": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 40, "context": "`` I am glad God made the rainbow , 'cause it is so pretty , '' said Maggie ; `` but I think Noah might have believed him without that , when he took such care of him in the ark", "context_len": 41}}}, "sid": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Sid picked up the leg , and then , with Phil mounting the stairs backward , carrying one end , and Tom advancing and holding the other , the task was begun", "context_len": 32}}}, "indefinable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "They were both well dressed in expensive clothes , and there was about them that indefinable air of `` sportiness '' which is so easily recognizable but hard to acquire", "context_len": 30}}}, "sportiness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "They were both well dressed in expensive clothes , and there was about them that indefinable air of `` sportiness '' which is so easily recognizable but hard to acquire", "context_len": 30}}}, "recognizable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "They were both well dressed in expensive clothes , and there was about them that indefinable air of `` sportiness '' which is so easily recognizable but hard to acquire", "context_len": 30}}}, "blocking": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 43, "context": "They stooped to aid him , and as the monkeys recovered from their surprise at the sudden disappearance of their enemies and began to search out the entrance to the cave , those within rolled the great stone to the entrance , fairly blocking it", "context_len": 45}}}, "tradesmen": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` Here , what do you fellows mean by blocking up the stairway ? Do n't you know that no tradesmen are allowed in this entrance ? ''", "context_len": 28}}}, "proctor": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` What ! Do you mean to say that you fellows are moving that fuzzy-wuzzy piece of architecture into this dormitory ? I 'll not stand for it ! I 'll complain to the proctor ! Why , it 's full of disease germs ! ''", "context_len": 46}}}, "sneeringly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Do any of you fellows want to bet ? '' he asked rather sneeringly , as he looked down at Tom , Phil and Sid", "context_len": 26}}}, "sporty": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "Thus it was that there came about no fight between Phil and the `` sporty freshman , '' as he began to be called", "context_len": 24}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "He had thrown his cap back on his head in a `` sporty , '' off-handish way , and he tried hard to impersonate a reckless young adventurer taking things as they came , and audacious enough to pick up a handy meal anyhow or anywhere", "context_len": 46}}}, "digs": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "I \u2019 ve named him Rover ; and don \u2019 t you think he knows when the tide is down just as well as I do ; then he goes to the beach , and digs clams with his nose ; he never goes a clamming at high water", "context_len": 49}}}, "disinfectant": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` Smell the germs ! Wow ! Get me some disinfectant , Gerhart", "context_len": 13}}}, "exaggerating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "You deliberately pushed me down , Tom Parsons , and I 'll fix you for it ! '' and Langridge limped about , exaggerating the hurt to his ankle", "context_len": 29}}}, "fathoming": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "There was an obvious meaning in Tom 's words , and Langridge had no trouble in fathoming it", "context_len": 18}}}, "kibosh": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "`` Why , you little freshie , '' exclaimed Phil , `` fair fights are the only kind we have at Randall ! We do n't have 'em very often , but every time we do Tom puts the kibosh all over your friend Langridge", "context_len": 45}}}, "sophomore": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 19, "context": "Hello ! There 's Langridge joining him now , Phil , '' and as Tom spoke they saw the sophomore come from a side path and walk along with the freshman", "context_len": 31}}}, "punctured": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "A lamp was smashed , and some of the electric terminals had been jarred from their posts , but not a tire had been punctured , and the machine seemed as capable as ever of taking the road", "context_len": 38}}}, "randall": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 0, "context": "Randall must kick , and the yells of delight that had greeted her first advance were silenced , while the supporters of the co-educational academy prepared to encourage their players by vocal efforts", "context_len": 33}}}, "athletics": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "These three colleges had formed the Tonoka Lake League in athletics , and the rivalry on the gridiron and diamond , as well as in milder forms of sport -- rowing , tennis , basketball and hockey -- ran high", "context_len": 40}}}, "gridiron": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 59, "context": "It was due to a great catch which Phil made in the deciding championship game , almost as much as to Tom 's wonderful pitching , that Randall had the banner , and Captain Holly Cross , of the eleven , had marked Phil for one of his men during the season which was about to open on the gridiron", "context_len": 60}}}, "basketball": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "Here we present three crackerjack stories of baseball , football , and basketball , written in the vernacular of the boy of to-day , full of action , suspense and thrills , in language every boy will understand , and which we know will be enthusiastically endorsed by all boys", "context_len": 50}}}, "gamble": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "Langridge was a good pitcher , but he secretly drank and smoked , to say nothing of staying up late nights to gamble ; and so he was not in good form", "context_len": 32}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Football is pretty much a gamble", "context_len": 6}}}, "phenomenal": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "When it came to the crucial moment he could not `` make good , '' and Tom was put in his place , in the pitching box , and by phenomenal work won the deciding game", "context_len": 36}}}, "supplanted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 81, "context": "The only person who was not won over by Archie 's friendly ways and frank American manners was Joe , who gloomily prophesied broken necks and kindred evils as the result of the children 's association with 'the young master from furrin parts ' ; but I fear there was a good deal of jealousy in this , for poor Joe had been a hero to the children in his modest way , and it was hard to find himself suddenly supplanted in their affections , especially by a rival with whom it was quite impossible to compete", "context_len": 98}}}, "championship": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 13, "context": "It was due to a great catch which Phil made in the deciding championship game , almost as much as to Tom 's wonderful pitching , that Randall had the banner , and Captain Holly Cross , of the eleven , had marked Phil for one of his men during the season which was about to open on the gridiron", "context_len": 60}}}, "vacating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` Now we 'll take the old armchair over , '' proposed Tom , when he and his chums had reached the room they were vacating", "context_len": 26}}}, "paraphernalia": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` Well , suppose we go down to my house after school , and look over our camping stuff , '' suggested Bart , for the tents , stoves and other paraphernalia was kept in his barn", "context_len": 37}}}, "looker": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` She 's an all right looker , '' answered the other with such enthusiasm that Tom glanced at him a trifle sharply", "context_len": 23}}}, "insistingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` You ca n't hurt this clock , '' declared Phil , and , as if to prove his words , the fussy little timepiece began ticking away again , as loudly and insistingly as ever", "context_len": 36}}}, "oppressively": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "It was curiously quiet -- oppressively so", "context_len": 7}}}, "erratically": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "In doing so he knocked from a chair a football , which bounded erratically about the room , as the spherical pigskin always does bounce", "context_len": 25}}}, "spherical": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "In doing so he knocked from a chair a football , which bounded erratically about the room , as the spherical pigskin always does bounce", "context_len": 25}}}, "bounce": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "In doing so he knocked from a chair a football , which bounded erratically about the room , as the spherical pigskin always does bounce", "context_len": 25}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 38, "context": "The Randall line held , and Holly booted the pigskin in fine shape , but with a groan almost of anguish the players and supporters of the college by the river saw the ball strike the cross-bar and bounce back", "context_len": 40}}}, "overwrought": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 6, "context": "It was such bathos after the overwrought state of their feelings that they sat down and laughed till the tears ran down their cheeks , treasuring the joke to tell to Archie when he should be well enough to receive visitors , and holding it up as a model to relieve Nancy 's fears", "context_len": 54}}}, "upholsterer": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Fred saucily asked him if he had come home to set up an upholsterer 's shop , knowing he could himself furnish plenty of stuffing for mattresses and sofas", "context_len": 29}}}, "renovated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "I really think we ought to send it to the upholsterer 's and have it renovated", "context_len": 16}}}, "sorting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "This suited his companions , and soon they were cleaning their guns , sorting cartridges and fitting them in their belts , taking care not to make the mistake Frank did , when he was treed by the wildcat ; and looking to their clothing and hunting boots", "context_len": 48}}}, "genially": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "Jones had genially assured him that on evenings when the weather became colder he would be welcome to sit and study by the open fire in the sitting-room , a concession for which he had been duly grateful", "context_len": 38}}}, "misogynist": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Aw , forget it , you old misanthropic specimen of a misogynist ! '' exclaimed Phil with a laugh", "context_len": 20}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` If you did it 's a judgment on you , misogynist that you are", "context_len": 15}}}, "fledglings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "In that time most of the students had settled in their new rooms , the freshmen found their places , some were properly hazed , and that ordeal for others was postponed until a future date , much to the misery of the fledglings", "context_len": 44}}}, "ban": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` His uncle has put a ban on it", "context_len": 9}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "But Stone \u2019 s own record is enough to ban him from decent company", "context_len": 14}}}, "formations": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` Now , fellows , '' said the coach , `` we 'll just have a little running , tackling , passing the ball , some simple formations and other exercises to test your wind and legs", "context_len": 37}}}, "cautioning": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Now and then they gave a word of advice , cautioning some player about wrong movements , or suggesting a different method", "context_len": 22}}}, "milksop": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` You 're a namby-pamby milksop ! '' exclaimed Phil with a laugh and a pat on the back , that took all the sting from the words", "context_len": 28}}}, "troglodytic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` You 're a misguided , crusty , hard-shelled troglodytic specimen of a misogynist ! '' exclaimed Tom", "context_len": 18}}}, "elaborately": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 29, "context": "I was not at all pleased with his manner when I met him at the Willows , ' frowning slightly at the remembrance ; for Archie 's cool and elaborately courteous treatment of her criticisms on that occasion had completely baffled her", "context_len": 42}}}, "averages": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Set it at eleven , and by the law of averages it ought to go off at five", "context_len": 18}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "I do n't believe Uncle Job himself averages over a dollar and a quarter a day , and he has a family to support", "context_len": 24}}}, "fumble": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 4, "context": "The result was a fumble , the phial slipped from Sid 's grasp , Tom made a grab for it , but missed , and Phil , with only one good hand , could do nothing", "context_len": 36}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "He would glance at Bart , start to speak , lower his eyes , and , turning pale as he seemed to remember , and turning red as he seemed to realize , would fumble at his watch fob , run his fingers through his hair and act flustered generally", "context_len": 50}}}, "whiffs": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "Did n't I see him sneak up to Lighton and put him wise to the fact that I 'd taken a few whiffs ? I only smoked half a cigarette in the dressing-room , but Clinton must have spied on me", "context_len": 41}}}, "butting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Well , I do , but I 'm not going to stand for Clinton butting in the way he did", "context_len": 21}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Lammie-noo had gone through to the kitchen and stood by the big stove bleating pitifully and occasionally butting the good Bingi who was as patient as a lamb himself", "context_len": 29}}}, "crony": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 8, "context": "Come on , '' and Langridge and his crony disappeared in the darkness , talking , meanwhile , of a dastardly plot they had evolved to disable Phil Clinton", "context_len": 29}}}, "professionals": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Phil Clinton has boxed with professionals , and beaten them , too", "context_len": 13}}}, "hitter": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "Phil went up against a heavy hitter and knocked him out in four rounds", "context_len": 14}}}, "deposed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 54, "context": "While halting for these foraging parties to return , a Persian nobleman came into the camp , and informed Alexander that Darius and the forces accompanying him were encamped about two days ' march in advance , but that Bessus was in command -- the conspiracy having been successful , and Darius having been deposed and made a prisoner", "context_len": 59}}}, "apologizes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Does that mean he apologizes ? '' asked Phil stiffly", "context_len": 11}}}, "smells": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 42, "context": "'What a queer life ! Just think of getting up every morning , and not knowing where you were going to be by night , and sleeping under that dirty tent ! I wonder what they have in that caldron ? It smells nice , at any rate , though rather too oniony", "context_len": 53}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "Laughed at in his sufferings by the savages , almost suffocated by the bad smells about the hut , taken out at times to be the sport of his captors , unable to eat , full of aches and pains , he was yet able to look up and say , `` Let the Lord do as He sees fit , '' and to read his Bible and feel refreshed", "context_len": 70}}}, "dope": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Worse ! I wonder what sort of dope they put in those cigarettes ? I like a good pipe or a cigar , but I 'm blessed if I can go those coffin nails ! Ah , that air smells good , '' and he breathed in deep of the September air at the window", "context_len": 56}}}, "weeded": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "Porter told them the beds must be kept nicely weeded , and watered when the weather was dry", "context_len": 18}}}, "clinching": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Hamilton , he remarked , clinching a point that he had been hammering at for a long time , that you ought to have bars across that window", "context_len": 28}}}, "dent": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "He drew back his foot , and his shoe made quite a dent in the side of the ball", "context_len": 19}}}, "spheroid": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The spheroid went to the side , sailing low , and out of bounds", "context_len": 14}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "Up and up the field the spheroid , yellow no longer , but dirty and grass-stained , was carried", "context_len": 19}}}, "tines": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "Tines seemed very determined , and the ensuing tumult would bring into the corridor a throng of students , so that Sid would never hear the last of the joke that had turned on him", "context_len": 35}}}, "sternest": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "And as he proceeded the unfavorable impression that had prevailed concerning him was gradually dispelled ; for surely he did not speak like a desperate character or a thief ; nor was Lawyer Frances , by his sternest and most accusing cross-questioning , able to confuse the lad or shake him in his statements", "context_len": 54}}}, "continuance": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 107, "context": "The Sultan accordingly went up to the pavilion [ 535 ] and entering the kiosk , [ 536 ] viewed it right and left and saw no manner defect in its lattices , but found them all perfect ; whereat he was astounded and embracing Alaeddin , fell a-kissing him and saying , `` O my son , what is this extraordinary thing ? In one night thou dost a work wherefrom the jewellers would fail in months ! By Allah , methinketh thou hast not thy fellow [ 537 ] in the world ! '' Quoth Alaeddin , `` God prolong thy life and perpetuate thy continuance ! Thy slave is not worthy of this praise", "context_len": 117}}}, "ennobling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "While surely , to a thoughtful mind , the constant sight of so much loveliness around tends to have a more ennobling effect than an environment of bricks and mortar and smoky chimneys , whatever the Londoners may say", "context_len": 39}}}, "churchill": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 0, "context": "Churchill about the noise the lads were making", "context_len": 8}}}, "waive": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` No , I waive mine , '' interrupted the Latin teacher , and he smoothed out some of the pages in the Greek book", "context_len": 25}}}, "abolish": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` But if they abolish football ! '' exclaimed Phil", "context_len": 10}}}, "signers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Or else we 'll hang separately , as one of the gifted signers of the Fourth of July proclamation put it , '' added Ed Kerr", "context_len": 27}}}, "statistics": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "In support of his contention he quoted statistics to show how dangerous the game was , how many had been killed at it , and how often innocent spectators , like himself , were sometimes hurt , though , he added , he would never willingly be a witness of such a brutal sport", "context_len": 54}}}, "vindicated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "He had made himself very important , and had , as he supposed , vindicated his dignity", "context_len": 17}}}, "venom": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "King , turning on him with considerable venom , as if he were quite to blame for the whole thing , `` but he has made up his mind to go into business", "context_len": 33}}}, "cluttered": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` You 've got it so cluttered up that I ca n't stand it ! Is n't it enough to have pictures stuck all over ? ''", "context_len": 27}}}, "objector": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` Here , you let that banner alone ! '' cried Tom , and he gave such a jerk to the chair on which Sid was standing that the objector to things artistic toppled to the floor with a resounding crash", "context_len": 41}}}, "daffy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` Always talking about girls ! I declare , since those pictures and things are up in the room , you two have gone daffy ! I 'll have 'em all down , first thing you know", "context_len": 37}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "She 's as daffy about it as I am", "context_len": 9}}}, "touchdown": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 20, "context": "Pounding their way into Oakdale \u2019 s territory , they marched on by short but sufficient gains toward yet another touchdown , the line of the visitors being pierced at almost every point save that defended by Ben Stone , which had been found practically invulnerable", "context_len": 46}}}, "padding": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "Still faster he went , Polulu padding beside him and keeping away by his presence not only jungle enemies but Bomba \u2019 s friends , the monkeys and the parrots , who gave him a wide berth when they saw the grim guardian that kept pace with him", "context_len": 48}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "There came the padding of feet and a pushing aside of the bushes , and Bomba could see the outlines of a great body of some member of the four-footed tribe advancing toward him", "context_len": 34}}}, "dislocation": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "The doctor , after a brief examination , pronounced it a bad dislocation , but then and there , with the help of the captain and coach , he reduced it , though the pain , as the bone snapped into place , made Phil sick and faint", "context_len": 48}}}, "ligaments": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` The doc was here a while ago , and left some new liniment that he said would soften up the strained muscles and ligaments", "context_len": 25}}}, "drip": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` I could smuggle some in the room , only the measly things drip so , and Proc", "context_len": 18}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "attends": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "What a fatality often attends small things ! Jethro had no suspicion that the company in advance was the one from which the two visitors had come the night before", "context_len": 30}}}, "dissipating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` I thought you athletic chaps did n't do any dissipating", "context_len": 11}}}, "liniment": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 36, "context": "You have every indication of having used the peculiar drug I speak of , yet you say you did not , and I do n't see how you could have , unless it got in the liniment by mistake", "context_len": 39}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 6, "context": "The breaking of the bottle of liniment destroyed one link in the chain against him , and it would be difficult to trace anything to Gerhart now", "context_len": 27}}}, "evolved": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 2, "context": "They had evolved the title from his name ; from the fact that , as one of the first students put it , the original Moses went up on a hill to establish the first church -- hence Church -- Hill ; and thus `` Moses", "context_len": 46}}}, "cripples": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "It is curious , but true , nevertheless , that most football elevens seem to rejoice in the number of cripples they can boast of", "context_len": 25}}}, "elevens": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "It is curious , but true , nevertheless , that most football elevens seem to rejoice in the number of cripples they can boast of", "context_len": 25}}}, "shebang": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 64, "context": "No fear of that , but he such a big , hulking chap and eats so much that he oughter be made to do something ; I \u2019 m going to put him on tother side of this wagon and make him b \u2019 leve it \u2019 s the most important post of all , and that if he drops asleep , the whole shebang will be wiped out by the redskins", "context_len": 72}}}, "countersign": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "Say , how do you get into the female department of this shebang , anyhow ? What 's the proper method of procedure ? Do I have to have the password and a countersign ? ''", "context_len": 36}}}, "ogress": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` The preceptress ; and a regular ogress into the bargain", "context_len": 11}}}, "categorical": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "If you pass muster with her first inspection , you 'll have to answer a lot of categorical questions covering your whole life history", "context_len": 24}}}, "nonplused": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Tom was nonplused", "context_len": 3}}}, "frigidly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Yes , I know , '' frigidly", "context_len": 8}}}, "parsons": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 0, "context": "Parsons , do n't forget to put all the speed you can into your runs , when getting down on kicks", "context_len": 21}}}, "thinker": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Madge was a bright girl , and a quick thinker", "context_len": 10}}}, "philly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Philly , as we call her , will be in her office by this time", "context_len": 15}}}, "sage": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` He is going wrong , '' said the fishing sage , nodding his head ; and this formed a fresh subject for discussion , especially as one of the knot of idlers recollected that a second cousin of Zekle Wynn 's was an idiot", "context_len": 45}}}, "grudgingly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 51, "context": "The whole erection was so steady and well made that , though Father , as chief inspector , stamped vigorously about , he could not make it shake , and was able to pass it as perfectly safe , and give his congratulations to the young architect , while even Joe grudgingly admitted that 'Master Forster had n't made half a bad job of it neither", "context_len": 66}}}, "lacks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` It lacks just eight minutes of two , '' he added", "context_len": 12}}}, "zane": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Zane", "context_len": 1}}}, "augured": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "Their spirits were high that night , and while Edmund drank to their success on the morrow , each man responded with a fervour which augured confidence in that morrow 's issue -- all save the wicked Edric , whose heart seemed far from his words", "context_len": 46}}}, "pajamas": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 14, "context": "He turned the light down low , and , as he put on his pajamas , he knew , by the regular breathing of Phil , that the injured lad had fallen into a slumber", "context_len": 35}}}, "rheumatic": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "The terrible rheumatic pains were better , it is true , and she could now be moved without causing her so much agony ; but she had a racking cough and much fever , and showed , in many other ways , how very sick she was", "context_len": 47}}}, "liquids": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Ah , Keene , '' remarked the science instructor , as he mixed some unpleasant-smelling liquids in a test tube", "context_len": 21}}}, "miscellaneous": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 39, "context": "The walls were adorned with school drawings and prize maps , fastened up with tacks ; a bookcase of much-read volumes filled the space between the fireplace and the window ; and in the corner might be found a miscellaneous collection of cricket-bats , fishing-rods , tennis-racquets , bows and arrows , croquet-mallets , sticks , balls and ninepins , and other articles very dear to the children 's hearts , but which Nancy generally classed impartially under the head of 'lumber", "context_len": 82}}}, "arnica": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` Say , if you 're going to get the arnica , I wish you 'd get a move on , '' implored Sid , who was stretched out on the sofa", "context_len": 32}}}, "declaiming": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "Holding his injured thumb tightly in his other hand , Sid finished climbing the stairs , declaiming , meanwhile , against his bad luck", "context_len": 24}}}, "wow": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` Right-O ! bow wow , '' she barked back at me , then she ran on after her mate who was joyfully welcoming his drove of golden dollars rolling down the road in such fine style", "context_len": 37}}}, "roommates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` Say , you fellows make noise enough , '' grumbled Phil at the entrance of his roommates", "context_len": 18}}}, "cad": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` I always thought you were a tattling cad ! '' burst out Langridge", "context_len": 14}}}, "fake": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 38, "context": "But , in spite of the shock engendered by the receipt of the telegram , Phil would not give up until the men fairly `` snapped '' into place , after he had given the signal for the fake tackle run and pass to the half-back", "context_len": 46}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Following some light practice , during which the fake tackle run and pass to half-back was worked to perfection , Sid , Tom and Phil went for a stroll along Sunny River", "context_len": 32}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` We are n't perfect in that fake tackle run yet , and I want to use it against Dodville", "context_len": 20}}}, "oriental": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Spoil it ? Spoil this tattered and torn specimen of a fake oriental ? '' queried Sid with a laugh", "context_len": 21}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` I think that will put a crimp in his playing ! '' Gerhart remarked , as he paused to light an oriental cigarette , or , rather , something that passed for one", "context_len": 34}}}, "dopes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Why did n't you let me do the doctoring ? You two dopes are n't able to look after yourselves ! Look at the rug now ! ''", "context_len": 28}}}, "massaging": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "He was soon vigorously massaging Sid 's injured hand , using a piece of flannel as directed by Phil , and was given a vote of thanks for the professional manner in which he did it", "context_len": 36}}}, "tendons": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "The rack is an instrument of wheels and pulleys , into which the victim is placed , and his limbs and tendons are stretched by it in a manner which produces most excruciating pain", "context_len": 34}}}, "twirled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Let me see the bottle , '' said the physician , as he twirled his glasses by their cord and looked puzzled", "context_len": 23}}}, "bulletin": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "The next day a notice was posted on the bulletin board in the gymnasium , stating that the 'varsity eleven would line up against the scrub that afternoon in secret practice", "context_len": 31}}}, "commenting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Which the coach did very thoroughly , getting at the weak spots in each man 's playing , and commenting wisely on it , at the same time showing him how he ought to play his position", "context_len": 37}}}, "fumbles": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Randall lost the pigskin on fumbles , and when Wescott got it again they kicked a field goal", "context_len": 18}}}, "harped": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "It was against the manner in which the nephew ceaselessly harped on what his relative said , though Ford was never allowed to tell what it was", "context_len": 27}}}, "grandstand": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` Well , now we 'll have a chance at the championship , when we meet Boxer Hall next , '' said Phil , who had watched the contest from the grandstand , though he was as nervous as a colt all the while", "context_len": 44}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The wealthy colonel was given to `` grandstand plays , '' and one of them had been the placing of a bronze pedestal and statue at the side of the driveway", "context_len": 31}}}, "ailed": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 2, "context": "What `` ailed the fellows , '' was soon seen , for even as he spoke , Flossy , who had been left shut up in the house lest he should frighten the bunnies , came tearing round a great rock , and rushed to the foot of the tree , where he commenced a great barking", "context_len": 57}}}, "teammates": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 26, "context": "If the captain thought so , however , he succeeded marvelously well in hiding his feelings , trying his best all the time to brace his teammates up , encouraging the equally staunch , chiding a few who showed symptoms of wavering , and entreating one or two who apparently had lost heart", "context_len": 53}}}, "grandstands": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "How the cheers broke forth then ! What a riot of color from the grandstands ! How the flags , ribbons and banners waved ! How the gay youths and grave men yelled themselves hoarse ! How the girls ' shrill voices sounded over the field !", "context_len": 47}}}, "bleachers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "What a mass of color the grandstands and bleachers presented ! Mingled with the youths and men were girls and women in bright dresses , waving brighter-hued flags", "context_len": 28}}}, "maroon": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "Then came cheer upon cheer from the grandstand , where waved the yellow and maroon of Randall , and Bean Perkins led in the song : `` We Have Come and We Have Conquered ! ''", "context_len": 36}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "You \u2019 re not going to maroon us too on that beach ? Isn \u2019 t losing one perfectly good plane enough to suit you ?", "context_len": 26}}}, "footballs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "There were grave men who wore tiny footballs on their coat lapels , a knot of ribbon denoting with which college they sided", "context_len": 23}}}, "lapels": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "There were grave men who wore tiny footballs on their coat lapels , a knot of ribbon denoting with which college they sided", "context_len": 23}}}, "madge": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "Madge was down in front , getting ready to lead them in a song", "context_len": 14}}}, "enthusing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "From the stand where the Randall supporters were gathered came that enthusing song -- the song they always sang at a big game -- '' Aut vincere aut mori '' -- '' Either we conquer or we die ! ''", "context_len": 40}}}, "bucking": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 12, "context": "So far there had not been a halt in the progress of bucking the line , but when , on the next play , Ed Kerr was called on to go through between left-end and tackle , he felt as if he had hit a number of bags of sand", "context_len": 50}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "Utterly wild and unbroken , the little rough-coated things showed their disapproval of this their first taste of civilization by every means in their power , rearing , bucking , and kicking to the best of their ability", "context_len": 38}}}, "vocal": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "As the miners gathered round a blazing log-fire built out of doors , which the cool air of evening made welcome , it was proposed that those who had any vocal gifts should exert them for the benefit of the company", "context_len": 41}}}, "quartering": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "They advanced the ball to the twenty-five yard line , and , though tempted to give the signal for a goal from the field , Phil refrained , as there was a quartering wind blowing", "context_len": 35}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Our young readers must bear in mind that these canoes could only go before the wind , or a little quartering , and therefore could not , like a boat , be luffed sharp into the wind , and beat out clear of danger ; hence the boys preferred to take the sail in , and trust to their oars , with which they could , if they saw a breaker , pull away from it", "context_len": 76}}}, "tongs": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Jack Walker tol \u2019 me he pitched into Hunk Rollins hammer an \u2019 tongs \u2019 cause Hunk was plaguing Jimmy Jones , and he said he was a-going to tell the professor the whole business", "context_len": 35}}}, "netted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "Smash through the line though her players did , for there seemed no stopping them , successful as the forward pass was , and with the gain netted by an on-side kick , Randall could do no better than to carry the ball to the Fairview ten-yard line", "context_len": 48}}}, "demoralize": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "The plays were to be executed in quick succession , and the coach depended on that to demoralize the cadet eleven", "context_len": 21}}}, "daringly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Do you want it ? '' she asked daringly", "context_len": 10}}}, "lapel": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "She blushed with pleasure at this grown-up caress , and taking a rose from the bowl she fastened it in the lapel of the boy 's coat", "context_len": 27}}}, "accented": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` Oh , we 'll make time to go with you ! '' exclaimed Madge , and she accented the last word", "context_len": 22}}}, "substituted": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "We hid away the pudding you made , and , at the last minute , substituted one of our own that contained all the odds and ends we could pick up in camp , held together with a lot of dough", "context_len": 41}}}, "florist": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "On the way home from the game Tom had stopped in a florist 's in Fairview and given an order , while Phil remained outside", "context_len": 25}}}, "chaperons": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 61, "context": "First thing I know you fellows will have this a regular boudoir ; and then where will I be ? I expect any day now you 'll be wanting to get rid of this old couch and chair , and get some mission furniture , so that you can have a five o'clock tea here , and invite some girls and chaperons", "context_len": 62}}}, "tomfool": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "You make me sick ! I tell you what it is , if you put any more of those tomfool decorations , like flags and photographs , in this room , I 'm going to quit ! '' and Sid spoke earnestly", "context_len": 42}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Oh , she \u2019 ll pick up a lot of tomfool notions , most likely , Uncle Joe admitted , and a whole heap of others that \u2019 ll come in mighty handy one of these days", "context_len": 37}}}, "misanthropic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Aw , forget it , you old misanthropic specimen of a misogynist ! '' exclaimed Phil with a laugh", "context_len": 20}}}, "noncommittal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Maybe I have , '' was the noncommittal answer", "context_len": 10}}}, "sop": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` It 's a wonder you would n't get a blotter and sop up some of that writing fluid , '' suggested Phil to Tom at last", "context_len": 27}}}, "gym": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 15, "context": "Come , entreated Jerry ; and they fled on past the silent academy , the gym and the athletic field on into the bleak night", "context_len": 25}}}, "proscribed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "They left Sid undressing and went out , it not being a proscribed hour", "context_len": 14}}}, "punching": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "They saw Gerhart holding Wallops , who was a lad small for his age , while Langridge was punching him in the face , accompanying each blow with the remark :", "context_len": 31}}}, "wallops": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Wallops , the messenger , was shrinking away , anxious to escape unobserved , though he cast a look of gratitude at Phil", "context_len": 23}}}, "chastisement": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 20, "context": "So I spoke on the Christian duties of love and forgiveness , and exhorted all present to take joyfully the chastisement of the Lord , even like holy Job ; and that it would all tend to their eternal good , through Him who , when He was reviled , reviled not again", "context_len": 53}}}, "quartet": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` We 'll make a quartet of it , if you like", "context_len": 12}}}, "tattling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "Coward or no , I 'm not going to prove my courage for the sake of gratifying busy tattling people", "context_len": 20}}}, "pugilist": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Yes -- I suppose I 've got to -- I ca n't fight a professional pugilist , '' he added with an uneasy laugh", "context_len": 25}}}, "haltingly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "What was it that drew him haltingly , almost fearfully , across the tiny room to stop beneath a picture on the wall and , with his hungry eyes upraised , stare at it intently ?", "context_len": 36}}}, "bartender": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "It had come to the point where that night where the bartender had refused to sell any more liquor , warning having been given that sales to minors were becoming too frequent", "context_len": 32}}}, "minors": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "It had come to the point where that night where the bartender had refused to sell any more liquor , warning having been given that sales to minors were becoming too frequent", "context_len": 32}}}, "clinton": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Clinton", "context_len": 1}}}, "cads": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` What cads those fellows are , '' remarked Phil a little later , when he and Tom , having had a refreshing shower bath , were preparing for bed in their room", "context_len": 33}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Not among the best end of the boys , but some of the fellows are awful cads", "context_len": 17}}}, "chinning": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "What are you chinning about ? ''", "context_len": 7}}}, "subs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Some of the subs will play , I fancy", "context_len": 9}}}, "despising": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` I hope Holly does n't make the mistake of despising an enemy , '' went on Sid", "context_len": 18}}}, "unstrung": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "I 'm all unstrung -- nervous -- that 's what 's the matter", "context_len": 13}}}, "sidelines": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "I expect any moment to be summoned to the sidelines to get a telegram saying -- saying -- -- ''", "context_len": 20}}}, "dislocate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "I 'm going to pilot our fellows to a big score or dislocate my other shoulder", "context_len": 16}}}, "enervated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "There was just enough snap in the air to prevent one from feeling enervated , yet there was hardly a hint of winter", "context_len": 23}}}, "psychology": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 52, "context": "Are you going to ring in beautiful scenery , calm , peaceful atmosphere , a sense of loneliness , and then switch off on to girls ? Is that what you 're driving at ? Because if it is I want to know , and I 'm going back and read some psychology", "context_len": 53}}}, "limpid": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` Just look there , where the river curves , how the trees lean over to be kissed by the limpid water", "context_len": 22}}}, "shrimp": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Why , you little shrimp , I 've a good notion to chuck you into the river ! ''", "context_len": 20}}}, "dutch": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Dutch was silent for a few minutes , and then he proposed a plan which was received with exclamations of delight", "context_len": 21}}}, "clashes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` We have not forgotten that we were once freshmen , and that we had many clashes with the second-years", "context_len": 20}}}, "boosting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Garvey Gerhart , by virtue of the `` boosting '' which Langridge had given him , had secured command of the first-year forces", "context_len": 23}}}, "timekeepers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "The preliminaries for the rush were soon arranged , timekeepers and umpires selected , and , with the bright moon shining down on the scene , the battle began", "context_len": 29}}}, "umpires": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The preliminaries for the rush were soon arranged , timekeepers and umpires selected , and , with the bright moon shining down on the scene , the battle began", "context_len": 29}}}, "conglomeration": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "A knot of lads would go down , with a struggling pile on top of them , and the conglomeration would writhe about until it disentangled", "context_len": 26}}}, "writhe": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "A knot of lads would go down , with a struggling pile on top of them , and the conglomeration would writhe about until it disentangled", "context_len": 26}}}, "pivot": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 6, "context": "It was a kind of central pivot for the year to turn on , and 'five years come fair-day , ' or 'the last fair-day but one agone , ' was the general method of calculating time amongst the villagers", "context_len": 40}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "He might pivot his body to the blaze , but he would always be chilled", "context_len": 15}}}, "snooze": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` I wish I could snooze , '' spoke Phil", "context_len": 10}}}, "conditioned": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "Got turned back in class to-day , and Pitchfork , who 's a regular fiend at it , as well at Latin , warned me that I 'd be conditioned if I did n't look out", "context_len": 36}}}, "corking": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` I want three corking good plays to be worked in sequence when we get to within reaching distance of the twenty-five-yard line , '' said the coach", "context_len": 28}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` It was a corking good shot , '' exclaimed Fenn , admiringly", "context_len": 13}}}, "memorize": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` So memorize these plays carefully , and we 'll try to work them", "context_len": 14}}}, "adjoined": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "There was a big attendance at the grounds , which adjoined the military academy , about twenty miles from Randall College", "context_len": 21}}}, "barbarism": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "They are a relic of barbarism ! ''", "context_len": 8}}}, "transom": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Through the glass transom over the door of the room he saw a red glare", "context_len": 15}}}, "yanking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` Here , quit ! '' cried Sid , making an effort to pull back the coverings on which Tom was yanking", "context_len": 22}}}, "yanked": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` Come on , fellows , let 's get some of our stuff out ! I want my football pictures , '' and with that Sid rushed to the wall and yanked down the only bit of ornamentation he cared for -- a lithograph of a Rugby scrimmage", "context_len": 48}}}, "ornamentation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "`` Come on , fellows , let 's get some of our stuff out ! I want my football pictures , '' and with that Sid rushed to the wall and yanked down the only bit of ornamentation he cared for -- a lithograph of a Rugby scrimmage", "context_len": 48}}}, "grabbing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Come on ! '' he yelled , grabbing up a pile of his clothes from a chair", "context_len": 18}}}, "balancing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 61, "context": "Such very Spartan fare was the result of his lecture that he wisely fixed her a weekly allowance , and left her to manage as best she could upon it , and this plan answered far better , for she had a natural aptitude for arithmetic , and soon learned to make the various items fall into their proper places , balancing her little account-book in quite a professional manner", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Deprived thus of his balancing poles , a quick flirt of the pony to one side would have flung him into the water , but \u2019 Ceph , if he was aware of it ( and it would seem he ought to have been ) , did not seize the chance", "context_len": 51}}}, "snell": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Snell", "context_len": 1}}}, "phil": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 0, "context": "Phil looked at his trousers , and then he began slowly to take them off", "context_len": 15}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "PHIL THE FIDDLER", "context_len": 3}}}, "fleshy": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` I put soap powder in this to have handy when I washed the dishes , '' explained the fleshy youth , `` and you flavored the cakes with it , Frank", "context_len": 32}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` A snake , this time of year ? Not much , but it 's a whopping big mud turtle , '' cried the fleshy lad", "context_len": 26}}}, "scrabble": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "Gathered on the walk about Booker Memorial Chapel , whence for many terms freshmen had , by traditional college custom , been barred , the first-year lads made all sorts of jokes concerning the scrabble that had ensued among the sophomores when the cry of fire was raised", "context_len": 48}}}, "blink": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 51, "context": "It was to be held in a hall in Haddonfield , and many were the precautions taken by the committee to prevent any of the hated sophomores from attending , or getting to the place beforehand , lest they might , by some untoward act , `` put it on the blink , '' as Holly Cross used to say", "context_len": 60}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Bessie would sit down beside him and pat his rough head , and talk to him in a sweet , coaxing voice , and he would blink his eyes at her and flap his heavy tail upon the ground in a way that he would do for no one else", "context_len": 50}}}, "tastefully": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The hall was tastefully arranged with flowers and a bank of palms , behind which the orchestra was to be hidden", "context_len": 21}}}, "orchestra": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "The hall was tastefully arranged with flowers and a bank of palms , behind which the orchestra was to be hidden", "context_len": 21}}}, "ballroom": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "A little before the first arrivals at the freshman dance had reached the hall , a figure might have been seen moving quickly about the ballroom in the dim illumination from the half-turned-down lights", "context_len": 34}}}, "stickier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` This is stickier than any of those things , '' spoke Gerhart", "context_len": 13}}}, "adhesive": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The ballroom was like one big sheet of adhesive flypaper , and the dancers , walking about , felt their shoes pull up with queer little noises every time they took a step", "context_len": 33}}}, "flypaper": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "The ballroom was like one big sheet of adhesive flypaper , and the dancers , walking about , felt their shoes pull up with queer little noises every time they took a step", "context_len": 33}}}, "alcohol": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` I mixed up a sticky preparation of glue , varnish , gum and so on , made it into a powder , and put it in alcohol", "context_len": 28}}}, "evaporated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "The alcohol kept it from sticking until after the march , and then , when the alcohol evaporated , it left the gum ready to do its work", "context_len": 28}}}, "lassies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` Still , it was a bit rough on the lassies", "context_len": 11}}}, "shoelace": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` She gave it to me , '' explained Tom , and his shoelace seemed suddenly to have come unfastened , so it was necessary to stoop over to tie it", "context_len": 31}}}, "mistiness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "He forced back a mistiness that was gathering like a film over his eyes", "context_len": 14}}}, "film": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "One looking closer into his eyes might notice a dull film overspreading them , for Willie Richards was nearly blind , would be quite blind in a few weeks , the doctors said", "context_len": 33}}}, "engendered": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "But , in spite of the shock engendered by the receipt of the telegram , Phil would not give up until the men fairly `` snapped '' into place , after he had given the signal for the fake tackle run and pass to the half-back", "context_len": 46}}}, "relatively": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Well , thank you for that ; but I mean relatively", "context_len": 12}}}, "cog": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "But I 'm a cog in the wheels of the machine , and you know how it is when you put a new wheel in a bit of apparatus", "context_len": 29}}}, "busting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` What are you trying to do -- smash it all to pieces ? Ca n't you get out of a chair without busting it ? ''", "context_len": 27}}}, "exclamatory": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` I -- I did n't mean to , '' spoke Sid so gently , and in such a contrast to Tom 's fiery words , that Phil could not restrain an exclamatory chuckle", "context_len": 34}}}, "simulated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` Better put an iron brace on , if Sid is going to do double back somersaults in it , '' went on Tom with simulated indignity", "context_len": 27}}}, "disrobe": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` No study to-night , '' remarked Phil , as he began to disrobe", "context_len": 14}}}, "perfidy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` And when , '' [ quoth she ] ; `` I saw this [ seeming ] lack of wit in him , I fell to laughing at him , unknowing his perfidy and his intent ; so I took an old lamp that was in my husband 's pavilion and sent it by the eunuch , who exchanged it with him for a new lamp ; and next day , O my father , at daybreak , we found ourselves in Africa , with the palace and all that was therein ; and I knew not the properties of the lamp which I had exchanged , till my husband Alaeddin came to us and contrived against the Maugrabin a device whereby he delivered us from him", "context_len": 127}}}, "naturalist": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 29, "context": "Why had Nascanora made Hondura captive with Casson and the squaw ? Was it because Hondura and his tribe had been on friendly terms with Bomba and the old naturalist ? Or did Nascanora think that the Araos tribe was becoming too powerful , and had he hoped by depriving them of their chief to render his people helpless and throw confusion and panic into their hearts ?", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The latter was a naturalist , who had withdrawn from civilization and settled in a little cabin in the remotest part of the Amazonian region", "context_len": 25}}}, "burrs": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Some one has taken the stickers off a lot of chestnut burrs and scattered them in your bed", "context_len": 19}}}, "stickers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` I 'll show you , '' said Phil , and he carefully took off the sheets , folding them up so that the chestnut stickers would not be scattered", "context_len": 30}}}, "pasteboard": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` Soap powder , you mean ! '' exclaimed Fenn , as he made a dash for the box that served as a cupboard , and took out a pasteboard package that had contained cocoanut", "context_len": 35}}}, "beehive": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` It 's like being in a beehive", "context_len": 8}}}, "ransacking": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "You may think you have the right to demand your own if you choose to break a compact made for your own good , but you have no right to be guilty of the liberty and meanness of ransacking another man 's belongings in search of it", "context_len": 47}}}, "inventive": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "But it was not so easy as they had supposed to think up a joke to play on the inventive freshman , that would be commensurate with the trick he had perpetrated on them", "context_len": 34}}}, "grins": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "Bart made a first-class auctioneer -- everybody said so after the sale was over , and the pleased grins and the good-natured attention of his audience assured the young novice of this as he concluded the introductory speech", "context_len": 38}}}, "fistic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Tom was sorely tempted to come to fistic conclusions with the freshman , but Sid and Phil dissuaded him , promising to unite with him on some scheme of vengeance", "context_len": 30}}}, "unappeased": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` There 's a chance to take a fall out of him , '' suggested Tom , whose appetite for vengeance was still unappeased", "context_len": 24}}}, "boned": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The three `` boned '' away until midnight , and after an inspection of their beds , to make sure that no contraband articles were between the sheets , they turned out the light and were soon slumbering", "context_len": 38}}}, "slumbering": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "Behind him the heavens are growing angry with lurid light , before him the earth lies in stillness and silence ; the moonbeams slumbering on placid river , glittering on frozen pool , or silvering happy homesteads -- happy hitherto", "context_len": 40}}}, "anonymous": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` If the writer wants to resort to such tactics as leaving an anonymous letter on the doorstep , we can retaliate by playing the spy on him", "context_len": 28}}}, "originals": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "It was a comparatively simple matter , the way the signals had been devised , to so change them so that another team , even with a copy of the originals , would have found it impossible to know in advance what the plays were to be", "context_len": 47}}}, "lapsing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Nonsense ! Do n't talk that way , me lad ! '' objected Bricktop , lapsing into brogue , as he always did when very much in earnest", "context_len": 29}}}, "brogue": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "Kathleen O'Riley was a bright Irish girl , with the prettiest suspicion of a brogue imaginable , and that winning manner which seems specially to belong to the children of the Emerald Isle", "context_len": 33}}}, "aggregation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` Are you all ready ? '' asked the referee , and Captain Holly Cross cast a quick eye on his team , which , spread out on their field , was like an aggregation of eager foxhounds , waiting for the start", "context_len": 43}}}, "foxhounds": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "`` Are you all ready ? '' asked the referee , and Captain Holly Cross cast a quick eye on his team , which , spread out on their field , was like an aggregation of eager foxhounds , waiting for the start", "context_len": 43}}}, "punt": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "But he only gained three yards , and it was inevitable that Boxer would punt", "context_len": 15}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "It was too late to get away , however , and so he waited until Davis , paddling straight in , grounded the punt upon a bit of gravelly beach and sprang out", "context_len": 33}}}, "penalized": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 8, "context": "Right on top of this the locals were penalized for holding , but the rising courage of the visitors was dampened when the home team pulled off a handsome forward pass that yielded double the distance needed", "context_len": 37}}}, "trainer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "But the thoughtless action of the coach brought its punishment , for Boxer was penalized ten yards on account of their trainer coming on the field without permission", "context_len": 28}}}, "outgeneral": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Though Boxer was a bit weak on defense , they played a snappy game , and seemed to be able to outgeneral their opponents", "context_len": 24}}}, "booting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "No time was lost in booting the pigskin out of danger , and before another formation could be made the whistle blew , and the first half was over", "context_len": 29}}}, "scoring": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "But Wescott tore through for another touch-down , while the solitary one gained in the first half was the limit of the scoring the visitors could do", "context_len": 27}}}, "assail": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "He realized that , whereas the big cats usually hesitated to attack unless they themselves were attacked or were sorely pressed by hunger , here in this desolate region they would assail him boldly , fearlessly , knowing little of man and his strange powers", "context_len": 45}}}, "dispense": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 75, "context": "At last the weather became very cold , and being without a fire , his sufferings were great , until his ferocious gaoler , finding him quite stiffened , brought up a brazier of coals , which saved his prisoner 's life , while it filled the room with smoke , which could only escape by the crevices in walls and roof , for to open a window would have been as bad as to dispense with the fire , such was the state of the outer air", "context_len": 88}}}, "cousinly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "The slender tie of relationship between them was no bar to an affection that differed in degree from cousinly", "context_len": 19}}}, "biblical": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The long casements , with representations of biblical scenes , were a soft glow of delicate hues", "context_len": 17}}}, "crimp": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` I think that will put a crimp in his playing ! '' Gerhart remarked , as he paused to light an oriental cigarette , or , rather , something that passed for one", "context_len": 34}}}, "hurdling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Now , if they allow hurdling in football , you 'd be right in it for jumping over the line to make a touch-down", "context_len": 25}}}, "substitutes": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "Eliot did not wholly hide his displeasure over this , for he had expected that the players , the substitutes and the coach would all sit at one long table", "context_len": 30}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "This would be quite in character with the style of our present manuscript , which constantly substitutes sz ( sad ) for s ( sin ) , e", "context_len": 28}}}, "trash": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 47, "context": "When massa go a-drivin ' in de carry-all and pair , Little piccaninnies run behind to shout Hurrah ! and stare ; Den de overseer he come up , and use his big , long lash , And say de road was neber meant to harbour nigger trash ! Shoo , shoo ! piccaninnies , etc", "context_len": 56}}}, "lassitude": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Phil was better the next day , but when he went out to practice with the lads , there was a lassitude in his movements , and a lack of snap in his manner of running the team , that made several open their eyes", "context_len": 45}}}, "punters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The rivals of Randall had a couple of backs who were excellent punters , and the visitors were kept busy chasing the ball", "context_len": 23}}}, "punting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "When they had the ball they managed to gain considerable ground , and as much punting as possible was done", "context_len": 20}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "A ball was tossed out , and immediately they began passing , punting and falling upon it", "context_len": 17}}}, "bilious": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "They imagined all sorts of things , and were not a little relieved when the doctor told them that , at worst , Phil only had a bad attack of bilious fever", "context_len": 32}}}, "camera": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 58, "context": "`` I say , you fellows , '' remarked Sid one sunny November afternoon , when all three chums were in the room after lectures , `` do n't you want to take a walk with me ? I 've got to do some observation work in my biology course , and I 'm going to take my camera along and make some pictures", "context_len": 64}}}, "guarantees": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "There may be valuable papers , there may be jewelry -- in fact , some of the parcels have a given value up to two hundred dollars -- but the express company guarantees nothing and you bid at your own risk", "context_len": 41}}}, "films": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "I 'll be ready in a jiffy , '' and he proceeded to load his camera with films", "context_len": 18}}}, "evolution": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "On the way he found several specimens which he needed in his evolution work , and in thinking about them , and his success in photographing the fox , he forgot about the plight he was in", "context_len": 37}}}, "photographic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "The three chums were soon strolling along the banks of the river , Sid on the lookout for late-staying birds or some animal or reptile which he might add to his photographic collection", "context_len": 33}}}, "focusing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` A vulpes pennsylvanicus argentatus ! '' answered Sid as he turned the focusing screw", "context_len": 15}}}, "chumps": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` It 's a fox , you chumps ! '' came from Sid", "context_len": 13}}}, "shutter": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 52, "context": "Peggy often laughed afterwards when she remembered how she escorted the pair up the rickety steps into the granary , and prevented them from falling through the trap-door into the stable below , and guided them between the sacks of grain in the dark loft , where , flinging open the wooden shutter , she let in a stream of sunlight , and disclosed to view the ancient chest", "context_len": 69}}}, "scary": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` What do you want to photograph such scary things as foxes for , anyhow ? ''", "context_len": 17}}}, "dryer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` I 'm going to find a dryer path , '' said Phil , and Tom agreed with him", "context_len": 19}}}, "biological": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Besides , I may find in this swamp some material I can use in my biological experiments", "context_len": 17}}}, "hummock": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "A moment later he slipped from a grass hummock and went into the muddy bog , up to his waist", "context_len": 20}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "slough": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 36, "context": "They raised the neighborhood , and followed the horse \u2019 s tracks to William Griffin \u2019 s door , and then it got dark , and they lost them ; however , they hunted in the slough holes , and all about , a good part of the night , for it was cold , and they knew if he laid out he \u2019 d perish", "context_len": 66}}}, "laborer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "But what a plight he was in ! Plastered with swamp-ooze to his waist , he looked like some sewer laborer", "context_len": 21}}}, "globules": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 41, "context": "The mud had dried somewhat on his trousers and shoes , and , incidentally , on his face and hands , for he had , unconsciously , run his hands over his countenance once or twice , so that the mud globules had increased in surface area", "context_len": 47}}}, "photographing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "On the way he found several specimens which he needed in his evolution work , and in thinking about them , and his success in photographing the fox , he forgot about the plight he was in", "context_len": 37}}}, "henderson": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Henderson ! '' And Sid went down on his knees to gather up the helix mollusc\u00e6 , while Professor Tines hurried from the room", "context_len": 24}}}, "chide": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Why , Siddie , '' he went on , as a mother might chide a little boy , `` wherever have you been ? You 're all mud ! Oh , such a state as your trousers are in ! Whatever will papa say , Siddie ? ''", "context_len": 49}}}, "fraternities": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` Are you going to the Kappa Delta dance ? '' asked Phil of Tom one night , referring to an annual affair of one of the Greek letter fraternities", "context_len": 30}}}, "paraphrased": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "You 're first in war , first in football , and first in -- the affairs with the ladies , '' he paraphrased", "context_len": 23}}}, "bombazine": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "Phil took Madge , and very charming she looked in a new gown of -- oh , well , what difference does it make what her dress was like , anyhow ? Besides , I do n't know whether it was bombazine or chiffon , and the more I try to describe it the worse I will get tangled , so if you 'll take my word for it , as well as Phil 's , who ought to know , she looked very pretty indeed", "context_len": 86}}}, "foresworn": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Sid was asleep , and the quarter-back moved about softly , so as not to disturb him , for Sid had foresworn such dissipations as fraternity dances", "context_len": 27}}}, "trespassing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 43, "context": "But the gamekeeper was a surly fellow , and if he saw the children lingering near he would tell them that his master `` could n't abear boys nor girls either , '' and always was most severe if any people were caught trespassing on his land", "context_len": 47}}}, "gab": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Say , I wish you fellows would cut out that gab ! '' suddenly exclaimed Sid , turning over in bed", "context_len": 22}}}, "shying": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Get out , you grumpy old misogynist ! '' exclaimed Phil , shying a sofa cushion at his chum", "context_len": 20}}}, "pollywogs": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` He had seen some pollywogs , or young frogs , in the brook the day before , and his mind had been quite full of them ever since ; and he was very anxious to catch one , and have it for his own", "context_len": 45}}}, "douse": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Get to bed and douse the glim", "context_len": 8}}}, "glim": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Get to bed and douse the glim", "context_len": 8}}}, "assertions": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "In spite of Tom 's assertions to the contrary , Phil could not help feeling that a coldness had sprung up between himself and his chum", "context_len": 26}}}, "snappier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "It ought to be snappier", "context_len": 5}}}, "roster": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Sid reached for a college roster , and turned to the freshman class list", "context_len": 14}}}, "anonymously": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "Other paragraphs in the report read thus : `` Received anonymously five large cheeses ; received a box of dessert knives and forks , a cruet , a silver soup ladle and a silver cup ; from Clifton , twelve tons house coals ; from Bedminster , a monster loaf , 200 lbs", "context_len": 53}}}, "awestruck": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` I wonder , '' said Tommy , in an awestruck , thoughtful voice , `` what Hal will do with a whole shilling ? Will he spend it all at once , do you think ? ''", "context_len": 38}}}, "tailpiece": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "[ Illustration : Chapter I tailpiece ]", "context_len": 7}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "[ Illustration : Chapter II tailpiece ]", "context_len": 7}}}, "crossness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Though Hal 's crossness at breakfast had made Drusie feel rather sad , it was impossible for her to unhappy for long on such a beautiful morning ; and when Helen suggested that they should take a few of the rabbits with them to the clover field she cheerfully agreed", "context_len": 50}}}, "aggravate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Helen , who did not care very much for Jumbo , declared that he did that on purpose to aggravate them , for they all finished their food long before he was half-way through his , and then they had nothing else to do but to sit and watch him", "context_len": 50}}}, "distrustfully": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` H 'm , '' said Helen , looking at him rather distrustfully ; `` Jumbo too can be dreadfully naughty when he likes , and he rather looks as if he meant it to-day", "context_len": 35}}}, "sniffs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Jumbo smelt it , and lifting up his black nose gave one or two sniffs , and then darting past them at a rate surprising in a rabbit of his age made straight for the gap in the hedge ; and , of course , after that there was no more time for conversation , for where Jumbo went the other rabbits followed", "context_len": 63}}}, "condescend": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "She reproached him for having appealed to Hannah , a servant in his father 's house , for aid ; and in her turn had to hear his reproaches for believing that he would condescend to such a thing , and received an emphatic and solemn denial that he had been guilty of this , or that he had ever let Hannah know of the straits he was in", "context_len": 69}}}, "jumbo": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "majestically": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 32, "context": "But from the meanest to the best swimmer there , every one paused to watch Mark Penelly standing statue-like up against the black rock , waiting till a great ninth wave came majestically rolling in , sweeping over the outer rocks -- the Shangles -- and then with a boom leaping at Carn Du , running up it , as it were , in a mighty column of water , some twenty feet even on a calm day", "context_len": 78}}}, "nibbling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` I say , what a jolly lot of rabbits you have got ! '' the boy said , looking down at the other five , who were busy nibbling away at the grass , without seeming to care in the least what happened to Jumbo ; `` but are n't you afraid of their running away ? ''", "context_len": 59}}}, "headforemost": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 58, "context": "`` They generally behave beautifully , '' Drusie said , who , because the other three were rather shy , was obliged to do all the talking herself ; `` but something must have startled Jumbo when we were at the top of the hill , for he set off at a tremendous scamper , and tumbled in headforemost before we knew what was happening to him", "context_len": 67}}}, "wicket": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 30, "context": "Hal flushed with pride and pleasure at this praise , but the others thought that he looked a shade disappointed as his friend placed himself again in front of the wicket", "context_len": 31}}}, "hutches": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "But Drusie and Jim were too excited at the prospect of a game to listen to her words of warning , and as soon as the rabbits had been hastily bundled into their hutches they raced back to the tree where Hal was waiting for them", "context_len": 46}}}, "wides": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "-- '' Drusie , you can stand behind the tree and be wicket-keeper , for , unless Jim has improved wonderfully since I went away , most of his balls will be fearful wides", "context_len": 34}}}, "fielders": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Jim 's bowling was not very difficult to deal with , and Hal knocked the balls about pretty much as he pleased , and gave the fielders , and especially Helen , plenty of running about", "context_len": 36}}}, "ungratefully": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` No , I do n't suppose you will , '' said Hal ; and then he added ungratefully , `` That is the worst of playing with a set of girls ; one never gets any practice", "context_len": 38}}}, "hits": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "If he hits you or throws you overboard you know you have made some mistake and you make haste to find out what it is", "context_len": 25}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "A bird was knocked over , then a squirrel went down , and , in rapid succession he repeated this until he had fifteen hits to his credit , out of a possible sixteen", "context_len": 34}}}, "fluke": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Pooh ! '' he said contemptuously ; `` that was a pure fluke", "context_len": 14}}}, "remonstrating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Oh , I say , '' Jim said in a remonstrating tone , `` is that the way you play at your school ? ''", "context_len": 26}}}, "bragging": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "But he was too annoyed to be fair , and as a punishment for what he chose to call Drusie 's bragging , he tucked his bat under his arm , and told them that he was not going to play with them any more", "context_len": 45}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "He had noticed a little earlier his big brother , Ira , a loutish , overgrown fellow who had gone around with his hands in his pockets sneering at the innocent fun the smaller boys were indulging in , and bragging about his own especial Fourth of July supply of fireworks which were to come from some mysterious source not clearly defined", "context_len": 62}}}, "innings": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 41, "context": "' I 've not any grudge against them as has money , ' observed old Ephraim oracularly , 'but when it 's used to turn them as has n't out of their own , it 's time them Socialists had a innings and stepped in", "context_len": 45}}}, "gooseberries": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 39, "context": "It was somewhat neglected , it is true , but perhaps it looked none the less picturesque for that , and certainly no one would be disposed to quarrel with the beautiful ripe strawberries and the sweet little yellow gooseberries with the hairy skins , or the big red plums that hung upon the old brick walls", "context_len": 57}}}, "brambly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "That the Wilderness was a brambly place could not be denied", "context_len": 11}}}, "blackberry": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 76, "context": "Here was a quantity of ground covered with brush , logs , and bushes : to have hauled all this away would have been an endless job , and after that the ground could neither be ploughed nor planted , being entirely matted with green roots , and cold and sour ; besides , the moment the sun was let into it , sprouts would begin to spring up from the stumps , and weeds , blackberry , and raspberry bushes from the ground , and cover it all over", "context_len": 90}}}, "disused": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "This fort had once been a summer-house , but it had long since been disused , and would , no doubt , have fallen into decay , had not the children hit upon the idea of making it the scene of their pitched battles , and had so propped it up and strengthened it that it was impossible to take except by surprise", "context_len": 63}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Lem was taken to a little disused tool-house , where he was locked up , and one of the hired men put on guard outside , so that he might do no mischief ; Mr", "context_len": 35}}}, "undefended": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "But , of course , even a brief absence from the fort left the flag undefended , and there was always a chance that , while one of the attackers was being pursued , some of the others might steal up and succeed in going off with the flag", "context_len": 49}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "Then the awful war cry of the Danes arose from earth to heaven , chilling the very blood and , disdaining all further concealment , the murderous warriors rushed forward , doubtless expecting to find the place almost undefended , and to carry the defences at a rush", "context_len": 48}}}, "kneaded": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Superintended by Drusie , they all worked very busily at the ammunition , and as they kneaded cannon-balls and pellets they laid out a plan of attack for the following Tuesday", "context_len": 31}}}, "dodges": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` He may have brought back some new dodges , '' said Drusie hopefully", "context_len": 14}}}, "fielding": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "The idea of Dodds playing with a girl ! I say , how he would laugh ! -- We might have you , though , sometimes , Jim ; you would be useful for fielding", "context_len": 35}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 47, "context": "Hal had thought that Dodds would be far too big and grown up to care about playing with girls ; but when he found out that Dodds actually enjoyed playing cricket with them , and thought a great deal of Drusie 's bowling and Helen 's smart fielding , he began to think that he had made a mistake in supposing that he had grown too old for them", "context_len": 69}}}, "recipe": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "On previous birthdays they had always been satisfied with lemonade as a drink , but Drusie , feeling that this was a special occasion , had considered that lemonade was , perhaps , hardly a suitable form of refreshment ; and so , from a recipe which she was proud to think was entirely out of her own head , she had concocted a bottle of red wine", "context_len": 68}}}, "concocted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 62, "context": "On previous birthdays they had always been satisfied with lemonade as a drink , but Drusie , feeling that this was a special occasion , had considered that lemonade was , perhaps , hardly a suitable form of refreshment ; and so , from a recipe which she was proud to think was entirely out of her own head , she had concocted a bottle of red wine", "context_len": 68}}}, "delicacies": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 82, "context": "The canons of the cathedral , the priests of the other churches , the sheriff of the county , the reeve of the borough , the burgesses , all had their places , and the banquet began ; huge joints being carried round to each individual , from which , with his dagger , he cut what he fancied and deposited it on his plate ; then wine , ale , and mead were poured foaming into metal tankards , and lighter delicacies followed", "context_len": 84}}}, "fielded": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "He had merely bowled and fielded for Dodds", "context_len": 8}}}, "huffy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` Oh no , they are not , '' he said ; `` but they have kept on liking things that I do n't care about , and they get huffy when I do n't play with them", "context_len": 38}}}, "willy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "He only hoped that Dodds would not get tired of bowling to him , and turn him out willy nilly", "context_len": 20}}}, "dawdling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "If it had not been for Dodds , he might have been with them now , instead of dawdling away the whole of the morning doing nothing", "context_len": 27}}}, "absences": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "And this was the secret of much of his altered behaviour , of his mysterious absences , and more than all of his frequent pale looks and irritable moods", "context_len": 29}}}, "skulk": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "She advanced into the open , thinking , as she did so , how , if there had been any one to defend the fort , they would have been obliged to skulk from bush to bush , taking advantage of every scrap of cover", "context_len": 45}}}, "wiliness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Yet , at the same time , they could not help admiring the wiliness which the enemy had shown in thus reserving his fire", "context_len": 24}}}, "reserving": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 21, "context": "For at the Giant Cataract was the village of Nascanora , he whose nose Bomba had crushed , he who was reserving a special place on the top of his wigwam for the head of the jungle boy who had shamed him before his followers and defied him", "context_len": 48}}}, "flagstaff": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 35, "context": "Springing on to a projecting ledge just outside one of the loopholes , Jim 's head was already above the level of the summit , and his outstretched arm was within a foot of the flagstaff , when something hurtled through the air , and , to Jim 's intense astonishment , a coil of rope fell heavily over his shoulders , and slipped to his waist", "context_len": 67}}}, "slipperiness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Grasping the rope with both hands , he kept the knot from growing tighter ; then sliding through the noose with the slipperiness of an eel , he dropped to the ground", "context_len": 32}}}, "unluckily": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "But unluckily he caught his foot in the noose , and although he immediately twisted it free , he fell sprawling to the ground", "context_len": 24}}}, "bramble": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 41, "context": "Dear little pilgrims ! there were jewels in their way such as they did not dream of ; but it was only earnest seeking such as theirs which could find them ; for they lay hidden beneath many a thorn and bramble and unsightly weed ; and they were to be found only by the help of this very jewel of prayer which shone so brightly that its light guided the little feet to the dark places where the hidden gems lay", "context_len": 82}}}, "lassoed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Help , help ! '' she shouted ; `` I am lassoed", "context_len": 13}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "[ Illustration : Tommy , lassoed ]", "context_len": 7}}}, "contorted": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "Then he was even in a worse plight than Helen , for she , at least , had the use of her hands ; and , though he flung himself backwards , and twisted and contorted his body in every conceivable way , he could not release himself", "context_len": 48}}}, "conceivable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "Then he was even in a worse plight than Helen , for she , at least , had the use of her hands ; and , though he flung himself backwards , and twisted and contorted his body in every conceivable way , he could not release himself", "context_len": 48}}}, "hailstorm": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "Helen dashed off to do as she was told , but as she was flying across the clearing she was suddenly brought up by a perfect hailstorm of bullets , which played round her in all directions , and caused her to fly back to the camp with the astounding information that it was not Hal who had been defending the fort , but somebody else", "context_len": 66}}}, "duffers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` If you had not behaved like a set of duffers who had all lost their heads , I could have told you that myself , '' said Hal crushingly", "context_len": 30}}}, "awaked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "He had awaked a quarter of an hour ago , feeling all right again", "context_len": 14}}}, "defends": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Whoever it is , '' Drusie said , `` he defends the fort awfully well", "context_len": 16}}}, "hawthorns": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Once Drusie , who had wriggled herself through the thick hawthorns at the back of the fort , was within an ace of taking the flag ; but , just as she had climbed up on the roof , the defender , whose face was completely hidden by his helmet , made a grab at her , and she was obliged to fly for her life", "context_len": 66}}}, "peer": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 40, "context": "Then he leaped up , stumbled against his chair , seized it , felt his way in the darkness to the door , placed the chair and mounted it , till , grasping the iron bars above , he could peer out through the grating", "context_len": 45}}}, "skirmished": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Without losing a moment , the boys scrambled over a fence , skirmished onward under the screen of the flat car , dodged beneath it , raced across the narrow stretch separating the spur from the main track , and climbed aboard the forward coach of the train", "context_len": 48}}}, "sever": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` Is your rifle loaded ? Then , if it is , for mercy sakes fire and see if you can sever that main feed wire , '' and the lineman pointed to a thick conductor , which was shooting out blue sparks , and which had charged all the other wires with the deadly current", "context_len": 56}}}, "sawed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "My young master not knowing what to do with one creature on earth and another in the air , tightened the reins until he nearly sawed my tender mouth in two , while he threw up his young head and cried in his sweet voice , `` I would n't shoot a gull , for I 'm frightened to death of a gun ! ''", "context_len": 65}}}, "commendation": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "King laid down the paper-cutter and turned back sympathetically ; albeit several friends in the old town had kept alive for Emily Hastings 's memory much commendation that she did not yield to her parents ' choice of the superannuated wealthy foreigner they had selected as a husband for her", "context_len": 50}}}, "distrusted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "But with good reason they distrusted the white men , who knew their treacherous nature", "context_len": 15}}}, "fierceness": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 30, "context": "He had felt that this time must come , but now the dread of it passed from him instantly , and he was almost frightened by that feeling of eager fierceness and uncontrollable rage which had possessed him in the hour when he was led to wreak physical violence on Hayden for the destruction of little Jerry \u2019 s fiddle", "context_len": 60}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "For gentle as Polulu was with the boy who had saved him when trapped by the tree , he was fierceness itself as far as his fellows were concerned", "context_len": 29}}}, "jet": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` Three ! '' he shouted : and turning like lightning to meet this fresh foe , who he guessed would prove the most formidable , the boy saw an immense jet of water spurt high into the air", "context_len": 39}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` They are little jet black monkeys , just like the ones that played in the land of the Snow Feast", "context_len": 21}}}, "capitulate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Then the fort had no choice but to capitulate , and the day was won by the besiegers", "context_len": 18}}}, "squaring": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "But I have learned by experience that any fellow can \u2019 t afford to try squaring up scores with an enemy by fighting or any sort of personal violence ; I \u2019 ve found out he only injures himself the most , and I believe there must be other and better ways of getting even", "context_len": 55}}}, "rift": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "Even as he lifted his eyes toward the overcast sky , the pure white moon burst through a widening rift and poured its light like a benediction upon the silent world", "context_len": 31}}}, "berrying": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "Right in the middle of summer , too , when you can have the most fun of any time in the year , if you only had a chance to get it ! There \u2019 s berrying and bee-hunting and swimming and fishing and -- and lots of things", "context_len": 49}}}, "pensiveness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "I have a feeling as if the Crusaders and the Elizabethan lady and gentleman on the monuments would miss us when we go away , ' said Peggy , relapsing into pensiveness once more", "context_len": 34}}}, "shouldering": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "He cast his eyes critically toward the sun , hesitated for another minute , and then , shouldering his hoe , started down the hill toward the lake ; but before he had gone half-way to the water \u2019 s edge he stopped and stood still , nervously chewing a spear of June-grass , and glancing alternately back at the cornfield and forward to the tempting waters of the lake", "context_len": 70}}}, "cornfield": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 58, "context": "`` She made you take back old Miss Mapes ' handkercher , and made me go and tell Miss Jones she was sorry for unhookin ' her clothes-line and lettin ' down the clothes in the dirt ; and , oh ! do you think , there 's the biggest kind of a squash down in Todd 's cornfield , and I was a goin ' to get it for him , and Dol coaxed me not", "context_len": 77}}}, "fairs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Tom Brown don \u2019 t have to work every day in the week , and he can go to town every Saturday if he wants to , and go to fairs , and have pocket-money to spend ; and I don \u2019 t have anything , not even when I earn it", "context_len": 52}}}, "waywardness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "He paused , and looked across the field to where Joe , who had reached his old place , was bending over a long row of corn ; and his heart filled with fatherly sympathy for the lad in spite of his waywardness and obstinacy", "context_len": 45}}}, "paradoxical": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Jennie stood for a moment silent and wondering at this paradoxical statement", "context_len": 12}}}, "smarted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 92, "context": "The sandals were anything but comfortable , and her feet hurt dreadfully on the stones , while every gorse-bush she passed seemed to be stretching out spiky fingers to scratch her bare legs ; she was tired after her morning 's adventures on the moors , and the eight miles seemed to lengthen out to an interminable vista , in spite of the way being downhill ; sundry bumps and bruises , which she had never noticed at the time , began to ache now , and the bee-stings on her neck smarted , until she hardly knew how to bear the pain", "context_len": 103}}}, "manger": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 120, "context": "with pebbles , and devoured the queen-cakes which Lilian had placed on the kitchen window-sill to cool ; he had snatched Joe 's breast-pin -- a glittering imitation diamond -- from his Sunday tie , under the very nose of that injured youth , and had stolen so many small articles that if anything were missing the children would have a grand search for Master Jack 's hiding-place , and would generally turn out the lost treasure from among an odd collection of trifles -- scraps of bright-coloured rags , bits of broken glass , hairpins , pen-nibs , pencil-ends , together with pieces of bread and half-picked bones which the thief had concealed in some cunning corner inside a manger or under the roof of the loft", "context_len": 128}}}, "latched": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Then he went out at the stable door , shut and latched it , hurried across the barnyard and out upon the grassy expanse at the side of the highway", "context_len": 30}}}, "unlatched": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The man unlatched the stable door , opened it noiselessly , and went in", "context_len": 14}}}, "ave": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Nothing venture , nothing \u2019 ave , he said , as he leaped clumsily to the beast \u2019 s back", "context_len": 20}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "I \u2019 m sorry to \u2019 ave put you to the trouble of comin \u2019 out \u2019 ere , sir , but circumstances over which , as I may say , I have no control has made it inconwenient for me to meet you in your \u2019 ouse", "context_len": 48}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Well , yes , sir , I \u2019 ave", "context_len": 9}}}, "misbehaved": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "He oughtn \u2019 t to be left in bed later just because he misbehaved yesterday", "context_len": 15}}}, "surcingle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The bridle \u2019 s gone , too , an \u2019 the blanket an \u2019 the surcingle", "context_len": 16}}}, "witnessing": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 32, "context": "The three conspirators proposed `` borrowing '' a few specimens , smuggling them into Stumpy 's collection , which was kept in a shack in his back yard , and , after witnessing his surprise , they would take Professor Long 's reptiles back to the school", "context_len": 47}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` A Dane witnessing against a free-born Englishman ? Can it be endured ? '' cried Ethelred", "context_len": 17}}}, "joe": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 0, "context": "Joe when he butted into you ?", "context_len": 7}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Joe did as he was told", "context_len": 6}}}, "sackcloth": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "He may have the horse , but he must not come back here until he comes in sackcloth and ashes", "context_len": 20}}}, "idolized": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "The father had passed down the stairs and out at the door , while mother and daughter sat long together , mingling their tears over the unhappy fate of the boy whom both had idolized , and whose strange folly had made him , to all intents and purposes , an exile from his home", "context_len": 55}}}, "unwelcoming": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Many and many a night , as he lay under some unwelcoming roof , or still oftener with the open sky above him , he had dreamed of that gentle mother who used always to fold the soft covering over him , and give him the good-night kiss", "context_len": 48}}}, "chastened": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "And the young lady tossed back her tangled locks , shook her fist at the anticipated pile of darning ; then , putting on an air of chastened and becoming meekness , as being most likely to soothe Aunt Helen 's wrath , she marched sturdily into the house", "context_len": 49}}}, "raindrop": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Barty gave another jump , but this time it was not because a raindrop had startled him", "context_len": 17}}}, "immoderately": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Blixey laughed immoderately at this , and poured the coffee with an unsteady hand", "context_len": 14}}}, "surfaces": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "Barry noticed with amazement how cleverly Dora Wilcox had painted their top surfaces to look like wreckage to a passing plane", "context_len": 21}}}, "renamed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Whatever their names had been before , this incident had definitely renamed them", "context_len": 13}}}, "shambling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "He had not been long there when a big , uncouth-looking boy came shambling in and sat down on a box near by", "context_len": 23}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Bart did not allow the horse to lag , but his best pace was a poor shambling trot", "context_len": 18}}}, "relentlessly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Seizing it on the opposite side from where Joe stood , he thrust it violently outward , pushing Joe with it , backing him across the deck , backing him relentlessly till the edge of the boat was reached", "context_len": 39}}}, "impenetrably": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "It was a cold , rainy , and impenetrably dark night on the tow-path", "context_len": 14}}}, "deepen": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "Moreover they had asked him the question , and any lack of frankness on his part might deepen the suspicion they already entertained as to his motives", "context_len": 27}}}, "mouthfuls": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "Dallas by this time was sitting up and spitting out last mouthfuls of water , so his uncle saw he was in no danger", "context_len": 24}}}, "rills": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "Little rills and springs , cold as the ice itself , and delicious to drink , slowly trickled from each end of the glen , but though they ran all summer long , they never seemed to make any difference in the great mass which lay within", "context_len": 47}}}, "sympathizing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "He talked to the horse , too , in the old familiar way ; telling him of his troubles , pitying him for his own hard lot , sympathizing with him , until he fancied that tears stood in the horse \u2019 s eyes", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "She bundled the remains of the feast into her basket , and had just descended the stairs , escorted by the sympathizing Peggy and Bobby , when round the corner of the large haystack suddenly loomed the tall figure and black alpaca skirts of Mrs", "context_len": 45}}}, "frightfulness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "So they trudged on together , boy and horse , each helping and comforting the other , -- on in distress and despair , through cold and rain and mud , into the darkness , the dreariness , the frightfulness of another night !", "context_len": 44}}}, "neigh": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "He recognized his young master with a subdued neigh , and tossed his head impatiently", "context_len": 15}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "When Dick , therefore , heard the whistle and recognized the youth , he paused only long enough to make sure there was no mistake , and then with a neigh of pleasure , he trotted toward him", "context_len": 38}}}, "inexperienced": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 31, "context": "The whole performance took but a moment , but there was an unmistakable air of guilt and terror about Lewis which did not fail to make itself apparent even to the inexperienced eye of Charlie", "context_len": 35}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 55, "context": "How sagacious a line of conduct was this ! Most ardent and enthusiastic young men , in the circumstances in which he was placed , would have been elated and vain at their elevation , and would have replaced the old and well-tried servants of the father with personal favorites of their own age , inexperienced and incompetent , and as conceited as themselves", "context_len": 64}}}, "paymaster": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "Come up to the paymaster , young man ! There 's an advance till salary day , and well you 've earned it", "context_len": 23}}}, "employes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "Local authorities deferred to him in all matters pertaining to the canal and its employes , unless the law were formally invoked", "context_len": 22}}}, "acknowledges": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "But he acknowledges having taken him from Rosencamp , the operator explained", "context_len": 12}}}, "bravado": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` I 'm going to do my best not to be sent back here , '' said Lewis , striving to continue his bravado , although his heart was sinking as he began to realize more and more in what a predicament he had placed himself", "context_len": 46}}}, "bondsman": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Are you going to institute proceedings against this boy ? If you are , he will be placed under bonds , and I shall become his bondsman", "context_len": 27}}}, "luxuriance": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "He no longer paid heed to the beauty of the day , the splendor of the sun , or the rich luxuriance of the early autumn foliage", "context_len": 27}}}, "shrunken": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "The man was shrunken , knock-kneed , eccentric in dress and manner , and leaned heavily on his cane", "context_len": 19}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "His traveling suit was wet , shrunken and shapeless", "context_len": 9}}}, "cackling": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Then she threw back her head and laughed , a harsh , cackling laugh that caused Bomba to wince and shrink back as from the sharp thrust of a knife", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 82, "context": "There was the flower garden , delicious with all kinds of roses now in full bloom ; the very swing where papa used to swing when he was a boy , the stream and pond where he used to sail his boats and set up his water-mills ; and beyond all , the large farm-yard with its many outbuildings , looking almost like a village by itself ; while from one of the great barns whose wide doors stood open came the cackling of poultry and cooing of pigeons , the lowing of cows and oxen , and bleating of calves , all the pleasant noises of a large and thrifty farm", "context_len": 112}}}, "verbenas": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "The tiny fences were pulled up , and scattered far and wide ; lady-slippers , mignonette , verbenas , and all the other simple flowers which had flourished so well , and given such pride and delight to the little gardeners , were rooted up and trampled into the earth ; and , worse than all , the beloved heliotrope and geranium were torn leaf from leaf and sprig from sprig , while their main stems had been twisted and bent , till no hope remained that even these could be revived", "context_len": 92}}}, "probe": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "He resolved to do what he could to probe the matter to the bottom", "context_len": 14}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "' I am determined , ' Miss Martin went on , 'to probe this matter to the bottom , and I now call upon any girl who may have any knowledge on the subject to rise up and tell what she knows", "context_len": 42}}}, "townsmen": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "On the morrow they allowed the body to be taken to London , where the bishops , Ednoth of Dorchester and Elfhelm of London , received it , with all the townsmen , and buried the holy relics in St", "context_len": 40}}}, "jurisdiction": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "For some years the city was without bishops , owing to its insecure position during the strife between Wessex and Mercia , but later it appears as the seat of the great Mercian bishopric , retaining its jurisdiction until after the Norman conquest , when the see was transferred to Lincoln", "context_len": 51}}}, "callipers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Callipers sat there in the wagon , under the eye of the deputy sheriff , with stoical indifference on his face", "context_len": 21}}}, "stumpy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "It was a common horse-thief , -- a bow-legged , stumpy fellow by the nickname of Callipers", "context_len": 17}}}, "ouse": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "I \u2019 m sorry to \u2019 ave put you to the trouble of comin \u2019 out \u2019 ere , sir , but circumstances over which , as I may say , I have no control has made it inconwenient for me to meet you in your \u2019 ouse", "context_len": 48}}}, "ad": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 4, "context": "no page number - ad - The Boy Allies with the Army changed `` ALIES '' to `` ALLIES '' THE BOY ALIES WITH THE GREAT ADVANCE", "context_len": 27}}}, "criminate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "But now , look \u2019 ere ; you expects me to criminate myself , do you ?", "context_len": 17}}}, "summat": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "\u2019 Is clothes , if I must remark on \u2019 em , seemed to be summat the worse for wear", "context_len": 20}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "When John remarked that he was `` summat ruff , '' the gentleman at whose house Mr", "context_len": 17}}}, "flooding": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 17, "context": "The sun had so far descended that the valley was in shadow , but it was still flooding the hilltops with its yellow light ; and in its glow the figure of a boy on a horse , almost a mile away , was distinctly outlined", "context_len": 46}}}, "hilltops": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "The sun had so far descended that the valley was in shadow , but it was still flooding the hilltops with its yellow light ; and in its glow the figure of a boy on a horse , almost a mile away , was distinctly outlined", "context_len": 46}}}, "reascend": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Now , again , in sight of them all , the boy succeeded in stopping the horse , in turning his head , and forcing him to reascend the hill ; and once more the horse whirled about and plunged down the road toward the house", "context_len": 46}}}, "frontispieces": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The volumes are well printed , illustrated with frontispieces , and attractively bound in new styles , with separate designs by the best artists for each cover", "context_len": 27}}}, "attractively": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The volumes are well printed , illustrated with frontispieces , and attractively bound in new styles , with separate designs by the best artists for each cover", "context_len": 27}}}, "cole": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "COLE", "context_len": 1}}}, "snapdragons": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "SNAPDRAGONS", "context_len": 1}}}, "stephen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "STEPHEN", "context_len": 1}}}, "italics": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "underscores": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 3, "context": "Text enclosed by underscores is in italics ( italics ) , and text enclosed by equal signs is in bold ( bold )", "context_len": 23}}}, "inaccuracies": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "Printer \u2019 s , punctuation , and spelling inaccuracies were silently corrected", "context_len": 12}}}, "variable": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "-- Archaic and variable spelling has been preserved", "context_len": 8}}}, "hyphenation": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 4, "context": "Variations in spelling and hyphenation were retained as they appear in the original publication , except that obvious typographical errors have been corrected", "context_len": 23}}}, "matthews": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "MATTHEWS", "context_len": 1}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "Matthews", "context_len": 1}}}, "pard": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 2, "context": "Why , pard , this isn \u2019 t a time to be sorry about anything ! You \u2019 ve still got the colonel \u2019 s gold in your safe , and I \u2019 m the happiest stray in all New York ! You hear that , Matt ? and he whirled and caught his chum by both hands", "context_len": 58}, "UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 33, "context": "Jugged ! Jugged by a country constable , just when a telegram might save the day for us in New York ! Sufferin \u2019 cats ! Can \u2019 t we do something , pard ? We \u2019 re not going to let a couple of hayseeds knock us out like this , are we ?", "context_len": 55}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "I \u2019 ve been trying to think of that ever since we got through the gates , and headed this way , but I can \u2019 t seem to remember , pard", "context_len": 32}}}, "burg": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "We \u2019 ve been here five days , and the traffic squad has been some busy with our bubble-wagon , but if there \u2019 s any part of this burg we haven \u2019 t seen , now \u2019 s the time to get out a search warrant , and go after it", "context_len": 52}}}, "gasoline": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 4, "context": "For five days the gasoline motor had whirled the boys in every direction , an automobile rushing them around the city , with side trips to Coney Island , north as far as Tarrytown , and across the river as far as Fort Lee , while a power boat had given them a view of the bay and the sound", "context_len": 60}}}, "manufactory": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Matt , having lived in the Berkshires , and having put in some time working for a motor manufactory in Albany , had visited the metropolis many times", "context_len": 28}}}, "aviators": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "When we used that Traquair a\u00ebroplane , Joe , we were aviators", "context_len": 12}}}, "scooted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "When we scooted through the air we were aviating , eh ? Well , between you and me and the brindle maverick , I \u2019 d rather aviate than do anything else", "context_len": 32}}}, "aviating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "When we scooted through the air we were aviating , eh ? Well , between you and me and the brindle maverick , I \u2019 d rather aviate than do anything else", "context_len": 32}}}, "brindle": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "A moment later Ben found he had his hands full in defending himself , for the second dog , a huge brindle mastiff , having a protruding under-jaw and reddish eyes , leaped at his throat , his teeth gleaming", "context_len": 40}}}, "maverick": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "If that gold is here , I \u2019 m the happiest maverick that ever strayed from the Southwest ; if it \u2019 s not here , I \u2019 m due to get unpleasant tidings from the colonel", "context_len": 37}}}, "aviate": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "When we scooted through the air we were aviating , eh ? Well , between you and me and the brindle maverick , I \u2019 d rather aviate than do anything else", "context_len": 32}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "downtown": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "After that , we \u2019 ll get a car and start for downtown", "context_len": 13}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The cowboy would go downtown by the subway and Matt would use a surface car", "context_len": 15}}}, "jubilantly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Presently his surprise gave way to a look of delight , and he chuckled jubilantly", "context_len": 15}}}, "baited": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "The colonel \u2019 s got one end of the string , \u2019 way off there in Tucson , and the other end is here in New York with a baited hook tied to it", "context_len": 34}}}, "bonanza": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "The colonel got next to it , and he told me about the Pauper \u2019 s Dream \u2019 claim , which needed only a fifty-foot shaft to make it show up a bonanza", "context_len": 33}}}, "petered": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "When that shaft got down fifty feet , ore indications had petered out complete ; and when it got down a hundred feet , there wasn \u2019 t even a limestone stringer -- nothing but country rock , with no more yellow metal than you \u2019 d find in the sand at Far Rockaway", "context_len": 54}}}, "stringer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "When that shaft got down fifty feet , ore indications had petered out complete ; and when it got down a hundred feet , there wasn \u2019 t even a limestone stringer -- nothing but country rock , with no more yellow metal than you \u2019 d find in the sand at Far Rockaway", "context_len": 54}}}, "brownstone": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "You might go over it out loud , while I sit back here , drink in your words , and try to imagine myself the big high boy with a brownstone front on Easy Street", "context_len": 35}}}, "milling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Within the last three months we have picked up a vein of free milling ore which assays one thousand dollars to the ton -- and there \u2019 s a mountain of it", "context_len": 32}}}, "assays": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Within the last three months we have picked up a vein of free milling ore which assays one thousand dollars to the ton -- and there \u2019 s a mountain of it", "context_len": 32}}}, "hustling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "Now , from an item I read in the papers , I find you are at Catskill , New York , with that young motor wonder , Matt King , so I am hustling this letter right off to you", "context_len": 40}}}, "bullion": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 34, "context": "I reckon I won \u2019 t get lost , strayed , or stolen while I \u2019 m attending to this business of the colonel \u2019 s , but from the time I take that bullion out of the bank , Wednesday afternoon , until I get it into some safe place again , you \u2019 ve got to hang onto me", "context_len": 61}}}, "capitalists": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "You can help the deal along by taking the bullion to these capitalists , along with my affidavit -- which is with the bars -- stating that the gold came out of a week \u2019 s run at the Pauper \u2019 s Dream \u2019 with our little ten-stamp mill", "context_len": 49}}}, "billings": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "BILLINGS", "context_len": 1}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Billings , of Tucson , Arizona , stolen", "context_len": 8}}}, "skeptical": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "They separated , McGlory rather dazed and skeptical about his prospective fortune , and Matt more confident and highly delighted over his chum \u2019 s unexpected good luck", "context_len": 28}}}, "motorist": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Within half an hour after leaving his chum , the young motorist was in the Flatiron Building , asking the man on duty at the elevators where he could find Mr", "context_len": 31}}}, "elevators": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "Within half an hour after leaving his chum , the young motorist was in the Flatiron Building , asking the man on duty at the elevators where he could find Mr", "context_len": 31}}}, "ascensions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Lafitte , Cameron had told Matt , was a member of the A\u00ebro Club , had owned a balloon of his own , and had made many ascensions from the town of Pittsfield , Massachusetts -- which was near Matt \u2019 s old home in the Berkshire Hills ; but , Cameron had also said , Lafitte had given up plain ballooning for dirigibles , and , finally , had turned his back on dirigibles for heavier-than-air machines", "context_len": 78}}}, "ballooning": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 61, "context": "Lafitte , Cameron had told Matt , was a member of the A\u00ebro Club , had owned a balloon of his own , and had made many ascensions from the town of Pittsfield , Massachusetts -- which was near Matt \u2019 s old home in the Berkshire Hills ; but , Cameron had also said , Lafitte had given up plain ballooning for dirigibles , and , finally , had turned his back on dirigibles for heavier-than-air machines", "context_len": 78}}}, "dirigibles": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "compasses": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Matt introduced himself to the young man , who was a draughtsman for Lafitte , and who immediately laid aside his compasses and pencil , and climbed down from his high stool to grasp the caller \u2019 s hand", "context_len": 39}}}, "lafitte": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Lafitte", "context_len": 1}}}, "hydrogen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Suppose , said he , that something was discovered which had fifty times the buoyancy of hydrogen gas , that the buoyancy could be regulated at will by electrically heated platinum wires -- would that revolutionize this flying proposition ?", "context_len": 40}}}, "electrically": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "Suppose , said he , that something was discovered which had fifty times the buoyancy of hydrogen gas , that the buoyancy could be regulated at will by electrically heated platinum wires -- would that revolutionize this flying proposition ?", "context_len": 40}}}, "platinum": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Suppose , said he , that something was discovered which had fifty times the buoyancy of hydrogen gas , that the buoyancy could be regulated at will by electrically heated platinum wires -- would that revolutionize this flying proposition ?", "context_len": 40}}}, "revolutionize": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Suppose , said he , that something was discovered which had fifty times the buoyancy of hydrogen gas , that the buoyancy could be regulated at will by electrically heated platinum wires -- would that revolutionize this flying proposition ?", "context_len": 40}}}, "balloons": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "The drawback , in the matter of dirigible balloons , lay in the fact that the huge bag , necessary to keep them aloft , made them the sport of every wind that blew", "context_len": 34}}}, "idled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Crossing over into Madison Square , Matt idled away his time , roaming around and building air castles for McGlory", "context_len": 20}}}, "brokers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "You see , explained Dimmock , it was easier for Pearl to work alone , and pretend to be a messenger for the brokers", "context_len": 24}}}, "snag": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "This was startling news for Matt , inasmuch as it seemed to indicate that McGlory had encountered a snag of some kind in the matter of the mine", "context_len": 28}}}, "granger": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "You don \u2019 t look much like a granger , laughed Matt , taking in the messenger \u2019 s trim , up-to-date garments", "context_len": 23}}}, "crossways": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "I don \u2019 t care how long it is , just so I can assure myself that nothing is going crossways with the Pauper \u2019 s Dream", "context_len": 27}}}, "touring": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 50, "context": "Matt recounted the manner in which he had been beguiled into the open country by the supposed messenger ; and he told about the accident to the taxicab , the revelation that the supposed youth was a girl , the finding of the driver , the passing of the red touring car with McGlory in the tonneau , the work of Dimmock and Sanders , a mile west of Rye , and the journey through Connecticut and into Massachusetts , finishing with his meeting with McGlory", "context_len": 86}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "She must have seen the touring car pass the taxicab , and she must have known that Joe McGlory was in the car", "context_len": 23}}}, "wobble": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "There was a wobble , a wild lurch sidewise , a brief jump across the road , and a terrific jolt as the machine came to a halt", "context_len": 28}}}, "lurch": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 57, "context": "Whatever price the rich Colonel Harrington was paying Lem Wacker for his co\u00f6peration , it was not enough to blind that individual to a realization of the fact that accident had placed in Wacker 's grasp the great haul of his life , and he was making off with this fortune , leaving the colonel in the lurch", "context_len": 58}}}, "derby": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Her long tresses had been replaced under the derby hat , and she looked sufficiently boyish to keep up the deception -- so far as the driver was concerned", "context_len": 29}}}, "reddening": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "He could bear it no longer , the contrast was too painful , he must break the sweet charm , the hallowed song , for the sky was reddening yet more luridly behind him , and each moment he expected to see Dorchester burst forth into flames", "context_len": 47}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "One was on the summit of the range culminating on the spot now known as Edgehill , lying about ten miles south ; but on the west Malvern Heights had caught the flame , and on the far north the Leicestershire hills sent forth their reddening fire in more than one spot", "context_len": 52}}}, "tresses": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "I suppose , Miss Granger , said he , that I should have known , from the way those yellow tresses were smoothed upward at the back of your head , that -- that you were not what you were trying to appear ; but , of course , I wasn \u2019 t looking for any such deception as this", "context_len": 60}}}, "skein": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "He was caught in a twisted skein of events , and was the more perturbed because he could not think of any possible object the girl might have in trying to deceive him", "context_len": 33}}}, "taxi": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 27, "context": "I was up in the air when I heard Motor Matt say he was to stop at Rye , said the voice that had talked with the taxi driver", "context_len": 29}}}, "tonneau": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 56, "context": "Matt recounted the manner in which he had been beguiled into the open country by the supposed messenger ; and he told about the accident to the taxicab , the revelation that the supposed youth was a girl , the finding of the driver , the passing of the red touring car with McGlory in the tonneau , the work of Dimmock and Sanders , a mile west of Rye , and the journey through Connecticut and into Massachusetts , finishing with his meeting with McGlory", "context_len": 86}}}, "misstatement": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Random , but the girl had certainly made a misstatement when she said that the cowboy and the broker had hurried off in advance of the taxicab", "context_len": 27}}}, "broker": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 7, "context": "Random , and he turned to the broker , I \u2019 m sorry you couldn \u2019 t meet up with my pard , but I \u2019 ll bring him around to your office Wednesday", "context_len": 34}}}, "misstatements": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "But then , the girl had made many misstatements", "context_len": 9}}}, "trifles": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 83, "context": "with pebbles , and devoured the queen-cakes which Lilian had placed on the kitchen window-sill to cool ; he had snatched Joe 's breast-pin -- a glittering imitation diamond -- from his Sunday tie , under the very nose of that injured youth , and had stolen so many small articles that if anything were missing the children would have a grand search for Master Jack 's hiding-place , and would generally turn out the lost treasure from among an odd collection of trifles -- scraps of bright-coloured rags , bits of broken glass , hairpins , pen-nibs , pencil-ends , together with pieces of bread and half-picked bones which the thief had concealed in some cunning corner inside a manger or under the roof of the loft", "context_len": 128}}}, "terminals": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "A lamp was smashed , and some of the electric terminals had been jarred from their posts , but not a tire had been punctured , and the machine seemed as capable as ever of taking the road", "context_len": 38}}}, "reappearance": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "They were far too deeply impressed with a conviction of the decision and energy of Alexander 's character , as manifested in the whole course of his proceedings since he began to reign , and especially by his sudden reappearance among them so soon after this outbreak against his authority , to imagine that there was now any hope for them except in determined and successful resistance", "context_len": 67}}}, "tentatively": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "He felt of his legs , raising them tentatively and working them till the blood flowed back in their veins again", "context_len": 21}}}, "tartly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "If I had , she answered , somewhat tartly , I should have spoken about it", "context_len": 16}}}, "brokerage": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "McGlory wondered a little at the cheerful way in which Random left Griggs to look after the brokerage business ; at the same time , the cowboy felt not a little flattered to have Random neglect his personal affairs for the purpose of meeting Matt", "context_len": 45}}}, "stepladder": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "There were a stepladder and a chair in front of the blackboard , and off to one side was a machine in a glass case with a high basket standing under it", "context_len": 32}}}, "ticker": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 55, "context": "While the cowboy sat in his chair in front of the blackboard , wondering how long he could wait for Random or Griggs and yet be at the Flatiron Building as per appointment with Matt , a man sauntered in , looked at an office boy who was just going out with an armful of ticker tape , and then approached McGlory", "context_len": 62}}}, "layout": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "His glance roamed speculatively about the place , taking in the layout critically , then finally Bart was conscious that his shrewd , burrowing eyes were scanning him closely", "context_len": 29}}}, "effusively": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Random grabbed his hand effusively", "context_len": 5}}}, "deprecatingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Random waved his hand deprecatingly", "context_len": 5}}}, "barroom": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Random walked out into Liberty Street , rounded a corner , entered a door , passed through a barroom , and finally piloted the cowboy into a small apartment , furnished with two chairs , a table , and an electric fan", "context_len": 42}}}, "highballs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "I can recommend their Scotch highballs , and as for cocktails , they put up a dry Martini here that goes down like oil , and stirs you up like a torchlight procession", "context_len": 33}}}, "cocktails": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "I can recommend their Scotch highballs , and as for cocktails , they put up a dry Martini here that goes down like oil , and stirs you up like a torchlight procession", "context_len": 33}}}, "cackled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` My dears , my dears , '' cackled Daddy , `` come down to the river hollow , come down , come down", "context_len": 24}}}, "seltzer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "I reckon , neighbor , and he cocked up his eye at the waiter , that I \u2019 ll trouble you for a seltzer lemonade , mixed with a pickled cherry and the cross-section of a ripe orange", "context_len": 38}}}, "fatalities": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "That points him straight for fireworks and fatalities", "context_len": 8}}}, "schedules": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Sufferin \u2019 schedules ! cried the cowboy , starting up", "context_len": 10}}}, "bushwhacking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "A cab carried them to the Flatiron Building , and Random waited on the walk while McGlory went bushwhacking for Matt", "context_len": 21}}}, "aviator": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "Nary , he wouldn \u2019 t , returned the puzzled McGlory , I reckon he \u2019 s talking with an aviator , upstairs , and has lost track of the time", "context_len": 31}}}, "piked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "There \u2019 s something unexpected happened to Pard Matt , said McGlory , or he wouldn \u2019 t have piked off like this", "context_len": 23}}}, "cinch": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "It \u2019 s a cinch , Mr", "context_len": 7}}}, "congested": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "The pursuit led to the outskirts of the town , and , as the streets became less congested there was danger of the boys being detected in their chase , but fortunately for them the man took a notion to swing down through a side street and retrace his steps , back toward the centre", "context_len": 55}}}, "rammed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "I saw he was not a person to aggravate a boy into revolt ; however when Big Chief scowled and came to stare out in my direction , both hands rammed sulkily into his pockets , his father stepped after him", "context_len": 41}}}, "bowlder": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "He nursed a slight abrasion on his chin , looked at Bart sheepishly , and then stepped over to a big bowlder and rested against it", "context_len": 26}}}, "dippy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Great Scott ! Say , he didn \u2019 t look like he was dippy", "context_len": 14}}}, "resigning": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Resigning himself to the situation , Matt listened while the purr of a motor came to his ears and died away in the direction of New York", "context_len": 27}}}, "cycle": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "From off to the left Matt could hear the pounding of a motor as it took up its cycle", "context_len": 19}}}, "gag": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "We \u2019 ll take this coat from Motor Matt \u2019 s head and replace it with a gag -- a twisted handkerchief will do", "context_len": 24}}}, "adroitly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Once they halted at a filling station to replenish their supply of gasoline , but the man in charge of the supply tank was kept adroitly in ignorance of the fact that there was a prisoner in the tonneau", "context_len": 39}}}, "enwrapped": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "By degrees a numbness crept along Matt \u2019 s limbs , and a drowsiness enwrapped his brain", "context_len": 17}}}, "bluffs": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "No more than a hundred yards distant the pony of Dick Lightfoot was standing motionless , with his head upraised and staring in alarm at the opposite bluffs", "context_len": 28}}}, "tracery": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 50, "context": "Most of it had been carried away to build the large barns and stables , but the foundations could still be plainly traced , with here and there part of a wall thickly covered with ivy , the ruins of a shattered column , a delicate little piece of window tracery , or a few steps of corkscrew staircase", "context_len": 59}}}, "ineffectively": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "He gurgled ineffectively behind the gag", "context_len": 6}}}, "inhaled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Matt inhaled deep breaths of the pine-scented air", "context_len": 8}}}, "ozone": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The ozone held tonic properties and freshened him wonderfully", "context_len": 9}}}, "sanders": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 0, "context": "Sanders , standing on the footboard of the car , leaned into the tonneau and helped Dimmock remove the cords that bound Matt \u2019 s arms and legs", "context_len": 28}}}, "cranked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Sanders cranked up , climbed into his seat , and the car moved on through the forest aisle , the searchlights boring bright holes in the dark", "context_len": 27}}}, "complaisant": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Dimmock had a complaisant air -- entirely the air of a man whose plans are succeeding , and with ultimate victory assured", "context_len": 22}}}, "juggling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "What was the use of all this juggling with taxicabs and touring cars ? continued Matt", "context_len": 16}}}, "taxicabs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "What was the use of all this juggling with taxicabs and touring cars ? continued Matt", "context_len": 16}}}, "graveled": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "To the right of the house was a vine-covered pergola , and between the stables and the pergola ran the graveled drive , leading around the house from the front gate", "context_len": 31}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "At just fifteen minutes of midnight the strange trio passed up the graveled walk leading to the Martin mansion", "context_len": 19}}}, "fanlight": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "A glow appeared suddenly in the fanlight over the door", "context_len": 10}}}, "untie": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 45, "context": "They took the cart and the yoke to preserve as sacred relics , consecrating them to Jupiter ; and Gordius tied the yoke to the pole of the cart by a thong of leather , making a knot so close and complicated that nobody could untie it again", "context_len": 48}}}, "footboard": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Sanders , standing on the footboard of the car , leaned into the tonneau and helped Dimmock remove the cords that bound Matt \u2019 s arms and legs", "context_len": 28}}}, "leveled": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 35, "context": "The left arm hung to his hip while the other lay on the top of the rock , and was extended full length , the hand closed around the butt of a revolver which was leveled at the mountainous brute , lumbering heavily forward with his head swinging from side to side", "context_len": 52}}}, "footboards": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "There was a good deal of rattling , for the mudguards and footboards were loose , but the motor itself was working as well as the day it had come from the factory", "context_len": 33}}}, "rasp": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "As he closed it behind him he heard the rasp of a key in the lock , and the click of a thrown bolt", "context_len": 24}}}, "bookcases": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "He was in a large room , lined with bookcases", "context_len": 10}}}, "busts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "White busts looked out from niches in the wall , and comfortable chairs were scattered around", "context_len": 16}}}, "niches": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "White busts looked out from niches in the wall , and comfortable chairs were scattered around", "context_len": 16}}}, "twisters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Sufferin \u2019 brain twisters ! he muttered", "context_len": 7}}}, "tenterhooks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Can \u2019 t you choke off , pard , begged the cowboy , and tell me how they played tag with you ? Sufferin \u2019 tenterhooks , but this business has got me all at sea", "context_len": 36}}}, "muddled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 72, "context": "Beyond the fact that a lady in white was supposed to haunt both the ruins and the oak-wood , weeping and wringing her hands in orthodox ghostly fashion , the children had not been able to learn much of the story , for there were so many and divergent accounts of it , all told with uncertainty as to names and dates , and in that very oracular -- not to say muddled -- style sometimes indulged in by rustic historians", "context_len": 81}}}, "bobbled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "Then Baboo Bajorum got up and made a low bow also , and all the pirates made bows and the Good Wolf made a bow and Saturday made one and Blue Crest bobbled her head up and down most gracefully", "context_len": 40}}}, "fumed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "While the Indians talked in their guttural language , sometimes sitting for many minutes of silence between their laconic sentences , Bomba fretted and fumed , eager to be once more on the trail of the headhunters", "context_len": 37}}}, "dazedly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "Confusedly , he tried to think of the things she had said to him , but beneath the wild spell of that performance he could reason nothing out , and could only stare dazedly at this wreck of what had once been a great genius", "context_len": 45}}}, "poorhouses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Sufferin \u2019 poorhouses ! he mumbled", "context_len": 6}}}, "swimmingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Consider yourselves so , and we \u2019 ll get along swimmingly", "context_len": 11}}}, "blockheads": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Sufferin \u2019 blockheads ! grunted McGlory", "context_len": 6}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "Then he got a better place ; but , for a time , had to bear much abuse from his master , who declared that , although he had come across many blockheads from Cumberland , George was the stupidest one of all ! Still he bore the reproaches of his employer good-naturedly , and before long made his mark", "context_len": 60}}}, "flipped": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Tibbits placed the other sheet in the envelope and flipped it back to McGlory", "context_len": 14}}}, "tinhorn": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "You blamed tinhorn , cried McGlory , are you making a play to get hold of those two bars of bullion ?", "context_len": 22}}}, "leer": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "A sly leer accompanied the words", "context_len": 6}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` Mack'rel nets do n't tumble overboard and nigh upon drownd people without somebody makes 'em , '' said Zekle with a cunning leer", "context_len": 24}}}, "facility": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` But I say , Lena , '' he said , with a sudden change of subject and with his usual , easy-going facility for putting aside for the time being anything which troubled him , `` I say , is n't it queer that the girl you are all trying to win this prize for should be the sister of Seabrooke ? How things do come around , to be sure", "context_len": 72}}}, "faro": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "You \u2019 re one of these tinhorns , Tibbits , observed the cowboy , who \u2019 d stand up a stage or snake a game of faro", "context_len": 27}}}, "bottled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "What will McGlory do ? chuckled Tibbits ; what can he do ? You boys are safely bottled up here", "context_len": 20}}}, "futility": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Tom Hadley saw the futility of resistance , and quietly allowed himself to be confined in the same manner as his companion", "context_len": 22}}}, "counseled": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "Many of his Macedonian advisers counseled him not to make such an attempt ; but Alexander would not listen to any such cautions", "context_len": 23}}}, "passably": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "And the fit of them , too , was passably good ; but it chanced that McGlory \u2019 s outfit was a full dress suit , and Matt \u2019 s was a Norfolk jacket outfit -- a get-up he cordially detested", "context_len": 41}}}, "discrimination": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "I didn \u2019 t use much discrimination , in McGlory \u2019 s case , and that \u2019 s a fact , said Tibbits , with a laugh , but I brought what I could find in uncle \u2019 s wardrobe that looked as though it would fit", "context_len": 47}}}, "understudy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Tibbits , Dimmock , the girl , and the fellow who \u2019 s to understudy you , will get away from here to-night", "context_len": 23}}}, "flue": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "There \u2019 s a sharp turn in the flue , and the opening isn \u2019 t any more \u2019 n six inches wide", "context_len": 23}}}, "galoot": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "The galoot out there is right on the job , said he , and moved to the door", "context_len": 18}}}, "picklock": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Bending out a key ring , which he happened to have in his pocket , he contrived a picklock ; but no sooner did he begin operations than a voice from the hall ordered him to stop", "context_len": 37}}}, "pickings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Pretty small pickings for fellows like Dimmock and Tibbits", "context_len": 9}}}, "grafters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "I can size them up for that sort of grafters", "context_len": 10}}}, "pergola": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "What interested Matt particularly , however , was a red touring car in the drive , close to the pergola", "context_len": 20}}}, "eliminate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "These points they eliminate two by two , all but the last ( if the number of the points of a row be odd ) or the last two ( if it be even ) of each row , by which means they obtain sixteen points , single or double", "context_len": 50}}}, "cue": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "This had at once given Bart his cue for playing a joke , and it might be mentioned that the fun-loving youth never let go by a chance to play a trick", "context_len": 32}}}, "squirm": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "Peggy was so amazed by the suddenness of the attack that for the moment she offered no resistance ; but , finding a storm of blows descending on her head like hail , she managed to squirm out of Betsy 's ungentle grasp , and fled after Bobby down the lane , followed by a shower of epithets from the gate , where the old woman stood shaking her fist until long after the children were out of sight", "context_len": 79}}}, "doorknob": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The guard in the hall shook the doorknob", "context_len": 8}}}, "hike": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "I don \u2019 t need any of your new-fangled notions ; when my eyes go back on me , I \u2019 ll resign and hike over the divide", "context_len": 28}}}, "chuff": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "sputter": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Chuff , chuff , chuff-chuff ! The sputter died impotently", "context_len": 10}}}, "impotently": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Chuff , chuff , chuff-chuff ! The sputter died impotently", "context_len": 10}}}, "manipulating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Manipulating the switch , and the lever controlling the fuel supply , he tried again", "context_len": 15}}}, "barriers": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 7, "context": "There was a hurried removal of the barriers on the other side of the door , and then the old squaw came rushing out and threw her arms about Bomba \u2019 s neck , crying and laughing in the same breath", "context_len": 41}}}, "drunkenly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "Even as the thought formed in his mind , there came a crash , a jar that shook the automobile in every part , and made it reel drunkenly , and a clash of broken glass", "context_len": 36}}}, "straightaway": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "After a wild stagger , the car seemed to gather itself for a spring ; then it flung itself onward into the road , turned , and glided off on the straightaway", "context_len": 32}}}, "flyer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "Dazed and bewildered , McGlory lifted himself in the rocking tonneau and looked at Matt , who was still in the driver \u2019 s seat , still bending over the wheel , and still coaxing the demoralized red flyer to its best gait", "context_len": 43}}}, "earthquakes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Sufferin \u2019 earthquakes ! gasped McGlory , looking back at the gates", "context_len": 12}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` like an earthquake like the earthquakes '' ; but the second `` like '' ( mithl ) is certainly a mistranscription for `` of '' ( min )", "context_len": 29}}}, "cyclones": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Sufferin \u2019 cyclones ! exclaimed McGlory , keeping close watch of the road behind ; after that jolt it would be a wonder , pard , if something didn \u2019 t go wrong with the motor", "context_len": 36}}}, "complicate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "That would complicate the affair", "context_len": 5}}}, "tinkering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "I \u2019 m hoping Miles and Barney will pass us , and give us a little time to do some more tinkering , replied Matt", "context_len": 25}}}, "mudguards": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "There was a good deal of rattling , for the mudguards and footboards were loose , but the motor itself was working as well as the day it had come from the factory", "context_len": 33}}}, "horticultural": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "I ran till I was black in the face , Matt , doubling back , dodging around flower beds , and getting mixed up with all kinds of horticultural arrangements", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "This was a form of amusement which appealed far more to Miss Forster 's mind , for her flowers suffered much from drought in summer time , and she was a keen competitor at the local horticultural shows , exhibiting some of the largest carnations and the roundest dahlias in the neighbourhood", "context_len": 52}}}, "thicken": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Five minutes more of rapid traveling saw the houses thicken along the road", "context_len": 13}}}, "guying": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "McGlory , too , came in for a fair share of guying", "context_len": 12}}}, "blockades": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Sufferin \u2019 blockades ! cried McGlory , as Matt shut off the power and put on the brake", "context_len": 18}}}, "manipulated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The man who had manipulated the chain advanced upon the boys from his side of the road , a badge of authority in the form of a tin star", "context_len": 29}}}, "lockup": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 21, "context": "Doubtless his schoolmates knew all about it by this time ; they had heard of his arrest and imprisonment in the lockup , and they had told one another what they thought of it", "context_len": 34}}}, "scalawags": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "I can tell , just by the look of you , you \u2019 re a pair of scalawags", "context_len": 18}}}, "larceny": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "I \u2019 ll haul ye up in front o \u2019 him , soon enough , an \u2019 if he don \u2019 t hold ye to a higher court to answer for the larceny of one benzine buggy , I \u2019 ll miss my guess", "context_len": 44}}}, "benzine": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "I \u2019 ll haul ye up in front o \u2019 him , soon enough , an \u2019 if he don \u2019 t hold ye to a higher court to answer for the larceny of one benzine buggy , I \u2019 ll miss my guess", "context_len": 44}}}, "younkers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "I \u2019 m too old a hand to be come over by two younkers like you", "context_len": 16}}}, "jaybirds": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Sufferin \u2019 jaybirds ! growled McGlory", "context_len": 6}}}, "partitioned": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The second floor of the building was partitioned into two rooms", "context_len": 11}}}, "hayseeds": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "Jugged ! Jugged by a country constable , just when a telegram might save the day for us in New York ! Sufferin \u2019 cats ! Can \u2019 t we do something , pard ? We \u2019 re not going to let a couple of hayseeds knock us out like this , are we ?", "context_len": 55}}}, "casing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "getaway": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "I \u2019 ll take my chances on the hard time if we can make a getaway", "context_len": 16}}}, "jugged": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "We haven \u2019 t done anything we ought to be jugged for , have we ? demanded McGlory", "context_len": 18}}}, "ushering": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "Charley overheard the words , and he had already seen the cashier talking with Jenkins and ushering the girl into the private room", "context_len": 23}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The work of preparing for the ushering in of the New Year was almost completed", "context_len": 15}}}, "carpetbag": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Before Matt could answer , the conductor came along the aisle , ushering a gray-whiskered man who was carrying a carpetbag", "context_len": 21}}}, "hokey": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "By hokey , murmured the Leeville man , when the recital was finished , if that \u2019 s the truth , young feller , you an \u2019 your friend ought to have a medal", "context_len": 34}}}, "trainman": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "While the engineer , firemen , and some of the brakemen worked over the disabled engine , another trainman walked back to the nearest telegraph office to summon a relief engine", "context_len": 31}}}, "lection": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "It \u2019 ll cost him his job , next \u2019 lection , you can lay to that", "context_len": 17}}}, "attach\u00e9s": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Pearl stepped to the desk , and the cashier pressed an electric call for one of the bank attach\u00e9s", "context_len": 19}}}, "employee": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 9, "context": "There was no doubting the word of a trusted employee like McCarthy , and Bart realized that he felt very badly over the matter", "context_len": 24}}}, "jenkins": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 0, "context": "Jenkins drew out a big silver watch whose ticking could be heard by all", "context_len": 14}}}, "youngish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "He \u2019 s a youngish chap , seventeen or eighteen , I should say , rather effeminate in appearance , and wears -- --", "context_len": 24}}}, "foil": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The main thing was to foil Tibbits and keep the bullion", "context_len": 11}}}, "sizing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The ability to take a man \u2019 s sizing at a glance had saved him from many a pitfall", "context_len": 19}}}, "lopped": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The cowboy lopped down on the railing", "context_len": 7}}}, "vouch": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "That \u2019 s good , as far as it goes , said the cashier , but who \u2019 s to vouch for Motor Matt ?", "context_len": 25}}}, "muzzling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "I can \u2019 t say that I object very much to the muzzling order , remarked Captain Peyton", "context_len": 18}}}, "trespassers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Our captain had procured the animal for a friend of his , who lived somewhere in the country , and wanted such a dog to keep off tramps and other trespassers", "context_len": 31}}}, "hydrophobia": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "A kind of hydrophobia would sometimes be communicated by a snake-bitten monkey to its mates , and by them to any human being that came within their reach while the epidemic was raging", "context_len": 33}}}, "gibson": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Gibson ! The dog is raving mad !", "context_len": 8}}}, "traversing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "A globe of fire traversing the arc of a circle , rose from beyond the embers of the barns , and , sailing through the air , fell upon the roof , which , owing to the intense heat from the conflagration which had raged around , was in a very dry and inflammable state", "context_len": 55}}}, "firearm": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "The captain had a revolver in the cabin , but while we were becalmed off the Orkney Islands he had shot away all his cartridges at sea birds that came near the ship , so that now the firearm was useless", "context_len": 41}}}, "rabid": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Our attempts served only to irritate the rabid animal , so that he was now perfectly frantic , leaping , howling , and rushing about in a terrible manner", "context_len": 29}}}, "forestay": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "I \u2019 ve just come down the forestay", "context_len": 8}}}, "rabies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Probably the sudden immersion threw him into one of those convulsive fits so common in the rabies , for , after a few minutes of violent tumbling , he sank outright , and we saw no more of him", "context_len": 39}}}, "poising": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Since the memorable time when Captain Cook sailed into Botany Bay in 1769 and saw the naked native Australian poising erect to hurl his peculiar weapon , the boomerang has continued to excite the curiosity and amazement of the civilized world ; and truly the finding of such a scientific weapon in the hands of this so-called lowest order of mankind is an astonishing fact , to be simply accepted as another oddity of this odd , topsy-turvy corner of the world", "context_len": 82}}}, "cockatoos": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "This last is used for light hunting , such as killing ducks , cockatoos , and parrots , and is the one that is referred to when speaking of the boomerang", "context_len": 31}}}, "crudeness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 59, "context": "In my collection I have them varying from almost straight to a shape like that of the letter V , nearly straight , curved , plain , ornamented , some with strange carvings , and all varying according to different sections of the country and individual tribes , each having its own make or style , showing respectively rough crudeness or considerable finish , and being especially characteristic in the ends or points -- all of which a boomerang connoisseur will distinguish at once , and locate as to tribe and section", "context_len": 92}}}, "connoisseur": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 79, "context": "In my collection I have them varying from almost straight to a shape like that of the letter V , nearly straight , curved , plain , ornamented , some with strange carvings , and all varying according to different sections of the country and individual tribes , each having its own make or style , showing respectively rough crudeness or considerable finish , and being especially characteristic in the ends or points -- all of which a boomerang connoisseur will distinguish at once , and locate as to tribe and section", "context_len": 92}}}, "humpy": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 7, "context": "The nomad black fellow makes his primitive humpy , or hut , in a location chosen temporarily , according to his necessities for hunting , fishing , and the like , by cutting a young sapling half through about four feet from the ground , and bending it over to a horizontal position , thus forming a ridge pole , against which boughs and strips of bark are laid", "context_len": 69}}}, "nomad": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The nomad black fellow makes his primitive humpy , or hut , in a location chosen temporarily , according to his necessities for hunting , fishing , and the like , by cutting a young sapling half through about four feet from the ground , and bending it over to a horizontal position , thus forming a ridge pole , against which boughs and strips of bark are laid", "context_len": 69}}}, "presto": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "He does not like the wind , and if it changes , presto ! the humpy , too , is changed in a twinkling", "context_len": 24}}}, "scrubby": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Starting from the humpy , we crossed a little stretch of scrubby country , and struck into the sun-fretted gum-tree forest , locally known as the bush", "context_len": 27}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "He cut down a scrubby spruce , and trimming off the limbs within six or eight inches of the trunk , sharpened the points of them ; he then hitched the oxen to this hedge-hog , as he called it , and hauled it over the ground , thus scratching the earth over the grain", "context_len": 55}}}, "pliable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "In this state the wood is very pliable , and from time to time he took a crook off , held it between his toes , knees , and teeth , and twisted out all its inequalities", "context_len": 37}}}, "inequalities": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "In this state the wood is very pliable , and from time to time he took a crook off , held it between his toes , knees , and teeth , and twisted out all its inequalities", "context_len": 37}}}, "chipping": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "More chipping , then more roasting , and the growing boomerang was now and again tossed carelessly on the ground just to see how it would act , while he glanced at it sideways , gave it a poke with his foot , and reminded me of a sedate old tom cat playing with a mouse", "context_len": 56}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "They were made of a stone resembling flint , sharp at the point , and on each edge , but the edges were irregular , showing that they were made by chipping", "context_len": 32}}}, "partridge": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 67, "context": "After whirling through the air for nearly two hundred feet it began to rise , and its flight curved toward the left , taking in a circle of a hundred yards or more in diameter , and fell close to our feet , while throughout its whole course of nearly a thousand feet it kept up a harsh , whirring sound , like the wings of a partridge in full flight , the rotary motion giving it the appearance of a ring or wheel moving through space", "context_len": 87}}}, "rotary": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 73, "context": "After whirling through the air for nearly two hundred feet it began to rise , and its flight curved toward the left , taking in a circle of a hundred yards or more in diameter , and fell close to our feet , while throughout its whole course of nearly a thousand feet it kept up a harsh , whirring sound , like the wings of a partridge in full flight , the rotary motion giving it the appearance of a ring or wheel moving through space", "context_len": 87}}}, "beginner": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "Do you know of any deserving young beginner lately set up whom it lies in the power of the Junto any way to encourage ? He knew from personal experience how much it meant to a young man to have friends to give him support and advice", "context_len": 47}}}, "whimper": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Baby ceased her loud cries , and stretched out her dimpled arms to her uncle , with a beseeching whimper ; Hafed paused in his antics , and stood like a statue at sight of his master ; and the three other children all turned to him with exclamations of `` Oh , Uncle Ruthven ; we 're so glad ! '' and `` Please do n't leave us , '' from Maggie and Bessie ; and `` Make dat Patty be off wiz herself , '' from Franky", "context_len": 89}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Is Bomba a woman to listen to such things and tremble ? Shall he whimper as the monkey whimpers when he hears the roar of the jaguar ? No ! Bomba will go", "context_len": 33}}}, "clair": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Clair", "context_len": 1}}}, "cobb": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Cobb", "context_len": 1}}}, "newsdealers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "For sale by all newsdealers , or will be sent to any address on receipt of price , 5 cents per copy , in money or postage stamps , by", "context_len": 30}}}, "newsdealer": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "You can have your newsdealer order them or they will be sent direct by the publishers to any address upon receipt of the price in money or postage stamps", "context_len": 29}}}, "weeklies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "If you want any back numbers of our weeklies and can not procure them from your newsdealer , they can be obtained direct from this office", "context_len": 26}}}, "drowns": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Another young man , annoyed by the hero 's apparent lack of courage , does something very nasty and unkind which very nearly drowns our hero", "context_len": 26}}}, "multiplied": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "The nations exchanged their commodities far more easily and extensively in consequence of these facilities , and the means of comfort and enjoyment were multiplied and increased in thousands and thousands of huts in the great cities of Egypt , and in the rural districts along the banks of the Nile", "context_len": 51}}}, "mightiness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "Plash ! directly after ; but the sounds those two words express , multiplied and squared if you like , till the effect upon the senses is , on the first hearing , one of dread mingled with awe at the mightiness of the power of the sea", "context_len": 48}}}, "shale": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 65, "context": "`` For hundreds upon hundreds of years the winds and tides have combined to rid themselves of this obstacle to their progress , the winds urging the waves that come rolling in from the vast Atlantic , gathering force as they increase in speed , like one rushing at a leap ; and at last leap they do , upon the great black mass of shale , tons upon tons in weight , seeming as if they would sweep it clear away , and rush on in mad ruin to tumble the fishing luggers together and shatter them like eggs as they lie softly rubbing together in the harbour", "context_len": 109}}}, "luggers": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 93, "context": "`` For hundreds upon hundreds of years the winds and tides have combined to rid themselves of this obstacle to their progress , the winds urging the waves that come rolling in from the vast Atlantic , gathering force as they increase in speed , like one rushing at a leap ; and at last leap they do , upon the great black mass of shale , tons upon tons in weight , seeming as if they would sweep it clear away , and rush on in mad ruin to tumble the fishing luggers together and shatter them like eggs as they lie softly rubbing together in the harbour", "context_len": 109}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Others would make for one of the cinnamon-sailed luggers lying at anchor , to go round and back , or would get into one of the boats ; while some , more venturesome , or really more confident in their powers over the water , would go boldly out , perhaps a mile , to meet some lugger coming in from the fishing-ground , sure of being taken aboard and riding back abreast of the boulders where they had left their clothes", "context_len": 82}}}, "shatter": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 96, "context": "`` For hundreds upon hundreds of years the winds and tides have combined to rid themselves of this obstacle to their progress , the winds urging the waves that come rolling in from the vast Atlantic , gathering force as they increase in speed , like one rushing at a leap ; and at last leap they do , upon the great black mass of shale , tons upon tons in weight , seeming as if they would sweep it clear away , and rush on in mad ruin to tumble the fishing luggers together and shatter them like eggs as they lie softly rubbing together in the harbour", "context_len": 109}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "For some were sunk and many were shatter 'd , and so could fight us no more -- God of battles , was ever a battle like this in the world before ?", "context_len": 33}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Hush ! Speak not , Bartow , or you will break the spell and shatter it into a thousand tinkling fragments", "context_len": 21}}}, "glistens": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "They have worn the rock smooth , so that it glistens like glass in the morning sun , for , as if aware of the folly of urging on its regiments of well-mounted cavalry to come dashing in upon the wild white-maned sea-horses , or the more sober lines of heavy infantry in uniforms of green and blue , the sea has for countless ages bombarded Carn Du with stone-shot in the shape of great boulders", "context_len": 76}}}, "limpet": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "These have ground and polished off every scrap of seaweed , every barnacle , limpet , and sea-anemone , leaving the rock all smooth and bare , while the boulders lie piled to the east in a heap , where the waves that try to take the rock in flank leap amongst them , and roll them over higher and higher , to come rumbling down as if they were tiny pebbles instead of rounded masses of granite and spar-veined stone a quarter , half , and a hundredweight each", "context_len": 90}}}, "hundredweight": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 88, "context": "These have ground and polished off every scrap of seaweed , every barnacle , limpet , and sea-anemone , leaving the rock all smooth and bare , while the boulders lie piled to the east in a heap , where the waves that try to take the rock in flank leap amongst them , and roll them over higher and higher , to come rumbling down as if they were tiny pebbles instead of rounded masses of granite and spar-veined stone a quarter , half , and a hundredweight each", "context_len": 90}}}, "oilskins": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "It was there that the great Austrian full-rigged ship came on , during one black and raging night ; when one minute from the harbour , and off the cliff , the fishermen in their oilskins could see the lights of a vessel -- the next minute , nothing", "context_len": 49}}}, "feathery": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 19, "context": "There was the giant mora tree , two hundred feet high , aglow with clusters of scarlet blossoms , feathery palms , the bright yellow trumpet flower with blooms so large that they were worn as hats by the Indian women and children , huge fuchsias with their purplish tubular bells , heliotrope , verbenas , orchids , glowing with all the colors of the rainbow", "context_len": 66}}}, "shaley": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 63, "context": "For there was something very stirring in the act , and a stranger to the place would hold his breath in dread as he saw Mark Penelly , who was the finest swimmer at the port of Carn Du , climb up the side of the great black rock upon some fine summer evening , then go round along the narrow shelf of shaley stone , till he stood alone there forty feet above the sea , his white figure as he rested against the black rock , every muscle standing out from his well-knit frame , and his arms crossed , looking like some antique statue in its niche", "context_len": 110}}}, "shags": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 65, "context": "Dotted about the swelling surface there would be the heads of plenty of swimmers -- men and lads -- some going smoothly along , mounting the rollers as they came in , and descending softly into the hollows ; others again swimming to meet each wave , then rising a little , and with a plunge like a duck or one of the great bronze-black shags , or cormorants , that sat upon the rock-shelves , diving right through the mass of water , to come out fairly on the other side", "context_len": 92}}}, "cormorants": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 68, "context": "Dotted about the swelling surface there would be the heads of plenty of swimmers -- men and lads -- some going smoothly along , mounting the rollers as they came in , and descending softly into the hollows ; others again swimming to meet each wave , then rising a little , and with a plunge like a duck or one of the great bronze-black shags , or cormorants , that sat upon the rock-shelves , diving right through the mass of water , to come out fairly on the other side", "context_len": 92}}}, "buoys": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "He was puzzled , for , save a few luggers swinging from the little buoys that dotted the surface of the sea , there was not a sign of an accident by the upsetting of a boat , or of any one struggling in the water", "context_len": 46}}}, "lugger": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 57, "context": "Others would make for one of the cinnamon-sailed luggers lying at anchor , to go round and back , or would get into one of the boats ; while some , more venturesome , or really more confident in their powers over the water , would go boldly out , perhaps a mile , to meet some lugger coming in from the fishing-ground , sure of being taken aboard and riding back abreast of the boulders where they had left their clothes", "context_len": 82}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "On board the lugger the fishermen were busily running the net from one compartment of the vessel into the other , still shaking the fish out as they went on , for a sudden squall at the fishing-ground had compelled them to haul in their nets hastily and run for home", "context_len": 51}}}, "capsize": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "They looked upon it as a business -- as part of their education -- for no boy or man was counted fit to go out in a boat who could not leap overboard and swim alongside , or , during a capsize , keep himself afloat , and help to turn the boat and bale her out", "context_len": 57}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "On he went down the river , the canoe caught now by one current , now by another , sometimes dipping to one side at so sharp an angle that it seemed it must capsize , then righting itself and dancing on again over the frothing black water -- a frail barrier between Bomba and destruction", "context_len": 56}}}, "bale": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 54, "context": "They looked upon it as a business -- as part of their education -- for no boy or man was counted fit to go out in a boat who could not leap overboard and swim alongside , or , during a capsize , keep himself afloat , and help to turn the boat and bale her out", "context_len": 57}}}, "statuesque": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "There one moment stood the statuesque figure , white as a cameo cut in the black rock , the next moment there was a gleam of something flashing through the air , and passing into the deep blue wave , which , as if by the contact of the figure , broke into silvery foam , rushing back like a vast cascade towards the Shangles", "context_len": 65}}}, "cameo": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "There one moment stood the statuesque figure , white as a cameo cut in the black rock , the next moment there was a gleam of something flashing through the air , and passing into the deep blue wave , which , as if by the contact of the figure , broke into silvery foam , rushing back like a vast cascade towards the Shangles", "context_len": 65}}}, "neckerchief": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` Yes , '' said the young man quietly , as he proceeded to divest himself of his neckerchief and let loose his thick white throat ; `` nice night for it", "context_len": 32}}}, "bather": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "He was now near enough to see that it was a man -- evidently a bather -- who was struggling for his life and in terrible danger", "context_len": 27}}}, "gills": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 59, "context": "Their hands , too , were sore and parboiled from the friction of the line and constant soaking in the water , especially those of John and Fred , who did not know how to take out the hook without putting their fingers into the fish \u2019 s mouth , and scratching and cutting them with his teeth and gills", "context_len": 60}}}, "conceitedly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` Yes , to be sure , '' said Mark conceitedly", "context_len": 11}}}, "fishers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "Mark Penelly had gone over to the other side of the lugger while the conversation was going on , and he did not face the man he looked upon as his rival ; while Harry , unnoticed by the busy fishers as he swam round , went on , touching the sides of the lugger as he lightly swam , but only the next moment to find himself entangled in a quantity of the thin mackerel net , which seemed somehow to descend upon him like a cloud , and before he could realise the fact he was under water , hopelessly fettered by the net , and feeling that if he could not extricate himself directly he should be a dead man", "context_len": 123}}}, "continuation": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` Galland evidently supposed , in error , that Petis de la Croix 's forthcoming work was a continuation of his `` Contes Turcs '' published in 1707 , a partial translation ( never completed ) of the Turkish version of `` The Forty Viziers , '' otherwise `` The Malice of Women , '' for which see Le Cabinet des Fees , vol", "context_len": 64}}}, "wrack": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "In this case , then , tangled and helpless as he was , Harry Paul could only think for a few moments of the time when he swam into the sea-cave at Pen Point at high tide , and felt the long strands of the bladder wrack curl and twist round his limbs like the tentacles of some sea-monster ; and he realised once more the chilling sense of helpless horror that seemed to numb his faculties", "context_len": 77}}}, "tentacles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 55, "context": "In this case , then , tangled and helpless as he was , Harry Paul could only think for a few moments of the time when he swam into the sea-cave at Pen Point at high tide , and felt the long strands of the bladder wrack curl and twist round his limbs like the tentacles of some sea-monster ; and he realised once more the chilling sense of helpless horror that seemed to numb his faculties", "context_len": 77}}}, "faculties": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 76, "context": "In this case , then , tangled and helpless as he was , Harry Paul could only think for a few moments of the time when he swam into the sea-cave at Pen Point at high tide , and felt the long strands of the bladder wrack curl and twist round his limbs like the tentacles of some sea-monster ; and he realised once more the chilling sense of helpless horror that seemed to numb his faculties", "context_len": 77}}}, "squall": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 34, "context": "On board the lugger the fishermen were busily running the net from one compartment of the vessel into the other , still shaking the fish out as they went on , for a sudden squall at the fishing-ground had compelled them to haul in their nets hastily and run for home", "context_len": 51}}}, "cornish": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Cornish to strip and birch me", "context_len": 6}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Cornish will be able to trust me again", "context_len": 8}}}, "meshes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "He had struggled so hard that he had wound it round and round him , and so tight was it in places that , without hesitation , the master pulled out his great jack-knife and cut the meshes in three or four places", "context_len": 43}}}, "recrimination": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Plenty more angry recrimination went on as all tugged at the long oars , and the lugger began to move slowly through the water towards the little harbour ; but if Harry Paul 's life had depended upon the services of the doctor at Carn Du he would never have seen the sun rise on the morrow 's dawn", "context_len": 59}}}, "urgings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "By degrees , though , the cloud that had been over his understanding seemed to float away , and as they were nearing the harbour he began to recall the urgings he had received to leap from Carn Du , which now stood up black and forbidding on his left ; the swim out to the lugger and round ; and then -- '' Well , how do you feel now , lad ? '' said the master", "context_len": 78}}}, "buoy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "The buoy outside the harbour was reached , however , directly after , and as soon as the oars were laid in all hands were busy for the next two hours shaking out and landing mackerel ready for basketing and sending across country to catch the early morning train", "context_len": 49}}}, "mas": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "mastering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Zekle Wynn already had his hand upon the door when , mastering the strange feeling of dread that had seized him , Mark Penelly caught him by the arm and held him tightly :", "context_len": 34}}}, "weals": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "There could be no doubt but that some of the strokes administered would leave blue weals , though Zekle did not get many", "context_len": 23}}}, "writhed": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 75, "context": "He felt better after this and went in ; but that night the excitement of the adventure and the effects of his immersion were sufficient to keep him awake hour after hour , while when he dropped off into an uneasy slumber it was for his mind to be haunted by dreams in which he was being dragged down into the depths of the sea by a strange monster that clung to his limbs and writhed about him , making him shudder as he felt the chilling embrace", "context_len": 88}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The native writhed and twisted , the eyes bloodshot with terror at the threat", "context_len": 14}}}, "variation": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "They traveled all day without meeting with any unusual adventure , pausing only briefly at noon to roast some jaboty eggs they found in the forest that gave a welcome variation to their meat diet", "context_len": 35}}}, "sculled": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "Harry uttered a hoarse cry as he still sculled along , his eyes fixed upon the spot where the hand had disappeared , and then tracing in imagination the course the drowning man would take as he was swept along beneath the surface , he made for the place", "context_len": 49}}}, "sculling": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Half an hour 's sculling took him out to a great buoy close by some sunken rocks ; and having made fast his boat to the rusty , barnacle-encrusted ring , he proceeded to bait his lines , and lowered down the leads into the deep water below", "context_len": 48}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "It was in imagination , but his mental calculation was not far wrong , for within a few yards of where it might be expected , and not ten from where he was now sculling , he saw something roll up as it were to the surface , there was a gleam of white in the sunlit water , and then it was disappearing again , when , acting upon the impulse of the moment , Harry loosened his hold of the oar , took two steps forward over the thwarts , and leaped into the sea", "context_len": 97}}}, "conger": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "A sharp snatch at his line , followed by a long peculiar drag , told him what was at his bait ; and after a little giving and taking , he drew a heavy twining conger eel over the boat 's edge , having no little difficulty in preventing it from tangling his line , for it was quite a yard in length , and proportionately thick", "context_len": 67}}}, "tangling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 51, "context": "A sharp snatch at his line , followed by a long peculiar drag , told him what was at his bait ; and after a little giving and taking , he drew a heavy twining conger eel over the boat 's edge , having no little difficulty in preventing it from tangling his line , for it was quite a yard in length , and proportionately thick", "context_len": 67}}}, "boulder": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 23, "context": "His captive was , however , soon safe in the large basket , and he had hardly closed the lid and placed a boulder used as ballast upon it before a tug at his other line made the thole-pin rattle , and after a little hauling he dragged in a gloriously-coloured gurnard , whose outspread fins looked like the wings of some lovely butterfly", "context_len": 64}}}, "gurnard": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 51, "context": "His captive was , however , soon safe in the large basket , and he had hardly closed the lid and placed a boulder used as ballast upon it before a tug at his other line made the thole-pin rattle , and after a little hauling he dragged in a gloriously-coloured gurnard , whose outspread fins looked like the wings of some lovely butterfly", "context_len": 64}}}, "wrasse": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Then he drew in , one after the other , a couple of wrasse , all grey and green and gold , with their protuberant mouths and curious teeth , after which there was a pause , and , drawing up one of his lines , Harry placed thereon a much larger hook , bound with wire right up the cord that held it", "context_len": 64}}}, "protuberant": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Then he drew in , one after the other , a couple of wrasse , all grey and green and gold , with their protuberant mouths and curious teeth , after which there was a pause , and , drawing up one of his lines , Harry placed thereon a much larger hook , bound with wire right up the cord that held it", "context_len": 64}}}, "hake": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` It must be either a great conger or a monster hake , or else it 's a small shark", "context_len": 20}}}, "refulgent": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "While the latter was growing moment by moment more refulgent , the former caught the wondrous dyes , till the water seemed everywhere like molten gold with ruddy and empurpled reflections where the sea gave a gentle heave", "context_len": 38}}}, "dyes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "While the latter was growing moment by moment more refulgent , the former caught the wondrous dyes , till the water seemed everywhere like molten gold with ruddy and empurpled reflections where the sea gave a gentle heave", "context_len": 38}}}, "empurpled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "While the latter was growing moment by moment more refulgent , the former caught the wondrous dyes , till the water seemed everywhere like molten gold with ruddy and empurpled reflections where the sea gave a gentle heave", "context_len": 38}}}, "hitches": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "He felt in despair , for he was all eagerness now to see the monster he had hooked , when a happy thought suggested itself , and in an instant he had made three or four hitches round one of the oars with the end of the line , and cast it overboard", "context_len": 53}}}, "weedy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 58, "context": "The poor fellow seemed to have gone out too far , and , in his ignorance , had been drawn into the fierce current -- one that no one dwelling about Carn Du would have ventured to approach ; and , unless help were soon afforded , there would be a dead body cast up somewhere by a weedy cove just about the turn of the tide", "context_len": 67}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "After that came the giant and dwarf , the former a weedy specimen of unwholesome overgrowth , while the latter looked as if he had been reared upon gin to keep him small", "context_len": 33}}}, "exactness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "A curious shuddering sensation ran through him , and the remembrance of what he had gone through on the previous day came back with a strange exactness , in which he seemed to feel once more the cold clinging touch of the net upon his bare skin , and for the moment he felt as if he were paralysed", "context_len": 59}}}, "buttresses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "On the one side there was the open sea , on the other the piled-up granite , which rose up like hand-built buttresses , composed of vast squared masses rising tier upon tier", "context_len": 33}}}, "eddy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 41, "context": "He did not know where he was going , but he felt that his only chance was to go with the current till he should be swept near some of the outlying rocks , when they might be drawn into an eddy , and so be able to climb up on to the shell-covered stones , and wait there till they were seen", "context_len": 63}}}, "ebbing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "Every now and then , too , the water washed over his face , telling him that his position was lower , and at last , when all seemed to be over and his strength was ebbing away , he raised his head for a last farewell look-out for help , and one of his hands struck against a rock", "context_len": 60}}}, "strewed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "Aunt Helen was married in September , with the Rector to read the service , and Father to give her away , and Lilian and Peggy for bridesmaids , while the Sunday-school children strewed the path from the church-door with flowers , and Bobby flung rice and old shoes after the carriage , and all the village people came to watch , and say how sweet and pretty she looked , and to wish health and happiness to 'Mrs", "context_len": 79}}}, "mussels": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "Almost as he touched it the stream bore him by , but there was another mass close at hand , hung with tresses of seaweed and thickly strewed with mussels , and here he got a hold for a few moments , in spite of the drag of the rushing water", "context_len": 51}}}, "summoning": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 15, "context": "At the end of a few minutes , however , he felt stronger , and summoning up his energies for the effort , he got one hand higher , then the other , and clung there half out of the water", "context_len": 41}}}, "dives": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "It was a strange reaction as he hung there -- he , the brave and daring swimmer , famed for his dives off Carn Du , held up by the man he had always denounced as a terrible coward ; whom he had hated from boyhood almost , without cause , and whom really , under the impulse of a horrible temptation , he had on the previous night tried to hamper in his swimming , though not really to drown", "context_len": 81}}}, "gushes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "The former was content to lie there gazing into his enemy 's eyes , for his strength was returning with every breath ; that breathing was less laboured , and , in place of his heart throbbing and jumping , sending hot gushes of blood , as it were , choking to his throat , it began to settle steadily down to its ordinary labours in the breast of a strongly-built , healthy , temperate man", "context_len": 76}}}, "unchanging": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "In his abject misery and dread , Mark Penelly saw , in the stern gaze before him , anger and a vindictive desire for revenge ; he saw therein fierce hate , and an implacable , unchanging condemnation ; he felt that Harry was sustaining him there where he had dragged him to make his sufferings more acute , and that , after holding him up for a while , he would loosen his hold , causing him to sink at once into the deep water by the rocks , and be swept away by the tremendous current", "context_len": 98}}}, "meted": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "He judged Harry Paul , in fact , by the same measure as he would have meted out to an enemy himself ; and so terrible were his thoughts , so horrifying to him was the thought of the death from which he had escaped , that he was robbed of all energy ; he had not strength to do more than hang there clinging to the weeds with desperate clutch , and , with only his head out of water , gaze up in Harry 's stern eyes", "context_len": 89}}}, "horrifying": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "He judged Harry Paul , in fact , by the same measure as he would have meted out to an enemy himself ; and so terrible were his thoughts , so horrifying to him was the thought of the death from which he had escaped , that he was robbed of all energy ; he had not strength to do more than hang there clinging to the weeds with desperate clutch , and , with only his head out of water , gaze up in Harry 's stern eyes", "context_len": 89}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The feeling which succeeded the first horrifying shock was one of profound pity for the victim", "context_len": 16}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "The reference was to a happening that had taken place near the Giant Cataract on a night that Bomba had met Nascanora in the midst of a perilous and horrifying scene", "context_len": 31}}}, "ac": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Yes -- yes -- I did ; but it was an accident -- an ac -- ''", "context_len": 18}}}, "quailed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "Harry Paul made no effort to drag the wretched man out , but gazed thoughtfully downward into his eyes , while , under the influence of that stern gaze , Penelly quailed and shuddered , his blue lips parted , his eyes seem to start , but he could not speak", "context_len": 51}}}, "sunday": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "SUNDAY 62", "context_len": 2}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "SUNDAY BEFORE ADVENT", "context_len": 3}}}, "archie": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "ARCHIE 180", "context_len": 2}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Archie came up during the morning bearing a pretty writing-case , an offering from himself and Miss Forster , while a parcel had arrived from India , containing the loveliest carved sandal-wood box , inlaid with mother-of-pearl , to show that Aunt Helen had not forgotten her little niece", "context_len": 49}}}, "sooth": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 42, "context": "Put away from thee this extravagance : [ 326 ] who shall undertake an affair like this and demand it of the Sultan ? Meknoweth not how thou wilt do to make this request of the Sultan , and if thou speak sooth , [ 327 ] by whom wilt thou make it ? '' `` O my mother , '' rejoined Alaeddin , `` by whom [ should I make ] a request like this , when thou art at hand , and whom have I trustier [ 328 ] than thyself ? Wherefore my intent is that thou shalt make this request for me", "context_len": 106}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Then , having done all he could to sooth the pain and staunch the bleeding , Bomba offered Polulu food and drink", "context_len": 22}}}, "swain": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "'Good sooth ! I know not be she wench or swain ; Her face proclaims her one , her deeds the other ! '", "context_len": 24}}}, "lilian": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 0, "context": "Lilian 's experience of concerts was confined to an occasional afternoon performance in the Warford Assembly Rooms , or the military band in the Spa gardens ; but she bravely hid her longings for better things , for she knew that a musical education would be an utter impossibility in the family circumstances , and that Father had strained a point already to allow her to learn from Herr Frankenburg , who was the most expensive teacher in the school", "context_len": 80}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "Lilian was away , spending a few days in Shrewsbury with a distant cousin ; Father had gone to Warford , and had taken Bobby with him ; even Rollo had disappeared on some mysterious errand not entirely disconnected with bones , so the young lady was left for once to her own devices", "context_len": 54}}}, "pottering": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "But I think the crowning achievement was an automatic currying-brush , which was to be wound up and fixed on to the horse 's back , and was to do the work 'in just half the time old Fleming takes pottering over it", "context_len": 43}}}, "turret": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "The quaint timbered house , with its carved gables and red-tiled roof , was built in at one end into a kind of square tower or keep , with tiny turret windows and winding staircase , getting just a little ruinous in places , but held firmly together by masses of ivy , which clung round it like a green mantle", "context_len": 61}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Tree tops nearly grazed her belly turret as she swept over a blunt headland , into sight of the camouflaged ships", "context_len": 21}, "NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 19, "context": "As a matter of fact , Curly Levitt said when they were out of hearing , Jackson \u2019 s turret is so banged up that it \u2019 s useless", "context_len": 29}}}, "abbey": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Passing through Oxen ford , Kirtlington ( where Bishop Sidesman of Crediton died at the Great Council , whose body is buried in the abbey ) , Beranbyrig , and Warwick , we reached the domains of Aescendune", "context_len": 38}}}, "foundations": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 17, "context": "Most of it had been carried away to build the large barns and stables , but the foundations could still be plainly traced , with here and there part of a wall thickly covered with ivy , the ruins of a shattered column , a delicate little piece of window tracery , or a few steps of corkscrew staircase", "context_len": 59}}}, "cooing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "There were rows and heaps of mossy stones covered with nettles and elder-bushes , with patches of green grass in between , where the cows grazed and the pigeons flew about , cooing gently", "context_len": 34}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 86, "context": "There was the flower garden , delicious with all kinds of roses now in full bloom ; the very swing where papa used to swing when he was a boy , the stream and pond where he used to sail his boats and set up his water-mills ; and beyond all , the large farm-yard with its many outbuildings , looking almost like a village by itself ; while from one of the great barns whose wide doors stood open came the cackling of poultry and cooing of pigeons , the lowing of cows and oxen , and bleating of calves , all the pleasant noises of a large and thrifty farm", "context_len": 112}}}, "jackdaws": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "In the ivy the jackdaws were always busy , and the children had many a perilous climb trying to reach the coveted nests", "context_len": 23}}}, "primroses": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 13, "context": "The woods around were full of wild flowers , briar roses , delicate primroses , and the bright red columbine , and even here and there , a late anemone ; the little star-like flower , looking almost as if it had dropped from heaven , and wondered to find itself alone and solitary , so far away from its sister stars", "context_len": 62}}}, "gillyflowers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "On the other side of the house stretched the garden -- such a sweet , old-fashioned garden , where roses , lilies , and gillyflowers were all mixed up with the currants and gooseberries and cabbages", "context_len": 36}}}, "hairy": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 50, "context": "Baboo Bajorum listened as attentively as he had done before , and almost as soon as he began to speak Barty saw him do that thing with his face which made him look as if he were smiling , and even before Barty had finished he put out his big hairy hand and patted him again on the head", "context_len": 59}, "UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 44, "context": "A nice , fat , curly-headed little boy , standing on a ship 's side , waving a white handkerchief and shouting in a loud and friendly manner , is a very different thing from a Baboo Bajorum shaking a long , black , hairy arm and roaring , so the Impolite Pirates stopped rowing and began to listen", "context_len": 59}}}, "tapestry": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "There was a large oak staircase , with wide , shallow steps , leading to a panelled gallery , where hung swords , and rusty armour , and moth-eaten tapestry , and many an ancient relic of the past ; while in the best bedroom was a great carved four-post bed , hung with faded yellow curtains , where Queen Elizabeth herself was said to have slept in much state for two nights on her journey from Shrewsbury to Wrexham", "context_len": 80}}}, "wrappers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "The big drawing-room had been shut up for many years ; the Queen-Anne chairs and china-cabinets were swathed in wrappers , and the ornaments put away in boxes ; but sometimes the children would steal in and open the shutters to look at the portraits which hung upon the oak-panelled walls -- stately gentlemen with wigs and lace frills , whose eyes seemed to follow you about the room ; haughty dames with powdered hair and patches ; stiff little girls in hoops and mittens , and pretty young ladies attired as shepherdesses or classic goddesses , with cupids and nymphs in the background", "context_len": 104}}}, "wigs": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 57, "context": "There were tombs in memory of them in a side transept of the church -- stalwart Crusaders , lying with legs crossed and meek hands folded in prayer ; stout Elizabethan squires and their dames , with ruffs round their necks , and rows of prim little kneeling children beneath them ; full-faced Jacobean worthies in curled wigs , with sculptured cherubs weeping over extinguished torches ; and there was a high old pew with a carved canopy over it , and an escutcheon bearing a coat of arms with a dragon on it , which , when Peggy was very little , she had always associated with the dragon in the Book of Revelation , and had an uneasy feeling that its eye was upon her all", "context_len": 128}}}, "shepherdesses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 92, "context": "The big drawing-room had been shut up for many years ; the Queen-Anne chairs and china-cabinets were swathed in wrappers , and the ornaments put away in boxes ; but sometimes the children would steal in and open the shutters to look at the portraits which hung upon the oak-panelled walls -- stately gentlemen with wigs and lace frills , whose eyes seemed to follow you about the room ; haughty dames with powdered hair and patches ; stiff little girls in hoops and mittens , and pretty young ladies attired as shepherdesses or classic goddesses , with cupids and nymphs in the background", "context_len": 104}}}, "cupids": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 98, "context": "The big drawing-room had been shut up for many years ; the Queen-Anne chairs and china-cabinets were swathed in wrappers , and the ornaments put away in boxes ; but sometimes the children would steal in and open the shutters to look at the portraits which hung upon the oak-panelled walls -- stately gentlemen with wigs and lace frills , whose eyes seemed to follow you about the room ; haughty dames with powdered hair and patches ; stiff little girls in hoops and mittens , and pretty young ladies attired as shepherdesses or classic goddesses , with cupids and nymphs in the background", "context_len": 104}}}, "nymphs": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 100, "context": "The big drawing-room had been shut up for many years ; the Queen-Anne chairs and china-cabinets were swathed in wrappers , and the ornaments put away in boxes ; but sometimes the children would steal in and open the shutters to look at the portraits which hung upon the oak-panelled walls -- stately gentlemen with wigs and lace frills , whose eyes seemed to follow you about the room ; haughty dames with powdered hair and patches ; stiff little girls in hoops and mittens , and pretty young ladies attired as shepherdesses or classic goddesses , with cupids and nymphs in the background", "context_len": 104}}}, "mullioned": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "It was by far the oldest part of the house ; the great beams of the roof , roughly hewn out with an axe and black with age , had been a portion of the ancient castle , and so had the mullioned windows , with their deep , old-fashioned seats and diamond panes , filled with green , uneven glass", "context_len": 61}}}, "cosiest": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "It looked a cheerful place , with its polished-oak dressers and shining brasses , and on a winter 's evening , when the shutters were closed and the settle drawn close to the fire , it seemed the cosiest spot in the world ; and Peggy and Bobby would often escape from the sober atmosphere of the dining-room to pull their little stools into the ingle nook , and listen to Nancy 's wonderful tales of ghosts and goblins , which seemed twice as thrilling when the wind was howling like a banshee in the chimney , and rattling the doors till they could fancy that spirit fingers were tapping on the panels , and only waiting a chance to catch them in the dark passages , and", "context_len": 128}}}, "banshee": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 74, "context": "'s evening , when the shutters were closed and the settle drawn close to the fire , it seemed the cosiest spot in the world ; and Peggy and Bobby would often escape from the sober atmosphere of the dining-room to pull their little stools into the ingle nook , and listen to Nancy 's wonderful tales of ghosts and goblins , which seemed twice as thrilling when the wind was howling like a banshee in the chimney , and rattling the doors till they could fancy that spirit fingers were tapping on the panels , and only waiting a chance to catch them in the dark passages , and sent such cold shivers running down their backs that they grew almost too frightened to go to bed", "context_len": 128}}}, "transept": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "There were tombs in memory of them in a side transept of the church -- stalwart Crusaders , lying with legs crossed and meek hands folded in prayer ; stout Elizabethan squires and their dames , with ruffs round their necks , and rows of prim little kneeling children beneath them ; full-faced Jacobean worthies in curled wigs , with sculptured cherubs weeping over extinguished torches ; and there was a high old pew with a carved canopy over it , and an escutcheon bearing a coat of arms with a dragon on it , which , when Peggy was very little , she had always associated with the dragon in the Book of Revelation , and had an uneasy feeling that its eye was upon her all", "context_len": 128}}}, "ruffs": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "There were tombs in memory of them in a side transept of the church -- stalwart Crusaders , lying with legs crossed and meek hands folded in prayer ; stout Elizabethan squires and their dames , with ruffs round their necks , and rows of prim little kneeling children beneath them ; full-faced Jacobean worthies in curled wigs , with sculptured cherubs weeping over extinguished torches ; and there was a high old pew with a carved canopy over it , and an escutcheon bearing a coat of arms with a dragon on it , which , when Peggy was very little , she had always associated with the dragon in the Book of Revelation , and had an uneasy feeling that its eye was upon her all", "context_len": 128}}}, "cherubs": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 61, "context": "There were tombs in memory of them in a side transept of the church -- stalwart Crusaders , lying with legs crossed and meek hands folded in prayer ; stout Elizabethan squires and their dames , with ruffs round their necks , and rows of prim little kneeling children beneath them ; full-faced Jacobean worthies in curled wigs , with sculptured cherubs weeping over extinguished torches ; and there was a high old pew with a carved canopy over it , and an escutcheon bearing a coat of arms with a dragon on it , which , when Peggy was very little , she had always associated with the dragon in the Book of Revelation , and had an uneasy feeling that its eye was upon her all", "context_len": 128}}}, "escutcheon": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 64, "context": "with legs crossed and meek hands folded in prayer ; stout Elizabethan squires and their dames , with ruffs round their necks , and rows of prim little kneeling children beneath them ; full-faced Jacobean worthies in curled wigs , with sculptured cherubs weeping over extinguished torches ; and there was a high old pew with a carved canopy over it , and an escutcheon bearing a coat of arms with a dragon on it , which , when Peggy was very little , she had always associated with the dragon in the Book of Revelation , and had an uneasy feeling that its eye was upon her all service time , and if she did not behave properly it might come down in great wrath and devour", "context_len": 128}}}, "cajole": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 65, "context": "But one wet afternoon , finding David alone in the harness-room , where the old man had lighted a fire to make some wonderful decoction of foxgloves wherewith to doctor a cow with a strained leg , they seated themselves on a sack of potatoes in front of the cheerful blaze , and with the aid of a little judicious flattery and coaxing managed to cajole him into a true and circumstantial account of the family ghost", "context_len": 77}}}, "spendthrift": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "But they had been a happy-go-lucky , spendthrift race , loving to ride to hounds and to entertain liberally better than to look after their affairs", "context_len": 26}}}, "woodlands": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "Little by little the fine property had been wasted away , till , when Peggy 's father succeeded to the estate , he found it to consist of scarcely more than the old house with the surrounding farm and woodlands , together with such a multitude of debts , mortgages , and other encumbrances , that it was truly a barren heritage", "context_len": 62}}}, "encumbrances": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 53, "context": "Little by little the fine property had been wasted away , till , when Peggy 's father succeeded to the estate , he found it to consist of scarcely more than the old house with the surrounding farm and woodlands , together with such a multitude of debts , mortgages , and other encumbrances , that it was truly a barren heritage", "context_len": 62}}}, "harvesting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "What a pleasant ride that was : out of the field where the bars had been let down ; past other fields ready , or nearly ready , for the harvesting ; pale green oats , and golden wheat , the white , sweet-scented buckwheat , and the tall Indian corn ; then through the orchard where a flock of sheep were feeding , past the locust grove , and then into the farmyard ; stopping at last between the open doors of the great barn !", "context_len": 87}}}, "disciplinarian": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Aunt Helen fondly imagined herself to be a great disciplinarian , but her own lively youth was still such a recent remembrance that her eyes were wont to twinkle and the corners of her mouth to twitch in the middle of her severest scoldings , and the children always had a feeling that so long as they did not do anything rude or wrong , or run into any very imminent danger , their escapades were secretly condoned by their aunt , who admired pluck and spirit , however much she might feel it incumbent upon her to lecture them", "context_len": 100}}}, "severest": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 42, "context": "Aunt Helen fondly imagined herself to be a great disciplinarian , but her own lively youth was still such a recent remembrance that her eyes were wont to twinkle and the corners of her mouth to twitch in the middle of her severest scoldings , and the children always had a feeling that so long as they did not do anything rude or wrong , or run into any very imminent danger , their escapades were secretly condoned by their aunt , who admired pluck and spirit , however much she might feel it incumbent upon her to lecture them", "context_len": 100}}}, "escapades": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 74, "context": "Aunt Helen fondly imagined herself to be a great disciplinarian , but her own lively youth was still such a recent remembrance that her eyes were wont to twinkle and the corners of her mouth to twitch in the middle of her severest scoldings , and the children always had a feeling that so long as they did not do anything rude or wrong , or run into any very imminent danger , their escapades were secretly condoned by their aunt , who admired pluck and spirit , however much she might feel it incumbent upon her to lecture them", "context_len": 100}}}, "condoned": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 77, "context": "Aunt Helen fondly imagined herself to be a great disciplinarian , but her own lively youth was still such a recent remembrance that her eyes were wont to twinkle and the corners of her mouth to twitch in the middle of her severest scoldings , and the children always had a feeling that so long as they did not do anything rude or wrong , or run into any very imminent danger , their escapades were secretly condoned by their aunt , who admired pluck and spirit , however much she might feel it incumbent upon her to lecture them", "context_len": 100}}}, "unflagging": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "She had always a hundred projects on hand , each one of which she was ready to pursue with unflagging zeal and that absorbing interest which is the secret of true enjoyment", "context_len": 32}}}, "outlived": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 112, "context": "Happily both her father and Aunt Helen shared his views , and , knowing Peggy 's generous , affectionate nature , were able to lead her more by love than severity ( for with human hearts it is often like the fable of the sun and the wind : they will respond to a kindly touch , while harshness will only make them sullen and obstinate ) , and they further held the opinion that it is better for a child to have many interests and much energy , even though these qualities prove a little troublesome , than to grow up clipped to the prim pattern of those who may have outlived their enthusiasms", "context_len": 115}}}, "stale": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Such natures as Peggy 's taste life to the full ; for them it is never a stale or worthless draught", "context_len": 21}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "Peggy came to the end of her book at last , and life seemed so stale and flat anywhere out of the South Seas that she wandered down the garden for a little diversion", "context_len": 34}}}, "bluest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Below them the pink apple-blossom spread like a rosy cloud against the bluest of skies , and a blackbird in a neighbouring bush was trilling his loudest", "context_len": 27}}}, "trilling": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "It was a glorious morning , with the larks singing overhead and the thrushes trilling in the bushes , and that delicious smell of the earth which we often notice in early spring , and which makes the blood run through our veins like rising sap", "context_len": 46}}}, "loudest": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 19, "context": "It was pleasant work wandering among the green meadows picking the sweet-smelling flowers , while the larks sang their loudest overhead , and the little brook babbled by on its path to the river -- more especially pleasant when they remembered that by this time next week school would have begun again , with its attendant woes of Latin grammar and French composition", "context_len": 63}}}, "easter": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "Easter was here at last , and down at the Willows Archie had come home like the breath of spring , Miss Forster declaring that he did her more good than all her medicine bottles , and that his lively ways would make her almost her usual self again , while at the Abbey he had a royal welcome", "context_len": 59}}}, "gander": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "' I do n't know , ' replied Peggy , idly picking off bits of twig , and throwing them at the old gander , which had strayed underneath", "context_len": 29}}}, "cuckoo": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 15, "context": "The trees were clothed with the pale , tender green of April , and a cuckoo , the first of the year , flew out of the copse , and , cuckooing loudly , sped over to where the larch-trees were bursting out into a crowd of tassels", "context_len": 48}}}, "cuckoos": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "I 'm sure we ought to get one about here : I 've heard such lots of cuckoos lately", "context_len": 19}}}, "tomboys": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "People did n't call Joan of Arc and Grace Darling tomboys , though they did other things besides stay at home and darn stockings", "context_len": 24}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "I am thankful to say none of my girls are tomboys ! If you will take my advice , Miss Vaughan , you will urge your brother to see at once about getting a good , strict governess to take charge of these children when you leave", "context_len": 47}}}, "dubbing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 70, "context": "To do Peggy justice , I think she was right , for though she delighted in outdoor life , she was in no sense a rough or ill-mannered child , and loved pretty things and dainty ways as well as quieter Lilian ; but it was a case of a dog with a bad name , for however indignantly she might remonstrate , people had got into the habit of dubbing her 'tomboy , ' and at that valuation she seemed likely to remain", "context_len": 84}}}, "valuation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 78, "context": "To do Peggy justice , I think she was right , for though she delighted in outdoor life , she was in no sense a rough or ill-mannered child , and loved pretty things and dainty ways as well as quieter Lilian ; but it was a case of a dog with a bad name , for however indignantly she might remonstrate , people had got into the habit of dubbing her 'tomboy , ' and at that valuation she seemed likely to remain", "context_len": 84}}}, "undulating": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "The walk which Bobby had proposed this afternoon was somewhat of a scramble , for the country rose behind the Abbey into undulating hills , which were fairly steep , though not so high as the Welsh mountains , and were covered for the most part with gorse and rough grass , where the sheep and young bullocks were turned out to graze", "context_len": 63}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "He was coursing over the undulating ground , when his gaze rested on a building half a mile away and in the line of Dick \u2019 s run", "context_len": 28}}}, "toboggan": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 59, "context": "At a turn of the road a great slab of Welsh slatestone lay at a sloping angle , shelving down for a distance of about twenty feet , and with its surface so flat and even , and so smooth and polished by the weather , that it made a natural sliding-board , down which it was delightful to toboggan at full speed", "context_len": 63}}}, "enthralling": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "Easter will soon be here , and Archie will come home , and then we shall all have glorious times again ! ' And Peggy 's momentary discontent vanished like snow in sunshine at the enthralling prospect of several new projects which her ingenious friend intended carrying out , and of the picnics , woodland scrambles , and other delights which the holidays would bring in their train", "context_len": 68}}}, "copse": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "One Saturday afternoon Peggy had had a long scramble through copse and bracken , over fences and along stubble fields , and had filled her big basket almost full , somewhat to the detriment of her costume and fingers , and she was now working her way home along the edge of the pasture , picking as she went", "context_len": 59}}}, "kingcups": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "'Let 's take the short cut , ' cried Bobby , hopping nimbly over the fence into the meadow , where the kingcups were lying , such a bright mass of gold in the sunshine that you might have thought the stars had fallen from the sky and were shining in the fields instead", "context_len": 54}}}, "scuttled": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "This was temper -- poor lad ! he had been so proud of himself with his boasting about what he would do if a wild animal appeared , and when it did come he had scuttled to the nearest shelter", "context_len": 40}}}, "pheasant": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Little rabbits scuttled away before them into the hedgerows , and a cock pheasant , disturbed in his afternoon nap , flew with a great whir into the coppice close by", "context_len": 31}}}, "cunningest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "A few minutes ' search resulted in a delighted 'S'sh ! ' from Bobby , for on a little ledge of rock under an overhanging tussock of grass was the cosiest , cunningest nest in the world , and the yellowhammer herself sat on it , looking at them with her bright little eyes , half undecided whether to stay or to fly away in alarm", "context_len": 66}}}, "swopped": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "I had five , but I swopped two of them with Frank Wilson for a redstart 's", "context_len": 17}}}, "redstart": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "I had five , but I swopped two of them with Frank Wilson for a redstart 's", "context_len": 17}}}, "tits": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "Oh , look , Bobby ! There goes one of the tits ! I saw just where she flew from", "context_len": 20}}}, "whew": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Whew ! is n't the grass scratchy on your legs ! '", "context_len": 12}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Whew ! It 's strong enough", "context_len": 6}}}, "prickles": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Prickles generally slept most of the day in a snug box lined with hay , but in the evenings he woke up , and Peggy would carry him into the kitchen , where he devoured black-beetles , much to his own delight and Nancy 's satisfaction", "context_len": 46}}}, "lichens": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "The tiny window was hardly a foot square , and had no glass in it , and a beautiful root of parsley fern grew luxuriantly on the sill , while all the crevices of the walls seemed full of lichens , bound together by the crimson stems of the creeping pellitory", "context_len": 51}}}, "builds": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "It was made of the softest moss and lichens , fashioned together in the shape of a bottle with the neck downwards ; for the tit must have some place in which to bestow her long tail , and she builds her home to suit her person", "context_len": 47}}}, "declensions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Joe had certainly not misled them , and Bobby 's memory , though defective as regarded Latin declensions and historical facts , was unerring where it was a case of locating birds'-nests", "context_len": 32}}}, "locating": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "Bart could see by their gestures that they were animatedly discussing the situation , and they seemed to be closely looking over the ground with a view to locating a site for a new express shed", "context_len": 36}}}, "chaffinch": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "He found three thrushes ' nests low down in the elder-bushes , all filled with gaping yellow mouths , the pretty little chaffinch 's up in the ivy-tree , with only two speckly eggs as yet , and Jenny Wren 's household , hidden away in a bank , full of so many children that she surely resembled the old woman who lived in a shoe , and it was a marvel how she could remember which little chirping atom she had fed last", "context_len": 84}}}, "prepares": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "' '' In April the cuckoo comes , In May she 'll stay , In June she changes her tune , In July she prepares to fly , Come August , go she must , '' '", "context_len": 37}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "-- Alexander prepares to march east", "context_len": 6}}}, "ornithology": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 64, "context": "There seemed little doubt about it , for the egg really looked quite different to the others ; so the treasured find was safely put away in the small box , to be shown to Joe , who was wise in such lore , though he only knew the birds by their country names , and had never heard of such a science as ornithology", "context_len": 65}}}, "slur": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "The slur stung Bomba , but he knew the importance of keeping his temper , and no change of expression was visible in his face", "context_len": 25}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "It won \u2019 t do you any good to slur Ben Stone , and I don \u2019 t think you \u2019 d better call him names before me", "context_len": 28}}}, "lotions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "People came to her from remote farms to have warts charmed away or the toothache cured ; she dressed burns and wounds , and concocted lotions for sore eyes and bad legs", "context_len": 32}}}, "concoction": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "She was bending over her iron pot now , stirring the concoction with a long-handled spoon", "context_len": 16}}}, "executing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "'You 'll do that without her if you try to swing head downwards on one leg like that , ' said Peggy ; for Bobby was executing some marvellous gymnastics on the top rail of the fence", "context_len": 37}}}, "gymnastics": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "'You 'll do that without her if you try to swing head downwards on one leg like that , ' said Peggy ; for Bobby was executing some marvellous gymnastics on the top rail of the fence", "context_len": 37}}}, "dipper": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 1, "context": "A dipper was hopping about from stone to stone in the centre of the stream , pruning her sleek feathers , and calling her lively 'chit , chit ' to her mate", "context_len": 32}}}, "pruning": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "A dipper was hopping about from stone to stone in the centre of the stream , pruning her sleek feathers , and calling her lively 'chit , chit ' to her mate", "context_len": 32}}}, "overhung": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 65, "context": "Around the southern side , and close to the mountain , which here rose still farther up , up , steep and rugged , to the Point , or Indian 's Head , wound the road ; and a dangerous road it looked , with the deep waters of the lake on one side , the rough mountain on the other where the huge boulders overhung the travellers as they passed on", "context_len": 72}}}, "cowslips": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "After the adventure at the mill-wheel , Aunt Helen , judging wisely that 'Satan finds some mischief still for idle hands to do , ' sent the children into the fields with Lilian to gather cowslips to make cowslip beer", "context_len": 40}}}, "bakehouse": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "' I 'm sure we must have enough now , ' said Lilian , turning out her sixteenth basket of blossoms into the ever-increasing pile in the bakehouse", "context_len": 28}}}, "pips": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "'It will take a fearfully long time to pick them all , ' remarked Peggy , starting bravely to work on her task of pulling the yellow pips away from the green calyces", "context_len": 33}}}, "calyces": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "'It will take a fearfully long time to pick them all , ' remarked Peggy , starting bravely to work on her task of pulling the yellow pips away from the green calyces", "context_len": 33}}}, "unromantic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "'You wo n't say so when you come to taste it , ' said unromantic Bobby , who was fond of cool , fizzy drinks in summer", "context_len": 27}}}, "fizzy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "'You wo n't say so when you come to taste it , ' said unromantic Bobby , who was fond of cool , fizzy drinks in summer", "context_len": 27}}}, "pinkest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "It had the brightest of brown eyes , the pinkest of tongues , and the shrillest of barks , and it was altogether such a dear , enchanting , soft , curly morsel of puppyhood that Peggy took it to her arms and her heart at the same moment", "context_len": 49}}}, "shrillest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "It had the brightest of brown eyes , the pinkest of tongues , and the shrillest of barks , and it was altogether such a dear , enchanting , soft , curly morsel of puppyhood that Peggy took it to her arms and her heart at the same moment", "context_len": 49}}}, "granary": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "Peggy often laughed afterwards when she remembered how she escorted the pair up the rickety steps into the granary , and prevented them from falling through the trap-door into the stable below , and guided them between the sacks of grain in the dark loft , where , flinging open the wooden shutter , she let in a stream of sunlight , and disclosed to view the ancient chest", "context_len": 69}}}, "hedgehog": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Then there was Prickles , a bright little hedgehog , which Peggy had rescued from some village boys who were using the poor little fellow as a football", "context_len": 28}}}, "mewing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 54, "context": "The children had made a nest of grass for him inside a basket , and fed him on worms and scraps of raw meat until he was old enough to fly , when he would follow them everywhere about the farmyard and outbuildings , calling 'Jack , Jack ! ' which , with the mewing of a cat , the gobble of the old turkey-cock , and a close imitation of David 's winter cough , made up the extent of his accomplishments", "context_len": 83}}}, "purloined": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "He had purloined David 's scarf from the saddle-room , and dropped it into the horse-trough , had filled Bobby 's hat with pebbles , and devoured the queen-cakes which Lilian had placed on the kitchen window-sill to cool ; he had snatched Joe 's breast-pin -- a glittering imitation diamond -- from his Sunday tie , under the very nose of that injured youth , and had stolen so many small articles that if anything were missing the children would have a grand search for Master Jack 's hiding-place , and would generally turn out the lost treasure from among an odd collection of trifles -- scraps of bright-coloured rags , bits of broken glass , hairpins , pen-nibs , pencil-ends , together with pieces of bread", "context_len": 128}}}, "hairpins": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 95, "context": "with pebbles , and devoured the queen-cakes which Lilian had placed on the kitchen window-sill to cool ; he had snatched Joe 's breast-pin -- a glittering imitation diamond -- from his Sunday tie , under the very nose of that injured youth , and had stolen so many small articles that if anything were missing the children would have a grand search for Master Jack 's hiding-place , and would generally turn out the lost treasure from among an odd collection of trifles -- scraps of bright-coloured rags , bits of broken glass , hairpins , pen-nibs , pencil-ends , together with pieces of bread and half-picked bones which the thief had concealed in some cunning corner inside a manger or under the roof of the loft", "context_len": 128}}}, "whinnying": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "Then there was Pixie , the pony , who would come whinnying up from the further side of a field to poke her soft nose into the children 's pockets for pieces of bread or lumps of sugar ; there were numerous cats who lived in the barns and stables , and Tabbyskins , the stately gray Persian , who usually sat sunning herself on the pigsty wall , keeping a strict eye on naughty Jack , who was wont to harry her if he got the chance", "context_len": 88}}}, "bantams": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Bobby had a pretty set of bantams , whose small eggs afforded him much delight and some slight profit , for Aunt Helen bought them from him at threepence a dozen -- a transaction which he always recorded in chalk upon the hen-house door , the pennies being carefully put by towards the purchase of a pair of fantail pigeons , which was at present the summit of his ambition", "context_len": 70}}}, "fantail": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 58, "context": "Bobby had a pretty set of bantams , whose small eggs afforded him much delight and some slight profit , for Aunt Helen bought them from him at threepence a dozen -- a transaction which he always recorded in chalk upon the hen-house door , the pennies being carefully put by towards the purchase of a pair of fantail pigeons , which was at present the summit of his ambition", "context_len": 70}}}, "boredom": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "Aunt Helen was very long-suffering with respect to hobbies , for she rightly thought that the more a child 's life is filled with interests , the more chance it has of growing up an intelligent and broad-minded individual , and of escaping from that lethargy of boredom which swallows up the lives of many young people who ought to know better how to amuse themselves in God 's beautiful world", "context_len": 71}}}, "crayons": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Here was the old piano on which they practised , here the ink-pots and rulers for their home-lessons , with their paint-boxes , crayons , and drawing-books", "context_len": 27}}}, "moths": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "A cupboard in the corner was devoted to a kind of museum , where they kept their collection of birds ' eggs , a few butterflies , moths , and beetles , Lilian 's pressed wild-flowers , a box full of shells and fossils which they had brought home from their one never-to-be-forgotten visit to the seaside , a curious plaited basket filled with bone rings , shell bracelets , and other curiosities , sent by a sailor cousin from the South Sea Islands , and an odd assortment of stones , old coins , foreign stamps , crests and postmarks , which represented landmarks in the history of past fads", "context_len": 111}}}, "beetles": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "A cupboard in the corner was devoted to a kind of museum , where they kept their collection of birds ' eggs , a few butterflies , moths , and beetles , Lilian 's pressed wild-flowers , a box full of shells and fossils which they had brought home from their one never-to-be-forgotten visit to the seaside , a curious plaited basket filled with bone rings , shell bracelets , and other curiosities , sent by a sailor cousin from the South Sea Islands , and an odd assortment of stones , old coins , foreign stamps , crests and postmarks , which represented landmarks in the history of past fads", "context_len": 111}}}, "fossils": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "A cupboard in the corner was devoted to a kind of museum , where they kept their collection of birds ' eggs , a few butterflies , moths , and beetles , Lilian 's pressed wild-flowers , a box full of shells and fossils which they had brought home from their one never-to-be-forgotten visit to the seaside , a curious plaited basket filled with bone rings , shell bracelets , and other curiosities , sent by a sailor cousin from the South Sea Islands , and an odd assortment of stones , old coins , foreign stamps , crests and postmarks , which represented landmarks in the history of past fads", "context_len": 111}}}, "bracelets": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 68, "context": "A cupboard in the corner was devoted to a kind of museum , where they kept their collection of birds ' eggs , a few butterflies , moths , and beetles , Lilian 's pressed wild-flowers , a box full of shells and fossils which they had brought home from their one never-to-be-forgotten visit to the seaside , a curious plaited basket filled with bone rings , shell bracelets , and other curiosities , sent by a sailor cousin from the South Sea Islands , and an odd assortment of stones , old coins , foreign stamps , crests and postmarks , which represented landmarks in the history of past fads", "context_len": 111}}}, "postmarks": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 100, "context": "A cupboard in the corner was devoted to a kind of museum , where they kept their collection of birds ' eggs , a few butterflies , moths , and beetles , Lilian 's pressed wild-flowers , a box full of shells and fossils which they had brought home from their one never-to-be-forgotten visit to the seaside , a curious plaited basket filled with bone rings , shell bracelets , and other curiosities , sent by a sailor cousin from the South Sea Islands , and an odd assortment of stones , old coins , foreign stamps , crests and postmarks , which represented landmarks in the history of past fads", "context_len": 111}}}, "fads": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 110, "context": "A cupboard in the corner was devoted to a kind of museum , where they kept their collection of birds ' eggs , a few butterflies , moths , and beetles , Lilian 's pressed wild-flowers , a box full of shells and fossils which they had brought home from their one never-to-be-forgotten visit to the seaside , a curious plaited basket filled with bone rings , shell bracelets , and other curiosities , sent by a sailor cousin from the South Sea Islands , and an odd assortment of stones , old coins , foreign stamps , crests and postmarks , which represented landmarks in the history of past fads", "context_len": 111}}}, "chimneypiece": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "Lilian 's canary hung in the sunniest window , Peggy 's silkworms lay in a box on the sideboard , and Bobby 's white mice reposed in much comfort in a cage on the chimneypiece", "context_len": 35}}}, "ninepins": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 58, "context": "The walls were adorned with school drawings and prize maps , fastened up with tacks ; a bookcase of much-read volumes filled the space between the fireplace and the window ; and in the corner might be found a miscellaneous collection of cricket-bats , fishing-rods , tennis-racquets , bows and arrows , croquet-mallets , sticks , balls and ninepins , and other articles very dear to the children 's hearts , but which Nancy generally classed impartially under the head of 'lumber", "context_len": 82}}}, "cuddle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "'He 'll just cuddle down on the rug at the foot of my bed , and go to sleep like an angel ; I know he will ! '", "context_len": 29}}}, "hushing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "'He 's far better than a baby , ' declared Peggy , 'for he does n't want hushing and rocking , and he wo n't cry in the night", "context_len": 29}}}, "sportive": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "He heaved himself out of his wrappings , and with a delighted yelp fell upon Peggy 's curls , worrying them with little gasps of joy , till she had to dive beneath the bedclothes to escape her too sportive pet", "context_len": 41}}}, "tempestuous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Peggy was at her wits ' end , but she finally seized the tempestuous ball of fluff , and dropped him to the bottom of the empty clothes-basket", "context_len": 28}}}, "wickerwork": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "He could make such a noise , however , that he effectually banished slumber ; he twisted and turned , he bit at the wickerwork , and scratched with his claws , and after ten minutes of much commotion managed to tip over the basket and crawl out in triumph , to renew his attacks upon the shoes and other property of the despairing girls", "context_len": 65}}}, "bedfellow": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "Though she had not again tried him for a bedfellow , he was still the companion of her walks , and the most winsome playmate on wet days , and Peggy firmly believed that he understood every word she said to him", "context_len": 42}}}, "albatross": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "The body of the dead kitten was securely tied round his neck with a piece of rope , and , like the albatross in the ballad of the 'Ancient Mariner , ' it proved such a weight of shame that the guilty dog did not dare to show his face to his friends , but slunk away to hide his dishonoured head in dark corners of the barn or stackyard", "context_len": 70}}}, "nastiest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "All the nastiest lessons , Peggy thought , came on Mondays -- grammar and arithmetic , dates and French verbs , and all those horrid fussy things which take a great deal of learning without being specially interesting in themselves", "context_len": 40}}}, "classroom": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 9, "context": "It was unfortunate for Peggy that the third form classroom was furnished with double desks , for as Miss Crossland would permit no changing of places , she was obliged to sit by her enemy for the rest of the term , to their equal discomfort and annoyance", "context_len": 48}}}, "eyeglasses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Miss Crossland glared at her through her eyeglasses", "context_len": 8}}}, "schoolgirls": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 73, "context": "Her light flaxen hair and pale , fair complexion gave her a smooth , shining appearance , and somehow Peggy always thought her manners were smooth and shining too , for she had a way of wriggling out of any little difficulty and unpleasantness , so that the blame rested upon other people , and was always ready to take a mean advantage , or play some of those little underhand tricks which schoolgirls know only too well", "context_len": 78}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "They were a very jolly set of schoolgirls whom Aunt Helen came out on to the front steps to welcome", "context_len": 20}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Tea was a lively meal , for Father was full of jokes and funny stories , and Aunt Helen enjoyed schoolgirls ' society almost as much as the children themselves , while Nancy 's delicacies melted away like snow in summer", "context_len": 41}}}, "undeclared": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "Peggy 's frank , downright nature held Emily in much contempt , and , as she made no effort to conceal her opinion , the dislike was mutual , and a kind of undeclared war existed between the two", "context_len": 39}}}, "pointer": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "One daring spirit even mounted the platform , and waved the pointer in supposed imitation of Miss Crossland 's majestic style", "context_len": 21}}}, "lunchbox": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "'What made you so late , Peggy ? ' asked Nora Pemberton in the intervals of ecstatic delight over a white mouse , hidden away in a desk-mate 's lunchbox", "context_len": 30}}}, "shod": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "'Pixie lost a shoe , and we thought she would go lame , so we almost crawled along ; and when we got her in , we had to tell them to be sure and have her shod by four o'clock , and of course it all takes time", "context_len": 49}}}, "mistrust": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Miss Crossland looked at them suspiciously ; perhaps ten years of teaching had caused her to mistrust such amazing diligence", "context_len": 20}}}, "abstruse": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "Sissie Wilson bit the end of her pencil in abstruse calculation , Emily Thompson was deep in the pages of her arithmetic , while most of the girls were adding up columns as if for dear life", "context_len": 37}}}, "reproved": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 1, "context": "Leonnatus reproved him for this extravagance , and told him that when he became master of the countries where these costly gums were procured , he might be as prodigal of them as he pleased , but that in the mean time it would be proper for him to be more prudent and economical", "context_len": 54}}}, "speckled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 48, "context": "They spent quite a long time in the shooting-gallery , and won a cocoa-nut , which Bobby proudly carried round the fair , and they had their photographs taken in a group , but some air-bubbles having unfortunately got on to the plate , their countenances came out speckled as though they were suffering from a virulent attack of small-pox ; but Peggy kept it , all the same , as a memento of the occasion", "context_len": 76}}}, "outraged": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "The Danes , clad in black armour , with their ponderous battle-axes , and fierce visages , upon which no gentle ray of mercy had yet shone ; the English , their minds set upon avenging the outraged national honour , the desolated homes , the slaughtered families : the Danes bent on maintaining their cruel superiority ; the English bent on reversing it or dying : the Danes hitherto victorious on nearly every field ; the English turning upon their oppressors as men to whom the only thing which could make life tolerable was victory", "context_len": 96}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "He maintained that no one but Percy knew that he had taken the money in charge ; no one but Percy had any object in finding it , and he appeared and professed himself perfectly outraged that any one `` should have dared '' to open his trunk , bureau and so forth", "context_len": 53}}}, "sevenpence": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "A newspaper cost sevenpence ; there were no national schools or Sunday schools , no penny publications , no penny postage , no railways , no gas , and no free libraries , and no free education ! Yet so resolute was he in his desire for education that , though he was not even allowed a candle after the elders went to bed , he would sit up till late at night reading by the glow of the embers", "context_len": 80}}}, "droned": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Though inwardly raging , Peggy was forced to put on an outward appearance of submission and good behaviour , and the lessons droned on somehow until the morning was over", "context_len": 30}}}, "lamented": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 41, "context": "He was appointed by Dunstan , just then on the point of leaving England to escape the rage of the wicked and unhappy Edwy , and continued to exercise the authority until the year 975 , the year in which our lamented king , Edgar the Magnanimous , departed to his heavenly rest , with whose decease peace and prosperity seemed likewise to depart", "context_len": 64}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The body of the lamented monarch was laid in state in St", "context_len": 12}}}, "classrooms": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 47, "context": "After dinner one of the elder girls suggested rounders , and the game grew so enthralling that time flew by until the bell , ringing for afternoon school , sent the players , hot and rosy with their exertions , hurrying up the great staircase to their classrooms", "context_len": 48}}}, "compiled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "'This book of manuscript notes , which I had compiled myself from various sources , and valued greatly , I lent to be copied by the third form", "context_len": 28}}}, "convict": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "No other thought entered the head of the terrified Lewis than that Seabrooke himself had seen him at his shameful work , and that he had chosen to confront and convict him with it here in the presence of the rest of the school", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 74, "context": "She had a confused idea that Lilian was trying to come to her across a row of benches , and was being held back by a teacher ; but otherwise she scarcely knew what was happening , except that she seemed to be the centre for all the eight hundred eyes in the room , till Miss Pope took her by the shoulder and marched her away like a warder escorting a very small convict to gaol", "context_len": 77}}}, "discreditable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "That it should come to this -- that she , Peggy Vaughan , who , whatever might be her faults , had always held such an unstained reputation for honour and truthfulness , should be deemed capable of such a mean and discreditable action seemed too hard to be borne", "context_len": 50}}}, "outcast": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 38, "context": "Very precious it was , too , in the eyes of the earthly father , who watched the scene , and looking from his own tenderly cared for , daintily dressed darlings , to the forlorn , ragged outcast , thanked God that for all three alike had the blessed words been spoken , `` Suffer little children to come unto me", "context_len": 62}}}, "hooray": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "They think Emily Thompson was a sneak to go telling tales like that , without asking you first if you had really done it , and they 're so sorry for you that they say they 'll give you a `` hooray '' when you come out", "context_len": 47}}}, "yong": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "'As for ze ozer yong ladies , zey have no more musique in zem zan an old hurdy-gurdy", "context_len": 18}}}, "composers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "All zat vill please zem is a tune to make dance , but for ze vorks of ze best composers zey have no heart at all", "context_len": 26}}}, "operas": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 45, "context": "Lilian 's secret ambition was to go to Germany to study at the Leipzig Conservatoire , which Herr Frankenburg always described as the very home and abode of the spirit of music , and made her sadly envious with accounts of the wonderful concerts and operas which might be enjoyed in that favoured city , where the very street vendors would not be tolerated if they cried their wares out of tune", "context_len": 72}}}, "healthiest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "Rollo had been newly washed and combed , the rabbits ' hutch had been scrubbed , the arbour swept out , the museum tidied , and Nancy had baked a supply of cakes and tarts calculated to provide for the healthiest appetites", "context_len": 42}}}, "racy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Susie and Mary Hirst were the daughters of a rising Warford physician , and were pleasant and amusing , though they had not the racy humour of Dorothy Gower", "context_len": 29}}}, "stacked": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "They managed to construct a broom from some of the longest pieces of heather , and swept the crumbs neatly out at the front-door ; they hung up the frying-pan , the kettle , and the bellows in their accustomed places , and stacked the cups and plates in the old box which served as a cupboard", "context_len": 57}}}, "bike": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "'Perhaps not , but you could n't bike to school in the pouring rain or snow", "context_len": 16}}}, "ottoman": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "'What 's this ? ' asked Mary , hunting through a pile of old music inside the ottoman , and fishing out a manuscript page in Lilian 's neat hand", "context_len": 30}}}, "shoo": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 0, "context": "SHOO ! PICCANINNIES", "context_len": 3}}}, "piccaninnies": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 12, "context": "'Way down in Georgia where de sugar am grow , All de piccaninnies want to suck de canes , you know ! An ' dey 're hidin ' round an ' peepin ' , like de 'possum all de day , Till Uncle Sambo bring his stick an ' chase 'em all away !", "context_len": 54}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "stews": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "When de kitchen-fire am blazin ' , an ' Aunt Dinah stews an ' bakes , All de piccaninnies gather just to smell her pies an ' cakes ; An ' dey cluster round de window like de bees upon a comb , Till Auntie Dinah she get mad , an ' turn an ' drive 'em home ! Shoo , shoo ! piccaninnies , etc", "context_len": 66}}}, "bakes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "When de kitchen-fire am blazin ' , an ' Aunt Dinah stews an ' bakes , All de piccaninnies gather just to smell her pies an ' cakes ; An ' dey cluster round de window like de bees upon a comb , Till Auntie Dinah she get mad , an ' turn an ' drive 'em home ! Shoo , shoo ! piccaninnies , etc", "context_len": 66}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Mother takes the biggest dish in the house and bakes a smashing great pie", "context_len": 14}}}, "winningly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "' I thought you might like some chocolates , ' he said winningly , handing a noble box to Mary Hirst with an air of much generosity", "context_len": 27}}}, "schoolgirl": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Accustomed to little quiet schoolgirl parties , she had not thought there would be such a large and fashionable assembly of guests , and had hoped that she would have her dear Maud to herself for a short time , at any rate , and be shown the doves , and the white pony , and the Indian cabinet , and the other treasures which her friend had so often described to her during the dancing-class", "context_len": 76}}}, "glibly": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "'That 's your own , Lilian , ' said the astute Kathleen , 'for you said the writing was hard to make out , and yet you read it straight off , quite glibly", "context_len": 34}}}, "respirator": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "'There was an old German named Fr\u00fchl , Who a respirator wore as a rule", "context_len": 15}}}, "muzzled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "You knew your dogs were vicious , and you have been notified by the authorities to chain them up and never to let them loose unless they were muzzled", "context_len": 29}}}, "bronchitis": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "This proved a favourite , for poor Herr Fr\u00fchl , the German master , was famous for his bronchitis and his bad temper , and the general opinion ascribed the authorship to Dorothy , though she would not acknowledge her laurels", "context_len": 41}}}, "riband": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "'Is it a red riband , then , ye 'll be after askin ' me to wear ? ' inquired the indignant young Irishwoman", "context_len": 24}}}, "cess": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "'It 's the shade of the tyrant , bad cess to it ! and do n't suit me complexion neither", "context_len": 20}}}, "slander": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "' A vile slander ! And if I discover the authorship , I 'll bring an action for libel", "context_len": 19}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Did he dare to slander me in that way ? '' demanded Florence , so indignantly that her jailer drew back in some alarm", "context_len": 25}}}, "libel": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "' A vile slander ! And if I discover the authorship , I 'll bring an action for libel", "context_len": 19}}}, "retentive": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Still he survived his river catastrophe ; and , though he gained little book learning , possessed such a good and retentive memory , and was so observant , that his mind became stored with vivid impressions of the scenes and surroundings of his youth , which he related with great effect in after-life", "context_len": 54}}}, "prep": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "[ Footnote 15 : Galland died in 1715 , leaving the last two volumes of his translation ( which appear by the Diary to have been ready for the prep on the 8th June , 1713 ) to be published in 1717", "context_len": 42}}}, "almshouses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "He was past work now , and lived in one of the village almshouses , subsisting partly on the parish dole and partly on private charity ; for though Mr", "context_len": 30}}}, "subsisting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "He was past work now , and lived in one of the village almshouses , subsisting partly on the parish dole and partly on private charity ; for though Mr", "context_len": 30}}}, "dole": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "He was past work now , and lived in one of the village almshouses , subsisting partly on the parish dole and partly on private charity ; for though Mr", "context_len": 30}}}, "gradely": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "'Better , Miss Peggy -- gradely better since I 've kept a potato in my pocket", "context_len": 16}}}, "parables": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "'It 's about something else , too , ' replied Peggy : 'miracles and parables and epistles , and -- oh ! lots of things", "context_len": 25}}}, "ministering": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "'Perhaps I might read you something else , then ? ' volunteered Peggy , determined to be a ministering angel , despite the evident unwillingness of her prot\u00e9g\u00e9", "context_len": 28}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "He studied the language of the tribes amongst whom he was ministering ; and soon the people were able to sing in their own tongue , `` There is a fountain filled with blood , '' `` Jesus shall reign where'er the sun , '' and other beautiful hymns which delight the hearts of those in our own land", "context_len": 59}}}, "prot\u00e9g\u00e9": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 57, "context": "Divested of his disguise , no longer a fear-filled roustabout fugitive , Bart 's strange friend had found a steady , lucrative position at the hotel , and Bart felt that he had certainly been the means of doing some real good in the world every time he looked at the happy , contented face of his prot\u00e9g\u00e9", "context_len": 58}}}, "socialist": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Peggy turned out the drawer by the simple process of emptying it on the table , and disclosed a very miscellaneous collection of literature -- socialist pamphlets , agnostic newspapers , and radical tracts were mixed up with teetotal treatises , missionary leaflets , and the parish magazine", "context_len": 48}}}, "agnostic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Peggy turned out the drawer by the simple process of emptying it on the table , and disclosed a very miscellaneous collection of literature -- socialist pamphlets , agnostic newspapers , and radical tracts were mixed up with teetotal treatises , missionary leaflets , and the parish magazine", "context_len": 48}}}, "teetotal": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "Peggy turned out the drawer by the simple process of emptying it on the table , and disclosed a very miscellaneous collection of literature -- socialist pamphlets , agnostic newspapers , and radical tracts were mixed up with teetotal treatises , missionary leaflets , and the parish magazine", "context_len": 48}}}, "treatises": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "Peggy turned out the drawer by the simple process of emptying it on the table , and disclosed a very miscellaneous collection of literature -- socialist pamphlets , agnostic newspapers , and radical tracts were mixed up with teetotal treatises , missionary leaflets , and the parish magazine", "context_len": 48}}}, "leaflets": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "Peggy turned out the drawer by the simple process of emptying it on the table , and disclosed a very miscellaneous collection of literature -- socialist pamphlets , agnostic newspapers , and radical tracts were mixed up with teetotal treatises , missionary leaflets , and the parish magazine", "context_len": 48}}}, "almanac": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Sheets of ballads , which Ephraim had bought as a boy , lay side by side with a tattered copy or two of Zadkiel 's prophetic almanac , some advertisements of patent medicines , a recipe for sheep-dip , and a Wesleyan hymn-book", "context_len": 43}}}, "woodcuts": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "Peggy gazed eagerly at an ancient chap-book , which set forth the stories of Dick Turpin and Jack Sheppard , interspersed with rude woodcuts of the gallows and whipping-post ; but she heroically put it aside , as being unsuitable for the day", "context_len": 43}}}, "gentlewomen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "She was one of Nature 's gentlewomen , for her kind heart prompted those little gracious , courteous acts which in a higher class we call good breeding", "context_len": 28}}}, "framing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "She made quite a picture in her short linsey-woolsey petticoat , with the check apron , her plaid shawl crossed over her cotton bodice , and the frilled white cap framing the kind old face , with its apple cheeks and soft white hair", "context_len": 44}}}, "hives": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 24, "context": "' I suppose not , ' said Peggy , settling herself on a low wooden seat , at a safe distance from the agitated hives , and letting her glance wander round the little garden , where the tall yellow lilies reared their stately heads over a mass of sweet cottage flowers , pinks and forget-me-nots , poppies and double daisies , sweet-williams -- loved of the bees -- pansies , lupins , and snap-dragons ; over the cottage , where the white roses climbed up the thatch to the very chimneys , and where through the open doorway could be seen the neat kitchen , with its red-brick floor , the settle placed by the fireside , the tall grandfather 's clock ticking away in the corner", "context_len": 128}}}, "poppies": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 57, "context": "' I suppose not , ' said Peggy , settling herself on a low wooden seat , at a safe distance from the agitated hives , and letting her glance wander round the little garden , where the tall yellow lilies reared their stately heads over a mass of sweet cottage flowers , pinks and forget-me-nots , poppies and double daisies , sweet-williams -- loved of the bees -- pansies , lupins , and snap-dragons ; over the cottage , where the white roses climbed up the thatch to the very chimneys , and where through the open doorway could be seen the neat kitchen , with its red-brick floor , the settle placed by the fireside , the tall grandfather 's clock ticking away in the corner", "context_len": 128}}}, "lupins": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 64, "context": "Peggy , settling herself on a low wooden seat , at a safe distance from the agitated hives , and letting her glance wander round the little garden , where the tall yellow lilies reared their stately heads over a mass of sweet cottage flowers , pinks and forget-me-nots , poppies and double daisies , sweet-williams -- loved of the bees -- pansies , lupins , and snap-dragons ; over the cottage , where the white roses climbed up the thatch to the very chimneys , and where through the open doorway could be seen the neat kitchen , with its red-brick floor , the settle placed by the fireside , the tall grandfather 's clock ticking away in the corner , and the oak dresser , with", "context_len": 128}}}, "stopper": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Davis sat with her grandchild by her knee , a small round-eyed boy , whose thumb was stuck perpetually , like a stopper , in his mouth , and who stood watching the bees with stolid indifference", "context_len": 37}}}, "davis": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "Davis does", "context_len": 2}}}, "solacing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "Willie did not look enthusiastic about the attractions of the flowers , but he allowed himself to be led away , staring at his new guardian with round eyes of solemn distrust , and solacing himself with his thumb", "context_len": 39}}}, "dada": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "My dada says he do n't hold with folks as ca n't keep the Sabbath holy", "context_len": 16}}}, "snowdrops": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "'Oh yes , for I looked at the end chapter , and she dies beautiful , and they plant snowdrops on her grave ; and her big brother , what 's so unkind to her , gets drowned through goin ' boatin ' on Sunday , ' replied Polly , regarding Peggy as if she thought her courses might lead her to a similar watery fate", "context_len": 66}}}, "tinkling": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 52, "context": "It was very still and peaceful in there , and she liked to sit and think how the Abbey must have looked in those strange , bygone days when the little steps had led to a dormitory , and the broken pillars had held up the roof of a church , whose tinkling bell had rung out at sunset , calling to prayer those old monks who slept so quietly in their forgotten graves", "context_len": 74}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Hush ! Speak not , Bartow , or you will break the spell and shatter it into a thousand tinkling fragments", "context_len": 21}}}, "shopkeeper": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "Having made the purchase at last , he was on the point of leaving when the shopkeeper said :", "context_len": 19}}}, "anomaly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "They had few invitations , for there was little visiting now at the Abbey ; the anomaly of a country gentleman who ploughed his own fields was felt to be too much for the neighbouring squires , and one by one the 'county ' had ceased to call", "context_len": 48}}}, "winnower": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Truly adversity is a great winnower of friendships", "context_len": 8}}}, "chaff": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "It is but the staunchest who will stick to us through our troubles , while those who love us for what we have , instead of what we are , fall away like chaff at the first breath of ill-fortune", "context_len": 40}}}, "sprigged": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "She took it very patiently , poor child ! and came downstairs to see the others start off in the little pony-trap , Bobby resplendent in a clean white sailor suit , and Peggy in a pale-blue sprigged muslin dress , which Aunt Helen had toiled hard to finish in time", "context_len": 51}}}, "parasols": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 55, "context": "There were gentlemen , young , old and middle-aged , some in tourist tweeds , some in boating flannels , and some in sober black , most of them with books or road-maps in their hands , while a sprinkling of ladies , both grave and frivolous , in light summer costumes and with gay parasols , completed the party", "context_len": 60}}}, "middleton": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Middleton", "context_len": 1}}}, "homemade": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "The Vaughans had not been brought up to think much about clothes , but Peggy felt suddenly , with a little pang , that the muslin frock , which they had all thought so pretty when she started off , looked hopelessly homemade and unfashionable compared with the elaborate toilets of most of the other guests", "context_len": 56}}}, "unfashionable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "The Vaughans had not been brought up to think much about clothes , but Peggy felt suddenly , with a little pang , that the muslin frock , which they had all thought so pretty when she started off , looked hopelessly homemade and unfashionable compared with the elaborate toilets of most of the other guests", "context_len": 56}}}, "toilets": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "The Vaughans had not been brought up to think much about clothes , but Peggy felt suddenly , with a little pang , that the muslin frock , which they had all thought so pretty when she started off , looked hopelessly homemade and unfashionable compared with the elaborate toilets of most of the other guests", "context_len": 56}}}, "estimating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Mademoiselle was dealing out the mallets and arranging the sides with much energy , estimating the players with a keen eye to their possible capacities", "context_len": 25}}}, "zen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Tiens ! You have missed ! It is zen ze turn of Mees Marguerite", "context_len": 14}}}, "pegged": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "Peggy 's next piece of play was so brilliant that it decided the game , and , to the great delight of her little partners , they all pegged out , amid the cold applause of their opponents and the ecstatic admiration of Mademoiselle", "context_len": 44}}}, "conservatories": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "Peggy forebore to remark that it was not her fault , and , cheering up a little , she joined the select circle whom Maud was conducting through the greenhouses and conservatories", "context_len": 32}}}, "puss": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "But Carlo 's mistress had not calculated upon his love of sport , for no sooner did the naughty dog see the white kitten than he simply went for it , and puss only saved her life by springing up a yew-tree close by", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "She was up the yew-tree in two leaps and a bound , and by crawling along an overhanging bough , clinging to a branch , and making a long arm , she managed to seize puss by the scruff of her neck , and release her from her dangerous position", "context_len": 50}}}, "neville": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Neville ! ' exclaimed Maud", "context_len": 5}}}, "mooning": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "'The boys playing cricket by themselves , and the girls mooning about , keeping their frocks nice ? Scandalous ! Come along , all of you , and we 'll get up some mixed sports", "context_len": 35}}}, "organizer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Neville was a capital organizer , and had such a delightful way with children that they all made friends with him in five minutes", "context_len": 24}}}, "impounded": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "He impounded the grown-ups in the sports , and his jolly , hearty good spirits seemed so infectious that almost before they realized what they were doing , elegant ladies were holding up skipping-ropes , and smart young gentlemen flung off their coats , and forgot their high collars in the enthusiasm of the contests", "context_len": 55}}}, "handicaps": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Long jumps , high jumps , handicaps , sack-races , three-legged races -- she was ready to compete in all , and , to her delight , won several prizes , while Bobby , too , gained his share of laurels", "context_len": 41}}}, "gushing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "Middleton , gushing over her small guest , now she found she was a success", "context_len": 15}}}, "raspberries": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "My dear child , do n't you think you might choose a less juicy seat than a tray full of raspberries ? '", "context_len": 23}}}, "unharness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "' I 'll go and help Lilian unharness Pixie", "context_len": 9}}}, "entomology": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Bobby had taken to entomology , and panted over the hot pastures , chasing butterflies with unflagging zeal", "context_len": 18}}}, "teacup": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "' I found one of your beasties inside my hat , and another in my teacup , ' she complained", "context_len": 20}}}, "masterpieces": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 66, "context": "To be sure , the perspective of her cottages was apt to be rather peculiar at times , and the Welsh mountains turned out a more vivid shade of purple , and the fields a far more brilliant green than Nature ever painted them ; but it was all good practice , and the admiring Peggy thought that no Royal Academician could have produced such charming masterpieces", "context_len": 67}}}, "playtime": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "There was a little work , too , to make the playtime all the sweeter -- fruit to be picked , peas and beans to shell , the garden to weed , and great piles of bread and cheese to be cut and carried out into the fields for the harvesters ' 'drinkings", "context_len": 53}}}, "harvesters": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 50, "context": "There was a little work , too , to make the playtime all the sweeter -- fruit to be picked , peas and beans to shell , the garden to weed , and great piles of bread and cheese to be cut and carried out into the fields for the harvesters ' 'drinkings", "context_len": 53}}}, "hairbreadth": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "' It was rather a grown-up book , perhaps , for a little girl , but it was all about pirates , and sailors , and hairbreadth escapes , of so wildly exciting a nature , that she read on till she almost wept with disappointment to think she was not a boy to go to sea and meet with such thrilling adventures", "context_len": 63}}}, "melancholics": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Lilian had always taken rather a delight in what Nancy called 'the melancholics", "context_len": 13}}}, "proclaiming": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 64, "context": ", making friends with them and questioning them of buying and selling and giving and taking and dear and cheap , till , one day of the days , he arose in the morning and donning his clothes , went forth , intending , as of wont , for the jewellers ' market ; but , as he went , he heard the crier proclaiming aloud on this wise , `` By commandment of the Lord of Beneficence , the king of the age and monarch of the time and the tide , let all the folk shut their shops and stores and enter their houses , for that the Lady Bedrulbudour , daughter of the Sultan , purposeth to go to the bath , and whoso transgresseth", "context_len": 128}}}, "patriarch": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "'Let him alone , you naughty boy ! Whatever mischief will you be in next ? ' cried Aunt Helen , flying to the rescue of the patriarch of her poultry-yard , and enforcing her remarks by sounding raps on the culprit 's curly brown pate", "context_len": 46}}}, "enforcing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 47, "context": "Instead of doing either of these things , Alexander decided on the very bold step of setting out himself , at the head of an army , to march into southern Greece , for the purpose of presenting in person , and , if necessary , of enforcing his claim to the same post of honor and power which had been conferred upon his father", "context_len": 65}}}, "drinkings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "'We 're baking again to-day , but the harvesters take so much bread for the `` drinkings", "context_len": 17}}}, "carpeted": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 49, "context": "A rapid stream ran through the woods , dashing down over great boulders , making little cascades and waterfalls as it went , with here and there a deep , clear pool , where the trout lay snugly under the stones ; the rocks under the overhanging trees were carpeted with the softest and greenest of moss , and tall ferns grew right down to the water 's edge , mingling with trailing ivy and creeping moneywort", "context_len": 77}}}, "moneywort": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 76, "context": "A rapid stream ran through the woods , dashing down over great boulders , making little cascades and waterfalls as it went , with here and there a deep , clear pool , where the trout lay snugly under the stones ; the rocks under the overhanging trees were carpeted with the softest and greenest of moss , and tall ferns grew right down to the water 's edge , mingling with trailing ivy and creeping moneywort", "context_len": 77}}}, "smocked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "A pair of stout brown legs showed under the smocked holland pinafore , her white sun-hat hung upon her back , and she clasped a dilapidated doll in her arms", "context_len": 30}}}, "appraising": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "It made Lilian 's blood boil to see him appraising the old oak , examining the curtains and carpets , and taking notes of the pictures and books", "context_len": 28}}}, "thirsting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Slowly , slowly the words dropped from her lips ; then came one or two fluttering sighs ; and a little ransomed soul , thirsting for the water of life , had flown away , and was safe within the bosom of Him who has said , `` Suffer little children to come unto me", "context_len": 55}}}, "outweigh": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 45, "context": "At one time he received a long letter from Antipater , full of complaints against her ; but Alexander , after reading it , said that they were heavy charges it was true , but that a single one of his mother 's tears would outweigh ten thousand such accusations", "context_len": 50}}}, "swilling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "We can leave our things here , under the tree , till we come back , ' said Peggy , hastily swilling the teacups in the stream and packing them up in the basket", "context_len": 34}}}, "teacups": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 58, "context": "Father and Bobby were asleep in two minutes , but Peggy lay awake for a long time , watching the shadows of the peat fire flicker upon the rough beams of the roof , till at length fire and heather merged into a dreamland kingdom , where she was walking with Rollo upon the clouds , and fishing teacups out of a flowing sea below", "context_len": 65}}}, "solving": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "But Lilian popped down on the grass , and pulled off her shoes and stockings as the most speedy way of solving the difficulty , and hustled Miss Peggy home with all possible haste , to be dried ; for the water was very cold , and her teeth were chattering in her head , while her dripping garments left such a moist track as she went that Bobby unfeelingly compared her to a watering-cart", "context_len": 75}}}, "unfeelingly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 69, "context": "But Lilian popped down on the grass , and pulled off her shoes and stockings as the most speedy way of solving the difficulty , and hustled Miss Peggy home with all possible haste , to be dried ; for the water was very cold , and her teeth were chattering in her head , while her dripping garments left such a moist track as she went that Bobby unfeelingly compared her to a watering-cart", "context_len": 75}}}, "bairns": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 54, "context": "Poor Aunt Helen was torn in two between the lover of her youth and the children of her adoption ; but I really believe , when she saw Bobby 's tears , that if Father had not put his foot down firmly , she would have thrown up everything , and clung to her bairns", "context_len": 55}}}, "infold": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "Hungry , and chilly from the damp moisture that oozed down the walls , the poor children sat quietly huddled together watching the cold gray surface of the water , which seemed like some cruel monster ever creeping nearer and nearer to infold them in its treacherous grasp", "context_len": 48}}}, "banisters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "'Father , where are you going ? ' cried Peggy one morning , sliding down the banisters in her hurry to see why her father was pulling out his great fishing-basket from the cupboard under the stairs", "context_len": 37}}}, "beguiling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "'Oh , Father ! ' cried Peggy , flinging her arms round his neck in her most beguiling manner , ' couldn't you take Bobby and me with you this time ? We have never been once yet , and you ca n't think how much we want to go", "context_len": 50}}}, "relent": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Vaughan doubtfully , beginning to relent to Peggy 's coaxings", "context_len": 10}}}, "devoutly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 54, "context": "'Oh , thank you ! Of course we 'll promise ! ' cried Peggy , clapping her hands , and flying off in the seventh heaven of joy to inform Bobby of the delightful prospect , nearly upsetting Nancy and the breakfast-tray in her mad career , and causing that worthy girl to wish devoutly that schools had no holidays", "context_len": 60}}}, "moorland": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "On and up , on and up , with the great blue hills always rising higher before them , till at length even the stone walls vanished , and they were on the bare moorland , with only a slight foot-track for a road", "context_len": 44}}}, "beeches": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Far in the distance , among the trees , the outline of the Abbey rose gray against the background of soft beeches ; a little dark cloud , the only one in the whole expanse of clear blue sky , hung over it like a warning of distant danger , and Father sighed as he looked , for to him it appeared as if the shadow of ruin were already creeping near , and stretching a threatening hand over the old home", "context_len": 82}}}, "sedges": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "He hunted in the brook for sedges , captured a Red Admiral to grace Bobby 's collection , filled his pockets with sweet gale and asphodel for Lilian 's dried vases , and made himself such a delightful companion that the children agreed that Father on a holiday was out and out the best playmate they knew", "context_len": 57}}}, "asphodel": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "He hunted in the brook for sedges , captured a Red Admiral to grace Bobby 's collection , filled his pockets with sweet gale and asphodel for Lilian 's dried vases , and made himself such a delightful companion that the children agreed that Father on a holiday was out and out the best playmate they knew", "context_len": 57}}}, "polypody": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "The farm proved , on a nearer acquaintance , to be a little one-storey dwelling built of rough stones , with a roof so covered with a mass of green polypody fern as to completely hide the slate underneath", "context_len": 39}}}, "yelping": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "Barking , biting , and snarling , the combatants had continually to be seized by the tails , forcibly separated , and kicked yelping back to their duties , where they stood with bristling ears , growling at each other through the hurdles , and showing their teeth like a pack of wolves", "context_len": 53}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Suddenly he heard a yelping bark , followed by a shrill cry of agony , and there was a great commotion in a clump of bushes about a hundred feet directly in front of him", "context_len": 35}}}, "plovers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "Oh , the boundless delight of those rolling miles of heather , which looked like a crimson sea spreading onward towards the horizon , and the delicious smell of the sweet-gale as they trod it underfoot ! Great flocks of plovers flew before them , screaming their 'pee-wit , pee-wit ! ' and here and there they roused a snipe or a woodcock , while wild little ponies scampered off like the wind , indignant at having their solitude disturbed", "context_len": 80}}}, "woodcock": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 62, "context": "Oh , the boundless delight of those rolling miles of heather , which looked like a crimson sea spreading onward towards the horizon , and the delicious smell of the sweet-gale as they trod it underfoot ! Great flocks of plovers flew before them , screaming their 'pee-wit , pee-wit ! ' and here and there they roused a snipe or a woodcock , while wild little ponies scampered off like the wind , indignant at having their solitude disturbed", "context_len": 80}}}, "spongy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "The path ran for fully a mile over a bog , and the children had to follow very closely in Father 's footsteps to keep a safe track over the soft , spongy surface , for Joe had told them dark tales of pedlars , travelling from Llanrhos to Gorswen , who had sunk into those treacherous brown pools and been heard of no more", "context_len": 65}}}, "pedlars": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "The path ran for fully a mile over a bog , and the children had to follow very closely in Father 's footsteps to keep a safe track over the soft , spongy surface , for Joe had told them dark tales of pedlars , travelling from Llanrhos to Gorswen , who had sunk into those treacherous brown pools and been heard of no more", "context_len": 65}}}, "vaughan": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Vaughan", "context_len": 1}}}, "parsley": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "The tiny window was hardly a foot square , and had no glass in it , and a beautiful root of parsley fern grew luxuriantly on the sill , while all the crevices of the walls seemed full of lichens , bound together by the crimson stems of the creeping pellitory", "context_len": 51}}}, "luxuriantly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "The tiny window was hardly a foot square , and had no glass in it , and a beautiful root of parsley fern grew luxuriantly on the sill , while all the crevices of the walls seemed full of lichens , bound together by the crimson stems of the creeping pellitory", "context_len": 51}}}, "pellitory": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "The tiny window was hardly a foot square , and had no glass in it , and a beautiful root of parsley fern grew luxuriantly on the sill , while all the crevices of the walls seemed full of lichens , bound together by the crimson stems of the creeping pellitory", "context_len": 51}}}, "peat": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 47, "context": "It was growing quite dark before Father returned , and I think , though neither of them would have confessed it , the children were both rather relieved to hear his cheery whistle outside the door , for it was just a little eerie sitting by the peat fire in that lonely cottage , without a sound to break the vast silence , and the knowledge that the nearest human habitation lay fully three miles away ; and Bobby had already asked Peggy if she believed in ghosts , and whether it was true what Joe had told him that the lights you sometimes see at night hovering over a bog are the souls of children who have never been baptized ; and though Peggy had professed to", "context_len": 128}}}, "bivouac": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Come along , my two subalterns , this is camp life , and you must learn to bivouac like true soldiers", "context_len": 21}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "They found the remains of the camp-fires , and all the marks usually left upon a spot which has been used as the bivouac of an army", "context_len": 27}}}, "plaids": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "We should just wrap ourselves in our plaids , and lie under the `` silent stars", "context_len": 16}}}, "nightshirts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "' I do n't think I shall ever want sheets and nightshirts , and horrid things of that sort , again", "context_len": 21}}}, "armfuls": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "They pulled great armfuls of the delicious purple heather , and laid them on the floor close to the fire , for Father said it was chilly at nights after the sun had gone down , and they would need all the warmth they could get", "context_len": 46}}}, "teaspoons": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "There was no table-cloth , but that did not matter in the least , and the absence of teaspoons was regarded as rather an advantage than otherwise", "context_len": 27}}}, "shelved": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 80, "context": "In her plan to give Joe a fright , she had never thought of Father , who returned by the same road ; and now that horrible white object was stationed just in the very danger-spot where a plunging horse might mean a matter of almost certain death , for the wall of the five-acre field abutted the road on one side , and on the other there was only a narrow patch of grass between the steep bank which shelved down sheer into the river , while the closed gate stopped any chance of a dash forward", "context_len": 98}}}, "anglers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The tea-things were hastily collected from the stream , and the amateur anglers set to work with much enthusiasm but no success , for the fish absolutely declined to bite , in spite of the tempting bait , and lay sulkily under the stones at the bottom of the pool", "context_len": 50}}}, "luminary": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "In spite of the best resolutions for early rising , nobody woke with the sun after all , and that luminary had plenty of time to creep round and peep in through the little window before Father sprang up from his bed of heather , and exclaiming that they were late , set the children to blow the peats into life again while he took his morning bath in the lake", "context_len": 71}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Never was the big luminary more welcome", "context_len": 7}}}, "mermaids": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "After sleeping all night in their clothes the cool plunge in the clear water was delightfully refreshing , and they sat about like mermaids on the rocks , basking in the sunshine , and watching a ring-ousel teaching her three big babies to fly , till Father called out that if they did not hurry up and come in at once he should eat all the breakfast before they arrived", "context_len": 70}}}, "basking": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "After sleeping all night in their clothes the cool plunge in the clear water was delightfully refreshing , and they sat about like mermaids on the rocks , basking in the sunshine , and watching a ring-ousel teaching her three big babies to fly , till Father called out that if they did not hurry up and come in at once he should eat all the breakfast before they arrived", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "Then there were the trogons , motmots and kingfishers glowing with iridescent hues , flocks of scarlet macaws , flamingoes almost equally gorgeous , each standing on one long slender leg and basking in the sun ; herons , plover , toucans and scores of other curious birds that make the Amazon jungle the most wonderful natural aviary on earth", "context_len": 60}}}, "rashers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "It was real fun frying rashers of bacon over the fire , especially when Peggy nearly upset the pan in her excitement , and Bobby absentmindedly sat upon the teapot , which he had put to keep warm among the peats", "context_len": 41}}}, "absentmindedly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "It was real fun frying rashers of bacon over the fire , especially when Peggy nearly upset the pan in her excitement , and Bobby absentmindedly sat upon the teapot , which he had put to keep warm among the peats", "context_len": 41}}}, "smeary": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 54, "context": "' First of all the breakfast things had to be cleared away , and carried down to the stream , but , to Peggy 's dismay , the greasy bacon plates utterly refused to wash clean , however long they were left to soak in the pool , and came up in the same smeary condition in which she had put them in", "context_len": 63}}}, "kettleful": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "'Tilt them up in a row against the cottage wall , and pour a kettleful of boiling water over them , ' said practical Bobby , who generally had some suggestion to offer", "context_len": 33}}}, "wisp": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 66, "context": "I do not know what Nancy would have thought of such a method of washing up , but it answered splendidly all the same , for the greasy water drained away into the grass , and the fresh breeze dried the plates without any need of a towel , and Peggy even managed to clean out the frying-pan with the help of some fern-leaves and a wisp of grass , an achievement of which she felt quite proud", "context_len": 78}}}, "frames": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "'If only we could stay up here a good long time we 'd bring lots of things from home , and paint pictures for the walls , and put them in cork frames , and I really believe , if I tried , I could make up one of those hearthrugs out of little scraps of cloth all pinched up and sewn on , like Nancy made last winter for her sister 's wedding present", "context_len": 75}}}, "hearthrugs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "'If only we could stay up here a good long time we 'd bring lots of things from home , and paint pictures for the walls , and put them in cork frames , and I really believe , if I tried , I could make up one of those hearthrugs out of little scraps of cloth all pinched up and sewn on , like Nancy made last winter for her sister 's wedding present", "context_len": 75}}}, "relinquishing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Peggy was more than willing , and relinquishing her schemes of household improvement to hunt up the milk-can as a handy receptacle , followed him out into the sunshine , to search among the heather for the little low-growing , red-leaved shrubs with their crop of small purple berries", "context_len": 49}}}, "bootlace": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "They had just reached the side of a little incline when Peggy 's bootlace , which she had neglected to fasten all the morning , tripped her up , and over she went , rolling into a prickly gorse-bush , while Bobby , who was so close behind that he could not stop himself , fell over her , and collapsed into a boggy hollow , where he lay panting for breath until Peggy picked herself up and hauled him out", "context_len": 81}}}, "furies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Away fled the children , racing over the moor as if the furies were at their heels", "context_len": 17}}}, "boggy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 63, "context": "They had just reached the side of a little incline when Peggy 's bootlace , which she had neglected to fasten all the morning , tripped her up , and over she went , rolling into a prickly gorse-bush , while Bobby , who was so close behind that he could not stop himself , fell over her , and collapsed into a boggy hollow , where he lay panting for breath until Peggy picked herself up and hauled him out", "context_len": 81}}}, "aspersions": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 69, "context": "Seabrooke went about the business of the day with all his accustomed regularity and precision , but with a sort of defiant and I-am-going-to-stick-to-it air about him which in itself incited the other boys to covert thrusts and innuendoes tending to throw distrust upon his version of the story and to make known their thorough sympathy with Percy , not only for his loss , but also for the aspersions cast upon him by the young pupil-teacher", "context_len": 77}}}, "cataracts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "There were foaming cataracts here and there among the rocks , just like Niagara on a small scale , and there were dear little quiet pools at the edges , where the still water was overhung by ferns and rushes , that sheltered caddice-worms , and boat-flies , and whirligig water-beetles , and all sorts of other delights for the collection", "context_len": 61}}}, "spiky": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "The sandals were anything but comfortable , and her feet hurt dreadfully on the stones , while every gorse-bush she passed seemed to be stretching out spiky fingers to scratch her bare legs ; she was tired after her morning 's adventures on the moors , and the eight miles seemed to lengthen out to an interminable vista , in spite of the way being downhill ; sundry bumps and bruises , which she had never noticed at the time , began to ache now , and the bee-stings on her neck smarted , until she hardly knew how to bear the pain", "context_len": 103}}}, "rents": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "The earth was opening in great rents , which sometimes widened and at other times snapped shut , engulfing houses , trees and sometimes hapless human beings , whose screams of terror were dreadful to hear", "context_len": 36}}}, "smudges": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "As for Peggy , a young gipsy tramp would have looked more respectable , for the brown holland dress , which had started out stiff and clean yesterday morning , was smeared with whinberry juice , black smudges from the kettle , and green stains from the mossy stones in the stream , and clung around her bare legs in damp , clammy folds , while the drenching rain had reduced the poppies in her hat to a scarlet pulp , which dripped down in crimson tears upon her cheek", "context_len": 90}}}, "spicy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Price was taking tea in her back-parlour , and indulging in such spicy gossip with her particular friend Miss Jones that the children passed by unnoticed , and Peggy began to congratulate herself that they were almost out of danger", "context_len": 40}}}, "snored": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "Even Jack , the magpie , seemed to feel the heat , and refused to amuse his young mistress , while Prickles snored on in his box of hay , oblivious to all coaxings and blandishments", "context_len": 36}}}, "blandishments": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Even Jack , the magpie , seemed to feel the heat , and refused to amuse his young mistress , while Prickles snored on in his box of hay , oblivious to all coaxings and blandishments", "context_len": 36}}}, "reaper": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "'If they want to go to sleep , I suppose they must ! I wonder if it would be worth while going down to the harvest field ? I 'm afraid David would n't let me drive the reaper", "context_len": 39}}}, "pelted": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 4, "context": "Down the pasture she pelted , her red hat looking like a new species of poppy among the grass , and , taking a flying leap over the fence , made her way along the dusty road to a place where a large willow overhung the path", "context_len": 47}}}, "poppy": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "Down the pasture she pelted , her red hat looking like a new species of poppy among the grass , and , taking a flying leap over the fence , made her way along the dusty road to a place where a large willow overhung the path", "context_len": 47}}}, "matchwood": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "The tree was old , so old and decayed that the upper side of the trunk had worn away altogether , leaving nothing but a hollow , crumbling shell , as rotten and dry as matchwood", "context_len": 36}}}, "pebble": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Peggy allowed him to get well beneath her , then , taking a pebble from her pocket , she let it fall plump in the middle of his white hat", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "He was stooping to pick up a smooth , round pebble from the bottom of the stream , when he saw a pollywog making its way from beneath a stone which lay half in , half out of the water", "context_len": 40}}}, "rubicund": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "The miller sat up with a jump , and reined in the old horse , staring into the tree with such blank amazement on his rubicund countenance that Peggy had to stuff her fingers into her mouth to stifle her mirth", "context_len": 41}}}, "frizzy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "He was rather a nice-looking boy , with merry brown eyes , a freckled nose , and frizzy chestnut hair , which stood up like a mop all over his head , and he had a particularly jolly , breezy air about him", "context_len": 43}}}, "unearth": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "' A squirrel , I 'll be bound ! I guess it 's no use you hiding 'way up there , old fellow ! I 'll unearth you before you 're much older ! ' And he commenced such an onslaught of stones that , to avoid the descending shower , Peggy tried to creep deeper into the hollow cavity where she was lying", "context_len": 64}}}, "convincingly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Roger \u2019 s arm fell across his shoulders and Roger went on talking to him quietly and convincingly , making it plain that his proper course was to return to school the following day exactly as if nothing had happened", "context_len": 40}}}, "godfathers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "'Whew ! what a catechism ! My name 's Archie , if you want to know , and my godfathers and godmother gave it to me at my baptism", "context_len": 29}}}, "quizzically": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "Franky put his head on one side , and looking quizzically at the old lady , said , `` Oo went down-'tairs for oo bastet wis a bone in oo foot , so oo tan do for my tart wis a bone in oo foot", "context_len": 45}}}, "vaulting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Peggy replied by lightly vaulting over the obstacle , and following her new friend with much promptitude , giving him a condensed history of herself and family as she ran to keep up with his long strides", "context_len": 37}}}, "joiner": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "Do you reckon I 'd get the village joiner to fix it ? Precious much good he 'd be at a job like this , the clumsy old idiot ! But the wheel 's nothing", "context_len": 35}}}, "grindstone": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "' A grindstone ! How splendid ! Why , it 's going round ever so fast when you put on that catch ! '", "context_len": 24}}}, "revolve": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "It 's only made with pins set round in a circle on a piece of board , with a nail on a pivot in the centre to revolve round and strike them", "context_len": 32}}}, "hydraulic": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "So she watched with delight the growth of the hydraulic pump , groaning a little over the dirty boots and muddied clothes that ensued , but assuring her friends that the 'dear boy ' was a perfect genius , and would make his mark in the world , and relating the story of his achievements with most unbounded pride and satisfaction", "context_len": 61}}}, "excellence": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` The man accordingly told him of the divine gifts of the holy woman Fatimeh and her piety and the excellence of her devotion ; then , taking him by the hand , he carried him without the city and showed him the way to her abiding-place , which was in a cavern on the top of a little hill ; whereupon the Maugrabin thanked him amain for his kindness [ 636 ] and returned to his place in the Khan", "context_len": 81}}}, "unsurpassed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "Peggy beamed with delight , for she wanted to see more of Archie 's wonderful work , and also the cakes and jam at the Willows had a reputation for excellence quite unsurpassed in the neighbourhood", "context_len": 36}}}, "stagnating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "I want some fresh young life in the place , to keep me from quite stagnating", "context_len": 16}}}, "stagnation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Since his arrival , Archie had certainly done his best to preserve his aunt from any danger of stagnation , for his fertile brain kept her in a perpetual tremor as to what the 'dear boy ' would do next", "context_len": 40}}}, "mechanics": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 7, "context": "The boy had a great talent for mechanics , and was very painstaking in carrying out all the minute details of his work", "context_len": 23}}}, "windiest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "He had manufactured a marvellous hat-grip , warranted to defy the windiest of weather , and presented it to the housemaid ; but when the poor girl tried to take off her Sunday hat , she found it so tightly fixed to her hair that it took the combined efforts of the other servants , aided by the liberal use of a pair of scissors , to remove the construction from her head", "context_len": 73}}}, "kittle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "'Horses is kittle cattle , and it are n't right to play no tricks with 'em ! '", "context_len": 18}}}, "newfangled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "But the old horse would have nothing to say to such a newfangled contrivance , and , with a snort of alarm , had nearly kicked through the side of his stall , sending the currying-brush flying in one direction and Master Archie head over heels in another", "context_len": 48}}}, "alarum": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Never mind , I can use the wheels to make an alarum , and fix it up in the harness-room , to go off at any time you like , Fleming ! '", "context_len": 33}}}, "utilizing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 54, "context": "But Fleming showed such a rooted distrust for anything that was intended to 'go off , ' and , indeed , such absolute abhorrence of any further mechanical contrivance in the vicinity of the stables , that Archie had transferred his attentions to the garden , where he was full of a scheme for utilizing the water power of the little stream to irrigate the soil , after the fashion of the Nile in Egypt , in a series of canals between the beds , and had already made the hose-pipe work with capital effect by means of a siphon and an old barrel", "context_len": 104}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "There was still a little daylight remaining , and he persisted in utilizing every moment of it to gain another mile or two before he called a halt for the night", "context_len": 31}}}, "siphon": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 99, "context": "But Fleming showed such a rooted distrust for anything that was intended to 'go off , ' and , indeed , such absolute abhorrence of any further mechanical contrivance in the vicinity of the stables , that Archie had transferred his attentions to the garden , where he was full of a scheme for utilizing the water power of the little stream to irrigate the soil , after the fashion of the Nile in Egypt , in a series of canals between the beds , and had already made the hose-pipe work with capital effect by means of a siphon and an old barrel", "context_len": 104}}}, "roundest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "This was a form of amusement which appealed far more to Miss Forster 's mind , for her flowers suffered much from drought in summer time , and she was a keen competitor at the local horticultural shows , exhibiting some of the largest carnations and the roundest dahlias in the neighbourhood", "context_len": 52}}}, "muddied": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "So she watched with delight the growth of the hydraulic pump , groaning a little over the dirty boots and muddied clothes that ensued , but assuring her friends that the 'dear boy ' was a perfect genius , and would make his mark in the world , and relating the story of his achievements with most unbounded pride and satisfaction", "context_len": 61}}}, "groupings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "It represented a considerable amount of skill and artistic taste on the part of himself and his handy-man , for the rare plants and exquisite groupings of contrasting blossoms would have done credit to a more imposing establishment , and he had as choice a collection of shrubs as could be grown anywhere in the county", "context_len": 56}}}, "howell": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Howell ! Have they lost his heart in the churchyard , and ca n't anybody find it for him ? '", "context_len": 21}}}, "stonemason": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 74, "context": "The lych-gate was locked , but she toiled over the steep steps that spanned the wall , and wading through the long grass under the yew-trees , found the spot , all covered with flowers , which Lilian had pointed out on Sunday , where 'Mary , the wife of the Reverend Philip Howell , ' slept , 'in sure and certain hope of the resurrection to eternal life , ' and where the stonemason had been already busy with the newly-added line : 'Also Raymond , only son of the above", "context_len": 92}}}, "tolerance": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "Since then a great friendship had existed between these two , made all the stronger , perhaps , by the fifty years that divided them , for old folk have often more tolerance and sympathy for childhood than have those whose eyes are still blinded by the bustle of life , and , whatever Peggy might be to others , with Mr", "context_len": 62}}}, "hobby": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "' I think flowers are your hobby , next to the parish , ' said Peggy , as she watched the Rector tying up his carnations , touching each blossom as carefully as if it were a child , with a tender pride in its loveliness", "context_len": 46}}}, "thousandfold": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "Then how was it that those great eyes looking into his , those sweet lips parted as though to speak to him , touched a chord in him that had never before resounded , and increased a thousandfold his longing for that vague and beautiful thing that he had never known ?", "context_len": 52}}}, "carnation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "' I took a nice carnation plant down to old Mrs", "context_len": 11}}}, "smithy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Johnson at the smithy , ' said Peggy , 'but she did n't seem at all pleased", "context_len": 17}}}, "wither": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "'Poor old soul ! She 's let her heart wither up instead of ripening ! We must all do one or the other , Peggy , child , as we grow older", "context_len": 32}}}, "ripen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Worldly prosperity is like a strong electric light -- it may bring out flowers , but it will never ripen character ; so do n't forget that , or ever exchange it for the true sunlight", "context_len": 36}}}, "asters": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 50, "context": "For reasons of his own the Rector kept Peggy late that evening , and they made a little tour of the garden , selecting what they called their prize plants , putting indiarubber bands round carnation-buds to keep them from bursting , and tying up the most promising stocks and asters with a scrupulous care , working until the light had almost failed and the sky stood out yellow against the outline of the cypress-trees", "context_len": 75}}}, "dahlia": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "' I have n't found the courage yet , ' returned the other huskily , picking a prize dahlia to pieces with a recklessness that seemed wanton in Peggy 's eyes", "context_len": 31}}}, "recklessness": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "Three times he beat the creature down with his club , but for all this punishment the rage and fury of the animal increased , and it continued to return to the attack with vicious recklessness", "context_len": 36}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Tom Hadley looked a little doubtful , for he could see that the enemy , though apparently single-handed , was a man of powerful frame and apparently fearless even to recklessness", "context_len": 31}}}, "dank": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "A raw wind , chill and dank , came from the east , soughing fitfully through the bare limbs of the trees and sending fallen leaves scurrying along the ground", "context_len": 30}}}, "catalogues": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "Miss Jones , the village dressmaker , was installed in the Rose Parlour , and appeared to be stitching morning , noon , and night ; the postman 's bag was quite heavy with patterns and catalogues of Indian outfits ; while distant relations and old acquaintances , who had neglected the Abbey for years , kept Nancy in a state of perpetual agitation by turning up suddenly to pay congratulatory calls", "context_len": 72}}}, "outfits": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "Miss Jones , the village dressmaker , was installed in the Rose Parlour , and appeared to be stitching morning , noon , and night ; the postman 's bag was quite heavy with patterns and catalogues of Indian outfits ; while distant relations and old acquaintances , who had neglected the Abbey for years , kept Nancy in a state of perpetual agitation by turning up suddenly to pay congratulatory calls", "context_len": 72}}}, "congratulatory": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 70, "context": "Miss Jones , the village dressmaker , was installed in the Rose Parlour , and appeared to be stitching morning , noon , and night ; the postman 's bag was quite heavy with patterns and catalogues of Indian outfits ; while distant relations and old acquaintances , who had neglected the Abbey for years , kept Nancy in a state of perpetual agitation by turning up suddenly to pay congratulatory calls", "context_len": 72}}}, "dowagers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 89, "context": "Peggy and Bobby liked the fun of the preparations , though they crept out of the way of the visitors as much as possible , for to be shown up in the drawing-room involved an amount of dressing and tidying which did not fit in at all with their ideas of holiday enjoyment , and they much preferred a picnic tea in the orchard , with Rollo and the rabbits for company , to the more select charms of the best china and the stately patronage of the neighbouring dowagers", "context_len": 90}}}, "sweeten": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 70, "context": "It was certainly Peggy who suggested sitting on the top of the haystack , and making it sway backwards and forwards like a swing , a delightful sensation while it lasted , but which ended suddenly in the collapse of the whole top of the stack , much to Father 's wrath , for it took David a couple of hours to repair the damage , and certainly did not sweeten the old man 's already crusty temper", "context_len": 78}}}, "inopportune": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "It was also Peggy 's most inopportune idea to ride the brown cow round the pasture on the very afternoon that Mrs", "context_len": 22}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Thus within twenty-four hours of his inopportune arrival , he had a place to stay and a job", "context_len": 18}}}, "inelegantly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "She gave Bobby a leg up on to Brindle 's broad back , and had just succeeded in scrambling inelegantly behind him , when the astonished quadruped paused in her task of cropping the succulent grass and buttercups , and realizing that something unusual was oppressing her , fled in mad career over the meadow , with the delighted children clinging desperately to her horns", "context_len": 65}}}, "cropping": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 32, "context": "She gave Bobby a leg up on to Brindle 's broad back , and had just succeeded in scrambling inelegantly behind him , when the astonished quadruped paused in her task of cropping the succulent grass and buttercups , and realizing that something unusual was oppressing her , fled in mad career over the meadow , with the delighted children clinging desperately to her horns", "context_len": 65}}}, "succulent": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 34, "context": "She gave Bobby a leg up on to Brindle 's broad back , and had just succeeded in scrambling inelegantly behind him , when the astonished quadruped paused in her task of cropping the succulent grass and buttercups , and realizing that something unusual was oppressing her , fled in mad career over the meadow , with the delighted children clinging desperately to her horns", "context_len": 65}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "He would eat meat if he were starving , but he preferred the cocoanuts that he had only to break open to get at their succulent contents", "context_len": 27}}}, "oppressing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 45, "context": "She gave Bobby a leg up on to Brindle 's broad back , and had just succeeded in scrambling inelegantly behind him , when the astonished quadruped paused in her task of cropping the succulent grass and buttercups , and realizing that something unusual was oppressing her , fled in mad career over the meadow , with the delighted children clinging desperately to her horns", "context_len": 65}}}, "miry": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "'Gee up ! Let her go ! Hooray ! ' yelled Bobby , striking such a whack on Brindle 's heaving side that she settled the matter by suddenly lying down to roll , and depositing her encumbrances in the miry ditch", "context_len": 42}}}, "progeny": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Davenport discouraged the acquaintance as much as possible , never feeling easy even for the life and limbs of her progeny when they were in the society of 'those terrible young Vaughans , ' and revenging herself by scathing remarks upon manners and deportment , which were extremely trying to the feelings of Aunt Helen , who naturally thought her children superior in every way to 'those mealy-faced little Davenports , who look as if they had not the strength or spirits to enjoy themselves , even if they were allowed to try", "context_len": 93}}}, "revenging": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Davenport discouraged the acquaintance as much as possible , never feeling easy even for the life and limbs of her progeny when they were in the society of 'those terrible young Vaughans , ' and revenging herself by scathing remarks upon manners and deportment , which were extremely trying to the feelings of Aunt Helen , who naturally thought her children superior in every way to 'those mealy-faced little Davenports , who look as if they had not the strength or spirits to enjoy themselves , even if they were allowed to try", "context_len": 93}}}, "davenport": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 0, "context": "Davenport ! If she came to live any nearer than four miles away , I declare I would pack my boxes and beg to be sent off to school ! '", "context_len": 31}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Davenport a rich relation nor was she herself in the friendless condition necessary for the full requirements of fiction , so she was afraid the round of amusements and social triumphs generally enjoyed by the heroine would not fall to her share", "context_len": 42}}}, "pullets": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "Davenport 's remarks , which had been wafted along in gusts , died away in the distance as she departed down the pasture to inspect the hen-coops , and hint broadly for the gift of a couple of young pullets , 'for yours are such an excellent breed , dear Miss Vaughan , and such capital winter layers", "context_len": 58}}}, "doubloons": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Smugglers always had bags of money -- spade guineas , you know , and Spanish doubloons , and all those kinds of things you read about in books", "context_len": 28}}}, "twines": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The mouth was overgrown with hazel-bushes and brambles and long , trailing twines of ivy , and it seemed to the children as if no foot but theirs had disturbed it for a long time", "context_len": 35}}}, "cranny": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 50, "context": "How could he save Aleck ? The fire was plainly at the foot of the stairs , even if they were not already burning , while those stifling clouds of smoke rolled between them and the doors of the haymow , and were now pouring up through every chink and cranny of the floor on which he stood", "context_len": 58}}}, "keg": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "'Those will be the smugglers ' cupboards that Joe spoke about , ' said Peggy , flashing the lantern into every nook and cranny , but with no success , for there was never a sign of a box of tea or a keg of brandy to be seen", "context_len": 49}}}, "flapped": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "Porter call him by his name , `` Coxcomb , '' which he knew quite well ; he only flapped his wings and walked farther away , screaming louder than ever", "context_len": 31}}}, "peggy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "Peggy held up the lantern over her head , and a number of bats , disturbed by the light , dropped from the roof where they had been suspended and whirled round the cave , 'cheeping ' angrily for some moments , and opening their tiny jaws at the children in quite a threatening manner , till Bobby clapped his hands , and they flew off to find their way into some deeper retreat", "context_len": 74}}}, "foolhardiness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Peggy flashed the light over the dark abyss to the still more gloomy depths beyond , but even her foolhardiness did not prompt her to try so perilous a bridge", "context_len": 30}}}, "explorations": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "Sir Samuel Baker , who died at the end of the year 1893 , aged seventy-three , will always be remembered for the splendid work he did in the Soudan during the four years he ruled there , and for his explorations in Africa", "context_len": 44}}}, "teatime": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "But that hero suggesting that it must be after teatime , she cordially agreed with him , and they began to retrace their steps to the entrance , feeling just a little disappointed , for somehow they had imagined a smugglers ' cave would be a jolly , dry sort of a place , with at least a few remains of its former tenants strewn about -- a pistol or two , perhaps , or a coil of rope , or a rusty dagger , just sufficient to give an air of romance to the adventure , even if the missing treasure were not forthcoming", "context_len": 105}}}, "hailstones": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Crouched down on the floor by the entrance , the children waited for the storm to pass by , wondering at the vivid pink flashes and the size of the hailstones which beat in through the hazel-bushes", "context_len": 37}}}, "runnel": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "A little runnel of water , flowing uncomfortably near , brought Peggy to her feet with a sudden cry", "context_len": 19}}}, "clucking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "Here and there a dead sheep or pig drifted by , or a portion of a haystack ; a hurdle floating like a raft bore on it a few disconsolate fowls , clucking dismally ; while an occasional wash-tub or upturned table showed that the river must have already flooded some of the low-lying cottages higher up on its course", "context_len": 60}}}, "sureness": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Each moment it seemed to flow with even swifter current , and to be rising with terrible sureness nearer and nearer the mouth of the cave", "context_len": 26}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "He chose what he regarded as the lesser of the two evils , relying upon his strength and his sureness of foot to carry him and his burden to the opposite side", "context_len": 32}}}, "rumblings": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "It was already growing dusk , and distant rumblings among the hills showed that the storm was still raging over the Welsh border , and sending down its torrents of rain to swell the already overflowing river", "context_len": 37}}}, "igniting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "She tried to strike a light , but the match was damp , and fizzled away without igniting", "context_len": 18}}}, "relight": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "This method proved a success , and she was able to relight the lantern , laying Bobby gently down on the floor , hoping he might not wake", "context_len": 28}}}, "pickaxe": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "There was silence for a few minutes , and then the children began to hear the welcome sound of a pickaxe , and Father 's voice every now and then , shouting a word of encouragement to them", "context_len": 38}}}, "blotched": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 64, "context": "It did not take very long for the children to be hauled up by Joe at the top , and they found themselves standing among the Abbey ruins in the early gray of the dawn , with Aunt Helen rushing to catch them in her arms , and Lilian hugging them and sobbing over them by turns , while Nancy , her face all blotched and swollen with crying , kept hovering round to put in a kiss whenever the others gave her a chance , and even old David cleared his throat hard , and 'blessed the Lord they were safe", "context_len": 102}}}, "briefest": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "In the briefest conceivable time the whites discharged their weapons , but in that flitting interval , their enemies had shifted their position , and the bullets whizzed harmlessly past in the grass , or buried themselves in the earth", "context_len": 40}}}, "quinsy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Nancy prophesied quinsy and pleurisy and rheumatic fever , but the Vaughans were a hardy race , and not even a cold resulted , in spite of her gloomy prognostications", "context_len": 30}}}, "lockets": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 59, "context": "Things felt very flat when the excitement was all over and the last of the guests had left the Abbey , and the children found themselves wishing that life could be a perpetual round of bouquets and favours and wedding-cake , not forgetting presents to the bridesmaids , for Lilian and Peggy were the proud possessors of charming gold lockets set with turquoises , with portraits of Aunt Helen inside them , 'the gifts of the bridegroom , ' as the local paper described them , while a silver watch and chain consoled Bobby for not taking a more prominent part in the ceremony , and made him declare that his new uncle was ' a brick , and no mistake", "context_len": 121}}}, "turquoises": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 62, "context": "Things felt very flat when the excitement was all over and the last of the guests had left the Abbey , and the children found themselves wishing that life could be a perpetual round of bouquets and favours and wedding-cake , not forgetting presents to the bridesmaids , for Lilian and Peggy were the proud possessors of charming gold lockets set with turquoises , with portraits of Aunt Helen inside them , 'the gifts of the bridegroom , ' as the local paper described them , while a silver watch and chain consoled Bobby for not taking a more prominent part in the ceremony , and made him declare that his new uncle was ' a brick , and no mistake", "context_len": 121}}}, "milkmen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 52, "context": "People knew the time of their passing , and looked out for them daily ; kindly women would come to cottage doorways to nod and smile ; the inn-keeper at the Halfway Arms set his clock by their arrival ; they had struck up quite a friendship with the postman , two milkmen , and the driver of a fish-cart , while the old man who broke stones by the roadside always nodded and gave them 'Good-morning , ' and fired a facetious remark or two after them , which the children imagined he must relieve the monotony of his work by composing during the intervals of their coming and going", "context_len": 111}}}, "scalded": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "Pigs , poultry , and young animals had to be fed , and the eggs gathered in daily ; the dairy claimed constant attention , for the pans must be scalded and the pails polished bright , and though Joe did the milking and churning , it was Lilian 's business to superintend the making up of the butter , and to weigh each pound with her own hands and print it with the Abbey stamp", "context_len": 76}}}, "reminder": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 69, "context": "First , the hearth , on which no spot or speck was to be seen , must be brushed up anew ; next , the corner of the table-cloth was to be twitched into place , and a knife laid more exactly into straight line ; then a ball , belonging to one of the younger children , was picked up and put in the toy-basket , with the reminder to little Tommy that father was coming , and the room must be kept in good order", "context_len": 87}}}, "reviewing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 68, "context": "But the stock , alas ! had accumulated a skim of green mould on the surface , and generally betrayed such symptoms of distress that it was fit for nothing but the pig-trough ; and when Lilian , warned by its awful example , visited her neglected shelves , she found so many forgotten scraps put away into odd corners that she straightway formed the excellent resolution of reviewing the larder daily after breakfast , having an uneasy sensation that it was one of the golden rules which Aunt Helen had particularly impressed upon her , but which , like many others , had slidden into the background of her remembrance", "context_len": 111}}}, "sirloins": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "'We 're quite tired of great sirloins of beef and legs of mutton , ' she announced", "context_len": 17}}}, "dustpan": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "And she departed with broom and dustpan , leaving her young mistress in charge of the kitchen", "context_len": 17}}}, "frizzling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 51, "context": "She was quite sure she had not been absent more than ten minutes , but when she returned to the kitchen she was greeted by a strong smell of burning , and rushing to her pan , found that every drop of water had boiled out , and the veal was frizzling at the bottom into a hard black mass", "context_len": 60}}}, "untempting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "It was Saturday , so the children were at home , but even their healthy appetites were not equal to the very untempting dish which was set before them , for the unfortunate veal had boiled away to ribbons , and all the goodness had gone into the lost gravy , while the pulpy remains tasted so hopelessly burnt as to be perfectly uneatable", "context_len": 64}}}, "pulpy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 53, "context": "It was Saturday , so the children were at home , but even their healthy appetites were not equal to the very untempting dish which was set before them , for the unfortunate veal had boiled away to ribbons , and all the goodness had gone into the lost gravy , while the pulpy remains tasted so hopelessly burnt as to be perfectly uneatable", "context_len": 64}}}, "uneatable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 63, "context": "It was Saturday , so the children were at home , but even their healthy appetites were not equal to the very untempting dish which was set before them , for the unfortunate veal had boiled away to ribbons , and all the goodness had gone into the lost gravy , while the pulpy remains tasted so hopelessly burnt as to be perfectly uneatable", "context_len": 64}}}, "vegetarian": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "'The vegetables are quite nice , at any rate , ' began Father cheerfully ; then seeing Lilian 's swimming eyes : 'Never mind , little woman ; experience is always dearly bought , and a vegetarian dinner wo n't hurt us for once in a way", "context_len": 47}}}, "cayenne": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "After this Lilian determined to provide more generously , and astonished the grocer by ordering three pounds of cayenne pepper , and a like quantity of mace ( embarrassing possessions which lingered in the spice-cupboard for years before they were eventually finished ) , and generally running the household on so liberal a scale that Father had to interfere and preach economy", "context_len": 62}}}, "recipes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "About this time , too , Lilian took to poring over the 'Ladies ' Column ' in the newspaper , and trying the various recipes which were given therein", "context_len": 29}}}, "mince": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` You mince it all up with herbs , and make it into a kind of pasty , and it sounds most delicious", "context_len": 23}}}, "gladiolus": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "But oh , Nancy , stop ! Let me look ! These are not onions ; they 're the gladiolus bulbs that David has just taken up from the garden ! What a mercy you did not put them into the pasty ! We might all have been poisoned ! '", "context_len": 51}}}, "bulbs": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "But oh , Nancy , stop ! Let me look ! These are not onions ; they 're the gladiolus bulbs that David has just taken up from the garden ! What a mercy you did not put them into the pasty ! We might all have been poisoned ! '", "context_len": 51}}}, "thyme": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "'It says , `` Take a little dried thyme , sage and sweet marjoram , '' ' read Lilian , with her finger on the recipe , ' '' together with a few pieces of lemon-peel", "context_len": 36}}}, "marjoram": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "'It says , `` Take a little dried thyme , sage and sweet marjoram , '' ' read Lilian , with her finger on the recipe , ' '' together with a few pieces of lemon-peel", "context_len": 36}}}, "morbidly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "At first the cares of her new position were a terrible burden on the poor child 's mind , for she was , if anything , too conscientious , and almost morbidly anxious to do right and fill the place which Aunt Helen had left so empty", "context_len": 47}}}, "oversleep": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "She would wake at four o'clock in the morning , and not dare to close her eyes again , for fear Nancy should oversleep herself , and the children be late for school", "context_len": 33}}}, "creaks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "It is amazing , when the house is perfectly quiet , how many creaks and peculiar noises make themselves heard which we never think of noticing in the daytime", "context_len": 29}}}, "threshing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "He is so tired with the threshing just now , poor darling ! and , as he said one night : `` You see , my dear , I have so many troubles of my own at present that the trials of the French peasantry of a hundred years ago seem an affair of quite minor importance", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "There was more fun as he unrolled the numerous wrappings of the package to disclose a small metal disc used in a threshing machine", "context_len": 24}}}, "survivals": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "The quaint sayings of the villagers are often as full of humour as those Scotch books over which people rave so much , and many of their stories are such interesting survivals of ancient folklore that I have often longed to collect them in writing", "context_len": 45}}}, "tends": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "While surely , to a thoughtful mind , the constant sight of so much loveliness around tends to have a more ennobling effect than an environment of bricks and mortar and smoky chimneys , whatever the Londoners may say", "context_len": 39}}}, "heartburning": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "' I am afraid there seems very little festivity or party-going for you , dear child , and I should have been glad to hear you had been asked out rather more ; but , after all , much society often means much rivalry and heartburning", "context_len": 46}}}, "amber": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The trees were turning to russet and amber and gold , and the swallows had long since flown away", "context_len": 19}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "pounced amber ) signifies also `` saffron '' ; but the obbligato juxtaposition of aloes and sandal-wood tends to show that what is meant is the well-known product of the sperm-whale", "context_len": 31}}}, "hoppers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The hop-gathering was over , and the hoppers -- a motley crew -- had returned to the slums of Stafford and Birmingham , the men not sorry to cease hard work , but the poor , draggled women and the little children with a wistful good-bye to the green fields , and all , I think , with a half sigh of regret for the Rector , who had toiled unceasingly among them during their brief stay , hoping , if by ever so little , to raise the hopeless lives and the sodden minds to a knowledge of higher things", "context_len": 101}}}, "hybrid": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "They were real gipsies , too , not the fair-haired hybrid pretenders who go about in neat caravans with muslin curtains to the windows , and wicker baskets slung on the top , but a dark-eyed , Spanish-looking crew , who put up a tent with a pole and a ragged blanket , and stewed their supper in a black caldron hung from three sticks set in the ground", "context_len": 69}}}, "core": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "He was keen , practical business to the core , and no sentiment about him , for he arose promptly with the farewell words , shook hands with Bart in an off-hand way , and was gone like a flash to catch his train to the city", "context_len": 47}}}, "planets": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "'Tell your fortune , my little master ? Tell your fortune , my pretty young lady ? Cross the poor gipsy 's hand with a silver sixpence , that the planets may work", "context_len": 33}}}, "palpable": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "But Peggy and Bobby were not so simple as to be taken in by such palpable guile , and they walked on , with the old crone following persistently in their wake , at first clamouring for some trifle and then hurling curses at their heads", "context_len": 46}}}, "guile": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 117, "context": "This , then , is what befell Alaeddin with the Maugrabin ; but Alaeddin , for all this , was not altogether [ 625 ] quit of the accursed enchanter , withal his body had been burned and given to the winds ; for that the accursed one had a brother viler than he [ and yet more skilled ] in magic and geomancy and astrology ; [ nay , they were even ] as saith the proverb , `` A bean and it was cloven in twain ; '' [ 626 ] and each dwelt in one quarter of the world , so they might fill it [ 627 ] with their sorcery and craft and guile", "context_len": 118}}}, "cobbler": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "The terrified children took to their heels , and I do n't really know what might have happened , only fortunately the familiar figure of old Barlow , the village cobbler , appeared stumping down the road in the distance", "context_len": 40}}}, "snuffy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "He was not generally a very attractive person , being snuffy in appearance and crusty in temper , but to-night no fairy prince could have been more appreciated , and they flew to him , each seizing one of his horny hands with an enthusiasm which much surprised him", "context_len": 49}}}, "jams": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "The blackberries were ripe , too , and there were many expeditions in search of them , mostly in their own fields , for the hedgerows abounded in the purple fruit , and Lilian 's housekeeping soul was at present bent on jams and cordials", "context_len": 45}}}, "cordials": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "The blackberries were ripe , too , and there were many expeditions in search of them , mostly in their own fields , for the hedgerows abounded in the purple fruit , and Lilian 's housekeeping soul was at present bent on jams and cordials", "context_len": 45}}}, "reining": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "'That is the house , ' said the first , reining in his horse just below where Peggy was standing , and pointing with his riding-whip at the Abbey", "context_len": 29}}}, "rebuilding": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "I suppose the stables would all want rebuilding , and no doubt that wood could be cleared , and turned into pleasure-grounds", "context_len": 22}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "The Indians ! panted Bomba , as he slammed the door shut and slipped into place the heavy bar he had fashioned while he was rebuilding the hut", "context_len": 28}}}, "sweepstakes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "This grass-land would be grand for a racer or two ; I 've my eye on the Melton sweepstakes yet", "context_len": 20}}}, "overhearing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Eavesdropping was certainly not one of Peggy 's besetting sins , but she could not help overhearing all that was said , and as the pair rode on down the path towards the gate , she picked up her basket and walked in the same direction on her own side of the hedge , so as to get a good look at them if possible", "context_len": 65}}}, "sublimely": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "In a fury of wrath she seized the offending coin and flung it after its donor , but , like many of our keenest shafts , it fell harmless , for she missed her aim , and the horsemen rode on , sublimely unconscious of the storm they had left behind them", "context_len": 52}}}, "blackberrying": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "I 'm afraid I do look rather untidy with blackberrying , but all the same they might have seen the difference", "context_len": 21}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` There , '' said Jane , `` I do n't believe Queen Victoria herself had a better set-out when she went blackberrying", "context_len": 23}}}, "fra": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The thin un in front be Mester Reade , the lawyer fra ' Warford , and a tight-fisted rascal he be , too , from all they say", "context_len": 28}}}, "distiller": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "It 's Heaven help them as Lawyer Reade gets his teeth into ! And the big , stout man be Mester Norton , the distiller , him as has those works t'other side o ' Warford , and runs half the drink-shops in the town , to say naught o ' the country publics he owns , too", "context_len": 58}}}, "teetotalers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "The teetotalers calls him `` Old Blazes , '' partly to favour his red face and partly 'at he keeps the whisky traffic going so briskly", "context_len": 26}}}, "dissevered": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "That it was possible for the Vaughans and the Abbey to be ever dissevered had never before entered into her calculations , and the prospect was so terrible that she thrust it from her with scorn", "context_len": 36}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "They fastened the exhausted and dying Bessus to these trees , one limb of his body to each , and then releasing the stems from their confinement , they flew upward , tearing the body asunder , each holding its own dissevered portion , as if in triumph , far over the heads of the multitude assembled to witness the spectacle", "context_len": 61}}}, "languish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "You must not suppose that all this time Peggy 's acquaintance with Archie Forster had been allowed to languish", "context_len": 19}}}, "edification": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "He kindly gave Father the benefit of his experience of farming in Colorado ( greatly to the latter 's edification ) ; he amused Lilian with his funny stories , and was a source of open-mouthed wonder to Nancy , who thought his achievements only second to a conjurer she had once seen at Gorswen fair", "context_len": 56}}}, "severer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "By good rights Master Archie ought to have been away at school , but a too active brain in a fast-growing body had brought about such a delicacy that the doctor forbade any severer study than a few hours ' daily reading with the curate , and recommended as much fresh air and exercise as possible", "context_len": 56}}}, "incited": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "Seabrooke went about the business of the day with all his accustomed regularity and precision , but with a sort of defiant and I-am-going-to-stick-to-it air about him which in itself incited the other boys to covert thrusts and innuendoes tending to throw distrust upon his version of the story and to make known their thorough sympathy with Percy , not only for his loss , but also for the aspersions cast upon him by the young pupil-teacher", "context_len": 77}}}, "durable": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 44, "context": "The question of decorations was much discussed , for while Father suggested hanging the walls with sacking , and Lilian voted for garlanding them with wild flowers , both ideas were rejected , the one as too prosaic and the other as not sufficiently durable , and it was not until Peggy conceived the brilliant thought of lining their dwelling with moss that a satisfactory solution was arrived at", "context_len": 69}}}, "grizzlies": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 29, "context": "Dad put an axe into my hand , and taught me to chop kindling before I was out of petticoats , and when we went up the Rockies shooting grizzlies we built log-cabins , and I can tell you there was no carpenter to fetch there nearer than a hundred miles , so I guess I ought to know how to handle a saw and fix up a bit of lumber", "context_len": 71}}}, "wattled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "In the midst of this platform a small hut gradually grew up , the walls of stout hazel-stakes wattled across with branches and willow-withs , while the roof was neatly thatched with reeds", "context_len": 33}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "They were all busily engaged within the hut , nailing sheets of moss over the wattled walls , when a curious squeaking noise began to attract Peggy 's attention", "context_len": 29}}}, "garlanding": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "The question of decorations was much discussed , for while Father suggested hanging the walls with sacking , and Lilian voted for garlanding them with wild flowers , both ideas were rejected , the one as too prosaic and the other as not sufficiently durable , and it was not until Peggy conceived the brilliant thought of lining their dwelling with moss that a satisfactory solution was arrived at", "context_len": 69}}}, "squeaking": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "Animals were lowing , bellowing , bleating , whinnying , or squeaking in every note of the octave ; cocks crew , ducks quacked , dogs barked , and men talked and shouted in Welsh and English till a chance comer might have imagined himself in pandemonium", "context_len": 47}}}, "brotherly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "'You goose ! Vipers do n't squeal , at any rate , ' said Archie , whose friendship had reached a degree of intimacy that was distinctly brotherly ; and gingerly pulling asunder the neatly-woven grass , he disclosed to view a plump yellow dormouse , whom they had evidently disturbed in his winter quarters", "context_len": 55}}}, "dormouse": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 44, "context": "'You goose ! Vipers do n't squeal , at any rate , ' said Archie , whose friendship had reached a degree of intimacy that was distinctly brotherly ; and gingerly pulling asunder the neatly-woven grass , he disclosed to view a plump yellow dormouse , whom they had evidently disturbed in his winter quarters", "context_len": 55}}}, "frosts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 64, "context": "As this seemed the most humane suggestion , Master Dormouse was tucked up in bed once more , and , still protesting , was carried to a sunny bank under an apple-tree , and stowed away under a protecting clump of leaves , where his plaintive voice gradually subsided , and he settled down for five months of oblivion , to ignore the winter frosts and storms until the April sunshine should tempt him out of his lair", "context_len": 78}}}, "ransacked": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 95, "context": "Naturally this called forth inquiry , and it soon became known that Percy had entrusted to Seabrooke 's keeping a large sum of money , lest he should himself be tempted to spend any portion of it , as it was to be reserved for a special purpose ; that Seabrooke before going to the dinner on the previous evening had put it , as he supposed , in a secure place , and that this morning the money was gone , while he had discovered slight but unmistakable evidence that his quarters had been ransacked in search of it", "context_len": 100}}}, "aeronaut": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Father , however , getting to hear of the project , forbade it so emphatically that the disgusted aeronaut was obliged to give way , and consoled himself by constructing a fire-balloon out of gay strips of pink and green tissue-paper , which , ignited by methylated spirits , was to be set off with great effect on Peggy 's birthday", "context_len": 61}}}, "methylated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "The hut had to be swept and dusted , late flowers to be gleaned from the garden to decorate the tea-table , cups and saucers packed up and conveyed in baskets , together with the little tin kettle and the methylated spirit lamp , as they could scarcely light a fire on the platform like they did for picnic teas in the woods", "context_len": 63}}}, "housewarming": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "As some slight amends for his disappointment , Lilian proposed that they should have a grand housewarming at Sky Cottage on Saturday afternoon , and invite Father to tea in the sanctum", "context_len": 32}}}, "artistically": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "They had brought a clean tablecloth from the kitchen drawer , and set the table quite artistically , with a jam-pot full of flowers in the centre , and little plates full of cakes grouped round it", "context_len": 37}}}, "shortbread": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Lilian put out a very tempting looking selection of rock-buns and ginger-nuts , and Archie produced a tin of real Scotch shortbread and some macaroons , a contribution from his aunt ; so with bread-and-butter , and a pot of the newly-made blackberry jam , there was quite a noble display", "context_len": 51}}}, "frill": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "But Lilian had kept her surprise in the background , and it was only when all was ready that she opened a basket , and proudly drew out her masterpiece , a substantial-looking cake , with a cut-paper frill , and white icing on the top , on which in pink sugary letters were inscribed the words : 'Success to Sky Cottage ! ' Certainly some of the capitals were a little staggery , and the ' y ' had strayed into the pink border round the edge , but it was felt to be a triumph of culinary art all the same , and gave quite a grace to the table", "context_len": 112}}}, "icing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "But Lilian had kept her surprise in the background , and it was only when all was ready that she opened a basket , and proudly drew out her masterpiece , a substantial-looking cake , with a cut-paper frill , and white icing on the top , on which in pink sugary letters were inscribed the words : 'Success to Sky Cottage ! ' Certainly some of the capitals were a little staggery , and the ' y ' had strayed into the pink border round the edge , but it was felt to be a triumph of culinary art all the same , and gave quite a grace to the table", "context_len": 112}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` If I had had that piece of cake , '' I said , `` I should have eaten the icing instead of leaving it in the water", "context_len": 28}}}, "culinary": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 98, "context": "But Lilian had kept her surprise in the background , and it was only when all was ready that she opened a basket , and proudly drew out her masterpiece , a substantial-looking cake , with a cut-paper frill , and white icing on the top , on which in pink sugary letters were inscribed the words : 'Success to Sky Cottage ! ' Certainly some of the capitals were a little staggery , and the ' y ' had strayed into the pink border round the edge , but it was felt to be a triumph of culinary art all the same , and gave quite a grace to the table", "context_len": 112}}}, "veterinary": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "At the last minute Father had been obliged to send his regrets and apologies , for the veterinary surgeon had arrived to doctor a sick horse , and he could not possibly leave the stables , so the tea-party must perforce begin without him , for the days were growing short now , and there was no time to spare", "context_len": 60}}}, "clinking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 59, "context": "Squeers remarked of her Yorkshire pie , it seemed 'quite a pity to cut into it ' ; but , seizing the knife , Peggy boldly severed the 's ' and the ' u , ' and with Aunt Helen 's wedding festivities fresh in their memories , the company drank the health of Sky Cottage in tea , clinking their cups together over the table in imitation of old Squire Henley", "context_len": 72}}}, "timorously": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "They were in the very midst of one of Archie 's most comical adventures , when a shout was heard underneath the tree , and going out on to the veranda , they beheld Nancy struggling timorously up the staircase , her evident anxiety to make some communication overcoming her natural abhorrence of such an airy structure", "context_len": 57}}}, "alpaca": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "She bundled the remains of the feast into her basket , and had just descended the stairs , escorted by the sympathizing Peggy and Bobby , when round the corner of the large haystack suddenly loomed the tall figure and black alpaca skirts of Mrs", "context_len": 45}}}, "thunderstruck": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Me ! to pay it ! '' ejaculated Uncle Job , thunderstruck", "context_len": 13}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Some would drop down as if thunderstruck , others would cry aloud , whilst others again would have convulsions", "context_len": 19}}}, "shamming": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Sheer waste of good time , I call it ; and a boy who can expend so much energy as to raise such a construction must be only shamming ill-health , and would be far better packed off to school", "context_len": 40}}}, "retailing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "Davenport that country lanes were unpleasant to drive along in the dark , so drawing on her gloves she routed her groom , a small , depressed-looking boy , out of the harness-room , where he was retailing his grievances to the awe-stricken Joe , and tucked her black skirts safely into her pony-carriage , assuring the children that it should not be long before she looked them up again , as she had promised their aunt to keep an eye on them after they were left alone", "context_len": 88}}}, "nightdress": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "There was the much-longed-for guinea-pig from Father , which had been smuggled up in a hamper , and kept with elaborate care in a remote portion of the barn ( a secret which Bobby found the utmost difficulty in preserving ) ; a nightdress case worked by Lilian 's neat fingers ; a cork frame from Bobby , made under Archie 's supervision ; a round pink-and-white wool mat , which Nancy had crocheted at odd times during the evenings ; and a little mug from Joe , with a Welshwoman in a tall hat on one side , and ' A present from Llandrindod ' on the other , bought on a visit to the famous wells which he had paid in company with the church choir", "context_len": 128}}}, "wax": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "The party consisted solely of themselves and Archie , for Father 's suggestion of the five Miss Davenports was received with howls of indignation ; and Lilian and Nancy 's combined efforts had produced a cake with twelve tiny coloured wax candles stuck into icing-sugar on the top , one for every year of Peggy 's life , and which had to be blown out in turns by the assembled company for good luck", "context_len": 74}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Frideswide 's ; there wax tapers shed a hallowed light on the sternly composed features of him who had been the bulwark of England ; and there choking sobs and bitter sighs every hour rent the air , and bore witness to a nation 's grief", "context_len": 46}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "In each were found a small inkstand , a pen , and two pencils , two sticks of sealing wax , and best of all , tiny note paper and envelopes stamped M", "context_len": 33}}}, "squibs": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 45, "context": "To be sure , the catherine-wheel stuck , and utterly refused to turn , in spite of all pokings and proddings , and the rockets only fizzled off near the ground , instead of shooting up as they ought to have done ; but the squibs and crackers were quite effective , and a train of gunpowder , laid down to represent a fiery serpent , blazed away in fine style", "context_len": 71}}}, "dwindled": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 42, "context": "The fire-balloon , however , was the success of the evening , for it shot straight up , and floated across the sky like a beautiful meteor , its pink and green sides giving a charming effect , till at length it dwindled away and became a mere speck in the distance , leaving the children more full of admiration than ever for Archie 's talents", "context_len": 66}}}, "microscopical": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Archie caught a severe chill hunting for microscopical specimens in the pond , and was in bed for some weeks , nursed by his doting aunt , consoling himself for his enforced idleness by planning such improvements at both the Willows and the Abbey as were calculated to make their owners ' hair stand on end", "context_len": 56}}}, "doting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Archie caught a severe chill hunting for microscopical specimens in the pond , and was in bed for some weeks , nursed by his doting aunt , consoling himself for his enforced idleness by planning such improvements at both the Willows and the Abbey as were calculated to make their owners ' hair stand on end", "context_len": 56}}}, "reassurances": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "He heard ghostly footfalls among the ruins and mysterious taps on the stable-window when he was suppering the horses , which , in spite of Lilian 's reassurances , he persisted in regarding as a warning , though for which of his numerous relatives it was intended kept him in a state of perpetual doubt and uneasiness", "context_len": 57}}}, "bullock": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "Vaughan suggested it might prove nothing worse than a young bullock with indigestion , she had dismissed the idea as almost profane", "context_len": 22}}}, "orthodox": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "Beyond the fact that a lady in white was supposed to haunt both the ruins and the oak-wood , weeping and wringing her hands in orthodox ghostly fashion , the children had not been able to learn much of the story , for there were so many and divergent accounts of it , all told with uncertainty as to names and dates , and in that very oracular -- not to say muddled -- style sometimes indulged in by rustic historians", "context_len": 81}}}, "divergent": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 48, "context": "Beyond the fact that a lady in white was supposed to haunt both the ruins and the oak-wood , weeping and wringing her hands in orthodox ghostly fashion , the children had not been able to learn much of the story , for there were so many and divergent accounts of it , all told with uncertainty as to names and dates , and in that very oracular -- not to say muddled -- style sometimes indulged in by rustic historians", "context_len": 81}}}, "foxgloves": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "But one wet afternoon , finding David alone in the harness-room , where the old man had lighted a fire to make some wonderful decoction of foxgloves wherewith to doctor a cow with a strained leg , they seated themselves on a sack of potatoes in front of the cheerful blaze , and with the aid of a little judicious flattery and coaxing managed to cajole him into a true and circumstantial account of the family ghost", "context_len": 77}}}, "wherewith": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` [ 662 ] Therewith he disappeared , and as for Alaeddin , when he heard this , his wit fled from his head and his joints trembled at the cry wherewith the Marid cried out at him ; but he took heart and leaving his closet , went in straightway to his wife and feigned to her that his head irked him , of his knowledge that Fatimeh was renowned for the secret of healing [ 663 ] all aches and pains", "context_len": 83}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 49, "context": "He used also to go to the market of the goldsmiths and the market of the jewellers , and there he would sit and look upon the different kinds of jewels and see them bought and sold ; whereby he became aware that the fruits of the trees , wherewith he had filled the purses , [ 318 ] whenas he was in the treasure , were neither glass nor crystal , but jewels , and knew that he had happened upon great wealth , such as kings might nowise compass", "context_len": 91}}}, "divergences": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "'When did it happen ? ' asked Peggy , hastily , hoping to nip in the bud one of the old fellow 's , rambling divergences from the point", "context_len": 29}}}, "testily": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "'Let me tell my tale in my own way , ' said David testily", "context_len": 14}}}, "thumbscrew": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "So they clapped a thumbscrew on Dame Eleanor , to sharpen her wits", "context_len": 13}}}, "disciple": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "But that Lord Grey of Wigmore was a disciple of Old Nick himself , and what does he do but catch hold of Dame Eleanor 's daughter , which were n't more ' an a little maid like Miss Peggy there , and put the screw on her thumb , thinkin ' it would loosen her mother 's tongue to hear her shriek", "context_len": 63}}}, "stabbed": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "At the first cry as was raised by that child Dame Eleanor went clean off her head , and , breakin ' away from them as held her , she seized up a dagger and stabbed herself through the breast , with a awful shout of laughter , shuttin ' her lips for ever from them as would wrest the secret from her", "context_len": 63}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Every word the little girl uttered stabbed Bomba to the heart", "context_len": 11}}}, "wraith": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 53, "context": "The children were beginning to feel quite delightfully creepy , and would have liked a further continuation of the spirit portion of the story ; but David 's decoction being now ready , his thoughts were with his invalid in the cow-house , and nothing could bring them back to Dame Eleanor 's wraith", "context_len": 54}}}, "ancestress": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "After this , Peggy and Bobby were determined to try and obtain a sight of their ancestress , and with much screwing up of their courage stole out one evening , and crept fearfully round the ruins , clutching each other 's hands very tightly , and jumping at every sound", "context_len": 51}}}, "clog": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 62, "context": "Frightened almost out of their wits , the children shrieked and ran , but stopped before they had gone twenty yards , for the noise had developed into a very decided 'Hee-haw ! ' and the moon , bursting through the clouds , revealed the long nose and twirling ears of the tinker 's broken-winded old donkey , who , dragging a clog on his leg , was giving vent to his feelings in his own peculiar fashion , his master having turned him into the ruins for a stolen feed during the night , no doubt with the intention of fetching him in the morning before anyone was up and about", "context_len": 112}}}, "treasuring": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "It was such bathos after the overwrought state of their feelings that they sat down and laughed till the tears ran down their cheeks , treasuring the joke to tell to Archie when he should be well enough to receive visitors , and holding it up as a model to relieve Nancy 's fears", "context_len": 54}}}, "scooped": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 6, "context": "Taking a large turnip , they scooped out the inside till there was nothing left but a hollow shell , cutting holes in it for eyes , nose , and mouth , and fixing a candle inside , so that when the light shone through it should resemble a horrible , grinning face", "context_len": 53}}}, "abutted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 56, "context": "In her plan to give Joe a fright , she had never thought of Father , who returned by the same road ; and now that horrible white object was stationed just in the very danger-spot where a plunging horse might mean a matter of almost certain death , for the wall of the five-acre field abutted the road on one side , and on the other there was only a narrow patch of grass between the steep bank which shelved down sheer into the river , while the closed gate stopped any chance of a dash forward", "context_len": 98}}}, "cajoled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "She was barely in time , for already one wheel was over the edge , but , the object of his fear being removed , Prince allowed himself to be cajoled into the road again , where he stood , panting and trembling in every limb", "context_len": 46}}}, "unshaken": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "But his faith in the genuine phantom remained unshaken all the same , and I do not think he would have ventured alone into the ruins after dark for the amount of his weekly wages , and money meant a good deal to poor hard-working Joe", "context_len": 46}}}, "hump": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "' I wish I were a cat ! ' said Peggy one morning , quite roused to envy by the contemplation of Tabbyskin 's toilet , 'to have no trouble with washing or dressing ; only just to jump out of bed , hump up your back and stretch yourself , drink your saucer of milk , and then lick yourself comfortably on the hearthrug in front of the dining-room fire", "context_len": 71}}}, "hearthrug": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 64, "context": "' I wish I were a cat ! ' said Peggy one morning , quite roused to envy by the contemplation of Tabbyskin 's toilet , 'to have no trouble with washing or dressing ; only just to jump out of bed , hump up your back and stretch yourself , drink your saucer of milk , and then lick yourself comfortably on the hearthrug in front of the dining-room fire", "context_len": 71}}}, "hibernating": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "I 'm sure Nature intended me for a hibernating animal , for I hate the cold", "context_len": 16}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The Danes are sleeping , hibernating like bears in their winter caves", "context_len": 12}}}, "schoolmistress": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "Lilian transformed the pulpit into a perfect bower , weaving in holly and laurels till the Rector hoped she would leave room for him inside it ; and Peggy and Bobby helped -- or hindered -- the schoolmistress as she adorned the font with a wonderful arrangement of cotton-wool and frosted leaves", "context_len": 52}}}, "font": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "Lilian transformed the pulpit into a perfect bower , weaving in holly and laurels till the Rector hoped she would leave room for him inside it ; and Peggy and Bobby helped -- or hindered -- the schoolmistress as she adorned the font with a wonderful arrangement of cotton-wool and frosted leaves", "context_len": 52}}}, "cues": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "Archie was full of enthusiasm over what Nancy called 'play-acting , ' and had soon initiated his friends into all the mysteries of drop-scenes , side-shifts , make-ups , cues , and footlights", "context_len": 33}}}, "footlights": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 61, "context": "But here the Rector again stepped in , and limited the performance to half an hour 's duration , begging them to choose something appropriate for a Sunday-school audience , and if possible with a moral , and further reminding them that a platform consisting of tables hastily pushed together after the tea was concluded would scarcely give facilities for either footlights , drop-scenes , or side-shifts , a curtain being the utmost he could undertake to manage in that direction", "context_len": 81}}}, "comedy": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Both tragedy and comedy raged in the Rose Parlour , the company feeling themselves equal to anything , from a representation of Hamlet to the famous scene between Sir Peter and Lady Teasle", "context_len": 33}}}, "stilted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "From such constant rehearsals the actors found themselves adopting a very stilted kind of conversation", "context_len": 15}}}, "certes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "They addressed each other as 'Ho , knave ! ' or 'Prithee , kind wench ! ' and would answer the simplest question by 'Yea , certes ! ' or 'A n't please thee , my lord", "context_len": 37}}}, "impresario": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "' Bobby took to carrying about an old horse-pistol which he had found in the lumber-room , and saying 'Oddsbodkins ' on all occasions , and Peggy put on such a general air of melodrama that it seemed scarcely possible for her to speak in plain prose ; Archie was impresario , stage-manager , scene-shifter , dresser , maker-up , and principal actor all combined , while Lilian waxed so enthusiastic that she even sacrificed the feathers out of her last summer 's best hat to adorn the slouched head-gear considered necessary for a due representation of Romeo", "context_len": 98}}}, "adorn": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 112, "context": "oriels , [ 651 ] and said to him , `` How deemest thou , O my Lady Fatimeh , of this wonderful pavilion ? '' [ 652 ] `` By Allah , O my daughter , '' replied he , `` it is indeed marvellous in the extreme , [ 653 ] nor methinketh is its like found in the world ; nay , it is magnificent exceedingly ; but oh , for one thing which would far increase it in beauty and adornment ! '' And the princess said to him , `` O my Lady Fatimeh , what is lacking to it and what is this thing which would adorn it ? Tell me of it ; I had thought that it was altogether perfect", "context_len": 128}}}, "slouched": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 88, "context": "' Bobby took to carrying about an old horse-pistol which he had found in the lumber-room , and saying 'Oddsbodkins ' on all occasions , and Peggy put on such a general air of melodrama that it seemed scarcely possible for her to speak in plain prose ; Archie was impresario , stage-manager , scene-shifter , dresser , maker-up , and principal actor all combined , while Lilian waxed so enthusiastic that she even sacrificed the feathers out of her last summer 's best hat to adorn the slouched head-gear considered necessary for a due representation of Romeo", "context_len": 98}}}, "inappropriate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "This gave quite a fresh aspect to affairs , and great were the discussions as to what piece should be chosen , everybody unfortunately wanting something different , and generally utterly inappropriate , or impossible to act", "context_len": 37}}}, "bier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "' I should be Juliet , you know , stretched out on the bier , and Archie would have to be Romeo , and come in , and think I was dead , and stab himself , and then of course I should wake up and stab myself , too ; and Peggy and Bobby could be the Montagues and Capulets , getting reconciled over our dead bodies", "context_len": 68}}}, "leanings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Archie , however , having leanings towards comedy , was not at all willing to play the r\u00f4le of the despairing lover", "context_len": 22}}}, "bargaining": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Bobby was vague as to any particular play , only bargaining he might be allowed to bring in his pistol , and do as much fighting as possible , and that his part should not require too much learning", "context_len": 39}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Bargaining in that throng was no easy matter , but their owners would dash in , seize a pony by the mane and tail , and by sheer force drag it away from its companions , the very small size of the animals rendering practicable what would have been impossible with a larger breed", "context_len": 54}}}, "dialogues": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "With wings very much clipped , the soaring actors had to throw aside Shakespeare and Sheridan , and apply themselves to books of recitations and dialogues for village schools", "context_len": 29}}}, "aspirations": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 59, "context": "Bomba wondered whether he would ever see that strange and wonderful city or others like it , wondered if he would always have to spend his life in the jungle , with none save Casson and a few natives for his friends , to none of whom he could speak of the longings that obsessed him , of the aspirations that seemed doomed forever to be thwarted", "context_len": 67}}}, "elocution": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "'Shall I ? ' said Peggy , flushing ; for it was her best piece at the elocution class , and she had been complimented on it by her teacher", "context_len": 30}}}, "rehearsing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The small piece chosen did not require much either in the way of rehearsing , scenery , or costumes , which was just as well , for the time was short , and the day of the tea-party seemed to arrive almost directly", "context_len": 43}}}, "frizzing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "By four o'clock the school was full of impatient children , dressed in their best , the girls with their heads such marvels of frizzing and curling that you could well imagine their hair had been screwed up in plaits and papers for several days previously , while the boys were shiny with soap and hair-oil", "context_len": 56}}}, "crumby": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Tea was over at last , and the crumby remains having been cleared away , the tables were pushed to one side of the great schoolroom to form the platform , while the benches were arranged in rows to accommodate the audience , which at present was indulging in an amount of noise only to be equalled by the Tower of Babel", "context_len": 62}}}, "heathens": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 104, "context": "Peggy , hard at work behind the scenes , put her eye to a hole in the curtain , and surveyed the prospect below , where children big and little were engaged in jumping over the forms , chasing each other round the stove , and generally acting more like monkeys at the Zoo than civilized human beings , while the Rector strove in vain to collect them at the empty end of the room to play games , and the curate , a shy young man fresh from Oxford , looked as if he would have preferred to wrestle with a consignment of heathens from the Cannibal Islands", "context_len": 109}}}, "hobbledehoys": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Wentworth 's upraised hands ; she set the boys to swing honey-pots , and the girls to play 'Drop the Handkerchief ' ; she boldly interposed her small person between the fists of two fighting hobbledehoys , and seized a little boy by the boot who had ventured to climb up the stove-pipe ; she welcomed some of the parents who had begun to arrive for the entertainment , and found them comfortable places on the benches , even nursing one of the babies while its mother went to the rescue of an older child , who was being forcibly held down and sat upon by several of its companions ; and by the time the bell was rung , and the audience requested to take their places", "context_len": 128}}}, "aspirates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "and getting so mixed up with his aspirates that he concluded with :", "context_len": 13}}}, "carols": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 51, "context": "The supper was over ; the wild boar roasted whole , the huge joints of mutton and beef , the made dishes , the various preparations of milk , had disappeared , the cheerful cup was handed round ; after which the tables were removed , the gleemen sang their Christmas carols , and all went merry as a `` marriage bell", "context_len": 62}}}, "warbled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The two Miss Prices from the Post-Office warbled a mild little duet , which gave unbounded satisfaction to their fond mamma , if to nobody else ; the blacksmith 's assistant roared out a sea song in a voice like a tempest ; and a young man on a visit to the miller gave a comic song , which quite took the company by storm", "context_len": 65}}}, "corduroy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "Archie , as the husband , in corduroy trousers , his shirt-sleeves rolled up , and a short clay pipe in his mouth , was a capital British workman , and his struggles in the performance of his domestic duties were the subject of much mirth", "context_len": 46}}}, "realistically": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 43, "context": "Peggy and Bobby as 'the children , ' with crumpled pinafores and smudged faces , were an equal success , for in watching Archie Peggy had forgotten her sudden shyness , and she now threw herself thoroughly into her part , howling most realistically when her nose was scrubbed in the wash-tub , or her hair combed with a fork ; while Bobby stole jam and resisted medicine with lifelike zeal", "context_len": 71}}}, "artistes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 58, "context": "The audience 'hoorayed ' and thumped with their thick boots on the floor , and shouted 'encore ' ; but as it was not possible to break the china , spill the medicine , and upset the kettle twice over without a considerable amount of preparation , they had to be content with the graceful bows which the artistes bestowed upon them", "context_len": 62}}}, "gentility": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Primrose 's efforts at gentility , declaring that the philosophy of the light-hearted Vicar exactly suited his own circumstances , and christening Lilian and Peggy 'Olivia ' and 'Sophia ' on the spot", "context_len": 33}}}, "exhibitors": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "As the days grew longer and lighter , the garden also claimed attention , and the children were busy digging , raking , and planting , for this year there were to be special classes in the flower-show for exhibitors under fifteen , and both Peggy and Bobby had secret hopes of a prize", "context_len": 54}}}, "bridesmaid": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Besides , you would want new shoes and gloves , and silk stockings , even if your bridesmaid 's dress would be smart enough", "context_len": 24}}}, "woodland": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 46, "context": "`` Happy , happy Ethelgiva ! '' said the mother as she kissed her darling , not without a maternal sigh , for she felt as if she were losing her only child , who had for so many a year been the light of their woodland home -- her only child , who had filled not simply her own place in their affections , but as far as she might the place of the loved Bertric", "context_len": 77}}}, "lavatory": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "They took Holmes major one day , and held him upside down with his head in the lavatory basin till he nearly choked , and they tied two others up as sparring-cocks to-day , and made them fight all dinner-time", "context_len": 40}}}, "tweaking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 74, "context": "It was a quiet spot , with long gardens leading to old-fashioned houses on the one side , and the tall palings of a cricket-field on the other -- just the spot where nobody would be likely to come along and make a disturbance , and so evidently Jones minor seemed to think , for he held Bobby pinned against the wall with one hand , while with the other he amused himself by tweaking his ears , pulling his hair , and any other tortures which his ingenious mind could suggest at the moment", "context_len": 95}}}, "ninepin": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The assault was so utterly unexpected that he rolled over like a ninepin", "context_len": 13}}}, "tweaked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "She punched his head , tweaked his ears , and hammered into the soft portions of his body until he roared for mercy , for , like all bullies , he was a coward at heart , and had a vague impression that some very superior force must suddenly have descended upon him", "context_len": 53}}}, "drivelling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "'The way you rolled him flat was the funniest sight I 've seen for many a year ! Get up , Jones , you sneaking , drivelling cur ! ' -- kicking the prostrate form of that fallen hero", "context_len": 39}}}, "lade": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "'She lade the villin flat on the erth , and I just wish you cud have seen her punch his hed , ' he wrote", "context_len": 25}}}, "nocked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "'She nocked him about like a pottatoe", "context_len": 7}}}, "wen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Peggy is awful strong wen her blud is up , and she sez she wuld do it agen to save my Life", "context_len": 22}}}, "fagging": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "I like fagging for him better than enything", "context_len": 8}}}, "christen": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "I can make a hog \u2019 s trough , and christen it a float , but to make one that will be stiff and light , and scull steady and true , there \u2019 s only one man round here can do it", "context_len": 43}}}, "unspoilt": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 57, "context": "It was funny to see how the young American citizen was merging into the British public schoolboy , for Archie was losing his Western accent , which only cropped out now and then when he was excited , and cricket and football were beginning to replace Indians and grizzlies in his conversation ; but he was totally unspoilt by his new life , and as jolly and hearty as ever", "context_len": 70}}}, "celandine": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 59, "context": "As she swung the gate open , and passed from the field into the lane , she saw a sight which for a moment made her hang back doubtfully , for a tramp lay stretched out full length asleep in the sunshine , his tattered clothes and broken boots a strange contrast to the bed of white daisies and celandine upon which he lay", "context_len": 64}}}, "pauperism": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "Everybody put on something new for the fair , and to have appeared on that occasion in a last year 's hat would have been an offence against public taste , or a confession of abject poverty scarcely removed above pauperism", "context_len": 41}}}, "bandboxes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 76, "context": "Nor was Nancy the only one who indulged in a little innocent vanity , for Joe , too , had been so fastidious in his choice of a red-and-blue spotted necktie and a walking-stick with an ivory handle that the children began to suspect the blacksmith 's rosy-cheeked daughter must be at the bottom of it ; and even David had taken a long-tailed coat and a beaver hat out of the retirement of some mysterious bandboxes and had been quite snappy and particular with Mrs", "context_len": 86}}}, "starching": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "David on the subject of the proper starching and ironing of his Sunday shirt", "context_len": 14}}}, "yokels": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "Tired-looking men in dirty shirt-sleeves were soon busy setting up swings and merry-go-rounds , theatres and shooting-galleries , while the arrival of a travelling menagerie was the occasion for the collecting of a whole crowd of small yokels , who studied the outside of the waggons with breathless delight", "context_len": 49}}}, "jackass": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "'Lion ! That be a jackass brayin ' , thee fule ! '", "context_len": 13}}}, "moke": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "'Jackass theeself ! Do n't I know a moke from a wild beast ? I tell 'ee 't is within the tent ! '", "context_len": 24}}}, "collies": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 19, "context": "The village street from end to end was an impassable block of charging bullocks , kicking ponies , barking collies , and bawling drovers , which overflowed past the Rectory and up to the Willows on the one hand , and nearly as far as the Abbey gates on the other", "context_len": 51}}}, "bawling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "The village street from end to end was an impassable block of charging bullocks , kicking ponies , barking collies , and bawling drovers , which overflowed past the Rectory and up to the Willows on the one hand , and nearly as far as the Abbey gates on the other", "context_len": 51}}}, "drovers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Most of the cows had been sold first thing , and were being driven away with much forcible language on the part of their drovers , and it was now the turn of the cart-horses , beautiful glossy creatures with tails tied up with straw and manes plaited with ribbons as if for May-day", "context_len": 54}}}, "octave": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Animals were lowing , bellowing , bleating , whinnying , or squeaking in every note of the octave ; cocks crew , ducks quacked , dogs barked , and men talked and shouted in Welsh and English till a chance comer might have imagined himself in pandemonium", "context_len": 47}}}, "quacked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Animals were lowing , bellowing , bleating , whinnying , or squeaking in every note of the octave ; cocks crew , ducks quacked , dogs barked , and men talked and shouted in Welsh and English till a chance comer might have imagined himself in pandemonium", "context_len": 47}}}, "manes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "Most of the cows had been sold first thing , and were being driven away with much forcible language on the part of their drovers , and it was now the turn of the cart-horses , beautiful glossy creatures with tails tied up with straw and manes plaited with ribbons as if for May-day", "context_len": 54}}}, "hurdles": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "So thither flocked the young and the old : the wood rangers and hunters from the forests of Newenham , where Herstan had right of wood cutting ; the men who wove baskets and hurdles of osier work from the river banks ; the theows who cultivated the home farm ; the ceorls who rented a hide of land here and a hide there -- all , the grandfather and the grandson , accepted the invitation to feast", "context_len": 78}}}, "booths": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Small booths sprang up like magic under the church wall , and cheap-jacks and travelling hucksters began to proclaim their wares", "context_len": 21}}}, "hucksters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Small booths sprang up like magic under the church wall , and cheap-jacks and travelling hucksters began to proclaim their wares", "context_len": 21}}}, "proclaim": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "It was the rule among Pony Express Riders that upon arriving within a half mile of a station , they should proclaim the fact by giving the coyote yell", "context_len": 29}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "`` And he said to her , `` God pardon thee ! '' [ 364 ] Then said she to him , `` O our lord the Sultan , I have a son , whose name is Alaeddin , and one day of the days he heard the crier proclaim that none should open his shop nor show himself in the thoroughfares of the city , [ 365 ] for that the Lady Bedrulbudour , the daughter of our lord the Sultan , was going to the bath", "context_len": 88}}}, "thrifty": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 110, "context": "There was the flower garden , delicious with all kinds of roses now in full bloom ; the very swing where papa used to swing when he was a boy , the stream and pond where he used to sail his boats and set up his water-mills ; and beyond all , the large farm-yard with its many outbuildings , looking almost like a village by itself ; while from one of the great barns whose wide doors stood open came the cackling of poultry and cooing of pigeons , the lowing of cows and oxen , and bleating of calves , all the pleasant noises of a large and thrifty farm", "context_len": 112}}}, "housewives": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The thrifty village housewives were doing a thriving business in tea and ginger-pop , for a cattle fair is thirsty work , and the inn was filled to overflowing", "context_len": 29}}}, "bandying": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "The crowd which surged along the main street was a laughing , merry-making crowd , indulging in a flow of broad chaff and humour , and bandying jokes with friends and strangers alike", "context_len": 33}}}, "posy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "'There 's Joe ! ' cried Bobby with deep interest , as that worthy passed sheepishly by , a posy of wall-flowers in his buttonhole and the blacksmith 's daughter hanging on his arm", "context_len": 34}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` I know , '' said Lem ; `` she wants to fix up her posy , like the gardener fixes 'em up to the big farm", "context_len": 27}}}, "buttonhole": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "'There 's Joe ! ' cried Bobby with deep interest , as that worthy passed sheepishly by , a posy of wall-flowers in his buttonhole and the blacksmith 's daughter hanging on his arm", "context_len": 34}}}, "humbugs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "'He 's actually buying them humbugs ! ' said Peggy", "context_len": 10}}}, "adjournment": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Divining the cause of this very unwonted lack of appetite on the part of his friends , Archie proposed an adjournment to one of the numerous stalls below", "context_len": 28}}}, "mint": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "How I thought of those who tithed mint and rue , and all manner of herbs , and passed over justice , mercy , and the love of God", "context_len": 29}}}, "showmen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "'All right , keep your hair on , old chaps ! ' cried Archie , nearly pulled to pieces among the rival showmen", "context_len": 23}}}, "stitched": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Number one proved to be the 'Wild Man from Borneo , ' a half-human looking creature with hairy arms ( 'Just monkey-skin stitched on to him , ' Archie assured the children ) , who sat jabbering in a corner of his cage , making occasional ape-like grabs at the clothing of the passers-by", "context_len": 54}}}, "jabbering": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "Number one proved to be the 'Wild Man from Borneo , ' a half-human looking creature with hairy arms ( 'Just monkey-skin stitched on to him , ' Archie assured the children ) , who sat jabbering in a corner of his cage , making occasional ape-like grabs at the clothing of the passers-by", "context_len": 54}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "Bomba heard the shrieks of the affrighted savages , the jabbering of the monkeys , and the roars of Polulu", "context_len": 20}}}, "overgrowth": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "After that came the giant and dwarf , the former a weedy specimen of unwholesome overgrowth , while the latter looked as if he had been reared upon gin to keep him small", "context_len": 33}}}, "memento": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 72, "context": "They spent quite a long time in the shooting-gallery , and won a cocoa-nut , which Bobby proudly carried round the fair , and they had their photographs taken in a group , but some air-bubbles having unfortunately got on to the plate , their countenances came out speckled as though they were suffering from a virulent attack of small-pox ; but Peggy kept it , all the same , as a memento of the occasion", "context_len": 76}}}, "cinematograph": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Archie peeped into the cinematograph , but judging it not very suitable for his little companions , marched them on to hear the gramophone instead , which was winding out a rather indistinguishable song", "context_len": 34}}}, "gramophone": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "Archie peeped into the cinematograph , but judging it not very suitable for his little companions , marched them on to hear the gramophone instead , which was winding out a rather indistinguishable song", "context_len": 34}}}, "ejection": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 111, "context": "Neither Peggy nor Bobby had ever seen a wild-beast show before , for those visits to the Zoo , which are the joy of little Londoners , do not fall to the share of country children , and Archie had quite a lively time keeping them out of harm 's way , for Peggy declared the leopard looked so exactly like Tabbyskins at home that she must positively try to stroke it , and was under the rope and up to the bars before Archie could seize her by her skirts and drag her back , while Bobby 's curiosity on the subject of jackals and hyenas nearly led to the ejection of the whole party from the tent", "context_len": 119}}}, "rodents": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The small rodents and the mongoose delighted Peggy , and they would have stopped for ever watching the monkeys , and feeding them on nuts and biscuits , but Archie had other plans", "context_len": 33}}}, "mongoose": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The small rodents and the mongoose delighted Peggy , and they would have stopped for ever watching the monkeys , and feeding them on nuts and biscuits , but Archie had other plans", "context_len": 33}}}, "acrobats": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "The flying lady fluttered across the tent so naturally that you quite forgot you could see the wire that supported her airy form ; the Japanese acrobats climbed ropes , hung head downwards from poles , and suspended themselves in a combination of marvellous attitudes , finally tying their agile bodies together in a knot ; a Hindoo conjuror performed marvels in the way of canaries from under pocket-handkerchiefs , umbrellas out of top-hats , even producing yards and yards of coloured ribbons out of his own open mouth , much to Bobby 's mystification ; while the horses pounded round the ring with quite commendable spirit , and a lady rider in gauze and spangles jumped through hoops on to their backs with reckless daring", "context_len": 126}}}, "ecstatically": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "Such an inducement sent even the attractions of the baby 'possum to the winds , and feeling that Archie was indeed a friend worth having , they trotted ecstatically under his wing to the great , flapping tent , where the flaming posters set forth the attractions of 'The Brothers Amalfi , ' 'Jingo , the Performing Pig , ' and the 'Marvellous Flying Girl", "context_len": 65}}}, "posters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "Such an inducement sent even the attractions of the baby 'possum to the winds , and feeling that Archie was indeed a friend worth having , they trotted ecstatically under his wing to the great , flapping tent , where the flaming posters set forth the attractions of 'The Brothers Amalfi , ' 'Jingo , the Performing Pig , ' and the 'Marvellous Flying Girl", "context_len": 65}}}, "spangles": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 115, "context": "The flying lady fluttered across the tent so naturally that you quite forgot you could see the wire that supported her airy form ; the Japanese acrobats climbed ropes , hung head downwards from poles , and suspended themselves in a combination of marvellous attitudes , finally tying their agile bodies together in a knot ; a Hindoo conjuror performed marvels in the way of canaries from under pocket-handkerchiefs , umbrellas out of top-hats , even producing yards and yards of coloured ribbons out of his own open mouth , much to Bobby 's mystification ; while the horses pounded round the ring with quite commendable spirit , and a lady rider in gauze and spangles jumped through hoops on to their backs with reckless daring", "context_len": 126}}}, "sorted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Peggy liked to see Rollo working with the sheep , the tips of his ears twitching and his faithful brown eyes bright with intelligence , as he cleverly sorted the lambs which Father had pointed out from the rest of the flock , and drove them neatly into the enclosure , coming up whimpering with pride for the praise which he knew awaited his efforts", "context_len": 65}}}, "bluebells": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "Instead of walking along the high-road , Peggy determined to take the path through the fields which skirted the preserves belonging to Lord Hazelford 's estate , for the finest primroses grew at the edge of the wood , and the earliest bluebells , and many a snug little nest might be found hidden away in those quiet hedgerows", "context_len": 59}}}, "blustering": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "It was around Christmas , and a cold blustering day , yet the water from the spring was quite warm , and had melted the snow for quite a distance in all directions", "context_len": 33}}}, "gatepost": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The two rolled over together , and Peggy clung trembling to the gatepost as she watched the confused heap at her feet , Rollo scratching , snarling , and biting like a wild beast , and the tramp kicking , fighting , and swearing in a way which made her blood go cold to hear", "context_len": 55}}}, "rushings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "Bobby , almost equally afflicted , had the added trial of trying to conceal his grief , for he regarded tears as unmanly , and the result was a peculiar shortness and roughness of manner , with frequent rushings away to the barn when his feelings overcame him", "context_len": 48}}}, "abbot": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 9, "context": "There 's a fine place round by the old abbot 's house , where the ground is soft , and we could dig easy ; and I 've a cousin in the slate-quarries at Bethogwen as has promised many a time to cut me a little tombstone as a present , if ever I was wantin ' one , so I 'll ask him to bring it next time he comes , and put Rollo 's name on it , and the day , and as how he died defendin ' you ; and we 'll fix it up nice , and plant flowers round , just as if it was the churchyard", "context_len": 113}}}, "obsequies": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 94, "context": "'Of course he will ! I wonder you can ask such a question ! Heaven would n't be heaven unless we found Rollo there ! We 'll wrap his body in the Union Jack , and pick all our best flowers to strew round him ; and you might fire off your old pistol over his grave , if Father will let you have any gunpowder -- a parting salute , you know , like they do for officers , ' said Peggy , cheering up a little at the thought of arranging the obsequies", "context_len": 95}}}, "blackthorn": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "'Suppose you dig just here , Joe , ' cried Peggy , selecting a spot where a blackthorn was bursting forth into a sheet of white blossom and the primroses were yellowest and best", "context_len": 34}}}, "yellowest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "'Suppose you dig just here , Joe , ' cried Peggy , selecting a spot where a blackthorn was bursting forth into a sheet of white blossom and the primroses were yellowest and best", "context_len": 34}}}, "tomes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "'They 're very funny ones , at any rate , ' said Peggy , picking up one of the despised tomes", "context_len": 21}}}, "shovelled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Joe carefully replaced the lid , and shovelled on the soil again , heaping it up , and smoothing it with the flat of his spade , in imitation of the village sexton", "context_len": 33}}}, "jonquils": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Father had refused to allow gunpowder , so the pistol was useless , but Peggy placed a wreath of white jonquils picked from her own garden upon the grave , and dropped so many tears over it that I do not think any dog could have been more truly mourned and regretted", "context_len": 52}}}, "epitaph": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "' I mean to make up a nice epitaph for him , in poetry if I can manage it -- something about his being such a beauty , and then dying doing his duty , because that would rhyme", "context_len": 39}}}, "halos": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "They fascinated her with their crude drawing and colours still vivid and bright -- saints with halos round their heads , kneeling rapt in prayer , with folded hands , in the midst of green fields and flowers , while the Virgin , clothed in blue and gold , appeared with a whole company of angels from the skies above ; patient martyrs , with wan faces upturned to heaven , while their persecutors flung stones , or heaped on the burning brands ; the blessed passing into the joys of Paradise , with the wicked writhing in the tormenting flames below ; and round all a curious illuminated bordering , where strange faces peered out of twisting foliage , and figures of birds and animals were intertwined", "context_len": 128}}}, "persecutors": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 64, "context": "still vivid and bright -- saints with halos round their heads , kneeling rapt in prayer , with folded hands , in the midst of green fields and flowers , while the Virgin , clothed in blue and gold , appeared with a whole company of angels from the skies above ; patient martyrs , with wan faces upturned to heaven , while their persecutors flung stones , or heaped on the burning brands ; the blessed passing into the joys of Paradise , with the wicked writhing in the tormenting flames below ; and round all a curious illuminated bordering , where strange faces peered out of twisting foliage , and figures of birds and animals were intertwined with patterns of flowers or the tail of a", "context_len": 128}}}, "brands": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 71, "context": "and bright -- saints with halos round their heads , kneeling rapt in prayer , with folded hands , in the midst of green fields and flowers , while the Virgin , clothed in blue and gold , appeared with a whole company of angels from the skies above ; patient martyrs , with wan faces upturned to heaven , while their persecutors flung stones , or heaped on the burning brands ; the blessed passing into the joys of Paradise , with the wicked writhing in the tormenting flames below ; and round all a curious illuminated bordering , where strange faces peered out of twisting foliage , and figures of birds and animals were intertwined with patterns of flowers or the tail of a capital letter", "context_len": 128}}}, "intertwined": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 116, "context": "and bright -- saints with halos round their heads , kneeling rapt in prayer , with folded hands , in the midst of green fields and flowers , while the Virgin , clothed in blue and gold , appeared with a whole company of angels from the skies above ; patient martyrs , with wan faces upturned to heaven , while their persecutors flung stones , or heaped on the burning brands ; the blessed passing into the joys of Paradise , with the wicked writhing in the tormenting flames below ; and round all a curious illuminated bordering , where strange faces peered out of twisting foliage , and figures of birds and animals were intertwined with patterns of flowers or the tail of a capital letter", "context_len": 128}}}, "palette": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "What patient fingers , she wondered , had toiled over these in days gone by , working with paint-pots and palette of gold to put the glory of paradise on his pages ? Had the world altered much in all these years ? And how little did the old artist think that his work would be found and marvelled at when he and his order were alike forgotten , and the very Abbey where he had lived and laboured had long since crumbled away !", "context_len": 85}}}, "creditor": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Norton , urged on by his solicitor , was a hard creditor , and would allow neither time nor mercy", "context_len": 20}}}, "rebuild": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 21, "context": "Norton had spoken freely of his plans in the neighbourhood , and it was well known that he intended to almost rebuild the house , pulling down the ruins and all the more early portion , and turning the whole into a handsome modern residence", "context_len": 45}}}, "windbags": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "'They be mostly windbags , they candidates , ' observed Tom Slater , the village blacksmith , settling himself more easily in a corner of the bench by the fireside , and holding up a stalwart finger for the pot-boy", "context_len": 40}}}, "distilleries": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "'Ay , he be that , and scant honest , ' cried the stout miller , with a lively remembrance of sundry hard transactions with grain for the distilleries , in which , to his chagrin , he had had distinctly the worst side of the bargain", "context_len": 47}}}, "churchwarden": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "'Earned by the sweat o ' my brow , Tom , ' said the miller , puffing away at his long churchwarden pipe", "context_len": 23}}}, "oracularly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "' I 've not any grudge against them as has money , ' observed old Ephraim oracularly , 'but when it 's used to turn them as has n't out of their own , it 's time them Socialists had a innings and stepped in", "context_len": 45}}}, "waxing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "So [ 541 ] he went daily waxing in goodliness of repute and in praise and the love of him redoubled in the hearts of the common folk and he was magnified in men 's eyes", "context_len": 36}}}, "boycott": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "'We might boycott Norton , may be , ' he observed thoughtfully , 'and mek the place too hot to hold him", "context_len": 22}}}, "dissolving": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "The ridge which caused Alden anxiety had been crossed , and now when he looked back he traced the outlines of the vague column of smoke that was slow in dissolving in the summer air", "context_len": 35}}}, "drier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 68, "context": "'Oh , I say , look here , you know , ' cried the boy , ruffling up his chestnut locks with both hands , which was a way he had when upset , 'if you go out to Australia , I shall come too ! I could persuade aunt to go in a jiffey -- the doctor said she 'd be a million times better in a drier climate -- and we 'd take the farm next to yours", "context_len": 80}}}, "jollier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 48, "context": "Now my poor old dad 's gone , and my mother 's married again , and the boys all trading off on their own account , I do n't want to go back to Colorado ; but I like colonial life , and farming would be a lot jollier than school , any way , for the fellows in my house are awful Johnnies , they can talk of nothing but games , and laugh at one for a crank if one tries to make things", "context_len": 86}}}, "ranches": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 53, "context": "There 's no end of jolly fun going on on board ship , I can tell you , and you 'll have a real good time on the voyage out , and at the ports , especially with me to show you around ; and when we get fixed up on our new ranches I guess you 'll allow that things are pretty first-rate ! '", "context_len": 66}}}, "monuments": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "I have a feeling as if the Crusaders and the Elizabethan lady and gentleman on the monuments would miss us when we go away , ' said Peggy , relapsing into pensiveness once more", "context_len": 34}}}, "tombstones": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 59, "context": "Did n't they come sailing over from Denmark and Normandy , and all sorts of places , to settle down in England , which was a new country then , just as we 're thinking of going out to Australia ? Five hundred years hence we shall be quite ancient history ourselves , and folks can romance over our tombstones if they feel inclined", "context_len": 64}}}, "volcano": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 39, "context": "William Lang told of his visit to Oak Swamp , and how he had fled at the sight of the boys , fearing they wanted to cause his arrest , and he also mentioned his trips to the mud volcano , and how he had run away at the sound of some one stirring in the campers ' tent , likewise how he had led the boys a chase about the town , just prior to the accident on the pole", "context_len": 81}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "With a final look at the place of the mud volcano the boys turned back toward camp", "context_len": 17}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "That mud volcano water is partly salty", "context_len": 7}}}, "upheaval": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 64, "context": "the trees would bud and the flowers blossom , and the crops grow , when they were not there to watch it all , for most of us have a kind of feeling that we are the important centre around which Nature turns , instead of only mere spectators of her varying moods , and sometimes it felt so impossible that such an utter upheaval in their lives could really come to pass that she would have to shake herself to believe that it was not all a bad dream ; but as she noticed the quiet preparations that went on , and the added worry on Father 's face , she realized that it was only too true , and that every day was bringing them nearer", "context_len": 128}}}, "repine": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "At first she had been inclined to grumble and repine , and say that life was using them hardly , but something which the Rector ( always the family confidant ) wrote to her in one of his frequent letters made her stop and think", "context_len": 45}}}, "confidant": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 29, "context": "At first she had been inclined to grumble and repine , and say that life was using them hardly , but something which the Rector ( always the family confidant ) wrote to her in one of his frequent letters made her stop and think", "context_len": 45}}}, "tripper": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "May drew to a close with a burst of warm weather , and the Whitsuntide holidays promised to prove all that the heart of the cheap tripper might desire , though beyond a chance cyclist or two that article was as unknown as the dodo in quiet Gorswen , where fortunately the charms of the scenery had not yet been spoilt by picnic parties leaving greasy sandwich papers and ginger-beer bottles in the woods , and demanding noisy entertainment in the village , nor the youth of the neighbourhood corrupted into hanging round the public-house doors to listen to the mirth and songs of the excursionists within , or offer faded bunches of flowers in exchange for halfpence", "context_len": 118}}}, "cyclist": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "May drew to a close with a burst of warm weather , and the Whitsuntide holidays promised to prove all that the heart of the cheap tripper might desire , though beyond a chance cyclist or two that article was as unknown as the dodo in quiet Gorswen , where fortunately the charms of the scenery had not yet been spoilt by picnic parties leaving greasy sandwich papers and ginger-beer bottles in the woods , and demanding noisy entertainment in the village , nor the youth of the neighbourhood corrupted into hanging round the public-house doors to listen to the mirth and songs of the excursionists within , or offer faded bunches of flowers in exchange for halfpence", "context_len": 118}}}, "dodo": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 44, "context": "May drew to a close with a burst of warm weather , and the Whitsuntide holidays promised to prove all that the heart of the cheap tripper might desire , though beyond a chance cyclist or two that article was as unknown as the dodo in quiet Gorswen , where fortunately the charms of the scenery had not yet been spoilt by picnic parties leaving greasy sandwich papers and ginger-beer bottles in the woods , and demanding noisy entertainment in the village , nor the youth of the neighbourhood corrupted into hanging round the public-house doors to listen to the mirth and songs of the excursionists within , or offer faded bunches of flowers in exchange for halfpence", "context_len": 118}}}, "halfpence": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 117, "context": "May drew to a close with a burst of warm weather , and the Whitsuntide holidays promised to prove all that the heart of the cheap tripper might desire , though beyond a chance cyclist or two that article was as unknown as the dodo in quiet Gorswen , where fortunately the charms of the scenery had not yet been spoilt by picnic parties leaving greasy sandwich papers and ginger-beer bottles in the woods , and demanding noisy entertainment in the village , nor the youth of the neighbourhood corrupted into hanging round the public-house doors to listen to the mirth and songs of the excursionists within , or offer faded bunches of flowers in exchange for halfpence", "context_len": 118}}}, "uprooting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "Then , suddenly , as the tidal wave tops all other waves , came a gigantic burst of wind that bore great trees before it as though they had been toys , bending them , breaking them , uprooting them , whirling them about as in a fantastic dance", "context_len": 49}}}, "tweeds": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "There were gentlemen , young , old and middle-aged , some in tourist tweeds , some in boating flannels , and some in sober black , most of them with books or road-maps in their hands , while a sprinkling of ladies , both grave and frivolous , in light summer costumes and with gay parasols , completed the party", "context_len": 60}}}, "antiquarians": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "The antiquarians did not seem to be discussing anything very learned just at present , for they had drawn out flasks , and packets of sandwiches , and were engaged in picnicking upon the stones in a truly modern fashion , while occasional bursts of laughter were wafted along the air", "context_len": 51}}}, "picnicking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "The antiquarians did not seem to be discussing anything very learned just at present , for they had drawn out flasks , and packets of sandwiches , and were engaged in picnicking upon the stones in a truly modern fashion , while occasional bursts of laughter were wafted along the air", "context_len": 51}}}, "bursts": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 44, "context": "Maggie did not answer , except by gently kissing the poor hurt hand , which lay upon the coverlet ; but it was plainly to be seen that she was a good deal excited ; and Uncle Ruthven , fearing one of her sudden bursts of crying , said the children had stayed long enough , and led them from the room", "context_len": 62}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "We will fill his body with it till he bursts", "context_len": 10}}}, "revelled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 51, "context": "The children could hear most of what he said , and a very interesting account of the Abbey it proved to be , with a description of the size and extent of the old buildings , and the life led by the ancient monks , which quite delighted Peggy , who revelled in Scott 's novels and historical stories , and which made the past days rise up so clearly before her that she could almost fancy the bell ringing for vespers , and hear the rustle of the gray robes of the friars as they passed silently up the chancel to their stalls in the choir", "context_len": 107}}}, "vespers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 81, "context": "The children could hear most of what he said , and a very interesting account of the Abbey it proved to be , with a description of the size and extent of the old buildings , and the life led by the ancient monks , which quite delighted Peggy , who revelled in Scott 's novels and historical stories , and which made the past days rise up so clearly before her that she could almost fancy the bell ringing for vespers , and hear the rustle of the gray robes of the friars as they passed silently up the chancel to their stalls in the choir", "context_len": 107}}}, "friars": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 93, "context": "The children could hear most of what he said , and a very interesting account of the Abbey it proved to be , with a description of the size and extent of the old buildings , and the life led by the ancient monks , which quite delighted Peggy , who revelled in Scott 's novels and historical stories , and which made the past days rise up so clearly before her that she could almost fancy the bell ringing for vespers , and hear the rustle of the gray robes of the friars as they passed silently up the chancel to their stalls in the choir", "context_len": 107}}}, "usurp": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "The members seemed to take copious notes in pocket-books , and asked occasional questions , one literary-looking lady , in spectacles and a large black hat with nodding feathers , being particularly insistent , and volunteering so much information that she threatened to usurp the place of the lecturer , and had to be gently suppressed , while an old gentleman distributed pamphlets broadcast , declaring he had had them specially printed for the occasion", "context_len": 75}}}, "starch": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "Seemingly he thought considerably more of his books than of such details as his toilet , for there was a very large expanse of gray sock visible above his dusty shoes , and his limp shirt-front looked guiltless of starch", "context_len": 40}}}, "grunts": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 44, "context": "It was evident that his pitch of enthusiasm , or perhaps physical endurance , was not equal to that of his companion , for he panted with heat as he held the other end of the yard measure , and gave vent to occasional grunts of disapprobation as he toiled painfully to the tops of mounds to get bird's-eye views of the outlined cells , or take snap-shots of the remains of the windows and columns", "context_len": 76}}}, "disapprobation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 46, "context": "It was evident that his pitch of enthusiasm , or perhaps physical endurance , was not equal to that of his companion , for he panted with heat as he held the other end of the yard measure , and gave vent to occasional grunts of disapprobation as he toiled painfully to the tops of mounds to get bird's-eye views of the outlined cells , or take snap-shots of the remains of the windows and columns", "context_len": 76}}}, "abbeys": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "'Interesting , most interesting ! Abounding at every yard with testimony of the past , and in some ways unique , and a valuable contribution to our list of early English abbeys", "context_len": 32}}}, "unsympathetically": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "'You will be having tea soon , ' said he of the two overcoats unsympathetically , 'and I am most anxious to compare your notes and measurements with my own", "context_len": 30}}}, "perilously": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "Not being quite certain how her efforts at hospitality might be received at headquarters , Peggy watched Nancy successfully out of the way , and dashing into the dairy , emerged presently with a jug of milk and two glasses perilously balanced on a tray , which she nearly upset in her efforts to elude the returning deity of the kitchen", "context_len": 61}}}, "nectar": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "'Is this nectar which you are offering us , fair nymph ? Doctor , this is indeed a godsend ! Allow me to pour you out a glass of this beverage", "context_len": 31}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Ah ! nectar indeed ! ' -- as he set down his empty tumbler", "context_len": 14}}}, "beverage": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 39, "context": "In a little while they were ready to start , each one with a few sandwiches , while Bart , in addition , carried a small coffee pot , and a supply of the ground material for making the beverage in the woods ; water could be had by melting snow over a fire they would build", "context_len": 57}}}, "bouncing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "The colonel had been bouncing around , groaning and yelling ever since he had awakened to a realization of his desperate plight", "context_len": 22}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Alden noted the play of the muscles between the shoulders , where they were not hidden by the bouncing quiver", "context_len": 20}}}, "queerer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "'It was something much queerer than that -- a wooden box full of old books , with writing you ca n't read , and strange little pictures all round the pages", "context_len": 31}}}, "breviaries": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "'And there seem to be some exquisite breviaries and `` Hours of the Virgin", "context_len": 14}}}, "bindings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "'Some of the bindings are queer , too , ' put in Peggy , hunting out one of her favourites", "context_len": 20}}}, "presides": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Price presides at the Post Office , rubicund and smiling ; and old Ephraim totters down from his cottage to the Bluebell Arms , a living testimony to the healthfulness of the district", "context_len": 33}}}, "healthfulness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "Price presides at the Post Office , rubicund and smiling ; and old Ephraim totters down from his cottage to the Bluebell Arms , a living testimony to the healthfulness of the district", "context_len": 33}}}, "helpmeet": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "She makes an excellent helpmeet , and an ideal clergyman 's wife", "context_len": 12}}}, "squandered": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "But already the Abbey has prospered to such an extent that little by little the Vaughans are buying back some of the land which their ancestors had squandered , and in time the estate may once more be of some importance in the county", "context_len": 44}}}, "ranch": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 11, "context": "But Blue -- Elizabeth , you know that this is your ranch , and that your father wanted you brought up to know all about it , so as to be able to manage things for yourself a bit -- at a pinch", "context_len": 43}}}, "ashe": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Ashe came up , this here place needs weeding", "context_len": 9}}}, "refolded": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "Roger slowly refolded the paper , looking the other over closely", "context_len": 11}}}, "elizabeth": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "ELIZABETH", "context_len": 1}}}, "ridiculousness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "You see , she added , quickly , flushing over the ridiculousness of her reply , I \u2019 m not used to being called anything but Blue Bonnet", "context_len": 28}}}, "questioningly": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 18, "context": "The pony lifted his head , with the blades of grass dripping from his jaws , and looked questioningly at the youth , who whistled again and walked in his direction", "context_len": 31}}}, "unimaginative": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "She was short and fair ; rather unimaginative and decidedly conscientious", "context_len": 11}}}, "armchairs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "They went out to the shady front piazza where Blue Bonnet drew forward a couple of wicker armchairs", "context_len": 18}}}, "irresponsible": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Her husband , a good workman but irresponsible , had , like Keimer , taken off to the West Indies to escape debts", "context_len": 23}}}, "goldsmith": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "When [ 312 ] the goldsmith heard this , `` Out on this accursed one , '' cried he , `` who fleeceth the servants of God the Most High ! '' Then he looked at Alaeddin and said to him , `` O my son , this Jew is a cheat , who hath cheated thee and laughed at thee , for that the silver of this thy platter is pure and fine ; and I have weighed it and find its worth threescore diners and ten ; so , an it please thee take its price , take [ it ]", "context_len": 103}}}, "platter": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 69, "context": "When [ 312 ] the goldsmith heard this , `` Out on this accursed one , '' cried he , `` who fleeceth the servants of God the Most High ! '' Then he looked at Alaeddin and said to him , `` O my son , this Jew is a cheat , who hath cheated thee and laughed at thee , for that the silver of this thy platter is pure and fine ; and I have weighed it and find its worth threescore diners and ten ; so , an it please thee take its price , take [ it ]", "context_len": 103}}}, "diner": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` `` And how much , '' asked the goldsmith , `` useth he to give thee to its price ? '' And Alaeddin said , `` He useth to give me a diner", "context_len": 34}}}, "threescore": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 84, "context": "When [ 312 ] the goldsmith heard this , `` Out on this accursed one , '' cried he , `` who fleeceth the servants of God the Most High ! '' Then he looked at Alaeddin and said to him , `` O my son , this Jew is a cheat , who hath cheated thee and laughed at thee , for that the silver of this thy platter is pure and fine ; and I have weighed it and find its worth threescore diners and ten ; so , an it please thee take its price , take [ it ]", "context_len": 103}}}, "diners": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 85, "context": "When [ 312 ] the goldsmith heard this , `` Out on this accursed one , '' cried he , `` who fleeceth the servants of God the Most High ! '' Then he looked at Alaeddin and said to him , `` O my son , this Jew is a cheat , who hath cheated thee and laughed at thee , for that the silver of this thy platter is pure and fine ; and I have weighed it and find its worth threescore diners and ten ; so , an it please thee take its price , take [ it ]", "context_len": 103}}}, "trickery": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 68, "context": "`` Now the Lady Bedrulbudour had no knowledge of the lamp and its properties , neither knew she that this it was which had brought Alaeddin her husband to that great estate , and it was the utmost of her desire to prove and see the wit of this man who bartered new for old , nor was any one aware of the Maugrabin enchanter 's craft and trickery", "context_len": 69}}}, "sufficiency": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 30, "context": "Then he bade lay breakfast ; [ 503 ] so they laid [ it ] and they all broke their fast ; and after they had eaten and drunken their sufficiency and had finished and the servants had removed the tables from before them , Alaeddin turned to the Sultan and said to him , `` O my lord , [ belike ] Thy Grace will vouchsafe to honour me this day at the morning-meal [ 503 ] with the Lady Bedrulbudour , thy precious daughter , and be Thy Grace 's company all thy viziers and the chief officers of thy state", "context_len": 103}}}, "squandering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 63, "context": "Thenceforward , whenassoever the price of one platter was spent , he would carry another to the old goldsmith , and on this wise he and his mother increased in substance ; but they ceased not to live at their sufficiency , [ 313 ] midwise [ betwixt rich and poor ] , [ 314 ] without excessive spending [ 315 ] or squandering", "context_len": 64}}}, "striplings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "As for Alaeddin , he left idleness and the commerce of striplings and took to consorting with grown men ; [ 316 ] nay , he would go every day to the market of the merchants and sit with the great and the small of them and question of the ways and fashions of commerce and the prices of articles of merchandise [ 317 ] and otherwhat", "context_len": 67}}}, "consorting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "As for Alaeddin , he left idleness and the commerce of striplings and took to consorting with grown men ; [ 316 ] nay , he would go every day to the market of the merchants and sit with the great and the small of them and question of the ways and fashions of commerce and the prices of articles of merchandise [ 317 ] and otherwhat", "context_len": 67}}}, "jewellers": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 119, "context": "Thou sayst , O my mother , that I have no gift to present to the Sultan , according to the wont of the folk , whilst in fact I have by me a gift and an offering , the like whereof methinketh no king ever possessed , no , nor aught to match therewith ; for [ 338 ] thou must know , O my mother , that the fruits , which I brought in the purses [ 339 ] from the treasure and which I deemed glass or crystal , are very jewels , methinketh all the kings of the world may not compass the least of them , and I , of my companying with the jewellers , know that they are precious stones", "context_len": 127}}}, "whenas": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 59, "context": "He used also to go to the market of the goldsmiths and the market of the jewellers , and there he would sit and look upon the different kinds of jewels and see them bought and sold ; whereby he became aware that the fruits of the trees , wherewith he had filled the purses , [ 318 ] whenas he was in the treasure , were neither glass nor crystal , but jewels , and knew that he had happened upon great wealth , such as kings might nowise compass", "context_len": 91}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 67, "context": "`` O my son , '' replied she , `` true it is that the present is exceedingly costly and precious and as thou sayest , none hath the like thereof ; but who shall dare to come forward and seek of the Sultan his daughter Bedrulbudour ? Nay , I dare not adventure myself and say to him , ' I want thy daughter , ' whenas he asketh me , 'What wouldst thou ? ' Marry , O my son , , my tongue will be tied", "context_len": 89}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 74, "context": "Now , by the decree of destiny , Fatimeh came down on the morrow to the city and the enchanter , going forth the Khan in the morning , saw the folk crowding together ; so he went up , to see what was toward , and found Fatimeh standing , whilst every one who had a pain or an ache came to her , seeking her blessing and soliciting her prayers , and whenas she stroked him , he was made whole of his ailment", "context_len": 86}}}, "donning": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 55, "context": "He ceased not to go daily to the market of the jewellers and to clap up acquaintance with the folk , making friends with them and questioning them of buying and selling and giving and taking and dear and cheap , till , one day of the days , he arose in the morning and donning his clothes , went forth , intending , as of wont , for the jewellers ' market ; but , as he went , he heard the crier proclaiming aloud on this wise , `` By commandment of the Lord of Beneficence , the king of the age and monarch of the time and the tide , let all the folk shut their shops and stores and enter their houses , for", "context_len": 128}}}, "whoso": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 110, "context": "and dear and cheap , till , one day of the days , he arose in the morning and donning his clothes , went forth , intending , as of wont , for the jewellers ' market ; but , as he went , he heard the crier proclaiming aloud on this wise , `` By commandment of the Lord of Beneficence , the king of the age and monarch of the time and the tide , let all the folk shut their shops and stores and enter their houses , for that the Lady Bedrulbudour , daughter of the Sultan , purposeth to go to the bath , and whoso transgresseth the commandment , his punishment shall be death and his blood be on his own head", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 55, "context": "Accordingly , [ 387 ] after he had supped , he went into his chamber and locking the door on himself , fetched the lamp and rubbed it ; whereupon the genie at once appeared to him and said , `` Seek what thou wilt , for I am thy slave and the slave of whoso hath in his hand the lamp , I and all the slaves of the lamp", "context_len": 71}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "' Consequently whoso entrusteth a head to the rewwas is preoccupied and sleeps not", "context_len": 14}}}, "goodliness": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 29, "context": "He passed no folk but they were descanting upon the palace and its magnificence and talking of Alaeddin 's grace and comeliness and his bounty and munificence and the goodliness of his manners and disposition ; so [ 555 ] he went up to one of those who were extolling Alaeddin on this wise and said to him , `` Prithee , fair youth , who is this whom you describe and praise ? '' `` O man , '' replied the other , `` meseemeth thou art a stranger and comest from afar ; but , granting thou art from a far country , hast thou not heard of the Amir Alaeddin , whose repute , methought , filled the earth , and of his palace ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "saith": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 90, "context": "His mother was perplexed at his case , unknowing what had befallen him , and bethought herself that belike he was sick ; so she came up to him and questioned him , saying , `` O my son , an thou feel aught of pain or otherwhat , tell me , that I may go fetch thee a physician , more by token there is presently in the city a physician from the land of the Arabs , whom the Sultan hath sent to bring hither , and report saith of him that he is exceeding skilful ; so [ tell me ] if thou art sick , that I may go and call him to thee", "context_len": 118}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 93, "context": "Accordingly , when she came up , he saw her and viewed her well , beyond his wish ; and from that time till now , O King of the Age , life hath not been pleasant to him [ 366 ] and he will e'en have me seek her of Thy Grace , [ 367 ] so thou mayst marry her with him , and I can not do away this conceit from his wit , for that the love of her hath gotten possession of his vitals , so that he saith to me , 'Know , O mother mine , that , except I attain my desire , assuredly I am a dead man", "context_len": 117}}}, "describers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 72, "context": "Presently , the Sultan 's daughter came forth and went round about the city and its thoroughfares and diverted herself by viewing it ; then she repaired to the bath and when she came thither , she lifted her face-veil , as she entered ; whereupon her face shone out , as it were the resplendent sun or a precious pearl , and she was as saith of her one of her describers :", "context_len": 74}}}, "kohl": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Who sprinkled the kohl of enchantment upon her eyes And gathered the bloom of the rose from her cheeks , fruit-wise ? And who was it let down the curtained night of her hair And eke through its glooms made the light of her forehead rise ?", "context_len": 47}}}, "extolled": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 26, "context": "When she raised the veil from her face and Alaeddin saw her , he said , `` Verily , her fashion glorifieth the Great Creator and extolled be the perfection of Him who made her and graced her with this beauty and goodliness ! '' And his back was cloven in sunder , [ 321 ] when he saw her ; his thought was confounded and his understanding [ 322 ] dazed and the love of her gat hold upon his whole heart ; so he turned back and returning home , went in to his mother , like one distraught", "context_len": 101}}}, "sunder": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 51, "context": "When she raised the veil from her face and Alaeddin saw her , he said , `` Verily , her fashion glorifieth the Great Creator and extolled be the perfection of Him who made her and graced her with this beauty and goodliness ! '' And his back was cloven in sunder , [ 321 ] when he saw her ; his thought was confounded and his understanding [ 322 ] dazed and the love of her gat hold upon his whole heart ; so he turned back and returning home , went in to his mother , like one distraught", "context_len": 101}}}, "gat": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 77, "context": "When she raised the veil from her face and Alaeddin saw her , he said , `` Verily , her fashion glorifieth the Great Creator and extolled be the perfection of Him who made her and graced her with this beauty and goodliness ! '' And his back was cloven in sunder , [ 321 ] when he saw her ; his thought was confounded and his understanding [ 322 ] dazed and the love of her gat hold upon his whole heart ; so he turned back and returning home , went in to his mother , like one distraught", "context_len": 101}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Moreover , he plundered their goods and possessions and gat him spoil beyond count or reckoning , wherewith he returned in triumph , [ having gained ] a great victory , and entered the city , which had adorned itself for him of its joy in him", "context_len": 47}}}, "betided": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Then she told her mother the queen all that had betided her that night and how they had taken her bridegroom , leaving her alone , and how after a little there came another youth and lay down in the place of her bridegroom , putting a sword between himself and her ; `` and in the morning '' [ quoth she ] `` he who had brought us thither returned and taking us up , carried us back to our place here : and hardly had he brought us hither and left us when my father the Sultan entered and I had neither heart nor tongue to answer him for stress of fright and trembling which possessed me", "context_len": 120}}}, "ail": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "She bespoke him and he answered her neither yea nor nay ; then she brought him the morning-meal , as he abode on this wise , and said to him , `` O my son , what hath betided thee ? Doth there ail thee aught ? Tell me what hath befallen thee , for that , against thy wont , I bespeak thee and thou answerest me not", "context_len": 69}}}, "bespeak": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 42, "context": "`` When [ 592 ] she had made an end of her story , Alaeddin said to her , `` Tell me , what does this accursed one purpose with thee ; what saith he to thee and of what doth he bespeak thee and what is his will of thee ? '' `` Every day , '' answered the princess , `` he cometh to me once and no more and seeketh to draw me to his love , willing me take him in thy stead and forget and renounce thee ; nay , he told me that my father the Sultan had cut off thy head", "context_len": 108}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 47, "context": "But , when he saw that she heartened herself for that which he set forth to her of its virtues , he feared lest she should talk of this to the folk , so he said to her , `` O my mother , beware lest thou bespeak any of the lamp and its uses , for that this is our fortune ; be careful [ 345 ] and exceed not in speech thereof to any one , lest we lose it and lose this our present prosperity , for that it is from it", "context_len": 95}}}, "unknowing": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` And when , '' [ quoth she ] ; `` I saw this [ seeming ] lack of wit in him , I fell to laughing at him , unknowing his perfidy and his intent ; so I took an old lamp that was in my husband 's pavilion and sent it by the eunuch , who exchanged it with him for a new lamp ; and next day , O my father , at daybreak , we found ourselves in Africa , with the palace and all that was therein ; and I knew not the properties of the lamp which I had exchanged , till my husband Alaeddin came to us and contrived against the Maugrabin a device whereby he delivered us from him", "context_len": 127}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "He would leave the gully here and strike homeward toward the cabin of Pipina , where Casson lay , all unknowing of the danger that threatened him", "context_len": 27}}}, "belike": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "His mother was perplexed at his case , unknowing what had befallen him , and bethought herself that belike he was sick ; so she came up to him and questioned him , saying , `` O my son , an thou feel aught of pain or otherwhat , tell me , that I may go fetch thee a physician , more by token there is presently in the city a physician from the land of the Arabs , whom the Sultan hath sent to bring hither , and report saith of him that he is exceeding skilful ; so [ tell me ] if thou art sick , that I may go and call him to thee", "context_len": 118}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 89, "context": "Tell me an thou have a want , that I may accomplish it unto thee ; '' whereupon she kissed the earth a second time and called down blessings upon him , then answered , `` Ay , O King of the Age , as thy head liveth , I have indeed a want ; but before all things do thou give me thine assurance , [ 362 ] so I may make bold to prefer my suit to the hearing of our lord the Sultan , for that belike Thy Grace will find it a strange one", "context_len": 98}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "And belike my father is vexed with me ; wherefore I prithee , O my mother , tell him the cause of this my case , so he be not wroth with me for my failure to answer him neither blame me , but excuse me", "context_len": 46}}}, "usurped": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 89, "context": "the crier proclaiming that none should open his shop nor stand in the road , so the Lady Bedrulbudour might pass to the bath ; but I saw her even as she is , for that , when she came to the door of the bath , she lifted her veil , and when I noted her favour and viewed that noble form of hers , there befell me , O my mother , a passion of yearning for love of her and desire of her [ 324 ] usurped mine every part ; nor can I ever more have ease , except I get her , and I purpose , therefore , to demand her of the Sultan her father in the way of law and righteousness", "context_len": 128}}}, "encompass": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "When Alaeddin 's mother heard her son 's speech , she thought little of his wit and said to him , `` O my son , the name of God encompass thee ! Meseemeth thou hast lost thy wit ; return to thy senses , [ 325 ] O my son , and be not like the madmen ! '' `` Nay , O my mother , '' replied he , `` I have not lost my wits nor am I mad ; and this thy speech shall not change that which is in my mind , nor is rest possible to me except I get the darling of my heart , the lovely Lady Bedrulbudour", "context_len": 116}}}, "madmen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 57, "context": "When Alaeddin 's mother heard her son 's speech , she thought little of his wit and said to him , `` O my son , the name of God encompass thee ! Meseemeth thou hast lost thy wit ; return to thy senses , [ 325 ] O my son , and be not like the madmen ! '' `` Nay , O my mother , '' replied he , `` I have not lost my wits nor am I mad ; and this thy speech shall not change that which is in my mind , nor is rest possible to me except I get the darling of my heart , the lovely Lady Bedrulbudour", "context_len": 116}}}, "trustier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 87, "context": "Put away from thee this extravagance : [ 326 ] who shall undertake an affair like this and demand it of the Sultan ? Meknoweth not how thou wilt do to make this request of the Sultan , and if thou speak sooth , [ 327 ] by whom wilt thou make it ? '' `` O my mother , '' rejoined Alaeddin , `` by whom [ should I make ] a request like this , when thou art at hand , and whom have I trustier [ 328 ] than thyself ? Wherefore my intent is that thou shalt make this request for me", "context_len": 106}}}, "peers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 103, "context": "son , '' quoth she , `` God deliver me from this ! What , have I lost my wits like thee ? Put away this thought from thy mind and bethink thee who thou art , O my son , -- the son of a tailor , the poorest and least of the tailors in this city , and I also am thy mother and my folk are exceeding poor ; so how wilt thou dare to demand the Sultan 's daughter , whom her father would not vouchsafe to marry with kings ' sons and Sultans , except they were his peers in puissance and rank and noblesse ; nay , were they one degree less than he , he would not give them his daughter", "context_len": 128}}}, "puissance": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 105, "context": "son , '' quoth she , `` God deliver me from this ! What , have I lost my wits like thee ? Put away this thought from thy mind and bethink thee who thou art , O my son , -- the son of a tailor , the poorest and least of the tailors in this city , and I also am thy mother and my folk are exceeding poor ; so how wilt thou dare to demand the Sultan 's daughter , whom her father would not vouchsafe to marry with kings ' sons and Sultans , except they were his peers in puissance and rank and noblesse ; nay , were they one degree less than he , he would not give them his daughter", "context_len": 128}}}, "noblesse": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 109, "context": "son , '' quoth she , `` God deliver me from this ! What , have I lost my wits like thee ? Put away this thought from thy mind and bethink thee who thou art , O my son , -- the son of a tailor , the poorest and least of the tailors in this city , and I also am thy mother and my folk are exceeding poor ; so how wilt thou dare to demand the Sultan 's daughter , whom her father would not vouchsafe to marry with kings ' sons and Sultans , except they were his peers in puissance and rank and noblesse ; nay , were they one degree less than he , he would not give them his daughter", "context_len": 128}}}, "madwoman": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 64, "context": "As for me , how can I adventure upon this danger and this effrontery ? Moreover , O my son , on what wise shall I demand thee his daughter of the Sultan and how shall I avail to go in to him ? Nay , if they question me , what shall I answer them ? Most like they will deem me a madwoman", "context_len": 65}}}, "clement": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "And suppose I gain admission to the presence , what shall I take by way of offering to the Sultan 's highness ? It [ 333 ] is true , O my son , that the Sultan is clement and rejecteth none that cometh to him for protection or craveth a boon of him , for that he is bountiful and beneficent unto all , great and small ; [ 334 ] but he bestoweth his favours upon those who are deserving thereof or who have done some feat of arms before him or have wrought for the service or defence of the realm ; and thou , O my son , tell me , what hast thou done for [ 335 ] the Sultan or the realm", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "The Sultan , that he might learn what her suit was and for that he was of his nature exceeding clement , gave her his assurance and bidding all who were with him go out forthright , abode alone [ with her ] , he and the Grand Vizier", "context_len": 49}}}, "attainment": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` And she said to him , `` Glad tidings , O my son ! Let thy heart rejoice and thine eye be solaced in the attainment of thy desire , for that the Sultan accepteth thine offering , to wit , the bride gift and the dowry of the Lady Bedrulbudour , and she is thy bride and this , O my son , is the night of your [ 443 ] bridal and thy going in to the Lady Bedrulbudour", "context_len": 82}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` If the achievement thereof ( or attainment thereunto ) will be possible unto thee [ by or by dint of ] fortitude , '' ]", "context_len": 26}}}, "hundredth": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 45, "context": "Be not concerned for the gift , for these be exceeding precious jewels , and know , O my mother , that I have gone many a time to the market of the jewellers and have seen them sell jewels , that had not an hundredth part [ 341 ] of the beauty of these of ours , at exceeding high prices such as man 's wit can not conceive", "context_len": 70}}}, "certified": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 78, "context": "the safe keeping of the lamp under the earth ; and he looked well into [ 547 ] the figures , both mothers and daughters , [ 548 ] but saw not the lamp , whereupon rage overrode him and he smote the sand a second time , that he might certify himself of Alaeddin 's death , but saw him not in the treasure ; whereat he redoubled in wrath , and yet more when it was certified to him that the lad was alive upon the surface of the earth and he knew that he had come forth from under the ground and had gotten the lamp , on account whereof he himself had suffered toil and torment such as passeth man 's power to endure", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` We have come to demand certified copies of all entries and receipts of this office covering the trunk in question , '' announced the young sprig of the law", "context_len": 30}}}, "magnifying": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "A short time before , they were contending against him ; but now , since he had established his ascendency , they all eagerly joined in the work of magnifying it and making it illustrious", "context_len": 35}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "He was a friend to all the boys in town , and Bart joined him without ceremony as he found him out on the lawn in his skull cap and dressing gown , studying a hornets ' nest with a magnifying glass", "context_len": 42}}}, "expounded": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "They abode in talk of the matter all that night and when the morning morrowed , Alaeddin 's mother arose and fortified her heart , more by token that her son expounded to her somewhat of the properties of the lamp and its uses , in that it would bring them all they sought", "context_len": 54}}}, "heartened": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "But , when he saw that she heartened herself for that which he set forth to her of its virtues , he feared lest she should talk of this to the folk , so he said to her , `` O my mother , beware lest thou bespeak any of the lamp and its uses , for that this is our fortune ; be careful [ 345 ] and exceed not in speech thereof to any one , lest we lose it and lose this our present prosperity , for that it is from it", "context_len": 95}}}, "grandees": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 22, "context": "After a while , the Divan being complete with the Viziers and the chiefs of the state and officers and Amirs and grandees , the Sultan appeared and the Viziers and other the officials and notables ranged themselves before him , whilst he sat down on the throne of his kingship and all who were present in the Divan stood before him , with hands clasped behind them , [ 348 ] awaiting his commandment to sit", "context_len": 77}}}, "notables": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "After a while , the Divan being complete with the Viziers and the chiefs of the state and officers and Amirs and grandees , the Sultan appeared and the Viziers and other the officials and notables ranged themselves before him , whilst he sat down on the throne of his kingship and all who were present in the Divan stood before him , with hands clasped behind them , [ 348 ] awaiting his commandment to sit", "context_len": 77}}}, "kingship": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "After a while , the Divan being complete with the Viziers and the chiefs of the state and officers and Amirs and grandees , the Sultan appeared and the Viziers and other the officials and notables ranged themselves before him , whilst he sat down on the throne of his kingship and all who were present in the Divan stood before him , with hands clasped behind them , [ 348 ] awaiting his commandment to sit", "context_len": 77}}}, "petitioners": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "So he bade them be seated and they all sat down , each in his several room ; then the petitioners [ 349 ] presented themselves before the Sultan and each affair was decided in its course , [ 350 ] till the Divan came to an end , when the King rose and entered the palace and each went his way", "context_len": 62}}}, "harem": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 75, "context": "[ 354 ] On this wise she abode a week 's space [ 355 ] and the Sultan saw her at each Divan ; so , when she went on the last day [ of the week ] and stood , according to her wont , before the Divan , till it was ended , but could not muster courage to enter [ 356 ] or say aught , the Sultan arose and entering the harem , turned to his chief Vizier , who was with him , and said to him , `` O Vizier , these six or seven days [ 357 ] past I have seen yonder old woman come hither at every Divan and I note that she still carrieth somewhat under her veil", "context_len": 128}}}, "forthright": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 79, "context": "he was wroth and cried out with a terrible great voice , saying , `` O denier of benefits , doth it not suffice thee that I and all the slaves of the Lamp are at thy service and wouldst thou eke have me bring thee our liege lady , for thy pleasure , and hang her in the dome of thy pavilion , to divert thee and thy wife ? By Allah , ye deserve that I should forthright reduce you both to ashes and scatter you to the winds ! But , inasmuch as ye are ignorant , thou and she , concerning this matter and know not its inward from its outward , [ 660 ] I excuse you , for that ye are innocent", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` So he came down forthright and went to meet the Sultan , who [ 529 ] said to him , when he saw him , `` Wherefore , O my son , hast thou done thus , and why sufferedst thou not the jewellers complete the lattice-work of the oriel , [ 530 ] so there might not remain a place in thy palace [ 531 ] defective ? '' `` O King of the Age , '' answered Alaeddin , `` I left it not imperfect but of my free will , nor did I lack of ableness to complete it", "context_len": 103}, "NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 69, "context": "`` Hearkening and obedience , '' replied the genie and disappearing , brought him in the twinkling of an eye all that he had commanded him withal , whilst in his hand he held a stallion , whose like is not among the horses of the Arabs of the Arabs , [ 456 ] with housings of the richest stuffs brocaded with gold ; whereupon Alaeddin called his mother forthright and delivered her the twelve slave-girls and gave her the [ twelve ] suits , [ 457 ] so she might dress herself [ 458 ] and go with them to the Sultan 's palace", "context_len": 105}}}, "crave": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 62, "context": "`` `` Ay , my lord , '' replied the Maugrabin , `` I am indeed a stranger and arrived but yesternight in this your town ; wherefore I beseech thee tell me of the divine gifts of this holy woman and where her place is , for that I have fallen into a calamity and would fain go to her and crave her of prayer , so haply God ( to whom belong might and majesty ) may deliver me from my stress , by means of her intercession", "context_len": 90}}}, "clemency": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` The King , for all he knew that this was a thing whereto the Vizier might not avail , no , nor the greatest King , [ 373 ] nevertheless exercised his clemency [ 374 ] and granted him the delay he sought ; then , turning to the old woman , he said to her , `` Go to thy son and tell him I give him [ my ] word that my daughter shall be in his name ; [ 375 ] but needs must I take order for her equipment ; [ 376 ] wherefore it behoveth him grant us a delay of three months", "context_len": 109}}}, "therefor": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "' Wherefore I crave Thy Grace 's clemency and hope that thou wilt pardon me and my son this effrontery neither be wroth with us therefor", "context_len": 26}}}, "treasuries": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 102, "context": "Then the slave of the lamp came to him and taking him up , set him down in his house and said to him , `` O my lord , dost thou need aught ? '' `` Yes , '' answered Alaeddin ; `` I will have thee bring me eight-and-forty mamelukes , [ 454 ] four-and-twenty to walk before me and four-and-twenty to walk behind me , with their horses and clothes and arms , and let all that is upon them and their horses be of stuffs costly and precious exceedingly , such as are not found in kings ' treasuries", "context_len": 103}}}, "whereto": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` The King , for all he knew that this was a thing whereto the Vizier might not avail , no , nor the greatest King , [ 373 ] nevertheless exercised his clemency [ 374 ] and granted him the delay he sought ; then , turning to the old woman , he said to her , `` Go to thy son and tell him I give him [ my ] word that my daughter shall be in his name ; [ 375 ] but needs must I take order for her equipment ; [ 376 ] wherefore it behoveth him grant us a delay of three months", "context_len": 109}}}, "foreboded": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "So much for the Sultan and as for Alaeddin , when his mother came to the house and entered and he saw her laughing of the excess of her joy , he foreboded good news and said , `` To God Everlasting [ 442 ] be praise ! Accomplished is that which I sought", "context_len": 54}}}, "eunuchs": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 53, "context": "`` `` How knewest thou this , '' asked Alaeddin , `` that the Vizier 's son goeth in this night to the Lady Bedrulbudour ? '' So she told him all she had seen of the decorations in the city , whenas she went to buy the oil , and how the eunuchs and chiefs of the state were drawn up awaiting the Vizier 's son , against he should come forth of the bath , for that this was the night of his going in", "context_len": 87}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "So the slave-girl went up into Alaeddin 's pavilion and returned with the lamp to the Lady Bedrulbudour , who bade the Aga of the eunuchs [ 567 ] go down and exchange it for a new one ; so he took it and going down , gave it to the Maugrabin and took of him a new lamp , with which he returned to the princess , who examined it and finding it new and real , fell to laughing at the Maugrabin 's [ lack of ] wit", "context_len": 90}}}, "oilman": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "At this wonder took her ; [ 384 ] he went to an oilman 's shop there open and buying oil of him , said to him , `` [ I conjure thee ] by thy life , O uncle , tell me what is toward to-day in this city , that the folk are making this decoration and the markets [ are shut ] and the houses all adorned and the troops drawn up in state ? '' Quoth he , `` O woman , methinketh thou art a stranger and art not of this city", "context_len": 97}}}, "woeful": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Then he returned to the khan in a woeful state of chagrin and colour and despite , for envy of Alaeddin , and [ 557 ] taking his geomantic instruments , [ 558 ] smote his [ tablet of ] sand , so he might learn where the lamp was , and found that it was in the palace and not with Alaeddin ; [ 559 ] whereat he rejoiced with an exceeding joy and said , `` Now it will be an easy matter for me to bereave this accursed of his life and I have a way to come at the lamp", "context_len": 105}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "methought": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 56, "context": "`` Quoth he , `` Speak ; what is the news ? '' And she said to him , `` The Sultan hath gone from his promise to thee in the matter of his daughter , the Lady Bedrulbudour , for that this very night the Vizier 's son goeth in to her ; and indeed methought at the time , [ 385 ] O my son , the Vizier would change the Sultan 's mind , even as I told thee that he bespoke him privily before me", "context_len": 89}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 64, "context": "wise and said to him , `` Prithee , fair youth , who is this whom you describe and praise ? '' `` O man , '' replied the other , `` meseemeth thou art a stranger and comest from afar ; but , granting thou art from a far country , hast thou not heard of the Amir Alaeddin , whose repute , methought , filled the earth , and of his palace , a wonder of the world , whereof both far and near have heard ? How is it thou hast heard nought of this nor of the name of Alaeddin , whom Our Lord increase in glory and prosper ? '' Quoth the Maugrabin , `` Marry , it is the utmost of my", "context_len": 128}}}, "supped": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Accordingly , [ 387 ] after he had supped , he went into his chamber and locking the door on himself , fetched the lamp and rubbed it ; whereupon the genie at once appeared to him and said , `` Seek what thou wilt , for I am thy slave and the slave of whoso hath in his hand the lamp , I and all the slaves of the lamp", "context_len": 71}}}, "genie": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 64, "context": "he took leave of the Sultan and going forth , mounted and rode , with his mamelukes before him and behind him , whilst the folk all prayed for him and said , `` By Allah , he is deserving ! '' till he came to his house and alighting from his stallion , entered his chamber and rubbed the lamp ; whereupon the genie stood before him and said to him , `` Seek what thou wilt , O my lord '' Quoth Alaeddin , `` I desire of thee an important service , to wit , that thou build me with all speed a palace before that of the Sultan , which shall be marvellous in its building , never saw kings its like , and", "context_len": 128}}}, "requirement": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` And Alaeddin said to him , `` I have no present requirement save that whereof I have bespoken thee", "context_len": 20}}}, "bespoken": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 49, "context": "`` And she said to him , `` O my son , the Sultan received me with all courtesy , according to his wont , and meseemeth he meant fairly by us , but [ for ] thine accursed enemy the Vizier ; for that , after I had bespoken the Sultan in thy name , even as thou badest me , reminding him that the term for which he had appointed us was past and saying to him , 'If Thy Grace would vouchsafe to give commandment for the marriage of thy daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour with my son Alaeddin , ' -- he turned to the Vizier and spoke to him", "context_len": 113}}}, "irked": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 62, "context": "When [ 413 ] the Vizier heard his son 's words , he grieved and was exceeding chagrined , for that he had thought to greaten his son and advance him by making him the King 's son-in-law ; so he bethought himself and was perplexed anent the matter and what was to do therein ; [ 414 ] and indeed it irked him sore that the marriage should be dissolved , for that he had long besought [ 415 ] the Ten [ 416 ] that he might compass the like of that affair ; [ 417 ] so he said to his son , `` Have patience , O my son , so we may see [ how it will be ] to-night , and we", "context_len": 128}}}, "brides": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 72, "context": "When the winter came on , Alexander and his army were about three or four hundred miles from home ; and , as he did not intend to advance much farther until the spring should open , he announced to the army that all those persons , both officers and soldiers who had been married within the year , might go home if they chose , and spend the winter with their brides , and return to the army in the spring", "context_len": 82}}}, "coyness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "The Queen , so she might not leave the Sultan angry with the Lady Bedrulbudour , said to him , `` O King of the Age , this is the wont of most brides , on their wedding-day , to be shamefast and show somewhat of coyness", "context_len": 47}}}, "prithee": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "And belike my father is vexed with me ; wherefore I prithee , O my mother , tell him the cause of this my case , so he be not wroth with me for my failure to answer him neither blame me , but excuse me", "context_len": 46}}}, "illusions": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 78, "context": "`` Moreover , she sent for the Vizier 's son privily and questioned him of the affair , whether the Lady Bedrulbudour 's speech was true or not ; but he , of his fear to lose his bride , lest she should go from his hand , said to her , `` O my lady , I know nothing of that which thou sayest ; '' wherefore the queen was certified that there had betided her daughter illusions and a dream", "context_len": 82}}}, "nuptials": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 69, "context": "`` Quoth the queen , `` Rise , O my daughter , and put away these illusions from thy thought ; nay , don thy clothes and see the rejoicing that is toward in the town on thine account and the festivities that they celebrate in the kingdom for thy sake and hear the drums and the singing and look upon the decorations , all in honour of thy nuptials , O my daughter", "context_len": 74}}}, "busked": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "In the morning Alaeddin arose and aroused his wife , whereupon her women came to her and dressed her and busked her and adorned her ; whilst he , on his part , donned the richest of raiment , [ 620 ] and both were like to fly for joy at their reunion with each other , after their separation , whilst the Lady Bedrulbudour was especially glad , for that she looked to see her father that day", "context_len": 79}}}, "minstrelsy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "The wedding rejoicings continued all that day , with dancing-women and singing-women , and all the instruments of mirth and minstrelsy were smitten , whilst the queen and the Vizier and his son were exceeding assiduous in keeping up the festivities , so the Lady Bedrulbudour should rejoice and her chagrin be dispelled ; nay , they left nought that day of that which exciteth unto liesse but they did it before her , so she should leave what was in her mind and be cheered", "context_len": 86}}}, "assiduous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "The wedding rejoicings continued all that day , with dancing-women and singing-women , and all the instruments of mirth and minstrelsy were smitten , whilst the queen and the Vizier and his son were exceeding assiduous in keeping up the festivities , so the Lady Bedrulbudour should rejoice and her chagrin be dispelled ; nay , they left nought that day of that which exciteth unto liesse but they did it before her , so she should leave what was in her mind and be cheered", "context_len": 86}}}, "maidenhead": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 62, "context": "`` Then , when the night came in and it was the season of sleep , Alaeddin arose and entering his chamber , rubbed the lamp , whereupon the genie appeared to him forthright and [ 404 ] he bade him bring the princess and her bridegroom , as on the past night , ere the Vizier 's son should take her maidenhead", "context_len": 63}}}, "swoon": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "When the Lady Bedrulbudour knew that her father was angry and saw the naked sword in his hand , she was like to swoon for fear ; [ 405 ] so she raised her head and said to him , `` Dear [ 406 ] my father , be not wroth with me , neither be thou hasty in thine anger , for that I am excusable in that which thou hast seen from me", "context_len": 75}}}, "sheathing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` When [ 409 ] the Sultan heard his daughter 's story , he was sore concerned and his eyes brimmed with tears ; then , sheathing the sword and coming up to her , he kissed her and said to her , `` O my daughter , why didst thou not tell me yesterday , so I might have warded off from thee the torment and affright which have befallen thee this night ? But no matter ; arise and put away from thee this thought , and to-night I will set over thee those who shall guard thee , so there shall not again befall thee that which befell yesternight", "context_len": 112}}}, "leasing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 84, "context": "Now Alaeddin had forgotten the lamp in his pavilion , [ 565 ] without locking it up in his treasury [ as was his wont ] , and one of the girls had seen it ; so she said to the princess , `` O my lady , methinketh I have seen an old lamp in my lord Alaeddin 's pavilion ; let us barter it with this man for a new one , so we may see an his speech be true or leasing", "context_len": 85}}}, "pleasance": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 54, "context": "Whereupon , `` O my father the Vizier , '' replied the youth , `` [ God ] preserve the Lady Bedrulbudour from leasing ! [ 410 ] Indeed , all she saith is true and these two nights past have been for us the sorriest of nights , instead of being nights of pleasance and delight", "context_len": 57}}}, "straitened": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "Marry , that which befell me was yet worse , for that , instead of sleeping with my bride in bed , I lay in the draught-house , a place dark and frightful , noisome of smell and accursed , and my ribs were straitened [ 411 ] with cold", "context_len": 50}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "And the troops broke up to receive such hospitality as the straitened times permitted men to indulge in", "context_len": 18}}}, "greaten": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "When [ 413 ] the Vizier heard his son 's words , he grieved and was exceeding chagrined , for that he had thought to greaten his son and advance him by making him the King 's son-in-law ; so he bethought himself and was perplexed anent the matter and what was to do therein ; [ 414 ] and indeed it irked him sore that the marriage should be dissolved , for that he had long besought [ 415 ] the Ten [ 416 ] that he might compass the like of that affair ; [ 417 ] so he said to his son , `` Have patience , O my son , so we may see [ how it will be ] to-night , and we", "context_len": 128}}}, "annulled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "So the marriage was annulled ; but the Sultan had forgotten his promise to Alaeddin 's mother and never again bethought him thereof , neither he nor the Vizier ; nor knew they whence came that which had happened", "context_len": 39}}}, "beseem": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 84, "context": "The [ 422 ] Vizier , who was like to die of envy and chagrin for that which had befallen his son , said in himself , `` How shall one like this marry the Sultan 's daughter and my son lose this honour ? '' So he said to the Sultan , [ 423 ] `` O my lord , it is an easy matter to rid ourselves of [ 424 ] this vagabond , [ 425 ] for that it would not beseem Thy Grace to give thy daughter to a man like this , of whom it is not known what he is", "context_len": 106}}}, "virgin": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 57, "context": "`` Quoth the Sultan , `` On what wise shall we rid ourselves of this man , seeing I have given him my word and a King 's word is his bond ? '' `` O my lord , '' answered the Vizier , `` my counsel is that thou require of him forty dishes of pure virgin gold , full of jewels , such as she [ 426 ] brought thee the other day , [ 427 ] and forty slave-girls to bear the dishes and forty black slaves", "context_len": 90}}}, "erst": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 78, "context": "The Maugrabin fell to examining it and knew that this all of it was the work of the Lamp ; so he said , `` Alack ! Alack ! Needs must I dig a pit for this accursed one , this tailor 's son , who could not come by a night 's supper ; but , an destiny enable me , I will send his mother back to spin at her wheel , like as she did erst , and as for him , it shall cost him [ 556 ] his life", "context_len": 94}}}, "forewent": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "His mother forewent them and the people of the quarter , when they saw that rare and magnificent sight , stood looking and marvelling and gazing upon the faces of the slave-girls and their grace and goodliness [ and their apparel ] , for that they were clad in clothes all inwoven with gold and studded with jewels ; nay , the least one 's clothes of them were worth thousands", "context_len": 71}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Then the Amirs and officers of state forewent him and brought him into the Divan , till he drew near the Sultan 's throne ; whereupon the latter came down forthright from his seat and embracing him , hindered him from kissing the carpet and seated him beside himself on his right hand", "context_len": 53}}}, "inwoven": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 51, "context": "His mother forewent them and the people of the quarter , when they saw that rare and magnificent sight , stood looking and marvelling and gazing upon the faces of the slave-girls and their grace and goodliness [ and their apparel ] , for that they were clad in clothes all inwoven with gold and studded with jewels ; nay , the least one 's clothes of them were worth thousands", "context_len": 71}}}, "outshone": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Moreover they looked at the dishes [ 431 ] and saw flashing therefrom a radiance that outshone the light of the sun , albeit each dish was covered with a piece of brocade , gold-inwrought and studded eke with precious jewels", "context_len": 41}}}, "ordinance": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "When the Sultan entered the palace and viewed its building and ordinance and saw its stones , which were of jade and agate , he was amazed [ 505 ] and his wit was bewildered at that affluence and wealth and magnificence ; so he turned to the Vizier and said to him , `` How sayst thou , O Vizier ? Hast thou in all thy days seen aught like this ? Are there found with the greatest of the kings of the world riches and gold and jewels such as these we see in this palace ? '' `` O my lord the King , '' answered the Vizier , `` this is a thing beyond the competence of a king of the sons of Adam", "context_len": 128}}}, "chamberlains": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 63, "context": "Now the Sultan had summoned to his presence the chiefs of his state and telling them that he had passed his word for the marriage of his daughter to Alaeddin , bade them await the latter , commanding them that , when he came , they should all go out to meet him ; moreover , he assembled the amirs and viziers and chamberlains and guards and captains of the troops and they were all awaiting Alaeddin at the door of the palace", "context_len": 83}}}, "ravish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "When the eunuchs and chamberlains and captains of the guard saw them , wonder took them and they were breathless for amaze at this sight , the like whereof they had never in their lives seen , and especially at the slave girls , each one of whom would ravish the wit of an anchorite", "context_len": 55}}}, "courtliness": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "The folk marvelled at Alaeddin 's bounty and at the excess of his munificence and were amazed when they saw that which graced him of beauty and goodliness and his courtliness and dignity ; yea , they extolled the perfection of the Compassionate One for this His noble creature and all of them great and small [ 462 ] called down blessings on him , albeit they knew him for the son of such an one the tailor ; yet none envied him , but all said , `` He is deserving", "context_len": 92}}}, "paring": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 112, "context": "these riches yet more than the Sultan , but envy was killing him and waxed on him more and more , when he saw that the Sultan was content with the bride-gift [ 435 ] and the dowry ; withal he could not gainstand the [ manifest ] truth and say to the Sultan , `` He is not worthy ; '' so he cast about to work upon him by practice , that he might hinder him from giving his daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour to Alaeddin , and accordingly said to him , [ 436 ] `` O my lord , all the treasures of the world were not worth a paring of thy daughter Bedrulbudour 's nails ; indeed , Thy Highness overrateth this upon her", "context_len": 128}}}, "betide": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": ") `` O my lady Fatimeh , '' rejoined the princess , `` be easy ; nothing shall betide save that which thou wiliest ; so rise now [ and come ] with me , that I may show thee the pavilion [ 648 ] which I purpose to order for thine inhabitance with us", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Longstone is a desolate rock , swept by the northern gales ; and woe betide the ship driven on its pitiless shores !", "context_len": 23}}}, "whilom": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "So she rejoiced , albeit she had been sad and sorry for her [ whilom ] bridegroom the Vizier 's son , -- she rejoiced , [ I say ] , with an exceeding joy , when she saw the jewels and the beauty of the damsels , and was cheered ; whilst her father rejoiced exceedingly in her joy , in that he saw her put off chagrin and dejection", "context_len": 71}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "When [ 602 ] the Maugrabin saw the love which the Lady Bedrulbudour professed to him and that she was changed from her whilom plight of grief , he thought that she had given up her hope of Alaeddin ; so he rejoiced greatly and said to her , `` O my soul , hearkening and obedience unto all that which thou wiliest and biddest me withal", "context_len": 67}}}, "goodlier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Then he said to her , `` O my daughter Bedrulbudour , doth this please thee ? Indeed , methinketh this thy bridegroom is goodlier [ 440 ] than the Vizier 's son , and God willing , O my daughter , thou shalt rejoice with him abundantly", "context_len": 48}}}, "solaced": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` And she said to him , `` Glad tidings , O my son ! Let thy heart rejoice and thine eye be solaced in the attainment of thy desire , for that the Sultan accepteth thine offering , to wit , the bride gift and the dowry of the Lady Bedrulbudour , and she is thy bride and this , O my son , is the night of your [ 443 ] bridal and thy going in to the Lady Bedrulbudour", "context_len": 82}}}, "certify": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "Nay , the Sultan , that he might certify me of his word , proclaimed thee his son-in-law before the folk and declared that this should be the wedding-night ; but he said to me , 'Let thy son come hither to me , so I may make acquaintance with him , and I will receive him with all honour and worship", "context_len": 62}}}, "amain": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Alaeddin kissed his mother 's hand and thanked her amain for her kindness ; [ 446 ] then he arose and entering his chamber , took the lamp and rubbed it ; whereupon the genie presented himself and said to him , `` Here am I ; seek what thou wilt", "context_len": 51}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 66, "context": "`` The man accordingly told him of the divine gifts of the holy woman Fatimeh and her piety and the excellence of her devotion ; then , taking him by the hand , he carried him without the city and showed him the way to her abiding-place , which was in a cavern on the top of a little hill ; whereupon the Maugrabin thanked him amain for his kindness [ 636 ] and returned to his place in the Khan", "context_len": 81}}}, "agate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "When the Sultan entered the palace and viewed its building and ordinance and saw its stones , which were of jade and agate , he was amazed [ 505 ] and his wit was bewildered at that affluence and wealth and magnificence ; so he turned to the Vizier and said to him , `` How sayst thou , O Vizier ? Hast thou in all thy days seen aught like this ? Are there found with the greatest of the kings of the world riches and gold and jewels such as these we see in this palace ? '' `` O my lord the King , '' answered the Vizier , `` this is a thing beyond the competence of a king of the sons of Adam", "context_len": 128}}}, "ravished": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "Moreover , he brought him into a place , which he found full of coffers overflowing with royal raiment , such as ravished the wit , gold-inwoven stuffs , Indian and Chinese , and brocades , and he showed him also many other places , all full of that which beggareth description , till at last he brought him into a stable , wherein were horses whose like is not found with the kings of the world ; and therewithin he showed him a storehouse , full of housings and saddles of price , all broidered with pearls and precious stones and so forth", "context_len": 104}}}, "sherbets": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 87, "context": "So they fared on with him in great state , with four of the Viziers for his sword-bearers , whilst all the troops and people of the city , both townsfolk and strangers , walked in procession before him , carrying flambeaux and drums and flutes and instruments of mirth and music , till they brought him to his palace , when he alighted and entering , sat down , as did also the Viziers and Amirs who were in his company , whilst the mamelukes brought sherbets and sweetmeats [ 493 ] and gave all who were with him in the procession to drink , albeit they were a multitude of folk whose number might not be told", "context_len": 119}}}, "ambergris": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Then sherbets were brought him and coffee with ambergris and he drank and arose ; whereupon there came to him a troop of slaves and clad him in those [ 449 ] sumptuous clothes [ 450 ] and he dressed and perfumed himself with essences and sweet-scented smoke", "context_len": 48}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "Moreover , she had with her eight and-forty slave-girls , whom Alaeddin had presented to her , in each one 's hand a great candle of camphor and ambergris , set in a candlestick of gold , studded with jewels ; and all the men and women in the palace went out with her and fared on before her , till they brought her to her bridegroom 's palace and carrying her up to her pavilion , [ 495 ] attired her in various robes [ 496 ] and displayed her", "context_len": 91}}}, "essences": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "Then sherbets were brought him and coffee with ambergris and he drank and arose ; whereupon there came to him a troop of slaves and clad him in those [ 449 ] sumptuous clothes [ 450 ] and he dressed and perfumed himself with essences and sweet-scented smoke", "context_len": 48}}}, "chiefest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "[ 451 ] Now thou knowest [ 452 ] that Alaeddin was the son of a poor man , a tailor : yet now none had thought it , [ 453 ] but had said , `` This is the chiefest of the sons of the kings , '' extolled be the perfection of Him who changeth and is not changed !", "context_len": 62}}}, "stuffs": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 59, "context": "`` Hearkening and obedience , '' replied the genie and disappearing , brought him in the twinkling of an eye all that he had commanded him withal , whilst in his hand he held a stallion , whose like is not among the horses of the Arabs of the Arabs , [ 456 ] with housings of the richest stuffs brocaded with gold ; whereupon Alaeddin called his mother forthright and delivered her the twelve slave-girls and gave her the [ twelve ] suits , [ 457 ] so she might dress herself [ 458 ] and go with them to the Sultan 's palace", "context_len": 105}}}, "stallion": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 53, "context": "Then he took leave of the Sultan and going forth , mounted and rode , with his mamelukes before him and behind him , whilst the folk all prayed for him and said , `` By Allah , he is deserving ! '' till he came to his house and alighting from his stallion , entered his chamber and rubbed the lamp ; whereupon the genie stood before him and said to him , `` Seek what thou wilt , O my lord '' Quoth Alaeddin , `` I desire of thee an important service , to wit , that thou build me with all speed a palace before that of the Sultan , which shall be marvellous in its building , never saw kings its like ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "trappings": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "Then bring me a stallion fit for the riding of the Chosroes and be his trappings all of gold , embossed with noble jewels ; and bring me eight-and-forty thousand diners , in each mameluke 's hand a thousand , for that I purpose presently to visit the Sultan ; wherefore delay thou not on me , since I can not go thither without all that whereof I have bespoken thee", "context_len": 71}}}, "mameluke": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "Then bring me a stallion fit for the riding of the Chosroes and be his trappings all of gold , embossed with noble jewels ; and bring me eight-and-forty thousand diners , in each mameluke 's hand a thousand , for that I purpose presently to visit the Sultan ; wherefore delay thou not on me , since I can not go thither without all that whereof I have bespoken thee", "context_len": 71}}}, "housings": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 55, "context": "`` Hearkening and obedience , '' replied the genie and disappearing , brought him in the twinkling of an eye all that he had commanded him withal , whilst in his hand he held a stallion , whose like is not among the horses of the Arabs of the Arabs , [ 456 ] with housings of the richest stuffs brocaded with gold ; whereupon Alaeddin called his mother forthright and delivered her the twelve slave-girls and gave her the [ twelve ] suits , [ 457 ] so she might dress herself [ 458 ] and go with them to the Sultan 's palace", "context_len": 105}}}, "brocaded": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 60, "context": "`` Hearkening and obedience , '' replied the genie and disappearing , brought him in the twinkling of an eye all that he had commanded him withal , whilst in his hand he held a stallion , whose like is not among the horses of the Arabs of the Arabs , [ 456 ] with housings of the richest stuffs brocaded with gold ; whereupon Alaeddin called his mother forthright and delivered her the twelve slave-girls and gave her the [ twelve ] suits , [ 457 ] so she might dress herself [ 458 ] and go with them to the Sultan 's palace", "context_len": 105}}}, "strewing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 48, "context": "`` Accordingly he arose and mounting , [ set forth ] , whilst the mamelukes rode before him and after him , ( extolled be the perfection of the Lord who created them with [ 459 ] that which clothed them of beauty and grace ! ) , strewing gold upon the folk before their lord Alaeddin , who overpassed them all of his grace and goodliness , and ask thou not of kings ' sons , [ 460 ] extolled be the perfection of the Giver , the Eternal ! Now all this was of the virtue of the wonderful lamp , [ 461 ] which gifted whoso possessed it with goodliness and grace and wealth and wisdom", "context_len": 119}}}, "munificence": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 26, "context": "He passed no folk but they were descanting upon the palace and its magnificence and talking of Alaeddin 's grace and comeliness and his bounty and munificence and the goodliness of his manners and disposition ; so [ 555 ] he went up to one of those who were extolling Alaeddin on this wise and said to him , `` Prithee , fair youth , who is this whom you describe and praise ? '' `` O man , '' replied the other , `` meseemeth thou art a stranger and comest from afar ; but , granting thou art from a far country , hast thou not heard of the Amir Alaeddin , whose repute , methought , filled the earth , and of his palace ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "amirs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 59, "context": "Now the Sultan had summoned to his presence the chiefs of his state and telling them that he had passed his word for the marriage of his daughter to Alaeddin , bade them await the latter , commanding them that , when he came , they should all go out to meet him ; moreover , he assembled the amirs and viziers and chamberlains and guards and captains of the troops and they were all awaiting Alaeddin at the door of the palace", "context_len": 83}}}, "viziers": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 95, "context": "Then he bade lay breakfast ; [ 503 ] so they laid [ it ] and they all broke their fast ; and after they had eaten and drunken their sufficiency and had finished and the servants had removed the tables from before them , Alaeddin turned to the Sultan and said to him , `` O my lord , [ belike ] Thy Grace will vouchsafe to honour me this day at the morning-meal [ 503 ] with the Lady Bedrulbudour , thy precious daughter , and be Thy Grace 's company all thy viziers and the chief officers of thy state", "context_len": 103}}}, "deputed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "When he arrived , he would have dismounted at the door , but there came up to him one of the Amirs , whom the Sultan had deputed to that office , and said to him , `` O my lord , the commandment is that thou enter , riding on thy charger , so thou mayst alight at the door of the Divan", "context_len": 64}}}, "lowliest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 58, "context": "Alaeddin did that which behoveth and befitteth unto kings of obeisance and invocation and said to him , `` O our lord the Sultan , thy Grace 's munificence hath vouchsafed [ 464 ] to accord me the Lady Bedrulbudour thy daughter , albeit I am unworthy of this great favour , for that I am of the lowliest of thy slaves ; wherefore I beseech God that He keep and continue thee", "context_len": 73}}}, "competence": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 72, "context": "the Vizier and said to him , `` How sayst thou , O Vizier ? Hast thou in all thy days seen aught like this ? Are there found with the greatest of the kings of the world riches and gold and jewels such as these we see in this palace ? '' `` O my lord the King , '' answered the Vizier , `` this is a thing beyond the competence of a king of the sons of Adam , nor might all the people of the earth together avail to build a palace like this ; nay , there are no craftsmen living able to do work like this , except it be , as I said to Thy Grace , by might of magic", "context_len": 128}}}, "comeliness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "He passed no folk but they were descanting upon the palace and its magnificence and talking of Alaeddin 's grace and comeliness and his bounty and munificence and the goodliness of his manners and disposition ; so [ 555 ] he went up to one of those who were extolling Alaeddin on this wise and said to him , `` Prithee , fair youth , who is this whom you describe and praise ? '' `` O man , '' replied the other , `` meseemeth thou art a stranger and comest from afar ; but , granting thou art from a far country , hast thou not heard of the Amir Alaeddin , whose repute , methought , filled the earth , and of his palace ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "thereat": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "Moreover , he noted Alaeddin 's eloquence and the elegance of his speech and was amazed thereat , he and all who were present with him in the Divan , whilst fire was kindled in the Vizier 's heart for envy of Alaeddin , so that he was like to die", "context_len": 51}}}, "auspice": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 65, "context": "`` `` O my lord the king , '' answered Alaeddin , `` I would fain build the Lady Bedrulbudour a palace , besorting her rank and station , and it may not be that I should go in to her without this ; but , God willing , the building shall , by the diligent endeavour of thy slave and by Thy Grace 's auspice , [ 472 ] be right speedily despatched", "context_len": 74}}}, "requisites": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 119, "context": "his mamelukes before him and behind him , whilst the folk all prayed for him and said , `` By Allah , he is deserving ! '' till he came to his house and alighting from his stallion , entered his chamber and rubbed the lamp ; whereupon the genie stood before him and said to him , `` Seek what thou wilt , O my lord '' Quoth Alaeddin , `` I desire of thee an important service , to wit , that thou build me with all speed a palace before that of the Sultan , which shall be marvellous in its building , never saw kings its like , and be it complete with all its requisites of kingly and magnificent furniture and so forth", "context_len": 128}}}, "kingly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 121, "context": "his mamelukes before him and behind him , whilst the folk all prayed for him and said , `` By Allah , he is deserving ! '' till he came to his house and alighting from his stallion , entered his chamber and rubbed the lamp ; whereupon the genie stood before him and said to him , `` Seek what thou wilt , O my lord '' Quoth Alaeddin , `` I desire of thee an important service , to wit , that thou build me with all speed a palace before that of the Sultan , which shall be marvellous in its building , never saw kings its like , and be it complete with all its requisites of kingly and magnificent furniture and so forth", "context_len": 128}}}, "jade": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "When the Sultan entered the palace and viewed its building and ordinance and saw its stones , which were of jade and agate , he was amazed [ 505 ] and his wit was bewildered at that affluence and wealth and magnificence ; so he turned to the Vizier and said to him , `` How sayst thou , O Vizier ? Hast thou in all thy days seen aught like this ? Are there found with the greatest of the kings of the world riches and gold and jewels such as these we see in this palace ? '' `` O my lord the King , '' answered the Vizier , `` this is a thing beyond the competence of a king of the sons of Adam", "context_len": 128}}}, "porphyry": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "`` So Alaeddin arose and the genie carried him , in the twinkling of an eye , to the palace , which when he saw , he was amazed at its building , for that all its stones were of jade and alabaster and porphyry and mosaic", "context_len": 47}}}, "ewers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "The genie carried him into a treasury full of all manner of gold and silver and precious jewels past count or reckoning , price or estimation ; then he brought him into another place , where he saw all the requisites of the table , platters and spoons and ewers and basins and cups , of gold and silver , and thence to the kitchen , where he found cooks , [ 476 ] with their cooking-gear and utensils , all on like wise of gold and silver", "context_len": 88}}}, "brocades": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "Moreover , he brought him into a place , which he found full of coffers overflowing with royal raiment , such as ravished the wit , gold-inwoven stuffs , Indian and Chinese , and brocades , and he showed him also many other places , all full of that which beggareth description , till at last he brought him into a stable , wherein were horses whose like is not found with the kings of the world ; and therewithin he showed him a storehouse , full of housings and saddles of price , all broidered with pearls and precious stones and so forth", "context_len": 104}}}, "broidered": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 95, "context": "Moreover , he brought him into a place , which he found full of coffers overflowing with royal raiment , such as ravished the wit , gold-inwoven stuffs , Indian and Chinese , and brocades , and he showed him also many other places , all full of that which beggareth description , till at last he brought him into a stable , wherein were horses whose like is not found with the kings of the world ; and therewithin he showed him a storehouse , full of housings and saddles of price , all broidered with pearls and precious stones and so forth", "context_len": 104}}}, "whereunto": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Alaeddin was amazed and bewildered at the greatness of these riches , whereunto the mightiest king in the world might not avail , and all the work of one night ; more by token that the palace was full of slaves and slave girls such as would bewitch a saint with their loveliness", "context_len": 53}}}, "bewitch": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "Alaeddin was amazed and bewildered at the greatness of these riches , whereunto the mightiest king in the world might not avail , and all the work of one night ; more by token that the palace was full of slaves and slave girls such as would bewitch a saint with their loveliness", "context_len": 53}}}, "belvedere": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 24, "context": "But the most marvellous of all was that he saw in the palace an upper hall [ 477 ] and [ 478 ] a belvedere [ 479 ] with four-and-twenty oriels , all wroughten of emeralds and rubies and other jewels , and of one of these oriels the lattice-work was by his desire left unfinished , [ 480 ] so the Sultan should fail of its completion", "context_len": 68}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "Then the Lady Bedrulbudour took him and showed him the belvedere [ 650 ] and the kiosk of jewels , with the four-and-twenty oriels , [ 651 ] and said to him , `` How deemest thou , O my Lady Fatimeh , of this wonderful pavilion ? '' [ 652 ] `` By Allah , O my daughter , '' replied he , `` it is indeed marvellous in the extreme , [ 653 ] nor methinketh is its like found in the world ; nay , it is magnificent exceedingly ; but oh , for one thing which would far increase it in beauty and adornment ! '' And the princess said to him , `` O my Lady Fatimeh , what is lacking to it", "context_len": 128}}}, "oriels": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "Then the Lady Bedrulbudour took him and showed him the belvedere [ 650 ] and the kiosk of jewels , with the four-and-twenty oriels , [ 651 ] and said to him , `` How deemest thou , O my Lady Fatimeh , of this wonderful pavilion ? '' [ 652 ] `` By Allah , O my daughter , '' replied he , `` it is indeed marvellous in the extreme , [ 653 ] nor methinketh is its like found in the world ; nay , it is magnificent exceedingly ; but oh , for one thing which would far increase it in beauty and adornment ! '' And the princess said to him , `` O my Lady Fatimeh , what is lacking to it", "context_len": 128}}}, "emeralds": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 72, "context": "or shubbak , it would seem that what is intended to be described is a lofty hall ( or sorer ) , erected on the roof of the palace , whether round or square we can not tell , but crowned with a dome or cupola and having four-and-twenty deep projecting windows or oriels , the lattice or trellis-work of which latter was formed ( instead of the usual wood ) of emeralds , rubies and other jewels , strung , we may suppose , upon rods of gold or other metal I have , at the risk of wearying my reader , treated this point at some length , as well because it is an important one as to show the almost insuperable difficulties that beset the", "context_len": 128}}}, "rubies": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 74, "context": "or shubbak , it would seem that what is intended to be described is a lofty hall ( or sorer ) , erected on the roof of the palace , whether round or square we can not tell , but crowned with a dome or cupola and having four-and-twenty deep projecting windows or oriels , the lattice or trellis-work of which latter was formed ( instead of the usual wood ) of emeralds , rubies and other jewels , strung , we may suppose , upon rods of gold or other metal I have , at the risk of wearying my reader , treated this point at some length , as well because it is an important one as to show the almost insuperable difficulties that beset the", "context_len": 128}}}, "beholder": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 55, "context": "At this juncture the Vizier presented himself and as he entered , he espied the new palace and the carpet and marvelled also ; so , when he came in to the Sultan , the twain fell to talking of this strange matter and marvelling , for that they saw a thing which amazed the beholder and dilated the heart ; and they said , `` Verily , methinketh kings may not avail unto the building of the like of this palace", "context_len": 82}}}, "upraise": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 115, "context": "the Sultan turned to the Vizier and said to him , `` How now ? Deemest thou Alaeddin worthy to be bridegroom to my daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour ? Hast thou seen and considered this royal building and all these riches which man 's wit can not comprehend ? '' The Vizier , of his envy of Alaeddin , answered him , saying , `` O King of the Age , indeed this palace and its building and all these riches may not be but by means of enchantment , for that no man among men , no , not the mightiest of them in dominion or the greatest in wealth , might avail to upraise and stablish [ the like of ] this building in one night", "context_len": 128}}}, "stablish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 117, "context": "the Sultan turned to the Vizier and said to him , `` How now ? Deemest thou Alaeddin worthy to be bridegroom to my daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour ? Hast thou seen and considered this royal building and all these riches which man 's wit can not comprehend ? '' The Vizier , of his envy of Alaeddin , answered him , saying , `` O King of the Age , indeed this palace and its building and all these riches may not be but by means of enchantment , for that no man among men , no , not the mightiest of them in dominion or the greatest in wealth , might avail to upraise and stablish [ the like of ] this building in one night", "context_len": 128}}}, "wast": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` Quoth the Sultan , `` I marvel at thee how thou still deemest evil of Alaeddin ; but methinketh it ariseth from thine envy of him , for that thou wast present when he sought of me a place whereon to build a palace for my daughter and I accorded him , before thee , [ leave to build ] a palace on this ground ; and he who brought me , to my daughter 's dower , jewels such that no kings possess one thereof , shall he lack ableness to build a palace like this ? '' When [ 483 ] the Vizier heard the Sultan 's speech and understood that he loved Alaeddin greatly , his envy of him increased ; withal he availed", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` `` And wast thou concerned anent this ? '' rejoined Alaeddin", "context_len": 12}}}, "dower": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 64, "context": "deemest evil of Alaeddin ; but methinketh it ariseth from thine envy of him , for that thou wast present when he sought of me a place whereon to build a palace for my daughter and I accorded him , before thee , [ leave to build ] a palace on this ground ; and he who brought me , to my daughter 's dower , jewels such that no kings possess one thereof , shall he lack ableness to build a palace like this ? '' When [ 483 ] the Vizier heard the Sultan 's speech and understood that he loved Alaeddin greatly , his envy of him increased ; withal he availed not to do aught against him , so he was dumb and could", "context_len": 128}}}, "smiting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Those ( says he ) who seek to discover hidden things and know the future have invented an art which they call tracing or smiting the sand ; to wit , they take paper or sand or flour and trace thereon at hazard four rows of points , which operation , three times repeated ( i", "context_len": 56}}}, "upreared": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 76, "context": "The Sultan still marvelled in himself at Alaeddin 's mother , how she had come to him in poor clothes , whilst her son had command of this exceeding wealth ; and as for the folk , who came to the Sultan 's palace , to gaze upon the wedding-festivities , when they saw Alaeddin 's palace and the goodliness of its building , there took them great wonderment how so magnificent a building had been upreared in one night and they fell all to praying for Alaeddin and saying , `` God prosper him ! By Allah , he is deserving", "context_len": 102}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Then the head would be upreared", "context_len": 6}}}, "bouts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Then , after they had played [ some ] bouts [ 491 ] in the plain and each had shown what was in him of horsemanship , ( but Alaeddin overpassed them all , ) the Sultan went to his palace and Alaeddin on like wise returned home", "context_len": 48}}}, "eventide": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "When it was eventide , the chiefs of the state and the Viziers went and taking Alaeddin , carried him in procession to the Royal Bath , the Renowned ; [ 492 ] so he entered and bathed and perfumed himself , then , coming forth , he donned a suit yet richer than the first and mounted , whilst the troops rode before him and the Amirs and Viziers", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "It was a dark and gloomy eventide : the sun , which had only made its appearance at intervals during the day , was fast sinking behind a heavy bank of clouds which filled the western horizon ; and the wind , which was freshening to a gale , seemed to bear the storm onward in its track , while it tore the few surviving leaves rudely from the trees , and whirled them in mazy windings", "context_len": 77}}}, "fourscore": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "[ 517 ] Now there stood before them fourscore damsels , each as it were she said to the full moon , `` Rise , so I may sit in thy place ; '' and in each one 's hand was an instrument of mirth and music", "context_len": 47}}}, "craftsmen": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 104, "context": "the Vizier and said to him , `` How sayst thou , O Vizier ? Hast thou in all thy days seen aught like this ? Are there found with the greatest of the kings of the world riches and gold and jewels such as these we see in this palace ? '' `` O my lord the King , '' answered the Vizier , `` this is a thing beyond the competence of a king of the sons of Adam , nor might all the people of the earth together avail to build a palace like this ; nay , there are no craftsmen living able to do work like this , except it be , as I said to Thy Grace , by might of magic", "context_len": 128}}}, "lattices": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 31, "context": "The Sultan accordingly went up to the pavilion [ 535 ] and entering the kiosk , [ 536 ] viewed it right and left and saw no manner defect in its lattices , but found them all perfect ; whereat he was astounded and embracing Alaeddin , fell a-kissing him and saying , `` O my son , what is this extraordinary thing ? In one night thou dost a work wherefrom the jewellers would fail in months ! By Allah , methinketh thou hast not thy fellow [ 537 ] in the world ! '' Quoth Alaeddin , `` God prolong thy life and perpetuate thy continuance ! Thy slave is not worthy of this praise", "context_len": 117}}}, "astonied": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 52, "context": "Then Alaeddin forewent the Sultan till he brought him to the high pavilion [ 507 ] and he looked at the belvedere [ 508 ] and its oriols [ 509 ] and lattices , [ 510 ] all wroughten of emeralds and rubies and other precious stones , and was amazed and astonied ; his wit was bewildered and he abode perplexed in his thought", "context_len": 65}}}, "lattice": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 64, "context": "with keszr or teyyareh and now with liwan or shubbak , it would seem that what is intended to be described is a lofty hall ( or sorer ) , erected on the roof of the palace , whether round or square we can not tell , but crowned with a dome or cupola and having four-and-twenty deep projecting windows or oriels , the lattice or trellis-work of which latter was formed ( instead of the usual wood ) of emeralds , rubies and other jewels , strung , we may suppose , upon rods of gold or other metal I have , at the risk of wearying my reader , treated this point at some length , as well because it is an important one as to", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "So they took all the jewels assigned them by the Sultan and wrought with them , but found that these also sufficed them not , nor might they complete withal the half of that which lacked of the lattice work of the oriel ; [ 524 ] whereupon the Sultan bade take all the jewels which should be found with the Viziers and chiefs of the state ; and accordingly they took them all and wrought therewith ; but this also sufficed not", "context_len": 83}}}, "oriel": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 102, "context": "asked Alaeddin , `` What is the cause that the lattice of yonder kiosk ( kushk ) is not complete ? '' The defective part is soon after referred to , no less than four times , as `` the lattice of the kiosk '' ( sheriyyetu ' l kushk ) , thus showing that , in the writer 's mind , kushk , liwan and shubbak were synonymous terms for the common Arab projecting square-sided window , made of latticework , and I have therefore rendered the three words , when they occur in this sense , by our English `` oriel , '' to whose modern meaning ( a window that juts out , so as to form a small apartment ) , they exactly correspond", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "a place of passage , but by extension a porch , a gallery , an opening , here ( and here only ) used by synecdoche for the oriel itself ) , and the famous roe 's egg is proposed to be suspended from `` the dome ( cubbeh ) of the upper chamber '' ( el keszr el faucaniyy ) , thus showing that the latter was crowned with a dome or cupola", "context_len": 74}}}, "meats": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "The Sultan sat between his daughter , the Lady Bedrulbudour , and his son-in-law Alaeddin , and when he put his hand to the food and tasted it , wonder took him at the richness of the meats and the exquisiteness of their seasonings", "context_len": 44}}}, "exquisiteness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "The Sultan sat between his daughter , the Lady Bedrulbudour , and his son-in-law Alaeddin , and when he put his hand to the food and tasted it , wonder took him at the richness of the meats and the exquisiteness of their seasonings", "context_len": 44}}}, "seasonings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "The Sultan sat between his daughter , the Lady Bedrulbudour , and his son-in-law Alaeddin , and when he put his hand to the food and tasted it , wonder took him at the richness of the meats and the exquisiteness of their seasonings", "context_len": 44}}}, "availing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "So he went up to them and viewed their work and how they wrought and saw that they were far from availing to do work like that [ of the rest ] of Alaeddin 's palace", "context_len": 36}}}, "jewelers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "She gave it to me , as she was going away on a little trip , to take to the jewelers to have the setting of some of the stones tightened , and I placed it in the cabinet with the reptiles for safe keeping , until school should be closed , Friday", "context_len": 53}}}, "incontinent": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "When it was morning , Alaeddin went up to view the jewelers ' work and saw that they had not completed half the lacking lattice-work ; whereupon he bade them incontinent undo all that they had wrought and restore the jewels to their owners", "context_len": 44}}}, "undid": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "They undid knots and loosened ropes like lightning , and in two minutes their life boats swung out and they scrambled into them and were dropped down into the water", "context_len": 30}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "Then they went to the Sultan and told him that Alaeddin had commanded them of this ; whereupon he asked them , `` What said he to you and why would he not have the lattice-work finished and why undid he that which you had done ? '' And they said to him , `` O my lord , we know nothing , save that he bade us undo all that we had done", "context_len": 74}}}, "eunuch": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 55, "context": "`` And when , '' [ quoth she ] ; `` I saw this [ seeming ] lack of wit in him , I fell to laughing at him , unknowing his perfidy and his intent ; so I took an old lamp that was in my husband 's pavilion and sent it by the eunuch , who exchanged it with him for a new lamp ; and next day , O my father , at daybreak , we found ourselves in Africa , with the palace and all that was therein ; and I knew not the properties of the lamp which I had exchanged , till my husband Alaeddin came to us and contrived against the Maugrabin a device whereby he delivered us from him", "context_len": 127}}}, "kiosk": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 16, "context": "Then the Lady Bedrulbudour took him and showed him the belvedere [ 650 ] and the kiosk of jewels , with the four-and-twenty oriels , [ 651 ] and said to him , `` How deemest thou , O my Lady Fatimeh , of this wonderful pavilion ? '' [ 652 ] `` By Allah , O my daughter , '' replied he , `` it is indeed marvellous in the extreme , [ 653 ] nor methinketh is its like found in the world ; nay , it is magnificent exceedingly ; but oh , for one thing which would far increase it in beauty and adornment ! '' And the princess said to him , `` O my Lady Fatimeh , what is lacking to it", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` The adjective ( nakiszeh ) is put in the feminine , to agree with `` lattice '' ( sheriyyeh ) , which is femminine , kiosk ( kushk ) being masculine", "context_len": 32}}}, "benefaction": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Moreover he exceeded in benefaction of the poor and the indigent [ 538 ] and used himself to distribute his alms to them with his own hand", "context_len": 27}}}, "alms": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Blind people applied for work who wished for alms instead ; and arrangements necessary for carrying out so large a scheme entailed a good deal of labour on Miss Gilbert 's part", "context_len": 32}}}, "archery": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Moreover , he fell to going everywhile [ 539 ] to the chase and the horse course and to practicing horsemanship and archery [ 540 ] before the Sultan , whilst the Lady Bedrulbudour redoubled in love of him , whenassoever she saw him disporting himself a horseback , and thought in herself that God had wrought exceeding graciously by her in that there had befallen her what befell with the Vizier 's son , so He might keep her for her true bridegroom Alaeddin", "context_len": 85}}}, "disporting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "Moreover , he fell to going everywhile [ 539 ] to the chase and the horse course and to practicing horsemanship and archery [ 540 ] before the Sultan , whilst the Lady Bedrulbudour redoubled in love of him , whenassoever she saw him disporting himself a horseback , and thought in herself that God had wrought exceeding graciously by her in that there had befallen her what befell with the Vizier 's son , so He might keep her for her true bridegroom Alaeddin", "context_len": 85}}}, "levied": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Now in those days certain of the Sultan 's enemies took horse against him ; so he levied troops to repel them and made Alaeddin chief thereof", "context_len": 27}}}, "enchanter": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 29, "context": "This , then , is what befell Alaeddin with the Maugrabin ; but Alaeddin , for all this , was not altogether [ 625 ] quit of the accursed enchanter , withal his body had been burned and given to the winds ; for that the accursed one had a brother viler than he [ and yet more skilled ] in magic and geomancy and astrology ; [ nay , they were even ] as saith the proverb , `` A bean and it was cloven in twain ; '' [ 626 ] and each dwelt in one quarter of the world , so they might fill it [ 627 ] with their sorcery and craft and guile", "context_len": 118}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "[ 631 ] When the Maugrabin enchanter heard this , he said in himself , `` Now have I found that which I sought", "context_len": 24}}}, "travail": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "When he returned to his country , he abode all this time , bewailing that which he had endured of toil and stress , so he might compass the lamp , yet had his travail all been wasted and the morsel had escaped from his hand , after it had reached his mouth ; and he still thought upon all this , bemoaning himself and reviling Alaeddin of the excess of his anger against him ; and whiles he said in himself , `` Since yonder whoreson is dead under the earth , I am content withal and I have hopes of the lamp , that I may yet achieve it , inasmuch as it is still safeguarded", "context_len": 118}}}, "whoreson": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 86, "context": "When he returned to his country , he abode all this time , bewailing that which he had endured of toil and stress , so he might compass the lamp , yet had his travail all been wasted and the morsel had escaped from his hand , after it had reached his mouth ; and he still thought upon all this , bemoaning himself and reviling Alaeddin of the excess of his anger against him ; and whiles he said in himself , `` Since yonder whoreson is dead under the earth , I am content withal and I have hopes of the lamp , that I may yet achieve it , inasmuch as it is still safeguarded", "context_len": 118}}}, "achieve": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 109, "context": "When he returned to his country , he abode all this time , bewailing that which he had endured of toil and stress , so he might compass the lamp , yet had his travail all been wasted and the morsel had escaped from his hand , after it had reached his mouth ; and he still thought upon all this , bemoaning himself and reviling Alaeddin of the excess of his anger against him ; and whiles he said in himself , `` Since yonder whoreson is dead under the earth , I am content withal and I have hopes of the lamp , that I may yet achieve it , inasmuch as it is still safeguarded", "context_len": 118}}}, "safeguarded": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 117, "context": "When he returned to his country , he abode all this time , bewailing that which he had endured of toil and stress , so he might compass the lamp , yet had his travail all been wasted and the morsel had escaped from his hand , after it had reached his mouth ; and he still thought upon all this , bemoaning himself and reviling Alaeddin of the excess of his anger against him ; and whiles he said in himself , `` Since yonder whoreson is dead under the earth , I am content withal and I have hopes of the lamp , that I may yet achieve it , inasmuch as it is still safeguarded", "context_len": 118}}}, "overrode": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 64, "context": "the most approved fashion [ 545 ] and adjusted [ 546 ] them , so he might see and certify himself of the death of Alaeddin and the safe keeping of the lamp under the earth ; and he looked well into [ 547 ] the figures , both mothers and daughters , [ 548 ] but saw not the lamp , whereupon rage overrode him and he smote the sand a second time , that he might certify himself of Alaeddin 's death , but saw him not in the treasure ; whereat he redoubled in wrath , and yet more when it was certified to him that the lad was alive upon the surface of the earth and he knew that he had come forth from", "context_len": 128}}}, "fatigues": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "So he said in himself , `` I have suffered many hardships for the sake of the lamp and have endured fatigues such as none but I might brook , [ 549 ] and now yonder accursed one taketh it without stress and it is evident [ 550 ] [ that ] , an he have learned the use thereof , there will be none in the world richer than he", "context_len": 71}}}, "sultanate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "When he came to the city of the sultanate , [ 553 ] wherein was Alaeddin , he entered and alighting at one of the khans , heard the folk talking of nought but the magnificence of Alaeddin 's palace ; then , after he was rested from his journey , he changed [ 554 ] his clothes and went down to go round about in the thoroughfares of the city", "context_len": 71}}}, "khans": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "When he came to the city of the sultanate , [ 553 ] wherein was Alaeddin , he entered and alighting at one of the khans , heard the folk talking of nought but the magnificence of Alaeddin 's palace ; then , after he was rested from his journey , he changed [ 554 ] his clothes and went down to go round about in the thoroughfares of the city", "context_len": 71}}}, "descanting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "He passed no folk but they were descanting upon the palace and its magnificence and talking of Alaeddin 's grace and comeliness and his bounty and munificence and the goodliness of his manners and disposition ; so [ 555 ] he went up to one of those who were extolling Alaeddin on this wise and said to him , `` Prithee , fair youth , who is this whom you describe and praise ? '' `` O man , '' replied the other , `` meseemeth thou art a stranger and comest from afar ; but , granting thou art from a far country , hast thou not heard of the Amir Alaeddin , whose repute , methought , filled the earth , and of his palace ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "extolling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "He passed no folk but they were descanting upon the palace and its magnificence and talking of Alaeddin 's grace and comeliness and his bounty and munificence and the goodliness of his manners and disposition ; so [ 555 ] he went up to one of those who were extolling Alaeddin on this wise and said to him , `` Prithee , fair youth , who is this whom you describe and praise ? '' `` O man , '' replied the other , `` meseemeth thou art a stranger and comest from afar ; but , granting thou art from a far country , hast thou not heard of the Amir Alaeddin , whose repute , methought , filled the earth , and of his palace ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "khan": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Then he returned to the khan in a woeful state of chagrin and colour and despite , for envy of Alaeddin , and [ 557 ] taking his geomantic instruments , [ 558 ] smote his [ tablet of ] sand , so he might learn where the lamp was , and found that it was in the palace and not with Alaeddin ; [ 559 ] whereat he rejoiced with an exceeding joy and said , `` Now it will be an easy matter for me to bereave this accursed of his life and I have a way to come at the lamp", "context_len": 105}}}, "geomantic": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` Then he returned to the khan in a woeful state of chagrin and colour and despite , for envy of Alaeddin , and [ 557 ] taking his geomantic instruments , [ 558 ] smote his [ tablet of ] sand , so he might learn where the lamp was , and found that it was in the palace and not with Alaeddin ; [ 559 ] whereat he rejoiced with an exceeding joy and said , `` Now it will be an easy matter for me to bereave this accursed of his life and I have a way to come at the lamp", "context_len": 105}}}, "bereave": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "Then he embraced Alaeddin and fell to kissing him and said to him , `` Excuse me , O my son , for that I was going [ 623 ] to bereave thee of thy life , through the wickedness of yonder accursed sorcerer who cast thee into this pit ; and indeed , O my son , I was excusable in that which I did with thee , inasmuch as I saw myself bereft of my daughter and mine only one , who is dearer to me than my kingdom , and thou knowest how fathers ' hearts yearn upon their children , more by token that I have but the Lady Bedrulbudour", "context_len": 114}}}, "coppersmith": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Accordingly he went to a coppersmith and said to him , `` Make me so many [ 560 ] lamps [ 561 ] and take of me their worth in full ; [ 562 ] but I will have thee despatch them quickly", "context_len": 44}}}, "bartering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 97, "context": "When they were finished , the Maugrabin paid him their price , even that which he sought , and taking the lamps , carried them to the khan , where he laid them in a basket and fell to going round about in the markets and thoroughfares of the city and crying out , `` Ho ! who will barter an old lamp for a new lamp ? '' When the folk heard him crying this , they laughed at him and said , `` Certes , this man is mad , since he goeth about , bartering new lamps for old", "context_len": 102}}}, "aforetime": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 92, "context": "of his love and affection for his daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour , was wont , every day , when he awoke from his sleep , to open the window and look at her therefrom ; so he arose on the morrow , according to his wont , and opened his chamber-window , so he might see his daughter ; but [ 571 ] when he put out his head and looked for Alaeddin 's palace , he beheld nothing but a place swept [ and level ] , like as it was aforetime , and saw neither palace nor inhabitants ; [ 572 ] whereat amazement clad him and his wit was bewildered and he fell to rubbing his eyes , so haply they were bleared or dimmed", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` `` O King of the Age , '' rejoined the Vizier , `` I told Thy Grace aforetime that this palace and these affairs were all of them [ the work of ] enchantment", "context_len": 35}}}, "haply": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 122, "context": "of his love and affection for his daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour , was wont , every day , when he awoke from his sleep , to open the window and look at her therefrom ; so he arose on the morrow , according to his wont , and opened his chamber-window , so he might see his daughter ; but [ 571 ] when he put out his head and looked for Alaeddin 's palace , he beheld nothing but a place swept [ and level ] , like as it was aforetime , and saw neither palace nor inhabitants ; [ 572 ] whereat amazement clad him and his wit was bewildered and he fell to rubbing his eyes , so haply they were bleared or dimmed", "context_len": 128}}}, "pinioning": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` Accordingly [ 574 ] the officers shackled him and pinioning him , haled him along in irons and entered the city with him", "context_len": 24}}}, "overthrow": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "The Sultan looked at his subjects and seeing them swarming upon him and climbing up to the palace , that they might overthrow it , commanded the headsman to hold his hand from Alaeddin and bade the crier go forth among the people and proclaim that he pardoned Alaeddin and took him [ again ] into favour", "context_len": 57}}}, "bountifully": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "When Alaeddin found himself released and saw the Sultan sitting , he went up to him and said to him , `` O my lord , since Thy Grace hath bountifully vouchsafed me my life , [ 577 ] favour me [ yet farther ] and tell me the manner of my offence", "context_len": 53}}}, "woefullest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "He abode in the city two days in the woefullest of case , knowing not how he should do to find his palace and the Lady Bedrulbudour , his bride , what while certain of the folk used to come to him privily with meat and drink", "context_len": 47}}}, "whitherward": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Then he went forth , wandering in the deserts and knowing not whitherward he should aim , and ceased not going till he came to a river ; whereupon , his hope being cut off for stress of chagrin that possessed him , he thought to cast himself into the stream ; but , for that he was a pious Muslim , professing the unity of God , he feared God in himself and stood on the bank ; of the stream to perform the ablution", "context_len": 86}}}, "soever": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` `` Then , '' said Alaeddin , `` since this is not possible unto thee , take me and set me down beside my palace , in what land soever it is", "context_len": 33}}}, "forswore": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 65, "context": "Now the princess , of the excess of her grief for her separation from her husband and the Sultan her father and of her sore distress at that which had betided her with the accursed Maugrabin enchanter , used every day to arise , at the first peep of dawn , [ 586 ] and sit weeping ; nay , she slept not anights and forswore meat and drink", "context_len": 69}}}, "handmaid": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` So saying , she took his cup and filling it for herself with wine , bade the handmaid give him her cup , wherein was wine mingled with henbane , even as she had taught her how she should do , for that all the slaves and slave-girls in the palace wished his death and were at one against him with the Lady Bedrulbudour", "context_len": 65}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Moreover , I need no rich viands , but every day do thou favour me and send me by thy handmaid a piece of bread and a draught of water to my closet ; and when I am minded to eat , I will eat in my closet alone", "context_len": 49}}}, "joyance": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "His wife , [ 589 ] the Lady Bedrulbudour , met him at the door and they embraced and kissed each other with all joyance , till they fell a-weeping of the excess of their gladness", "context_len": 36}}}, "cozened": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 61, "context": "`` [ 590 ] Quoth he , `` How came this about ? '' So she acquainted him with the whole matter from first to last , telling him how they had bartered the old lamp for a new one ; `` and next morning , '' added she , `` we found ourselves in this country and he who had cozened me and changed the lamp told me that he had wroughten these tricks upon us of the might of his magic , by means of the lamp and that he is a Maugrabin from Africa [ 591 ] and that we are now in his native land", "context_len": 109}}}, "enrichment": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Moreover , he useth to say to me of thee that thou art the son of poor folk and that he was the cause of thine enrichment and seeketh to cajole me with talk , but never hath he seen of me aught but tears and weeping or heard from me one soft word", "context_len": 54}}}, "druggists": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Then he fared on in the high road till he came to the city and entering , betook himself to the drug-market , where for two diners he bought of [ one of ] the druggists two drachms of rare strong henbane , the son of its minute , [ 595 ] and retracing his steps , returned to the palace", "context_len": 61}}}, "drachms": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "Then he fared on in the high road till he came to the city and entering , betook himself to the drug-market , where for two diners he bought of [ one of ] the druggists two drachms of rare strong henbane , the son of its minute , [ 595 ] and retracing his steps , returned to the palace", "context_len": 61}}}, "henbane": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` So saying , she took his cup and filling it for herself with wine , bade the handmaid give him her cup , wherein was wine mingled with henbane , even as she had taught her how she should do , for that all the slaves and slave-girls in the palace wished his death and were at one against him with the Lady Bedrulbudour", "context_len": 65}}}, "uncleanness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "`` When the Lady Bedrulbudour heard Alaeddin 's words , she said ! to him , `` This is a thing exceeding hard on me to do ; but it is lawful to slay this accursed , so we may be delivered from his uncleanness who hath made me rue thy separation and that of my father", "context_len": 57}}}, "tirewoman": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` Then Alaeddin ate and drank with his wife that which stayed his hunger and rising at once , went forth the palace ; whereupon the Lady Bedrulbudour summoned her tirewoman , who busked her and adorned her , and she rose and donned fine clothes and perfumed herself", "context_len": 49}}}, "hearkening": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 54, "context": "When [ 602 ] the Maugrabin saw the love which the Lady Bedrulbudour professed to him and that she was changed from her whilom plight of grief , he thought that she had given up her hope of Alaeddin ; so he rejoiced greatly and said to her , `` O my soul , hearkening and obedience unto all that which thou wiliest and biddest me withal", "context_len": 67}}}, "wiliest": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "Then he rubbed the lamp and the Marid , its slave , appeared to him and said , `` Here am I , O my lord ; what wiliest thou ? '' Quoth Alaeddin , `` I will of thee that thou take up this palace from this country and carry it to the land of China and set it in the place where it was erst , before the Sultan 's palace", "context_len": 73}}}, "unease": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "`` [ 603 ] So saying , he went out and returned after a little with their sufficiency of wine ; and the Lady Bedrulbudour said to him , `` Thou hast been at pains [ 604 ] [ for me ] , and I have put thee to unease , [ 605 ] O my beloved", "context_len": 57}}}, "entangle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "Now she was unique in eloquence and sweetness of speech and she proceeded to beguile him and bespeak him with words significant [ 608 ] and sweet , so she might entangle him yet straitlier in the toils of her love", "context_len": 41}}}, "coquetry": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "So he redoubled in desire for her and was like to die for love of her , when he saw from her that which she showed him of sweetness of speech and coquetry ; [ 610 ] his head swam with ecstasy [ 611 ] and the world became changed [ 612 ] in his eyes", "context_len": 56}}}, "purposing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "`` Then she kissed [ 613 ] his cup and drinking it off , set it down and came up to him and kissed him on the cheek ; [ 614 ] whereat he was like to fly for joy and purposing to do even as she had done , raised the cup to his mouth and drank it all off , without looking if there were aught therein or not ; but no sooner had he done this than he turned over on his back , like a dead man , and the cup fell from his hand", "context_len": 99}}}, "vying": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The Lady Bedrulbudour rejoiced at this and the damsels ran , vying with each other in their haste , [ 615 ] and opened the palace-door [ 616 ] to Alaeddin , their lord ; whereupon he entered and [ 617 ] going up to his wife 's pavilion , [ 618 ] found her sitting at the table and the Maugrabin before her , as one slain", "context_len": 68}}}, "sorcerer": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 88, "context": "Then Alaeddin brought them up to the upper pavilion , [ 621 ] where they sat down and the Sultan proceeded to question the princess of her case and of that which had befallen her , whilst [ 622 ] she acquainted him with all that had happened to her and said to him , `` O my father , I breathed not till yesterday , when I saw my husband , and he it is who delivered me from the bondage of a Maugrabin , an accursed sorcerer , methinketh there is not a filthier than he on the face of the earth ; and but for my beloved Alaeddin , I had not won free of him and thou hadst not seen me all thy life", "context_len": 128}}}, "carousel": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Then they removed to the chamber of wine and carousel , where they sat drinking and making merry and kissing one another with all eagerness , for that it was long since they had had easance together ; and they ceased not from this till the sun of wine rose in their heads and sleep took them ; whereupon they arose and lay down on their bed in all rest and delight", "context_len": 72}}}, "filthier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 95, "context": "Then Alaeddin brought them up to the upper pavilion , [ 621 ] where they sat down and the Sultan proceeded to question the princess of her case and of that which had befallen her , whilst [ 622 ] she acquainted him with all that had happened to her and said to him , `` O my father , I breathed not till yesterday , when I saw my husband , and he it is who delivered me from the bondage of a Maugrabin , an accursed sorcerer , methinketh there is not a filthier than he on the face of the earth ; and but for my beloved Alaeddin , I had not won free of him and thou hadst not seen me all thy life", "context_len": 128}}}, "severance": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Indeed , O my father , there possessed me grief and sore chagrin , not only for my severance from thee , but also for the loss of my husband , to whom I shall be beholden all the days of my life , seeing he delivered me from that accursed enchanter", "context_len": 52}}}, "yearn": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 99, "context": "Then he embraced Alaeddin and fell to kissing him and said to him , `` Excuse me , O my son , for that I was going [ 623 ] to bereave thee of thy life , through the wickedness of yonder accursed sorcerer who cast thee into this pit ; and indeed , O my son , I was excusable in that which I did with thee , inasmuch as I saw myself bereft of my daughter and mine only one , who is dearer to me than my kingdom , and thou knowest how fathers ' hearts yearn upon their children , more by token that I have but the Lady Bedrulbudour", "context_len": 114}}}, "viler": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 51, "context": "This , then , is what befell Alaeddin with the Maugrabin ; but Alaeddin , for all this , was not altogether [ 625 ] quit of the accursed enchanter , withal his body had been burned and given to the winds ; for that the accursed one had a brother viler than he [ and yet more skilled ] in magic and geomancy and astrology ; [ nay , they were even ] as saith the proverb , `` A bean and it was cloven in twain ; '' [ 626 ] and each dwelt in one quarter of the world , so they might fill it [ 627 ] with their sorcery and craft and guile", "context_len": 118}}}, "geomancy": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 63, "context": "This , then , is what befell Alaeddin with the Maugrabin ; but Alaeddin , for all this , was not altogether [ 625 ] quit of the accursed enchanter , withal his body had been burned and given to the winds ; for that the accursed one had a brother viler than he [ and yet more skilled ] in magic and geomancy and astrology ; [ nay , they were even ] as saith the proverb , `` A bean and it was cloven in twain ; '' [ 626 ] and each dwelt in one quarter of the world , so they might fill it [ 627 ] with their sorcery and craft and guile", "context_len": 118}}}, "astrology": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 65, "context": "This , then , is what befell Alaeddin with the Maugrabin ; but Alaeddin , for all this , was not altogether [ 625 ] quit of the accursed enchanter , withal his body had been burned and given to the winds ; for that the accursed one had a brother viler than he [ and yet more skilled ] in magic and geomancy and astrology ; [ nay , they were even ] as saith the proverb , `` A bean and it was cloven in twain ; '' [ 626 ] and each dwelt in one quarter of the world , so they might fill it [ 627 ] with their sorcery and craft and guile", "context_len": 118}}}, "bean": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 81, "context": "This , then , is what befell Alaeddin with the Maugrabin ; but Alaeddin , for all this , was not altogether [ 625 ] quit of the accursed enchanter , withal his body had been burned and given to the winds ; for that the accursed one had a brother viler than he [ and yet more skilled ] in magic and geomancy and astrology ; [ nay , they were even ] as saith the proverb , `` A bean and it was cloven in twain ; '' [ 626 ] and each dwelt in one quarter of the world , so they might fill it [ 627 ] with their sorcery and craft and guile", "context_len": 118}}}, "foulest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "Then he smote the sand a second time , so he might learn how and where he died , and found that he had died in the land of China and by the foulest of deaths and knew that he who slew him was a youth by name Alaeddin", "context_len": 49}}}, "equipping": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "So he rose at once and equipping himself for travel , set out and traversed plains and deserts and mountains months and months , till he came to the land of China [ and entering ] the city of the sultanate , wherein was Alaeddin , repaired to the Strangers ' Khan , where he hired him a lodging and rested there a little", "context_len": 64}}}, "compassing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Then he arose to go round about the thoroughfares of the city , that he might spy him out a means of compassing his fell purpose , the which was to take vengeance of his brother on Alaeddin", "context_len": 38}}}, "anchoress": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "When the Lady Bedrulbudour saw this , she cried out and said to him , `` What hath this holy anchoress done , that thou burthenest thyself with the sore burden of her blood ? Hast thou no fear of God , that thou dost this and hast slain Fatimeh , who was a holy woman and whose divine gifts were renowned ? '' Quoth he to her , `` I have not slain Fatimeh ; nay , I have slain him who slew her ; for that this is the brother of the accursed Maugrabin enchanter , who took thee and by his sorcery transported the palace with thee to the land of Africa", "context_len": 115}}}, "avouching": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 53, "context": "There he sat down and heard those who sat beside him talk of an old woman , an anchoress , by name Fatimeh , who still abode in her place without the city , serving [ God ] , and came not down into the town but two days in the month , avouching her to be possessed of divine gifts galore", "context_len": 62}}}, "galore": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 61, "context": "There he sat down and heard those who sat beside him talk of an old woman , an anchoress , by name Fatimeh , who still abode in her place without the city , serving [ God ] , and came not down into the town but two days in the month , avouching her to be possessed of divine gifts galore", "context_len": 62}}}, "devout": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` So [ 632 ] he went up to the folk who were speaking of the devout old woman 's supernatural powers and said to one of them , `` O uncle , I hear you talk of the divine gifts of one she-saint , [ 633 ] by name Fatimeh", "context_len": 51}}}, "fasts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "`` What , thou art in our city and hast not heard of the divine gifts of my Lady [ 635 ] Fatimeh ? Apparently , good man , [ 636 ] thou art a stranger , since thou hast never chanced to hear of the fasts of this holy woman and her abhorrence of the world and the goodliness of her piety", "context_len": 63}}}, "intercession": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 89, "context": "`` `` Ay , my lord , '' replied the Maugrabin , `` I am indeed a stranger and arrived but yesternight in this your town ; wherefore I beseech thee tell me of the divine gifts of this holy woman and where her place is , for that I have fallen into a calamity and would fain go to her and crave her of prayer , so haply God ( to whom belong might and majesty ) may deliver me from my stress , by means of her intercession", "context_len": 90}}}, "soliciting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 69, "context": "Now , by the decree of destiny , Fatimeh came down on the morrow to the city and the enchanter , going forth the Khan in the morning , saw the folk crowding together ; so he went up , to see what was toward , and found Fatimeh standing , whilst every one who had a pain or an ache came to her , seeking her blessing and soliciting her prayers , and whenas she stroked him , he was made whole of his ailment", "context_len": 86}}}, "anoint": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` So she gave him her clothes and head-bands and her kerchief and veil ; and he said to her , `` Now must thou anoint me , to boot , with somewhat , so my face may become like unto shine in colour", "context_len": 44}}}, "vial": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Accordingly Fatimeh went within the cavern and bringing out a vial of ointment , took thereof in her palm and anointed his face withal , whereupon it became like unto hers in colour", "context_len": 34}}}, "perfidious": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Now this was what the accursed sorcerer aimed at ; however , the better to accomplish his perfidious intent , [ 647 ] he [ dissembled and ] said to her , `` O my lady , I am a poor woman sitting in the desert and it beseemeth not that the like of me should abide in kings ' palaces", "context_len": 61}}}, "dissembled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "Now this was what the accursed sorcerer aimed at ; however , the better to accomplish his perfidious intent , [ 647 ] he [ dissembled and ] said to her , `` O my lady , I am a poor woman sitting in the desert and it beseemeth not that the like of me should abide in kings ' palaces", "context_len": 61}}}, "privacy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` But only to think of it , '' Grandpapa would go on to himself in the privacy of his own room , `` mountain children can be brought down and set into schools , and the Van Ruypen money do the old lady some good , -- and there is Pip , -- see what she has got there , -- and nobody to interfere with what she 'll spend on him", "context_len": 73}}}, "craftsman": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 64, "context": "`` asked the princess , `` and where shall we find its egg ? '' And the Moor said to her , `` O my lady , this is a great bird that taketh up camels and elephants in its talons and flieth with them , of its bigness and greatness ; it is mostly to be found in the mountain Caf and the craftsman who builded this palace [ 654 ] is able to bring its egg", "context_len": 78}}}, "abysses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` This is to me the easiest of all things ; so be easy , for it is enough that thou tell me of that which thou wishest and I will fetch it thee from the abysses of the world on the speediest wise", "context_len": 44}}}, "denier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "When the Marid heard Alaeddin 's words , his face frowned and he was wroth and cried out with a terrible great voice , saying , `` O denier of benefits , doth it not suffice thee that I and all the slaves of the Lamp are at thy service and wouldst thou eke have me bring thee our liege lady , for thy pleasure , and hang her in the dome of thy pavilion , to divert thee and thy wife ? By Allah , ye deserve that I should forthright reduce you both to ashes and scatter you to the winds ! But , inasmuch as ye are ignorant , thou and she , concerning this matter and know not its inward from its outward ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "liege": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 59, "context": "When the Marid heard Alaeddin 's words , his face frowned and he was wroth and cried out with a terrible great voice , saying , `` O denier of benefits , doth it not suffice thee that I and all the slaves of the Lamp are at thy service and wouldst thou eke have me bring thee our liege lady , for thy pleasure , and hang her in the dome of thy pavilion , to divert thee and thy wife ? By Allah , ye deserve that I should forthright reduce you both to ashes and scatter you to the winds ! But , inasmuch as ye are ignorant , thou and she , concerning this matter and know not its inward from its outward ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "hem": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "Now you know yourself that , although you took a great deal of pains , the hem was rather crooked and some of the stitches quite long , yet grandpapa was more pleased with that one than with the whole dozen which Aunt Helen hemmed , and which were beautifully done , because he knew that you had done the best you could , and that it was a great effort for you", "context_len": 73}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` And she wanted mamma to let her hem a pock'-han'kerchief and earn some money , '' said Bessie , `` but she ca n't , for the doctor says she mus n't use her eye while it 's so black", "context_len": 41}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` Mamma , '' said Maggie , drawing a long sigh , `` I think I 'll have a self-denial and hem those towels", "context_len": 24}}}, "usances": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Moreover he kissed the hem of his sleeve and welcomed him , [ 666 ] saying , `` O my Lady Fatimeh , I beseech thee do me a kindness , since I know thy usances in the matter of the healing of pains , for that there hath betided me a sore pain in my head", "context_len": 57}}}, "frauds": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Yea , this accursed one was his brother and came to this country and wrought these frauds , slaying Fatimeh and donning her clothes and coming hither , so he might take vengeance on me for his brother", "context_len": 38}}}, "misdoubt": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Moreover , it was he who taught thee to seek of me a Roc 's egg , so my destruction should ensue thereof ; and if thou misdoubt of my word , come and see whom I have slain", "context_len": 39}}}, "footnotes": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 0, "context": "FOOTNOTES :", "context_len": 2}}}, "unidentified": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "( as yet unidentified ) contained in the Royal Libraries of Paris", "context_len": 12}}}, "cautions": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Galland himself cautions us that the Stories of Zeyn Alasnam and Codadad do not belong to the Thousand and One Nights and were published ( how he does not explain ) without his authority", "context_len": 34}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "Willie had been going on fearlessly , listening to his gentle little guide ; but at the sound of the lady 's voice he started , and as she kept repeating her cautions , he grew nervous and uneasy ; while Bessie , instead of watching his steps and taking heed to her own , kept glancing up at her aunt with an uncomfortable sense of being watched by those sharp eyes", "context_len": 72}}}, "incidental": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 92, "context": "copies of the Thousand and One Nights known to exist in the public libraries of Europe Might yet cast some light upon the origin of the interpolated tales ; but , in view of the strong presumption afforded by internal evidence that they are of modern composition and form no part of the authentic text , it can hardly be expected , where the result and the value of that result are alike so doubtful , that any competent person will be found to undertake so heavy a task , except as incidental to some more general enquiry", "context_len": 98}}}, "uniformity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "[ Footnote 4 : For the sake of uniformity and convenience of reference , I use , throughout this Introduction , Galland 's spelling of the names which occur in his translation , returning to my own system of transliteration in my rendering of the stories themselves", "context_len": 47}}}, "transliteration": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "[ Footnote 4 : For the sake of uniformity and convenience of reference , I use , throughout this Introduction , Galland 's spelling of the names which occur in his translation , returning to my own system of transliteration in my rendering of the stories themselves", "context_len": 47}}}, "antedating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` The Edinburgh Review ( July , 1886 ) gives the date of the first edition as 1785 ; but this is an error , probably founded upon the antedating of a copy of the Cabinet des Fees , certain sets of which ( though not actually completed till 1793 ) are dated , for some publisher 's reason , 1785", "context_len": 61}}}, "supplemental": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "[ Footnote 7 : These four ( supplemental ) vols", "context_len": 10}}}, "morales": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 94, "context": "of the Cabinet des Fees ( printed in 1793 , though antedated 1788 and 1789 ) do not form the first edition of Chavis and Cazotte 's so-called Sequel , which was in 1793 added , by way of supplement , to the Cabinet des Fees , having been first published in 1788 ( two years after the completion-in thirty-seven volumes-of that great storehouse of supernatural fiction ) under the title of `` Les Veillees Persanes '' or `` Les Veillees du Sultan Schahriar avec la Sultane Scheherazade , histoires incroyables , amusantes et morales , traduites par M", "context_len": 99}}}, "corrector": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Burton in his estimate of these tales , which seem to me , even in Caussin de Perceval 's corrector rendering and in his own brilliant and masterly version , very inferior , in style , conduct and diction , to those of `` the old Arabian Nights , '' whilst I think `` Chavis and Cazotte 's Continuation '' utterly unworthy of republication , whether in part or `` in its entirety", "context_len": 73}}}, "masterly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "Burton in his estimate of these tales , which seem to me , even in Caussin de Perceval 's corrector rendering and in his own brilliant and masterly version , very inferior , in style , conduct and diction , to those of `` the old Arabian Nights , '' whilst I think `` Chavis and Cazotte 's Continuation '' utterly unworthy of republication , whether in part or `` in its entirety", "context_len": 73}}}, "republication": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 86, "context": "Among the twenty millions of people in the United States , there are perhaps two millions , between the ages of fifteen and twenty-five , who wish to become acquainted , in general , with the leading events in the history of the Old World , and of ancient times , but who , coming upon the stage in this land and at this period , have ideas and conceptions so widely different from those of other nations and of other times , that a mere republication of existing accounts is not what they require", "context_len": 95}}}, "entirety": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 72, "context": "Burton in his estimate of these tales , which seem to me , even in Caussin de Perceval 's corrector rendering and in his own brilliant and masterly version , very inferior , in style , conduct and diction , to those of `` the old Arabian Nights , '' whilst I think `` Chavis and Cazotte 's Continuation '' utterly unworthy of republication , whether in part or `` in its entirety", "context_len": 73}}}, "coadjutor": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "As Caussin de Perceval remarks , it is evident that Shawish ( whether from ignorance or carelessness ) must , in many instances , have utterly misled his French coadjutor ( who had no knowledge of Arabic ) as to the meaning of the original , whilst it is much to be regretted that a writer of exquisite genius and one of the first stylists of the 18th century , such as the author of the Diable Amoureux , ( a masterpiece to be ranked with Manon Lescaut and Le Neveu de Rameau , ) should have stooped to the commission of the flagrant offences against good taste and artistic morality which disfigure well nigh every line of the so-called `` Sequel to the 1001 Nights", "context_len": 126}}}, "stylists": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 64, "context": "As Caussin de Perceval remarks , it is evident that Shawish ( whether from ignorance or carelessness ) must , in many instances , have utterly misled his French coadjutor ( who had no knowledge of Arabic ) as to the meaning of the original , whilst it is much to be regretted that a writer of exquisite genius and one of the first stylists of the 18th century , such as the author of the Diable Amoureux , ( a masterpiece to be ranked with Manon Lescaut and Le Neveu de Rameau , ) should have stooped to the commission of the flagrant offences against good taste and artistic morality which disfigure well nigh every line of the so-called `` Sequel to the 1001 Nights", "context_len": 126}}}, "flagrant": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 103, "context": "As Caussin de Perceval remarks , it is evident that Shawish ( whether from ignorance or carelessness ) must , in many instances , have utterly misled his French coadjutor ( who had no knowledge of Arabic ) as to the meaning of the original , whilst it is much to be regretted that a writer of exquisite genius and one of the first stylists of the 18th century , such as the author of the Diable Amoureux , ( a masterpiece to be ranked with Manon Lescaut and Le Neveu de Rameau , ) should have stooped to the commission of the flagrant offences against good taste and artistic morality which disfigure well nigh every line of the so-called `` Sequel to the 1001 Nights", "context_len": 126}}}, "disfigure": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 112, "context": "As Caussin de Perceval remarks , it is evident that Shawish ( whether from ignorance or carelessness ) must , in many instances , have utterly misled his French coadjutor ( who had no knowledge of Arabic ) as to the meaning of the original , whilst it is much to be regretted that a writer of exquisite genius and one of the first stylists of the 18th century , such as the author of the Diable Amoureux , ( a masterpiece to be ranked with Manon Lescaut and Le Neveu de Rameau , ) should have stooped to the commission of the flagrant offences against good taste and artistic morality which disfigure well nigh every line of the so-called `` Sequel to the 1001 Nights", "context_len": 126}}}, "adulterous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 81, "context": "`` `` Far be it '' ( as the Arabs say ) that we should do so cruel a wrong to so well and justly beloved a memory as that of Jacques Cazotte as to attempt to perpetuate the remembrance of a literary crime which one can hardly believe him to have committed in sober earnest ! Rather let us seek to bury in oblivion this his one offence and suffer kind Lethe with its beneficent waters to wash this `` adulterous blot '' from his else unsullied name", "context_len": 89}}}, "unsullied": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 87, "context": "`` `` Far be it '' ( as the Arabs say ) that we should do so cruel a wrong to so well and justly beloved a memory as that of Jacques Cazotte as to attempt to perpetuate the remembrance of a literary crime which one can hardly believe him to have committed in sober earnest ! Rather let us seek to bury in oblivion this his one offence and suffer kind Lethe with its beneficent waters to wash this `` adulterous blot '' from his else unsullied name", "context_len": 89}}}, "invoke": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "It was not a matter in which he could invoke the assistance of the law", "context_len": 15}}}, "aladdin": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Aladdin", "context_len": 1}}}, "devotees": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "holy men , devotees , saints", "context_len": 6}}}, "magical": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 34, "context": "The crust was so flaky that it seemed to melt in the boy \u2019 s mouth , and the apple filling had a taste and flavor that had been imparted to it in some magical manner by the genius of the woman who seemed to bestow something sweet and wholesome upon the very atmosphere about her", "context_len": 56}}}, "ascendants": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "[ Footnote 26 : Or `` ascendants '' ( tewali )", "context_len": 11}}}, "copyist": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 71, "context": "`` The Sultan `` goes round in the kiosk '' and seeing `` the casement ( shubbak ) , which Alaeddin had purposely left defective , without completion , '' said to the Vizier , `` Knowest thou the reason ( or cause ) of the lack of completion of this casement and its lattices ? '' ( shearihi , or quaere , `` [ this ] lattice , '' the copyist having probably omitted by mistake the diacritical points over the final ha )", "context_len": 85}}}, "sobriquet": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 60, "context": "Zein-ud-din ( Adornment of the Faith ) [ he ] of the Images , Zein ( adornment ) not being a name used by the Arabic-speaking races , unless with some such addition as ud-Din ( `` of the Faith '' ) , and the affix ul Asnam ( `` [ He ] of the Images '' ) being a sobriquet arising from the circumstances of the hero 's after-life , unless its addition , as recommended by the astrologers , is meant as an indication of the latter 's fore-knowledge of what was to befall him thereafter", "context_len": 98}}}, "elliptical": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` This is a difficult passage to translate , owing to its elliptical form ; but the meaning is that the princess wished to assure Alaeddin that what had happened was not due to any slackening in the warmth of her affection for him", "context_len": 44}}}, "astrologers": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 79, "context": "Zein-ud-din ( Adornment of the Faith ) [ he ] of the Images , Zein ( adornment ) not being a name used by the Arabic-speaking races , unless with some such addition as ud-Din ( `` of the Faith '' ) , and the affix ul Asnam ( `` [ He ] of the Images '' ) being a sobriquet arising from the circumstances of the hero 's after-life , unless its addition , as recommended by the astrologers , is meant as an indication of the latter 's fore-knowledge of what was to befall him thereafter", "context_len": 98}}}, "poets": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "May was just such a month as the poets love to sing , and June , rich in its promise of fruit , had passed when the events we are about to relate occurred", "context_len": 34}}}, "min": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 41, "context": "' '' As far as I can see , Galland was quite right , a staircase ( and not a ladder ) being , in my judgment , meant in each case , and Sir Richard Burton 's translation of sullem min thelathin derejeh as `` a ladder of thirty rungs '' ( see ante p", "context_len": 56}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "des ) the deep or remote dawn '' ( min el fejri ' l ghemic , Syr", "context_len": 17}}}, "depose": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "The strong sentiment of love and devotion with which he inspired all around him at the commencement of his career , was now gone , and his generals and officers were continually planning schemes to depose him from the power which he seemed no longer to have the energy to wield ; or , at least , Alexander was continually suspecting that such plans were formed , and he was kept in a continual state of uneasiness and anxiety in discovering and punishing them", "context_len": 84}}}, "fulani": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "[ Footnote 43 : Keszr abouka ' l fulani ( vulg", "context_len": 11}}}, "vulg": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` It is certain to me , '' Constat mihi , fe-meikeni ( vulg", "context_len": 14}}}, "coined": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Burton , `` antique golden pieces '' ; but there is nothing to show that the gold was coined", "context_len": 20}}}, "misnomer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "palace , but commonly meaning , in modern Arabic , an upper story or detached corps de logis ( pavilion in the French sense , an evident misnomer in the present case )", "context_len": 33}}}, "adopts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "[ Footnote 56 : Here she adopts her son 's previous idea that the old man of the dream was the Prophet in person", "context_len": 24}}}, "alluvial": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "alluvial ) gold , gold in its native state , needing no smelting to extract it", "context_len": 16}}}, "smelting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "alluvial ) gold , gold in its native state , needing no smelting to extract it", "context_len": 16}}}, "pedestals": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "There were twelve of these statues , sculptured with great art , and they were borne along on elevated pedestals , with censers , and incense , and various ceremonies of homage , while vast multitudes of spectators lined the way", "context_len": 41}}}, "implying": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "Why was it that Casson had betrayed such intense excitement at the mention of Sobrinini \u2019 s name ? Why had he referred to her as Nini ? That sounded to Bomba like a pet name , implying long acquaintance and familiarity", "context_len": 42}}}, "lexicologists": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "It is by some lexicologists supposed to have arisen from the circumstance of a man answering another , who begged of him a wine-jar ( hubb ) , with the words , `` Ay , I will give thee a jar and a cover ( kerameh ) also , '' and to have thus become a tropical expression of ready compliance with a petition , as who should say , `` I will give thee what thou askest and more", "context_len": 80}}}, "thereunto": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 53, "context": "Being personally acquainted with the before-mentioned parties , what was more natural than that , on hearing that this heinous crime had been committed , I should become profoundly interested in the case and should resolve to give it my earnest attention with the determination of solving the deep , dark mystery appertaining thereunto ?", "context_len": 55}}}, "translates": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 1, "context": "Burton translates , `` astrology , '' and astrology ( or astronomy ) ; is the classical meaning of the word ; but the common meaning in modern Arabic is `` the science of physiognomy , '' cf", "context_len": 38}}}, "wolds": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Burton translates , `` the wildest of wolds , '' apparently supposing atfer to be a mistranscription for aefer , which is very possible", "context_len": 24}}}, "carib": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "[ Footnote 76 : Kewaribji , a word formed by adding the Turkish affix ji to the Arabic kewarib , plural of carib , a small boat", "context_len": 27}}}, "ila": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 43, "context": "`` Except that , O my son , the Vizier bespoke him a privy word ( kelam sirriyy ) ere he promised me ; then , after the Vizier bespoke him a word privily ( sirran ) , he promised me to ( ila ) three months", "context_len": 47}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "[ Footnote 109 : Netser ila necshetihim ( lit", "context_len": 9}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` to the contrary of him '' ( ila khilafihi )", "context_len": 11}}}, "saffron": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "pounced amber ) signifies also `` saffron '' ; but the obbligato juxtaposition of aloes and sandal-wood tends to show that what is meant is the well-known product of the sperm-whale", "context_len": 31}}}, "obbligato": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "pounced amber ) signifies also `` saffron '' ; but the obbligato juxtaposition of aloes and sandal-wood tends to show that what is meant is the well-known product of the sperm-whale", "context_len": 31}}}, "juxtaposition": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "pounced amber ) signifies also `` saffron '' ; but the obbligato juxtaposition of aloes and sandal-wood tends to show that what is meant is the well-known product of the sperm-whale", "context_len": 31}}}, "aloes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "pounced amber ) signifies also `` saffron '' ; but the obbligato juxtaposition of aloes and sandal-wood tends to show that what is meant is the well-known product of the sperm-whale", "context_len": 31}}}, "product": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "pounced amber ) signifies also `` saffron '' ; but the obbligato juxtaposition of aloes and sandal-wood tends to show that what is meant is the well-known product of the sperm-whale", "context_len": 31}}}, "interpolation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "It is possible that the mention of this latter may be an interpolation by some ignorant copyist , who , seeing two only of the three favourite Oriental scents named , took upon himself to complete the odoriferous trinity , so dear to Arab writers , by the addition of ambergris", "context_len": 51}}}, "trinity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "It is possible that the mention of this latter may be an interpolation by some ignorant copyist , who , seeing two only of the three favourite Oriental scents named , took upon himself to complete the odoriferous trinity , so dear to Arab writers , by the addition of ambergris", "context_len": 51}}}, "supposes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Burton reads yamin and supposes it to be a copyist 's error for yasmin , but this is a mistake ; the word in the text is clearly yas , though the final s , being somewhat carelessly written in the Arabic MS , might easily be mistaken for mn with an undotted noun", "context_len": 54}}}, "mn": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "Burton reads yamin and supposes it to be a copyist 's error for yasmin , but this is a mistake ; the word in the text is clearly yas , though the final s , being somewhat carelessly written in the Arabic MS , might easily be mistaken for mn with an undotted noun", "context_len": 54}}}, "asker": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "brasier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` the gleaming of a brasier '' ( berc kanoun )", "context_len": 11}}}, "eth": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Kanoun is the Syrian name of two winter months , December ( Kanoun el awwal or first ) and January ( Kanoun eth thani or second )", "context_len": 27}}}, "mystical": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "[ Footnote 86 : So as to form a magic barrier against the Jinn , after the fashion of the mystical circles used by European necromancers", "context_len": 26}}}, "necromancers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "[ Footnote 86 : So as to form a magic barrier against the Jinn , after the fashion of the mystical circles used by European necromancers", "context_len": 26}}}, "synecdoche": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "a place of passage , but by extension a porch , a gallery , an opening , here ( and here only ) used by synecdoche for the oriel itself ) , and the famous roe 's egg is proposed to be suspended from `` the dome ( cubbeh ) of the upper chamber '' ( el keszr el faucaniyy ) , thus showing that the latter was crowned with a dome or cupola", "context_len": 74}}}, "passim": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 29, "context": "[ Footnote 99 : Said in a quasi-complimentary sense , as we say , `` Confound him , what a clever rascal he is ! '' See the Nights passim for numerous instances of this", "context_len": 35}}}, "ala": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` to work upon her traces or course '' ( tesaa ala menakibiha )", "context_len": 14}}}, "es": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "sari": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` the thirsty one ( es szadi ) and the goer-forth by day or in the morning '' ( el ghadi ) ; but this is most probably a mistranscription for the common phrase es sari ( the goer by night ) wa ' l ghadi , often used in the sense of `` comers and goers '' simply", "context_len": 59}}}, "goer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "`` the thirsty one ( es szadi ) and the goer-forth by day or in the morning '' ( el ghadi ) ; but this is most probably a mistranscription for the common phrase es sari ( the goer by night ) wa ' l ghadi , often used in the sense of `` comers and goers '' simply", "context_len": 59}}}, "goers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 56, "context": "`` the thirsty one ( es szadi ) and the goer-forth by day or in the morning '' ( el ghadi ) ; but this is most probably a mistranscription for the common phrase es sari ( the goer by night ) wa ' l ghadi , often used in the sense of `` comers and goers '' simply", "context_len": 59}}}, "serai": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "szerai for serai ( palace ) , szufreh , for sufreh ( meal-tray ) , for hheresza for hheresa ( he guarded ) , etc", "context_len": 25}}}, "ra": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": ", whilst no one acquainted with the Arabic written character need be reminded how easy it is to mistake a carelessly written-r ( ra ) for d ( dal ) or vice-versa ]", "context_len": 33}}}, "caravanserai": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "[ Footnote 104 : The mosque being the caravanserai of the penniless stranger", "context_len": 13}}}, "trickster": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "of melaoun , accursed ) ; but the word in the text is plainly mulaa'bein ( objective dual of mulaa ' b , a trickster , malicious joker , hence , by analogy , sharper )", "context_len": 36}}}, "presentment": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Scriptural `` image and presentment '' ) wa szufretihim , i", "context_len": 11}}}, "alan": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "I am now become in confusion of or at him ( lianneni alan szirtu fi khejaleh ( properly khejleh ) minhu )", "context_len": 22}}}, "religiousness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": ", `` religiousness or devoutness ( diyaneh ) was by nature in him , '' i", "context_len": 16}}}, "devoutness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": ", `` religiousness or devoutness ( diyaneh ) was by nature in him , '' i", "context_len": 16}}}, "conscientiousness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Burton , `` He was by nature conscientious , '' which does not quite express the meaning of the text ; conscientiousness being hardly an Oriental virtue", "context_len": 27}}}, "scholastic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "This word is not sufficiently rendered by `` education , '' which modern use has practically restricted to scholastic teaching , though the good old English phrase `` to bring up '' is of course a literal translation of the Latin educare", "context_len": 42}}}, "howdah": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "[ Footnote 127 : Miheffeh , a kind of howdah with a flat roof or top", "context_len": 16}}}, "ruth": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "[ Footnote 129 : Burton adds here , `` Thou wouldst feel ruth for me", "context_len": 15}}}, "baud": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "[ Footnote 132 : [ A ] nehnu bedna baud an hukm", "context_len": 12}}}, "metonymically": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "The word hukm , which commonly signifies the exercise of government or judicial power , is here used metonymically in the sense of the place of dominion , the seat of government", "context_len": 32}}}, "interjection": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "[ Footnote 133 : Or `` God forbid ! '' ( Hhasha ) , a common interjection , implying unconditional denial", "context_len": 21}}}, "unconditional": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "[ Footnote 133 : Or `` God forbid ! '' ( Hhasha ) , a common interjection , implying unconditional denial", "context_len": 21}}}, "initial": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "[ Footnote 137 : Sic ( tentsur ) , but this is probably a copyist 's error for `` we may see '' ( nentsur ) , the difference being only a question of one or two diacritical points over the initial letter", "context_len": 43}}}, "bi": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 42, "context": "says , `` He entered a coffee-house ( kehweh , Syrian for kehawi ) , and there used to go in thereto all the notables of the city , and he heard a company , all of them engaged in ( ammalin bi , a very vulgar expression ) talking of the Amir Alaeddin 's palace , etc", "context_len": 58}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "[ Footnote 344 : Elladhi hu alan ca 'm bi maashina", "context_len": 11}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "condolence": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "[ Footnote 142 : Azaa , strictly the formal sitting in state to receive visits of condolence for the death of a relation , but in modern parlance commonly applied , by extension , to the funeral ceremonies themselves", "context_len": 39}}}, "parlance": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` a way , '' but in modern parlance `` a ladder '' or `` staircase '' which the story-teller uses interchangeably with sullem , in speaking of the stair leading down into the underground , thus showing that he considered the two words synonymous", "context_len": 45}}}, "castigation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "[ Footnote 148 : Burton adds here , `` Counsel and castigation were of no avail", "context_len": 16}}}, "astronomy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Instruments of astronomy or astrology '' ( tenjim ) ; but tenjim is also used in the sense of geomancy , in which operation , as before explained , astrology plays an important part , and the context shows that the word is here intended to bear this meaning", "context_len": 50}}}, "condole": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Burton `` condole with him [ over the past ]", "context_len": 10}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "[ Footnote 165 : Burton , `` I have none to condole with now save thyself '' ]", "context_len": 18}}}, "ked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "[ Footnote 162 : El bein ked efjaani fihi , i e", "context_len": 12}}}, "ana": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "ski": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "[ Footnote 185 : Uhheszszilu ana ma '' ski ana buddi men yuayyishani", "context_len": 13}}}, "fils": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "fils de famille ) , but here the sense seems to be as in the text", "context_len": 16}}}, "constrain": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "[ Footnote 187 : Or `` constrain not thyself for me , '' in do not be ashamed to say what thou wishes '' , lit", "context_len": 26}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` And each man , we constrain him to carry his actions ( ta ' r , lit", "context_len": 18}}}, "kana": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "[ Footnote 188 : Fe-in kana keman ( vulg", "context_len": 9}}}, "anna": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "for kema anna )", "context_len": 4}}}, "confirms": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "This passage confirms my reading of a former one ; see ante , p", "context_len": 14}}}, "vulgarism": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "[ Footnote 623 : Sic ( raihh ) , a common vulgarism in this text", "context_len": 15}}}, "chapiters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "1 Kings , vii 16 , `` two chapiters of molten brass", "context_len": 12}}}, "usefully": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` my lone , '' a Scotch expression , which might be usefully acclimatized in English prose and verse", "context_len": 19}}}, "acclimatized": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` my lone , '' a Scotch expression , which might be usefully acclimatized in English prose and verse", "context_len": 19}}}, "dir": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "[ Footnote 228 : Or `` pay attention , '' dir ( vulg", "context_len": 13}}}, "variant": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` The word ( zijeh ) here used is a dialectic ( Syrian ) variant of zewaj , marriage", "context_len": 19}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "a keszr , in its classical meaning of 'upper chamber , ' in which sense Lane indeed gives it as synonymous with the Turkish koushk , variant kushk , ) with four-and-twenty estrades ( liwan , a raised recess , generally a square-shaped room , large or small , open on the side facing the main saloon ) , all of it of emeralds and rubies and other jewels , and one estrade its kiosk was not finished", "context_len": 78}}}, "estrade": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 72, "context": "a keszr , in its classical meaning of 'upper chamber , ' in which sense Lane indeed gives it as synonymous with the Turkish koushk , variant kushk , ) with four-and-twenty estrades ( liwan , a raised recess , generally a square-shaped room , large or small , open on the side facing the main saloon ) , all of it of emeralds and rubies and other jewels , and one estrade its kiosk was not finished", "context_len": 78}}}, "transposes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "[ Footnote 235 : Burton transposes , `` where he entered the saloon and mounted the ladder ; '' but the context shows that the stair was a flight of steps leading up to the dais and not a ladder in it", "context_len": 42}}}, "spinels": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Burton adds , `` spinels and balasses", "context_len": 7}}}, "creates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "[ Footnote 245 : Burton says here , `` The text creates some confusion by applying sullem to staircase and ladder ; hence probably the latter is not mentioned by Galland and Co", "context_len": 33}}}, "rungs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "' '' As far as I can see , Galland was quite right , a staircase ( and not a ladder ) being , in my judgment , meant in each case , and Sir Richard Burton 's translation of sullem min thelathin derejeh as `` a ladder of thirty rungs '' ( see ante p", "context_len": 56}}}, "misconception": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "82 , note see FN 231 ) seems to me founded on a misconception , he being misled by the word `` fihi '' ( see my note ante , p", "context_len": 31}}}, "interchangeably": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` a way , '' but in modern parlance `` a ladder '' or `` staircase '' which the story-teller uses interchangeably with sullem , in speaking of the stair leading down into the underground , thus showing that he considered the two words synonymous", "context_len": 45}}}, "kan": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "[ Footnote 255 : Wa [ kan ] el aghreb an fi hadha ' l kenz [ kana ]", "context_len": 19}}}, "casuistry": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "[ Footnote 258 : A proverbial expression , meaning that , as he did not absolutely kill Alaeddin , though doing what was ( barring a miracle ) certain to cause his death , he could not be said to be his slayer ; a piece of casuistry not peculiar to the East , cf", "context_len": 55}}}, "hypocritical": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "the hypocritical show of tenderness with which the Spanish Inquisition was wont , when handing over a victim to the secular power for execution by burning alive , to recommend that there should be `` no effusion of blood", "context_len": 39}}}, "effusion": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "I could not remain at the hall myself , for , on such a night , it seemed necessary to be with my own people , and to be ready to seize any opportunity of saving the effusion of blood , or of giving protection to any who might seek refuge under the shelter of our roof , where murder would be sacrilege , a consideration of some importance where Christians , shame to say , were the murderers", "context_len": 79}}}, "solomon": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Solomon", "context_len": 1}}}, "registers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "The little Persian felt the cold very much , and delighted to hang about the fires and registers", "context_len": 18}}}, "fabled": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "Iest ) they appear ! ' '' Mohammed is fabled by Muslim theologians to have made a compact with the Jinn that they should not enter the houses of the faithful unless expressly summoned", "context_len": 34}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Of immense muscular strength , his blows came down like the fabled hammer of Thor , crushing helmet and breastplate alike before the well-tempered steel of his favourite weapon", "context_len": 29}}}, "certifying": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "He gave Adams a letter certifying to his having a claim of over three thousand dollars against the property , which he told Adams to show to his rich brother when he died , asserting that , although Colonel Harrington had shamefully neglected him , he would never dishonorably repudiate a claim of that kind", "context_len": 55}}}, "technical": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "a ninety-sixth part , `` carat '' being here used in its technical sense of a twenty-fourth part of anything", "context_len": 20}}}, "inconceivably": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "The tents of King Darius and his household were inconceivably splendid , and were filled with gold and silver vessels , caskets , vases , boxes of perfumes , and every imaginable article of luxury and show", "context_len": 37}}}, "jacks": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "It turned out that they were the right sort of jacks ; for , when the attendant went round to see if all was safe for the night , he found the three seated together , one of them reading aloud the Bible", "context_len": 43}}}, "juana": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "[ Footnote 291 : Elladhi iftekeda juana", "context_len": 7}}}, "theologians": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Iest ) they appear ! ' '' Mohammed is fabled by Muslim theologians to have made a compact with the Jinn that they should not enter the houses of the faithful unless expressly summoned", "context_len": 34}}}, "commandments": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` I am , in general , ready to obey all thy commandments '' ]", "context_len": 15}}}, "carat": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "a ninety-sixth part , `` carat '' being here used in its technical sense of a twenty-fourth part of anything", "context_len": 20}}}, "sixtieth": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "March , 1853 , saw Sir Colin Campbell in England ; but though he had passed his sixtieth year , most of which had been spent in his country 's service , his rest was not of long duration , as in 1854 he went out to the Crimea in command of the Highland brigade , consisting of the 42nd , 79th , and 93rd regiments", "context_len": 66}}}, "groat": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Burton , `` A copper carat , a bright polished groat", "context_len": 11}}}, "middling": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` Why , all your dolls are named Bessie , '' said Harry ; `` there are big Bessie and little Bessie and middling Bessie", "context_len": 25}}}, "augury": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "fortune as told by augury from the flight of birds , according to the method so much in favour with the ancients , but interpreted by the scholiasts as 'actions , ' each man 's actions being , according to them , the cause of his good and evil fortune , happiness or misery ) , on ( or about ,", "context_len": 61}}}, "idiomatic": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 4, "context": ") khatiraki is an idiomatic expression equivalent to the French , `` donnez-vous ( or prenez ) la peine '' and must be taken in connection with what follows , i", "context_len": 31}}}, "snip": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` No snip about it ! '' said Maggie ; `` and if I was everybody , I 'd tell Bessie every one of my secrets", "context_len": 26}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Then the huge mouth opened , displaying the horrible array of teeth that , if they once closed on the lad , would bite him in half as easily as a pair of shears would snip a thread", "context_len": 38}}}, "ti": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "[ Footnote 337 : Ijreker ti bi ' l hhecc", "context_len": 10}}}, "obligatory": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "[ Footnote 348 : The attitude implied in the word mutekettif and obligatory in presence of a superior , i", "context_len": 20}}}, "complainants": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "[ Footnote 349 : Or `` complainants , '' `` claimants", "context_len": 11}}}, "claimants": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "[ Footnote 349 : Or `` complainants , '' `` claimants", "context_len": 11}}}, "levee": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "[ Footnote 357 : Burton , `` levee days '' ]", "context_len": 11}}}, "mantilla": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Burton , `` mantilla", "context_len": 4}}}, "fe": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "[ Footnote 359 : Here the copyist , by the mistaken addition of fe ( so ) , transfers the `` forthright '' to the Vizier 's action of submission to the Sultan 's order", "context_len": 35}}}, "transfers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "[ Footnote 359 : Here the copyist , by the mistaken addition of fe ( so ) , transfers the `` forthright '' to the Vizier 's action of submission to the Sultan 's order", "context_len": 35}}}, "indemnity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "promise or assurance of indemnity , permission to speak freely , without fear of consequences", "context_len": 15}}}, "longanimity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Forbearance ( hhilm , clemency , longanimity , delay in requiting an evil-doer ) is incumbent from thine exalted highness unto ( ila ) three months", "context_len": 27}}}, "requiting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Forbearance ( hhilm , clemency , longanimity , delay in requiting an evil-doer ) is incumbent from thine exalted highness unto ( ila ) three months", "context_len": 27}}}, "ungrammatical": {"