word_index": 12, "context": "[ Footnote 424 : Or perhaps , `` we may with impunity rebut , '' etc", "context_len": 16}}}, "moneys": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "Burton , who takes a different view of the passage : `` Then the Minister ( although he marvelled at these riches even more than did the Sultan ) , whose envy was killing him and growing greater hour by hour , seeing his liege lord satisfied with the moneys and the dower and yet being unable to fight against fact , made answer , 'T is not worthy of her", "context_len": 71}}}, "enhancing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Burton , `` enhancing her kindly service", "context_len": 7}}}, "costliest": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "[ Footnote 450 : Burton , `` the costliest of clothes", "context_len": 11}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "[ Footnote 620 : Burton , `` his costliest raiment", "context_len": 10}}}, "hiya": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` not itself exactly with him '' ( ma hiya bi-eimhi indahu", "context_len": 12}}}, "dispread": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Burton , `` a mighty fine marriage-feast was dispread in the palace", "context_len": 12}}}, "hereunto": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` but the behoving on me for her service engageth ( or enforceth ) me to apply myself hereunto", "context_len": 19}}}, "au": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "[ Footnote 478 : Wa , but quaere au ( `` or '' ) ? ]", "context_len": 16}}}, "contused": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "[ Footnote 480 : The description of the famous upper hall with the four-and-twenty windows is one of the most contused and incoherent parts of the Nights and well-nigh defies the efforts of the translator to define the exact nature of the building described by the various and contradictory passages which refer to it", "context_len": 54}}}, "defies": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "[ Footnote 480 : The description of the famous upper hall with the four-and-twenty windows is one of the most contused and incoherent parts of the Nights and well-nigh defies the efforts of the translator to define the exact nature of the building described by the various and contradictory passages which refer to it", "context_len": 54}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "They will not fear when Bomba defies them", "context_len": 8}}}, "latticework": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 80, "context": "asked Alaeddin , `` What is the cause that the lattice of yonder kiosk ( kushk ) is not complete ? '' The defective part is soon after referred to , no less than four times , as `` the lattice of the kiosk '' ( sheriyyetu ' l kushk ) , thus showing that , in the writer 's mind , kushk , liwan and shubbak were synonymous terms for the common Arab projecting square-sided window , made of latticework , and I have therefore rendered the three words , when they occur in this sense , by our English `` oriel , '' to whose modern meaning ( a window that juts out , so as to form a small apartment ) , they exactly correspond", "context_len": 128}}}, "juts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 113, "context": "asked Alaeddin , `` What is the cause that the lattice of yonder kiosk ( kushk ) is not complete ? '' The defective part is soon after referred to , no less than four times , as `` the lattice of the kiosk '' ( sheriyyetu ' l kushk ) , thus showing that , in the writer 's mind , kushk , liwan and shubbak were synonymous terms for the common Arab projecting square-sided window , made of latticework , and I have therefore rendered the three words , when they occur in this sense , by our English `` oriel , '' to whose modern meaning ( a window that juts out , so as to form a small apartment ) , they exactly correspond", "context_len": 128}}}, "roe": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "a place of passage , but by extension a porch , a gallery , an opening , here ( and here only ) used by synecdoche for the oriel itself ) , and the famous roe 's egg is proposed to be suspended from `` the dome ( cubbeh ) of the upper chamber '' ( el keszr el faucaniyy ) , thus showing that the latter was crowned with a dome or cupola", "context_len": 74}}}, "cupola": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 64, "context": "the carelessness with which the text treats kushk as synonymous now with keszr or teyyareh and now with liwan or shubbak , it would seem that what is intended to be described is a lofty hall ( or sorer ) , erected on the roof of the palace , whether round or square we can not tell , but crowned with a dome or cupola and having four-and-twenty deep projecting windows or oriels , the lattice or trellis-work of which latter was formed ( instead of the usual wood ) of emeralds , rubies and other jewels , strung , we may suppose , upon rods of gold or other metal I have , at the risk of wearying my reader , treated this point at some length", "context_len": 128}}}, "enhanced": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "There was a suspiciousness in the action that enhanced Bart 's interest and curiosity , but he preserved his composure", "context_len": 20}}}, "jerid": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "[ Footnote 489 : Burton , `` with the jerid , '' but I find no mention of this in the text", "context_len": 22}}}, "caracoling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` he played '' ) applies to all kinds of martial exercises ; it may also mean simply , `` caracoling", "context_len": 21}}}, "ni": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Burton , `` the royal Hammam ( known as the Sult ni )", "context_len": 13}}}, "unveiling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` For the ceremony of displaying ( or unveiling ) the bride , see my `` Book of the Thousand Nights and One Night , '' Vol", "context_len": 27}}}, "alexander": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 0, "context": "Alexander the Great ; see my `` Book of the Thousand Nights and One Night , '' Vol", "context_len": 18}, "NOUN": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 0, "context": "Alexander remembered this reproof , and , finding vast stores of these expensive gums in Gaza , he sent the whole quantity to Leonnatus , telling him that he sent him this abundant supply that he might not have occasion to be so reserved and sparing for the future in his sacrifices to the gods", "context_len": 55}}}, "premier": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "[ Footnote 555 : Here Galland says , `` Il entra dans le lien le plus fameux et le plus frequente par les personnel de grande distinction , ou l'on s'assembloit pour boire d'une certaine boisson chance qui luy etoit connue des son premier voyage", "context_len": 45}}}, "fourchette": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "la fourchette", "context_len": 2}}}, "melodious": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "He had attended a country singing-school for two terms , and he was gifted with a strong and melodious voice", "context_len": 20}}}, "incoherence": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "so full of incoherence and confusion of terms that it is impossible to reconcile its inconsistencies", "context_len": 16}}}, "inconsistencies": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "The moral aim of the tale has been to depict the mental difficulties which our heathen forefathers had severally to encounter ere they could embrace Christianity -- difficulties chiefly arising from the inconsistencies of Christians -- and to set forth the example of one who , having found the `` pearl of great price , '' sold all he had and bought it , forsaking all that could appeal to the imagination of a warlike youth -- '' choosing rather to suffer affliction with the people of God , than to enjoy the pleasures of sin for a season", "context_len": 99}}}, "salaries": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` he increased ( or exceeded ) in the salaries ( or allowances ) of the poor and the indigent '' ( zada fi jewanicki ' l fukera wa ' l mesakin )", "context_len": 33}}}, "confections": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "[ Footnote 544 : Burton adds , `` and confections", "context_len": 10}}}, "stablest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` he set them down the stablest or skilfullest ( mustehhkem ) setting down", "context_len": 14}}}, "residual": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "These they divide into four figures , each representing the residual points of four lines , set one under another , and these four figures , which are called the mothers or primaries , they place side by side in one line", "context_len": 42}}}, "primaries": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 22, "context": "These new figures ( as well as those that follow ) are called the daughters or secondaries and are placed beside the primaries , by confrontation with which ( i , e , 5 with 1 , 6 with 2 , 7 with 3 and with 4 ) four fresh figures are obtained after the same fashion and placed side by side below the first eight", "context_len": 66}}}, "secondaries": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "These new figures ( as well as those that follow ) are called the daughters or secondaries and are placed beside the primaries , by confrontation with which ( i , e , 5 with 1 , 6 with 2 , 7 with 3 and with 4 ) four fresh figures are obtained after the same fashion and placed side by side below the first eight", "context_len": 66}}}, "confrontation": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "These new figures ( as well as those that follow ) are called the daughters or secondaries and are placed beside the primaries , by confrontation with which ( i , e , 5 with 1 , 6 with 2 , 7 with 3 and with 4 ) four fresh figures are obtained after the same fashion and placed side by side below the first eight", "context_len": 66}}}, "zodiac": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "Then ( says our author ) , the geomant proceeds to examine the sixteen figures thus obtained ( each of which has its name and its mansion , corresponding to one of the twelve signs of the zodiac or the four cardinal points , as well as its signification , good or bad , and indicates also , in a special way , a certain part of the elemental world ) and to note each figure according to its presage of weal or ill ; and so , with the aid of an astrological table giving the explanations of the various signs and combinations , according to the nature of the figure , its aspect , influence and temperament ( astrologically considered ) and the natural object it", "context_len": 128}}}, "elemental": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 64, "context": "author ) , the geomant proceeds to examine the sixteen figures thus obtained ( each of which has its name and its mansion , corresponding to one of the twelve signs of the zodiac or the four cardinal points , as well as its signification , good or bad , and indicates also , in a special way , a certain part of the elemental world ) and to note each figure according to its presage of weal or ill ; and so , with the aid of an astrological table giving the explanations of the various signs and combinations , according to the nature of the figure , its aspect , influence and temperament ( astrologically considered ) and the natural object it indicates , a judgment", "context_len": 128}}}, "presage": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 64, "context": "thus obtained ( each of which has its name and its mansion , corresponding to one of the twelve signs of the zodiac or the four cardinal points , as well as its signification , good or bad , and indicates also , in a special way , a certain part of the elemental world ) and to note each figure according to its presage of weal or ill ; and so , with the aid of an astrological table giving the explanations of the various signs and combinations , according to the nature of the figure , its aspect , influence and temperament ( astrologically considered ) and the natural object it indicates , a judgment is formed upon the question for a solution of which the", "context_len": 128}}}, "astrological": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 75, "context": "each of which has its name and its mansion , corresponding to one of the twelve signs of the zodiac or the four cardinal points , as well as its signification , good or bad , and indicates also , in a special way , a certain part of the elemental world ) and to note each figure according to its presage of weal or ill ; and so , with the aid of an astrological table giving the explanations of the various signs and combinations , according to the nature of the figure , its aspect , influence and temperament ( astrologically considered ) and the natural object it indicates , a judgment is formed upon the question for a solution of which the operation was undertaken", "context_len": 128}}}, "astrologically": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 102, "context": "each of which has its name and its mansion , corresponding to one of the twelve signs of the zodiac or the four cardinal points , as well as its signification , good or bad , and indicates also , in a special way , a certain part of the elemental world ) and to note each figure according to its presage of weal or ill ; and so , with the aid of an astrological table giving the explanations of the various signs and combinations , according to the nature of the figure , its aspect , influence and temperament ( astrologically considered ) and the natural object it indicates , a judgment is formed upon the question for a solution of which the operation was undertaken", "context_len": 128}}}, "dotting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "With the hundreds dotting the country all the way from the Missouri to Salt Lake City , it would seem that he ought to be in sight of one or more all the time , but he had not observed any since parting from his own friends", "context_len": 47}}}, "lien": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "[ Footnote 555 : Here Galland says , `` Il entra dans le lien le plus fameux et le plus frequente par les personnel de grande distinction , ou l'on s'assembloit pour boire d'une certaine boisson chance qui luy etoit connue des son premier voyage", "context_len": 45}}}, "plus": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "Our massed antiaircraft , plus the umbrella of our land-based fighter planes , will beat off any air attack our enemies may make", "context_len": 23}}}, "personnel": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "[ Footnote 555 : Here Galland says , `` Il entra dans le lien le plus fameux et le plus frequente par les personnel de grande distinction , ou l'on s'assembloit pour boire d'une certaine boisson chance qui luy etoit connue des son premier voyage", "context_len": 45}}}, "pas": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Il n ' y e-t pas plust '' t pris place qu'on lay versa de cette boisson dans une tasse et qu'on la luy presenta", "context_len": 25}}}, "tasse": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Il n ' y e-t pas plust '' t pris place qu'on lay versa de cette boisson dans une tasse et qu'on la luy presenta", "context_len": 25}}}, "gauche": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "gauche , il entendit qu'on s'entretenoit du palais d'Aladdin", "context_len": 9}}}, "palais": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "gauche , il entendit qu'on s'entretenoit du palais d'Aladdin", "context_len": 9}}}, "geomancer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Again , the implements of a geomancer of the higher order would include certain astrological instruments , such as an astrolabe , star-table , etc", "context_len": 25}}}, "astrolabe": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Again , the implements of a geomancer of the higher order would include certain astrological instruments , such as an astrolabe , star-table , etc", "context_len": 25}}}, "whirls": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "[ Footnote 576 : Sic ( dara haulahu thelatheta dauratin ) ; but qu 're should it not rather be , `` gave three sweeps or whirls with his sword round his head '' ? See my `` Book of the Thousand Nights and One Night , '' Vol", "context_len": 49}}}, "sheepsheads": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` The word here employed ( rewwas ) commonly signifies `` a man who cooks and sells sheepsheads , oxheads , etc", "context_len": 22}}}, "sellers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` `` The poor , '' adds he , `` at Cairo buy sheepsheads and for a trifle have them boiled in the bazaar by persons who are not only cooks , but sellers of sheepsheads , and are therefore called raa '' s , or in the Egyptian dialect rewwas", "context_len": 51}}}, "implication": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Whatever their faults and shortcomings -- and some of these had been grave enough -- such an idea , such an implication as this had never before presented itself to them -- that there was a thief in their midst , that one of their number had been guilty of flagrant dishonesty , of an absolute theft , and that of a large sum", "context_len": 64}}}, "decapitation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "It is , however , abundantly evident from the general context that this is the author 's intention in the passage in question , Alaeddin 's head being metaphorically in the hands of ( or pledged to ) the headsman , inasmuch as he had engaged to return and suffer decapitation in case he should not succeed in recovering the princess within forty days", "context_len": 64}}}, "grammatical": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 48, "context": "[ Footnote 584 : I suppose the verb which I render `` caused [ sleep ] get the mastery , '' to be ghelleba , II of gheleba , as the only way of making sense of this passage , though this reading involves some irregularity from a grammatical point of view", "context_len": 52}}}, "transposition": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Burton is apparently inclined to read tallectu by transposition , as he translates , `` I depend upon thy say", "context_len": 20}}}, "incommoded": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Thou hast burdened or incommoded thyself '' ( kellefta khatiraka ) , see previous note , p", "context_len": 18}}}, "equivocal": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "There was something equivocal in this speech , and Bill Mosely looked vexed", "context_len": 13}}}, "entente": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` equivocal , a double entente", "context_len": 6}}}, "haughtiness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "pride , haughtiness , but , by analogy , `` coquetry", "context_len": 11}}}, "intoxication": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "He saw four or five drunken men carrying a coffin containing the remains of a companion ; and such was their state of intoxication that they dropped it , and then broke out into foul language", "context_len": 36}}}, "interchangeable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` in '' ( fi ) ; but fi is evidently used here in mistake for bi , the two prepositions being practically interchangeable in modern Arabic of the style of our present text", "context_len": 34}}}, "vintner": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Burton , `` vintner", "context_len": 4}}}, "hoodwink": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` The meaning is that he dissembled his satisfaction at the princess 's proposal and made a show of refusal , so he might hoodwink her the more effectually", "context_len": 29}}}, "qualification": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` When `` the eye '' is spoken of without qualification , the `` evil eye '' is commonly meant ; here , however , it is evident that the reverse is intended", "context_len": 33}}}, "alden": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "ALDEN THE PONY EXPRESS RIDER", "context_len": 5}}}, "ellis": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "ELLIS", "context_len": 1}}}, "pulsing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Every man , woman and child seemed to feel the pulsing in the air", "context_len": 14}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "Their bodies wasted and their strength sapped by years of deprivation and torments at the hands of Jojasta could not throw off the fatigue as readily as the healthy jungle lad , whose veins were pulsing with vitality", "context_len": 38}}}, "sympathizer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Poor fellow ! he doesn \u2019 t look strong , remarked a sympathizer", "context_len": 13}}}, "aquiver": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "At the same instant , a wiry pony , with flashing eyes and dilated nostrils and fine muscles aquiver , made a tremendous leap which carried him almost to the middle of the street , and heading toward the river , plunged away under the prick of the spur , on a dead run", "context_len": 54}}}, "cowmen": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 42, "context": "The man might have been mistaken for a circus performer , in his brilliant uniform , with plated horn , pistol , scabbard and belt , gay , flower-worked leggings , jingling spurs and fine boots with high heels , such as cowmen and rustlers affect", "context_len": 46}}}, "rustlers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "The man might have been mistaken for a circus performer , in his brilliant uniform , with plated horn , pistol , scabbard and belt , gay , flower-worked leggings , jingling spurs and fine boots with high heels , such as cowmen and rustlers affect", "context_len": 46}}}, "arrowy": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "As the pony shot like a cannon ball out of the doors of the stable and sped with arrowy swiftness down the street , the two men with whom he had been in consultation within the structure stepped forward and watched him", "context_len": 42}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "He made astonishing bounds and skimmed with arrowy speed , but it was less than that of his pursuer", "context_len": 19}}}, "pouches": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 88, "context": "The broad-brimmed hat flaring up in front , the fluttering handkerchief about the neck , the fringed collar , the close fitting coat , cartridge belt at the waist , gloved hands , revolver at the hip , rifle across his thighs , fringed trouser legs , tucked just below the knees into the boots , spurs , keen , alert eyes , body leaning slightly forward but as firm in the saddle , as if the rider were a part of the pony ; these with mail pouches locked and secured in place , were the noticeable points of the man who dashed up at headlong speed his horse setting his legs and coming to a stop within a single bound", "context_len": 122}}}, "broncho": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "About the same time , Harry Roff , mounted on a mettled half-breed broncho , galloped eastward from Sacramento", "context_len": 19}}}, "redskin": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 13, "context": "Had Alden possessed his own gun , he would have brought down the redskin , for the distance was not great , and we remember he was a fine marksman , but the new weapon did not feel precisely like the one to which he was accustomed", "context_len": 47}}}, "exultingly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "There was no saying what boulder or rock sheltered a crouching redskin waiting exultingly with bow and arrow or rifle for the horseman to come within range", "context_len": 27}}}, "forded": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "Leaving the bathing place while there was yet time to reach home before dark , they came at last to a ford across the stream , the only spot where it could be safely forded , and as such known to the natives of the vicinity ; when their dogs began to whine , and to run with their noses to the ground , as if they had found something unusual to attract their attention", "context_len": 75}}}, "sparse": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 48, "context": "When they plunged into the mountains in the depth of winter , the temperature was often far below zero , but the undaunted rider kicked away the snow on the lee side of some boulder , kindled a fire of dead limbs , when he could find such sparse fuel , but more often he had nothing of that nature", "context_len": 60}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "He was still wandering and speculating , when he caught the gleam of water through the sparse willows that lined the trail", "context_len": 22}}}, "remounted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "He abandoned the animal and with the mail pouches slung over his shoulder , trotted to the next station , remounted and completed his run with only a small loss of time", "context_len": 32}}}, "freighters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The late Major Chorpenning , remembered as one of the most prominent of freighters across the plains , told me that more than once he had labored through the mountains in the depth of winter when the snow under his feet was sixty feet deep ! He was in Salt Lake City , talking with Brigham Young , when word came that the mail rider westward had been killed by Indians", "context_len": 71}}}, "slayers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Chorpenning found his watch , which strangely enough had not been taken away by his slayers , and within a rod of where he lay were the mail pouches , unharmed", "context_len": 31}}}, "topknot": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "A second glance showed that it was the topknot of a redskin , who dropped down before I could draw bead on him", "context_len": 23}}}, "mountaineer": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "I remember that the famous mountaineer Kit Carson was one of the doubters , but when convinced of what I had done , he declared it the most remarkable ride ever made by any man in crossing the plains", "context_len": 39}}}, "doubters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "I remember that the famous mountaineer Kit Carson was one of the doubters , but when convinced of what I had done , he declared it the most remarkable ride ever made by any man in crossing the plains", "context_len": 39}}}, "fleetest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "With the grim resolve to win or die in the attempt , Aubrey sent half a dozen of his toughest and fleetest ponies ahead , and had them stationed at different points , to be used by him as he came up to where they were waiting", "context_len": 47}}}, "automatically": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "Nature could not be denied , and he must have slept for hours at a time while automatically spurring his animal and holding his seat in the saddle", "context_len": 28}}}, "huzzahs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "Joseph , Missouri , on that afternoon in April , 1860 , when Alexander Carlyle , the first Pony Express rider , dashed out of the stables and galloped full speed down the street to the ferry , amid the huzzahs of the excited multitudes", "context_len": 45}}}, "consumptive": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "He was a consumptive , and had to give up the trying work at the end of two months", "context_len": 19}}}, "darky": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 32, "context": "Payne had a colored youth who had been turned over to him when an infant by his widowed mother , she having consented to become the wife of a big , lazy darky , with no love for other folks \u2019 children", "context_len": 42}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "I noticed that it didn \u2019 t take much pulling to keep that darky out of the muss", "context_len": 18}}}, "impended": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "In the event of Indian attack which impended at almost any hour of the day or night these loopholes were useful to the defenders", "context_len": 24}}}, "snowstorm": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "The assurance that it was much better to set out in the spring than in the autumn had not a feather \u2019 s weight with them : they would have been glad to head westward in the midst of a December snowstorm", "context_len": 42}}}, "ob": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 44, "context": "Marvin ; de way I knowed whar he was , was dat I knowed it ; I felt he had flopped ober to de left , so I knowed jes \u2019 de p \u2019 int to aim at ; dat \u2019 s de skience ob de hull bus \u2019 ness", "context_len": 50}, "NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 38, "context": "Who \u2019 s sleeping ? demanded Jethro , climbing heavily to his feet ; I war jest setting down to tie my shoestring when you come along and stepped on my head so it \u2019 s bent out ob plumb", "context_len": 40}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 27, "context": "Wish I war dat Pony Expressman , he muttered ; he \u2019 s gwine so fast dat de Injins won \u2019 t get de fust glimpse agin ob him", "context_len": 29}}}, "chastising": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Alden made a feint of chastising the African , who caught hold of his flapping hat to keep it on and dived three or four paces away", "context_len": 27}}}, "stomp": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Can \u2019 t get along widout me ; you knock him ober and I \u2019 ll stomp on him and smash", "context_len": 21}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Shagbark tole me how dem critters chaw and stomp and bite a feller ; I knowed dat if I brunged down dis one , it would make de oder two so mad dey \u2019 d come at me afore I could load up ag \u2019 in , and you wouldn \u2019 t hab any Jeth any more", "context_len": 57}}}, "oder": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "Shagbark tole me how dem critters chaw and stomp and bite a feller ; I knowed dat if I brunged down dis one , it would make de oder two so mad dey \u2019 d come at me afore I could load up ag \u2019 in , and you wouldn \u2019 t hab any Jeth any more", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "What \u2019 s to hender me sneaking Jilk out from de oder critters an going like blazes fur de Missouri riber ?", "context_len": 22}}}, "doffing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "And in order the better to carry out his threat , he began fiercely doffing his coat", "context_len": 17}}}, "muss": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 65, "context": "`` Oh ! we are Harry 's soldiers , I mean your soldiers , and Franky is , too ; and we have the nursery chairs for horses , and our dolls for sick soldiers , and we have the pillows for Sepoys , and we poke them ; and nurse do n't like it , 'cause she says we make a yumpus and a muss in the nursery", "context_len": 69}}}, "emphasizing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "That will suit me down to the ground , replied Alden , emphasizing his words also with a gesture of his fist", "context_len": 22}}}, "frow": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "What you all laughing at ? What \u2019 s de matter wid you , Tony Burke ? If yo \u2019 doan \u2019 shet up straight off , I \u2019 ll frow you down so hard you \u2019 ll make a bulge on toder side de yarth", "context_len": 46}}}, "holt": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Fus \u2019 holt ! shouted Jethro , his face lighting up with a grin at the advantage thus gained by his promptness", "context_len": 22}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Every time I git sorter billious and downcast an \u2019 dejec \u2019 ed I look \u2019 round till I find somebody that \u2019 s wuss off than I be , an \u2019 then I take holt an \u2019 try to give them a lift , an \u2019 that cheers me up an \u2019 makes me feel thankful an \u2019 content with my lot", "context_len": 63}}}, "wrestling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "The two loosely gripped hands in front , for be it remembered the method described was the old fashioned way of wrestling , and is still popular in many parts of the country", "context_len": 33}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "The prisoner strode up and down the limited area , wrestling with self , bending the will by prayer to submit to ignominy and pain , for he knew now that his father had abandoned him , and that he had to apprehend the worst ; still he did not regret the choice he had made , and he felt , as he prayed , peace and confidence descend like heavenly dew upon his soul", "context_len": 75}}}, "transcontinental": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "Although there were many incidents worth telling , we must skip them and come to the time when our friends were plodding some distance beyond the straggling town of Cheyenne , which was to attain importance during the building of the transcontinental railway eight or nine years later", "context_len": 48}}}, "snows": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 34, "context": "In doing this he had to pass over the range which he had come round before ; and , as it was winter , his army were , for a time , enveloped in snows and storms among the wild and frightful defiles", "context_len": 43}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "The course most of the time was in sight of the Platte River , which , swollen by the melting snows near the headwaters and the recent rains , was a broad , majestic stream", "context_len": 35}}}, "sandstorm": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "It led through flaming deserts for nearly half the way , where the deadly sandstorm , the torturing thirst and the sleepless enmity of Indians were a constant menace to the traveler", "context_len": 32}}}, "freighting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "The regular size of one of the freighting trains was twenty-five prairie schooners , each with from six to twelve yoke of oxen", "context_len": 23}}}, "schooners": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "In England they have boats with keels , masts , and sails , just like sloops and schooners ; they will sail on the wind , and beat to windward as well as the Perseverance", "context_len": 35}}}, "cody": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Cody , or Buffalo Bill", "context_len": 5}}}, "caravan": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "Every caravan route showed signs of the horrible trade , by the bones of those who had fallen and died from exhaustion , unable to keep their ranks in the gang", "context_len": 31}}}, "percussion": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Their rifles were muzzle-loaders , with percussion caps , but they used the conical bullet , and Alden had learned long before to shoot from the saddle with his horse on a run", "context_len": 33}}}, "bothersome": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Jethro Mix did well while standing , but he insisted that it was too blamed bothersome to hit anything when his horse was trotting or galloping", "context_len": 26}}}, "trapper": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 19, "context": "In two or three years among the Rockies he \u2019 ll make the best kind of a hunter or trapper , instid of wasting his time larning larning which ain \u2019 t no good to nobody", "context_len": 36}}}, "trapping": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "He preferred to fight Indians and cold and hunger for the sake of a few peltries , whose sale brought enough to support him in idleness between trapping seasons", "context_len": 29}}}, "brier": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 86, "context": "It was a fine summer 's evening towards the end of the month of July , and the sun was slowly setting behind the wood-crowned range of hills in the west , where the forest terminated the pastures of Aescendune ; the cattle were returning to their stalls ; the last load of hay was being transferred from the wain to the rick , and all things spoke of the calm and rest of a sweet night , fragrant with the breath of honeysuckle and wild brier , when nature herself seems to court luxurious repose", "context_len": 96}}}, "fleming": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 0, "context": "Fleming and some of his friends took part in the hunts , but they preferred to stay in camp on this day and let the trio prove their prowess", "context_len": 29}}}, "shagbark": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Shagbark crumpled up some dry fragments from a plug of tobacco , in the palm of his hand , punched them into the bowl of his pipe , switched a match along the side of his buckskins , applied the tiny flame , and rode to the head of the company without another word", "context_len": 54}}}, "browse": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "The second night after crossing the Missouri , when the wagons had been placed in a circle , the animals allowed to browse on the luxuriant grass , so well guarded that they could not wander afield , he came back and sat down among the group that were eating from the food spread on a blanket", "context_len": 57}}}, "younker": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 12, "context": "Thar \u2019 s a heap of sense in what ye say , younker ; that would have been the way of it , if the rider hadn \u2019 t dashed into \u2019 em afore he knowed it , and afore they could slip out of his way ; so they tried to shoot him from his saddle ; beats all natur \u2019 what poor shooters most of the varmints are", "context_len": 70}}}, "redskins": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 71, "context": "No fear of that , but he such a big , hulking chap and eats so much that he oughter be made to do something ; I \u2019 m going to put him on tother side of this wagon and make him b \u2019 leve it \u2019 s the most important post of all , and that if he drops asleep , the whole shebang will be wiped out by the redskins", "context_len": 72}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "He reflected that the neighborhood must be a tempting one to redskins or road agents , for the latter class of criminals was one of the pests of overland travel in the early days", "context_len": 34}}}, "intake": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "I wish I could tell , he answered , with a more prodigious intake than before", "context_len": 16}}}, "hab": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 52, "context": "Shagbark tole me how dem critters chaw and stomp and bite a feller ; I knowed dat if I brunged down dis one , it would make de oder two so mad dey \u2019 d come at me afore I could load up ag \u2019 in , and you wouldn \u2019 t hab any Jeth any more", "context_len": 57}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "I hain \u2019 t been hit at all ! he exclaimed with a new thrill this time of unalloyed bliss ; it must hab been de rheumatics dat shifted to my toe", "context_len": 32}}}, "lush": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "The oxen and horses cropped the lush grass which grew exuberantly , and their masters spent most of the time in the big covered wagons , where they were protected from the chilling storm", "context_len": 34}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "Breakfast was also in course of preparation , and men were corraling the animals that were cropping the lush grass and holding back from harness and yoke", "context_len": 27}}}, "exuberantly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "The oxen and horses cropped the lush grass which grew exuberantly , and their masters spent most of the time in the big covered wagons , where they were protected from the chilling storm", "context_len": 34}}}, "crispness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "The air was bright and clear , with just enough crispness to make walking or riding pleasant", "context_len": 17}}}, "starlike": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Once these starlike points glimmered to the south , once to the northwest and twice to the north , on the other side of the Platte", "context_len": 26}}}, "glimmered": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Once these starlike points glimmered to the south , once to the northwest and twice to the north , on the other side of the Platte", "context_len": 26}}}, "lumbered": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "When it is stated that more than 40,000 persons crossed the plains in 1849 , and that later 500 wagons were counted in one day as they lumbered past Fort Kearny , the wonder is that more trains did not meet and mingle", "context_len": 43}}}, "plainsmen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "The trousers were thrust into the tops of his boots , whose heels were high and pointed , and after the fashion among cowmen and plainsmen", "context_len": 26}}}, "lightens": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "He had none , but a few of the riders carry them ; this one doesn \u2019 t think he is likely to need any , and so he lightens the load of his horse that much", "context_len": 37}}}, "brierwood": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "His brierwood was between his lips , and from the thicket of whisker and mustache the blue vapor issued as if from the funnel of a small steamcraft", "context_len": 28}}}, "umbrageous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Wal , if he ain \u2019 t the mos \u2019 umbrageous rapscallion dat eber trod on two legs , growled Jethro as his friend passed him , hurrying to answer Shagbark \u2019 s call", "context_len": 34}}}, "rapscallion": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Wal , if he ain \u2019 t the mos \u2019 umbrageous rapscallion dat eber trod on two legs , growled Jethro as his friend passed him , hurrying to answer Shagbark \u2019 s call", "context_len": 34}}}, "tickler": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Have ye obsarved anything \u2019 tickler ?", "context_len": 7}}}, "binocular": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 10, "context": "It seems to me , replied Alden , lowering the binocular , that I can see a faint , bluish shadowy outline of something in the horizon", "context_len": 27}}}, "eyesight": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` Come , confess , Aunt Patty , '' he said ; `` acknowledge that it is to you this good fellow who has just left us owes his freedom from debt , his child 's eyesight , his release from cares which were almost too much even for his hopeful spirit ; acknowledge that you have generously sacrificed a long-cherished desire , given up the fruits of much saving and self-denial , to make those happy in whom you could have had no interest save as creatures and children of one common Father", "context_len": 94}}}, "grasshopper": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 69, "context": "No , he won \u2019 t ; he ain \u2019 t such a fool ; he \u2019 ll make a big sneak to the left and get past \u2019 em ; if it was among the mountains , he wouldn \u2019 t have half the chance , but he knows what to do and he \u2019 ll do it , as sure as ye are knee high to a grasshopper", "context_len": 70}}}, "sagacity": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 11, "context": "The truth was , that Alexander , though he had the sagacity to see that he was placed in circumstances where prodigious moral effect in strengthening his position would be produced by an act of great severity , was swayed by so many generous impulses , which raised him above the ordinary excitements of irritation and revenge , that he had every desire to make the suffering as light , and to limit it by as narrow bounds , as the nature of the case would allow", "context_len": 87}}}, "ness": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 49, "context": "Marvin ; de way I knowed whar he was , was dat I knowed it ; I felt he had flopped ober to de left , so I knowed jes \u2019 de p \u2019 int to aim at ; dat \u2019 s de skience ob de hull bus \u2019 ness", "context_len": 50}}}, "sable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "Alden , therefore , did not scruple to linger for a few minutes with his sable friend", "context_len": 17}}}, "rangers": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "So thither flocked the young and the old : the wood rangers and hunters from the forests of Newenham , where Herstan had right of wood cutting ; the men who wove baskets and hurdles of osier work from the river banks ; the theows who cultivated the home farm ; the ceorls who rented a hide of land here and a hide there -- all , the grandfather and the grandson , accepted the invitation to feast", "context_len": 78}}}, "varmints": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 68, "context": "Thar \u2019 s a heap of sense in what ye say , younker ; that would have been the way of it , if the rider hadn \u2019 t dashed into \u2019 em afore he knowed it , and afore they could slip out of his way ; so they tried to shoot him from his saddle ; beats all natur \u2019 what poor shooters most of the varmints are", "context_len": 70}}}, "roiled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "While the water was roiled and not specially attractive in appearance , no one felt any objection to it", "context_len": 19}}}, "spices": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "At the foot of the throne was the coffin , made , it is said , of solid gold , and containing , besides the body , a large quantity of the most costly spices and aromatic perfumes , which filled the air with their odor", "context_len": 46}}}, "insidious": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Since the sentinels could not pace to and fro , as is the usual custom , they were at much disadvantage in that respect , for we all know how insidious sleep is and how in many circumstances it is impossible to fight it off", "context_len": 45}}}, "peepers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Ye can steal back and forth on the inside of the circle of wagons , fur that will help keep yer peepers peeled , but ba \u2019 rs and beavers ! no man \u2019 s wuth shucks if he can \u2019 t stay awake till midnight , and them as comes on duty then will have had \u2019 nough sleep to last \u2019 em till daylight", "context_len": 66}}}, "ba": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Ye can steal back and forth on the inside of the circle of wagons , fur that will help keep yer peepers peeled , but ba \u2019 rs and beavers ! no man \u2019 s wuth shucks if he can \u2019 t stay awake till midnight , and them as comes on duty then will have had \u2019 nough sleep to last \u2019 em till daylight", "context_len": 66}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` 'Ba ba Lammie-noo Cuddle doon tae mammie", "context_len": 8}}}, "rs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Ye can steal back and forth on the inside of the circle of wagons , fur that will help keep yer peepers peeled , but ba \u2019 rs and beavers ! no man \u2019 s wuth shucks if he can \u2019 t stay awake till midnight , and them as comes on duty then will have had \u2019 nough sleep to last \u2019 em till daylight", "context_len": 66}}}, "beavers": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "Ye can steal back and forth on the inside of the circle of wagons , fur that will help keep yer peepers peeled , but ba \u2019 rs and beavers ! no man \u2019 s wuth shucks if he can \u2019 t stay awake till midnight , and them as comes on duty then will have had \u2019 nough sleep to last \u2019 em till daylight", "context_len": 66}}}, "soulful": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "This was a compliment for me , so I licked a bit of this nice man 's shoulder in a caressing way , and he turned and gazed deep into what he called my `` soft and soulful '' eyes", "context_len": 40}}}, "tother": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "No fear of that , but he such a big , hulking chap and eats so much that he oughter be made to do something ; I \u2019 m going to put him on tother side of this wagon and make him b \u2019 leve it \u2019 s the most important post of all , and that if he drops asleep , the whole shebang will be wiped out by the redskins", "context_len": 72}}}, "ticklish": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Ye know how ticklish things is to-night ; we must all take turns in watching fur the redskins that will be sure to try to steal in among the wagons and skulp us all ; ye are to stand guard till the middle of night , when some one else will take yer place , that is if ye live to give \u2019 em a chance", "context_len": 66}}}, "innards": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Shagbark , I doan \u2019 feel very well dis ebening : I hain \u2019 t felt well all day , sorter ob a big pain in my innards", "context_len": 28}}}, "wal": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "And nobody asks you to do any better ; wal , I reckon I \u2019 ve said \u2019 nough", "context_len": 19}}}, "distracts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "It distracts our attention from our work ; better give your whole mind to the business we have in hand ; if you see anything that doesn \u2019 t look right , let me know", "context_len": 35}}}, "poses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "This posture was so agreeable that he was sure he could hold it for several hours without fatigue ; but it is such poses that irks one the soonest", "context_len": 29}}}, "irks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "This posture was so agreeable that he was sure he could hold it for several hours without fatigue ; but it is such poses that irks one the soonest", "context_len": 29}}}, "lambast": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "If any ob de Injins try to sneak up , Al will be sure to see \u2019 em ; he \u2019 ll let me know in time to whirl \u2019 bout and lambast \u2019 em or git out ob de way myself", "context_len": 42}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "So I luft ; dem antelopes am wery rewengeful wery rewengeful and I \u2019 se too smart to gib \u2019 em de chance dey wanted to lambast me", "context_len": 28}}}, "pivoted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "When Jethro pivoted to the front for the third time , he held the position longer than usual", "context_len": 18}}}, "attenuated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "The orb was forever groping among these feathery masses , some of which were attenuated while others had enough body to eclipse the orb for a few minutes", "context_len": 28}}}, "atween": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "It \u2019 s easy to tell the difference atween the barking of a revolver and a big gun ; there warn \u2019 t any pistol used", "context_len": 26}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Ye \u2019 re right ; I wonder how many hundred of \u2019 em there is atween St", "context_len": 17}}}, "hornet": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "He run right into the hornet \u2019 s nest afore he seed it , and the varmints opened on him ; he must have throwed himself forrard on his hoss and the animal scratched gravel as them ponies know how to do", "context_len": 42}}}, "warns": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Because it warns us of our danger", "context_len": 7}}}, "shooters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 64, "context": "Thar \u2019 s a heap of sense in what ye say , younker ; that would have been the way of it , if the rider hadn \u2019 t dashed into \u2019 em afore he knowed it , and afore they could slip out of his way ; so they tried to shoot him from his saddle ; beats all natur \u2019 what poor shooters most of the varmints are", "context_len": 70}}}, "critters": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 5, "context": "Shagbark tole me how dem critters chaw and stomp and bite a feller ; I knowed dat if I brunged down dis one , it would make de oder two so mad dey \u2019 d come at me afore I could load up ag \u2019 in , and you wouldn \u2019 t hab any Jeth any more", "context_len": 57}}}, "disquieting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "He had not long to wait when he made a disquieting discovery", "context_len": 12}}}, "cuds": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 59, "context": "That had a good deal to do with it , but some of the hosses kept gitting on their feet and I had to watch \u2019 em close and make \u2019 em lay down agin ; there warn \u2019 t any such trouble with the oxen , fur they was glad \u2019 nough to lay down and chaw their cuds", "context_len": 60}}}, "coursed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 51, "context": "The limbs of the great tree swayed gently in the westerly breeze , and the moonbeams came slanting through them most delightfully , as the boys lay listening to the moan of the night wind , the sound of the surf along the shore , and watched the clouds as they coursed by the moon , all heightened by the novelty of their situation", "context_len": 64}}}, "tantalized": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 46, "context": "How his quick wit saved him ; the terrible dangers to which his indomitable spirit refused to yield , and which he finally surmounted ; how he found Sobrinini at last on her island of snakes and brought her back with him , only to be tantalized with imperfect revelations that made it still necessary to hunt out Japazy on Jaguar Island is told in the preceding volume of this series , entitled , Bomba , the Jungle Boy , at the Giant Cataract ; or , Chief Nascanora and His Captives", "context_len": 92}}}, "descry": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "In the flickering illumination , he could not descry anything out of the usual order of things", "context_len": 17}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Why might not Bomba lurk about the river bank to the north and descry Japazy \u2019 s coming ? Then he could boldly make himself known and get the interview that he had come so far to seek", "context_len": 38}}}, "flirted": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "And how the pony did go ! With a snort he flirted his head , as if he would shake off everything that held him back as easily as he blew aside the fleck of foam that alighted like a snowflake on the knee of his rider", "context_len": 47}}}, "shoestring": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Who \u2019 s sleeping ? demanded Jethro , climbing heavily to his feet ; I war jest setting down to tie my shoestring when you come along and stepped on my head so it \u2019 s bent out ob plumb", "context_len": 40}}}, "formulated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "He felt pleased and encouraged , for the superintendent 's cast-iron visage had softened considerably , and he manifested unmistakable interest as he reached out and took up and inspected the neatly formulated memoranda on the packing-box desk", "context_len": 38}}}, "fewness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The fewness in number of vehicles compelled their wide separation , for the circle was large", "context_len": 16}}}, "unexplainable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Through an unexplainable instinct he knew where some of the women and children were sleeping , and he intended to bound in among them , strike right and left with venomous fierceness , slaying the sleepers with lightning-like quickness , and then dart away in the moonlight", "context_len": 47}}}, "axletree": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "What does it mean , Alden ? she asked , failing to see the feet of the redskin who lay under the axletree", "context_len": 23}}}, "screening": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Screening himself in the shadow cast by the wagon , he proceeded to ram a charge down the barrel , while the guide did as he said he would do", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Bradford helped his little daughters through the screening bushes , and then drew back a few steps where he might still watch them , and hear all that passed , but where Dolly could not see him", "context_len": 37}}}, "hummer": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "I tell ye that younker is a hummer ; not yerself nor any man in the crowd is better than him at a time like this ; if his father and mother don \u2019 t want him I \u2019 m going to adopt him", "context_len": 44}}}, "maneuvered": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "Accordingly , as the army advanced to the banks , they maneuvered to form in order of battle , and prepared to continue their march as if there were no obstacle to oppose them", "context_len": 34}}}, "knifed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "Which is jes \u2019 what he wouldn \u2019 t have done ; he would have sneaked from the front , and whisked out of sight afore anyone could know what he \u2019 d done ; and , grimly added the guide , more \u2019 n likely he \u2019 d knifed one or two others on his way to the perarie agin", "context_len": 61}}}, "checkmate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "Assured as he was that the Indians of whom he had caught glimpses during the afternoon , and the reports of whose guns he had heard not far away , were drawing near the camp with the most sinister designs , he set out to checkmate them so far as it was possible", "context_len": 53}}}, "diagonal": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "The three animals in their panic came not straight toward the couple , but took a diagonal course which promised to bring them within easy range", "context_len": 26}}}, "tailboard": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Leaning his rifle against the tailboard , the hunter stooped , seized each ankle , and raising his hands so that they were at his own hips , and with a moccasin on either side , he ran fifty yards out on the plains", "context_len": 44}}}, "moccasin": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "Leaning his rifle against the tailboard , the hunter stooped , seized each ankle , and raising his hands so that they were at his own hips , and with a moccasin on either side , he ran fifty yards out on the plains", "context_len": 44}}}, "sunup": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "No , you won \u2019 t ; it \u2019 ll be gone afore sunup", "context_len": 14}}}, "nape": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "It looked to Alden Payne as if Jethro was eight feet long , when the guide was dragging him out of the front of the wagon by the nape of the neck", "context_len": 32}}}, "gushed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "From a score of places , representing so many different points of the compass , rifles gushed fire , the sharp cracks of the weapons rang out in the stillness , and the thud and pinging of rifle balls were everywhere", "context_len": 41}}}, "pinging": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "From a score of places , representing so many different points of the compass , rifles gushed fire , the sharp cracks of the weapons rang out in the stillness , and the thud and pinging of rifle balls were everywhere", "context_len": 41}}}, "abraded": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "Abner Fleming felt the zip of a bullet which grazed his forehead , and one of his neighbors on the other side of the circle was scraped by a pellet of lead which abraded an arm", "context_len": 36}}}, "startlingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Inevitably a painted redskin lying low in the grass took a shot at the dusky form and came startlingly near hitting him", "context_len": 22}}}, "rapscallions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "It \u2019 s dem dat will flop over like a buckwheat cake de minute dey fires , reflected Jethro , but how de mischief ken I know which way de rapscallions will turn ? Mos \u2019 folks am right-handed , and I guess dat \u2019 s de way this sarpint will flop", "context_len": 52}}}, "blindest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "It was the blindest kind of theorizing , but strange as it may seem , Jethro Mix was absolutely right in his conclusion", "context_len": 23}}}, "theorizing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "It was the blindest kind of theorizing , but strange as it may seem , Jethro Mix was absolutely right in his conclusion", "context_len": 23}}}, "guesswork": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 29, "context": "I \u2019 m glad to have so clear an explanation , gravely replied the gentleman ; but it seems to me there must have been a good deal of guesswork , for there was no way by which you could know of a certainty which way the right or the left he had moved", "context_len": 54}}}, "whisking": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "Bunny , either more hungry or less cautious than her mate and children , came whisking down the trunk of the walnut-tree , and in another moment was seated upon Bob 's shoulder , holding in her fore-paws the almond he had given her , and opening it with her sharp , pointed teeth", "context_len": 54}}}, "keyed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "He was whisking from point to point , keeping the men keyed up and instructing them what to do", "context_len": 19}}}, "suspending": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "There is something sublime in the idea of such a pause , made by the Macedonian phalanx and its wings , on the slopes of the hills , suspending its attack upon ten times its number , to give the mighty mass of their enemies the chances of a fair and equal contest", "context_len": 53}}}, "reckons": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "So I aimed at de left side from here , and I reckons I got him", "context_len": 16}}}, "irony": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The grim irony of the situation was increased by what followed", "context_len": 11}}}, "rekindled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "The women busied themselves in preparing the morning meal , a fire having been rekindled , and all ate with appetites such as come only to those who live in the open", "context_len": 32}}}, "chaw": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 57, "context": "That had a good deal to do with it , but some of the hosses kept gitting on their feet and I had to watch \u2019 em close and make \u2019 em lay down agin ; there warn \u2019 t any such trouble with the oxen , fur they was glad \u2019 nough to lay down and chaw their cuds", "context_len": 60}}}, "garrisoned": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "The oldest white settlement in the latter section was Fort Laramie since abandoned which was made a trading post in 1834 , rebuilt by the American Fur Company two years later , and sold to the United States and garrisoned as a fort in 1849", "context_len": 45}}}, "meditate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "The redmen more than once rode up within two or three hundred yards and seemed to meditate a closer approach , as if for barter", "context_len": 25}}}, "salutations": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "A wave of the hand from the flying horseman , without the slightest pull of rein , was the only acknowledgment made to the salutations of the emigrants", "context_len": 28}}}, "squalls": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "He met with no squalls this autumn , for when the weather looked at all unsettled he could work with Ben in the woods , and fall down the large pines for him to hew , which he dearly loved to do ; and , as it took a long time to hew out a large stick of timber , he had ample time to cut them down and trim them out", "context_len": 72}}}, "tain": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "Dat ain \u2019 t nuffin , he said airily ; de sarpint furgot dat I was on de lookout fur him and as soon as he fired and missed , why , I plugged him ; \u2019 tain \u2019 t wuth speaking \u2019 bout", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Mebbe \u2019 tain \u2019 t the same dorg , but he answers the deescription the Widder Jones give ; and it \u2019 s mighty queer a dorg should be hookin \u2019 it round here , where there ain \u2019 t no houses nigher than a quarter of a mile", "context_len": 49}}}, "fording": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "For a long distance Fenn tramped on , plowing through the woods , making turns now and then to avoid streams , partly frozen over , leaping them when he could , fording them at other times , for he had on high , water-proof hunting boots , but keeping as nearly as he could in the proper course", "context_len": 59}}}, "reinforce": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "By that time the air had become so chilly that the blaze was carefully nursed in order to reinforce the blankets", "context_len": 21}}}, "equestrian": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Two equestrian statues could not have been more motionless than they", "context_len": 11}}}, "lolling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "The man in the saddle looked down with renewed interest upon the burly African , who had set the stock of his rifle on the ground , folded his arms over the muzzle , and assumed a lolling attitude , as if the matter was of no concern to any of them", "context_len": 52}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "His piggish eyes were fixed upon the brave lad who saw the horrid front , the great red jaws parted , the slobbering tongue lolling out and the teeth showing", "context_len": 30}}}, "airily": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Dat ain \u2019 t nuffin , he said airily ; de sarpint furgot dat I was on de lookout fur him and as soon as he fired and missed , why , I plugged him ; \u2019 tain \u2019 t wuth speaking \u2019 bout", "context_len": 44}}}, "blubber": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Dunno \u2019 bout dat , said the African with dignity ; de wimmin folks and de children will blubber so hard when dey find I think ob leabin \u2019 dem dat Shagbark won \u2019 t be likely to allow it ; howsumeber , I \u2019 ll think it ober", "context_len": 49}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "The fat or blubber of a seal lies in one sheet over the meat , about two inches in thickness , and not at all mixed with it , as is the case with other animals", "context_len": 36}}}, "compressing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "If the chance ever does come , he \u2019 ll find me ready , said Alden , compressing his lips , for the memory of the insult rankled", "context_len": 28}}}, "rankled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "If the chance ever does come , he \u2019 ll find me ready , said Alden , compressing his lips , for the memory of the insult rankled", "context_len": 28}}}, "bison": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Although the American bison or buffalo has been virtually extinct for years , the animals were numbered by the hundred thousand on the western plains at the time we have in mind", "context_len": 32}}}, "herbage": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "The droves seen in the distance seemed often to cover a fourth of the horizon , and their dark , shaggy backs as they cropped the herbage and hitched continually forward , were like the fretted waves of the sea", "context_len": 40}}}, "jethro": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Jethro showed his natural timidity , and kept as close as he could to the veteran , while Alden indulged in spurts of his own which more than once brought results", "context_len": 31}}}, "meandered": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 47, "context": "Thrace was one great fertile basin or valley , sloping toward the center in every direction , so that all the streams from the mountains , increased by the rains which fell over the whole surface of the ground , flowed together into one river , which meandered through the center of the valley , and flowed out at last into the \u00c6gean Sea", "context_len": 64}}}, "pronghorn": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The American antelope or pronghorn is a native of the plains near the Rocky Mountains", "context_len": 15}}}, "buttocks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Nearly always the upper parts of its body are yellowish brown in color , while the under parts , the sides and the head and throat and the buttocks are white", "context_len": 31}}}, "theoretically": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Shagbark had described so often the method employed in hunting the antelope that the youths understood it theoretically", "context_len": 18}}}, "hypnotized": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "One or more of the animals would begin a timid , hesitating approach , frequently bounding or circling away for some distance , halting and advancing again , hypnotized by the singular sight whose nature they could not fathom without a closer view", "context_len": 43}}}, "waggery": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Shagbark ? asked Jethro , with so much misgiving as to rouse the waggery of the trapper", "context_len": 17}}}, "bagged": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "They bagged some small game , and Bart made a remarkable kill of a brace of partridges , getting one each with his left and right barrels , when it seemed that both birds would escape", "context_len": 36}}}, "chile": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "As fur dis chile , chattered Jethro , with his thick blanket wrapped about him to his ears ; I shan \u2019 t get warm fur sebenteen yeahs", "context_len": 28}}}, "gib": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "So I luft ; dem antelopes am wery rewengeful wery rewengeful and I \u2019 se too smart to gib \u2019 em de chance dey wanted to lambast me", "context_len": 28}}}, "blamable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Possibly he felt that he was blamable in the matter , for it had been his awful words that caused the senseless panic of the colored youth , and made him flee from before a harmless antelope , when the lad had a loaded rifle in his hands and knew how to use it", "context_len": 54}}}, "eddied": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The millions of big flakes , eddied and spun around and so filled the air that they could not see one another , when they were barely ten feet apart", "context_len": 30}}}, "yokes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "It was about two o \u2019 clock when the yokes were adjusted to the necks of the oxen , the horsemen swung into their saddles , and the cavalcade headed for Fort Laramie , on the other side of the mountain spur which bears the latter half of that name", "context_len": 50}}}, "combative": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Alden at last had overtaken the other train , and would meet the combative youth for whom he had looked in vain throughout the past weeks", "context_len": 26}}}, "evened": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Like many a mistaken youth , Alden was sure he could not be happy until he had evened up matters between them", "context_len": 22}}}, "roils": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "What roils me , added Alden to himself , with a flash of the eye and a compression of the lips , is that he must have taken my politeness last night for fear of him", "context_len": 36}}}, "guffawed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The veteran guffawed", "context_len": 3}}}, "affray": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "The latter was on the point of describing that affray on the streets of St", "context_len": 15}}}, "discourage": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 40, "context": "Perhaps it was pain and sickness ; perhaps the loss of friends ; perhaps some old , troublesome sin , sorely repented of , long struggled with , but which still returned again and again , to weary and almost discourage her as she toiled along in the road which led to the Father 's house", "context_len": 56}}}, "probabilities": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "He might discourage the resolution of Alden , though the probabilities were the other way", "context_len": 15}}}, "briarwood": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "There could be no mistaking the target , for the bowl of his briarwood pipe was shattered and sent flying into space , leaving only a stump of the stem between his lips", "context_len": 33}}}, "continental": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The present state of Colorado is traversed by the main axis or continental divide of the Rocky Mountains which there finds its greatest northern development", "context_len": 25}}}, "rainfall": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "Another part of the rainfall or melted snow winds its way ultimately to the Colorado and into the Gulf of California , while a third gropes to the Missouri and finally into the Gulf of Mexico", "context_len": 36}}}, "gropes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Another part of the rainfall or melted snow winds its way ultimately to the Colorado and into the Gulf of California , while a third gropes to the Missouri and finally into the Gulf of Mexico", "context_len": 36}}}, "deceptive": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "One of the most deceptive things is distance on the plains and among the mountains", "context_len": 15}}}, "chadwick": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 0, "context": "Chadwick", "context_len": 1}}}, "cinched": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Since it was not so to be , the two after the evening meal , cinched their saddle girths and rode out on the plain", "context_len": 25}}}, "girths": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Since it was not so to be , the two after the evening meal , cinched their saddle girths and rode out on the plain", "context_len": 25}}}, "prowlers": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "Bomba thanked his stars that it was daylight and that most of the nocturnal prowlers had retired to their dens where they would sleep the day away in preparation for the next foray", "context_len": 33}}}, "puma": {"freq": 64, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 64, "word_index": 27, "context": "But would they be glad ? Or would they , too , be ashamed of Bomba , the boy of the jungle , the boy of the puma skin , the boy who tore his food with his fingers when he ate , the boy who had shyly watched Gillis and Dorn at their meals and wished that he could eat as they did", "context_len": 64}}}, "wolverine": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "In the course of these hunts , the youths secured between them specimens of the coyote , puma , wild cat , wolverine and in one instance a black bear", "context_len": 30}}}, "coursers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Twice these coursers of the plains passed so near the camp that they exchanged greetings with the emigrants but neither did more than rein his pony down to a walk", "context_len": 30}}}, "scooting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "I think you \u2019 ll do the scooting before you bring down a grizzly , but Shagbark told us that we are not in a section where we are likely to meet any of those animals", "context_len": 36}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "If you want harmony on the team and that \u2019 s rather important just send him scooting", "context_len": 17}}}, "habitats": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Sometimes too wild animals leave their habitats and wander far afield", "context_len": 11}}}, "estray": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Hardly , unless he is an estray from some menagerie , and there isn \u2019 t any temptation for menageries to visit unsettled countries , said the amused Alden", "context_len": 29}}}, "menageries": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Hardly , unless he is an estray from some menagerie , and there isn \u2019 t any temptation for menageries to visit unsettled countries , said the amused Alden", "context_len": 29}}}, "ravines": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "The slope was steadily upward for a fourth of a mile , when it reached a nearly level plateau , and wound in and out among rocks , stunted pines , gnarled cedars , and ravines , interspersed with valleys and comparatively smooth stretches , with now and then a mountain torrent across which the travelers made their way with difficulty", "context_len": 61}}}, "scrimmages": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "You are approaching a dangerous region ; I have had two scrimmages with Indians within the last month", "context_len": 18}}}, "plateaus": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "It stirs one \u2019 s blood to dash over the plains , through the mountain files and across plateaus at headlong speed ; we have to make an average of over twelve miles an hour", "context_len": 35}}}, "miscreants": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "There were scores of places among the towering rocks and piles of stone that would hide a host of miscreants from sight", "context_len": 22}}}, "wantonness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "Young , vigorous , full of bounding life and hope , his cheering words lingered yet in the ears of the couple , and here he lay on the ground his life driven out by the arrow launched by a demon in wantonness , for Dick Lightfoot had never harmed a hair in the head of one of his kind", "context_len": 60}}}, "hurtle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Jethro was almost speechless , for he expected other deadly missiles to hurtle through the air at him and his companion", "context_len": 21}}}, "sagacious": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 1, "context": "How sagacious a line of conduct was this ! Most ardent and enthusiastic young men , in the circumstances in which he was placed , would have been elated and vain at their elevation , and would have replaced the old and well-tried servants of the father with personal favorites of their own age , inexperienced and incompetent , and as conceited as themselves", "context_len": 64}}}, "fleeter": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Firebug was naturally fleeter than Jilk , and having no burden to carry , easily held his place some yards in front", "context_len": 22}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "He was fleet , but the puma was fleeter", "context_len": 9}}}, "cl": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "It didn \u2019 t go cl \u2019 ar frough , but it \u2019 s jest as bad ; I can \u2019 t lib more dan a few minutes ; go it , Jilk !", "context_len": 34}}}, "lib": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "It didn \u2019 t go cl \u2019 ar frough , but it \u2019 s jest as bad ; I can \u2019 t lib more dan a few minutes ; go it , Jilk !", "context_len": 34}}}, "clumping": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Once again the tremulous whoops sounded above the clumping of the ponies \u2019 hoofs , but they came this time from the rear", "context_len": 23}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "At that moment above the jangling , clumping activity of the yards there arose on the night air one frightful , piercing shriek", "context_len": 23}}}, "crises": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "It is in the momentous crises of a person \u2019 s career that he often becomes sensible to trifles which would pass unnoticed at other times", "context_len": 26}}}, "birdshot": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "The rain might descend in torrents , hail , snow and sleet might batter horse and rider like fine birdshot , and the temperature might drop below zero , or throb with heat , still rider and horse who were like one creature must plunge on and ever on , so long as muscle and nerve could stand the terrific strain", "context_len": 61}}}, "timers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "How those old timers could ride and if necessary fight ! I add the following extract from an interesting letter received by me from Mr", "context_len": 25}}}, "shotgun": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "To his horror he found that they would not fit ! He had brought out his smaller gauge shotgun , and the cartridges in it were the only ones available", "context_len": 30}}}, "flinty": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "In the mean time , if there was any danger of thoughtlessness , it was removed by the sight of that feathered shaft protruding from the back of the poor fellow who lay on the flinty earth", "context_len": 37}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "At such times , when the surface was flinty or pebbly , he not only used the sense of feeling , but lighted a match", "context_len": 25}}}, "deadliness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "The reasonable supposition was that the redskin who had discharged the missile was in a position to drive others with equal deadliness and that he would do so", "context_len": 28}}}, "deerskin": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "He had the same squat , sturdy figure , the coarse black hair dangling about his bare shoulders , and growing low upon his forehead , the naked chest , the frowsy hunting shirt of deerskin , with leggings and dilapidated moccasins", "context_len": 42}}}, "forearm": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "In his eagerness to prove to the old crone that he was human and no ghostly visitor , Bomba drew forth his machete and thrust the sharp point of it into his brown , sinewy forearm", "context_len": 36}}}, "archer": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "The exclamation was caused by the discovery that after firing at the dusky archer in the ravine , he had neglected to reload his rifle , a piece of forgetfulness for which Shagbark never would have forgiven him", "context_len": 38}}}, "springboard": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "He leaped twenty feet , as if he had been hurled from a springboard and away he sped", "context_len": 18}}}, "outran": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "A grim fancy came to the youth that if his enemy had done so , the pony outran the missile", "context_len": 20}}}, "debouched": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "Dick the pony held his swift gallop for a half mile farther , when he debouched into an open country , similar in many respects to that which he had left behind him", "context_len": 33}}}, "arroyos": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "The plateau must not be looked upon as a smooth plain , for here and there it took a rolling form with arroyos and occasionally boulders that had to be skirted , but , compared with most of the country to the rear , it was an ideal course for a horse and his rider", "context_len": 55}}}, "snowflake": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "And how the pony did go ! With a snort he flirted his head , as if he would shake off everything that held him back as easily as he blew aside the fleck of foam that alighted like a snowflake on the knee of his rider", "context_len": 47}}}, "seizure": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "It was through such seizure of chances that the wonderful system of the Pony Express Riders amazed the country throughout the months the service lasted , until the telegraph and afterward the railway put it out of business", "context_len": 38}}}, "innovation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "It would seem that they would forbid the innovation , when all the circumstances are remembered , but that remained to be seen", "context_len": 23}}}, "exhaust": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "He at first tried to bluster , then to temporize , but the lawyer was stern and threatened to exhaust the resources of the law in behalf of his clients", "context_len": 30}}}, "brakes": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "So much the better ; for if we can hardly escape such impediments , I do not think we need fear that the Danes will find their way through the swamps and brakes", "context_len": 33}}}, "dwarfed": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "While scrutinizing the ridge which showed a considerable growth of dwarfed pine , Alden was startled to observe a thin column of smoke issuing from a point on the crest", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 44, "context": "He strolled about the open space , pausing now and then to survey through his glasses the snowy peaks which towered far into the sky to the westward , or at the lower hills to the north , where the gnarled pine , the dwarfed cedar , the rushing torrents and the gorges made up one of the many wild regions which abound over thousands of square miles of area", "context_len": 70}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "As he looked toward the island there came a stupendous crash that dwarfed everything that had preceded", "context_len": 17}}}, "compress": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` Each had a dream of some day cutting a rich vein or finding a nugget of extraordinary size which should compress into one day the profits of a year or two of ordinary success", "context_len": 35}}}, "molars": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "He had just inserted the metal between his molars in the despairing effort to reduce the size of the same , when his hair almost lifted the hat from his head", "context_len": 31}}}, "waddling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "His waddling , lumbering gait , and his vast bulk left no doubt of his identity", "context_len": 16}}}, "footed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Alden \u2019 s belief was that the monstrous animal would keep on with his ponderous gait in the grotesque attempt to overtake the fleet footed horse which was safe beyond his reach ; but to the consternation of the watcher the brute halted at the very spot where the fugitive had landed when he dropped from the saddle", "context_len": 58}}}, "snuffed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "In the gloom , they may have supposed he was lying low on his horse \u2019 s back , but they fired together and snuffed out the steed supposed to be carrying him", "context_len": 33}}}, "snout": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "The bear suddenly raised his head and dipped his snout forward in several directions one after the other , snuffing as if he knew something was in the wind", "context_len": 29}}}, "intentness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Afraid even to peer around the edge of his hiding place , lest he should betray himself , he drew back , grasped his revolver and held his breath as he listened with all the intentness at his command", "context_len": 39}}}, "whiffing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "Could he believe his eyes ? The bear was not ten paces distant and swinging straight toward him ! The fact that just then he emitted another of his whiffing grunts made it appear that he had seen the youngster and was congratulating himself upon the certainty of a dainty tidbit for supper", "context_len": 53}}}, "tidbit": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "Could he believe his eyes ? The bear was not ten paces distant and swinging straight toward him ! The fact that just then he emitted another of his whiffing grunts made it appear that he had seen the youngster and was congratulating himself upon the certainty of a dainty tidbit for supper", "context_len": 53}}}, "piggish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "His piggish eyes were fixed upon the brave lad who saw the horrid front , the great red jaws parted , the slobbering tongue lolling out and the teeth showing", "context_len": 30}}}, "slobbering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "His piggish eyes were fixed upon the brave lad who saw the horrid front , the great red jaws parted , the slobbering tongue lolling out and the teeth showing", "context_len": 30}}}, "unshakable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "He had discovered his prey and was going for it with unshakable resolution", "context_len": 13}}}, "cones": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Look , here 's a piece of rubber boot , part of a shoe , some pine cones , some sticks of wood , stones , part of a rope , some brass cartridges and some flannel bandages", "context_len": 38}}}, "recharge": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "And he proceeded to recharge his own weapon", "context_len": 8}}}, "flanks": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 19, "context": "The messenger leaped like an acrobat into place , caught up the reins , touched with his spurs the flanks of the animal , which instantly responded with a bound , and was off on a headlong run", "context_len": 38}}}, "cramps": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "The animal must have felt much as did the trained dog , who , having pointed a bird , was picked up by his new and sympathetic master and carried off the field , under the belief that he had been suddenly taken with cramps", "context_len": 45}}}, "serenity": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "It did not add to Alden \u2019 s serenity of mind to notice that the course was gradually shifting to the left , and finally led directly toward the brush of smoke which still stained the blue tinted sky", "context_len": 39}}}, "telegraphic": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "It is on record that on one occasion the news of the signing of an important treaty at Washington affecting the Sioux was known to that tribe before the telegraphic messages could reach the army officers at the reservation", "context_len": 39}}}, "daubs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "It may be doubted whether the vari-colored daubs would have added to the hideousness of that face , which was broad with protuberant cheek bones , an immense mouth , low forehead and piercing black eyes", "context_len": 36}}}, "outrun": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "He was in pursuit , and since no man ever lived who could outrun a good horse , little chance was left for the fugitive", "context_len": 25}}}, "fiddled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "The watchful Alden suddenly saw the right hand of the fugitive dart over to the left shoulder , where the fingers fiddled for a moment", "context_len": 25}}}, "jocularity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "It was a strange piece of jocularity , but the genial hearted youth doubted whether it would be appreciated", "context_len": 19}}}, "forbearance": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` For our Maggie and Bessie , the dear little workers in the garden of the Lord , who tended the Christian plants of patience , kindness , and forbearance , till their lovely blossoms overran the evil weeds of malice and ill-will , and sowed the seeds of that which brought forth fruit for the glory of God", "context_len": 59}}}, "squinted": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "The little man squinted at the tips of the trees waving their skeleton branches in the crisp air , then brought a calm gaze to the excited old face : `` Not exactly ; but I do say when you make a boy like Ben turn from the path he has marked out for himself , all the education that culture would crowd on him is just so much to break down the boy", "context_len": 74}}}, "eaglet": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "No matter how well mounted a party of Indians might be , none could overhaul the peerless Dick , whose graceful legs were again doubling under him with marvelous rapidity and carrying him and his burden as an eagle bears its eaglet on its broad back", "context_len": 46}}}, "flip": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "We should have to come down now and then , when the hoofs would give us another flip upward and away we should go", "context_len": 24}}}, "bolting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` Dan 's cubby-hole was a small room shut off from the rest of the hayloft , where one of the farm hands kept his tools ; and here the boys went , shutting and bolting the door behind them", "context_len": 40}}}, "mustang": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 32, "context": "He had not had much practice in riding at the East , but the practice he had had in California had already made him a good rider , and even if the mustang had taken a fancy to buck he would have found it rather hard to dislodge our young hero", "context_len": 51}}}, "lather": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Dick was in a lather and his sides heaved", "context_len": 9}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The panting animal was covered with lather and glistening sweat", "context_len": 10}}}, "succinctly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "So he told his story as succinctly as he could", "context_len": 10}}}, "emergencies": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "Two or three others were soon wounded , but not seriously , and when a supply of water ready for all emergencies had been collected on the roof , the dangerous duty was over", "context_len": 34}}}, "unsaddled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Dick having been unsaddled was turned out to graze with three others", "context_len": 12}}}, "condiments": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "A barrel of flour , a big can of coffee , another of sugar and smaller boxes of spices and condiments were disposed of with more regard for convenience than appearance", "context_len": 31}}}, "woodcut": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The only picture on the walls was a woodcut from a newspaper , showing the homely features of Abraham Lincoln who had been nominated a short time before by the newly-born Republican party , for the presidency of the United States", "context_len": 41}}}, "slimmest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "They were too narrow for the slimmest man that ever lived to squeeze through", "context_len": 14}}}, "ventilation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "At other times , they helped in the way of ventilation and the lighting of the apartment", "context_len": 17}}}, "simmering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "But Jenkins filled each large cup from the capacious pot simmering on the stove , and the fragrant odor was delightful to the keen appetites", "context_len": 25}}}, "peppered": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "Only a fortnight before , the cabin underwent a siege for three days and nights from a large party of Piutes , who peppered the logs well", "context_len": 27}}}, "reconnoitering": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Alexander sent forward a strong reconnoitering party , ordering them to proceed cautiously , to ascend eminences and look far before them , to guard carefully against surprise , and to send back word immediately if they came upon any traces of the enemy", "context_len": 44}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "At the present day the operations of such a reconnoitering party are very much aided by the use of spy-glasses , which are made now with great care expressly for military purposes", "context_len": 32}}}, "thunderbolt": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Alden waved his hand and was off like a thunderbolt", "context_len": 10}}}, "equine": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "A horse is quick to learn what kind of man holds the reins , and woe to him whom the equine despises ! Bucephalus would not have needed any enlightenment had Harper or any one of the regular riders been in the saddle , but he wasn \u2019 t sure about the lusty young fellow who was trying to lord it over him", "context_len": 63}}}, "enlightenment": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "A horse is quick to learn what kind of man holds the reins , and woe to him whom the equine despises ! Bucephalus would not have needed any enlightenment had Harper or any one of the regular riders been in the saddle , but he wasn \u2019 t sure about the lusty young fellow who was trying to lord it over him", "context_len": 63}}}, "unhorsed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Once more it began , and once more the boy jockey was unhorsed and went flying over the animal 's head", "context_len": 22}}}, "obtruded": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "He had not yet decided in his own mind whether the former bore any relation to his passage through the gorge , and he was equally uncertain about the signal that now obtruded itself", "context_len": 34}}}, "steepness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Although the steepness had declined , he showed no disposition to increase his pace", "context_len": 14}}}, "incumbrance": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "When he left the train in company with Jethro he expected to rejoin his friends before the close of the afternoon and a blanket would have been an incumbrance , but quite acceptable now", "context_len": 34}}}, "fetlocks": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "The stream shallowed , and increasing his pace , the pony stepped out on the other bank , with the moisture dripping from his fetlocks", "context_len": 25}}}, "wetted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Only the lower part of his body , however , had been wetted", "context_len": 13}}}, "fetlock": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Speaking soothingly , Alden gently passed his hand down the graceful limb from the bent knee to the fetlock", "context_len": 19}}}, "tendon": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "There \u2019 s where the trouble is , he decided ; he must have strained a tendon , though I don \u2019 t feel any difference", "context_len": 26}}}, "fondled": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` Me -- nowhere ! '' chuckled Bart , winking deliberately and then , walking over to the horse , he fondled his long ears , with the remark : `` If I had a dandy rig like you 've got here , I bet I 'd go somewheres , though ! ''", "context_len": 53}}}, "foreleg": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Once more he nursed the foreleg and after a time , \u2019 Ceph set it down", "context_len": 16}}}, "whoa": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 29, "context": "Bradford smiled , and did not look as if she thought the `` spoiling '' would hurt her children very much ; and now , with a loud `` whoa , '' Mr", "context_len": 33}}}, "tote": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "These words were addressed to the saddle , which he flung impatiently to the ground : whoever chooses can find you ; I don \u2019 t tote you another rod", "context_len": 30}}}, "burdensome": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Weighing only twenty pounds , the pouches were not burdensome", "context_len": 10}}}, "pebbly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "At such times , when the surface was flinty or pebbly , he not only used the sense of feeling , but lighted a match", "context_len": 25}}}, "ignite": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Only when absolutely necessary did he intend to ignite his lucifers", "context_len": 11}}}, "lucifers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Only when absolutely necessary did he intend to ignite his lucifers", "context_len": 11}}}, "trailers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "He had never heard of the methods employed by veteran trailers , though Shagbark had practised them , but reflection caused Alden to try this one", "context_len": 26}}}, "corrugated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Forgetful for the moment of the peril of the act , the youth drew another match along the corrugated bottom of his little safe and held the speck of flame in front of him", "context_len": 34}}}, "congeal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "He was sure that half a degree colder would congeal it", "context_len": 11}}}, "sustain": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 25, "context": "The question was , whether Alexander should attempt to take his father 's place in respect to all this general power , and undertake to sustain and carry on his vast projects , or whether he should content himself with ruling , in quiet , over his native country of Macedon", "context_len": 51}}}, "submersion": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "His gun was gone beyond recovery , but he had his revolver , which like the contents of his match safe was not affected by the submersion", "context_len": 27}}}, "generated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The healthful , reviving glow must come from within , and that had to be generated by action", "context_len": 18}}}, "crystalline": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "In the crystalline air , it gleamed like the sun it really was", "context_len": 13}}}, "incisors": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "He shivered and felt wretched , with the soggy leathern pouches flapping his neck , like a grotesque tippet ; but ere long his incisors stopped their music , and the chills shook no more", "context_len": 35}}}, "orbs": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "The creature 's eyes had escaped , and , now with the yellow orbs blazing with deadly hate and anger , she leaped forward as though to serve the lad as she had served the fox", "context_len": 36}}}, "unwavering": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The glow was unwavering", "context_len": 4}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Fired at from roof and window by concealed foes , they marched on with unwavering courage , and those who reached the Residency had a reward such as can come to few in this life", "context_len": 35}}}, "searchingly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "Some unknown friend has done it all ; but who , I know no more than yourself , perhaps not so much ; '' and the policeman looked searchingly into Mr", "context_len": 31}}}, "wagers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "I have heard of a good many fool wagers , but I never knew anything more absurd than that", "context_len": 19}}}, "tuckered": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "I should have given you the chance , if you hadn \u2019 t been tuckered out ; the fellow who took the mail was as fresh as a daisy and eager for the trip", "context_len": 34}}}, "roil": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Nothing could have occurred to roil him more deeply", "context_len": 9}}}, "brusqueness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The man was nettled by the unconscious brusqueness of Alden \u2019 s manner", "context_len": 13}}}, "unenviable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Repulsed and turned back at all points Alden was in an unenviable frame of mind", "context_len": 15}}}, "inexcusably": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "He knew he had acted inexcusably toward Mr", "context_len": 8}}}, "discolored": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "We walked beyond the discolored barn which was shedding its shingles as a pony sheds his hair , and came to a dull old orchard where some quite nice crab apple trees stood knee deep in selfish weeds that were taking the goodness out of the soil", "context_len": 47}}}, "spittle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Cal swung one of his sinewy legs over the other knee , struck a fly crawling several feet away , with a well aimed volley of discolored spittle , and said :", "context_len": 32}}}, "pardner": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Hold on , pardner ; young Brandley ain \u2019 t running away from you", "context_len": 14}}}, "gorges": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 52, "context": "He strolled about the open space , pausing now and then to survey through his glasses the snowy peaks which towered far into the sky to the westward , or at the lower hills to the north , where the gnarled pine , the dwarfed cedar , the rushing torrents and the gorges made up one of the many wild regions which abound over thousands of square miles of area", "context_len": 70}}}, "jugful": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Not a thing ; he isn \u2019 t the first one the Indians got and he won \u2019 t be the last by a jugful", "context_len": 25}}}, "stubbed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "You neber had de rights ob dat ; I stubbed my toe , bumped agin him and knocked him agin you", "context_len": 21}}}, "squally": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "It mought be that when he seen things looked squally he slipped off his pony and took to kiver", "context_len": 19}}}, "dissimilar": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "The reason is , that each one of these accounts is intended for a different set of readers , who read with ideas and purposes widely dissimilar from each other", "context_len": 30}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Now Europe and Asia were , in those days , as now , marked and distinguished by two vast masses of social and civilized life , widely dissimilar from each other", "context_len": 31}}}, "narratives": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 5, "context": "The portions of Homer 's narratives which most excited his enthusiasm were those relating to the characters of the heroes who fought , on one side and on the other , at the siege , their various adventures , and the delineations of their motives and principles of conduct , and the emotions and excitements they experienced in the various circumstances in which they were placed", "context_len": 66}}}, "deterioration": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "DETERIORATION OF CHARACTER 234", "context_len": 4}}}, "achilles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "ACHILLES 97", "context_len": 2}}}, "regent": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "When he was only sixteen years of age , his father , Philip , made him regent of Macedon while he was absent on a great military campaign among the other states of Greece", "context_len": 34}}}, "ascendency": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 3, "context": "His influence and ascendency seemed firmly established now in the country of the Greeks , and in due time he returned to Macedon , and celebrated at \u00c6g\u00e6 , which was at this time his capital , the establishment and confirmation of his power , by games , shows , spectacles , illuminations , and sacrifices to the gods , offered on a scale of the greatest pomp and magnificence", "context_len": 70}}}, "attachments": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "He formed strong attachments , was grateful for kindnesses shown to him , considerate in respect to the feelings of all who were connected with him in any way , faithful to his friends , and generous toward his foes", "context_len": 40}}}, "organizations": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "It was Washington 's aim to mature and perfect organizations which would move on prosperously of themselves , without him ; and he was continually withdrawing his hand from action and control in public affairs , taking a higher pleasure in the independent working of the institutions which he had formed and protected , than in exercising , himself , a high personal power", "context_len": 64}}}, "citadels": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "He took possession only of the citadels , and of such governmental property as he found there , and he continued the same taxes , the same laws , and the same tribunals as had existed before his invasion", "context_len": 39}}}, "caparisoned": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "The Persians had enormous armies , perfectly provided for , with beautiful tents , horses elegantly caparisoned , arms and munitions of war of the finest workmanship , and officers magnificently dressed , and accustomed to a life of luxury and splendor", "context_len": 42}}}, "inured": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "Of course it was considered extremely important that the child destined to be a general should become robust and powerful in constitution from his earliest years , and that he should be inured to hardship and fatigue", "context_len": 37}}}, "incursion": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 25, "context": "He organized armies , crossed the boundary between Europe and Asia , and spent the twelve years of his career in a most triumphant military incursion into the very center and seat of Asiatic power , destroying the Asiatic armies , conquering the most splendid cities , defeating or taking captive the kings , and princes , and generals that opposed his progress", "context_len": 63}}}, "conquests": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 25, "context": "It is said that Alexander , finding that the various objects of human ambition which he had been so rapidly attaining by his victories and conquests for the past few years were insufficient to satisfy him , began now to aspire for some supernatural honors , and he accordingly conceived the design of having himself declared to be the son of a god", "context_len": 63}}}, "endued": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "As he gradually advanced in the years of his boyhood , it was observed by all who knew him that he was endued with extraordinary qualities of mind and of character , which seemed to indicate , at a very early age , his future greatness", "context_len": 46}}}, "grades": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "In ancient times , however , the great business of the officers , certainly in all the subordinate grades , was to lead on the men , and set them an example by performing themselves deeds in which their own great personal prowess was displayed", "context_len": 45}}}, "preceptor": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "As soon as he was old enough to learn , they appointed him a preceptor also , to teach him such branches as were generally taught to young princes in those days", "context_len": 32}}}, "narrations": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "These tales are narrations of the exploits and adventures of certain great warriors at the siege of Troy -- a siege which lasted ten years -- and they are written with so much beauty and force , they contain such admirable delineations of character , and such graphic and vivid descriptions of romantic adventures , and picturesque and striking scenes , that they have been admired in every age by all who have learned to understand the language in which they are written", "context_len": 83}}}, "delineations": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 41, "context": "These tales are narrations of the exploits and adventures of certain great warriors at the siege of Troy -- a siege which lasted ten years -- and they are written with so much beauty and force , they contain such admirable delineations of character , and such graphic and vivid descriptions of romantic adventures , and picturesque and striking scenes , that they have been admired in every age by all who have learned to understand the language in which they are written", "context_len": 83}}}, "philosophers": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "In the latter part of his course of education he was placed under the charge of Aristotle , who was one of the most eminent philosophers of ancient times", "context_len": 29}}}, "depository": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Alexander was aware of this depository before he advanced to meet Darius , and , on the day of the battle of Arbela , as soon as the victory was decided , he sent an officer from the very field to summon Susa to surrender", "context_len": 45}}}, "metaphysical": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "He made great progress in metaphysical philosophy and mathematics , by which means his powers of calculation and his judgment were greatly improved", "context_len": 23}}}, "monarchy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "As he approached Persepolis , he conceived the idea that , as this city was the capital and center of the Persian monarchy , and , as such , the point from which had emanated all the Persian hostility to Greece , he owed it some signal retribution", "context_len": 48}}}, "artificially": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "They had stories to tell him about the famous hanging gardens , which were artificially constructed in the most magnificent manner , on arches raised high in the air ; and about a vine made of gold , with all sorts of precious stones upon it instead of fruit , which was wrought as an ornament over the throne on which the King of Persia often gave audience ; of the splendid palaces and vast cities of the Persians ; and the banquets , and f\u00eates , and magnificent entertainments and celebrations which they used to have there", "context_len": 98}}}, "banquets": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 82, "context": "They had stories to tell him about the famous hanging gardens , which were artificially constructed in the most magnificent manner , on arches raised high in the air ; and about a vine made of gold , with all sorts of precious stones upon it instead of fruit , which was wrought as an ornament over the throne on which the King of Persia often gave audience ; of the splendid palaces and vast cities of the Persians ; and the banquets , and f\u00eates , and magnificent entertainments and celebrations which they used to have there", "context_len": 98}}}, "f\u00eates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 85, "context": "They had stories to tell him about the famous hanging gardens , which were artificially constructed in the most magnificent manner , on arches raised high in the air ; and about a vine made of gold , with all sorts of precious stones upon it instead of fruit , which was wrought as an ornament over the throne on which the King of Persia often gave audience ; of the splendid palaces and vast cities of the Persians ; and the banquets , and f\u00eates , and magnificent entertainments and celebrations which they used to have there", "context_len": 98}}}, "agitations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "He perceived that a part of the difficulty was caused by the agitations which the horse experienced in so strange and new a scene , and that he appeared , also , to be somewhat frightened by his own shadow , which happened at that time to be thrown very strongly and distinctly upon the ground", "context_len": 56}}}, "exacts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "On the contrary , horses seem to love to yield obedience to man , when the individual who exacts the obedience possesses those qualities of coolness and courage which their instincts enable them to appreciate", "context_len": 35}}}, "tractable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "He became very tractable and docile , yielding a ready submission to his master in every thing", "context_len": 17}}}, "regency": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Without doubt Alexander had , in this regency , the counsel and aid of high officers of state of great experience and ability", "context_len": 23}}}, "ablest": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "He felt some solicitude in giving his young son so important a charge , but he endeavored to guard against the danger of an unfortunate result by putting the ablest generals on Alexander 's side , while he reserved those on whom he could place less reliance for his own", "context_len": 50}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "He called a council of his ablest and best friends and advisers , consisting of the principal officers of his army , and laid the facts before them", "context_len": 28}}}, "strikingly": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 60, "context": "The death of Parmenio and of his son in this violent manner , when , too , there was so little evidence of their guilt , made a very general and a very unfavorable impression in respect to Alexander ; and not long afterward another case occurred , in some respects still more painful , as it evinced still more strikingly that the mind of Alexander , which had been in his earlier days filled with such noble and lofty sentiments of justice and generosity , was gradually getting to be under the supreme dominion of selfish and ungovernable passions : it was the case of Clitus", "context_len": 107}}}, "aggrandizement": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "It is more probable , therefore , that policy , and a regard for his own aggrandizement , rather than compassion for the suffering , led him to honor his captive queens", "context_len": 32}}}, "qualifying": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "He felt within himself powers and capacities qualifying him to take his father 's place , and reap for himself the harvest of glory and power which seemed to await the Grecian armies in the coming campaign", "context_len": 37}}}, "reconciliation": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 60, "context": "It is well said that no man is utterly bad , and perhaps he possessed domestic virtues which were thought unworthy of the attention of the chroniclers ; but as they picture him -- now prompting Ethelred to deeds of treachery against the Danes , now joining those Danes themselves , and surpassing them in cruelty -- now seeking pretended reconciliation , only to betray his foe more surely , and in all this aided and supported by the weak , unprincipled king -- as thus pictured there is scarcely a blacker character in history", "context_len": 95}}}, "hollowness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Nor could Philip justly complain of the hollowness and falseness of these professions of friendship", "context_len": 15}}}, "falseness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Nor could Philip justly complain of the hollowness and falseness of these professions of friendship", "context_len": 15}}}, "athens": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Athens is situated some distance back from the sea , and has a small port , called the Pir\u00e6us , at the shore -- a long , straight avenue leading from the port to the city", "context_len": 36}}}, "defiles": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 12, "context": "To stand stealthily among these dark and somber mountains , with the defiles and passes below filled with the columns of his small but undaunted army , and to look onward , a few miles beyond , and see the countless fires of the vast hosts which had got between him and all hope of retreat to his native land ; to feel , as he must have done , that his fate , and that of all who were with him , depended upon the events of the day that was soon to dawn -- to see and feel these things must have made this night one of the most exciting and solemn scenes in the conqueror 's life", "context_len": 120}}}, "fictitious": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "How far the circumstances of this whole story are true , and how far fictitious , no one can tell ; the story itself , however , as thus related , has come down from generation to generation , in every country of Europe , for two thousand years , and any extrication of one 's self from a difficulty by violent means has been called cutting the Gordian knot to the present day", "context_len": 74}}}, "censers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "There were twelve of these statues , sculptured with great art , and they were borne along on elevated pedestals , with censers , and incense , and various ceremonies of homage , while vast multitudes of spectators lined the way", "context_len": 41}}}, "homage": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 21, "context": "The ignorant multitude accordingly , in those days , looked up to a living king with almost the same reverence and homage which they felt for their deified heroes ; and these deified heroes furnished them with all the ideas they had of God", "context_len": 44}}}, "impious": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "This was not , however , so impious as it would at first view seem , for the gods whom the ancients worshiped were , in fact , only deifications of old heroes and kings who had lived in early times , and had acquired a reputation for supernatural powers by the fame of their exploits , exaggerated in descending by tradition in superstitious times", "context_len": 65}}}, "deified": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "gorgeously": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "These troops escorted princes , embassadors , generals , and great officers of state , all gorgeously decked in their robes , and wearing their badges and insignia", "context_len": 28}}}, "theater": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "All the statues had entered except that of Philip , which was just at the door , and Philip himself was advancing in the midst of the space left for him , up the avenue by which the theater was approached , when an occurrence took place by which the whole character of the scene , the destiny of Alexander , and the fate of fifty nations , was suddenly and totally changed", "context_len": 73}}}, "counselors": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 7, "context": "The king then called his generals and counselors together , informed them of the facts , and made known to them his determination to return immediately through the Gates of Syria and attack the Persian army", "context_len": 36}}}, "demosthenes": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "Demosthenes , who had been silent while Alexander was present in Greece , during his first visit there , had again been endeavoring to arouse opposition to Macedonian ascendency , and to concentrate and bring out into action the influences which were hostile to Alexander", "context_len": 45}}}, "orator": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 21, "context": "The same interposition in behalf of the poet 's family and descendants spoke directly to the heart of every poet , orator , historian , and philosopher throughout the country , and tended to make all the lovers of literature his friends", "context_len": 42}}}, "arousing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "In order , however , that I might make no error , I again sought Timothy Davis immediately after breakfast , and , without arousing his suspicions by letting him become aware of my motive , I perceived that a small patch of cloth , corresponding in every particular with the one before the court , had been torn from the right sleeve of his coat", "context_len": 66}}}, "curtail": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Many of his most powerful orations were made for the purpose of arousing his countrymen to resist his ambitious plans and to curtail his power", "context_len": 25}}}, "philippics": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "denunciatory": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "These orations were called his Philippics , and from this origin has arisen the practice , which has prevailed ever since that day , of applying the term philippics to denote , in general , any strongly denunciatory harangues", "context_len": 39}}}, "harangues": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "These orations were called his Philippics , and from this origin has arisen the practice , which has prevailed ever since that day , of applying the term philippics to denote , in general , any strongly denunciatory harangues", "context_len": 39}}}, "assassinating": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "Many persons have accordingly supposed that the plan of assassinating Philip was devised in Greece ; that Demosthenes was a party to it ; that Pausanias was the agent for carrying it into execution ; and that Demosthenes was so confident of the success of the plot , and exulted so much in this certainty , that he could not resist the temptation of thus anticipating its announcement", "context_len": 68}}}, "departments": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Alexander next decided not to make any change in his father 's appointments to the great offices of state , but to let all the departments of public affairs go on in the same hands as before", "context_len": 37}}}, "incompetent": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 57, "context": "How sagacious a line of conduct was this ! Most ardent and enthusiastic young men , in the circumstances in which he was placed , would have been elated and vain at their elevation , and would have replaced the old and well-tried servants of the father with personal favorites of their own age , inexperienced and incompetent , and as conceited as themselves", "context_len": 64}}}, "gayety": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "There was Corinth , which was distinguished for the gayety and pleasure which reigned there", "context_len": 15}}}, "isthmus": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "Their strong personal attachment to Alexander ; their confidence that whatever he should plan and attempt would succeed ; the novelty and boldness of this design of reaching an island by building an isthmus to it from the main-land -- these and other similar considerations excited the ardor and enthusiasm of the troops to the highest degree", "context_len": 57}}}, "promote": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "They were nearly all soldiers , and all the arrangements of the state and of society , and all the plans of education , were designed to promote military ambition and pride among the officers and fierce and indomitable courage and endurance in the men", "context_len": 45}}}, "congress": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "There was in those days a sort of general congress or assembly of the states of Greece , which was held from time to time , to decide questions and disputes in which the different states were continually getting involved with each other", "context_len": 43}}}, "disputes": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "There was in those days a sort of general congress or assembly of the states of Greece , which was held from time to time , to decide questions and disputes in which the different states were continually getting involved with each other", "context_len": 43}}}, "wields": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Common men take pleasure in yielding to the influence and ascendency of one whose spirit they see and feel stands on a higher eminence and wields higher powers than their own", "context_len": 31}}}, "instate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "They expressed a cordial readiness to instate him in the position which his father had occupied", "context_len": 16}}}, "defiled": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "They accordingly defiled along under these cliffs , marching , as they say , sometimes up to the waist in water , the swell rolling in upon them all the time from the offing", "context_len": 34}}}, "defile": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 10, "context": "The marching of an army through a narrow and dangerous defile like this always causes detention and delay , and Alexander hastened forward in hopes to overtake Darius before he should reach it", "context_len": 33}}}, "confederation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "That such a youthful prince should be selected by so powerful a confederation of nations as their leader in such an enterprise as they were about to engage in , indicates a most extraordinary power on his part of acquiring an ascendency over the minds of men , and of impressing all with a sense of his commanding superiority", "context_len": 59}}}, "unembarrassed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "Alexander returned to Macedon from his expedition to the southward in triumph , and began at once to arrange the affairs of his kingdom , so as to be ready to enter , unembarrassed , upon the great career of conquest which he imagined was before him", "context_len": 47}}}, "invests": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "It invests the spectacle with a solemn grandeur", "context_len": 8}}}, "extends": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "It will be observed by looking upon the map , that the chain of mountains called Mount Taurus extends down near to the coast , at the northeastern corner of the Mediterranean", "context_len": 32}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "It is only , however , so far as the land is formed by the deposits of the Nile , that this scene of verdure and beauty extends", "context_len": 28}}}, "southwardly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "A branch of this mountain range , called Rhodope , extends southwardly from about the middle of its length , as may be seen by the map", "context_len": 27}}}, "thrace": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Thrace was one great fertile basin or valley , sloping toward the center in every direction , so that all the streams from the mountains , increased by the rains which fell over the whole surface of the ground , flowed together into one river , which meandered through the center of the valley , and flowed out at last into the \u00c6gean Sea", "context_len": 64}}}, "hoary": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "His must be a lonely life at home ; there are no other children , but from time to time hoary warriors , upon whose souls lies , I fear , the guilt of much innocent blood , find a home there", "context_len": 42}}}, "phenomena": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "He was violent and impetuous in temper , rejoicing in the devastation of winter , and in all the sublime phenomena of tempests , cold , and snow", "context_len": 28}}}, "tempests": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Fortunately the tropical tempests , though fierce while they last , are not of long duration , and Bomba knew that he would not be delayed long on his journey", "context_len": 30}}}, "invulnerability": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The barbarians fled in all directions , terrified at the force and invulnerability of men whom loaded wagons , rolling over their bodies down a steep descent , could not kill", "context_len": 31}}}, "chieftains": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 5, "context": "A few hours later the chieftains were all summoned to a council in the king 's tent , and when , after a short session , they came forth , the general order was given to break up the encampment , and move towards the southwest for the winter , for all the resources of the country around were exhausted", "context_len": 60}}}, "barbarian": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 51, "context": "While things were in this state -- the Thebans having understood that Alexander had been killed at the north , and supposing that , at all events , if this report should not be true , he was , without doubt , still far away , involved in contentions with the barbarian nations , from which it was not to be expected that he could be very speedily extricated -- the whole city was suddenly thrown into consternation by the report that a large Macedonian army was approaching from the north , with Alexander at its head , and that it was , in fact , close upon them", "context_len": 109}}}, "embarrass": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 54, "context": "It is a serious undertaking to get a great body of men and horses across a broad and rapid river , when the people of the country have done all in their power to remove or destroy all possible means of transit , and when hostile bands are on the opposite bank , to embarrass and impede the operations by every mode in their power", "context_len": 65}}}, "unexplored": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "The river was so distant , so broad and rapid , and its banks were bordered and defended by such ferocious foes , that to cross its eddying tide , and penetrate into the unknown and unexplored regions beyond , leaving the broad , and deep , and rapid stream to cut off the hopes of retreat , implied the possession of extreme self-reliance , courage , and decision", "context_len": 69}}}, "hostages": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "He made satisfactory treaties with them all ; took hostages , where necessary , to secure the observance of the treaties , and then recrossed the Danube and set out on his return to Macedon", "context_len": 35}}}, "recrossed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "He made satisfactory treaties with them all ; took hostages , where necessary , to secure the observance of the treaties , and then recrossed the Danube and set out on his return to Macedon", "context_len": 35}}}, "contentions": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 48, "context": "While things were in this state -- the Thebans having understood that Alexander had been killed at the north , and supposing that , at all events , if this report should not be true , he was , without doubt , still far away , involved in contentions with the barbarian nations , from which it was not to be expected that he could be very speedily extricated -- the whole city was suddenly thrown into consternation by the report that a large Macedonian army was approaching from the north , with Alexander at its head , and that it was , in fact , close upon them", "context_len": 109}}}, "invest": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "The besiegers have to invest the city on all sides to cut off all supplies of provisions , and then , in those days , they had to construct engines to make a breach somewhere in the walls , through which an assaulting party could attempt to force their way in", "context_len": 51}}}, "elapses": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "The army within , sheltered behind the parapets of the walls , and standing there in a position above that of their assailants , have such great advantages in the contest that a long time often elapses before they can be compelled to surrender", "context_len": 44}}}, "surrenders": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "I am rich , or I shall be in a few months , when my guardian surrenders his trust , and I will take care that you are well provided for", "context_len": 31}}}, "malignity": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "It is horrible , because the soldiers , exasperated to fury by the resistance which they meet with , and by the awful malignity of the passions always excited in the hour of battle , if they succeed , burst suddenly into the precincts of domestic life , and find sometimes thousands of families -- mothers , and children , and defenseless maidens -- at the mercy of passions excited to phrensy", "context_len": 72}}}, "phrensy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 71, "context": "It is horrible , because the soldiers , exasperated to fury by the resistance which they meet with , and by the awful malignity of the passions always excited in the hour of battle , if they succeed , burst suddenly into the precincts of domestic life , and find sometimes thousands of families -- mothers , and children , and defenseless maidens -- at the mercy of passions excited to phrensy", "context_len": 72}}}, "perpetration": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Tigers do not spring upon their prey with greater ferocity than man springs , under such circumstances , to the perpetration of every possible cruelty upon his fellow man", "context_len": 29}}}, "assaulter": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "But in carrying a city by storm , which takes place usually at an unexpected time , and often in the night , the maddened and victorious assaulter suddenly burst into the sacred scenes of domestic peace , and seclusion , and love -- the very worst of men , filled with the worst of passions , stimulated by the resistance they have encountered , and licensed by their victory to give all these passions the fullest and most unrestricted gratification", "context_len": 81}}}, "soldiery": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "The fleet bore the troops of savage soldiery safely -- too safely -- across the waters of the Solent , to the estuary formed by the Medina , where now thousands of visitors seek health and repose , and the towers of Osborne crown the eastern eminences", "context_len": 47}}}, "discriminated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Then , in respect to the rest of the population , he discriminated carefully between those who had favored the rebellion and those who had been true to their allegiance to him", "context_len": 32}}}, "outweighed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "And if , in the case of any family , it could be shown that one individual had been on the Macedonian side , the single instance of fidelity outweighed the treason of the other members , and the whole family was saved", "context_len": 43}}}, "concentrate": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "It was at this inauspicious moment that the Danes charged the palisades again with deadly fury , while the attention of all was drawn to the flames ; so fierce was the attack , that it was necessary once more to concentrate all the strength of the besieged to repel them ; and the fire gained in strength , roared and hissed in its fury , seizing for its prey the whole roof of the eastern wing of the building", "context_len": 80}}}, "investiture": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "His unexpected appearance , when they imagined him entangled among the mountains and wilds of unknown regions in the north ; his sudden investiture of Thebes ; the assault ; the calm deliberations in respect to the destiny of the city , and the slow , cautious , discriminating , but inexorable energy with which the decision was carried into effect , all coming in such rapid succession , impressed the Grecian commonwealth with the conviction that the personage they had to deal with was no boy in character , whatever might be his years", "context_len": 95}}}, "pardoning": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "His pardoning the priests , whether they had been for him or against him , made every friend of religion incline to his favor", "context_len": 24}}}, "interposition": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "The same interposition in behalf of the poet 's family and descendants spoke directly to the heart of every poet , orator , historian , and philosopher throughout the country , and tended to make all the lovers of literature his friends", "context_len": 42}}}, "historian": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "The same interposition in behalf of the poet 's family and descendants spoke directly to the heart of every poet , orator , historian , and philosopher throughout the country , and tended to make all the lovers of literature his friends", "context_len": 42}}}, "ordaining": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "His magnanimity , also , in deciding that one single friend of his in a family should save that family , instead of ordaining , as a more short-sighted conqueror would have done , that a single enemy should condemn it , must have awakened a strong feeling of gratitude and regard in the hearts of all who could appreciate fidelity to friends and generosity of spirit", "context_len": 67}}}, "conqueror": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 117, "context": "To stand stealthily among these dark and somber mountains , with the defiles and passes below filled with the columns of his small but undaunted army , and to look onward , a few miles beyond , and see the countless fires of the vast hosts which had got between him and all hope of retreat to his native land ; to feel , as he must have done , that his fate , and that of all who were with him , depended upon the events of the day that was soon to dawn -- to see and feel these things must have made this night one of the most exciting and solemn scenes in the conqueror 's life", "context_len": 120}}}, "minority": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 66, "context": "Thus , as the news of the destruction of Thebes , and the selling of so large a portion of the inhabitants into slavery , spread over the land , its effect was to turn over so great a part of the population to a feeling of admiration of Alexander 's character , and confidence in his extraordinary powers , as to leave only a small minority disposed to take sides with the punished rebels , or resent the destruction of the city", "context_len": 83}}}, "spares": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "-- Alexander spares Lampsacus", "context_len": 4}}}, "captivating": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "This short story of 180 pages is captivating from start to finish , and the masculine reader takes off his hat , and the feminine reader courtesies , to this matron of the tubs , with her homely heroism and true kindness of heart", "context_len": 44}}}, "evince": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "Considering his extreme youth , and the captivating character of the enterprise , it is strange that he should have exercised so much deliberation and caution as his conduct did really evince", "context_len": 32}}}, "concurred": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "The generals of the army concurred in this opinion , and Alexander advanced toward Tyre", "context_len": 15}}}, "commotions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Alexander did die in Asia , his vast kingdom at once fell into pieces , and it was desolated with internal commotions and civil wars for a long period after his death", "context_len": 32}}}, "vicegerent": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "He concluded to make Antipater his vicegerent in Macedon during his absence , and to take Parmenio with him into Asia", "context_len": 21}}}, "retaliate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "One or two hundred years before this time , Darius , a king of Persia , had invaded Greece with an army of five hundred thousand men , and yet he had been defeated and driven back , and now Alexander was undertaking to retaliate with a great deal less than one tenth part of the force", "context_len": 57}}}, "pasturage": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "I \u2019 m sure , if we had sheep , and flax , and pasturage , and I had a loom , and the house full of blankets , and sheets , and nice things , all given to us , I shouldn \u2019 t be half so happy as I am in trying to get them", "context_len": 57}}}, "conjoined": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "These two principles are very distinct in their nature , though often conjoined", "context_len": 13}}}, "appropriation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "What he had apprehended the colonel would hardly have confessed even to himself ; but the truth was that he had suspected Percy of nothing less than the appropriation of some sum which he was compelled to replace or to face open disgrace", "context_len": 43}}}, "invoking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "They felt , when sacrificing to these gods , that they were invoking their presence and sympathy", "context_len": 17}}}, "enchantingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "The Muses , in honor of whom , as well as Jupiter , this great Macedonian festival was held , were nine singing and dancing maidens , beautiful in countenance and form , and enchantingly graceful in all their movements", "context_len": 40}}}, "propitiated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "He offered sacrifices to such of the gods as he supposed it would please the soldiers to imagine that they had propitiated", "context_len": 22}}}, "feastings": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "The princes and potentates of all the neighboring countries assembled to partake of his hospitality , to be entertained by the games , the plays , the spectacles , and the feastings , and to unite in swelling his court and doing him honor", "context_len": 44}}}, "blunted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Connected with these sacrifices and feastings , there were athletic and military spectacles and shows -- races and wrestlings -- and mock contests , with blunted spears", "context_len": 27}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` They have suffered much , my lad ; and suffering , as is often the case , has blunted their feelings", "context_len": 22}}}, "inspirited": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Thus inspirited by new desires for human praise , and trusting in the sympathy and protection of powers which were all that they conceived of as divine , the army prepared to set forth from their native land , bidding it a long , and , as it proved to most of them , a final farewell", "context_len": 57}}}, "soothsayers": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 18, "context": "It changed their agitation and terror into a feeling of suspense , in awaiting the answer of the soothsayers , which was far less painful and dangerous ; and at length , when the answer came , it allayed their anxiety and fear altogether", "context_len": 44}}}, "calamities": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "The mother was terrified , and , to avert these calamities , gave the child to a slave as soon as it was born , and ordered him to destroy it", "context_len": 31}}}, "foster": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "He grew in strength and beauty , and gave early and extraordinary proofs of courage and energy , as if he had imbibed some of the qualities of his fierce foster mother with the milk she gave him", "context_len": 38}}}, "independently": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "Though it is complete in itself , and may be read independently , the chief characters introduced will be recognized as old friends by the readers of `` The Young Explorer , '' the volume just preceding , not omitting Ki Sing , the faithful Chinaman , whose virtues may go far to diminish the prejudice which , justly or unjustly , is now felt toward his countrymen", "context_len": 68}}}, "pique": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "In fact , she was herself the goddess of discord , and , independently of her cause of pique in this case , she loved to promote disputes", "context_len": 28}}}, "impartial": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "But in modern times , many impartial readers , in reviewing calmly these events , think that there is reason to doubt whether Alexander , if he had set out on his great expedition without Parmenio , would have succeeded at all", "context_len": 42}}}, "herdsman": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "All this time Paris remained on the mountain , a simple shepherd and herdsman , not knowing his relationship to the monarch who reigned over the city and kingdom on the plain below", "context_len": 33}}}, "disguising": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "It happened , however , that a short time afterward there was another opportunity to contend for the same bull , and Paris , disguising himself as a prince , appeared in the lists , conquered every competitor , and bore away the bull again to his home in the fastnesses of the mountain", "context_len": 54}}}, "elope": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "He accordingly made a journey into Greece , visited Sparta , formed an acquaintance with Helen , persuaded her to abandon her husband and her duty , and elope with him to Troy", "context_len": 33}}}, "besieging": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "Alexander , in the same manner , when besieging the city , found that he must depend upon the neighboring countries for supplies of food ; and he accordingly sent requisitions for such supplies to several places , and , among others , to Judea", "context_len": 45}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Then Edmund retired into Wessex to raise more troops , and during his absence the Danes took the offensive again , once more besieging London in vain , while they harried all the neighbouring districts until Edmund returned with a large army , drove them into Kent , and gave them such a fearful defeat at Otford that they fled in despair to the Isle of Sheppey , and all men said Edmund would have destroyed them utterly , had not Edric persuaded him to stop the pursuit at Aylesford", "context_len": 90}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "How Bomba saved the camp of Gillis and Dorn , rubber hunters , from a night attack by jaguars -- how he trapped the deadly cooanaradi , the most dreaded serpent of the South American wilds , when it pursued him ; his adventures with alligators and anacondas ; the besieging of his cabin by the headhunters ; how his friends of the forest came to his aid when he was fearfully beset ; all this is narrated in the first volumes of this series , entitled : Bomba , the Jungle Boy ; or , The Old Naturalist \u2019 s Secret", "context_len": 102}}}, "invulnerable": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "There was a river called the Styx , the waters of which were said to have the property of making any one invulnerable", "context_len": 23}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 63, "context": "If the enemy remained at a distance , and threw javelins or darts at the phalanx , they fell harmless , stopped by the shields which the soldiers wore upon the left arm , and which were held in such a manner as to form a system of scales , which covered and protected the whole mass , and made the men almost invulnerable", "context_len": 64}}}, "patroclus": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Patroclus was at first successful , but was soon killed by Hector , the brother of Paris", "context_len": 17}}}, "remit": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "He did not remit his exertions till he had slain Hector , and then he expressed his brutal exultation , and satisfied his revenge , by dragging the dead body at the wheels of his chariot around the walls of the city", "context_len": 42}}}, "rivalries": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The subjects interested him ; the accounts of the contentions , the rivalries , the exploits of these warriors , the delineations of their character and springs of action , and the narrations of the various incidents and events to which such a war gave rise , were all calculated to captivate the imagination of a young martial hero", "context_len": 59}}}, "divinity": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "But , though Alexander was thus willing to impress his ignorant soldiers with a mysterious veneration for his fictitious divinity , he was not deceived himself on the subject ; he sometimes even made his pretensions to the divine character a subject of joke", "context_len": 44}}}, "dolphins": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "Each of the lofty ships bore the ensign of its commander ; some carried at the prow the figures of lions , some of bulls , dolphins , dragons , or armed warriors , gaudily painted or even gilded ; while others bore from their mast the ensign of voracious birds -- the eagle , the raven -- which appeared to stretch their wings as the flag expanded in the wind", "context_len": 71}}}, "scepter": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Instead of a scepter Neptune carried a trident", "context_len": 8}}}, "trident": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "A trident was a sort of three-pronged harpoon , such as was used in those days by the fishermen of the Mediterranean", "context_len": 22}}}, "harpoon": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "A trident was a sort of three-pronged harpoon , such as was used in those days by the fishermen of the Mediterranean", "context_len": 22}}}, "frontiers": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 28, "context": "To convey a body by a regular funeral procession , formed as soon after the death as the arrangements could be made , from Babylon to the eastern frontiers of Egypt , a distance of a thousand miles , was perhaps as grand a plan of interment as was ever formed", "context_len": 51}}}, "resumes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "-- Alexander resumes his march", "context_len": 5}}}, "bridegrooms": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "-- A detachment of bridegrooms", "context_len": 5}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "The bridegroom soldiers , full of exultation and pleasure , set forth on their return to Greece , in a detachment under the charge of three generals , themselves bridegrooms too", "context_len": 31}}}, "elevations": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "The round gently-swelling Sussex downs rose on the southern horizon , guarding the sea , while around them were once cultivated fields which the foe had reaped , while quick streams wound in between the gentle elevations , crowned with wood , and here and there the mere spread its lake-like form", "context_len": 52}}}, "forerunners": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "These men are called , in modern times , scouts ; in Alexander 's day , and in the Greek language , they were called prodromi , which means forerunners", "context_len": 30}}}, "intimidate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 45, "context": "He knew , too , that if this were done it would create a strong sensation throughout the whole country , impressing every one with a sense of the energy and power of the army which he was conducting , and would thus tend to intimidate the enemy , and facilitate all future operations", "context_len": 54}}}, "facilitate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 50, "context": "He knew , too , that if this were done it would create a strong sensation throughout the whole country , impressing every one with a sense of the energy and power of the army which he was conducting , and would thus tend to intimidate the enemy , and facilitate all future operations", "context_len": 54}}}, "javelins": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "If the enemy remained at a distance , and threw javelins or darts at the phalanx , they fell harmless , stopped by the shields which the soldiers wore upon the left arm , and which were held in such a manner as to form a system of scales , which covered and protected the whole mass , and made the men almost invulnerable", "context_len": 64}}}, "resistless": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "It was a huge animal , ferocious , dogged , stubborn , insensible to pain , knowing no fear , and bearing down with resistless and merciless destruction upon every thing that came in its way", "context_len": 36}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Alexander relied simply on the strength , the courage , the energy , and the calm and steady , but resistless ardor of his men , arranging them in simple combinations , and leading them forward directly to their work", "context_len": 40}}}, "brigades": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "Again and again commanders told him of the fearful havoc made in the ranks of their brigades , and asked either for support or to be allowed to withdraw their men", "context_len": 31}}}, "constituent": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "In marching , it was separated into these its constituent parts , and sometimes in battle it acted in divisions", "context_len": 20}}}, "exaggerations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "They may have been literally and fully true , or they may have been exaggerations of circumstances somewhat resembling them which really occurred , or they may have been fictitious altogether", "context_len": 31}}}, "magnify": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 51, "context": "The Greek historians , on the other hand , make no mention of this visit to Jerusalem ; and some persons think that it was never made , but that the story arose and was propagated from generation to generation among the Jews , through the influence of their desire to magnify the importance and influence of their worship , and that Josephus incorporated the account into his history without sufficiently verifying the facts", "context_len": 74}}}, "hazarding": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "He had been opposed to the plan of hazarding a battle", "context_len": 11}}}, "governmental": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "He took possession only of the citadels , and of such governmental property as he found there , and he continued the same taxes , the same laws , and the same tribunals as had existed before his invasion", "context_len": 39}}}, "tribunals": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "They are only technically shielded from being called crimes by the fact that the earth has no laws and no tribunals high enough to condemn such enormous burglaries as that of one quarter of the globe breaking violently and murderously in upon and robbing the other", "context_len": 46}}}, "diversified": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "The narrative of this progress , as given by the ancient historians , is diversified by a great variety of adventures and incidents , which give great interest to the story , and strikingly illustrate the character of Alexander and the spirit of the times", "context_len": 45}}}, "omens": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "The omens could thus almost always be interpreted any way , and sagacious generals only sought in them the means of confirming the courage and confidence of their soldiers , in respect to the plans which they adopted under the influence of other considerations altogether", "context_len": 45}}}, "nobleness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 51, "context": "No doubt this was an admirable stroke of policy ; for , as the number could not be large , their absence could not materially weaken his force , and they would , of course , fill all Greece with tales of Alexander 's energy and courage , and of the nobleness and generosity of his character", "context_len": 57}}}, "disseminating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "It was the most effectual way possible of disseminating through Europe the most brilliant accounts of what he had already done", "context_len": 21}}}, "windings": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 76, "context": "It was a dark and gloomy eventide : the sun , which had only made its appearance at intervals during the day , was fast sinking behind a heavy bank of clouds which filled the western horizon ; and the wind , which was freshening to a gale , seemed to bear the storm onward in its track , while it tore the few surviving leaves rudely from the trees , and whirled them in mazy windings", "context_len": 77}}}, "imitates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Its beautiful windings through verdant and fertile valleys were so renowned , that every stream which imitates its example is said to meander to the present day", "context_len": 27}}}, "recrossing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "He also directed that the detachment which had gone home should , on recrossing the Hellespont , on their return , proceed eastward to Gordium , thus making that city the general rendezvous for the commencement of his next campaign", "context_len": 40}}}, "thong": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "They took the cart and the yoke to preserve as sacred relics , consecrating them to Jupiter ; and Gordius tied the yoke to the pole of the cart by a thong of leather , making a knot so close and complicated that nobody could untie it again", "context_len": 48}}}, "extrication": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 52, "context": "How far the circumstances of this whole story are true , and how far fictitious , no one can tell ; the story itself , however , as thus related , has come down from generation to generation , in every country of Europe , for two thousand years , and any extrication of one 's self from a difficulty by violent means has been called cutting the Gordian knot to the present day", "context_len": 74}}}, "southeasterly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "He went on successfully for some weeks , moving in a southeasterly direction , and bringing the whole country under his dominion , until , at length , when he reached Tarsus , an event occurred which nearly terminated his career", "context_len": 41}}}, "envoys": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "He found a great number of envoys and embassadors from the various states of Greece at the Persian court , and these persons fell into his hands among the other captives", "context_len": 31}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The Thebans , scattered and disorganized as they were , had sent envoys in this way", "context_len": 16}}}, "pageantry": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "All this pomp and pageantry , with inefficient weapons , and inefficient men to wield them , will be of no avail against their invincible courage and energy ; and the best disposition that you can make of all your gold , and silver , and other treasures , is to send it away and procure good soldiers with it , if indeed gold and silver will procure them", "context_len": 69}}}, "habituated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "The Greeks were habituated to energetic speaking as well as acting , but Charidemus did not sufficiently consider that the Persians were not accustomed to hear such plain language as this", "context_len": 31}}}, "avenger": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "And now I , once his foe , but his friend and avenger now , devote myself to hunt the murderer", "context_len": 21}}}, "altars": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "They had also silver altars , on which they kept constantly burning what they called the sacred fire", "context_len": 18}}}, "magi": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Then came a long procession of priests and magi , who were dressed also in very splendid robes", "context_len": 18}}}, "equerries": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The equerries , that is , the attendants who had charge of this horse , were also all dressed in white , and each carried a golden rod in his hand", "context_len": 31}}}, "sculptures": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "It was ornamented with long rows of prows of ships , taken by Alexander in his victories , and by statues , and columns , and sculptures , and gilded ornaments of every kind", "context_len": 34}}}, "tiara": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "He wore a tiara upon his head of very costly and elegant workmanship , and enriched , like the rest of his dress , with brilliant ornaments", "context_len": 27}}}, "eminences": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 46, "context": "The fleet bore the troops of savage soldiery safely -- too safely -- across the waters of the Solent , to the estuary formed by the Medina , where now thousands of visitors seek health and repose , and the towers of Osborne crown the eastern eminences", "context_len": 47}}}, "somber": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "To stand stealthily among these dark and somber mountains , with the defiles and passes below filled with the columns of his small but undaunted army , and to look onward , a few miles beyond , and see the countless fires of the vast hosts which had got between him and all hope of retreat to his native land ; to feel , as he must have done , that his fate , and that of all who were with him , depended upon the events of the day that was soon to dawn -- to see and feel these things must have made this night one of the most exciting and solemn scenes in the conqueror 's life", "context_len": 120}}}, "bewailed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The mother and wife of Darius bewailed their hard fate with cries and tears , and continued all the evening in an agony of consternation and despair", "context_len": 27}}}, "technically": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "They are only technically shielded from being called crimes by the fact that the earth has no laws and no tribunals high enough to condemn such enormous burglaries as that of one quarter of the globe breaking violently and murderously in upon and robbing the other", "context_len": 46}}}, "kindlier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 56, "context": "The two young men were of the same age , and , though Alexander had the good sense to retain in power all the old and experienced officers which his father had employed , both in the court and army , he showed that , after all , ambition had not overwhelmed and stifled all the kindlier feelings of the heart , by his strong attachment to this young companion", "context_len": 70}}}, "emissaries": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Now Alexander , in considering what disposition he should make of these emissaries from his own land , decided to regard them all as traitors except the Thebans", "context_len": 28}}}, "hesitates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "-- Alexander hesitates in regard to Tyre", "context_len": 7}}}, "conflagration": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 42, "context": "Lights appeared in two or three places , and soon it became evident that the ruthless foe had gained their object , as the barns and stables lit up in all directions , and the manor house was surrounded by the double conflagration , so that every object was as distinctly visible as in open daylight", "context_len": 56}}}, "collects": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "We 'll try and find where he belongs , who he is , and why he collects turtles", "context_len": 18}}}, "sinks": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "If this crate fills and sinks too quickly , we \u2019 ll drown with her , but it \u2019 s worth the risk", "context_len": 23}}}, "credits": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "-- What credits to be given to the adventure", "context_len": 9}}}, "edifices": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 69, "context": "It is true that the walls rose directly from the surface of the water , and of course a considerable part of their elevation was required to bring them up to the level of the surface of the land ; and then , in addition to this , they had to be carried up the whole ordinary height of a city wall to afford the usual protection to the edifices and dwellings within", "context_len": 73}}}, "thrift": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 56, "context": "The city itself , however , though contracted in extent on account of the small dimensions of the island , was very compactly built and strongly fortified , and it contained a vast number of stately and magnificent edifices , which were filled with stores of wealth that had been accumulated by the mercantile enterprise and thrift of many generations", "context_len": 60}}}, "geographers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Extravagant stories are told by the historians and geographers of those days , in respect to the scale on which the structures of Tyre were built", "context_len": 26}}}, "disavowing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "They sent complimentary messages to Alexander , congratulating him on his conquests , and disavowing all feelings of hostility to him", "context_len": 21}}}, "scriptures": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Alexander sent parties of men into the mountains of Lebanon , where were vast forests of cedars , which were very celebrated in ancient times , and which are often alluded to in the sacred scriptures", "context_len": 36}}}, "inconceivable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The flames ran all over the vessel with inconceivable rapidity", "context_len": 10}}}, "retaliating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "They treated the captives which they took on either side with greater and greater cruelty , each thinking that they were only retaliating worse injuries from the other", "context_len": 28}}}, "rams": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The engines approached nearer and nearer to the walls , until the battering rams bore directly upon them , and breaches began to be made", "context_len": 25}}}, "stairways": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "The vast throng then rolled along upon the top of the wall till they came to stairways and slopes by which they could descend into the city , and , pouring down through all these avenues , they spread over the streets , and satiated the hatred and rage , which had been gathering strength for seven long months , in bursting into houses , and killing and destroying all that came in their way", "context_len": 75}}}, "slaughtering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "After the soldiers were weary with the work of slaughtering the wretched inhabitants of the city , they found that many still remained alive , and Alexander tarnished the character for generosity and forbearance for which he had thus far been distinguished by the cruelty with which he treated them", "context_len": 50}}}, "unfavorable": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 33, "context": "The death of Parmenio and of his son in this violent manner , when , too , there was so little evidence of their guilt , made a very general and a very unfavorable impression in respect to Alexander ; and not long afterward another case occurred , in some respects still more painful , as it evinced still more strikingly that the mind of Alexander , which had been in his earlier days filled with such noble and lofty sentiments of justice and generosity , was gradually getting to be under the supreme dominion of selfish and ungovernable passions : it was the case of Clitus", "context_len": 107}}}, "characterize": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "He lost the modesty and gentleness which seemed to characterize him in the earlier part of his life , and began to assume the moral character , as well as perform the exploits , of a military hero", "context_len": 38}}}, "imperiousness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "And yet we find our feelings of sympathy for his character , and interest in his fate , somewhat alienated by the indications of pride , imperiousness , and cruelty which begin to appear", "context_len": 34}}}, "fabricated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "One thing is certain , however , that there are many military heroes of whom such stories would not be even fabricated", "context_len": 22}}}, "sterility": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "The reason of this sterility seems to be the absence of water", "context_len": 12}}}, "aspires": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "-- Alexander aspires to divine honors", "context_len": 6}}}, "subjugation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "After completing the subjugation of Tyre , Alexander commenced his march for Egypt", "context_len": 13}}}, "requisitions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Alexander , in the same manner , when besieging the city , found that he must depend upon the neighboring countries for supplies of food ; and he accordingly sent requisitions for such supplies to several places , and , among others , to Judea", "context_len": 45}}}, "pontifical": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The priests are to be dressed in their pontifical robes and go forth , and the inhabitants are to follow them in a civic procession", "context_len": 25}}}, "profoundest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "When , however , he saw the procession , and came near enough to distinguish the appearance and dress of the high priest , he stopped , seemed surprised and pleased , and advanced toward him with an air of the profoundest deference and respect", "context_len": 45}}}, "administers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "It is through his encouragement and aid that I am here , and I am ready to worship and adore the God whose service he administers", "context_len": 26}}}, "oblations": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "There Alexander united with them and with the Jews of the city in the celebration of religious rites , by offering sacrifices and oblations in the Jewish manner", "context_len": 28}}}, "predictions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Alexander was , as Josephus relates , very much pleased at the sight of these ancient predictions , and the interpretation put upon them by the priests", "context_len": 27}}}, "propagated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "The Greek historians , on the other hand , make no mention of this visit to Jerusalem ; and some persons think that it was never made , but that the story arose and was propagated from generation to generation among the Jews , through the influence of their desire to magnify the importance and influence of their worship , and that Josephus incorporated the account into his history without sufficiently verifying the facts", "context_len": 74}}}, "verifying": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 71, "context": "The Greek historians , on the other hand , make no mention of this visit to Jerusalem ; and some persons think that it was never made , but that the story arose and was propagated from generation to generation among the Jews , through the influence of their desire to magnify the importance and influence of their worship , and that Josephus incorporated the account into his history without sufficiently verifying the facts", "context_len": 74}}}, "besiege": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "Meanwhile the English continued the pursuit until a small remnant of Danes repassed the river ; only a small remnant of the party which , as it will be easily guessed , instigated by Edric , had sallied forth to besiege the place where Edmund had found refuge , who had so recently provoked the bitter hostility of Sweyn", "context_len": 59}}}, "unsubdued": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Betis did not reply , but looked upon Alexander with a calm , and composed , and unsubdued air , which incensed the conqueror more and more", "context_len": 27}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Darius , whom he considered his great enemy , was still in the field unsubdued", "context_len": 15}}}, "frankincense": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 51, "context": "When Alexander was a young man in Macedon , before his father 's death , he was one day present at the offering of sacrifices , and one of his teachers and guardians , named Leonnatus , who was standing by , thought he was rather profuse in his consumption of frankincense and myrrh", "context_len": 54}}}, "myrrh": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 53, "context": "When Alexander was a young man in Macedon , before his father 's death , he was one day present at the offering of sacrifices , and one of his teachers and guardians , named Leonnatus , who was standing by , thought he was rather profuse in his consumption of frankincense and myrrh", "context_len": 54}}}, "sparing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 52, "context": "Maggie had found that `` trudging up the hills '' took the breath out of a little girl , and papa 's words soon proved themselves true ; but she plodded along perseveringly , flushed and panting , holding to papa 's hand , and happy in her belief that she was sparing the horses by her own exertions", "context_len": 59}}}, "deposits": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "Such land is called alluvial ; it is always level , and , as it consists of successive deposits from the turbid waters of the river , made in the successive inundations , it forms always a very rich soil , deep and inexhaustible , and is , of course , extremely fertile", "context_len": 53}}}, "unexampled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Egypt has been celebrated for its unexampled fertility from the earliest times", "context_len": 12}}}, "fertility": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "Nor must it be supposed that these islands of fertility and verdure are always small", "context_len": 15}}}, "imponderable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The wind will drive away every thing imponderable , leaving only the heavy sands , to drift in storms , like fields of snow", "context_len": 24}}}, "dells": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "They are occasioned by springs which arise in little dells , and which saturate the ground with moisture for some distance around them", "context_len": 23}}}, "saturate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "They are occasioned by springs which arise in little dells , and which saturate the ground with moisture for some distance around them", "context_len": 23}}}, "irrigation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The whole tract under the influence of this irrigation clothes itself with verdure", "context_len": 13}}}, "trackless": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "They are the resort and the refuge of the traveler and the pilgrim , who seek shelter and repose upon them in their weary journeys over the trackless wilds", "context_len": 29}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "They stayed at Fort Enterprise ; but , long before the spring returned , they found their food was all but finished , and the nearest place to get more was five hundred miles away , over a trackless desert of snow", "context_len": 42}}}, "expenditures": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "His temple was in this Oasis , and it was surrounded by a considerable population , which was supported , in a great degree , by the expenditures of the worshipers who came as pilgrims , or otherwise , to sacrifice at his shrine", "context_len": 44}}}, "imparts": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "And when , at night , impressed with the sense of solemnity which night always imparts to strange and novel scenes , they looked up to the bright round moon , pleased with the expression of cheerfulness and companionship which beams always in her light , to find her suddenly waning , changing her form , withdrawing her bright beams , and looking down upon them with a lurid and murky light , it was not surprising that they felt an emotion of terror", "context_len": 84}}}, "anatomical": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "There is a peculiarity in the anatomical structure of this animal by which he can take in , at one time , a supply of water for many days", "context_len": 29}}}, "oases": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "In his native state he lives in the oases and in the valleys", "context_len": 13}}}, "exigency": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "In the exigency of the moment , however , at Alexander 's death , the generals who were in his court at the time assembled forthwith , and made an attempt to appoint some one to take the immediate command", "context_len": 40}}}, "annunciation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "The priests , after conferring in secret with the god in the temple , came out with the annunciation that Alexander was indeed his son , and they paid him , accordingly , almost divine honors", "context_len": 36}}}, "artisans": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "They do not know that they are heroes -- that 's the best of it ! It is a fact to be thankful for that everywhere throughout the land , beneath the rough jackets of our artisans and labourers , beat hearts as true and fearless as those which have stormed the fort or braved the dangers of the battlefield", "context_len": 60}}}, "commodities": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The nations exchanged their commodities far more easily and extensively in consequence of these facilities , and the means of comfort and enjoyment were multiplied and increased in thousands and thousands of huts in the great cities of Egypt , and in the rural districts along the banks of the Nile", "context_len": 51}}}, "caltrop": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "[ Footnote E : It receives its name from a kind of thistle called the caltrop", "context_len": 16}}}, "undisputed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "Darius was dead , and he was himself the undisputed master of all western Asia", "context_len": 15}}}, "redemption": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "Alexander refused to accede to any of the plans and propositions which Darius made and offered for the redemption of his wife and mother , but insisted on retaining them as his prisoners", "context_len": 33}}}, "advantageously": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "Darius 's plan was , therefore , not to intercept Alexander on his march , but to choose some great and convenient battle-field , where he could collect his forces , and marshal them advantageously , and so await an attack there", "context_len": 42}}}, "lamed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Jimmy Jones , a puny , pale-faced child of eight , permanently lamed by hip disease , which made one leg shorter than the other , was hanging on the rickety gate , as usual , and seemed to be waiting Ben \u2019 s appearance , hobbling out to meet him when he came along the road", "context_len": 57}}}, "caltrops": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Darius caused caltrops to be scattered in the grass and along the roads , wherever the army of Alexander would be likely to approach his troops on the field of battle", "context_len": 31}}}, "thistle": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "[ Footnote E : It receives its name from a kind of thistle called the caltrop", "context_len": 16}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Last Monday mornin \u2019 she break de skillet ; she kill my dog ; she put thistle under my horse \u2019 s tail when I goes to de store , so he fling me over his head -- most break my neck", "context_len": 42}}}, "rearrange": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Alexander , having crossed the river , encamped for a day or two on the banks , to rest and refresh , and to rearrange his army", "context_len": 27}}}, "predisposed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Brice 's day , predisposed the company to doubt the genuineness of Alfgar 's conversion", "context_len": 15}}}, "vastness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "It mingles with a scientific appreciation of the vastness of the movements and magnitudes by which the effect is produced , and while the one occupies the intellect , the other impresses the soul", "context_len": 34}}}, "magnitudes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "It mingles with a scientific appreciation of the vastness of the movements and magnitudes by which the effect is produced , and while the one occupies the intellect , the other impresses the soul", "context_len": 34}}}, "impresses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "It mingles with a scientific appreciation of the vastness of the movements and magnitudes by which the effect is produced , and while the one occupies the intellect , the other impresses the soul", "context_len": 34}}}, "issues": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Parmenio expressed surprise at his sleeping so quietly at a time when such vast issues were at stake", "context_len": 18}}}, "excellencies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "It was simple , compact , and whatever of value it possessed consisted in those substantial excellencies which would give the bearer the greatest efficiency on the field of battle", "context_len": 30}}}, "scythes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "They also had chariots , with scythes placed at the axles , which they were accustomed to drive among their enemies and mow them down", "context_len": 25}}}, "corrupting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "What the living men , standing erect in arms , and full of martial vigor , could not do , was easily and effectually accomplished by their dead bodies corrupting on the plain", "context_len": 33}}}, "effluvia": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "It had to be abandoned ; and the horrible effluvia and pestilence which it emitted drove all the inhabitants of the country away", "context_len": 23}}}, "reigning": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "Alexander marched his troops rapidly off the ground , leaving , as the direct result of the battle , a wide extent of country depopulated and desolate , with this vast mass of putrefaction and pestilence reigning in awful silence and solitude in the midst of it", "context_len": 47}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "We see the same analogy reigning at the present day , when the sons of the wealthy , who are born to fortune , substitute pride , and arrogance , and vicious self-indulgence and waste for the modesty , and prudence , and virtue of their sires , by means of which the fortune was acquired", "context_len": 56}}}, "spoliation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "In fact , it is probable that Alexander 's slaughter of the Persian army at Arbela , and subsequent spoliation of Susa , constitute , taken together , the most gigantic case of murder and robbery which was ever committed by man ; so that , in performing these deeds , the great hero attained at last to the glory of having perpetrated the grandest and most imposing of all human crimes", "context_len": 72}}}, "shielded": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "They are only technically shielded from being called crimes by the fact that the earth has no laws and no tribunals high enough to condemn such enormous burglaries as that of one quarter of the globe breaking violently and murderously in upon and robbing the other", "context_len": 46}}}, "burglaries": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "They are only technically shielded from being called crimes by the fact that the earth has no laws and no tribunals high enough to condemn such enormous burglaries as that of one quarter of the globe breaking violently and murderously in upon and robbing the other", "context_len": 46}}}, "barricades": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "They built walls and barricades across the narrow passages", "context_len": 9}}}, "declivities": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "There was a city on one of the declivities of the mountain , and a small river , issuing from springs in the ground , came down on the other side", "context_len": 31}}}, "impracticable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "But he had contrived to send detachments around by circuitous and precipitous paths , which even the mountaineers had deemed impracticable , and thus attack his enemies suddenly and unexpectedly from above their own positions", "context_len": 35}}}, "thais": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Thais did all in her power to captivate and please Alexander during the feast by her vivacity , her wit , her adroit attentions to him , and the display of her charms , and at length , when he himself , as well as the other guests , were excited with wine , she asked him to allow her to have the pleasure of going herself and setting fire , with her own hands , to the great palace of the Persian kings in the city", "context_len": 87}}}, "xerxes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Xerxes , who had built the great palace of Persepolis , had formerly invaded Greece and had burned Athens , and now Thais desired to burn his palace in Persepolis , to gratify her revenge , by making of its conflagration an evening spectacle to entertain the Macedonian party after their supper", "context_len": 52}}}, "incendiaries": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "There is , however , something very solemn and awful in a great conflagration at night , and very few incendiaries can gaze upon the fury of the lurid and frightful flames which they have caused to ascend without some misgivings and some remorse", "context_len": 44}}}, "ungovernable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 98, "context": "The death of Parmenio and of his son in this violent manner , when , too , there was so little evidence of their guilt , made a very general and a very unfavorable impression in respect to Alexander ; and not long afterward another case occurred , in some respects still more painful , as it evinced still more strikingly that the mind of Alexander , which had been in his earlier days filled with such noble and lofty sentiments of justice and generosity , was gradually getting to be under the supreme dominion of selfish and ungovernable passions : it was the case of Clitus", "context_len": 107}}}, "invectives": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Alexander would not comply with these wishes , but he paid her personally every attention in his power , and bore all her invectives and reproaches with great patience and good humor", "context_len": 32}}}, "signet": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Alexander went on , allowing him to read , and then , when the letter was finished he took the signet ring from his finger and pressed it upon Heph\u00e6stion 's lips , a signal for silence and secrecy", "context_len": 39}}}, "degradation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "As for myself , I might yield to the conqueror , and have him assign to me some province or kingdom to govern as his subordinate ; but I will never submit to such a degradation", "context_len": 36}}}, "delusive": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "All this apparent enthusiasm , however , was very delusive and unsubstantial", "context_len": 12}}}, "stipulate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "If Alexander should pursue him , and be likely to overtake and conquer him , he then thought that , by giving up Darius as a prisoner , he could stipulate for liberty and safety , and perhaps great rewards , both for himself and for those who acted with him", "context_len": 51}}}, "imprisoning": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 57, "context": "The painful freeing of his right hand , the fear that in the fall he might have sustained an injury that would cripple him and leave him a prey to the first beast of the jungle that might roam that way , the dread that he might never be able to free himself from those ruthless , imprisoning branches had blinded him to another and more imminent peril that threatened", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "The task of cutting away the imprisoning branches was a laborious one , flat upon his back as he was and having only the use of his left hand", "context_len": 29}}}, "nobleman": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "While halting for these foraging parties to return , a Persian nobleman came into the camp , and informed Alexander that Darius and the forces accompanying him were encamped about two days ' march in advance , but that Bessus was in command -- the conspiracy having been successful , and Darius having been deposed and made a prisoner", "context_len": 59}}}, "traitorous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "At such a time the soul turns from traitorous friends to an open foe with something like a feeling of confidence and attachment", "context_len": 23}}}, "canteens": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "They crawl along the ground to get at the canteens of their dead companions , in hopes to find , remaining in them , some drops to drink ; and if there is a little brook meandering through the battle-field , its bed gets filled and choked up with the bodies of those who crawled there , in their agony , to quench their horrible thirst , and die", "context_len": 69}}}, "prosperously": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "It was Washington 's aim to mature and perfect organizations which would move on prosperously of themselves , without him ; and he was continually withdrawing his hand from action and control in public affairs , taking a higher pleasure in the independent working of the institutions which he had formed and protected , than in exercising , himself , a high personal power", "context_len": 64}}}, "compunctions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Some compunctions of conscience may have visited his heart at seeing thus before him the ruin he had made", "context_len": 19}}}, "embalmed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "The body had been given , first , to be embalmed , according to the Egyptian and Chaldean art , and then had been placed in a sort of sarcophagus , in which it was to be conveyed to its long home", "context_len": 42}}}, "inflation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "He accordingly made floats and rafts , which were supported by skins made buoyant by inflation , or by being stuffed with straw and hay", "context_len": 25}}}, "permanence": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "It is a curious instance in proof of the permanence and stability of the great characteristics of human nature , through all the changes of civilization and lapses of time , that Bessus gave the same answer that wrong-doers almost always give when brought to account for their wrongs", "context_len": 49}}}, "stability": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "It is a curious instance in proof of the permanence and stability of the great characteristics of human nature , through all the changes of civilization and lapses of time , that Bessus gave the same answer that wrong-doers almost always give when brought to account for their wrongs", "context_len": 49}}}, "lapses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "It is a curious instance in proof of the permanence and stability of the great characteristics of human nature , through all the changes of civilization and lapses of time , that Bessus gave the same answer that wrong-doers almost always give when brought to account for their wrongs", "context_len": 49}}}, "scourged": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "Alexander ordered him to be publicly scourged ; then he caused his face to be mutilated in a manner customary in those days , when a tyrant wished to stamp upon his victim a perpetual mark of infamy", "context_len": 38}}}, "assassinates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "-- Alexander assassinates Clitus", "context_len": 4}}}, "seraglio": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "He provided himself a seraglio of three hundred and sixty young females , in whose company he spent his time , giving himself up to every form of effeminacy and dissipation", "context_len": 31}}}, "carousals": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "The decision , the energy of character , the steady pursuit of great ends by prudence , forethought , patient effort , and self-denial , all disappeared ; nothing now seemed to interest him but banquets , carousals , parties of pleasure , and whole days and nights spent in dissipation and vice", "context_len": 53}}}, "binds": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The tie which binds mere companions in pleasure together is always very slight and frail", "context_len": 15}}}, "sires": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "We see the same analogy reigning at the present day , when the sons of the wealthy , who are born to fortune , substitute pride , and arrogance , and vicious self-indulgence and waste for the modesty , and prudence , and virtue of their sires , by means of which the fortune was acquired", "context_len": 56}}}, "conspiracies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Courts and camps are always the scenes of conspiracy and treason , and Alexander was continually hearing of conspiracies and plots formed against him", "context_len": 24}}}, "originator": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "He let Peter Pope imagine that he was the originator of every idea that he , Bart himself , suggested", "context_len": 20}}}, "disbelief": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Alexander gave him his hand , said that he was convinced that he was innocent , and had acted as he did from disbelief in the existence of the conspiracy , and not from any guilty participation in it", "context_len": 39}}}, "participation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "Alexander gave him his hand , said that he was convinced that he was innocent , and had acted as he did from disbelief in the existence of the conspiracy , and not from any guilty participation in it", "context_len": 39}}}, "pulleys": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The rack is an instrument of wheels and pulleys , into which the victim is placed , and his limbs and tendons are stretched by it in a manner which produces most excruciating pain", "context_len": 34}}}, "concerted": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "He delivered his letters , and they together concerted the plans for carrying the execution into effect", "context_len": 17}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "The last word had scarcely left Bomba \u2019 s lips when a terrific chorus of yells rang out and a concerted rush of savages was made on the door of the cabin", "context_len": 32}}}, "imposture": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "He had no means of detecting the imposture , for it was very customary in those days for letters to be written by secretaries , and to be authenticated solely by the seal", "context_len": 33}}}, "authenticated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "He had no means of detecting the imposture , for it was very customary in those days for letters to be written by secretaries , and to be authenticated solely by the seal", "context_len": 33}}}, "disparage": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Alexander became excited , and began to speak , as he was now often accustomed to do , boastingly of his own exploits , and to disparage those of his father Philip in comparison", "context_len": 34}}}, "freeborn": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Clitus obeyed , saying , as he moved away , `` He is right not to bear freeborn men at his table who can only tell him the truth", "context_len": 29}}}, "outcries": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "The guests rose in confusion , and with many outcries pressed around him", "context_len": 13}}}, "criminating": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "To him Seabrooke , without waiting to be questioned , made known his complaint , and again displayed the note in proof thereof , accusing Lewis Flagg of stealing it and then placing it in his trunk for the purpose of criminating him , hoping that it might be found there before school broke up", "context_len": 55}}}, "excesses": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 54, "context": "On one of these occasions , the man who conquered drank , it is said , eighteen or twenty pints of wine , after which he lingered in misery for three days , and then died ; and more than forty others , present at the same entertainment , died in consequence of their excesses", "context_len": 55}}}, "abandons": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "-- He abandons his old friends", "context_len": 6}}}, "carousal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "-- A prolonged carousal", "context_len": 4}}}, "basso": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "-- Ornaments and basso relievos", "context_len": 5}}}, "scars": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "It is true the wounds of the battle had left scars , but they were healed or hidden by this strong-hearted woman , who seldom referred to them save in a buoyant manner", "context_len": 33}}}, "rashly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "In doing this , however , he put himself forward so rashly and inconsiderately that his ladder was broken , and while the rest retreated he was left alone upon the wall , whence he descended into the town , and was immediately surrounded by enemies", "context_len": 46}}}, "incisions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "The surgeons first very carefully cut off the wooden shaft of the arrow , and then , enlarging the wound by incisions , they drew out the barbed point", "context_len": 29}}}, "endanger": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 56, "context": "The Danes found it hard to repress their laughter at the thought of the reception which awaited the travellers at home ; they had no idea of spoiling it by attacking them , although the numbers were about equal ; besides , they had got all the plunder and spoil , and a battle would only endanger the success already obtained", "context_len": 61}}}, "pints": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "On one of these occasions , the man who conquered drank , it is said , eighteen or twenty pints of wine , after which he lingered in misery for three days , and then died ; and more than forty others , present at the same entertainment , died in consequence of their excesses", "context_len": 55}}}, "deputation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "A deputation from the council at once waited upon Edmund , and in the name of the city , and , as they took the liberty of adding , of every true man in England , they proferred him his father 's crown", "context_len": 43}}}, "foretell": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "The study of the motions and appearances of the stars , with a view to foretell the course of human affairs , was the science of astrology", "context_len": 27}}}, "prognosticate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "They naturally imagined that these changes had some connection with human affairs , and they endeavored to prognosticate from them the events , whether prosperous or adverse , which were to befall mankind", "context_len": 33}}}, "indubitable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "They informed him that they had found indubitable evidence in the stars that , if he came into Babylon , he would hazard his life", "context_len": 25}}}, "debilitating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "It was not merely by the debilitating influence of vicious indulgence on the nervous constitution that this effect was produced", "context_len": 20}}}, "predisposes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "It not only increases the power of real dangers , but predisposes the mind to all sorts of imaginary fears", "context_len": 20}}}, "monumental": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "He had a part of the city wall pulled down to furnish a site for a monumental edifice", "context_len": 18}}}, "prows": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "It was ornamented with long rows of prows of ships , taken by Alexander in his victories , and by statues , and columns , and sculptures , and gilded ornaments of every kind", "context_len": 34}}}, "sirens": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "There were images of sirens on the entablatures near the roof , which , by means of a mechanism concealed within , were made to sing dirges and mournful songs", "context_len": 30}}}, "entablatures": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "There were images of sirens on the entablatures near the roof , which , by means of a mechanism concealed within , were made to sing dirges and mournful songs", "context_len": 30}}}, "dirges": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "There were images of sirens on the entablatures near the roof , which , by means of a mechanism concealed within , were made to sing dirges and mournful songs", "context_len": 30}}}, "projector": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "There was a mountain in Greece , Mount Athos , which a certain projector said could be carved and fashioned into the form of a man -- probably in a recumbent posture", "context_len": 32}}}, "alternations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "His time was spent , in short , in strange alternations : resolution and energy in forming vast plans one day , and utter abandonment to all the excesses of dissipation and vice the next", "context_len": 35}}}, "overborne": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "It was a mournful spectacle to see his former greatness of soul still struggling on , though more and more faintly , as it became gradually overborne by the resistless inroads of intemperance and sin", "context_len": 35}}}, "evasion": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` No , '' repeated Lena , still more vexed that he should suppose her to be capable of such an evasion , which to her sense of uprightness would have been as bad as speaking a falsehood by word of mouth ; `` no , of course I did not , that would have been telling her , would it not ? One can do a falsehood as well as tell it , '' and although she had intended no reflection upon her brother , no thrust at him , Percy was ashamed as he remembered how often , during the last few months , he had done this very thing ; how he had shuffled and evaded , and thought it no great harm as long", "context_len": 128}}}, "decease": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 56, "context": "He was appointed by Dunstan , just then on the point of leaving England to escape the rage of the wicked and unhappy Edwy , and continued to exercise the authority until the year 975 , the year in which our lamented king , Edgar the Magnanimous , departed to his heavenly rest , with whose decease peace and prosperity seemed likewise to depart", "context_len": 64}}}, "anarchy": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "He never thought of the welfare of the countries which he had subjected to his sway , or did any thing to guard against the anarchy and civil wars which he knew full well would break out at once over all his vast dominions , as soon as his power came to an end", "context_len": 54}}}, "imbecile": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "He had no talents or capacity whatever , and was chosen by the rest on that very account , each one thinking that if such an imbecile as Arid\u00e6us was nominally the king , he could himself manage to get possession of the real power", "context_len": 45}}}, "inconsolable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "We should have expected that Sysigambis , the bereaved and widowed mother of Darius , would have been among those who would have exulted most highly at the conqueror 's death ; but history tells us that , instead of this , she mourned over it with a protracted and inconsolable grief", "context_len": 52}}}, "harangued": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The citizens assembled in the public places , and congratulated and harangued each other with expressions of the greatest exultation", "context_len": 20}}}, "commissioners": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "They sent commissioners into all the states of the Peloponnesus to organize a league , offensive and defensive , against Macedon", "context_len": 21}}}, "sarcophagus": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "The body had been given , first , to be embalmed , according to the Egyptian and Chaldean art , and then had been placed in a sort of sarcophagus , in which it was to be conveyed to its long home", "context_len": 42}}}, "interment": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 42, "context": "It would seem incredible that such a mind as his could really admit such an absurd superstition as the story of his divine origin , and we must therefore suppose that he gave this direction in order that the place of his interment might confirm the idea of his superhuman nature in the general opinion of mankind", "context_len": 57}}}, "overlaid": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The spokes and staves of the wheels were overlaid with gold , and the extremities of the axles , where they appeared outside at the centers of the wheels , were adorned with massive golden ornaments", "context_len": 36}}}, "centers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "The spokes and staves of the wheels were overlaid with gold , and the extremities of the axles , where they appeared outside at the centers of the wheels , were adorned with massive golden ornaments", "context_len": 36}}}, "concomitants": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "On the four sides of the carriage were basso relievos , that is , sculptured figures raised from a surface , representing Alexander himself , with various military concomitants", "context_len": 29}}}, "pendents": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Around the pavilion , too , there was a fringe or net-work of golden lace , to the pendents of which were attached bells , which tolled continually , with a mournful sound , as the carriage moved along", "context_len": 39}}}, "tolled": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "Around the pavilion , too , there was a fringe or net-work of golden lace , to the pendents of which were attached bells , which tolled continually , with a mournful sound , as the carriage moved along", "context_len": 39}}}, "interred": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "He preferred , for some reason or other , that the body should be interred in the city of Alexandria", "context_len": 20}}}, "renovation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "It is a monument , too , that time will not destroy ; its position and character , as Alexander foresaw , by bringing it a continued renovation , secure its perpetuity", "context_len": 32}}}, "perpetuity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "It is a monument , too , that time will not destroy ; its position and character , as Alexander foresaw , by bringing it a continued renovation , secure its perpetuity", "context_len": 32}}}, "typesetters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Minor changes have been made to correct typesetters ' errors , and to ensure consistent spelling and punctuation in this etext ; otherwise , every effort has been made to remain true to the original book", "context_len": 36}}}, "summaries": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The chapter summaries in this text were originally published as banners in the page headers , and have been moved to beginning of the chapter for the reader 's convenience", "context_len": 30}}}, "headers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "The chapter summaries in this text were originally published as banners in the page headers , and have been moved to beginning of the chapter for the reader 's convenience", "context_len": 30}}}, "crake": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Crake", "context_len": 1}}}, "brice": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 0, "context": "Brice ! art thou come again ? What dost thou glare at me for ? 'T was not I who defiled thy festival with blood", "context_len": 25}}}, "legitimately": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Yet the Author has distinctly felt that fiction must always , in such cases , be subordinate to truth , and that it is only legitimately used as a vehicle of instruction when it fills up the gaps in the outline , without contradicting them in any respect , or interfering with their due order and sequence", "context_len": 57}}}, "contradicting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "Yet the Author has distinctly felt that fiction must always , in such cases , be subordinate to truth , and that it is only legitimately used as a vehicle of instruction when it fills up the gaps in the outline , without contradicting them in any respect , or interfering with their due order and sequence", "context_len": 57}}}, "depicted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "The reign of Ethelred the Unready , from his accession , after the murder of his innocent brother , until the scene depicted in the nineteenth chapter of the tale , was a tragedy ever deepening", "context_len": 36}}}, "herein": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "The death of this unhappy man is taken , in all its main details , from a comparison of the chroniclers , as are also all the chief historical events herein noted", "context_len": 32}}}, "contemporary": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "The majesty of the churches was indeed ( as a contemporary wrote ) great , but thatched roofs consorted ill with the remains of shattered column and pedestal , and with the fragmentary ruins of the grand amphitheatre , which were yet partly visible , although the stones which had been brought from Bath to build it had been employed largely in church architecture", "context_len": 64}}}, "englishmen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Englishmen are henceforth at least a match for their oppressors , and the result of the long contest is the conversion of their foes to Christianity , their king setting the example , and the union of the two races -- not the submission of one to the other", "context_len": 49}}}, "incomprehensible": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "But more incomprehensible than the existence of so bad a man in such a dark age is the renewed confidence ever accorded him , when , after more than once betraying the armies of his country into the hands of their foes , and fighting openly in the hostile cause , he is again forgiven , nay , received into favour , and sent once more to command the men he has already deceived , until he repeats the experiment , and when it fails is again admitted into confidence", "context_len": 90}}}, "solutions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "To some extent the Author has endeavoured to find possible solutions of the mystery , but mystery it will remain until the day when all secrets are known", "context_len": 28}}}, "interlard": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Therefore it would be mere affectation to copy the later orthography of Chaucer , or to interlard one 's sentences with obsolete words", "context_len": 23}}}, "vicar": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "In his descriptions of Dorchester and Abingdon he has been aided by the kind information received from the present vicar of the magnificent Abbey Church , still existing in the former ancient town , and by the extensive information contained in the Chronicle of the Abbey of Abingdon , edited by the Rev", "context_len": 53}}}, "monastic": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 43, "context": "Up to this period we have availed ourselves of extracts from the Diary of Father Cuthbert ; but the events of the following four years , as recorded in that record , although full of interest for the antiquarian or the lover of monastic lore , would possess scant interest for the general reader , and have also little connection with the course of our tale ; therefore we will convey the information they contain , which properly pertains to our subject , in few words , and those our own , returning occasionally to the Diary", "context_len": 97}}}, "dedicates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The author dedicates this tale to his brother , engaged , like himself , in that most responsible task , the education of youth , in memory of those happy days when they pored together in rapturous delight over old legend or romantic lore in their father 's home at that very Clifton ( now Clifton Hampden ) familiar to hearers or readers of the tale as the home of Herstan , and the scene of the heroic defence of the English dwelling against the Danes", "context_len": 86}}}, "firesides": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "It will be a great reward for the Author 's toil should this little volume similarly gladden many firesides during the approaching Christmas , and perhaps cause some to thank God for the contrast between the Christmas of 1007 and that of 1874", "context_len": 43}}}, "thousandth": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Yet surely no records of the past , extant , tell us of such dark threatening clouds as hang over the realm of England at this time ; when the thousandth year since our blessed Lord 's nativity having passed , we seem to be entering on those awful plagues which the Apocalypse tells us must precede the consummation of all things", "context_len": 62}}}, "nativity": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "Yet surely no records of the past , extant , tell us of such dark threatening clouds as hang over the realm of England at this time ; when the thousandth year since our blessed Lord 's nativity having passed , we seem to be entering on those awful plagues which the Apocalypse tells us must precede the consummation of all things", "context_len": 62}}}, "boar": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 7, "context": "The supper was over ; the wild boar roasted whole , the huge joints of mutton and beef , the made dishes , the various preparations of milk , had disappeared , the cheerful cup was handed round ; after which the tables were removed , the gleemen sang their Christmas carols , and all went merry as a `` marriage bell", "context_len": 62}}}, "excels": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "The great hall is floored and lined with oak , while the chapel -- the Priory Church the people call it -- excels for limning and gilding , as well as for the beauty of its tapestry , any church in this part of Mercia", "context_len": 45}}}, "limning": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "The great hall is floored and lined with oak , while the chapel -- the Priory Church the people call it -- excels for limning and gilding , as well as for the beauty of its tapestry , any church in this part of Mercia", "context_len": 45}}}, "thane": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 58, "context": "Taking my life in my hands , I penetrated their fortalice , and administered the last sacrament to her ; but they threatened my life for entering their domains , and , perhaps , had I been but a simple priest , and not also , small boast as it is , the son of a powerful English thane , whom they feared to offend , I had died in doing my duty", "context_len": 73}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Elfwyn is now thane , and I , the third boy , was given to the Church , for which I had ever felt a vocation , perhaps from my love to my godfather", "context_len": 34}}}, "monastery": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 13, "context": "688 ) , was so angry at the long delay in building the monastery , that at first he revoked the grant of his predecessors to Hean , but becoming reconciled , gave all his energy to the work , and Cloveshoo x , or Abingdon , became a monastic town , and its history commences as a house of God from Ina , about A", "context_len": 66}}}, "muniment": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "The monastery is well endowed with lands by the liberality of its first founder , as appears in the deeds preserved in our great muniment chest", "context_len": 26}}}, "arable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "We have ten hides of woodland , wherein none may cut wood save for our use in the winter ; five hides of arable land , and the same extent of pasturage for cattle", "context_len": 34}}}, "serfs": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 24, "context": "Supper being ended , Elfwyn rose to go out , and his example was followed by Alfgar and Bertric , and several of the serfs , who from the lower end of the ample board had heard with much alarm the previous conversation", "context_len": 43}}}, "glebe": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Now for the care of the culture thereof we have a hundred serfs attached to the glebe , who , we trust , do not find us unkind lords", "context_len": 29}}}, "novices": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 34, "context": "We had a large flat-bottomed boat on the river which washes the monastery garden ; they had placed all the sacred vessels and the treasure of the priory therein , and had sent the novices and lay brethren to seek their safety with the rest in the woods , only the brethren , properly so called , remaining", "context_len": 58}}}, "lauds": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "We keep the monastic hours , duly rising at daybreak to sing our lauds , and lying down after compline , with the peace and blessing of Him who alone maketh us dwell in safety", "context_len": 35}}}, "compline": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 19, "context": "We keep the monastic hours , duly rising at daybreak to sing our lauds , and lying down after compline , with the peace and blessing of Him who alone maketh us dwell in safety", "context_len": 35}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Nay , I must return to compline ; I may be wanted tonight , and ought to be at my post , '' said I", "context_len": 26}}}, "nurtured": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "His household is well-ordered and happy -- nurtured in the admonition of the Lord", "context_len": 14}}}, "builder": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` Before he reached the fire , three persons had been rescued by the police , who took them down from the second-floor window by means of a builder 's ladder ; and , on his arrival , there were seven persons in the third floor , six in the left-hand window , and one in the right-hand window", "context_len": 59}}}, "maker": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "I was once offered the high office of abbot in one of our great Benedictine houses , but I wished to be near my own people and my father 's house , and here I trust I shall stay till I seek a continuing city , whose builder and maker is God", "context_len": 52}}}, "coronation": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "But upon the dying sinner 's ears it fell as an echo of the long , long past ; of that day when the litany arose before his coronation at Kingston , and the prophetic curse of Dunstan", "context_len": 38}}}, "patriot": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Many of those to whom he spoke have since died the martyr 's or the patriot 's death , but as yet no evil has reached us at Aescendune , although many parts of Wessex , nay , all the sea coast and the banks of the great rivers have been wasted with fire and sword , and the money which has been given the barbarians has been worse than wasted , for they only come for more", "context_len": 78}}}, "prospers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Nothing prospers , and thoughtless people say the saints are asleep", "context_len": 11}}}, "domestics": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "Around the priory live the serfs , the theows , and ceorls of the estate , each in his own little cottage , save the domestics , who live at the Hall , which is only half-a-mile distant", "context_len": 38}}}, "minster": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 11, "context": "The time passed rapidly away , and we went to the minster church at three , when nones and evensong were said together , for we could not keep the people till the proper hour for the latter office , owing to the darkness of November", "context_len": 46}}}, "homily": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "It was full this day at the high mass , and I preached them a homily upon the Saints , great part of which I took from a sermon I once heard the holy Dunstan preach", "context_len": 36}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "They had all duly performed the religious duties of the day , and had been greatly edified by the homily of Father Cuthbert at mass ; and now innocent mirth was to close the hallowed day -- mirth which they well believed was not alien to the birthday of Him who once sanctified the marriage festivities at Cana by His first miracle", "context_len": 62}}}, "reprisals": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Still we have many of the pagans living as settlers in our neighbourhood , whose presence is tolerated for fear of the reprisals which might follow any acts of hostility against them", "context_len": 32}}}, "boasts": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Some say he boasts of being a descendant of that Anlaf who once ravaged England , and was defeated at Brunanburgh", "context_len": 21}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "He did not fully believe Bill Mosely 's ridiculous boasts of his own prowess , but he was nevertheless disposed to overrate the man who made so many pretensions", "context_len": 29}}}, "ravaged": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "Did the English march to a town under the impression the Danes were about to attack it , they found no foe , but heard the next day that some miserable district at a distance had been cruelly ravaged", "context_len": 39}}}, "fortalice": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Taking my life in my hands , I penetrated their fortalice , and administered the last sacrament to her ; but they threatened my life for entering their domains , and , perhaps , had I been but a simple priest , and not also , small boast as it is , the son of a powerful English thane , whom they feared to offend , I had died in doing my duty", "context_len": 73}}}, "sacrament": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Taking my life in my hands , I penetrated their fortalice , and administered the last sacrament to her ; but they threatened my life for entering their domains , and , perhaps , had I been but a simple priest , and not also , small boast as it is , the son of a powerful English thane , whom they feared to offend , I had died in doing my duty", "context_len": 73}}}, "longs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Because I believe the lad longs to be a Christian , but does not dare speak to any one", "context_len": 20}}}, "discomposed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Today I noticed Alfgar , the son of Anlaf , at the high mass , and felt a little discomposed at the relaxation of discipline , which , contrary to the canons of the church , permits the unbaptized , as well as persons who ought rightly to be deemed excommunicate , or at least penitents , to be present at the holy mysteries", "context_len": 64}}}, "unbaptized": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "Today I noticed Alfgar , the son of Anlaf , at the high mass , and felt a little discomposed at the relaxation of discipline , which , contrary to the canons of the church , permits the unbaptized , as well as persons who ought rightly to be deemed excommunicate , or at least penitents , to be present at the holy mysteries", "context_len": 64}}}, "excommunicate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "Today I noticed Alfgar , the son of Anlaf , at the high mass , and felt a little discomposed at the relaxation of discipline , which , contrary to the canons of the church , permits the unbaptized , as well as persons who ought rightly to be deemed excommunicate , or at least penitents , to be present at the holy mysteries", "context_len": 64}}}, "penitents": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 55, "context": "Today I noticed Alfgar , the son of Anlaf , at the high mass , and felt a little discomposed at the relaxation of discipline , which , contrary to the canons of the church , permits the unbaptized , as well as persons who ought rightly to be deemed excommunicate , or at least penitents , to be present at the holy mysteries", "context_len": 64}}}, "worshippers": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "I marked that after service he entered the woods , as if he shunned the society of his fellow worshippers , and there I followed him , coming upon him at last , as if by accident , in a chestnut glade , the leaves of which strewed the ground -- emblem of our fading mortality", "context_len": 56}}}, "lineaments": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "He has a most attractive face -- so thoughtful , yet so manly ; his mother 's gentle lineaments seem to have tempered the somewhat fierce and haughty bearing of his sire , as they meet in the countenance of their child", "context_len": 42}}}, "exhort": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "Early in the morning , before the high mass , whereat the neighbourhood is generally present , I received a missive from the sheriff , bidding me , in the name of the King , to exhort my people to remain at home tonight , since danger is afoot , and there is likely , he says , to be a rising on the part of the pagans who dwell amongst us", "context_len": 72}}}, "hurtful": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "So I preached upon the Saint of the day , who was pre-eminently a man of peace , and I took occasion to tell my people that there were many hurtful men about , who , like their master , Satan , were seeking whom they might devour , and that , like that master , they chose the night for their misdeeds , seeing they loved darkness rather than light", "context_len": 71}}}, "bellwether": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "I see him -- the black bellwether", "context_len": 7}}}, "discoursing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "Most of them evidently were men of peace , but not all , as I have already hinted ; and , as I drew near a group standing behind the great yew tree , I heard one of these latter discoursing to his fellows", "context_len": 44}}}, "cutthroat": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Why should not the Englishman be a match for the Dane ? ay , more than a match for the cutthroat heathen ? Here we stand on a rock with our defence secure ; and here we will live or die in defence of our women and children", "context_len": 48}}}, "blasphemy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Just then I interrupted them , for I could no longer hear the blasphemy", "context_len": 14}}}, "outrages": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` What new outrages have occurred ? '' I asked", "context_len": 10}}}, "pardonable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "I hardly knew what to answer , the outrage was so recent , and the excitement of the speaker so pardonable , as I could but feel", "context_len": 27}}}, "holier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` But , my boy , there must be yet a higher , a holier motive", "context_len": 16}}}, "evensong": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` Then you must come home with me , and share the noon meat , after which I can give you my time until evensong", "context_len": 25}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "The time passed rapidly away , and we went to the minster church at three , when nones and evensong were said together , for we could not keep the people till the proper hour for the latter office , owing to the darkness of November", "context_len": 46}}}, "nones": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "The time passed rapidly away , and we went to the minster church at three , when nones and evensong were said together , for we could not keep the people till the proper hour for the latter office , owing to the darkness of November", "context_len": 46}}}, "catechumen": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "He made no objection , and we returned to the Priory together , where he took his noon meat in the guest chamber , and I devoted all the time between the meal and nones to an examination of my catechumen", "context_len": 41}}}, "mazy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 75, "context": "It was a dark and gloomy eventide : the sun , which had only made its appearance at intervals during the day , was fast sinking behind a heavy bank of clouds which filled the western horizon ; and the wind , which was freshening to a gale , seemed to bear the storm onward in its track , while it tore the few surviving leaves rudely from the trees , and whirled them in mazy windings", "context_len": 77}}}, "underlings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` I can not divine , unless he has some deed of blood on hand which he wishes to have undisturbed , all to himself and his underlings", "context_len": 28}}}, "consenting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "But I will do violence to myself , and will write all things accurately , in order it may serve to show that there were those amongst us who were not consenting parties , who entered not into the counsels of those men of blood , whom may God `` reward after their deeds , and according to the wickedness of their own inventions", "context_len": 64}}}, "ceorl": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` They dared to say , '' replied the ceorl who had before spoken , `` they had the sanction of the king", "context_len": 23}}}, "sanction": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` They dared to say , '' replied the ceorl who had before spoken , `` they had the sanction of the king", "context_len": 23}}}, "sacrilege": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 62, "context": "I could not remain at the hall myself , for , on such a night , it seemed necessary to be with my own people , and to be ready to seize any opportunity of saving the effusion of blood , or of giving protection to any who might seek refuge under the shelter of our roof , where murder would be sacrilege , a consideration of some importance where Christians , shame to say , were the murderers", "context_len": 79}}}, "debauches": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` I do not think he will ; for after one of these debauches he generally sleeps far into the next day", "context_len": 22}}}, "portentous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "The novices and lay brothers , ignorant of the peril , had retired to rest ; but we , who knew the portentous state of things around us , could not have slept had we retired", "context_len": 36}}}, "leaded": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "It was so windy that we could hardly stand upon the leaded roof , and although we gazed around , nought met our eyes until we were on the point of returning", "context_len": 32}}}, "cowls": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "So , putting our cowls over our heads , we sallied forth into the black night -- black and dark save where the light of the fire illumined the horizon , and even cast a faint ray upon our own path", "context_len": 41}}}, "podagra": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "He had the podagra , ( or gout ) , and ought not to have ventured forth ; but zeal would not let him rest", "context_len": 25}}}, "hedged": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Verily our path is hedged about with thorns", "context_len": 9}}}, "chamberlain": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 19, "context": "Loud ravings , as of one in delirium , penetrated the passage from the chamber , amidst which the chamberlain knocked again", "context_len": 22}}}, "abjured": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "I held up the crucifix , which hung at my girdle , on high ; I threw my arm over his head , and abjured them under the name of Christ , and as they feared the curse of the Church , to forbear", "context_len": 44}}}, "luridly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "He could bear it no longer , the contrast was too painful , he must break the sweet charm , the hallowed song , for the sky was reddening yet more luridly behind him , and each moment he expected to see Dorchester burst forth into flames", "context_len": 47}}}, "theology": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` The good prior wishes me to talk theology", "context_len": 9}}}, "delirium": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 25, "context": "At first the events of that fearful night seemed all like a dream , and mingled themselves with the strange spectres which haunted me in delirium ; but afterwards the real separated itself from the unreal , and I knew that my father and all his friends , my Danish uncles amongst them , had perished with the whole household assembled there that fatal day", "context_len": 65}}}, "frailer": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 55, "context": "The old pile had been badly preserved by the Saxon conquerors , but it had been built of that solid architecture which seems almost to defy the assaults of time , and which in some cases , after fifteen centuries , preserves all its characteristics , and promises yet to preserve them , when our frailer erections lie crumbled in the dust", "context_len": 62}}}, "spiritually": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "It seemed useless to delay longer , as he was fully prepared both intellectually and spiritually , nay , has been so for some time , only the tragic event which deprived him of his Danish kinsfolk had distracted him for a time from spiritual things", "context_len": 46}}}, "commentary": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Such a commentary was the massacre of St", "context_len": 8}}}, "pertains": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 78, "context": "Up to this period we have availed ourselves of extracts from the Diary of Father Cuthbert ; but the events of the following four years , as recorded in that record , although full of interest for the antiquarian or the lover of monastic lore , would possess scant interest for the general reader , and have also little connection with the course of our tale ; therefore we will convey the information they contain , which properly pertains to our subject , in few words , and those our own , returning occasionally to the Diary", "context_len": 97}}}, "paragraphs": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 1, "context": "Other paragraphs in the report read thus : `` Received anonymously five large cheeses ; received a box of dessert knives and forks , a cruet , a silver soup ladle and a silver cup ; from Clifton , twelve tons house coals ; from Bedminster , a monster loaf , 200 lbs", "context_len": 53}}}, "ealdorman": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 73, "context": "On the third or fourth day of the feast he took him to hunt in a wood where he had prepared an ambuscade , and while all the rest were engaged in the chase , the common hangman of Shrewsbury , one Godwin `` port hund , '' or the town 's hound , bribed by Edric to commit the crime , sprang from behind a bush , and foully assassinated the innocent ealdorman", "context_len": 74}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "Edric 's eloquence , and the strong circumstantial evidence against the prisoner , carried the day , and the ealdorman even proposed that execution should be speedy , `` lest , '' he whispered , `` Canute should interfere to screen his instrument", "context_len": 43}}}, "pillaged": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "They took the money , laughing at his simplicity , and three weeks afterwards pillaged Thetford , and burnt it", "context_len": 20}}}, "locusts": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "The following year a famine so severe visited England , that even the Danes forebore to ravage so poor a land ; but in 1006 , the next year , they overspread Wessex like locusts", "context_len": 35}}}, "domesticated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` Yes , my boy -- and in a wild state they keep themselves clean , but when domesticated they ca n't", "context_len": 22}}}, "penitential": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "At this time there was some hope amongst the people that God had at length heard the petition breathed so often in the penitential wail of the Litany -- '' From the cruelty of our pagan enemies , good Lord , deliver us '' -- and they forgot that the massacre on St", "context_len": 53}}}, "rick": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 62, "context": "It was a fine summer 's evening towards the end of the month of July , and the sun was slowly setting behind the wood-crowned range of hills in the west , where the forest terminated the pastures of Aescendune ; the cattle were returning to their stalls ; the last load of hay was being transferred from the wain to the rick , and all things spoke of the calm and rest of a sweet night , fragrant with the breath of honeysuckle and wild brier , when nature herself seems to court luxurious repose", "context_len": 96}}}, "tolling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The priory bell was tolling for compline , and thither many of the people , released from their labour , were wending their way", "context_len": 24}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The tolling of the alarm bells , the sound of distant shouts , the blowing of trumpets rolled in a confused flood of noise across the intervening space -- a distance of between two and three miles -- and manifested the intense alarm of the city , so cruelly aroused from dreams of peace", "context_len": 54}}}, "wending": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "He felt that every dollar he earned brought nearer the day when he would feel justified in turning his back upon the gold-fields of California and wending his homeward way to Hampton", "context_len": 32}}}, "nightingale": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Star after star peeped out ; one sweet-voiced nightingale began her song , trilling through the air ; another enviously took up the strain", "context_len": 24}}}, "tuneful": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Hilda thought the earth had never seemed so much like heaven , and she imagined the tuneful birds sang their vesper song in union with the monks , whose solemn and plaintive chant awoke the echoes of the priory church", "context_len": 40}}}, "vesper": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Hilda thought the earth had never seemed so much like heaven , and she imagined the tuneful birds sang their vesper song in union with the monks , whose solemn and plaintive chant awoke the echoes of the priory church", "context_len": 40}}}, "trackway": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "I saw one at the summit of the hills which the trackway crosses between our county and Oxfordshire , when I last returned form Beranbyrig v , and I think that one gives the present alarm", "context_len": 36}}}, "watchtower": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Ascending the hill , they directed their steps towards the highest point , where an old watchtower had once been reared , composed of timber , and overlooking the forest", "context_len": 30}}}, "vassals": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Summon and arm all our vassals , and await the sheriff 's orders ; the king will communicate to us through him", "context_len": 23}}}, "baleful": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "There he sat motionless for a long time , trying to divine by what baleful freak of circumstances he had been brought to this wretched plight ; but , without knowledge of the facts to work upon , he found himself floundering helplessly and blindly in a mire of uncertainty", "context_len": 50}}}, "tempts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Wessex scarcely tempts the plunderer now ; neither does East Anglia", "context_len": 11}}}, "plunderer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Wessex scarcely tempts the plunderer now ; neither does East Anglia", "context_len": 11}}}, "matin": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "The dawn was already breaking in the east , and even as he gazed upon the purpling skies the birds began their matin songs of praise , and the valley awoke", "context_len": 31}}}, "episcopal": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "The sealed packet only contained a formal summons to the general rendezvous of the forces , which was to take place at Dorchester , the episcopal city of the great Midland diocese , and situated in a central position , where Wessex and Mercia could easily unite the flower of their youth", "context_len": 52}}}, "spouses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Husbands had to bid the last goodbye -- it might be the very last -- to their spouses , sons to their aged parents , fathers to their children", "context_len": 29}}}, "grandsire": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 86, "context": "But alas ! there were no traditions of victory to encourage them ; only gloomy remembrances of defeat ; and , but for the stern call of duty which bade them , as men and Christians , go to the succour of their brethren , the majority would have preferred to remain at home and abide the worst , although they knew full well that submission utterly failed to mitigate the ferocious cruelty of their oppressors , who slew alike the innocent babe and the grey-haired grandsire", "context_len": 87}}}, "foully": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 69, "context": "On the third or fourth day of the feast he took him to hunt in a wood where he had prepared an ambuscade , and while all the rest were engaged in the chase , the common hangman of Shrewsbury , one Godwin `` port hund , '' or the town 's hound , bribed by Edric to commit the crime , sprang from behind a bush , and foully assassinated the innocent ealdorman", "context_len": 74}}}, "assassinated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 70, "context": "On the third or fourth day of the feast he took him to hunt in a wood where he had prepared an ambuscade , and while all the rest were engaged in the chase , the common hangman of Shrewsbury , one Godwin `` port hund , '' or the town 's hound , bribed by Edric to commit the crime , sprang from behind a bush , and foully assassinated the innocent ealdorman", "context_len": 74}}}, "garners": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "God had blessed our increase , and our garners were full with all manner of store ; women and children had mainly been the reapers , but the Lady Hilda herself had been present amongst them , and so had her daughter , my niece , Ethelgiva , even sometimes labouring with their own hands", "context_len": 55}}}, "sportsmen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 81, "context": "Two young theows , whose fathers had gone to the war , but who had been left behind as being too young to share its dangers , although in the flush of early youth , accompanied them , and were soon loaded with the lighter game their masters had killed , while a deer they had slain was hung in the trees , where a wolf could not reach it , and where wayfarers were not likely to pass until the sportsmen should return for their own", "context_len": 87}}}, "footmarks": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "I had already interrogated the two churls ; their story was but too evidently true ; and I learned that they had discovered the footmarks of the Northmen in crossing a ford that afterwards , while returning hastily home , they stumbled upon them , and Alfgar and Bertric were taken", "context_len": 51}}}, "picketed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "But all their horses were left picketed by the stream , under the care of three of the youngest warriors , and there Alfgar was left , safely bound to a tree , for his captors could not trust him", "context_len": 40}}}, "distanced": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 45, "context": "`` Well , yes , Bessie , '' she said , with some seeming reluctance ; `` since you ask me so plainly , I must say that had Lena been able to continue in competition with the rest , I think she would have distanced every one", "context_len": 48}}}, "wounding": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 43, "context": "It is only just over a hundred years since John Howard died ; yet in his day persons could be put to death for stealing a horse or a sheep , for robbing dwellings , for defrauding creditors , for forgery , for wounding deer , for killing or maiming cattle , for stealing goods to the value of five shillings , or even for cutting a band in a hop plantation", "context_len": 72}}}, "solicitations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "He tried to assist him , and turned a deaf ear to all solicitations for the few moments that they could have availed", "context_len": 23}}}, "baffling": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Brice 's night , baffling his would-be assassins , and yet lives ? He thought thee dead , and only sought vengeance , when he heard from the captured prisoner of Elfwyn 's band that thou wert yet alive , and he is come to seek thee", "context_len": 47}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "I seized upon it with avidity , for I was assured it would prove of vast importance in the solution of the dark and baffling mystery", "context_len": 26}}}, "assassins": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Brice 's night , baffling his would-be assassins , and yet lives ? He thought thee dead , and only sought vengeance , when he heard from the captured prisoner of Elfwyn 's band that thou wert yet alive , and he is come to seek thee", "context_len": 47}}}, "writhes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` Does the eagle mourn over the death of the dove , or heed what pangs the kid may suffer which writhes beneath its talons ? If you are of the race of warrior kings , act like one", "context_len": 39}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "And while he holds that slippery throat with the clutch of desperation , as the snake twists and writhes and tries to bite , it may be well , for the benefit of those who have not read the preceding volumes of this series , to tell who Bomba was and what had been his adventures up to the time at which this story opens", "context_len": 65}}}, "sinew": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "We can not enter more closely into the secret which gave the martyr his strength ; we know not the visions of heavenly joy which may have overpowered the present pain , we know not whether He who gave this elaborate framework of flesh and blood , nerve and sinew , miraculously suspended the full operation of His laws , as is elsewhere recorded of other martyrs", "context_len": 67}}}, "miraculously": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 51, "context": "We can not enter more closely into the secret which gave the martyr his strength ; we know not the visions of heavenly joy which may have overpowered the present pain , we know not whether He who gave this elaborate framework of flesh and blood , nerve and sinew , miraculously suspended the full operation of His laws , as is elsewhere recorded of other martyrs", "context_len": 67}}}, "vikings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "It had often been a sharp struggle to him , nursed in heroic times , learned in battle songs , and of the very blood of the vikings , to avoid the feeling that Christianity was not the religion of the brave ; now the difficulty was over , and who shall say that the first joy of the martyr 's soul was not the knowledge that his sufferings had already borne such fruit to God !", "context_len": 77}}}, "forewarned": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "So soon as I saw the party safely away from the town , I left them under the guidance of some ancient foresters , who knew every woodland path , and hastened to my brethren , who had been duly forewarned , and were awaiting my arrival", "context_len": 47}}}, "dovecote": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 66, "context": "`` Here , then , my boy , on this spot where thou wert once cradled , renounce all these Christian follies and superstitions , and thou shalt go back with me to the camp of King Sweyn , where thou shalt be received as the descendant of warrior kings , and shalt forget that thou , the falcon , wert ever the inmate of the dovecote", "context_len": 67}}}, "inopportunely": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Alfgar sighed , and accompanied his father , so inopportunely found , back to the camp", "context_len": 16}}}, "conservatives": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 60, "context": "All his heart was with his English friends ; he felt that in going to the Danish camp he was really going to his death , for although within a few years the conversion of the Northmen took place , yet at this period their hatred of Christianity was simply ferocious , and his father belonged to the old heathen conservatives of his day , as did all his kinsfolk", "context_len": 70}}}, "heaths": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Before them stretched an open country , where wild heaths alternated with cornfields , and wooded hills were of frequent occurrence upon the landscape", "context_len": 24}}}, "unopposed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Their passage had been quite unopposed ; all the fighting men were in Wessex ; and those who had seen the Danish party had fled with terror -- they had not stopped long to plunder , but had speared one or two unfortunate victims who fell in their way , a sight which sickened Alfgar", "context_len": 55}}}, "speared": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "Their passage had been quite unopposed ; all the fighting men were in Wessex ; and those who had seen the Danish party had fled with terror -- they had not stopped long to plunder , but had speared one or two unfortunate victims who fell in their way , a sight which sickened Alfgar", "context_len": 55}}}, "distancing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "The following day they continued their march to the southeast , sometimes hiding in woods , for the country was mainly occupied by Ethelred 's troops ; sometimes pursued by larger bodies of horsemen , but always successful in distancing them , until , at the approach of eventide , they came in sight of the entrenched camp of the northern host", "context_len": 62}}}, "sparingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Entering , they beheld a strange scene : huts rudely constructed of the branches of trees , intermingled sparingly with tents , were disposed at regular intervals", "context_len": 27}}}, "renounced": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "But the ferocious warriors rebelled against him , and were headed by his unnatural son , Sweyn , who , although baptized , renounced Christianity , and fought to restore the bloodstained worship so congenial to the heart of a sea king", "context_len": 42}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Benjamin found the aroma of frying fish so tempting that he there and then renounced Mr", "context_len": 16}}}, "bloodstained": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "But the ferocious warriors rebelled against him , and were headed by his unnatural son , Sweyn , who , although baptized , renounced Christianity , and fought to restore the bloodstained worship so congenial to the heart of a sea king", "context_len": 42}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Get those wounds dressed at once , he ordered the eight bloodstained ragamuffins who faced him near Rosy \u2019 s shell-torn fuselage", "context_len": 22}}}, "sussex": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Sussex and Kent were now wholly powerless ; East Anglia was little better ; Wessex trembled , for every inlet was a path for the robbers , and the turn of Mercia drew near", "context_len": 34}}}, "ravens": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Bravely , my king , the ravens have tasted flesh", "context_len": 11}}}, "ungracefully": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The youth made his salutations , not ungracefully , yet with an air of reserve which the king noticed", "context_len": 19}}}, "curing": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` But why should you wish for a fault which would grieve your friends and trouble yourself only that you may be at the pains of curing it , Maggie ? You have faults enough , dear ; and if they are not what may be called very terrible , they are quite serious enough to need all your attention , and you should be thankful that it does not require a harder struggle to overcome them", "context_len": 77}}}, "aspen": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "`` Yes , '' said the king ; `` we know a way of curing the folly , '' when , even as he spoke , a spasm , as of mental agony , passed over him , and he shook like an aspen , but it was gone in a minute", "context_len": 52}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "He was about to launch his battle-axe through the air in search of the daring minstrel , when the same dread expression of unutterable agony we have before mentioned passed over his face ; he trembled as an aspen , and sank , as one paralysed , into his chair , while his glaring eyes seemed to behold some horrid apparition unseen by all beside", "context_len": 65}}}, "appertained": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "The quarters of Anlaf were in the northwestern angle of the camp ; they consisted of huts hastily constructed from the material which the neighbouring woods supplied , and one or two tents , the best of which , stolen property , appertained to the chieftain", "context_len": 46}}}, "hares": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "The sun was now sinking behind the huge rounded forms of some chalk hills in the west , when the camp became gradually illuminated by the light of numberless fires , whereat oxen were roasted whole , and partridges and hares by the dozen , for the Danes were voracious in their appetites", "context_len": 53}}}, "bards": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Alfgar seemed the only silent member of the company ; the warriors related their successes , and boasted of their exploits , and the bards sang their ferocious ditties , until all were tired , and the quiet moon looked down upon the sleeping camp", "context_len": 45}}}, "ditties": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Alfgar seemed the only silent member of the company ; the warriors related their successes , and boasted of their exploits , and the bards sang their ferocious ditties , until all were tired , and the quiet moon looked down upon the sleeping camp", "context_len": 45}}}, "passionless": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "He could not help looking as if to take leave of his father ; but Anlaf stood as mute and passionless as a statue", "context_len": 24}}}, "gleeman": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 65, "context": "He told his tale , how he had conceived suspicions that the Danes intended a winter descent ; how he had risked his life ( in the exuberance of youthful daring ) to ascertain the truth ; how , trusting to his knowledge of Carisbrooke , wherein he had spent many pleasant days in his boyhood , he had ventured amongst the Danes as a gleeman , in imitation of Alfred of old ; how there he had assisted , unsuspected , at a meeting of the council in the great hall , and heard it decided to invade England , and finally how he had escaped", "context_len": 107}}}, "churls": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "I had already interrogated the two churls ; their story was but too evidently true ; and I learned that they had discovered the footmarks of the Northmen in crossing a ford that afterwards , while returning hastily home , they stumbled upon them , and Alfgar and Bertric were taken", "context_len": 51}}}, "spawn": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "Fifty men ! and how could we resist them ? The poor old gleemen expressed their readiness to fight for the old hall , and so did even the boys ; but these accursed pagans are the very spawn of the evil one , and fight like fiends , whom they equal in skill , so that I saw at once there was no chance in resistance", "context_len": 67}}}, "morasses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "In the depths of the forest , about ten miles from Aescendune , in the opposite direction to that in which the enemy lay , is a solitary valley , surrounded by such morasses and quagmires that only those who know the paths could safely journey thither", "context_len": 47}}}, "quagmires": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "In the depths of the forest , about ten miles from Aescendune , in the opposite direction to that in which the enemy lay , is a solitary valley , surrounded by such morasses and quagmires that only those who know the paths could safely journey thither", "context_len": 47}}}, "forester": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "But the valley is fertile , and my father years ago built a substantial farm house with outbuildings there , which has ever since been occupied by our chief forester", "context_len": 30}}}, "pastimes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "In such a way only could they hope to escape the wretches to whom bloodshed and cruelty are pastimes", "context_len": 19}}}, "foresters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "So soon as I saw the party safely away from the town , I left them under the guidance of some ancient foresters , who knew every woodland path , and hastened to my brethren , who had been duly forewarned , and were awaiting my arrival", "context_len": 47}}}, "dimmer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The light of the conflagration grew dimmer as we rowed down the stream , but it still lighted up the heavens with an angry glare", "context_len": 25}}}, "redness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "All the household were buried in sleep when we knocked at the doors -- a proof that they had not observed the redness in the skies , or little sleep , I trow , would they have taken", "context_len": 38}}}, "trow": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "All the household were buried in sleep when we knocked at the doors -- a proof that they had not observed the redness in the skies , or little sleep , I trow , would they have taken", "context_len": 38}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` Me trow dem at Patty , '' said Franky", "context_len": 10}}}, "irreverently": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Now the saints help thy lying tongue , '' thus irreverently he spoke , `` do holy men travel like robbers in dead of night ? ''", "context_len": 28}}}, "hasted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "His wife hasted to light a good fire , and to prepare us a breakfast ; in short , we had fallen amongst the faithful , and we met great hospitality , for which may God repay the worthy host , Goodman Wiglaf", "context_len": 43}}}, "glades": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "So we embraced each other and parted ; and we two watched , with loving hearts , until the glades of the forest hid our brethren , dear to us in the Lord , from our sight , dimmed as were our eyes with tears", "context_len": 45}}}, "disbelieve": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` He affected to disbelieve the intelligence , and said sarcastically that the safety of Wessex could not be neglected for Aescendune", "context_len": 22}}}, "edmund": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 0, "context": "Edmund has lost many men in the course of the last few months ; and with the remainder he hid in our woods , ready to protect us `` to the last breath , '' as he said , `` in his body", "context_len": 43}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Edmund rushed to command the defence at the gate , leaving Alfgar to superintend that upon the right hand , and Herstan on the left", "context_len": 25}}}, "churl": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Then the words of the churl Beorn , who had been taken prisoner , as the messenger had told us , came fresh to my mind", "context_len": 26}}}, "inquires": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Nay , all the camp inquires", "context_len": 7}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Only do not tarry ; Sweyn himself inquires how long his obstinacy is to be endured", "context_len": 17}}}, "funereal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "So we took one of our ships , stuffed it full with things that would burn easily , made a funereal pile on the deck , and laid him thereon in state , with a mantle fit for a king thrown over him", "context_len": 43}}}, "stoicism": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The old warrior was silent , and , in spite of his stoicism , Anlaf thought a tear stood in his eye", "context_len": 22}}}, "begotten": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` All fudge and priestly pratings , begotten of idleness and dreams", "context_len": 12}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "It is 'God so loved the world that he gave his only begotten Son , that whosoever believeth in him should not perish , but have eternal life", "context_len": 28}}}, "valhalla": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Valhalla and Niffelheim are much more reasonable ; at all events they are parts of a creed which has made its followers the masters of the world", "context_len": 27}}}, "tapered": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` It was in the form of an enormous serpent ; the sharp head formed the prow , with hissing tongue protruding forth , and the long tail tapered over the poop", "context_len": 32}}}, "wintered": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "Eight years before , in the year 998 , they had wintered on the island , and since that time had regarded it as a Danish colony", "context_len": 27}}}, "situate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The king and his favourite chieftains took up their abode at Carisbrooke , situate about eight miles up the stream , but above the spot where it ceases to be navigable", "context_len": 31}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The ferryman , whose hut is situate at the bend of the river below the Synodune hills , where people cross for Wittenham , says that late on the night in question a boat with four people passed down the river , and that it struck him that one only rowed , while two of the rest seemed guarding the fourth passenger", "context_len": 62}}}, "invigorating": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "The beautiful coasts and inlets were crowded with villas , and invalids then , as now , sought the invigorating breezes , from all parts of the island of Britain , and even from the neighbouring province of Gaul", "context_len": 39}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Alfgar drank moderately , for sooth to say it was invigorating and welcome that cold day , but Higbald finished the bowl then and there , and then staggering down , drew the outer bolt in such a way that it missed the staple , which fact he was too drunk to perceive", "context_len": 53}}}, "villa": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "The ramparts whereon he was standing were of Roman workmanship , built so solidly that they had resisted every attack of man or of time ; while down below lay the ruins of a magnificent villa , once occupied by the Roman governor of the island", "context_len": 46}}}, "mimic": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` And the king proposes to enrol you amongst his chosen warriors on that day ; he has marked the skill you have displayed in the mimic contests with spear or sword , your skill as a horseman , and he wishes to see whether in actual battle you will fulfil the promise of the parade ground", "context_len": 57}}}, "apostasy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 54, "context": "But two days , and the dread choice had to be made -- the crisis in the life of Alfgar , a crisis which has its parallel in the lives of many around us -- approached , and he had to choose between Christ and Odin , between the death of the martyr and apostasy", "context_len": 55}}}, "scalds": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Again Odin and Frea , with their children , and the heroes sung by the scalds , in the war songs which he heard echoing from around the fire at that moment :", "context_len": 33}}}, "sensuality": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Oh , how hollow the mirth and revelry that night ! How he loathed the singing , the drunken shouting , the fierce imprecation over the wine cup -- the sensuality , which now distinguished his bloodthirsty companions", "context_len": 38}}}, "debauch": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 47, "context": "`` Upwards it leads to the banqueting hall , and you can comprehend my escape this evening , '' said he ; `` but our path is now downwards , unless you would like to go up and see the drunken beasts of murderers snoring off their debauch upon the floor as they fell ; oh , that it were lawful for a Christian man to cut their throats as they lie ; many innocent lives would be saved thereby , which those brutes will live to destroy", "context_len": 88}}}, "maniacal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The eyes , now weak with debauch , had glowed with the maniacal fury of the berserkir in the battlefield", "context_len": 20}}}, "parricide": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 44, "context": "`` Because I know the secrets of the prison house -- knew them before the Danes had murdered the once happy dwellers in this garden of England , which they have made a howling wilderness ; hence I escaped the wrath of the furious parricide , whom the saints destroy , with ease , and laughed in security at their vain efforts to take me ; but we must waste no time ; it yet wants five hours to daybreak ; within those five hours we must reach the opposite shore", "context_len": 91}}}, "ode": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "But in the innermost chamber of this awful home was the abode of Raging Despair ; and in the final verse of his terrible ode the scald sang :", "context_len": 29}}}, "scald": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "But in the innermost chamber of this awful home was the abode of Raging Despair ; and in the final verse of his terrible ode the scald sang :", "context_len": 29}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Charlie crammed the brush under the pot to heat water to scald the birds , that they might pick them the faster", "context_len": 22}}}, "retributive": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Alfgar had seen the apostate in his moment of retributive agony , and he shuddered", "context_len": 15}}}, "superseding": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "He drank , drank until it was empty , then fell back and reposed with an idiotic smile superseding the ferocious expression his face had so lately worn", "context_len": 28}}}, "erections": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 56, "context": "The old pile had been badly preserved by the Saxon conquerors , but it had been built of that solid architecture which seems almost to defy the assaults of time , and which in some cases , after fifteen centuries , preserves all its characteristics , and promises yet to preserve them , when our frailer erections lie crumbled in the dust", "context_len": 62}}}, "tiling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "The roof was semicircular , and composed of minute bricks , seeming to form one solid mass ; the floor of tiling , arranged in patterns , which could still be obscurely traced by the light of the lamp left by the charity of Sidroc to the prisoner ; for the dungeon was of bad reputation ; lights had been seen there at unearthly hours , when the outer door was fast and no inmate existed", "context_len": 76}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "A company built it five years ago with grand plans for shipping mottled tiling slate all over the country", "context_len": 19}}}, "flooring": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "Mechanically he cast his eyes around the cell , and tried to trace out the pattern of the flooring , when he saw that the central figure , around which the circles and squares converged , was justice , with the scales , and the motto , `` Fiat justitia", "context_len": 50}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "My young master was intensely interested as his uncle talked to him about the width of stalls , the size of windows , the proper kind of concrete flooring which should have wooden stall racks , the ventilating flues and rolling doors", "context_len": 42}}}, "converged": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "Mechanically he cast his eyes around the cell , and tried to trace out the pattern of the flooring , when he saw that the central figure , around which the circles and squares converged , was justice , with the scales , and the motto , `` Fiat justitia", "context_len": 50}}}, "justitia": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "Mechanically he cast his eyes around the cell , and tried to trace out the pattern of the flooring , when he saw that the central figure , around which the circles and squares converged , was justice , with the scales , and the motto , `` Fiat justitia", "context_len": 50}}}, "omnipotent": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "`` He knew the meaning of the words , for Father Cuthbert had taught him some Latin , and the conviction flashed upon him that , sooner or later , all the wrong and evil about him would be righted by the power of a judge as omnipotent as unerring", "context_len": 50}}}, "parental": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "For the shame which he unwillingly brought upon a father who was stern , yet not unkind or void of parental love , was the bitterest ingredient in the cup", "context_len": 30}}}, "ingredient": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "For the shame which he unwillingly brought upon a father who was stern , yet not unkind or void of parental love , was the bitterest ingredient in the cup", "context_len": 30}}}, "borderland": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "And while he slept he was wakened , yet but partly wakened , by a voice which seemed to belong to the borderland 'twixt sleep and waking", "context_len": 27}}}, "acclivity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Climbing out with some difficulty , and disturbing in the process hundreds of bat-mice and not a few rats , they found themselves in the midst of some old ruins at the foot of the acclivity whereon the fortress was built , and below them the brook ran rapidly to join the river", "context_len": 53}}}, "gory": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` Now the warrior 's cup of joy was full , When he drank the blood of his foe , Where the slain lay thick on the gory hill , And torrents of blood from every rill reddened the river below , For Odin 's hall is the Northman 's heaven -- ''", "context_len": 53}}}, "assaulted": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "In the early dawn of that day the Mahdi assaulted the town in overwhelming force -- whether helped by treachery is not exactly known ; and before his well-fed , well-trained hosts , the feeble worn-out garrison gave way , the walls were scaled , the city taken , and the hero who had won the affection of many nations fell amidst the people he had come to save", "context_len": 69}}}, "vipers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 48, "context": "He told how the fierce Ragnar sailed for England , how his fleet was wrecked , but still how , with the relics of his forces , he assaulted Northumbria , and was taken captive by Ella the king , who threw him into a hole filled with vipers and toads", "context_len": 51}}}, "enfolded": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "The light was gradually growing stronger , and their chance of escape seemed feeble , when Alfgar saw before them a dense cloud of mist rolling round the eastern promontory , and uttered a cry of joy as it enfolded them", "context_len": 41}}}, "sucker": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Mickey , hand me that fish we didn \u2019 t cook , and I \u2019 ll show Lieutenant Newton just what sort of a sucker he is to doubt your word", "context_len": 31}}}, "parricides": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "Go , and tell King Sweyn that Edmund viii the Etheling , son of Ethelred of England , has been his gleeman , and hopes he enjoyed the song which told the doom of parricides", "context_len": 35}}}, "stationing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "After the death of Birinus , the zeal of his successors failed to evangelise the southeastern districts of Wessex , until , at length , came Wilfrid , fervent in zeal , and , stationing himself at Selsey , near Chichester , evangelised both Sussex and Wessex , sending out missionaries like-minded with himself , even into the most inaccessible wilds", "context_len": 61}}}, "viceroys": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Centwin was then king of Sussex , but various petty states were tributary to him , and ruled by viceroys", "context_len": 20}}}, "revoked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "688 ) , was so angry at the long delay in building the monastery , that at first he revoked the grant of his predecessors to Hean , but becoming reconciled , gave all his energy to the work , and Cloveshoo x , or Abingdon , became a monastic town , and its history commences as a house of God from Ina , about A", "context_len": 66}}}, "aggressions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "752 , Cuthred of Wessex defeated Ethelbald of Mercia at Burford , hard by , and protected Abingdon from further aggressions", "context_len": 21}}}, "wintering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "We do not dare to hope that we have seen the last of our troubles ; the Danes are wintering in the Wight , ready for fresh mischief next spring and summer", "context_len": 32}}}, "nov": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Nov", "context_len": 1}}}, "habiliments": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "And , in truth , their habiliments were not very royal", "context_len": 11}}}, "meetly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` My son , '' said the abbot , `` rest here this night in our poor house ; tomorrow we will find you both horses and fitting apparel , and ye shall go meetly to the king , who is the guest of the bishop", "context_len": 46}}}, "dorchester": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Dorchester was at this period the most important city of the Midland counties , for it was the seat of the great bishopric which extended its sway over nearly the whole of Mercia", "context_len": 33}}}, "apostle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Here the apostle of Wessex , Birinus , had converted and baptized Cynegils , king of that country , Oswald , the saintly king of Northumbria , being present , and receiving him fresh from the regenerating waters as his adopted son", "context_len": 42}}}, "regenerating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "Here the apostle of Wessex , Birinus , had converted and baptized Cynegils , king of that country , Oswald , the saintly king of Northumbria , being present , and receiving him fresh from the regenerating waters as his adopted son", "context_len": 42}}}, "diffused": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Here , the next year , Cuichelm , his brother , was baptized , and from this centre Christianity was widely diffused", "context_len": 22}}}, "bishoprics": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Dorchester became successively the seat of two great bishoprics -- the one West Saxon , the other Mercian", "context_len": 18}}}, "supersede": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Therefore Dorchester long enjoyed a wide celebrity and greater influence , than the city , Oxenford , which , lying at a distance of ten miles , was destined to supersede it eventually", "context_len": 33}}}, "humbler": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 43, "context": "Ben found himself wondering that this fellow who had so many things apparently all a boy \u2019 s heart could desire should be so free-and-easy and should mingle every day without the least air of priggishness or superiority with other lads in much humbler circumstances", "context_len": 45}}}, "consorted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "The majesty of the churches was indeed ( as a contemporary wrote ) great , but thatched roofs consorted ill with the remains of shattered column and pedestal , and with the fragmentary ruins of the grand amphitheatre , which were yet partly visible , although the stones which had been brought from Bath to build it had been employed largely in church architecture", "context_len": 64}}}, "tunics": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 61, "context": "The light of day was rapidly fading ; a light breeze brought down the remaining leaves from the trees , or whirled them about in all directions ; winter was plainly about to assume the mastery of the scene , as was evident from the clothing the people wore , the thick fur and warm woollen cloaks which covered their light tunics", "context_len": 62}}}, "uncomely": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 7, "context": "He was of middle stature and not uncomely , but there was a look of vacillation about his face , which would have struck even an indifferent physiognomist , while his thin lips , which he was constantly biting ( when he was not biting his nails ) , seemed to indicate a tendency towards cruelty", "context_len": 56}}}, "vacillation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "He was of middle stature and not uncomely , but there was a look of vacillation about his face , which would have struck even an indifferent physiognomist , while his thin lips , which he was constantly biting ( when he was not biting his nails ) , seemed to indicate a tendency towards cruelty", "context_len": 56}}}, "quadrangular": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "It was spacious , built around a quadrangular courtyard , with cloisters surrounding the lowest storey and the smooth shaven lawn , in the centre of which a granite cross was upraised", "context_len": 32}}}, "storey": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "It was spacious , built around a quadrangular courtyard , with cloisters surrounding the lowest storey and the smooth shaven lawn , in the centre of which a granite cross was upraised", "context_len": 32}}}, "synods": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "On the opposite side of the quadrangle was the great hall where synods were held , and where , on state occasions , such as a royal visit , the banquet was prepared", "context_len": 33}}}, "ethelred": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Ethelred took his place ; upon his head a thin circlet of gold confined his flowing locks already becoming scant , but , as their natural colour was light , not otherwise showing signs of age : he was only in his fortieth year", "context_len": 44}}}, "fortieth": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "Ethelred took his place ; upon his head a thin circlet of gold confined his flowing locks already becoming scant , but , as their natural colour was light , not otherwise showing signs of age : he was only in his fortieth year", "context_len": 44}}}, "haft": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 51, "context": "His tunic was finely embroidered in colours around the neck , and was below of spotless white , secured by a belt richly gilded , whereon was a sheath for the dagger or knife , which was used for all occasions , whether in battle or in meal time , the haft being inlaid with precious stones", "context_len": 57}}}, "chanters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Ednoth said grace , and the chanters responded", "context_len": 8}}}, "reeve": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 20, "context": "The canons of the cathedral , the priests of the other churches , the sheriff of the county , the reeve of the borough , the burgesses , all had their places , and the banquet began ; huge joints being carried round to each individual , from which , with his dagger , he cut what he fancied and deposited it on his plate ; then wine , ale , and mead were poured foaming into metal tankards , and lighter delicacies followed", "context_len": 84}}}, "borough": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "The canons of the cathedral , the priests of the other churches , the sheriff of the county , the reeve of the borough , the burgesses , all had their places , and the banquet began ; huge joints being carried round to each individual , from which , with his dagger , he cut what he fancied and deposited it on his plate ; then wine , ale , and mead were poured foaming into metal tankards , and lighter delicacies followed", "context_len": 84}}}, "burgesses": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 26, "context": "The canons of the cathedral , the priests of the other churches , the sheriff of the county , the reeve of the borough , the burgesses , all had their places , and the banquet began ; huge joints being carried round to each individual , from which , with his dagger , he cut what he fancied and deposited it on his plate ; then wine , ale , and mead were poured foaming into metal tankards , and lighter delicacies followed", "context_len": 84}}}, "levies": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Many days we shall not want it -- many days ? Why , the levies must all be out within twenty-four hours , and the Danes are not strong enough to maintain themselves here", "context_len": 35}}}, "outmanoeuvred": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` We were defending our hearths and our homes , yet we were everywhere outmanoeuvred and beaten", "context_len": 17}}}, "imputations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "The king slightly coloured , for he resented all imputations on his favourite , and was about to make a sharp reply , when a voice which made him start , replied :", "context_len": 33}}}, "advices": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` The enemy , by my last advices , lies quiet in the Isle of Wight , '' said Edric", "context_len": 20}}}, "spake": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 31, "context": "The company sat in amazement , while the hand of Edric played convulsively with the hilt of his dagger ; meanwhile Edmund ate , and gave to Alfgar , ere he spake again", "context_len": 33}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "So spake I to my companions", "context_len": 6}}}, "miscarriage": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` And in that council I heard that the Danes had a secret friend in the English army , who ever gave them due warning of our movements , and who caused all the miscarriage of our last campaign", "context_len": 39}}}, "espy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Alfgar , son of Anlaf , answer ; whom didst thou espy talking with Sweyn ? ''", "context_len": 18}}}, "minions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` What , here , my royal guard ! -- here ! here ! your King is insulted -- insulted , and by his son and his son 's minions", "context_len": 30}}}, "expiration": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "No one spoke to him ; all the company seemed utterly bewildered , as well they might be until , after the expiration of an hour , during which time Ednoth had left the hall , and the company broke up by degrees , an officer of the court came and whispered in his ear that Edmund awaited him without the gates", "context_len": 62}}}, "prowls": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` And let me tell you , '' added Higbald , `` that it will be certain death to try to get away ; for if you could escape me , my dog Wolf , who prowls about by day and night , would tear you in pieces before any one could help you", "context_len": 54}}}, "metamorphosed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "A stormy scene had meanwhile taken place in an interior chamber of the palace of the bishop , which had been metamorphosed into a council chamber for the king", "context_len": 29}}}, "shire": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "There were present Ethelred himself , his irrepressible son , the traitor Edric , the bishop , the sheriff of the shire , and the reeve of the borough , with the captain of the hus-carles , or royal guard", "context_len": 40}}}, "gnashing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Hypocrite ! '' said Edmund , with a sound like the gnashing of teeth", "context_len": 15}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "Oh , if Japazy were only here ! Oh , if Bomba could meet him face to face and wrest the truth from him ! The lad paced the room , gnashing his teeth with impatience", "context_len": 36}}}, "backbiters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Perhaps it is well that the accusation so often urged by backbiters against our faithful subject Edric should -- ''", "context_len": 20}}}, "calends": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` I do not know when the Greek calends are , nor do I want to ; my mother spent her time , I thank God , in teaching me to speak the truth , and to be true to my country , and not in teaching me outlandish gibberish", "context_len": 50}}}, "gibberish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "`` I do not know when the Greek calends are , nor do I want to ; my mother spent her time , I thank God , in teaching me to speak the truth , and to be true to my country , and not in teaching me outlandish gibberish", "context_len": 50}}}, "verification": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "He filled out a special receipt that acknowledged besides the usual delivery , a verification of the amount of the inclosure , its acceptance as correct , and left a blank for the names of two witnesses", "context_len": 37}}}, "misinterpreted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` Have we not forgotten in whose presence we are ? The king , who was quite ignorant of the mistaken zeal which misinterpreted his wishes that day , can not bear to be reminded of it", "context_len": 37}}}, "reliquary": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Here , '' he said , putting his hand to his bosom , and drawing out a reliquary , `` is a holy thorn plucked from St", "context_len": 27}}}, "venerator": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` Art thou not ashamed , my son , to have brought such a charge against the venerator of the Saints , one of the few in whom faith yet lives ? ''", "context_len": 33}}}, "bushels": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "They heard that down at Arlington someone was offering them to the storekeepers at one dollar for two bushels , investigated , detected Dale Wacker peddling the peppers from factory bags , and found that his uncle , Lem , was mixed up in the affair", "context_len": 46}}}, "demonstrating": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Bern Hayden likewise practiced well , putting all his usual snap and dash into everything he did , his accomplishments plainly demonstrating why he had been generally singled out as the fellow who would certainly be chosen as captain of next year \u2019 s team", "context_len": 45}}}, "inflaming": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "He passed through all the streets inflaming the curiosity of the watchmen ; the darkness ( for there were very few lamps or lights of any kind , in those days , for public use ) was intense , a drizzling rain was falling , and at length , weary , wet , and dispirited , he returned to the palace , and found that the council , tired of waiting , had at length broken up", "context_len": 77}}}, "ambushes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "A fool might -- I beg pardon -- were not all our plans known beforehand ? Did not all our enterprises fail ? Were not all our ambushes anticipated ? Did we not fall into all theirs ? If they had had a prophet like Elisha , who told the king of Israel all Benhadad said in his council chamber , they could n't have managed better", "context_len": 67}}}, "complacence": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "The captor eyed his captive with a look of conscious pride , and said with some complacence , `` You see , and perhaps repent , your rashness in the accusation you made", "context_len": 33}}}, "repossess": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Well , the gentle Sweyn has taken your evasion very much to heart , and earnestly desires to repossess himself of your person ; but for this , my easiest plan would have been to rid myself of so troublesome a witness in a more speedy manner , and you might ere this have fed the fishes of the Thames", "context_len": 60}}}, "apostates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Well , when Sweyn comes , he may , if he will , make a public example to all apostates in your honoured person ; meanwhile Edmund thinks you have deserted him", "context_len": 32}}}, "hypocrisy": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 65, "context": "Dogood \u2019 s first letter , as well as subsequent ones in which she expressed herself , wittily and clearly , on such varied subjects as the folly of fashionable dress , the character of the so-called weaker sex , the ill effects of liquor , the inferior quality of New England poetry , the need of insurance for widows and old maids , the hypocrisy of certain pretenders to religion , and the uselessness of sending dullards to Harvard simply because their fathers could afford to pay their way", "context_len": 90}}}, "unblushing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "The wickedness of Edric seemed so great , his hypocrisy so unblushing , that in his simple faith Alfgar could not believe that he would be allowed to succeed", "context_len": 29}}}, "ungodly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "He tried in vain to open any intercourse with his ferocious jailor , whose ward was sometimes shared by a comrade , when there was much ungodly revelry below , and snatches of Danish war songs mingled with profane oaths", "context_len": 40}}}, "innocency": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` Seek innocency , and take heed to the thing that is right : for that shall bring a man peace at the last", "context_len": 24}}}, "disbelieved": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 41, "context": "`` I have risked my life for my country , '' he said , `` and now that I bring tidings which ought to circulate through the land like the wind , and rouse every man to action , I am disbelieved", "context_len": 42}}}, "tithed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "How I thought of those who tithed mint and rue , and all manner of herbs , and passed over justice , mercy , and the love of God", "context_len": 29}}}, "mattins": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "I have sat up to say my mattins and finish this diary", "context_len": 12}}}, "sickbed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "All this week I have been watching by the sickbed of the Etheling", "context_len": 13}}}, "ferryman": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The ferryman , whose hut is situate at the bend of the river below the Synodune hills , where people cross for Wittenham , says that late on the night in question a boat with four people passed down the river , and that it struck him that one only rowed , while two of the rest seemed guarding the fourth passenger", "context_len": 62}}}, "elaborated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` And so was I , '' said he , `` and I have persuaded the king to place guards and watchers all through the coasts opposite the Wight , and with Edric 's aid we elaborated a goodly plan", "context_len": 40}}}, "remissness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "I wonder whether the same remissness prevails elsewhere", "context_len": 8}}}, "terra": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` I thought that it was midnight of Christmas Eve , and that I was attending mass , when , just as the words were sung by the choir , 'Pax in terra , ' the scene suddenly changed , and I stood in the dark on the chalk hills which overlook the Solent ; by my side was a beacon ready laid for firing", "context_len": 65}}}, "sanctified": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 52, "context": "They had all duly performed the religious duties of the day , and had been greatly edified by the homily of Father Cuthbert at mass ; and now innocent mirth was to close the hallowed day -- mirth which they well believed was not alien to the birthday of Him who once sanctified the marriage festivities at Cana by His first miracle", "context_len": 62}}}, "decking": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "But I must go and assist in decking the church for the midnight festivity", "context_len": 14}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Three or four , by some freak , chewed the mast half through at a point four feet above the decking", "context_len": 21}}}, "brazier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "At last the weather became very cold , and being without a fire , his sufferings were great , until his ferocious gaoler , finding him quite stiffened , brought up a brazier of coals , which saved his prisoner 's life , while it filled the room with smoke , which could only escape by the crevices in walls and roof , for to open a window would have been as bad as to dispense with the fire , such was the state of the outer air", "context_len": 88}}}, "intoxicating": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "At last Alfgar could but conclude it was Christmastide , for Higbald was joined by two comrades , and they sang and rioted below in a way which showed that they had got plenty of intoxicating drink , and were making free with it", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "But when Japazy has taken too much intoxicating drink he has talked sometimes to himself and Abino \u2019 s ears have been open to his words", "context_len": 26}}}, "kindles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` When we can not get blood we can drink the red wine , The Sea King sang in his might ; For it maddens the brain , it gives strength to the arm , And kindles the soul in the fight", "context_len": 42}}}, "clasps": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "He clasps his sword with desperate tenacity , in which a foe might read his doom , and rushes on , crushing through the brushwood", "context_len": 25}}}, "tenacity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "He clasps his sword with desperate tenacity , in which a foe might read his doom , and rushes on , crushing through the brushwood", "context_len": 25}}}, "tramples": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Onward , onward , he tramples through bush and bramble , until he sees his progress suddenly arrested by the dark-flowing river", "context_len": 22}}}, "unlooses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "He unlooses the chain , but with difficulty , and precious moments are lost", "context_len": 14}}}, "kneels": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Alfgar sees the brute swimming after the boat ; he ceases to use the pole , but takes his sword , kneels on the stern of the boat , and waits for the mastiff", "context_len": 34}}}, "forepaws": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "It gains the boat , and tries to mount , when the keen steel is driven between the forepaws to its very heart", "context_len": 23}}}, "dyeing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "One loud howl , and it floats down the stream , dyeing the waters with its life-blood", "context_len": 17}}}, "silvering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "Behind him the heavens are growing angry with lurid light , before him the earth lies in stillness and silence ; the moonbeams slumbering on placid river , glittering on frozen pool , or silvering happy homesteads -- happy hitherto", "context_len": 40}}}, "homesteads": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "Behind him the heavens are growing angry with lurid light , before him the earth lies in stillness and silence ; the moonbeams slumbering on placid river , glittering on frozen pool , or silvering happy homesteads -- happy hitherto", "context_len": 40}}}, "edified": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "They had all duly performed the religious duties of the day , and had been greatly edified by the homily of Father Cuthbert at mass ; and now innocent mirth was to close the hallowed day -- mirth which they well believed was not alien to the birthday of Him who once sanctified the marriage festivities at Cana by His first miracle", "context_len": 62}}}, "osier": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 82, "context": "The building was large and irregular , and had been added to at various times , the hall , looking over the river , forming its most conspicuous portion ; but it had not originally been built for purposes of defence , and could not have endured the Danish assault for a moment , but for external defences , utterly independent of the building , which had been recently added ; a mound , surmounted by crossed palisades , skilfully strengthened by osier bands , and a deep outer ditch , now full of snow , surrounded the building on three sides", "context_len": 102}}}, "rented": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 54, "context": "So thither flocked the young and the old : the wood rangers and hunters from the forests of Newenham , where Herstan had right of wood cutting ; the men who wove baskets and hurdles of osier work from the river banks ; the theows who cultivated the home farm ; the ceorls who rented a hide of land here and a hide there -- all , the grandfather and the grandson , accepted the invitation to feast", "context_len": 78}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "She was the older daughter , a strong , healthy girl of seventeen , who never helped her mother about the work , who dressed in such cheap finery as she could obtain by hook or crook , who took music lessons on a rented melodeon paid for out of her mother \u2019 s hard earnings , who felt herself to be a lady unfortunately born out of her sphere , and who was unquestionably ashamed of her surviving parent and her brother and sister", "context_len": 85}}}, "serf": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "At length a solitary serf came to the gate :", "context_len": 10}}}, "palisades": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 77, "context": "The building was large and irregular , and had been added to at various times , the hall , looking over the river , forming its most conspicuous portion ; but it had not originally been built for purposes of defence , and could not have endured the Danish assault for a moment , but for external defences , utterly independent of the building , which had been recently added ; a mound , surmounted by crossed palisades , skilfully strengthened by osier bands , and a deep outer ditch , now full of snow , surrounded the building on three sides", "context_len": 102}}}, "vestments": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Yes , he went to the church to bring in the sacred vessels and vestments", "context_len": 16}}}, "obscuring": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "The drawbridge was up , and no danger seemed near ; the trees were in deep shadow , for the clouds , obscuring the moon , made the night very dark", "context_len": 31}}}, "fatally": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 50, "context": "How Bomba was swallowed up in an earthquake and hurled into a subterranean cavern that promised to become a living tomb ; by what a marvelous combination of nerve and good fortune he reached the open air and sunlight ; how he finally found Jojasta after the latter had been fatally hurt by the fall of his temple ; the partial but not sufficient information he received from the dying man regarding his parents ; the obstacles he surmounted and the perils he escaped are told in the preceding volume of this series , entitled : Bomba the Jungle Boy at the Moving Mountain ; or , The Mystery of the Caves of Fire", "context_len": 114}}}, "unavenged": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Neither wilt thou escape Edric Streorn , and I shall not die unavenged", "context_len": 13}}}, "gush": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 53, "context": "I never in my life had heard such a sweet even flow of song from the mouth of any living being , and I had once belonged to the son of a famous concert singer and had in her home heard many beautiful voices -- why ! it was like the lovely upward gush of a fountain -- clear , pure and exquisite", "context_len": 63}}}, "tracked": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 60, "context": "I might track her easy , I suppose , but since she did n't seem to wish it to be known who she was or where she came from , I would n't feel it was showing my gratitude for the obligations she 's laid me under , and you see by the printing she do n't wish to be tracked even by her handwriting", "context_len": 65}}}, "billowing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Michael burst forth into billowing eddies of smoke and flame", "context_len": 10}}}, "discharges": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "Meanwhile flights of arrows came from the distance , and settled around them ; but they were spent before arrival in most cases , for the defenders kept the ground clear for a large circle around by their well-sustained discharges", "context_len": 40}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "After this he had his first taste of Arctic adventure , having received a commission from the Government to explore the Coppermine , one of the great rivers of Canada , which discharges its waters into the Arctic Ocean", "context_len": 39}}}, "imprinted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "One kiss she imprinted eagerly on his brow , and dismissed him back to his perilous task without allowing herself one sigh", "context_len": 22}}}, "fabric": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 29, "context": "Here , Your Honor , you may see the torn place in this lad \u2019 s coat-sleeve , and you may also perceive beyond question that the shred of fabric discovered by Piper clinging to the nail in Bernard Hayden \u2019 s locker corresponds with the material of this garment", "context_len": 50}}}, "miscalculated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "The ball was scooped from the ground in a marvelous manner , and Merwin , having miscalculated , clutched at the heels of the fellow who had secured the pigskin clutched but could not hold fast , even though his fingers touched the stocky ankles of Ben Stone", "context_len": 48}}}, "inflammable": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 53, "context": "A globe of fire traversing the arc of a circle , rose from beyond the embers of the barns , and , sailing through the air , fell upon the roof , which , owing to the intense heat from the conflagration which had raged around , was in a very dry and inflammable state", "context_len": 55}}}, "inauspicious": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "It was at this inauspicious moment that the Danes charged the palisades again with deadly fury , while the attention of all was drawn to the flames ; so fierce was the attack , that it was necessary once more to concentrate all the strength of the besieged to repel them ; and the fire gained in strength , roared and hissed in its fury , seizing for its prey the whole roof of the eastern wing of the building", "context_len": 80}}}, "trembler": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` All is lost ! all is lost ! '' cried some panic-stricken trembler , as he saw the flames spread", "context_len": 21}}}, "unfulfilled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Alas ! the prediction was unfulfilled xii", "context_len": 7}}}, "impediments": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "So much the better ; for if we can hardly escape such impediments , I do not think we need fear that the Danes will find their way through the swamps and brakes", "context_len": 33}}}, "stag": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "So speaking , while we all stood motionless , he crept near his victim , and drawing an arrow to its head , while all we saw was the branching horns of the stag , he let it fly", "context_len": 39}}}, "rotted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "A brook coming down from the hills had overflowed the land until a swamp or quagmire had been formed , whereon huge trees rotted in slime , while creeping plants hid the deformity of decay", "context_len": 35}}}, "deformity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "A brook coming down from the hills had overflowed the land until a swamp or quagmire had been formed , whereon huge trees rotted in slime , while creeping plants hid the deformity of decay", "context_len": 35}}}, "disciplining": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Edmund , assisted by Alfgar , has begun his task of disciplining and training all our able-bodied men", "context_len": 18}}}, "bode": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "The king Ethelred has appointed Edric Streorn ealdorman of Mercia ; we are in his district , and fear it may bode evil to us all", "context_len": 26}}}, "currently": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "She referred him , with her own full consent , to her father ; and Elfwyn says , not unwisely , that he can not consent until the land is at peace ; that it is currently reported that Thurkill , a Danish earl , is at hand with an immense fleet , and that to marry might both hamper a warrior 's hands and be the means of bringing up children for the sword", "context_len": 75}}}, "earl": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 43, "context": "She referred him , with her own full consent , to her father ; and Elfwyn says , not unwisely , that he can not consent until the land is at peace ; that it is currently reported that Thurkill , a Danish earl , is at hand with an immense fleet , and that to marry might both hamper a warrior 's hands and be the means of bringing up children for the sword", "context_len": 75}}}, "postpones": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "He fully accepts Alfgar 's suit , but postpones the day till peace seems established , that is `` sine die", "context_len": 21}}}, "sine": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "He fully accepts Alfgar 's suit , but postpones the day till peace seems established , that is `` sine die", "context_len": 21}}}, "waylaid": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "He states that forces being gathered from all parts of England , the Danes were waylaid , and must have been beaten , but that Edric persuaded the king not to fight when the victory was in his hands , and so they escaped", "context_len": 44}}}, "whitsuntide": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Whitsuntide", "context_len": 1}}}, "persuades": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Edric persuades the king to go eastward , and the Danes are westward", "context_len": 13}}}, "archbishop": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` Englishmen ! brethren ! '' he cried , `` you see your foe , the ruthless destroyers of your land and kinsfolk ; the pagan murderers of your archbishop , the sainted Alphege", "context_len": 34}}}, "impoverished": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "But he not only firmly refused to give money , but forbade his impoverished people to do so on his account", "context_len": 21}}}, "hustings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Then , on the following Saturday , they led him to their hustings ( or assembly ) , and shamefully slaughtered him , casting upon him bones and the horns of oxen", "context_len": 32}}}, "intercessions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Oh that the martyr 's intercessions may be heard for his afflicted country xiii", "context_len": 14}}}, "perpetrating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Sweyn came along Watling Street , perpetrating the most monstrous cruelties ; in short , he and his committed the worst evil that any army could do", "context_len": 27}}}, "damnation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "On the eve of the festival he filled up the measure of his damnation by daring to exact an enormous tribute from the town where rests the uncorrupt body of the precious martyr St", "context_len": 34}}}, "disparaged": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Not content with this , he disparaged the blessed martyr 's merits , daring to say there was no sanctity about him", "context_len": 22}}}, "sanctity": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "Paul 's Cathedral , amidst tears of joy , and cries which even the sanctity of the place could not wholly restrain , `` God bless King Edmund ! '' The solemn oath of fidelity was administered , and when all was over , with mingled tears and acclamations , those who had met to bury the late king greeted with joy his son and successor", "context_len": 66}}}, "frowardness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "But , thus setting no bounds to his frowardness , Divine vengeance did not suffer the blasphemer to prolong his miserable existence", "context_len": 22}}}, "amend": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Ethelred has returned , and is again king ; he has promised to amend his evil ways , and to be ruled by faithful and wise counsellors", "context_len": 27}}}, "canute": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 0, "context": "Canute was near , his ships had been seen entering the Thames , and his determination to take the city , which had so often resisted the Danish arms , had been freely and frankly expressed", "context_len": 36}}}, "eastertide": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Eastertide", "context_len": 1}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Eastertide , 1016", "context_len": 3}}}, "burghs": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Would this had been all ! He invited Sigeferth and Morcar , two of the chief Thanes in the seven burghs , to supper with him ; and there , when he had made them heavy with wine , he caused them to be cruelly murdered by hired ruffians", "context_len": 49}}}, "slumbered": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Alas ! the Divine vengeance has not slumbered long after the late cruel deed", "context_len": 14}}}, "stratagems": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Edric , who is steeped in stratagems and deceit , plotted against his life again and again , whereupon Edmund broke up the camp in indignation , and took a separate course with all the warriors who would follow his standard", "context_len": 41}}}, "epiphany": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Epiphany , 1016", "context_len": 3}}}, "professes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "But many Englishmen have given their allegiance to Canute , who now professes himself a Christian , saying they will not serve Ethelred any more", "context_len": 25}}}, "bowmen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "They rode within bow shot of the gates , which were closed , and there they paused , for a score of bowmen held their shafts to their ears", "context_len": 29}}}, "discontinuing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "So the people made way without discontinuing their acclamations , and Edmund and his train rode on till they reached the precincts of St", "context_len": 24}}}, "viaticum": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 57, "context": "The door opened , and Edward left the room after a conference with the physician , who sat in a corner of the room compounding drugs at a small table ; a few minutes passed in silence , when he returned and held the door open for the bishop of London , who entered , bearing the viaticum , as the last communion of the sick was then called , and attended by an acolyte , who bore a lighted taper before him and carried a bell", "context_len": 87}}}, "reassemble": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "He dismounted , saying , `` Alfgar , follow me ; '' and commended his troops to the hospitality of the citizens , bidding them to reassemble before St", "context_len": 29}}}, "paleness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "His face , which time and evil passions had deeply wrinkled , was of a deadly paleness ; his eyes were encircled by a livid tint , and stared as if they would start from their orbits ; his breathing was rapid and interrupted , but at the moment when Edmund entered he was silent", "context_len": 55}}}, "orbits": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "His face , which time and evil passions had deeply wrinkled , was of a deadly paleness ; his eyes were encircled by a livid tint , and stared as if they would start from their orbits ; his breathing was rapid and interrupted , but at the moment when Edmund entered he was silent", "context_len": 55}}}, "amour": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Alfred , her eldest son -- for Edmund was the offspring of an early amour of the king -- was on the other side of the bed , a well-made youth , combining in his features the haughty bearing of his Norman maternal ancestors with the English traits of his father ; but now his expression was one of distress and anxiety , which was yet more deeply shared by his younger brother , Edward , who even at this period manifested that strong sense of religious obligation and that early devotion which in later years caused him to be numbered amongst canonised saints", "context_len": 104}}}, "acolyte": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 74, "context": "The door opened , and Edward left the room after a conference with the physician , who sat in a corner of the room compounding drugs at a small table ; a few minutes passed in silence , when he returned and held the door open for the bishop of London , who entered , bearing the viaticum , as the last communion of the sick was then called , and attended by an acolyte , who bore a lighted taper before him and carried a bell", "context_len": 87}}}, "prelate": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "And all present knelt round the bed , while the plaintive cry arose from the lips of the prelate , and was echoed from all around :", "context_len": 27}}}, "litany": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "But upon the dying sinner 's ears it fell as an echo of the long , long past ; of that day when the litany arose before his coronation at Kingston , and the prophetic curse of Dunstan", "context_len": 38}}}, "supplication": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "And so the litany for the dying rolled solemnly along , with its intense burning words of supplication , its deep agony of prayer , its loving earnestness of intercession", "context_len": 30}}}, "sockets": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "Brice 's day ; Elfhelm of Shrewsbury and his sons , with their empty sockets , and their eyes hanging down ; Sigeferth , Morcar , and a thousand others", "context_len": 30}}}, "maledictions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "His frightful maledictions overpowered the supplications around his bed ; but they died away in silence -- silence so long continued , that suspicion soon became certainty", "context_len": 27}}}, "supplications": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "His frightful maledictions overpowered the supplications around his bed ; but they died away in silence -- silence so long continued , that suspicion soon became certainty", "context_len": 27}}}, "abbots": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "So soon as the news of the death of Ethelred travelled abroad , the bishops , abbots , ealdormen , and thanes of southern England , despairing of the cause of the house of Cerdic , met together at Southampton , and renouncing Ethelred and his descendants , elected Canute to be their king , while he swore that both in things spiritual and temporal he would maintain their liberties", "context_len": 70}}}, "thanes": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 21, "context": "So soon as the news of the death of Ethelred travelled abroad , the bishops , abbots , ealdormen , and thanes of southern England , despairing of the cause of the house of Cerdic , met together at Southampton , and renouncing Ethelred and his descendants , elected Canute to be their king , while he swore that both in things spiritual and temporal he would maintain their liberties", "context_len": 70}}}, "renouncing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "So soon as the news of the death of Ethelred travelled abroad , the bishops , abbots , ealdormen , and thanes of southern England , despairing of the cause of the house of Cerdic , met together at Southampton , and renouncing Ethelred and his descendants , elected Canute to be their king , while he swore that both in things spiritual and temporal he would maintain their liberties", "context_len": 70}}}, "ratify": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "It yet remained to be seen whether the choice of the realm would ratify this decisive step on the part of the citizens of London", "context_len": 25}}}, "dowager": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Emma , the queen dowager , was deeply mortified , even while she confessed the heritage was hardly worth having", "context_len": 20}}}, "parry": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "castled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Onward flew pursuers and pursued -- onward till Windsor 's height , with its castled hall , appeared in sight , and tempted them to seek refreshment for man and beast", "context_len": 31}}}, "betaken": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Such people as still existed without the fortified towns had betaken themselves to the woods , or the recesses of the deep swamps and forests , as the people of Aescendune had done", "context_len": 33}}}, "snowball": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Onward , and like a rolling snowball , they gathered as they went , until they entered Wiltshire with ten thousand men , and , crossing the country , reached the opposite border with all the brave men of Wilts added to their numbers", "context_len": 44}}}, "sheen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "While thus engaged , they saw the flashing of spear and shield in the east , reflecting the setting sun , and speedily the whole country seemed to glow with the sheen of weapons", "context_len": 34}}}, "campfires": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "The watch was duly set ; campfires were lighted , and joints of meat suspended over them ; barrels of wine and mead were broached , for all the country around contributed with loving willingness to the support of its defenders ; and when hunger was appeased the patriotic song arose from the various fires , and stirring legends of the glorious days of old , when Danes and Norsemen fled before the English arms , nerved the courage of the men for the morrow 's stern conflict", "context_len": 88}}}, "adversaries": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "After the kidnapping traders had been harrying the islands , one of the chiefs said that , if the bishop would only bring a man-of-war and get him vengeance on his adversaries , he would be exalted like his Father above", "context_len": 41}}}, "improbably": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` The chances of a warrior 's life are so many that he may not improbably be gone , but remember you found another father at Carisbrooke", "context_len": 27}}}, "furze": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` My lord , would you look closely at that little clump of furze upon the banks of the brook ? ''", "context_len": 22}}}, "sleepiest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "`` Only to appear with more effect ; they will be creeping like snakes coming to be scotched ; they wo n't find a man like Edric at the head of the English army now -- one who always chose the sleepiest and deafest men for sentinels", "context_len": 47}}}, "deafest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "`` Only to appear with more effect ; they will be creeping like snakes coming to be scotched ; they wo n't find a man like Edric at the head of the English army now -- one who always chose the sleepiest and deafest men for sentinels", "context_len": 47}}}, "disengaging": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 77, "context": "Young Hermann 's sword broke against a Danish axe ; he rushed in and got within the swing of the weapon ; both wrestled for the deadly steel , they fell , rolled over and over on the grass ; at length Hermann grasped his opponent 's throat like a vice with his mailed hand , and held till the arms of his foe hung nerveless by the side and the face grew black , when , disengaging his right hand , he found his dagger , and drove it to the victim 's heart", "context_len": 95}}}, "entrenchments": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "Great , therefore , was the surprise , when he led his forces without the entrenchments , with the observation that the breasts of Englishmen were their best bulwarks", "context_len": 29}}}, "newest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 54, "context": "The English hero divided his army into three divisions : The right wing , where he posted around his own person the chosen band whom he had trained during the last few years of retirement ; the left wing , chiefly composed of the men of Wessex ; the centre , the weakest and newest recruits , whom he posted there with as deep a design as led Hannibal to use the same strategy at Cannae", "context_len": 76}}}, "chastised": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Canute 's voice was heard crying , `` Now , warriors , behold the hounds ye have so often chastised await your chastisement once more", "context_len": 25}}}, "blent": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Then came the rush : the crash of steel upon steel , the hideous melee , where friend and foe seemed blent in one dense struggling mass ; the cries which pain sometimes extorted from the bravest ; the shouts of the excited combatants , until Edmund 's centre gave way", "context_len": 51}}}, "hewed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "He and his cleft their way in -- hewed it through living masses of flesh ; trampled writhing bodies under foot ; their very horses seemed to laugh at the spear and sword , until before him Edmund saw Canute himself", "context_len": 41}}}, "severing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "On the Tuesday the English were rapidly getting the better , when the traitor Edric , severing the head of a fallen Englishman named Osmaer , held it up , shouting :", "context_len": 32}}}, "ecclesiastics": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` And present at that last banquet were Ednoth , the bishop of Dorchester , and other ecclesiastics , who had come to pray for the host and to succour the dying with ghostly aid", "context_len": 35}}}, "plunderers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "It was a sad sight to see the field of battle strewn for miles with the dead and dying , while gangs of plunderers swarmed in all directions", "context_len": 28}}}, "deformities": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "But the saddest scene in the flight lay on a gentle eminence , commanding a view of the field , whose deformities night mercifully shrouded from view , although the murmurs of the wounded reached them even there in one long subdued wailing moan", "context_len": 44}}}, "sacerdotal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "There , on that little hill , lay bishops and abbots in their sacerdotal apparel", "context_len": 15}}}, "embassy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "At this moment an embassy was seen approaching from Edmund 's army", "context_len": 12}}}, "covet": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` Generous prince , '' said he , `` hitherto I have had a covetous desire of your kingdom , but now I do yet more earnestly covet your friendship ; your father and my father have each reigned over the land , let us divide the inheritance like brothers", "context_len": 50}}}, "womb": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "And so England entered upon a peace of fifty years , only broken by an event yet in the womb of time , the Norman Conquest", "context_len": 26}}}, "climes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The more substantial viands were removed , generous wines from warmer climes were introduced , but there was no need of a harper or of minstrels , save Edmund himself , or of legends and tales to those whose lives had passed amidst scenes of excitement", "context_len": 46}}}, "harper": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "The more substantial viands were removed , generous wines from warmer climes were introduced , but there was no need of a harper or of minstrels , save Edmund himself , or of legends and tales to those whose lives had passed amidst scenes of excitement", "context_len": 46}}}, "goodwife": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "There is something in a life of adventure which warms the blood and makes time pass swiftly ; my goodwife and I sometimes tire of each other 's company , as I expect Ethelgiva and you will in time", "context_len": 39}}}, "dissemble": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` This is a business ! What am I to say ? I can not dissemble , and pretend to love him , were he ten times my brother-in-law", "context_len": 29}}}, "tosses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Night after night he tosses on his uneasy bed , and wishes that it were day", "context_len": 16}}}, "perjuries": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Art thou willing to accept such penance as the Church , in consideration of thy perjuries , thy murders , which man may not avenge , since treaties protect thee -- but which God will surely remember , if thou repent not -- to accept such penance , I say , as the Church shall impose ? ''", "context_len": 58}}}, "meagreness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "The chamber occupied by the king was a long roomy place , containing a single bedstead of carved wood , surmounted by the usual distinctive canopy , from which tapestried hangings depended , and upon which scriptural subjects were woven ; the furniture of the room partook of the usual meagreness of the times", "context_len": 54}}}, "antechamber": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "The entrance was through a small antechamber , wherein , on a humbler bedstead , Alfgar slept", "context_len": 17}}}, "trespasses": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "It would be very hard , and I 'm afraid I would n't quite like to do it ; but I would try to think how often Jesus forgave me , and I would say , 'forgive me my trespasses ' as I forgive Lem and Dolly , and maybe that would make it easier", "context_len": 55}}}, "canon": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "One very touching ceremony , handed down from early times , was still observed in England -- the `` kiss of peace , '' occurring at some period before the close of the canon of the mass , when all the members of the cathedral chapter , or of the choir , as the case might be , solemnly saluted each other", "context_len": 62}}}, "earls": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "He had occupied it one well-remembered season , during which , in that very banqueting hall where we have introduced our readers , Sigeferth and Morcar , the earls of the seven burghs , were treacherously murdered at the banquet after Edric had previously made them heavy with wine", "context_len": 49}}}, "voyager": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "He was as calm and self-possessed as a man could be -- as a smiling sea under the summer sky -- smiling so that the heedless voyager knows not what hideous trophies or past storms the smiling depths conceal", "context_len": 39}}}, "adorning": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The ladies sat up nearly all night making the wedding robes complete , and richly adorning them -- Hilda , Bertha , and Ethelgiva , with many skilful handmaidens", "context_len": 29}}}, "handmaidens": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "The ladies sat up nearly all night making the wedding robes complete , and richly adorning them -- Hilda , Bertha , and Ethelgiva , with many skilful handmaidens", "context_len": 29}}}, "sustains": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "'T is easy to stand in the deadly gap and keep the foe from a beleaguered citadel : men praise the deed , and there is a feeling of conscious pride which sustains one , but the truly great deeds are those which no chronicler records", "context_len": 46}}}, "buttery": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Yes ; I entertained him at the buttery as you requested", "context_len": 12}}}, "endowing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` Well , he came with a proposal from Canute that we should join in building and endowing a church at Assingdun , where a priest may ever say mass for the souls of our dead , whether English or Dane", "context_len": 41}}}, "percolating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "At length he heard a sound which might have been produced by falling rain percolating through the roof , drop , drop upon the floor , but it was strange , for there was no sound of rain outside at that moment", "context_len": 42}}}, "glutinous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Just then he felt his naked feet come into contact with some wet substance , slightly glutinous , on the floor , and shuddered at the contact", "context_len": 27}}}, "procuring": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "The process of procuring light then from flint , steel , and tinder was very slow , and it was some minutes before he had a taper lighted , when its beams disclosed to his horror-stricken sight Edmund , weltering in his blood ; a dagger had been driven suddenly and swiftly to his heart , and he had died apparently without a struggle", "context_len": 64}}}, "prodigy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "Edric entered , and with woe , real or affected ( no one could tell ) , painted in his face , approached the body ; and Elfwyn and Herstan beheld , or thought they beheld , a prodigy : they thought they saw the eyes open , and regard Edric , and that they saw the blood well up in the wound", "context_len": 63}}}, "imperfections": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "All the imperfections of his character now faded away ; he seemed so brave , yet so loving , so invincible in combat , yet so gentle and forgiving , as he had shown in forgiving even -- even -- even -- said Alfgar to his own wounded bleeding heart -- even in forgiving his murderer", "context_len": 56}}}, "heeding": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 39, "context": "First , the mischievous puppy started a little black and white rabbit , and sent it scampering away as fast as its feet could carry it , rushing after it among all the underbrush and briars , and never heeding the coaxing calls of his little mistresses or the louder and sterner voices of their brothers ; then coming back he rushed into a brook which ran by the way , and after rolling himself in it till the water was dripping from his silky coat , he shook himself and sent a shower of drops over the clean white dresses of the little girls ; and then finding the hole of a wood-chuck , he began scratching and burying himself in the earth in a frenzy to", "context_len": 128}}}, "celebrant": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The bishop was the celebrant , for Father Cuthbert was to have officiated at the celebration of the marriage of his son in the faith", "context_len": 25}}}, "officiated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The bishop was the celebrant , for Father Cuthbert was to have officiated at the celebration of the marriage of his son in the faith", "context_len": 25}}}, "diviner": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 55, "context": "The solemn pealing of the bell for the mass at the hour of daybreak fell upon Alfgar 's ears , and he turned almost mechanically to the cathedral , yet with vague desire to communicate all his griefs and troubles to a higher power than that of man , and to seek aid from a diviner source", "context_len": 57}}}, "monasteries": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 67, "context": "The news of the murder of Edmund spread far and wide , and awakened deep sorrow and indignation , not only amongst his friends and subjects , but even amongst his former enemies , the Danes , now rapidly yielding to the civilising and softening influences of Christianity , following therein the notable example of their king , Canute , who was everywhere restoring the churches and monasteries he and his had destroyed , and saying , with no faltering voice , albeit , perhaps , with a very inadequate realisation of all the words implied , `` As for me and my house , we will serve the Lord", "context_len": 110}}}, "flocking": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "At intermission the boys came flocking around him , and some of the girls smiled upon him in a friendly manner", "context_len": 21}}}, "drugged": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` I think our wine was drugged the night before , or I should not have slept so soundly", "context_len": 19}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` He 's as sound as a drugged top , '' she replied", "context_len": 13}}}, "wainscoting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` Then I will get a carpenter and search the wainscoting ; and I will see whether I can learn anything about the wine , '' said Elfwyn", "context_len": 28}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Above the wainscoting a few tasty pictures hung on the dark red walls", "context_len": 13}}}, "solemnities": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Meanwhile the crafty Edric , who excused himself from attendance on the solemnities , tarried at Oxenford , and with him tarried also Elfwyn , Herstan , and the other friends of the unfortunate prisoner , to secure , as they were able , that justice should be rendered him", "context_len": 50}}}, "assessor": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "The court was composed of wise and aged men , ealdormen , thanes , and burgesses had places , and the bishop of Dorchester sat by Edric as assessor", "context_len": 29}}}, "espousing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "But amongst those who stood nearest to the place he must occupy were his betrothed , her mother , Bertha , and young Hermann , who had already got into several quarrels through his fierce espousing of the cause of the accused", "context_len": 42}}}, "purging": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` It is my office , '' said he , `` by the canons of King Athelstane , to assist secular judges in purging away accusations , therefore I will ask the accused a few questions", "context_len": 36}}}, "andrew": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Andrew", "context_len": 1}}}, "ably": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "It afterwards transpired that the bishop pleaded in Alfgar 's favour , while Herstan ably seconded him ; but all was in vain", "context_len": 23}}}, "wartime": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Somehow time doesn \u2019 t count much with wartime friendships", "context_len": 10}}}, "airport": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 45, "context": "By the way , did either of you hear or see anything of our old messmate , Glenn Crayle ? After all the surprises of the past twenty-four hours , I wouldn \u2019 t be surprised to see him waiting for us at the new airport", "context_len": 46}}}, "paratroops": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "The raid was a cover-up for a landing of paratroops and field guns on a ridge above the field", "context_len": 19}}}, "planes": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 25, "context": "Either they had been left there for servicing , or the Japs had felt so secure that they didn \u2019 t bother to scatter their planes around the field at dispersal points", "context_len": 32}}}, "airplane": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "He was to report at the new airplane repair base immediately upon being discharged", "context_len": 14}}}, "blonde": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "Through the hatchway dropped a small , bandy-legged man whose short blonde hair bristled like the fuzz of a newly hatched duckling", "context_len": 22}}}, "heals": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Be thankful that nineteen years heals just twice as fast as forty-five ! Er by the way at eleven thirty you will report to Captain O \u2019 Grady on the west ramp outside the hospital", "context_len": 35}}}, "ramp": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "Be thankful that nineteen years heals just twice as fast as forty-five ! Er by the way at eleven thirty you will report to Captain O \u2019 Grady on the west ramp outside the hospital", "context_len": 35}}}, "wheelchair": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "A mere second lieutenant had no place in such company ! Then he glimpsed Captain O \u2019 Grady in a wheelchair , chatting with the highest-ranking officer", "context_len": 27}}}, "handclasp": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "He saluted , met the general \u2019 s handclasp , and stepped back", "context_len": 13}}}, "tableau": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "It fascinated and transfixed the military man , and Bart Stirling , staring also at the strange tableau , was overcome with perplexity and mystification", "context_len": 25}}}, "runway": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 3, "context": "Out on the runway at five o \u2019 clock Barry \u2019 s crew found their new ship waiting , complete with tail gunner", "context_len": 23}}}, "bombarding": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "The two friends proceeded to do a war-dance , bombarding each other with questions", "context_len": 14}}}, "bailed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "His B-26 Marauder had run out of gas near the northern tip of Queensland one night , and its crew had bailed out", "context_len": 23}}}, "useable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Fred Marmon , Cracker Jackson , Tony Romani , Curly Levitt , and Soapy Babbitt , with his broken shoulder still a little stiff , but useable", "context_len": 27}}}, "explode": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "The young express agent was conscious that he shouted outright in his nightmare , for the trunk he was dreaming about as it struck him seemed to explode into a thousand pieces", "context_len": 32}}}, "bombardier": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 2, "context": "The little bombardier shook his numbed fingers , grabbed the right-hand machine gun and swung it broadside", "context_len": 17}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "For the rest of the trip Rosy O \u2019 Grady \u2019 s pilots and bombardier kept their eyes peeled for suspicious looking market gardens , but none appeared", "context_len": 28}}}, "fuzz": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Through the hatchway dropped a small , bandy-legged man whose short blonde hair bristled like the fuzz of a newly hatched duckling", "context_len": 22}}}, "sawbones": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The sawbones will be looking solemn and saying : That chunk of shrapnel went deeper than we thought", "context_len": 18}}}, "battlefront": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "He learned that his new battlefront base was to be another jungle airport , farther west along the New Guinea coast", "context_len": 21}}}, "airports": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "We \u2019 ve wiped out an air base from which the Nips could have raided Queensland , Port Moresby , and any of our northeast airports with equal ease", "context_len": 29}}}, "aluminum": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "Barry solved the problem by cutting the fish into fillets and broiling these on the blade of one of the raft \u2019 s aluminum oars", "context_len": 25}}}, "messmate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "By the way , did either of you hear or see anything of our old messmate , Glenn Crayle ? After all the surprises of the past twenty-four hours , I wouldn \u2019 t be surprised to see him waiting for us at the new airport", "context_len": 46}}}, "doggoned": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "I wouldn \u2019 t be surprised , either , but I \u2019 d be pretty doggoned sore", "context_len": 17}}}, "grasslands": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Then , almost before her crew realized the change , the high grasslands of New Guinea were sweeping beneath her belly", "context_len": 21}}}, "cumulus": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Far to the east lay the Gulf of Papua , with a mass of cumulus clouds tumbling above it", "context_len": 19}}}, "hillbillies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "I \u2019 d like to get a look at one of these native villages , and give the local hillbillies a thrill at the same time", "context_len": 26}}}, "hedgehopping": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "But we won \u2019 t do any hedgehopping with a quarter of a million dollars worth of Fortress", "context_len": 18}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "The explosion of a Jap shell just above the hedgehopping Marauder was answered by a two-second burst of Chick \u2019 s gun", "context_len": 22}}}, "bumpy": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "If the air isn \u2019 t bumpy I might take Rosy down to five hundred feet when and if you spot a thatch-roofed metropolis", "context_len": 24}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "He had done it , flying through air that was bumpy with antiaircraft bursts , ignoring the darting Zero fighters that stabbed at his ship from above", "context_len": 27}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "At 18,000 the air was bumpy", "context_len": 6}}}, "oblongs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Moment by moment the tiny squares and oblongs of different colors took the shape of cultivated gardens", "context_len": 17}}}, "wowing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "We \u2019 re wowing \u2019 em , all right , whooped Hap Newton", "context_len": 13}}}, "scaring": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Just see those grass skirts scatter ! You ought to be ashamed of scaring the ladies this way , Barry !", "context_len": 21}}}, "cockeyed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "If that isn \u2019 t an airport I \u2019 m cockeyed", "context_len": 11}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Wait till that cockeyed ape gets out of sight , can you , Barry ? he asked in a choked voice", "context_len": 21}}}, "interphone": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 12, "context": "Speaking of shark-meat , Fred Marmon \u2019 s voice came over the interphone , would somebody be kind enough to slap a bandage on my back ? It feels like a cubed steak", "context_len": 33}}}, "headsets": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Man all the guns ! Barry \u2019 s order crackled in the headsets", "context_len": 13}}}, "antiaircraft": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 12, "context": "He had done it , flying through air that was bumpy with antiaircraft bursts , ignoring the darting Zero fighters that stabbed at his ship from above", "context_len": 27}}}, "tracer": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "Despite their awkward burden , they broke into a run , conscious that any second might bring the snarling of Zero engines overhead , and a hail of tracer bullets", "context_len": 30}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Now he saw the cause of their erratic dodging a flight of Mitchell B-25 \u2019 s diving at them , with tracer bullets streaking from their guns", "context_len": 27}}}, "flak": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 20, "context": "We won \u2019 t make so many hits , but we \u2019 ll make the Japs disperse , so their flak won \u2019 t be so concentrated", "context_len": 27}}}, "installations": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "Amboina City , with its piers , its big coaling station and its naval installations , offered the biggest group of targets", "context_len": 22}}}, "runways": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "They left the runways looking like a raw , black wound in the earth , with a thick cloud of dust hanging over it", "context_len": 24}}}, "pasting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "Oh , boy , are those Japs due for a royal pasting ! They \u2019 ll probably send in a few squadrons of Australian Havocs and North American Mitchells with regular bomb loads to mop up the shipping in the main harbor", "context_len": 42}}}, "shellfire": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "No shellfire greeted them as they made their run over the Jap airfield", "context_len": 13}}}, "airfield": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 21, "context": "If we hadn \u2019 t come along , they would have left a force here strong enough to take over our airfield and perhaps two or three more", "context_len": 28}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "at Port Darwin airfield", "context_len": 4}}}, "contacted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Soapy Babbitt contacted their new airport on the Mau River and received the answer to come in", "context_len": 17}}}, "airdromes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "In this manner the new airdromes all up and down the coast would be quickly furnished with equipment and defenses", "context_len": 20}}}, "defenses": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "In this manner the new airdromes all up and down the coast would be quickly furnished with equipment and defenses", "context_len": 20}}}, "incendiary": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "In addition , she carried a few score of four-pound incendiary bombs", "context_len": 12}}}, "gibe": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Chick Enders ignored the gibe", "context_len": 5}}}, "yelped": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 8, "context": "We \u2019 ll take the destroyer first , yelped Chick Enders , cuddling his bombsight", "context_len": 15}}}, "fronds": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "The thing is a Jap vessel camouflaged with palm fronds", "context_len": 10}}}, "radio": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Sergeant Babbitt , you will radio the airport what we have seen , and say that we are about to attack", "context_len": 21}, "NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 28, "context": "If that were the case it could mean only one thing : The bombing raid had smashed every installation of value at the air base , including the radio", "context_len": 29}}}, "multiple": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Hard upon his words came the blast a multiple explosion so terrific that it tossed the great Fortress like a feather", "context_len": 21}}}, "ailerons": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Later her crew found that it had torn all the fabric off her ailerons and elevators", "context_len": 16}}}, "hosed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Through the side gun-port Fred Marmon hosed lead at the deck of the nearest transport", "context_len": 15}}}, "superstructure": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "Its superstructure leaped into the air and fell overside in twisted pieces", "context_len": 12}}}, "crippling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The five-hundred-pound bomb struck her stern , crippling her steering gear", "context_len": 11}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "A bullet had gouged her forearm , making a painful but not a crippling wound", "context_len": 15}}}, "phone": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "Course is southwest by south , Curly Levitt \u2019 s voice came over the phone", "context_len": 15}}}, "patching": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Sweet Rosy O \u2019 Grady looks to me as if she needs a little patching before she goes hunting more trouble", "context_len": 21}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Anybody else need patching up ?", "context_len": 6}}}, "superstructures": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "I think they strung their nets over the masts and superstructures and fastened the tops of jungle trees to them", "context_len": 20}}}, "stewing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "All of us would rather be upstairs fighting the weather than stewing in our own juice down here", "context_len": 18}}}, "greaseballs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Rosy \u2019 s four engines were whooping it up as the greaseballs warmed them", "context_len": 14}}}, "bombed": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 38, "context": "Between each announcement , the second lieutenants softly chorused : Woof , woof ! Woof , woof ! When Barry lifted a large baked potato from the serving dish it was announced that The Big Dog is getting bombed up", "context_len": 40}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "When we land , we \u2019 ll stick with Rosy until she \u2019 s bombed and serviced for another run , Chick suggested", "context_len": 23}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The bombed B-26 was blazing on the beach", "context_len": 8}}}, "braked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "His voice faded out beneath the thunder of five thousand horses , as Rosy O \u2019 Grady strained at her braked wheels", "context_len": 22}}}, "blanketing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Suddenly the blanketing mist closed them in completely", "context_len": 8}}}, "vapors": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Among deep blue shadows the rolling vapors gleamed with gold and pink", "context_len": 12}}}, "flier": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "That \u2019 s a sight worth any flier \u2019 s life", "context_len": 11}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "Many a flier has been set adrift without water and lived to tell the tale", "context_len": 15}}}, "hunch": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 7, "context": "In this game a bit of a hunch is sometimes worth a barrel of reasoning", "context_len": 15}}}, "bombsight": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "Before he had finished speaking , Chick \u2019 s fingers were busy at the bombsight \u2019 s knobs , compensating for the intended drop", "context_len": 24}}}, "battlewagon": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Chick , be ready with that bombsight ! We might come out right over a Jap battlewagon !", "context_len": 18}}}, "altimeter": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "Barry kept his eyes glued to the altimeter : a thousand feet , nine hundred , eight hundred Suddenly they were through , with the rolling ocean so near that its white-topped waves seemed to reach up for them", "context_len": 39}}}, "zoomed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "Empty shell cases tumbled smoking to the floor behind Barry , as he zoomed the Fortress into the nearest mass of clouds", "context_len": 22}}}, "bulletproof": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Rows of holes like stitching appeared here and there in the Aichi \u2019 s fuselage , but the greenhouse of the Jap plane appeared bulletproof", "context_len": 25}}}, "hunched": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "Hundreds of creeping insects crawled slimily beneath the roof and now and then dropped upon the shoulders and head of Bomba , as he sat hunched up and brooding beside the body of the dead monkey , Tatuc , leader of the flock", "context_len": 43}}}, "nicked": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Every gunner was nicked and bleeding , yet all stuck by their guns", "context_len": 13}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "With a long , nicked , blue platter , that served as a tray , Mrs", "context_len": 16}}}, "ricocheting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Barry Blake wiped the blood off his forehead , where a ricocheting shell fragment had cut him", "context_len": 17}}}, "resounded": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 32, "context": "Then how was it that those great eyes looking into his , those sweet lips parted as though to speak to him , touched a chord in him that had never before resounded , and increased a thousandfold his longing for that vague and beautiful thing that he had never known ?", "context_len": 52}}}, "streaking": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "Now he saw the cause of their erratic dodging a flight of Mitchell B-25 \u2019 s diving at them , with tracer bullets streaking from their guns", "context_len": 27}}}, "radioed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Circling at reduced speed within the sheltering cloud blanket , Barry radioed a brief report of the convoy \u2019 s location , direction , and probable size", "context_len": 27}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Evidently they had taken off right after his radioed report", "context_len": 10}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Ceiling one thousand feet and dropping fast , came the airfield \u2019 s radioed report", "context_len": 15}}}, "cuddling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "We \u2019 ll take the destroyer first , yelped Chick Enders , cuddling his bombsight", "context_len": 15}}}, "spewing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The destroyer broke in two , spewing into the waves shapeless things that had been men and machinery", "context_len": 18}}}, "freighter": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "Chick had laid his half-ton egg on the freighter \u2019 s stern , blowing it clean off", "context_len": 17}}}, "knobs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Before he had finished speaking , Chick \u2019 s fingers were busy at the bombsight \u2019 s knobs , compensating for the intended drop", "context_len": 24}}}, "compensating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Before he had finished speaking , Chick \u2019 s fingers were busy at the bombsight \u2019 s knobs , compensating for the intended drop", "context_len": 24}}}, "wallop": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "The shock of them was almost simultaneous with the wallop of the bomb blast", "context_len": 14}}}, "gunning": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 14, "context": "I , said John , mean to paint my steers \u2019 yoke , my gunning float , sled , and the boat father made me , if we can get enough ; and I \u2019 ll paint my bedroom , then put some into whitewash and paint the walls", "context_len": 49}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Halloo , Ben ! cried Joe ; thought you had taken an oath that you would never live another spring without a gunning float", "context_len": 24}}}, "cubed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "Speaking of shark-meat , Fred Marmon \u2019 s voice came over the interphone , would somebody be kind enough to slap a bandage on my back ? It feels like a cubed steak", "context_len": 33}}}, "serviced": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "When we land , we \u2019 ll stick with Rosy until she \u2019 s bombed and serviced for another run , Chick suggested", "context_len": 23}}}, "jeep": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "You \u2019 d probably get jeep jitters \u2019 and scare the life out of him if you were at the joy-stick , Hap Newton laughed under his breath", "context_len": 28}}}, "spotting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "And er congratulations on spotting that Jap task force , Blake ! You \u2019 ve probably saved us a lot of ships and fighting men", "context_len": 25}}}, "airfields": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "By that time Allied airfields from all over eastern New Guinea as well as Australia and the Solomons would be sending planes to the attack", "context_len": 25}}}, "unbelievingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "He glanced unbelievingly at his watch", "context_len": 6}}}, "yelps": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Suddenly , above the yelps and growls of the brute , the lad in the tree heard a hail far off in the woods", "context_len": 24}}}, "comber": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Green water crashed on the forecastle as the flagship buried her bow under a giant comber", "context_len": 16}}}, "foredeck": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Again her foredeck dipped , rolled , plunged into the trough of a mighty sea", "context_len": 15}}}, "inbred": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Only their inbred habit of obedience had kept them from shooting themselves or committing hara kiri through the past week of inglorious suffering", "context_len": 23}}}, "troopship": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "She had written to a soldier on board the troopship Crocodile , and he showed the letter to a sailor friend , who remarked : `` That is good : we poor fellows have no friend", "context_len": 36}}}, "inferno": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "Was Casson in that blazing hut ? Was poor Pipina , faithful friend , caught in that flaming inferno ?", "context_len": 20}}}, "blasted": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "Fred Marmon \u2019 s gun blasted the Nip plane an instant before the B-26 plunged into the clouds", "context_len": 18}}}, "sharpshooter": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "From a three-mile height he had hit the wave-tossed Jap ship with the accuracy of a sharpshooter", "context_len": 17}}}, "stabilizer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Rosy O \u2019 Grady \u2019 s stabilizer showed ragged holes , and Cracker Jackson had been stunned by a direct hit on the ball turret", "context_len": 25}}}, "unarmored": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The deadly but unarmored little fighters were torching down all over the sky", "context_len": 13}}}, "torching": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The deadly but unarmored little fighters were torching down all over the sky", "context_len": 13}}}, "smokestacks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "They passed over , from stern to stem , and dropped their bombs at point-blank range sometimes down the enemy \u2019 s smokestacks", "context_len": 23}}}, "fracture": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "That \u2019 s a compound fracture , man ! he replied", "context_len": 11}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "It was a castanha nut , one of those huge , heavy nuts that , falling on a man \u2019 s head , may fracture his skull", "context_len": 27}}}, "gangrene": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "If you don \u2019 t get proper treatment now , it may gangrene", "context_len": 13}}}, "lifeboats": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Between them the sea was covered with rafts , landing barges , lifeboats and wreckage of every description , but they made no attempt to take aboard survivors", "context_len": 28}}}, "throttles": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "In the co-pilot \u2019 s seat , Hap Newton sat nursing the throttles , changing the bomber \u2019 s air speed from moment to moment", "context_len": 25}}}, "banking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Zigzagging , banking , spiralling , he made the big bomber climb like a cat in a fit", "context_len": 18}}}, "spiralling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Zigzagging , banking , spiralling , he made the big bomber climb like a cat in a fit", "context_len": 18}}}, "unorthodox": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Some of her antiaircraft were still firing , but Rosy \u2019 s unorthodox actions fooled them completely", "context_len": 17}}}, "sieves": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Their barges must look like sieves already", "context_len": 7}}}, "bail": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "Personally I \u2019 m for taking the risk , but if there \u2019 s one man who doesn \u2019 t like the idea , we \u2019 ll turn back and bail out over Mau River", "context_len": 35}}}, "tourniquet": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "He dusted a handful of sulfanilamide powder into the wound , removed the tourniquet , and used most of the kit \u2019 s gauze in a snug bandage", "context_len": 28}}}, "atolls": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Get out your charts and see if there aren \u2019 t some atolls or small islands somewhere this side of that weather front", "context_len": 23}}}, "levelheaded": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Don \u2019 t get your hopes too high , their levelheaded navigator warned them", "context_len": 14}}}, "ripcord": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Tony would have a bare chance to live if we pulled his ripcord and chucked him out", "context_len": 17}}}, "unnamed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "It was broken finally by Curly Levitt \u2019 s voice giving Barry the compass course for an unnamed islet that they might hope to reach ahead of the fog", "context_len": 29}}}, "helicopter": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "A helicopter might have found a landing place , but not a bomber with a 90-mile-per-hour landing speed", "context_len": 18}}}, "maniacs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Within her , six men cheered like maniacs", "context_len": 8}}}, "sulfanilamide": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "He dusted a handful of sulfanilamide powder into the wound , removed the tourniquet , and used most of the kit \u2019 s gauze in a snug bandage", "context_len": 28}}}, "demount": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Maybe you \u2019 d better demount one of them and take it along for an emergency", "context_len": 16}}}, "tripod": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Those fifty-caliber babies are too heavy to carry far , or to use without a tripod", "context_len": 16}}}, "unburied": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "On the farther side from their plane the Fortress men found the burned remnants of a native village and a few unburied corpses", "context_len": 23}}}, "scrawny": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "On their return to the Fortress , they frightened a number of scrawny island chickens that flew squawking into the jungle", "context_len": 21}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The old woman sighed and touched her forehead again with her scrawny finger", "context_len": 13}}}, "squawking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "On their return to the Fortress , they frightened a number of scrawny island chickens that flew squawking into the jungle", "context_len": 21}}}, "spuds": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "And mashed spuds and apple pie and", "context_len": 7}}}, "vitamin": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Dinner tonight will be roast wild pig , palm cabbage , and cocoanut milk with a vitamin pill for dessert", "context_len": 20}}}, "porker": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "The others literally cleaned the bones of their little roast porker", "context_len": 11}}}, "campfire": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "Then , by the light of the campfire , they sat about , munched their sandwiches , drank the strong coffee , and talked of their afternoon 's adventure", "context_len": 29}}}, "incoming": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "An incoming wave might cause the huge bomber to ground-loop at the moment of her take-off , but that was a chance that had to be taken", "context_len": 27}}}, "slipstream": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The mighty slipstream ripped jungle foliage and tossed the fragments of her camouflage screen", "context_len": 14}}}, "airspeed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "She rushed down the narrow steel runway , her airspeed gauge climbing fast", "context_len": 13}}}, "windblown": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "When Barry eased back on the controls she lifted her twenty-five tons as lightly as a windblown leaf", "context_len": 18}}}, "woof": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 19, "context": "Bart 's opinion was unexpectedly confirmed the next moment , for the animal turned and uttered a loud `` woof ! '' as it sniffed at the snow at the foot of the bush , evidently in search of something to eat", "context_len": 42}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "At last Flossy was persuaded to be quiet , with the exception of a low grumbling `` woof '' now and then , as if he still thought his wrongs too great to be borne ; and the children returned to their butter", "context_len": 43}}}, "exuberant": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 59, "context": "As he thought of his visit to Roger Eliot \u2019 s home , of his reception by Roger \u2019 s family , of that dinner in the handsome dining room , and of Roger \u2019 s earnest pledge on hearing his story to stand by him and be his friend , a strange and wonderful feeling of lightness and exuberant happiness possessed him and made him long to shout and sing", "context_len": 71}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Hayden was applauded , and his hand was shaken until he repulsed the exuberant crowd that surged around him", "context_len": 19}}}, "woofer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "At this point an exuberant woofer from Texas lost control", "context_len": 10}}}, "effete": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Tilting his head far back , he gave tongue to a genuine coyote howl that raised the hair on the necks of more than one effete Easterner", "context_len": 27}}}, "bumptious": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The bumptious ex-cowboy was penalized by being made to sing Deep in the Heart of Texas with his mouth full of olives", "context_len": 22}}}, "olives": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "The bumptious ex-cowboy was penalized by being made to sing Deep in the Heart of Texas with his mouth full of olives", "context_len": 22}}}, "stressed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The orders from headquarters stressed a high record of bombing hits", "context_len": 11}}}, "assignment": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Rosy O \u2019 Grady \u2019 s crew , all except Tony and Cracker Jackson , were overjoyed at their new assignment", "context_len": 21}}}, "hangar": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "They lay awake talking it over until Barry curtly ordered them to drive it into the hangar and get some sleep", "context_len": 21}}}, "undershot": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "He greeted each of his new crew mates with an undershot smile and a brief Pleased to meet yiz !", "context_len": 20}}}, "tinting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "As the sun rose , tinting the peaks of New Guinea \u2019 s high backbone ahead of them , he turned over the controls to Hap Newton", "context_len": 27}}}, "crowbar": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Those crowbar wrists of yours work swell at the wheel of a Fortress , but this little lady won \u2019 t stand for rough handling", "context_len": 25}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "Bart went to a near railroad scrap heap and selected a long iron rod crowbar crooked at the end", "context_len": 19}}}, "seeped": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "A bitter chill seeped into the plane", "context_len": 7}}}, "masks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Automatically they reached for their oxygen masks", "context_len": 7}}}, "lifesavers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Those things were lifesavers when you got up above 20,000", "context_len": 10}}}, "washbowl": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "We \u2019 re like four tiny flies buzzing across a giant \u2019 s washbowl , Barry thought", "context_len": 17}}}, "smallness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "A flier certainly comes to feel his smallness in time and space !", "context_len": 13}}}, "loafed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The four planes loafed along at about 200 m", "context_len": 9}}}, "conserve": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "Ashati helped him , while Neram went off in search of jaboty eggs so that they could conserve their remaining supply of meat", "context_len": 23}}}, "haziest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "We haven \u2019 t the haziest notion yet what we \u2019 ve come here to do There \u2019 s the field , Hap ! It looks a lot better than the one we left this morning", "context_len": 36}}}, "insultingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "A rather handsome , sleek-haired second lieutenant stared at them insultingly , then turned his back and moved to a farther table", "context_len": 22}}}, "sorehead": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "He \u2019 s just as much of a sorehead as he ever was , muttered Chick Enders", "context_len": 17}}}, "jitters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "You \u2019 d probably get jeep jitters \u2019 and scare the life out of him if you were at the joy-stick , Hap Newton laughed under his breath", "context_len": 28}}}, "uniformed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "They saw no more of Glenn Crayle than his neatly uniformed back until the meal was over and the B-26 bomber officers assembled in the briefing room", "context_len": 27}}}, "briefing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "They saw no more of Glenn Crayle than his neatly uniformed back until the meal was over and the B-26 bomber officers assembled in the briefing room", "context_len": 27}}}, "strategical": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "You are to leave sometime tonight on a mission of high strategical importance", "context_len": 13}}}, "demolition": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "We can not at this moment tell you why its demolition is so important to our war strategy", "context_len": 18}}}, "seaplane": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 13, "context": "Billy Smith , son of an eminent explorer , learns to pilot a seaplane aboard a Naval Air Station , and immediately fares forth upon a series of the most exciting cruises and mysterious quests by air , by land and by sea that have ever fallen to the lot of man", "context_len": 52}}}, "assignments": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "As you receive your assignments , please note them down , gentlemen", "context_len": 12}}}, "duplication": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "With a limited number of bombs , we must have no duplication", "context_len": 12}}}, "coastal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Captain Bartlett , Lieutenant Haskins , and Thurman Smith were given the heavy coastal fortifications just beyond", "context_len": 17}}}, "steersmen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "In these the hundred-odd flying officers were rushed through the spray-filled darkness to a point offshore which the steersmen seemed to find by instinct", "context_len": 24}}}, "huger": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "There lay the carrier , a long , dim shape that grew rapidly huger until the speedboat paused close to her towering side", "context_len": 23}}}, "speedboat": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "There lay the carrier , a long , dim shape that grew rapidly huger until the speedboat paused close to her towering side", "context_len": 23}}}, "boatload": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Each boatload of airmen climbed hurriedly to the dark port that opened into the ship \u2019 s bowels", "context_len": 18}}}, "bunking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The enlisted members of the B-26 crews were already on board , bunking forward with the petty officers", "context_len": 18}}}, "visibility": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "But over the island itself our forts and Liberators will need visibility unlimited", "context_len": 13}}}, "meteorologists": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "If the meteorologists have guessed wrong , it will be just too bad", "context_len": 13}}}, "painstakingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "The brass hats who had planned this secret attack so painstakingly must know what sort of weather they could count on", "context_len": 21}}}, "meteorology": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Meteorology was almost an exact science nowadays", "context_len": 7}}}, "headlines": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Probably the sleek-haired pilot was thinking of this Amboina job as offering a splendid chance to make the news headlines", "context_len": 20}}}, "releases": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The new bomb releases were objects of especial care", "context_len": 9}}}, "radial": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The B-26 was already in the catapult ; her Double Wasp radial motors were roaring at full throttle", "context_len": 18}}}, "jittery": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "I never was so jittery taking off from a bomb-pitted jungle strip", "context_len": 12}}}, "boost": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "Wake up ! you 've struck a higher rung on the ladder , and I 'll wager they 'll boost you fast", "context_len": 22}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "There was risk , whichever way they turned , and to know that every hour cut their distance from the continent by eight or nine more miles was a great boost to their morale", "context_len": 34}}}, "detectors": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Their plane detectors had probably caught the first hum of Barry \u2019 s engines now multiplied by ten or twelve as the catapult launchings proceeded", "context_len": 25}}}, "wispy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "When at last the mist thinned to a few wispy streamers the swift little B-26 fairly hugged the water", "context_len": 19}}}, "reefed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Barry reefed back sharply , gaining altitude in the few precious seconds before the delayed action blast arrived", "context_len": 18}}}, "seaplanes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "He hopped over the little rise between them and found himself above his next objective between forty and fifty Jap seaplanes", "context_len": 21}}}, "engined": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "A few Aichi T98 \u2019 s and a number of single engined Nakajimas made up the rest", "context_len": 17}}}, "cowlings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Barry swept over five of the huge Kawanishis , while Chick Enders and Mickey Rourke ripped at their engine cowlings , floats and keels", "context_len": 24}}}, "keels": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "In England they have boats with keels , masts , and sails , just like sloops and schooners ; they will sail on the wind , and beat to windward as well as the Perseverance", "context_len": 35}}}, "radioman": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Are you telling me , Fred ? the radioman returned", "context_len": 10}}}, "betters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "I won \u2019 t be able to sit down in the presence of my betters for a couple of weeks , anyway", "context_len": 22}}}, "busters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "From far behind came the muffled WHUMP , WHUMP , of block busters falling on Amboina and the Lata airfield", "context_len": 20}}}, "windless": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "The air at that altitude was clear and almost windless", "context_len": 10}}}, "fishhook": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "Say , Lieutenant , came Fred Marmon \u2019 s reply , did you ever try to bandage a man \u2019 s seat with a roll of one-inch gauze ? I might do it if Soapy would hold still , but he \u2019 s wiggling like a worm on a fishhook", "context_len": 50}}}, "jitterbug": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Stand still , you jitterbug !", "context_len": 6}}}, "iodine": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "That iodine you sloshed on me burns like fire", "context_len": 9}}}, "sloshed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "That iodine you sloshed on me burns like fire", "context_len": 9}}}, "guy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Just wait till I start operating on your legs , wise guy !", "context_len": 13}}}, "intercommunication": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "A chorus of chuckles bubbled over the intercommunication system", "context_len": 9}}}, "ribbing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Everyone began ribbing Soapy and Fred , until the two sergeants were forced to join in the laughter at their expense", "context_len": 21}}}, "crackpot": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "That crackpot pilot is intendin \u2019 to give us a scare if he can", "context_len": 14}}}, "oncoming": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "Bern Hayden was in position to receive the ball , and , without removing his eyes from it , he realized that one or both of those oncoming men would be at hand to tackle him if he attempted to run", "context_len": 41}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "Look there , Crayle , he said , pointing to a black triangular fin that showed above the oncoming wave", "context_len": 20}}}, "zoom": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The crazy galoot is going to zoom right under our nose", "context_len": 11}}}, "revved": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Hap revved them up to the limit , but the spin continued", "context_len": 12}}}, "stunting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "He \u2019 s stunting now and waggling his wings at us", "context_len": 11}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "We have nowhere near enough gas to reach Port Darwin , thanks to his stunting , but if we put it with yours , we \u2019 d all be sure of getting home", "context_len": 33}}}, "waggling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "He \u2019 s stunting now and waggling his wings at us", "context_len": 11}}}, "fella": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "You \u2019 re taking the controls but you \u2019 re keeping the interval exactly as it is , fella , Barry Blake declared", "context_len": 23}}}, "skedaddling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Dropping one egg and skedaddling like a scared sparrow isn \u2019 t my idea of fun", "context_len": 16}}}, "octant": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Curly Levitt stepped up to the top gun turret with his octant and took his shot", "context_len": 16}}}, "scuff": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "He shoved the bomber \u2019 s nose down into the cloud scuff", "context_len": 12}}}, "tricycle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The tricycle gear touched the hard packed sand lightly and rolled to a smooth stop", "context_len": 15}}}, "crewmen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Her Jap crewmen were probably more surprised than the Americans", "context_len": 10}}}, "seagull": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Like a startled seagull she swept down the long , straight beach", "context_len": 12}}}, "plummeted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Swathed in fire , he plummeted into the water", "context_len": 9}}}, "functioning": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The crew of Sweet Rosy O \u2019 Grady would remain a smoothly functioning unit as long as it existed", "context_len": 19}}}, "faked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "He was wondering whether Crayle \u2019 s dazed manner was real or faked", "context_len": 13}}}, "unintelligibly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The man was mumbling unintelligibly when they reached him", "context_len": 9}}}, "torched": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "I \u2019 ve been feeling useless ever since that Mitsubishi torched down", "context_len": 12}}}, "stabbing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "As the B-26 climbed , they came in from all angles , stabbing at her with their tracers", "context_len": 18}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "If stabbing eyes could have killed , both would have sunk , mortally wounded , beneath the orchard trees", "context_len": 19}}}, "tracers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "As the B-26 climbed , they came in from all angles , stabbing at her with their tracers", "context_len": 18}}}, "needled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Again and again Barry \u2019 s plane was needled by bullets", "context_len": 11}}}, "intercom": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Barry heard the little Irishman \u2019 s yell over the intercom , and guessed its meaning", "context_len": 16}}}, "patrols": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 27, "context": "The thought made him fiercely determined to outwit the enemy , to bring all these people through the gantlet of Jap boats , planes , and shore patrols", "context_len": 28}}}, "foxing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "This way we \u2019 ll have a swell chance of foxing them", "context_len": 12}}}, "swipe": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Maybe we can swipe a Jap motor launch some night", "context_len": 10}}}, "parachute": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Get out of your parachute harness , and grab a brace", "context_len": 11}}}, "topside": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Fred , you and Soapy Babbitt loosen the topside hatch so it won \u2019 t jam when we come down", "context_len": 20}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Soapy Babbitt and Fred Marmon were first through the topside hatch , by Barry \u2019 s orders", "context_len": 17}}}, "levers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Hap , stand by those levers that spring the rubber rafts", "context_len": 11}}}, "wisecracks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "There were no more wisecracks", "context_len": 5}}}, "inflate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "I \u2019 ll inflate his lifejacket and boost him through the hatch", "context_len": 12}}}, "miscalculate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "If he should miscalculate and strike a crest , his plane would dive like a fish", "context_len": 16}}}, "fixtures": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Fixtures flew from the bulkhead above the radio panel", "context_len": 9}}}, "waterlogged": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "On top of the waterlogged plane , Barry twisted himself around like a cat , to face the hatch", "context_len": 19}}}, "combing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "His feet found a purchase on the hatch combing", "context_len": 9}}}, "headgear": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "She looked spiteful enough to throw it out of the window , but her husband , laughing at her , doffed his worn straw , coolly put on the elaborate headgear , and became thenceforward a target for the quips of the merry idlers about the door", "context_len": 47}}}, "doughnut": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "But the night before we took off from the flat-top I had a dream of floatin \u2019 helpless on a rubber doughnut while the bloody Japs strafed me from the air", "context_len": 31}}}, "waterproofed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "So I brought along a waterproofed tommy-gun , just in case me dream came true ! Ye can laugh at me if yez feel like it , gintlemen", "context_len": 28}}}, "nitwits": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "We \u2019 re the nitwits not to think of something like that ! Barry Blake confessed", "context_len": 16}}}, "eyelid": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Barry \u2019 s left eyelid flickered in a mysterious wink", "context_len": 10}}}, "mouthed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "While the others watched , open mouthed , Barry turned to a small , waterproofed case attached to the side of the raft", "context_len": 23}}}, "futuristic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Opening it he drew out an object that looked like a small alcohol stove built on futuristic lines", "context_len": 18}}}, "quart": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "In an hour we \u2019 ll have a quart and a pint of pure , distilled water", "context_len": 17}}}, "pint": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "Bring that to bear over the western pint of the Junk of Pork , at high-water mark ; then bring the north-west side of Smutty Nose , and the south-east side of Oak Island , just touching on to each other , and you \u2019 ll be on a kelp shoal , where there \u2019 s plenty of rock cod , and where it is so shallow that at low water you can see them bite", "context_len": 76}}}, "distilled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "In an hour we \u2019 ll have a quart and a pint of pure , distilled water", "context_len": 17}}}, "spearhead": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "From the bottom of the bait can Barry took a folded square of muslin and the sharp edged spearhead", "context_len": 19}}}, "gaffing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "There was even a small steel spearhead for gaffing large fish", "context_len": 11}}}, "broil": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Since we \u2019 re so near to land we can afford to use some of our still \u2019 s fuel to broil a tasty fish dinner", "context_len": 26}}}, "ravenously": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "But Bomba ate ravenously of the tapir meat , for he had had nothing to eat since morning of the day before", "context_len": 22}}}, "finny": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Within half an hour fifteen pounds of finny food lay on the bottom of the two rafts", "context_len": 17}}}, "fillets": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Barry solved the problem by cutting the fish into fillets and broiling these on the blade of one of the raft \u2019 s aluminum oars", "context_len": 25}}}, "haw": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "huh": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "So they drank more fish when they got thirsty , huh ! That \u2019 s the best joke I \u2019 ve heard since I was a dodo", "context_len": 27}}}, "diced": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "After making criss-cross cuts through each side of the five pound fish , he pulled the diced flesh from the bones and placed them in the cloth", "context_len": 27}}}, "cocktail": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "But please don \u2019 t ask me to guzzle your fish cocktail while I have a perfectly good still to make my own moonshine water", "context_len": 25}}}, "dribbled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "From the cloth , strongly twisted by Barry and the little sergeant , a stream of watery liquid dribbled into the bait can", "context_len": 23}}}, "drinkable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "It \u2019 s drinkable , the young skipper declared after the first taste , just as that naval officer on the flat-top told me it would be", "context_len": 27}}}, "guzzle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "But please don \u2019 t ask me to guzzle your fish cocktail while I have a perfectly good still to make my own moonshine water", "context_len": 25}}}, "stills": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "While their water stills boiled , the two raft crews began paddling toward the island", "context_len": 15}}}, "rhythmical": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "There was none only the rhythmical roaring of the surf on the smooth beach , and the slap-slap-slap of water against the rafts \u2019 flat bottoms", "context_len": 26}}}, "stiffen": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "I \u2019 m glad you think so , returned the captain ; for we certainly need him to stiffen the line", "context_len": 21}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "The hot wind was blowing so hard that the Good Wolf had to bend his head and stiffen himself on his four legs to stand up against it", "context_len": 28}}}, "catamaran": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 6, "context": "If a Jap patrol spots the catamaran while we \u2019 re gone , your job , and Mickey Rourke \u2019 s , is to fight clear of the beach and push out to sea", "context_len": 34}}}, "mystify": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "The two rubber rafts were left to float ashore and mystify any Jap patrol that might find them", "context_len": 18}}}, "fiber": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "She had brought palm fiber mats to cover the catamaran during the day and make it appear abandoned", "context_len": 18}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "For a moment , Barry had all he could do to keep his crew from tossing the fiber mats aside and standing up to wave", "context_len": 25}}}, "leeway": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Without a keel the catamaran would make a lot of leeway", "context_len": 11}}}, "couples": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The two native couples were quite young , in their \u2019 teens or early twenties", "context_len": 15}}}, "twenties": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "The two native couples were quite young , in their \u2019 teens or early twenties", "context_len": 15}}}, "sculptor": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "As they sat relaxed , balancing with the boat \u2019 s dip and sway , their shapely black bodies would have thrilled any sculptor", "context_len": 24}}}, "gantlet": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "The thought made him fiercely determined to outwit the enemy , to bring all these people through the gantlet of Jap boats , planes , and shore patrols", "context_len": 28}}}, "centerboard": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "But what do you mean by using paddles for a centerboard , Lieutenant ?", "context_len": 14}}}, "lessens": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Every mile that we cover lessens our danger , he declared , and every unnecessary hour we spend in enemy waters increases it", "context_len": 23}}}, "morale": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "There was risk , whichever way they turned , and to know that every hour cut their distance from the continent by eight or nine more miles was a great boost to their morale", "context_len": 34}}}, "teakwood": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Barry flung open the cover of the teakwood box she indicated", "context_len": 11}}}, "pillowcase": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Barry applied a cloth pad to each bullet hole , binding them tightly in place with strips of the old pillowcase", "context_len": 21}}}, "shirtsleeve": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Barry snatched out a threadbare pillowcase and a man \u2019 s ragged white shirtsleeve", "context_len": 14}}}, "puncture": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "A snake was coming toward him , the deadly jaracara , the South American rattlesnake , the slightest puncture of whose fangs meant death !", "context_len": 25}}}, "artery": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "His sword had already dealt with two of the foe , and was again uplifted , when a musket shot , fired at close quarters , severed an artery ; and the work on earth of this gallant man was over", "context_len": 41}}}, "clot": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Fortunately it had missed the larger artery and the blood had already begun to clot", "context_len": 15}}}, "okay": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "When Barry slapped him on the shoulder and told him , Everything \u2019 s okay ! the boy \u2019 s eyes had lost all trace of fright", "context_len": 27}}}, "gouged": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "A bullet had gouged her forearm , making a painful but not a crippling wound", "context_len": 15}}}, "claire": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Claire showed considerable skill in the bandaging", "context_len": 7}}}, "loon": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 38, "context": "I hated to think of this nice girl being scolded , so I took her back as quickly as we had come , she clinging to my back like a crab and making as much noise as a loon", "context_len": 39}}}, "taro": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Cocoanuts , bananas , smoked chicken and taro bread had been stored in the catamaran \u2019 s hollow hulls enough to last the entire company for a week", "context_len": 28}}}, "wolfed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Crayle wolfed down his share and reached for more", "context_len": 9}}}, "predicting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "There was no predicting what mad impulse might seize his twisted brain", "context_len": 12}}}, "mushroomed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Instead of ack-ack a vast pillar of smoke and flame mushroomed up from the smitten jungle", "context_len": 16}}}, "dishing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "So , he exclaimed hoarsely , that \u2019 s the way a real air-blitz sounds and looks from below ! The next time I \u2019 m laying big eggs on Hirohito \u2019 s little boys , I \u2019 ll know better what I \u2019 m dishing out to them !", "context_len": 50}}}, "meager": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "Such a room as this was the best Ben Stone could afford to pay for from his meager savings", "context_len": 19}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Always he hurried on , flogging himself to renewed effort , allowing only the most meager intervals for food and rest", "context_len": 21}}}, "afterglow": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "The night came swiftly , with no clouds to reflect the sun \u2019 s afterglow", "context_len": 15}}}, "loading": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 38, "context": "I tell you another thing you do , said the captain : negroes there use lots of baskets , that they carry on their heads , filled with oranges and other things ; they also use them in loading and unloading vessels , and sometimes they carry them by straps of green hide that go over their shoulders", "context_len": 58}}}, "flashlights": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Where the trail bent sharply to the left , he saw the flicker of flashlights", "context_len": 15}}}, "cratered": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Now , in the dim light , the place looked more like the cratered landscape of the moon than anything on earth", "context_len": 22}}}, "wrecks": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "There must have been a lot of \u2019 em caught on the ground , but I can \u2019 t see more than two or three wrecks from here", "context_len": 28}}}, "blitz": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Boy ! That blitz certainly was thorough", "context_len": 7}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "It was here that a number of Mitsubishi bombers had been lined up when the blitz opened", "context_len": 17}}}, "rimmed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Blasted , leafless trees that rimmed the field bore ghastly witness to the size of the bombs", "context_len": 17}}}, "servicing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Either they had been left there for servicing , or the Japs had felt so secure that they didn \u2019 t bother to scatter their planes around the field at dispersal points", "context_len": 32}}}, "dispersal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Either they had been left there for servicing , or the Japs had felt so secure that they didn \u2019 t bother to scatter their planes around the field at dispersal points", "context_len": 32}}}, "fuselages": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "He and Fred had found two Mitsubishi bombers with engines apparently unhurt and wings not too badly damaged , though the tail assemblies , fuselages and undercarriages were in sad shape", "context_len": 31}}}, "usable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "There aren \u2019 t enough usable parts in the whole line-up to build half a plane , so far as I can see", "context_len": 23}}}, "crazily": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "Half of it went down in a deafening crash , and the other half , in which Bomba \u2019 s room was located , swayed crazily , threatening at every instant to fall to the ground", "context_len": 36}}}, "aileron": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The plane \u2019 s left aileron dangled loosely", "context_len": 8}}}, "undercarriages": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "He and Fred had found two Mitsubishi bombers with engines apparently unhurt and wings not too badly damaged , though the tail assemblies , fuselages and undercarriages were in sad shape", "context_len": 31}}}, "undamaged": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Its greenhouse and rear fuselage were full of holes , but its working parts were undamaged", "context_len": 16}}}, "chow": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Come on let \u2019 s see what sort of chow and sleeping equipment the Japs have left us", "context_len": 18}}}, "flashlight": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "In the Jap officers \u2019 wrecked quarters they discovered a flashlight , and with its help located other things", "context_len": 19}}}, "islanders": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The islanders found all they needed in the flattened tents", "context_len": 10}}}, "canned": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` That 's right , '' agreed Fenn , `` but , as it is , we 'll have to get something soon , unless we want to live on canned stuff", "context_len": 32}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Thereupon Fenn gave over watching the turtles , and , a little later , seated cozily in the tent , the chums partook of a supper of canned baked beans , with hard-tack or pilot biscuit , and coffee", "context_len": 39}}}, "custodian": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Nanu , the wounded native , was made custodian of the tommy-gun while Mickey Rourke was working", "context_len": 17}}}, "stalled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Only Glenn Crayle stalled , when he thought he was not observed", "context_len": 12}}}, "unfilled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "By mid-afternoon one unfilled crater stood between them and freedom , and the workers , except Crayle , were all at the point of exhaustion", "context_len": 25}}}, "maddeningly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Their progress was maddeningly slow , yet they dared not leave the bush", "context_len": 13}}}, "spiralled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Above it the Jap bombers zoomed , and spiralled upward to join their formation", "context_len": 14}}}, "purports": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "It purports to be sent by Lieutenant Barry Blake of the United States Army Air Forces , who \u2019 s been missing since the raid on Amboina", "context_len": 27}}}, "earphones": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Without warning Tex O \u2019 Grady \u2019 s voice rang in the crew \u2019 s earphones", "context_len": 16}}}, "laboratory": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Books , and instruments from the laboratory , '' was Bart 's contribution to the opinions", "context_len": 17}}}, "minerals": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 45, "context": "They were pursued by strange men , when they landed to witness a large grain elevator on fire , and eventually they succeeded in causing the breaking up of a gang of Chinese smugglers , and a band of scoundrels who were secretly taking valuable minerals from a cave , under land owned by a man whom the boys had once befriended", "context_len": 62}}}, "jackknives": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Sometimes it was postage stamps , or postmarks , and again minerals , or jackknives , or butterflies", "context_len": 18}}}, "fad": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 43, "context": "`` I supposed that you 'd be gathering specimens of snowflakes , or samples of dog biscuit by this time , '' for he knew Fenn 's failing , and a month , at most , was the period devoted to any new fad", "context_len": 44}}}, "lizards": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "Then Bart and his two chums put back into the bags the snakes , lizards , turtles , toads and alligators belonging to the professor , and hurried back with them to the school", "context_len": 34}}}, "carbolic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Never mind , Alice will put on some carbolic salve or peroxide , '' said Bart , cheerfully", "context_len": 19}}}, "salve": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "Healing-salve ? fiddlestick ! I \u2019 ve seen Indians cut half to pieces , scalded , and burnt , and get well , and I never saw any salve among them", "context_len": 31}}}, "peroxide": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Never mind , Alice will put on some carbolic salve or peroxide , '' said Bart , cheerfully", "context_len": 19}}}, "cabinets": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` Some time between Friday night and this morning , '' went on the principal , `` this school was entered , and some objects were taken from the science cabinets", "context_len": 31}}}, "damaging": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` While I regret exceedingly that any persons , least of all pupils of this school , whom I may say we suspect , could so far forget themselves as to run the risk of damaging my collection of reptiles , that is not the worst I have to speak about", "context_len": 51}}}, "clews": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` I might add that I have certain clews as to who were the midnight visitors to the school , for one of them dropped his knife , and it has his name on it", "context_len": 35}}}, "gumption": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` No , but when we saw a stranger sneaking into the school , we should have had gumption enough to notify the authorities , '' insisted Bart", "context_len": 28}}}, "versed": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 22, "context": "Here they fitted the rigging , and altered the ship \u2019 s sails into those of a schooner ; and so well versed were Captain Rhines and his son in all nautical matters , that , by dint of splicing and piecing , they managed to get all the standing rigging , and nearly all the running gear , out of the materials of the wreck", "context_len": 66}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "The trail of the Indians was cold , but it was not difficult for one so versed in woodcraft as Bomba to pick it up", "context_len": 25}}}, "effigies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Clayton , the man in charge of the gallery , set the machinery in motion , and the painted effigies began to go around", "context_len": 24}}}, "repeater": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Bart raised the rifle -- a repeater -- to his shoulder , took quick aim , and fired", "context_len": 18}}}, "undefinable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "An undefinable air of suspicion hung over the four chums , though Fenn , from the fact that he had not entered the school , was , more or less , exempt", "context_len": 32}}}, "pulley": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "They were dipped into the rain-water barrel by means of a rope and pulley , and they were cast from `` the terrible height into the awful chasm , '' which ordeal consisted merely in being pushed down a space of about three feet , upon some hay , but being blindfolded was supposed to make up for the difference", "context_len": 60}}}, "geared": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Then they had to climb a steep `` mountain '' which was an old horse tread-mill , geared up unusually high , and finally had to `` drink the terrible cup , '' which was supposed to be some horrible mixture , but which was really only molasses , ginger and water", "context_len": 52}}}, "bottomless": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 1, "context": "The bottomless pit awaits ye , '' threatened Sandy , and then , one at a time , the four were carefully lowered over the side of the wagon , down into some depths , as they supposed , but in reality only a short distance , so strangely are distances rendered when one is blindfolded", "context_len": 56}}}, "squirming": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "Devering looked round at the squirming family and said , `` Another reform -- please get up quietly and push your chairs in to the table , but do n't rise till I do", "context_len": 34}}}, "whopping": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` A snake , this time of year ? Not much , but it 's a whopping big mud turtle , '' cried the fleshy lad", "context_len": 26}}}, "teamster": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 33, "context": "Two days later the boys returned to their winter camp , and , after nearly a month spent in the forest , they packed up , walked in to Cannistota , sent a teamster back for their goods , and took a train for home", "context_len": 45}}}, "jove": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` By jove , I believe it 's snowing ! I felt a flake on my face", "context_len": 17}}}, "flurries": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "It was a dark , lowering afternoon , and , since morning , the promise of more snow had been added to by several flurries of the white flakes", "context_len": 29}}}, "smokeless": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` I 've got some new cartridges , with smokeless powder , and I want to see how they work", "context_len": 20}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "The rifle cracked , but there was no cloud of smoke , for Bart was using his new smokeless cartridges", "context_len": 20}}}, "sliver": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "There was no smoke , for the powder was of the self-consuming type , but a bright sliver of flame shot from the muzzle of the gun , plainly visible in the fast-gathering darkness", "context_len": 34}}}, "hollering": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Yes , lovely , '' said Maggie , `` only except the boys hollering about me", "context_len": 17}}}, "pesky": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` The pesky critters wo n't stand still when they hear shootin '", "context_len": 13}}}, "hollered": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "I hollered some , to let you know I was here , and to quiet the team", "context_len": 17}}}, "countermand": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` Who is it , and is he badly hurt ? '' Then he had to be told how it was , and he hurried back into the house to countermand the order for the physician", "context_len": 36}}}, "alice": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Alice gathered up her bandages , and with her box of remedies retraced her steps", "context_len": 15}}}, "sycamore": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 10, "context": "There were patches of snow on the ground about the sycamore , but an examination of them did not disclose any human footprints , though there were squirrel and rabbit tracks which gave the boys hope that they would get plenty of game when they went to their winter camp", "context_len": 50}}}, "deduce": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` Let 's make another examination of the letter , detective style , and see what we can deduce from it", "context_len": 21}}}, "manilla": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "It took some time to compose a letter to the satisfaction of all four lads , as each one had some suggestion to make , but it was finally done , and enclosed in a strong , manilla envelope , ready to be left in the dead sycamore tree", "context_len": 49}}}, "precautionary": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "The appointed time found them at the place , and , as they came in sight of the tree , they adopted precautionary tactics previously agreed upon", "context_len": 27}}}, "underbrush": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 54, "context": "He had covered about three miles , and was beginning to think that he would have his trip in vain , when , as he went down into a little gully , where the snow lay rather deeper than on the level , he heard a noise , and saw a movement in the underbrush", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 33, "context": "First , the mischievous puppy started a little black and white rabbit , and sent it scampering away as fast as its feet could carry it , rushing after it among all the underbrush and briars , and never heeding the coaxing calls of his little mistresses or the louder and sterner voices of their brothers ; then coming back he rushed into a brook which ran by the way , and after rolling himself in it till the water was dripping from his silky coat , he shook himself and sent a shower of drops over the clean white dresses of the little girls ; and then finding the hole of a wood-chuck , he began scratching and burying himself in the earth in a frenzy to", "context_len": 128}}}, "thaws": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Oh , we 'll wait until it thaws , '' replied Ned", "context_len": 13}}}, "campers": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 56, "context": "William Lang told of his visit to Oak Swamp , and how he had fled at the sight of the boys , fearing they wanted to cause his arrest , and he also mentioned his trips to the mud volcano , and how he had run away at the sound of some one stirring in the campers ' tent , likewise how he had led the boys a chase about the town , just prior to the accident on the pole", "context_len": 81}}}, "stumbles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` If you can find a black stone , after a horse stumbles like that , it 's all right", "context_len": 20}}}, "conversationalist": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "The four chums talked of nothing but camp on the way to the station , and , as for Jed , he was so occupied in watching for signs and omens , good and bad , that he was not a brilliant conversationalist", "context_len": 43}}}, "standpipe": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Just as they approached the depot the driver pulled sharply to the right , turned out of the main road , and urged his horses in a circle around the standpipe of the water tank that supplied the locomotive tenders", "context_len": 40}}}, "tenders": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "Just as they approached the depot the driver pulled sharply to the right , turned out of the main road , and urged his horses in a circle around the standpipe of the water tank that supplied the locomotive tenders", "context_len": 40}}}, "telescoped": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "One glance sufficed to show that whatever the wreck was , it was not a terrible one , for there were no telescoped coaches , and , in fact , none seemed to have left the rails , while as for the engine that , too , seemed to be in its usual place at the head of the coaches", "context_len": 60}}}, "threshed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "The next day the boys went over and dug her potatoes , and threshed some beans and peas , which she had pulled and dried herself", "context_len": 26}}}, "toolbox": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "He hastened to the glass-fronted toolbox , fastened near the ceiling in the center of the car", "context_len": 17}}}, "snack": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` We 'll eat a light lunch , and take a snack with us , '' proposed Frank", "context_len": 18}}}, "hemlock": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 9, "context": "One alone lingered to cut some limbs from a hemlock bush , a whole armful of which he brought with him , and while the rest were passing the water from the beach , and pouring on the blazing wood-pile , he was switching out the flames , as they ran towards the beach , with a dexterity that showed he was no novice in fire-fighting", "context_len": 66}}}, "spouting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` No , '' he added , `` there must be a spouting , boiling spring around here", "context_len": 18}}}, "spouted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "It was now twilight , and as the flame struck one of these trees the little threads of pitch flashed like powder , and the flame , following them up the body of the tree with a rush and roar , spouted from the top in grand style , amid the loud shouts of the performers", "context_len": 56}}}, "geyser": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Up and up the water spouted , and in an instant its comparative purity was changed to a deep mud color , as a miniature geyser of earth and liquid shot upward", "context_len": 32}}}, "hibernate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "It 's as warm as toast around that mud volcano , and they do n't have to hibernate", "context_len": 18}}}, "phenomenons": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "The boiling spring , or mud volcano , as such phenomenons are variously called , consisted , in the main , of a large pool of muddy colored water , lying at the foot of a hill", "context_len": 37}}}, "volcanoes": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 27, "context": "He had climbed to the tops of high mountains and gone down into mines which lay far below the surface of the earth ; had peeped into volcanoes and been shut up among icebergs , at one time had slung his hammock under the trees of a tropical forest , at another had rolled himself in his blankets in the frozen huts of the Esquimaux ; had hunted whales , bears , lions , and tigers ; had passed through all manner of adventures and dangers by land and by sea ; and at last was really coming home , `` tired of his wanderings , to settle down beside his dear old mother and spend the rest of his days with her", "context_len": 123}}}, "geysers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "They are of a type similar to the hot geysers of Yellowstone Park , though not so large or numerous", "context_len": 20}}}, "shotguns": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Fenn and Frank had shotguns , and elected to try to bag some wild turkeys or partridges , so they went off to one side , while Bart and Ned , with their rifles , kept together", "context_len": 37}}}, "prearranged": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "`` We 'll have to get Fenn and Frank to help us , '' said Bart and he fired his rifle three times , in quick succession , and then , after a pause , twice , more slowly -- the prearranged call for assistance", "context_len": 45}}}, "steaks": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "As it was desired to allow the flesh to cool a bit before using it , the campers prepared a meal of the food they had in stock , reserving the bear steaks for supper", "context_len": 35}}}, "negligee": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "The campers rushed from the tent in various stages of negligee , and stared at a track of human footprints , clearly visible in the new-fallen snow", "context_len": 27}}}, "quicksand": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Fellows , he 's fallen in a quicksand ! '' yelled Bart", "context_len": 13}}}, "engulfed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Poor Fenn was engulfed almost to his shoulders , and was struggling ineffectually to get out", "context_len": 16}}}, "ineffectually": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Poor Fenn was engulfed almost to his shoulders , and was struggling ineffectually to get out", "context_len": 16}}}, "pancakes": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` How would pancakes go ? '' inquired Frank with a triumphant air", "context_len": 13}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 37, "context": "`` And I know who did it ! Frank Roscoe , you did this to get even with us for my mistake about putting soap powder in the cocoanut box , so that it got into the pancakes ! But that was n't my fault", "context_len": 45}}}, "flavoring": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` I never heard of cocoanut flavoring in pancakes , '' said Fenn dubiously , `` but maybe it 's all right", "context_len": 22}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "consistency": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "We 'll just put two or three dishes of this batter together in the pan , add a little more flour , and some salt , and it 'll be ready to bake , '' and , as he talked Fenn soon beat up the batter to the right consistency , for he had a knack of cooking", "context_len": 58}}}, "flavored": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` I put soap powder in this to have handy when I washed the dishes , '' explained the fleshy youth , `` and you flavored the cakes with it , Frank", "context_len": 32}}}, "punk": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 39, "context": "It was nearly eight o'clock when Bart got through with his supper , did his house chores , mended a broken toy pistol for one junior brother , made up a list of purchases of torpedoes , baby-crackers and punk for the other , and helped his sisters in various ways", "context_len": 51}}}, "treed": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` Maybe when I do n't come home to dinner the fellows will come looking for me , '' thought the treed lad", "context_len": 23}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "This suited his companions , and soon they were cleaning their guns , sorting cartridges and fitting them in their belts , taking care not to make the mistake Frank did , when he was treed by the wildcat ; and looking to their clothing and hunting boots", "context_len": 48}}}, "strolls": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "`` I do n't mind going alone , '' was Frank 's rejoinder , and this was true , for , however good a chum he might be to the other lads , he was rather an odd chap , and frequently went off on solitary strolls", "context_len": 47}}}, "deers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "You fellows have scared off all the deers and bears , '' and , with a light shotgun over his shoulder Frank set out", "context_len": 24}}}, "restock": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Frank walked on , his eyes alert for a sign of any game that would restock the camp larder , but , for a long time he saw nothing", "context_len": 29}}}, "covey": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "He advanced cautiously , thinking he might flush a covey of birds", "context_len": 12}}}, "whines": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "It had been the whines and barking of the fox that he had heard , and the groans had come when death followed the stroke of the sharp claws of the wildcat , for it was that savage and tawny beast that now glared at Frank -- a wildcat disturbed at its meal", "context_len": 53}}}, "tragedies": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "Tragedies like these were common in the cruel jungle where the life of one thrived on the death of others", "context_len": 20}}}, "preying": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The fox had been preying on a wild turkey , as was evidenced by the half-consumed carcass , and the feathers scattered all about", "context_len": 24}}}, "triggers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "He pulled both triggers together , and the recoil nearly sent him over backwards", "context_len": 14}}}, "loosed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "With a howl of rage the big beast loosed its hold , and dropped back to the earth", "context_len": 18}}}, "crotch": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 46, "context": "From a great pile of drift slabs , logs , and stumps that lay in the cove , Uncle Isaac had made two fires , -- one to sit by , and the other to cook by ; he had made at the small fire a crotch to hang the pot on , and placed stones to keep the fire in place under the kettle", "context_len": 65}}}, "fuse": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 6, "context": "With trembling fingers he lighted the fuse , and then , when it was burning well , he began to lower the paper of powder toward the wildcat", "context_len": 28}}}, "pickerel": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 112, "context": "They said there were great hills of strange-looking rocks , with a flat between them , and a brook running through it ; that the Indians used to live there ; and you could see the stones where they made their fires , and find arrow-heads , and Indian things that were buried there ; and Uncle Isaac knew where ; that somewhere along the side of the brook there was red paint , as good as ever was , and that Uncle Isaac had a room painted with it ; that there were partridges there , and way back was a pond , that the brook ran out of , with pickerel in it", "context_len": 115}}}, "vibrate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "Every nerve in Stone \u2019 s body seemed to vibrate", "context_len": 10}}}, "unpalatable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "They had no milk , of course , but Bart had been thoughtful enough to bring along some sugar , so the beverage was not at all unpalatable", "context_len": 28}}}, "munched": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "He had been ordered to take some cheese in the forenoon , so he bought a piece at about eightpence a pound ; and as he munched it came this thought : cheese wholesale cost but fivepence per pound ; would it not be possible to buy a piece wholesale and sell it to his friends , so that he too might have the benefit of getting it at this low price ?", "context_len": 73}}}, "reversal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "It was quite a reversal of the former situation", "context_len": 9}}}, "shadowing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` Maybe he was shadowing us -- or , rather , you fellows -- '' said Fenn , who , as will be remembered , was not present on that occasion", "context_len": 31}}}, "potful": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "But there was nothing to eat , and no coffee left , though Bart did manage to make a potful of what passed for it out of the grounds of what they had used the night before", "context_len": 37}}}, "snowbank": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` It 's William Perry ! Do n't you know him , fellows ? The lad whose mother took us in at the time of the blizzard -- William Perry -- whom we found in a snowbank in New York ! ''", "context_len": 42}}}, "miscalculation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "When it 's too bad , I stay in camp with the men , but I must have made a miscalculation this time , for I was caught in the storm", "context_len": 31}}}, "potpie": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` And we 'll have roast wild turkey , rabbit stew , partridge potpie and bear steak , also some venison , if Bart has any luck , '' went on Fenn", "context_len": 32}}}, "dyspepsia": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "Such a dinner as it was ! Eaten in the midst of a silent wilderness , with the keen sharp air of winter all about , the boys had appetites that would have been the envy and despair of a person troubled with dyspepsia", "context_len": 44}}}, "carve": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Let me carve it , '' begged Ned , with a look at Frank and Bart", "context_len": 17}}}, "hemlocks": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "All around , the woods were covered with a mantle of white , which had sifted down through the trees , while the firs , spruces , hemlocks and pines , which had heavy foliage that caught the white crystals , were mounds of white", "context_len": 45}}}, "depleted": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` For , while practically prisoners in their tents during the storm , they had eaten considerable , and the cupboard was somewhat depleted", "context_len": 24}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Though Casson and Pipina had had plenty while he was gone , their stock was now greatly depleted and needed replenishing", "context_len": 21}}}, "pheasants": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "No bears were to be seen , but partridge , pheasants and wild turkeys were plentiful , and , in addition to getting a supply of these , they shot several rabbits", "context_len": 32}}}, "cookie": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` That mysterious man again , I 'll wager a cookie ! '' exclaimed Bart", "context_len": 15}}}, "thieving": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` I 'm going to look for the thieving fox , '' declared Fenn", "context_len": 14}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` I hope so too ; but I do n't feel there 's any call on me to go and look after that thieving beggar , nor for you to trouble yourselves about her , after all she 's done to you , '' answered John", "context_len": 46}}}, "gulch": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 51, "context": "But the bears seemed to have warning of the approach of the young Nimrods , for none was in evidence , though there were tracks in the snow , which Bart , enthusiastic sportsman that he was , followed hopefully for some distance , until they disappeared down in a deep gulch , where even he did not think it wise to follow", "context_len": 63}}}, "gouge": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "It was either that or let him gouge me , and I did n't want to do that", "context_len": 18}}}, "slicking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "His chums were busy about camp , cleaning their guns , bringing in wood or water , and `` slicking up '' generally , and so paid little attention to Ned", "context_len": 31}}}, "snooping": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` He may come snooping around", "context_len": 6}}}, "handler": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Sure , '' added Frank , who was no less a good handler of knife and fork", "context_len": 18}}}, "antiseptics": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "It needs attention , and while fresh cool air , such as you get in camp , is good , I shall have to treat the wound with antiseptics", "context_len": 29}}}, "gunmaker": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` I want to take it to a gunmaker 's and get a screw set in a little deeper , '' referring to one on the lock mechanism", "context_len": 28}}}, "linemen": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 24, "context": "A cheer went up from the crowd at the plucky act of the lad , but it was quickly hushed as one of the linemen began to climb the pole , to assist down his comrade who had had such a narrow escape", "context_len": 43}}}, "pliers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Linemen were high up on poles arranging extra wires , and others , below , were passing up the colored bulbs , or pliers , and other tools needed by their mates on the high poles", "context_len": 36}}}, "busier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "There a still busier scene was observed", "context_len": 7}}}, "intersection": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "The lads found a gunsmith in his shop , not far from the intersection of the main streets , and he was soon at work on Bart 's rifle , talking as he worked", "context_len": 34}}}, "juices": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "They get poisonous juices on 'em", "context_len": 6}}}, "collide": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Fenn was walking ahead of his chums , looking in the store windows , and taking care that he did not collide with persons in the crowd , and so injure his sore arm", "context_len": 34}}}, "positiveness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` I should say so , '' returned Hadley , but it was noticeable that he spoke rather dubiously , and not with his usual positiveness", "context_len": 26}}}, "fleshier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Easy , Stumpy , '' murmured the tall lad , as he drew his fleshier companion out of hearing of the crowd in front of the animal store", "context_len": 29}}}, "lineman": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Him ? Oh , you mean him -- that -- why -- -- '' the lineman seemed to be choking -- '' a thief -- stole a diamond bracelet -- -- '' He had to stop to catch his breath , but whether it was from laughter , or because he choked , the lads could not decide", "context_len": 59}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "Pausing in their efforts to get out of the crowd , and take up the pursuit , Bart and Fenn saw the lineman leaning over in a dangerous position", "context_len": 29}}}, "adjustments": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Reaching the middle of the town the man paused to look at a lineman who was on top of a particularly tall pole , making some final adjustments to the wires and lamps", "context_len": 33}}}, "electrical": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "For many years past men of science have been engaged in ascending far up amongst the clouds for the purpose of finding out as much as possible about the various currents of air , the electrical state of the atmosphere , the different kinds of clouds , sound , temperature and such matters", "context_len": 53}}}, "electrician": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 43, "context": "Ca n't you -- hold on there -- do n't go away -- what 's that -- yes , I hear you but -- do n't go away -- do something -- pull out the switch -- do something -- never mind the electrician -- you do it -- do n't go away -- do n't go away -- Ah -- it 's too late ! ''", "context_len": 67}}}, "wiring": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "He thought the wiring was finished , and that the power was to remain turned on", "context_len": 16}}}, "dynamos": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "So he went out , and he left a green man in charge , who does n't know anything about the engines , or dynamos", "context_len": 25}}}, "slant": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "He would have to fire closer to the man , but the bullet would take an outward slant in cutting the wire , and there was less danger of it glancing off and wounding the lineman", "context_len": 36}}}, "lang": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "Lang", "context_len": 1}}}, "collectors": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` He is one of the greatest collectors of turtles in the world , '' and again he winked", "context_len": 19}}}, "postpaid": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Price per volume , 35 cents , postpaid", "context_len": 8}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "For sale by all booksellers , or sent postpaid on receipt of price by the Publishers", "context_len": 16}}}, "derisively": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Ho ! '' he snorted derisively , `` official of the company , eh ? Running things , eh ? ''", "context_len": 22}}}, "entitles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "entitles a man to know his rights", "context_len": 7}}}, "insinuated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Wacker ? '' insinuated Bart pleasantly", "context_len": 6}}}, "subbing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Lemuel Wacker was `` subbing '' as extra for the superannuated old cripple whose sole duty was to wave a flag as trains went by", "context_len": 25}}}, "superannuated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Lemuel Wacker was `` subbing '' as extra for the superannuated old cripple whose sole duty was to wave a flag as trains went by", "context_len": 25}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "King laid down the paper-cutter and turned back sympathetically ; albeit several friends in the old town had kept alive for Emily Hastings 's memory much commendation that she did not yield to her parents ' choice of the superannuated wealthy foreigner they had selected as a husband for her", "context_len": 50}}}, "stirling": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 0, "context": "Stirling later learned that their recipient had expressed herself perfectly delighted with the delicate , beautiful gift , but , being a true lady , Bart 's mother said nothing about the matter to those who would have been glad to spread a little gossip unfavorable to the dowdy society queen of Pleasantville", "context_len": 53}}}, "mealtimes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "For some months , however , his health had not been of the best , and Bart had been glad when he was impressed into service to relieve his father when laid up with his occasional foe , the rheumatism , or to watch the office at mealtimes", "context_len": 48}}}, "handcart": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "A little handcart was among the office equipment , and very often Bart did light delivering", "context_len": 16}}}, "trebled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "On this especial day , however , in addition to the regular freight , Fourth of July and general picnic and celebration goods more than trebled the usual volume , and they had hired a local teamster to assist them", "context_len": 40}}}, "storekeepers": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 49, "context": "The back room was now nearly full of cases of fruit , a grand boxed-up display of fireworks for Colonel Harrington , the village magnate , another for a local club , some minor boxes for private family use , and extra orders from the city for the village storekeepers", "context_len": 50}}}, "railroader": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Why , it 's this way , '' explained the dauntless Lem : `` I 'm an old railroader and a handy man of experience , I am , and I wanted to make a proposition to you", "context_len": 39}}}, "sampled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Why , when I was running the express office at Corydon , we sampled everything that came in", "context_len": 18}}}, "livening": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` See here , '' spoke the man , livening up suddenly", "context_len": 12}}}, "loutish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "He had noticed a little earlier his big brother , Ira , a loutish , overgrown fellow who had gone around with his hands in his pockets sneering at the innocent fun the smaller boys were indulging in , and bragging about his own especial Fourth of July supply of fireworks which were to come from some mysterious source not clearly defined", "context_len": 62}}}, "sixties": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` Old Hurricane '' the relic cannon had been dubbed when what was left of Company C , Second Infantry , came marching back home in the sixties", "context_len": 28}}}, "straddled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "There was not a boy in town who had not straddled the black ungainly relic , or tried to lift the heavy cannon balls that symmetrically surrounded its base support", "context_len": 30}}}, "symmetrically": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "There was not a boy in town who had not straddled the black ungainly relic , or tried to lift the heavy cannon balls that symmetrically surrounded its base support", "context_len": 30}}}, "flagpole": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 59, "context": "`` You may sing just as much as you wish here , '' he said ; `` singing is the cry of the soul , and I hope you will teach my youngsters to warble half as well as you do -- see , they are running up a flag for you , '' and he pointed to a flagpole on the lawn in front of the house", "context_len": 68}}}, "fieldpiece": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Bart made out a long fuse trailing three feet or more over the side of the old fieldpiece", "context_len": 18}}}, "qualm": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Bart gasped , thrilled , and experienced a strange qualm of dismay", "context_len": 12}}}, "portrayed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "It bore the inscription `` 1812 , '' and according to the colonel , portrayed a military man life-size , epaulettes , sword , uniform and all -- his maternal grandfather as he had appeared in the battle scene where he had lost a limb", "context_len": 45}}}, "vortex": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "Again and again he had viewed the fall , entranced , as the spray-crested torrent dashed over the lip of the cliff into the whirling vortex of water beneath that formed the first of the series of great rapids rushing onward to the river", "context_len": 44}}}, "roustabout": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 31, "context": "I do not know his name , but I have seen him several times , '' and Bart thought it best to reveal to his superior all he knew about the roustabout who had warned him of the burglary , who had assisted him in rescuing his father from the burning express shed , and who had vanished suddenly as people began to crowd to the scene of the blaze", "context_len": 70}}}, "outdistancing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "He plunged forward alone , not conscious that he was outdistancing his late companion", "context_len": 14}}}, "pried": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "The inebriate paid his money , chuckled and took the box to one side , hugging it like a pet child , reached over and picked up the hatchet from inside the railing , and pried open the corner of the box", "context_len": 42}}}, "implement": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "But the implement must have been injured in the fall of the tree , for as Bomba drew it taut the frayed string broke with an ominous snap", "context_len": 28}}}, "burglarious": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The object the mysterious robber was utilizing for burglarious purposes , was the signal flag used at the switch shanty where Lem Wacker had been doing substitute duty that day", "context_len": 30}}}, "fizzing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Rockets were fizzing , giant crackers exploding by the pack , and colored chemicals sending out a varied glow", "context_len": 19}}}, "fluster": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Told you so ! '' gasped the latter in a desperate fluster", "context_len": 13}}}, "obliterating": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "Gordon , obliterating the inscription , sent it anonymously to the Coventry relief fund", "context_len": 14}}}, "resonant": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "It was the Fourth of July , and early as it was , the air was resonant with the usual echoes of Independance Day", "context_len": 24}}}, "bonded": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` Father is a bonded employee of the express service", "context_len": 10}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Are you aware that you are offering a bribe to a bonded representative of the express company ? ''", "context_len": 20}}}, "ruminating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Bart stood silently ruminating for some minutes", "context_len": 7}}}, "unpainted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "About one hundred feet away was a rough unpainted shed-like structure", "context_len": 11}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "It was roughly put together , had but one story , was unpainted , and was altogether hardly equal , architecturally , to some of the huts which are to be found among the rocks at the upper end of Manhattan Island", "context_len": 42}}}, "unclaimed": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 29, "context": "It was , however , forty feet from any tracks , and for convenience sake , when the railroad gave up the burned building which they had occupied for unclaimed freight storage , it had been turned over to the express people", "context_len": 42}}}, "littering": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Next he started to carry outside and pile neatly at the blind end of the building all the boards , boxes and other debris littering up the room , swept it , and selected two packing cases and nailed them up into a convenient impromptu desk , manufactured a bench seat out of some loose boards , set his pen , ink and paper in order , and felt quite ready for business", "context_len": 73}}}, "utilize": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "He ran them down to the building he had determined to utilize , temporarily at least , as express headquarters for Pleasantville", "context_len": 22}}}, "tabs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Bart carried back with him a hammer and some nails , a broom , a marking pot and brush , pens , ink and a couple of tabs of paper", "context_len": 30}}}, "flagman": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Evans , '' he hailed , as he recognized the regular flagman on duty for whom Wacker had been substituting for three days past", "context_len": 24}}}, "cuspidor": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The irate old railroader kicked over the wooden cuspidor in disgust", "context_len": 11}}}, "surmising": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Bart went on his way surmising a good deal and suspecting more", "context_len": 12}}}, "extraneous": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "Bart dismissed this matter from his mind and all other disturbing extraneous affairs , massing all his faculties for the time being on getting properly equipped for business", "context_len": 28}}}, "jamb": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "This Bart nailed to the door jamb in such a way that it was visible from three directions", "context_len": 18}}}, "deliveries": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "To-day , again , only about 15 was received by the first four deliveries of letters ; at 5:45 I had for the third time that day prayer with my dear wife , entreating God to help us , and a little after 6 p", "context_len": 45}}}, "perishable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Its honours , they are false ; its knicknacks , they are perishable and useless ; whilst I live I value God 's blessing -- health ; and if you have that , as far as this world goes , you are rich", "context_len": 43}}}, "shipments": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "He had not heard of the fire , and he stared with interest at the ruins as Bart explained that , until some new arrangement was made , express shipments would be accepted and loaded by truck", "context_len": 37}}}, "freezers": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 4, "context": "There were four big freezers of ice cream , one for delivery at the town confectioner 's , one at the drug store soda fountain , and two for the picnic grounds , where an afternoon celebration was on the programme", "context_len": 41}}}, "receipts": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` We have come to demand certified copies of all entries and receipts of this office covering the trunk in question , '' announced the young sprig of the law", "context_len": 30}}}, "losers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` That is a list of losers by the fire , '' explained Bart", "context_len": 14}}}, "diagram": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "As he neared the `` new '' express shed , however , he passed around to its rear , and glancing out of a window there Bart saw that he had come to a halt , and was drawing a diagram of the tracks on a blank page in his memorandum book", "context_len": 52}}}, "importuning": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "He was still keeping up the pursuit and importuning the affronted official as both were lost to view behind a track of freights", "context_len": 23}}}, "switchman": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 9, "context": "Its proprietor was one Silas Green , once a switchman , later a prize fighter , always a hard drinker , and latterly so crippled with rheumatism and liquor that he was just able to get about", "context_len": 37}}}, "bart": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Bart looked up a trifle startled at the sharp hail , ten minutes later", "context_len": 14}}}, "scenting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Wacker ? '' inquired Bart calmly , though scenting trouble in the air", "context_len": 13}}}, "queered": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` You 've queered me ! '' roared Wacker , `` and I 'm going to have satisfaction -- yes , sir", "context_len": 22}}}, "enunciated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Bart started to return with his empty cart the way he had come , but about ten feet from the platform paused for a moment to take in the exceptionally flowery sentiment that was being enunciated by the speaker of the day", "context_len": 42}}}, "cannoneer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "The colonel gave the young express agent an awful look , and in an instant Bart knew that the military man had been informed of the identity of the audacious cannoneer of the evening previous", "context_len": 35}}}, "irrational": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "He 's an irrational firebrand when he takes a dislike to anybody , and Mrs", "context_len": 15}}}, "firebrand": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "He 's an irrational firebrand when he takes a dislike to anybody , and Mrs", "context_len": 15}}}, "devotee": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "The colonel was a pompous , self-important individual , intensely selfish and domineering , and his wife a thoughtless devotee of fashion and society", "context_len": 24}}}, "embroider": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Stirling did some very fine fancy work , and a few months previous to the opening of this tale the magnate 's wife had asked as a favor that she embroider some handkerchiefs as a wedding present for a relative", "context_len": 40}}}, "inclosing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "No glimpse of the Lake House or its cheerful surroundings could be seen , unless one peered around the edge of the inclosing mass of rock , and this the Colonel would not permit the children to do , lest they should fall into the water which washed at the very foot of the pretty retreat", "context_len": 56}}}, "staples": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "The village hardware store was open for the sale of powder , and Bart stopped there on his way back to the express office and purchased a padlock , two keys fitting it , and some stout staples and a hasp", "context_len": 41}}}, "hasp": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "The village hardware store was open for the sale of powder , and Bart stopped there on his way back to the express office and purchased a padlock , two keys fitting it , and some stout staples and a hasp", "context_len": 41}}}, "depress": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "The thoughts of his father 's plight , a haunting dread that Colonel Harrington might make him some trouble , and the uncertainty of continued work in the express service , all combined to depress his mind with anxiety and suspense , and he tried to dismiss the themes by whistling a quiet , soothing tune as he started to get the hammer to put the padlock in place", "context_len": 69}}}, "satirically": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Why do n't you work holidays , Stirling ? '' asked one of them satirically", "context_len": 16}}}, "lacquer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "It was an old-fashioned affair with a simple combination attachment , and so far as Bart could make out had suffered no damage beyond having its coat of lacquer and gilt lettering burned off", "context_len": 34}}}, "impetuousness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "He took the crisp ten-dollar bill , which the superintendent extended with an impetuousness that showed he was a genuine , warm-hearted man under the surface", "context_len": 26}}}, "investment": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` That quarter of a dollar you gave him was a grand investment , Stirling", "context_len": 15}}}, "uncontrollably": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` Oh , sir ! I thank you , '' said Bart spontaneously , and the tears of gladness and pride sprang to his eyes uncontrollably", "context_len": 26}}}, "bonding": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "I do not think the company bonding him will refuse to continue to be his surety", "context_len": 16}}}, "acceptably": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "If , at the end of that time your father is no better , I do not doubt that we will give you the regular appointment , if in the meantime you fill the bill acceptably", "context_len": 36}}}, "itemized": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "He itemized them , including some fifty unclaimed parcels of small bulk that had accumulated during the year", "context_len": 18}}}, "daze": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "When he got back to the colonel , Bart found the latter sitting propped up against the cinder heap , his eyes open , and breathing heavily , but still in a helpless kind of a daze", "context_len": 37}}}, "opulence": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "His great good fortune momentarily overwhelmed him , and he stood staring silently after the superintendent in a grand dream of opulence and ambition", "context_len": 24}}}, "plethoric": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Colonel Harrington shook , as he placed his cane under his arm and took out his big plethoric wallet", "context_len": 19}}}, "attorney": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 16, "context": "May it please Your Honor , said Sleuth loftily , I have been requested by the attorney for the defense to tell my story in my own way , and ere I have finished I will demonstrate to your satisfaction and the satisfaction of every person present that every word I speak has a bearing upon the matter and is necessary to explain the reasons which led up to my deduction involving the before-mentioned Timothy Davis in a network of his own weaving , from which I think he will scarcely escape with ease", "context_len": 94}}}, "dumbly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "He sat down dumbly on the broken chair , his hard , square hands lying helpless in his lap", "context_len": 19}}}, "volcanic": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "Bart saw very plainly that what rankled most with his volcanic visitor was the blow to his pride he had suffered that afternoon at the grove", "context_len": 26}}}, "stockholder": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` I 'm a stockholder in the B", "context_len": 8}}}, "gloating": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "A flash of lightning brighter than the rest showed him the Indian , on whose face was an expression of fiendish gloating", "context_len": 22}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "But how insincere those professions had been on the part of Ruspak and how deeply he had resented the affront to his dignity as a medicine man was evident now by the malignity in his eyes and the gloating smile on his lips", "context_len": 43}}}, "index": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Its index finger was pointed , inflexible as an iron rod , directly at the colonel", "context_len": 16}}}, "cinder": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "When he got back to the colonel , Bart found the latter sitting propped up against the cinder heap , his eyes open , and breathing heavily , but still in a helpless kind of a daze", "context_len": 37}}}, "firecrackers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Carey had to keep the spirited horses in strong check as they passed groups of boys , reckless of the quantity of firecrackers they deliberately fired off as the team neared them", "context_len": 32}}}, "cobblestones": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "The cab swerved and creaked , and the horses ' hoofs beat an alarming tattoo on the cobblestones", "context_len": 18}}}, "rebound": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "The swing the horses made just then sent his feet flying out in a wild circle , but he held on , and the rebound landed him on the seat", "context_len": 30}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "An arrow striking anywhere else would simply rebound from the tough hide without inflicting any material damage", "context_len": 17}}}, "rousted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The colonel had `` rousted '' up somewhat", "context_len": 8}}}, "undiminished": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "The heads of the horses were now held as in a vice , but they snorted and continued to plunge forward with undiminished speed", "context_len": 24}}}, "cabman": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Bart pointed and explained , and the cabman forged ahead with a gratified snort", "context_len": 14}}}, "potion": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Bart could see that either the druggist 's potion or his succeeding violent experience had quite restored the magnate to his original self", "context_len": 23}}}, "abrasion": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "He nursed a slight abrasion on his chin , looked at Bart sheepishly , and then stepped over to a big bowlder and rested against it", "context_len": 26}}}, "fob": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "He would glance at Bart , start to speak , lower his eyes , and , turning pale as he seemed to remember , and turning red as he seemed to realize , would fumble at his watch fob , run his fingers through his hair and act flustered generally", "context_len": 50}}}, "menacingly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "The speaker poked in his wallet and brought out a strip of paper , which Bart recognized as the one he had so menacingly waved in his face an hour previous at the express shed", "context_len": 35}}}, "receipted": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 52, "context": "Dawson to the station , asking for me ; and he told me that an old lady had been to his house yesterday , and after asking a lot of questions , had paid him a hundred and fifty dollars for undertaking the operation on Willie 's eyes , and took a receipted bill from him", "context_len": 56}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Martin , and receipted for by him in the presence of witnesses", "context_len": 12}}}, "negatively": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The colonel cast a penetrating look on Bart , who shook his head negatively", "context_len": 14}}}, "penetratingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The colonel again regarded him penetratingly , and then got into the cab", "context_len": 13}}}, "padlocking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Bart was , therefore , in high spirits as he left the express office , padlocking the door securely", "context_len": 19}}}, "grapevine": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Back at one end were some broken grapevine crates , and it was dim and shadowy there , so he called out", "context_len": 22}}}, "crates": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Back at one end were some broken grapevine crates , and it was dim and shadowy there , so he called out", "context_len": 22}}}, "propounded": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Money , jewelry , and such , I suppose ? '' propounded Lawyer Stebbings , who loaned money at a high rate of interest", "context_len": 25}}}, "staunchly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "It must ! '' declared Bart staunchly , `` See here , I want to ask you a few questions and then I want to give you some advice , or rather tender my very friendly services", "context_len": 37}}}, "scares": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "You 've done something grand in the last twenty-four hours -- do n't lose sight of that in mourning over your sins , if you have any , or in running away from some shadow that scares you", "context_len": 38}}}, "sounder": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "`` Of course you do n't , and wo n't , '' asserted Bart -- '' you want to give me pleasure , though , do n't you ? So you do as I suggest , and I 'll sleep a good deal sounder than if you did n't", "context_len": 49}}}, "magically": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "Suddenly Sobrinini paused , and fixing Bomba with a strange intent gaze , sang in a voice that had magically lost most of its raucous quality and for the moment had become the faint , sad echo of something that had been supremely beautiful -- sang a tender , haunting melody that touched some almost forgotten memory in Bomba \u2019 s heart and filled him with an exquisite pain", "context_len": 69}}}, "notated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "He also notated a green eye-shade , a pair of goggles , and the ingredients for making a dark brown face stain", "context_len": 22}}}, "goggles": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "A green patch and goggles , a deep brown face-stain , and a pair of thin artistically made `` side-burns '' comprised a puzzling make-up", "context_len": 25}}}, "knowingly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 41, "context": "I hope I may burn up right in this shed to-night if I 'm not telling the truth when I say to you that I never touched a dishonored penny , never harmed a soul , never did a wrong thing knowingly", "context_len": 42}}}, "mutterings": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "Her voice died away in disjointed mutterings , and from neither her nor Casson could he get anything clearer that evening nor in the days that followed", "context_len": 27}}}, "crabbed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "When I got back in hiding I spoke out , I told him something -- something that made his crabbed old soul wizen up , something that scared the daylights out of him", "context_len": 33}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "As he quickened his steps , he suddenly realized that he must pass the wretched little tumble-down home of Tige Fletcher , a dirty , crabbed , old recluse , who hated boys because he had been taunted and tormented by them , and who kept two fierce dogs , which were regarded as vicious and dangerous", "context_len": 57}}}, "wizen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "When I got back in hiding I spoke out , I told him something -- something that made his crabbed old soul wizen up , something that scared the daylights out of him", "context_len": 33}}}, "daylights": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "When I got back in hiding I spoke out , I told him something -- something that made his crabbed old soul wizen up , something that scared the daylights out of him", "context_len": 33}}}, "rocket": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "He dashed outside like a rocket , scanned the whole roadway expanse , and darted for the freight yards with the speed of the wind", "context_len": 25}}}, "rebate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "We want a rebate on that bill of lading", "context_len": 9}}}, "lading": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "We want a rebate on that bill of lading", "context_len": 9}}}, "malignantly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Wacker had made the announcement malignantly", "context_len": 6}}}, "shortage": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "I see you note here one hundred and fifty pounds shortage", "context_len": 11}}}, "profundity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Sorry to disturb the profundity of your calculations , Mr", "context_len": 11}}}, "voucher": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The company allowed for this extra service , but Bart had to take a separate voucher for each task done", "context_len": 20}}}, "injected": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Leslie concerning the sale of the unclaimed express packages , he felt a certain spice of pleasant anticipation injected into the business routine", "context_len": 23}}}, "dodger": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Suppose you take charge of this , and get up the matter for a dodger , too", "context_len": 17}}}, "dodgers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "Haven , an ex-editor , had written up a good `` puff '' for a local paper , inserted gratis an exciting comment and anticipation in reference to the impending sale , and Darry and Bob had printed fifteen hundred dodgers on their home press , very neat and presentable in appearance , and these had been judiciously distributed for miles around , and posted up in stores and depots", "context_len": 70}}}, "stunner": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Father was an editor , and I 'll get him to write up a first-class stunner of an advertisement", "context_len": 19}}}, "underbid": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` We can afford to underbid them all , '' declared Darry ; and so the matter was settled", "context_len": 19}}}, "peppers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "They heard that down at Arlington someone was offering them to the storekeepers at one dollar for two bushels , investigated , detected Dale Wacker peddling the peppers from factory bags , and found that his uncle , Lem , was mixed up in the affair", "context_len": 46}}}, "peddling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "They heard that down at Arlington someone was offering them to the storekeepers at one dollar for two bushels , investigated , detected Dale Wacker peddling the peppers from factory bags , and found that his uncle , Lem , was mixed up in the affair", "context_len": 46}}}, "buckboard": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "The same buckboard that had driven up the afternoon previous , came dashing to the platform as McCarthy spoke", "context_len": 19}}}, "ruminatively": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Bart stood so long looking ruminatively at the trunk that Darry Haven finally nudged his arm", "context_len": 16}}}, "shipper": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` That does n't satisfy the shipper if there is any loss", "context_len": 12}}}, "safeguards": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "I feel we ought to be extra careful until we get a new office with proper safeguards , and that expensive outfit staying here all night worries me", "context_len": 28}}}, "acquiesced": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "You set the lantern so it shows things inside , and I 'll keep an eye open , '' acquiesced the watchman", "context_len": 22}}}, "roundhouse": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Jeff was `` the caller '' for the roundhouse", "context_len": 9}}}, "ruminated": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Something 's up , '' ruminated Bart a little excitedly , as he ran down the stairs and opened the front door", "context_len": 23}}}, "irrepressibly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` What is it ? '' cried out Bart irrepressibly", "context_len": 10}}}, "dosed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` It 's guesswork , that , but the fact stands -- I was dosed", "context_len": 15}}}, "skulker": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "I 'm no skulker , as you well know", "context_len": 9}}}, "doped": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "I was doped", "context_len": 3}}}, "dabs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "There was n't , but at the bottom of the pail were two or three little sticky brown dabs", "context_len": 19}}}, "subbed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Lem Wacker subbed for him once , did n't he ? '' inquired Bart pointedly", "context_len": 16}}}, "auditor": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "It made him feel more crestfallen than ever to trace in the way his auditor took it , that he anticipated some pretty lively action when Mrs", "context_len": 27}}}, "apprised": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Harrington was apprised of her loss", "context_len": 6}}}, "disproving": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Bart backed down the steps , feeling secretly that Lem Wacker would have a hard time disproving a connection with the burglary", "context_len": 22}}}, "groggery": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The Sharp Corner was a second-class groggery and boarding house , patronized almost entirely by the poorest and most shiftless class of trackmen", "context_len": 23}}}, "shiftless": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "The Sharp Corner was a second-class groggery and boarding house , patronized almost entirely by the poorest and most shiftless class of trackmen", "context_len": 23}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "At the very start , said Ben , an uncomfortable look on his plain face , I have to confess that my father was always what is called a shiftless man", "context_len": 31}}}, "drinker": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Its proprietor was one Silas Green , once a switchman , later a prize fighter , always a hard drinker , and latterly so crippled with rheumatism and liquor that he was just able to get about", "context_len": 37}}}, "tainted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The dead , tainted air of the den made the young express agent almost faint", "context_len": 15}}}, "squarest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "Every Christmas day since then , Green had regularly sent a jug of liquor to his father , with word by the messenger that it was for `` the squarest man in Pleasantville , who had saved his life", "context_len": 39}}}, "unhitched": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` They sat watching the clock till closing time , one o'clock , went out , unhitched the horse , and drove off", "context_len": 23}}}, "stubs": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "All over the land were blackened stumps and stubs , from which the smoke rose , and among whose roots the fires were smouldering", "context_len": 24}}}, "prevaricate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "In the first place , Wacker would not be likely to talk with him -- if he did , he would be on his guard and prevaricate ; and , lastly , as long as he was asleep he was out of mischief , and helpless to interfere with Bart", "context_len": 50}}}, "consciously": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Bart shrugged his shoulders , but he was consciously uneasy", "context_len": 10}}}, "scientist": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "That venerable gentleman -- antiquarian , scientist and profound scholar -- had a queer little place at the edge of the town where he raised wonderful bees , and grew freak squashes inside glass molds in every grotesque shape imaginable", "context_len": 40}}}, "squashes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "That venerable gentleman -- antiquarian , scientist and profound scholar -- had a queer little place at the edge of the town where he raised wonderful bees , and grew freak squashes inside glass molds in every grotesque shape imaginable", "context_len": 40}}}, "molds": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "That venerable gentleman -- antiquarian , scientist and profound scholar -- had a queer little place at the edge of the town where he raised wonderful bees , and grew freak squashes inside glass molds in every grotesque shape imaginable", "context_len": 40}}}, "nicknames": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` I am going to ask you a foolish question , perhaps , Professor , '' continued Bart , `` for an accurate person like you of course took down only correct names , and not nicknames", "context_len": 37}}}, "solicit": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "His first intention was to get a conveyance at the livery stable , his first impulse to solicit the co-operation of the town police", "context_len": 24}}}, "theorized": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "It seemed probable , Bart theorized , that if they had made for hiding in any of their familiar woodland haunts , they had reached the same by driving through Millville before daylight , and when nobody was astir", "context_len": 39}}}, "woodcutter": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Bart finally found a woodcutter who knew where the Tollivers had had their camping place the week previous", "context_len": 18}}}, "takers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "After that it went begging for fifty cents and trade , and no takers", "context_len": 14}}}, "ornery": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Lem kicked the poor animal around as `` an ornery , no-good brute , '' and had to keep it tied up on his own premises all of the time to evade paying for a license tag", "context_len": 37}}}, "license": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Lem kicked the poor animal around as `` an ornery , no-good brute , '' and had to keep it tied up on his own premises all of the time to evade paying for a license tag", "context_len": 37}}}, "christmas": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Christmas came up to him timorously , sniffed all about his feet , and suddenly wagged its tail and put its feet up on him in a friendly manifestation of delight", "context_len": 31}}}, "jaunt": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Christmas was about twenty yards ahead of Bart at the end of a two miles ' jaunt , when he shied to the extreme edge of the road and drew to his haunches", "context_len": 33}}}, "depressions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Bart followed the tortuous windings of the narrow road , through brush , over hillocks , down into depressions , and finally into the timber", "context_len": 25}}}, "cautiousness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "A natural cautiousness checked him , however , and he was glad of it a minute later as he detected a rustling in the thick undergrowth back of the tree", "context_len": 30}}}, "impersonate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "He had thrown his cap back on his head in a `` sporty , '' off-handish way , and he tried hard to impersonate a reckless young adventurer taking things as they came , and audacious enough to pick up a handy meal anyhow or anywhere", "context_len": 46}}}, "nabob": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Wish I had this layout -- would n't I reach California like a nabob , though ! ''", "context_len": 19}}}, "ostentatiously": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Ransacking his pockets , with a comical , quizzical grin on his face , he produced a solitary nickel , placed it ostentatiously on the tree stump and remarked :", "context_len": 30}}}, "handout": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` Honesty is the best policy -- there you are , landlord ! and much obliged for the handout", "context_len": 19}}}, "jaunty": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 25, "context": "It was a pretty affair , the low basket , softly cushioned , the black ponies with their bright , glittering harness , and the jaunty groom in his neat livery ; but Bessie had no wish to get in it when Miss Adams said , `` Come , Bessie , jump in and take a ride", "context_len": 57}}}, "ingenuousness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Bart 's off-handed ingenuousness had completely disarmed the men", "context_len": 9}}}, "audaciously": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Did you ever see me around here before ? '' chaffed Bart audaciously", "context_len": 14}}}, "quizzes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` You seem to know enough not to answer questions about yourself , '' observed Buck -- '' try and be as clever if anybody quizzes you about this wagon", "context_len": 30}}}, "ingeniously": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` All right -- with a load of garden truck , eh ? '' propounded Bart ingeniously", "context_len": 17}}}, "hilariously": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` Crackey ! '' cried Bart , snapping the whip hilariously -- '' maybe this is one of those story-book happenings where a fellow strikes fame and fortune ! ''", "context_len": 30}}}, "rutty": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "It was not very smooth , and grew more stony and rutty as he proceeded , and there was a sharp climb for the horse as they reached a hilly landscape", "context_len": 31}}}, "gearing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Then Bart made a depressing discovery -- the hind gearing was locked with a chain running from wheel to wheel", "context_len": 20}}}, "pickaxes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "It was a regular construction case , and full of shovels , crowbars , pickaxes , sledges and drills", "context_len": 19}}}, "sledges": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "It was a regular construction case , and full of shovels , crowbars , pickaxes , sledges and drills", "context_len": 19}}}, "responsively": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The handles moved easily and responsively to the touch", "context_len": 9}}}, "terminus": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "`` You see , '' continued the farmer , `` if they handle them carefully at Pleasantville , and see that they catch the early express to the city from there , someone will be waiting to take them in charge at the terminus", "context_len": 44}}}, "bumper": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Fifteen minutes later , wringing wet with rain and perspiration , Bart drove the hand car up to a bumper just behind a little country depot , and leaped to the ground", "context_len": 32}}}, "plumbers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` There 's four men who live here who got the privilege of digging out slate for a big plumbers ' supply house in the city", "context_len": 26}}}, "manifesto": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Here , put your manifesto on that receipt , will you ? '' and Bart drew the slip of paper he had written on in the depot from his pocket", "context_len": 31}}}, "stuart": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Stuart", "context_len": 1}}}, "eyeglass": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Ah , '' he observed , turning upon Bart and critically adjusting a single eyeglass , `` is this the express agent ? ''", "context_len": 25}}}, "transcripts": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "-- Representative , would you kindly inform me for what purpose you want these transcripts ? ''", "context_len": 17}}}, "accredited": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` Sure , '' answered Bart with an off-handed alacrity that was distressing to the responsibility burdened personality of the accredited representative of Monroe , Purcell & Abernethy", "context_len": 28}}}, "mimicry": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "on the two documents , tendered them with exaggerated courtesy to his visitor , who he was well aware knew his name perfectly , and said , with the faintest suggestion of mimicry :", "context_len": 34}}}, "superciliously": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Stuart adjusted his eyeglass and superciliously regarded the document", "context_len": 9}}}, "colleagues": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "They had arranged to give their full time , and Bart noted that never were there more industrious and enthusiastic colleagues", "context_len": 21}}}, "gavel": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "Beside it was a raised platform , and this in turn held a rough board table on which lay a home-made gavel , and beside this was a high desk holding a blank book and a tin box", "context_len": 38}}}, "depots": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 69, "context": "Haven , an ex-editor , had written up a good `` puff '' for a local paper , inserted gratis an exciting comment and anticipation in reference to the impending sale , and Darry and Bob had printed fifteen hundred dodgers on their home press , very neat and presentable in appearance , and these had been judiciously distributed for miles around , and posted up in stores and depots", "context_len": 70}}}, "oculist": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Stirling could now distinguish night from day , and in a few weeks they intended to take him to an expert oculist in the city for special treatment", "context_len": 28}}}, "smokehouse": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` There 'd be two gallons of it -- enough to cover the smokehouse", "context_len": 14}}}, "demijohn": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "The inebriate stood disgustedly looking down at the label on the demijohn he had brought to light : `` Bubbly Spring Mineral Water", "context_len": 23}}}, "gleesome": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "A gleesome roar of merriment interrupted Bart as he called out the second lot", "context_len": 14}}}, "millinery": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The suggestion of millinery made the farmer 's wife a reckless bidder , and the lot brought two dollars", "context_len": 19}}}, "bidder": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "In other words , he confessed that Baker , Bart 's friend and the highest bidder for the mysterious express package , was a prisoner in his barn", "context_len": 28}}}, "doffed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "She looked spiteful enough to throw it out of the window , but her husband , laughing at her , doffed his worn straw , coolly put on the elaborate headgear , and became thenceforward a target for the quips of the merry idlers about the door", "context_len": 47}}}, "tab": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Bart hustled the various packages up for sale and disposition with briskness and dispatch , and Darry was more than busy keeping tab on his record book and piling the cash into the tin box", "context_len": 35}}}, "sourest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Peter Grimm , notoriously the stingiest man in Pleasantville , who raised the sourest apples in the town and spent most of his time watching the boys and picking up what fruit rolled outside of the fence , bided his time with watchful ferret eyes until a promising-looking package came along", "context_len": 51}}}, "ferret": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "Peter Grimm , notoriously the stingiest man in Pleasantville , who raised the sourest apples in the town and spent most of his time watching the boys and picking up what fruit rolled outside of the fence , bided his time with watchful ferret eyes until a promising-looking package came along", "context_len": 51}}}, "tile": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 51, "context": "The farmer with the plug hat he-hawed at the top of his voice , the miserable owner of the eggs got mad at him , some words ensued , the farmer started after him , the egg owner ran , once outside fired an egg which struck the smooth , shiny tile with a splatter , and the farmer came back into the express office holding his nose , bareheaded , and looking for his rejected straw head-covering", "context_len": 78}}}, "splatter": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 54, "context": "The farmer with the plug hat he-hawed at the top of his voice , the miserable owner of the eggs got mad at him , some words ensued , the farmer started after him , the egg owner ran , once outside fired an egg which struck the smooth , shiny tile with a splatter , and the farmer came back into the express office holding his nose , bareheaded , and looking for his rejected straw head-covering", "context_len": 78}}}, "bidders": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "It seemed to contain papers of some kind , and so many bidders had bought old deeds , contracts , plans , manuscripts and the like , utterly valueless to them , that the lot hung at twenty-five cents for several minutes", "context_len": 42}}}, "suspiciousness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "There was a suspiciousness in the action that enhanced Bart 's interest and curiosity , but he preserved his composure", "context_len": 20}}}, "grist": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "There was the usual grist of store packages , which Darry attended to , and several special envelopes", "context_len": 18}}}, "specials": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "He noticed that two of the specials were for the local bank , and the third for the big pickle factory of Martin & Company , at the edge of the town", "context_len": 32}}}, "dallied": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Say , Bart , '' spoke up Darry , as he dallied at the door , `` who was the fellow that bought that last package ? ''", "context_len": 29}}}, "bulldoze": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` He had the idea he could bulldoze me , '' said Bart bluntly , `` and found he could n't", "context_len": 21}}}, "bothers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "What bothers me is , why were both of them so anxious to get this package ? ''", "context_len": 18}}}, "install": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "The inspector asks me to go and see him , take some of our blanks , open a set of books for him , and try and install a system that will bring things around clearer", "context_len": 36}}}, "backer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Peter Pope 's backer -- see ? ''", "context_len": 8}}}, "flabby": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Bart , studying his flabby , uneasy face , wondered what was the matter with the man", "context_len": 17}}}, "perking": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "Yes , there were a pair of them , perking up their heads and tails , with a saucy , jaunty air , which seemed to say , `` Look at me ; here I am to tell you spring is coming", "context_len": 42}}}, "simplified": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "I 've got this business simplified down to a lead pencil and a scratch book , see ? ''", "context_len": 19}}}, "marveled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "He marveled still more as , watching Pope , he noticed he hesitated at the door of the express shed", "context_len": 20}}}, "rustlings": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "Motions as vague as shadows , faint rustlings that could not have been detected by an untrained ear , told him that something or someone was trailing him , keeping step with him , moving as swiftly and silently as he", "context_len": 41}}}, "aspiration": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Ah -- h ! '' breathed the man in a long aspiration of relief and enjoyment , `` that 's better", "context_len": 22}}}, "gossamer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "He took out a long gossamer rain coat that had been used as a pillow", "context_len": 15}}}, "burlap": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` Is n't there ? I 'll investigate , just the same , '' declared Pope , throwing back the trunk cover and delving in the heap of burlap", "context_len": 29}}}, "deadhead": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Careful , my friend ! '' spoke the deadhead sharply", "context_len": 11}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "The professor courteously thanked him and asked him to come and see the show free , and marched off quite contented with the result of his daring deadhead experiment", "context_len": 29}}}, "cobra": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "With the greatest caution he retired slowly from the place , and was just congratulating himself that he was out of danger when he trod on a cobra", "context_len": 28}}}, "carded": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "I want you to instruct the messenger to follow the directions carded on the boxes", "context_len": 15}}}, "marveling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Bart tore it open wonderingly , glancing first at the signature , and marveling at its unusual length", "context_len": 18}}}, "sender": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "256 by afternoon express , for Martin & Company , Pleasantville , contains fifteen thousand dollars in cash , sender Dunn & Son , Importers", "context_len": 25}}}, "tingled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "As he selected the big brown envelope marked `` Martin & Company , '' and bearing the express company 's shining green seals , his fingers tingled", "context_len": 27}}}, "relocked": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Roger Eliot entered , followed by a smooth-faced , middle-aged man ; and the constable , stepping inside , relocked the door and stood grimly on guard", "context_len": 27}}}, "prepaid": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The prepaid charges on the Martin & Company consignment were seven dollars and seventy-five cents , or five cents for every hundred dollars or fraction of it over the first fifty dollars , which was charged for at regular tariff rates , twenty-five cents", "context_len": 44}}}, "tariff": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "The prepaid charges on the Martin & Company consignment were seven dollars and seventy-five cents , or five cents for every hundred dollars or fraction of it over the first fifty dollars , which was charged for at regular tariff rates , twenty-five cents", "context_len": 44}}}, "rates": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "Then Acts of Parliament were passed , providing that gaolers should be paid out of the rates , that prisoners who were found not guilty should be set at liberty at once , that the prisons should be kept clean and healthy , and the prisoners properly clothed and attended to", "context_len": 51}}}, "cindered": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "He could hear a springy vehicle bound over the near tracks , and then its wheels cut the loose cindered road leading up to the express office", "context_len": 27}}}, "unbolting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Come in , '' Bart said , unbolting the door", "context_len": 11}}}, "degrade": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` Please do not degrade yourself and embarrass me , Colonel Harrington , by saying anything further on this score", "context_len": 20}}}, "filaments": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Bart writhed , choked on the loose strangling filaments of cotton , but could not utter a word", "context_len": 18}}}, "cohort": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The colonel stared in marveling astonishment as his cohort sprang through the open doorway", "context_len": 14}}}, "penitentiary": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "Cut this rope instantly that I may pursue him , or I give you my word that , as a partner in his crime , rich as you are , and influential as you are , you shall go to the State penitentiary", "context_len": 43}}}, "bungling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Unsteady and bungling as was his stroke , he severed the rope partly , and Bart burst his bonds free", "context_len": 20}}}, "curdling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Bart halted with a nameless shock , for the utterance was distinctly human and curdling", "context_len": 15}}}, "receding": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "He glanced after the receding train , fancying that Wacker might have got caught under the cars and was being dragged along with them", "context_len": 24}}}, "roadbed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "That roadbed was clear , however", "context_len": 6}}}, "bumpers": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 34, "context": "He knew the watchman , and asked the man to accompany him , explaining as they went along that Lem Wacker had got caught between two freights , was held a prisoner in the bumpers with his foot crushed , and pointed the sufferer out as they neared the freights", "context_len": 50}}}, "tintinnabulations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The last vibrating tintinnabulations of the clock died away as Bart discovered his enemy", "context_len": 14}}}, "jellied": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "One foot was clamped tight , a crushed , jellied mass between two bumpers", "context_len": 14}}}, "direfully": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The speaker , muttering direfully under his breath , was about to slam down the window", "context_len": 16}}}, "tendering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Will you sign this receipt ? '' asked Bart politely , tendering the slip of paper he had prepared at the office for this especial occasion", "context_len": 27}}}, "penciled": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Thank you , '' he added , as the pickle man scrawled a penciled signature at the bottom of the paper", "context_len": 22}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "Had I been present , doubtless , witnessing the discovery of the loot and the message penciled by the agitated hand of the unfortunate prisoner at the bar would have aided me greatly in drawing a correct and accurate deduction", "context_len": 40}}}, "null": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "`` It is , and -- something more ! I call on you and your witnesses to notice that the fifteen thousand dollars was not delivered to me until six minutes after twelve , too late to make the tender legal , which makes the contract null and void", "context_len": 49}}}, "blared": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` Do n't you torture me ! '' cried Wacker irritably -- '' do n't you let him , '' he blared out to the quacking magnate", "context_len": 27}}}, "quacking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` Do n't you torture me ! '' cried Wacker irritably -- '' do n't you let him , '' he blared out to the quacking magnate", "context_len": 27}}}, "temporized": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Bart doubted if the colonel had the courage to carry out his threat , but he temporized with the great man , got him to make enough admissions to somewhat clear the situation , and the long discussion ended with the announcement by Colonel Harrington that he `` would go", "context_len": 50}}}, "admissions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Bart doubted if the colonel had the courage to carry out his threat , but he temporized with the great man , got him to make enough admissions to somewhat clear the situation , and the long discussion ended with the announcement by Colonel Harrington that he `` would go", "context_len": 50}}}, "windowless": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "He took Bart through the beautiful grounds of his sumptuous home , and to a windowless padlocked room in the loft of the stable", "context_len": 24}}}, "caged": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "He tried to engage Baker in conversation , but the latter , his hands free now , paced the room nervously , acting like some caged animal", "context_len": 27}}}, "dishonorably": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 48, "context": "He gave Adams a letter certifying to his having a claim of over three thousand dollars against the property , which he told Adams to show to his rich brother when he died , asserting that , although Colonel Harrington had shamefully neglected him , he would never dishonorably repudiate a claim of that kind", "context_len": 55}}}, "repudiate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "He gave Adams a letter certifying to his having a claim of over three thousand dollars against the property , which he told Adams to show to his rich brother when he died , asserting that , although Colonel Harrington had shamefully neglected him , he would never dishonorably repudiate a claim of that kind", "context_len": 55}}}, "ridiculed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "He tore up the written statement Adams gave him , ridiculed his claims , and , no will being found , sold the place for a song and left Adams an invalid pauper", "context_len": 33}}}, "trumped": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "In the settlement with Colonel Harrington , Baker had received a letter exculpating him totally from the trumped up charge , and a check for five thousand dollars , which money was now held in trust by a bank to provide for little Dorothy 's future", "context_len": 46}}}, "exculpating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "In the settlement with Colonel Harrington , Baker had received a letter exculpating him totally from the trumped up charge , and a check for five thousand dollars , which money was now held in trust by a bank to provide for little Dorothy 's future", "context_len": 46}}}, "gratefulness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "Bart felt much gratified over the way all these tangled strands in the warp and woof of his young life had been straightened out , but he experienced a final blessing that filled him with unutterable joy and gratefulness", "context_len": 39}}}, "subscriber": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "Every subscriber , Junto member or not , paid a sum down to buy books from England , and there was an annual contribution for additional purchases", "context_len": 27}}}, "inconsistently": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Pirate Captain '' is inconsistently capitalized", "context_len": 7}}}, "capitalized": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Pirate Captain '' is inconsistently capitalized", "context_len": 7}}}, "cocoanuts": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 13, "context": "He would eat meat if he were starving , but he preferred the cocoanuts that he had only to break open to get at their succulent contents", "context_len": 27}}}, "leaved": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "And on top of the cliffs were big leaved plants and straight , slender palm trees which waved and waved like spreading green feathers", "context_len": 24}}}, "dwindle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` There is something in the air of desert islands that makes them dwindle away , '' the Good Wolf explained", "context_len": 21}}}, "splashy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "It was so cool and lovely and splashy and it was such fun to pretend he was going to let a wave catch him and then turn and run , shouting and laughing away from it , that for a few moments he almost forgot about the Good Wolf", "context_len": 49}}}, "greener": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "Then they climbed and climbed and climbed and the grass grew greener and thicker and there were flowers growing on every side and bushes with birds singing on them , and the birds were all sorts of lovely colors", "context_len": 39}}}, "gibbered": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "Just at first they almost gibbered because they did not know what to say , but when they were taken below and allowed to wash the smoke and powder off their faces and hands , and then were given cups of tea and muffins and raspberry jam , and then were shown all over the beautiful ship , they could not help but begin to be calm", "context_len": 67}}}, "squeaked": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "squeaky": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "It was a squeaky , gibbering little voice , and it sounded as if it said something very much like this :", "context_len": 22}}}, "gibbering": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "They had proceeded only a short distance when a horrible chattering overhead caused them to look up , and in the branches of a tree they saw two big apes gibbering and grinning at them , with the same awful look in their eyes that had marked the first one they had encountered", "context_len": 53}}}, "brittle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Even if you had a needle and thread to stitch them together they would break to pieces because they are so dry and brittle", "context_len": 24}}}, "chirps": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Blue Crest put her head on one side and gave three cheerful chirps", "context_len": 13}}}, "crannies": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "The small , dirty hut , with the hard ground for its floor , its miserable roof , and chinks and crannies which let in the wind and damp , was no place for a sick child on these cool August nights ; and now that Dolly could be moved without putting her to so much pain , it was thought best it should be done", "context_len": 66}}}, "scabbards": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "A few of them took out their swords and began to flourish them and then seemed suddenly to remember that sword waving might not seem polite , and very quickly put them back into their scabbards", "context_len": 36}}}, "mustaches": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` They have such crooked swords and such curly black mustaches", "context_len": 11}}}, "furry": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 19, "context": "So Doto is glad to see Bomba back ! said the boy as he fondled the animal \u2019 s furry head", "context_len": 21}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Good gracious ! '' I said to the junco , `` what is that furry brown creature curled up and clinging to the skirt of her gown ? ''", "context_len": 30}}}, "untwist": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "I was so fierce all the years before I became polite that I ca n't untwist my face , and when I am perfectly delighted I scowl as if I were going to bite people 's heads off", "context_len": 38}}}, "scowling": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` How they are all scowling , '' said Barty to the Captain", "context_len": 13}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "Who are you calling a coward ? Who are you pushing ? snarled the low-browed chap , scowling his blackest , and assuming his fiercest aspect , his huge hands clenched", "context_len": 31}}}, "smithereens": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` If Baboo Bajorum was to fire a broadside into them now , '' said the Good Wolf , `` he would blow them and their boats into smithereens", "context_len": 29}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 45, "context": "They had got into their boats and were rowing away from their sinking ship , but they plainly did not know which way to go , because they realized that if Baboo Bajorum began to fire his cannons at them he would blow them to smithereens", "context_len": 46}}}, "politest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "He ran to the end of the ship where Baboo Bajorum was standing watching the other ship sinking , and he took off his hat and made his deepest and politest bow", "context_len": 32}}}, "gorilla": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "In fact Barty thought he looked like a very nice gorilla indeed", "context_len": 12}}}, "bandanna": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Then they took off their hats and mopped their foreheads with their red bandanna handkerchiefs", "context_len": 15}}}, "eau": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Then they let down a rope ladder and a bottle of smelling salts and some eau de cologne to restore the Impolite Captain , and by the time he was restored and assisted up the rope ladder all the Polite Pirates were standing lined up on deck ready to receive him and his crew with low sweeping bows", "context_len": 58}}}, "copyrighted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "COPYRIGHTED 1885 BY McLOUGHLIN BROS", "context_len": 5}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "In 1899 we increased this immensely popular series of choice copyrighted books by representative American writers for the young to fifty titles", "context_len": 22}}}, "foreigner": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 41, "context": "King laid down the paper-cutter and turned back sympathetically ; albeit several friends in the old town had kept alive for Emily Hastings 's memory much commendation that she did not yield to her parents ' choice of the superannuated wealthy foreigner they had selected as a husband for her", "context_len": 50}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Bessie , '' said Maggie , as they sat contentedly eating it , `` do you not think foreigner boys are a great deal nicer than home-made boys ? ''", "context_len": 31}}}, "icily": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "King , icily ; `` I really consider the least said on this subject the better , perhaps", "context_len": 18}}}, "injures": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 58, "context": "`` Hold on there , man , '' roared the old gentleman , coming to an abrupt pause in his walk , `` do you mean to say , and do you take me for an idiot , which I should be if I believed it , that the more education a boy gets , the more he injures his chances for life ? ''", "context_len": 65}}}, "tiptoes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` He got up on his tiptoes and actually bestowed a pat on the stately shoulder", "context_len": 16}}}, "balked": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 36, "context": "Since Benjamin liked to read , what would he think of being a printer a trade that deals with pamphlets , books , everything made with words ? The idea appealed to Benjamin , though he balked when he learned he would be apprenticed to his brother until he was twenty", "context_len": 51}}}, "crispest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` I do n't recommend him , '' the old gentleman answered in his crispest manner", "context_len": 16}}}, "recommending": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Bless you , I do n't go about recommending people ; you know that", "context_len": 15}}}, "cabot": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Cabot", "context_len": 1}}}, "nonchalant": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "A very poor thing in my opinion ; but since he 's decided it that way , there 's no more to be said , '' and he waved it off with a nonchalant hand", "context_len": 35}}}, "emphasize": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "Van Meter got off from his chair , stalked up and down the office floor , bringing his hands every now and then smartly together , to emphasize his periods : `` I was but a slip of a lad when I got into the business groove , and I 've never been sorry I drudged it early", "context_len": 58}}}, "drudged": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 55, "context": "Van Meter got off from his chair , stalked up and down the office floor , bringing his hands every now and then smartly together , to emphasize his periods : `` I was but a slip of a lad when I got into the business groove , and I 've never been sorry I drudged it early", "context_len": 58}}}, "legion": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "The friends of the Peppers are legion and the number will be further increased by this book", "context_len": 17}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Of those who have been ready to lay down their lives for the welfare of Great Britain the number is legion", "context_len": 21}}}, "revolutionary": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "A delightful revolutionary romance of life , love , and adventure in old Concord", "context_len": 14}}}, "sizable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "We judge that Sally and her 'Bijah , who , though little , is `` sizable '' enough to meet her wants , will make the acquaintance of those who like a book that is not over-intellectualized nor yet lacking in soundness of heart and penetrating vision into human nature", "context_len": 50}}}, "sensibility": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Tubbs '' will furnish a hearty laugh and a quickened sensibility", "context_len": 11}}}, "botanist": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Beecroft , a skilled botanist and superior artist", "context_len": 8}}}, "stocky": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 30, "context": "As for me , I rather hope Stone does come out all right , for if he stays in the school he may play football , and I reckon a stocky chap like him will just about fill an aching void in the right wing of the line", "context_len": 48}}}, "propounding": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "He had the habit of propounding ancient conundrums and cracking stale jokes", "context_len": 12}}}, "ungrammatically": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Crane was a long , lank , awkward country boy , who spoke ungrammatically , in a drawling , nasal voice", "context_len": 21}}}, "drawling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Crane was a long , lank , awkward country boy , who spoke ungrammatically , in a drawling , nasal voice", "context_len": 21}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Well , said Sile , in his quaint , drawling way , you may git allfired tired an \u2019 sleepy , Sleuth ; but I agree with Chub in thinkin \u2019 it pritty likely Roger is a-takin \u2019 Stone up to his haouse", "context_len": 43}}}, "nasal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Crane was a long , lank , awkward country boy , who spoke ungrammatically , in a drawling , nasal voice", "context_len": 21}}}, "personalities": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 106, "context": "There was Charley Tuttle , called Chub for short , a roly-poly , round-faced , laughing chap , who was munching peanuts ; Tim Davis , nicknamed Spotty , even more freckled than Ben , thin-legged , sly-faced , and minus the two front teeth of his upper jaw ; Sam Rollins , a big , hulking , low-browed fellow , who lost no opportunity to bully smaller boys , generally known as Hunk ; Berlin Barker , a cold blond , rather good-looking , but proud and distant in his bearing ; and others who did not impress the new boy at all with their personalities", "context_len": 107}}}, "rah": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "comradery": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "I can \u2019 t hardly believe I \u2019 m lucky enough to have such a friend , half whispered the boy whose starved heart had yearned all his life for friendship and comradery", "context_len": 33}}}, "soured": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Fortune had not dealt kindly with him in the past , and his nature had been soured by her heavy blows", "context_len": 21}}}, "transpiring": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "The boys drew nearer and gathered about , eager to hear what was to follow , seeing immediately that something unusual was transpiring", "context_len": 23}}}, "reformatory": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 33, "context": "She was right in thinking Ben was not a weak boy nor one to be easily frightened ; but had she known that over him hung the dark , chilling shadow of the reformatory , she could not have wondered at the course he had contemplated pursuing , and she might have hesitated about so freely giving him advice", "context_len": 59}}}, "cuttingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Bern Hayden observed every change , and now he laughed shortly , cuttingly", "context_len": 13}}}, "counterfeiting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "Why , you \u2019 ve all heard of old Abner Stone , who was sent to prison for counterfeiting , and who was shot while trying to escape", "context_len": 28}}}, "numbly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Like one in a trance , he stumbled into the deserted gymnasium and sat alone and wretched on Roger Eliot \u2019 s locker , fumbling numbly at the knotted shoestrings", "context_len": 30}}}, "shoestrings": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "Like one in a trance , he stumbled into the deserted gymnasium and sat alone and wretched on Roger Eliot \u2019 s locker , fumbling numbly at the knotted shoestrings", "context_len": 30}}}, "reattempt": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "Nearly two years had passed , but he believed Lemuel Hayden to be a persistent and vindictive man ; and , having found the fugitive , that man might reattempt to carry out his once-baffled purpose", "context_len": 36}}}, "cowbells": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "The woods were dark and still , while harsh shadows were creeping nearer from the distant pastures where cowbells tinkled", "context_len": 20}}}, "tinkled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "The woods were dark and still , while harsh shadows were creeping nearer from the distant pastures where cowbells tinkled", "context_len": 20}}}, "lonesomely": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "In the grass by the roadside crickets cried lonesomely", "context_len": 9}}}, "pariah": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "Besides the fear of being sent to a reformatory , the one thought that crushed him was that he was doomed forever to be unlike other boys , to have no friends , no companions to be a pariah", "context_len": 39}}}, "picket": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "About two o'clock in the morning they came across a picket of Sepoys , and , thinking it safer not to try and avoid them , went up and asked the way", "context_len": 32}}}, "clapboards": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "As well as the fence , the weather-washed buildings , with loose clapboards here and there , stood greatly in want of paint and repairs", "context_len": 25}}}, "roomer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "The widow regarded as of material assistance in her battle against poverty the rent money of seventy-five cents a week , which her roomer had agreed to pay in advance", "context_len": 30}}}, "hobbling": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 46, "context": "Jimmy Jones , a puny , pale-faced child of eight , permanently lamed by hip disease , which made one leg shorter than the other , was hanging on the rickety gate , as usual , and seemed to be waiting Ben \u2019 s appearance , hobbling out to meet him when he came along the road", "context_len": 57}}}, "uncarpeted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "sp": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "It kep \u2019 him from workin \u2019 reg \u2019 ler an \u2019 kinder sp \u2019 iled his prospects , poor man ! an \u2019 left me in straightened circumstances when he passed away", "context_len": 33}}}, "soapsuds": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "She had entered while talking , bringing with her , besides the odor of food , another odor of soapsuds , which clung to her from her constant labor at the washtubs , where , with hard , backaching toil , she uncomplainingly scrubbed out a subsistence", "context_len": 47}}}, "washtubs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "She had entered while talking , bringing with her , besides the odor of food , another odor of soapsuds , which clung to her from her constant labor at the washtubs , where , with hard , backaching toil , she uncomplainingly scrubbed out a subsistence", "context_len": 47}}}, "uncomplainingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "She had entered while talking , bringing with her , besides the odor of food , another odor of soapsuds , which clung to her from her constant labor at the washtubs , where , with hard , backaching toil , she uncomplainingly scrubbed out a subsistence", "context_len": 47}}}, "rus": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Joel alwus uster say my doughnuts was the best he ever tasted , an \u2019 he did eat a monst \u2019 rus pile of \u2019 em", "context_len": 26}}}, "nohow": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "He don \u2019 t eat much , nohow", "context_len": 8}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Y \u2019 u never told me about your brother , Ben ; fact is , y \u2019 u never told me much about y \u2019 urself , nohow", "context_len": 28}}}, "ur": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Now you set right down , an \u2019 let the victuals stop y \u2019 ur mouth , she laughed", "context_len": 19}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "That dorg was wuth a hundrud dollars , an \u2019 I \u2019 ll make ye pay fer him , ur I \u2019 ll put ye in jail", "context_len": 27}}}, "reveled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Then the doughnuts such doughnuts as they were ! Ben could well understand how the late departed must have fairly reveled in his wife \u2019 s doughnuts ; and , if such perfect productions of the culinary art could produce the result , it was fully comprehensible why Mr", "context_len": 49}}}, "comprehensible": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "Then the doughnuts such doughnuts as they were ! Ben could well understand how the late departed must have fairly reveled in his wife \u2019 s doughnuts ; and , if such perfect productions of the culinary art could produce the result , it was fully comprehensible why Mr", "context_len": 49}}}, "flaky": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The crust was so flaky that it seemed to melt in the boy \u2019 s mouth , and the apple filling had a taste and flavor that had been imparted to it in some magical manner by the genius of the woman who seemed to bestow something sweet and wholesome upon the very atmosphere about her", "context_len": 56}}}, "quarries": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Land ! You don \u2019 t tell ! Why , he \u2019 s the son of Lemuel Hayden , who come here an \u2019 bought the limestone quarries over south of th \u2019 lake", "context_len": 34}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "He ain \u2019 t been here a year yet , but he \u2019 s built buildin \u2019 s an \u2019 run a branch railroad from the main road to the quarries , an \u2019 set things hummin \u2019 in great shape", "context_len": 41}}}, "northing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "I stick to it that I don \u2019 t b \u2019 lieve you ever done northing very bad , an \u2019 if you \u2019 re poor that ain \u2019 t your fault", "context_len": 32}}}, "digressed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Here she paused a moment to take breath , having digressed without intending to do so ; and once more Ben found himself wondering at her splendid courage and the cheerful heart she maintained in spite of troubles and afflictions that might well have crushed and broken the spirit of an ordinary woman", "context_len": 53}}}, "afflictions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "Here she paused a moment to take breath , having digressed without intending to do so ; and once more Ben found himself wondering at her splendid courage and the cheerful heart she maintained in spite of troubles and afflictions that might well have crushed and broken the spirit of an ordinary woman", "context_len": 53}}}, "squarer": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` I do n't say anything against the heathen ; he 's squarer than many a white man I 've met with , and he 's worth a dozen such men as Bill Mosely and Tom Hadley ; but , all the same , he would n't be much in a scrimmage", "context_len": 52}}}, "arraign": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Richardson was about to arraign him before the school", "context_len": 9}}}, "demonstrate": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 36, "context": "May it please Your Honor , said Sleuth loftily , I have been requested by the attorney for the defense to tell my story in my own way , and ere I have finished I will demonstrate to your satisfaction and the satisfaction of every person present that every word I speak has a bearing upon the matter and is necessary to explain the reasons which led up to my deduction involving the before-mentioned Timothy Davis in a network of his own weaving , from which I think he will scarcely escape with ease", "context_len": 94}}}, "unprepossessing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "When the others had finished and taken their seats , he still remained before the board , chalk in hand , an unprepossessing figure as he frowned hopelessly over his task", "context_len": 31}}}, "prof": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 0, "context": "Prof", "context_len": 1}}}, "wrongdoer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "I am not , however , one who thinks there is no chance of reform for a boy or man who has done wrong , and I think it is a fatal mistake to turn a cold shoulder on the repentant wrongdoer", "context_len": 42}}}, "rollins": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Rollins had been astonished , but he knew what was expected of him , and he began to bluster fiercely , taking a step toward Stone , who did not retreat or move", "context_len": 33}}}, "awesome": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "With his fists clenched and his uncomely face awesome to look upon , Ben Stone took one step and stood over Rollins , waiting for him to rise", "context_len": 28}}}, "ashen": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "Casson was now in a state of complete collapse , and Bomba was frightened at the ashen pallor of the old man \u2019 s face", "context_len": 25}}}, "savageness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Not half as hard as he ought to be hit ! exclaimed Ben , with such savageness that the lame lad was frightened", "context_len": 23}}}, "forefeet": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Even the baby lifted one of my forefeet and peered at it knowingly , saying as he did so , `` Heelths open , frogths thound", "context_len": 26}}}, "chittering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Perched on a limb , he uttered a joyful , chittering cry", "context_len": 12}}}, "mellowed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The sound of the bell , mellowed by the distance , seemed full of sadness and disappointment", "context_len": 17}}}, "balsamic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The cool shadows of the forest enfolded him , and the balsamic fragrance of spruce and pine and juniper soothed his troubled spirit", "context_len": 23}}}, "juniper": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "The cool shadows of the forest enfolded him , and the balsamic fragrance of spruce and pine and juniper soothed his troubled spirit", "context_len": 23}}}, "hawthorne": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "In a thicket he saw the scarlet gleam of hawthorne berries", "context_len": 11}}}, "moonseed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "There the blackberried moonseed clambered over the underbrush", "context_len": 8}}}, "hooded": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "When he crossed the brook and pushed on through this undergrowth , his feet and ankles were wet by water spilled from many hooded pitcher plants", "context_len": 26}}}, "marshland": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "At the border of the woods , where meadow and marshland began , he discovered clusters of pale-blue asters mingling with masses of rose-purple blazing star", "context_len": 26}}}, "pokeweed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Before him he sent scurrying a flight of robins , driven from their feast of pigeon berries amid the wine-stained pokeweed leaves", "context_len": 22}}}, "footpath": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Beyond Fletcher \u2019 s house there was a footpath from High street to the academy yard , and this was the course Ben wished to follow", "context_len": 26}}}, "mongrel": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "This time , however , the mongrel was met by a well-directed blow on the nose , and the terrible pain of it took all the fight out of him and sent him slinking and howling away , with his tail curled between his legs", "context_len": 45}}}, "fletcher": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Fletcher , if you escape being arrested and fined yourself , he declared", "context_len": 13}}}, "hovel": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 43, "context": "The old man continued to splutter and snarl and flourish his cane , upon which Tuttle and Cooper made a pretense of skurrying around in great haste for rocks to pelt him with , and he beat a hasty retreat toward his wretched hovel", "context_len": 44}}}, "roger": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Roger opened his lips to speak a word of encouragement , but suddenly decided that silence was best and waited for the other lad to resume", "context_len": 26}}}, "unemotional": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Stone , he said , in his kindly yet unemotional way , I can \u2019 t thank you enough for your brave defense of my sister", "context_len": 26}}}, "nervy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "It was a nervy thing to do , nodded Roger , and you may be sure I won \u2019 t forget it", "context_len": 22}}}, "thickened": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "It is not remarkable that he hesitated over this , or that as the shadows once more thickened in that room he sat for a long time on his trunk , his elbows on his knees and his chin on his hands , gazing blankly at the one leaden window", "context_len": 50}}}, "deduction": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 1, "context": "My deduction of the case is as follows : Eliot has a sister ; this sister is attacked by the vicious dogs of one Fletcher ; Stone rushes to her defense ; he beats off the said dogs and kills one of them ; the before-mentioned Eliot takes his before-mentioned sister home ; he relates to his folks how she was rescued from dire peril and a fearful fate by the before-mentioned Stone ; at once her parents wish to see and thank the said Stone ; Roger is dispatched post haste for the hero of the thrilling and deadly struggle ; said hero is carried off in triumph to the palatial residence of the before-mentioned parents", "context_len": 117}}}, "plaguing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Jack Walker tol \u2019 me he pitched into Hunk Rollins hammer an \u2019 tongs \u2019 cause Hunk was plaguing Jimmy Jones , and he said he was a-going to tell the professor the whole business", "context_len": 35}}}, "quaking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Little did Roger know of his companion \u2019 s inward quaking and apprehension , but it seemed too late to get out of it then , and Stone was compelled to face the ordeal", "context_len": 34}}}, "stableman": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "A stableman took charge of the horse and carriage , and they were met at the door by Amy Eliot , who had been watching for them", "context_len": 27}}}, "eliot": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 0, "context": "Eliot he was mad , \u2019 cause he said I wasn \u2019 t hurt so bad I couldn \u2019 t play ; and I s \u2019 pose he was mad yesterday , too", "context_len": 33}}}, "penuriousness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "He was very free and easy and plain-spoken , yet he had the reputation of being a hard business man , close-fisted to the point of penuriousness in all his dealings", "context_len": 31}}}, "cordwood": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "I was a poor boy myself , and I had a pretty hard time of it cutting cordwood and hoop-poles in winter and working wherever I could earn a dollar in summer ; but I stuck to it , and I managed to pull through all right", "context_len": 47}}}, "blighting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "For the time he forgot the cloud hanging over him , forgot Bernard Hayden and the blighting past , forgot that he was an outcast who could never again cross the threshold of Oakdale academy save to face disgrace and expulsion", "context_len": 41}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "It did not seem possible that such good fortune could come to him at last , just when , to all appearances , his hard luck had culminated in blighting disaster", "context_len": 31}}}, "facets": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "On the sideboard facets of cut glass sparkled and gleamed with many diamond colors", "context_len": 14}}}, "porti\u00e8res": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Silken tapestries hung as porti\u00e8res at the entrances of various rooms that opened from the main hall", "context_len": 17}}}, "jointed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "On a table were more magazines and books , part of a jointed fishing-rod , and a reel over which Roger had been puttering , as it did not run with the noiseless freedom that was necessary fully to please him", "context_len": 41}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "Look a \u2019 the rotten old fishin \u2019 tackle I \u2019 ve got in that bo \u2019 t ; if I had money I \u2019 d buy an elegant jointed rod , a triple action reel , a silk line , and any amount of hooks and flies and baits", "context_len": 50}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "So the planks of these boats are jointed , and the nails are clinched , and draw them as tight as a hoop does a barrel", "context_len": 26}}}, "puttering": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "On a table were more magazines and books , part of a jointed fishing-rod , and a reel over which Roger had been puttering , as it did not run with the noiseless freedom that was necessary fully to please him", "context_len": 41}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "I wager my head that they \u2019 ll both of them come over here and make it : what a good time they will have puttering over it , and passing their compliments upon each other ! It \u2019 s my opinion , that when them old men die they won \u2019 t be buried with their wives , but alongside of each other", "context_len": 64}}}, "priggishness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Ben found himself wondering that this fellow who had so many things apparently all a boy \u2019 s heart could desire should be so free-and-easy and should mingle every day without the least air of priggishness or superiority with other lads in much humbler circumstances", "context_len": 45}}}, "boundlessly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "This view of Roger \u2019 s domestic life , this glimpse of his home and its seeming luxuries , together with a knowledge of his unassuming ways , led Stone \u2019 s respect and admiration for him to increase boundlessly", "context_len": 40}}}, "biffed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "I thought likely you did when they told me how you biffed Hunk Rollins", "context_len": 14}}}, "scrapper": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Rollins is a scrapper , you know , although it is a fact that he usually picks his fights with smaller chaps", "context_len": 22}}}, "bullyragging": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Richardson caught you thumping Rollins when the fellow was bullyragging that lame kid ? Is that it , Stone ?", "context_len": 20}}}, "unbosoming": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Ben could not doubt the honest candor of his companion , but he shrunk from unbosoming himself , dreading to narrate the unpleasant story of the events which had made both Bern Hayden and his father his uncompromising enemies and had forced him to flee like a criminal from his native village in order to escape being sent to the State Reformatory", "context_len": 62}}}, "planner": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "He was not a bad man , but he was careless and neglectful , a poor planner and a poor provider", "context_len": 21}}}, "fiddling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "The silence in the barn was so profound that he could hear crickets fiddling in the thickets of brown , rain-washed grass outside", "context_len": 23}}}, "violinist": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "I used to tell him he would surely become a great violinist some day", "context_len": 14}}}, "engraver": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Driggs kept a little shop where he did watch and clock repairing , and he was noted for his skill as an engraver", "context_len": 23}}}, "counterfeiters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Several members of an organized body of counterfeiters had been captured , but these men did not manufacture their dies , and the Secret Service agents had traced the latter to Fairfield", "context_len": 32}}}, "bondsmen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Neither of them was able to find bondsmen , and so they went to jail", "context_len": 15}}}, "prosecutor": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The prosecutor did his best to confuse me , and when he failed he sarcastically complimented me on having learned my lesson well", "context_len": 23}}}, "deride": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "But most I came to hate Bern Hayden , who was a leader among the village boys , and who never lost a chance to taunt and deride me and call me the son of a jail-bird", "context_len": 37}}}, "disfavor": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "He took charge of the funeral , but almost as soon as he stepped off the train in Hilton he heard what a bad boy I was , and he looked on me with disfavor", "context_len": 35}}}, "baldwin": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Baldwin have been awfully good to me , and sometime , if I ever can , I \u2019 m going to pay them back for it", "context_len": 26}}}, "flub": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "Fine nothing ! Do you think that will be anything compared with what you did for me ? I should say not ! If I didn \u2019 t do that much I \u2019 d be a poor flub", "context_len": 38}}}, "culminated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "It did not seem possible that such good fortune could come to him at last , just when , to all appearances , his hard luck had culminated in blighting disaster", "context_len": 31}}}, "unfriendliness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "Every boy in the gathering turned to look at Ben as he drew near , and had he observed he must have seen there was nothing of unfriendliness in their faces", "context_len": 31}}}, "smarty": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "precariously": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Richardson rose beside his desk , on which he tapped lightly with his knuckles , and surveyed the scholars over his spectacles , which seemed to cling precariously to the tip of his thin , aquiline nose", "context_len": 37}}}, "brazen": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Hunk knew , and in an effort to appear unconcerned and a trifle defiant he was openly brazen", "context_len": 18}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` I do n't know what you mean , '' answered Mosely , determined to brazen it out and keep the horses if possible , for he was lazy and a pedestrian tramp would not have suited him very well", "context_len": 40}}}, "mistreated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "The principal continued by commending with some warmth the individual whose impulses led him , regardless of personal danger or the chance of being misunderstood , to stand up in defense of one who was being mistreated and abused", "context_len": 39}}}, "seatmate": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "Walker , Stone \u2019 s seatmate at school , was promptly sent out by the coach ; and the little fellow came running without hesitation , trembling with excitement , delighted because he was to have a chance in the game", "context_len": 41}}}, "sibilated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Gee ! sibilated Sleuth Piper", "context_len": 5}}}, "blight": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Do you believe that the errors of a parent should blight the life of his son ?", "context_len": 17}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "So the headhunters had achieved their end at last ! They had captured the helpless old man whom their superstition had led them to regard as a Man of Evil , a magician whose spells had brought blight on their crops and sickness to their people", "context_len": 46}}}, "unreasonably": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "You \u2019 re unreasonably angry now , Bern , but if you \u2019 ll take time to cool off and think it over , I \u2019 m confident you \u2019 ll perceive the mistake you \u2019 re disposed to make", "context_len": 40}}}, "punts": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "He was able to catch punts on the dead run ; when he fell on the ball he got it cleanly , never once permitting it to bound away from him ; and he could kick , too , his sturdy right leg sending the pigskin sailing far through the air", "context_len": 51}}}, "backfield": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "Again and again it was the players in the backfield , Eliot , Hayden or Barker , who checked the assaults and prevented still larger gains", "context_len": 26}}}, "humored": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Roger , however , seemed to consider this very natural , and laughed at him in a good humored way", "context_len": 20}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Fred laughed as if he would never stop when he heard of this , and humored the joke all he could", "context_len": 21}}}, "huffed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "His discomfiture was increased when he heard Berlin Barker telling some of the boys that he considered it a great misfortune that Hayden should become huffed and leave the team", "context_len": 30}}}, "prophesy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "He \u2019 ll think this over when he cools down , and I prophesy that he will come back", "context_len": 19}}}, "simmer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "You just better let things simmer along , and they \u2019 ll come out all right", "context_len": 16}}}, "repellently": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "I \u2019 m not at all interested in your team , Eliot , said Bern repellently", "context_len": 16}}}, "scheduling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Oakdale Academy Football Team , Dear Sir : Replying to yours of the 13th regarding the scheduling of one or more games between Oakdale and Clearport , would say that we have an open date on next Saturday , the 29th , and will play you here in Clearport if you care to come", "context_len": 54}}}, "autocrat": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Say , you \u2019 re a regular autocrat , ain \u2019 t ye ? You \u2019 re going to try to run things your way with an iron hand , ain \u2019 t ye ? Mebbe you \u2019 ll find out", "context_len": 41}}}, "prevarication": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "No prevarication , no shuffling would do here ; if he said anything , if he answered at all , it must be the truth and nothing but the truth", "context_len": 30}}}, "unpatriotic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "A chap who is so unpatriotic that he will quit his team because he had a personal grudge against some fellow on it is of no real value to the team anyhow ; and when he seeks to lead others to follow his example he \u2019 s worse than a traitor", "context_len": 51}}}, "stubbornness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "He has all of old Urian Eliot \u2019 s stubbornness", "context_len": 10}}}, "czar": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "You seem to fancy yourself a perfect czar , Roger", "context_len": 10}}}, "quailing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "His stout shoulders were squared , his feet planted firmly , and he fronted his foe without a symptom of quailing", "context_len": 21}}}, "purplish": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 48, "context": "There was the giant mora tree , two hundred feet high , aglow with clusters of scarlet blossoms , feathery palms , the bright yellow trumpet flower with blooms so large that they were worn as hats by the Indian women and children , huge fuchsias with their purplish tubular bells , heliotrope , verbenas , orchids , glowing with all the colors of the rainbow", "context_len": 66}}}, "transact": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "I \u2019 ve some private business to transact with this person", "context_len": 11}}}, "mulish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Eliot is mulish , and , having taken a stand , he dislikes to turn back ; but I know and others know that he would rejoice to be rid of you", "context_len": 32}}}, "masked": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "But masked foes , emeralds , and falling mountains are all in the day \u2019 s fun for these Adventure Boys", "context_len": 21}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "He follows this up , watches a masked duelist and , with the help of a girl , catches the murderer who justifies his deed on the plea of Slow Vengeance", "context_len": 31}}}, "harmoniously": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "In football practice , when necessary , they worked together harmoniously enough for the accomplishment of the plays in which they were involved", "context_len": 23}}}, "delegation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Merwin and a delegation , this pair held themselves aloof , finally walking up to the hotel behind the rest of the crowd", "context_len": 23}}}, "snobbishness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "As an exhibition of snobbishness , he said disgustedly , that was the limit", "context_len": 14}}}, "realizes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "There \u2019 s only one , returned the captain , who is at all dangerous , and I have an idea he realizes he can \u2019 t afford to make any real trouble", "context_len": 33}}}, "intensified": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "Later he found her son , Frank , and in their joint adventures the boys grew strongly attached to each other , and Frank \u2019 s stories of the wonders of civilization intensified Bomba \u2019 s longings to see those things for himself", "context_len": 43}}}, "checkerboard": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Between those posts the regular white chalk marks made a huge checkerboard", "context_len": 12}}}, "plunk": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Plunk ! Clearport \u2019 s full back , Ramsdal , kicked off , booting the ball into the teeth of the wind", "context_len": 22}}}, "buoyed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "But she was buoyed up by the thought that Richard Dewey was somewhere in the State , and that the two messengers whom she had sent out would eventually find him", "context_len": 31}}}, "sprinter": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Spotty was a really fast runner , being able to get over the ground with his thin legs in a way that should have given him a reputation as a sprinter", "context_len": 31}}}, "bucko": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Arrh , me bucko ! Good avening , grinned Carney", "context_len": 10}}}, "bayed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Merwin , who played quarter for his team , bayed a signal", "context_len": 12}}}, "crinkle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Winton , watching from his position at the side of the field , permitted a crinkle of a smile to flit across his face , even though he realized that the splendid and surprising defense had been accomplished , almost unaided , by two players", "context_len": 45}}}, "unmasked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Later certain weak spots which the coach was fearful of might be unmasked", "context_len": 13}}}, "locals": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 41, "context": "Tricked by a crisscross in the second scrimmage , Oakdale permitted Oakes to get round the right end , Spotty Davis being effectively and easily blocked by Stoker , while Crane let Butters through , and the left tackle of the locals flung himself before Hayden , preventing a tackle", "context_len": 50}}}, "resumption": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "In less than two minutes after the resumption of play the spectators perceived that a great change had taken place in the home team , for the Clearporters had returned to the field firmly resolved to redeem themselves , and they went into the struggle with a snap and dash that temporarily swept the visitors off their feet", "context_len": 58}}}, "punted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "The ball was punted out and caught , and Oakes held it for Ramsdal to lift it with a sure and handsome kick over the crossbar", "context_len": 26}}}, "crossbar": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "The ball was punted out and caught , and Oakes held it for Ramsdal to lift it with a sure and handsome kick over the crossbar", "context_len": 26}}}, "tiered": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "They had carried him off the field , and he was lying on a blanket behind the tiered seats , over the upper tier of which bent a row of sympathetic faces", "context_len": 32}}}, "overexerting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Did you think you had points enough ? Have you a notion that there \u2019 s danger of overexerting yourselves ? You should have had two more touchdowns , at least", "context_len": 31}}}, "touchdowns": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Did you think you had points enough ? Have you a notion that there \u2019 s danger of overexerting yourselves ? You should have had two more touchdowns , at least", "context_len": 31}}}, "hookworm": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Clearport was growing better toward the last of it , and you fellows acted as if you had caught the hookworm", "context_len": 21}}}, "trespasser": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "You trespasser on my sacred preserves ! I should have thought to say that myself", "context_len": 15}}}, "slug": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "I saw you , said Winton , in a low , calm tone , I saw you slug Stone on the jaw with your fist , Hayden ; it \u2019 s useless to deny it", "context_len": 35}}}, "slugged": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "Furthermore , what do you imagine the fellows would think of you if they even suspected that you had tried to knock Stone out and you might have succeeded if the half hadn \u2019 t ended just as you slugged him", "context_len": 41}}}, "crisscross": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "Even though Oakdale fought every inch of the ground , being at last fully aroused to the danger , Clearport repeatedly worked the crisscross with good effect and brought into play still another well-executed forward pass that landed them up against the goal-line , where , after being held for two downs , they finally pushed the ball over by barely six inches", "context_len": 63}}}, "stoker": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "Stoker laughed at Walker , but the little chap soon demonstrated that he was on the field to do his handsomest as long as he lasted ; and , despite the greater weight of the opposing end , he was able to keep the fellow busy", "context_len": 46}}}, "quarterback": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Merwin made a successful quarterback run ; Boothby gained a little ground through center ; and then Stone , breaking through Carney , slammed a runner down for a loss", "context_len": 30}}}, "dampened": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Right on top of this the locals were penalized for holding , but the rising courage of the visitors was dampened when the home team pulled off a handsome forward pass that yielded double the distance needed", "context_len": 37}}}, "chiding": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "If the captain thought so , however , he succeeded marvelously well in hiding his feelings , trying his best all the time to brace his teammates up , encouraging the equally staunch , chiding a few who showed symptoms of wavering , and entreating one or two who apparently had lost heart", "context_len": 53}}}, "fowling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "To add to the dismay of the visitors , they were penalized for fowling on their own thirty yard line , and the distance thus lost made the situation seem absolutely hopeless", "context_len": 32}}}, "jab": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Nevertheless , he had given his left ankle a wrench , and every step hurt like the jab of a knife", "context_len": 21}}}, "tackler": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "With his teeth set , he hugged the ball beneath one arm , the other thrown out stiffly to fend off a dark figure he saw coming at him ; and he left the would-be tackler jarred , dazed and knocked to his knees", "context_len": 44}}}, "palpitant": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Once more every spectator was standing , and from opposite sides of the field came cries of dismay and wildly palpitant shouts of joyous encouragement", "context_len": 25}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Then good nights were called , the big car swung slowly round and rolled away , and Jimmy came hopping forth , palpitant to know about the game", "context_len": 28}}}, "tufted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The sensation of gliding softly along as he lay back against the tufted leather cushions brought him a feeling of great satisfaction and peace", "context_len": 24}}}, "redly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The sun , peeping redly over the western rim of the world , smiled upon him , and nowhere in all the sky was there a cloud , even as large as a man \u2019 s hand", "context_len": 37}}}, "cal": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "I don \u2019 t cal \u2019 late I \u2019 ll ever have no opportunity to ride in one of them things , an \u2019 I guess I \u2019 d be scat to death if I did , \u2019 cause they go so fast", "context_len": 43}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "I don \u2019 t s \u2019 pose y \u2019 u have et anything since y \u2019 u was locked up ; but I cal \u2019 lated you \u2019 d git off , an \u2019 I \u2019 ve got Sadie tendin \u2019 a big roast , an \u2019 we \u2019 ll have a feed that \u2019 ll give y \u2019 u injunjesshun , which I guess y \u2019 u can stand once if Joel , my late departed , could endure it all his born days", "context_len": 85}}}, "covertly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Watching him , the widow understood , and covertly , even while she talked in the same cheerful , optimistic strain , she wiped her eyes more than once with the corner of her apron", "context_len": 35}}}, "optimistic": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Watching him , the widow understood , and covertly , even while she talked in the same cheerful , optimistic strain , she wiped her eyes more than once with the corner of her apron", "context_len": 35}}}, "jibe": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 51, "context": "I \u2019 m glad y \u2019 u have got some comp \u2019 ny to cheer y \u2019 u up , for sometimes y \u2019 u do sort of seem to need it , an \u2019 I know I can \u2019 t just fill the bill ; for old folks never do jibe in proper an \u2019 sympathetic with young folks", "context_len": 60}}}, "mom": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "He \u2019 s hungry , mom", "context_len": 6}}}, "unleashed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Pilot , unleashed , frisked before them", "context_len": 7}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The forces of the jungle would now be unleashed upon him , and in the matter of mere physical strength they were stronger than he was", "context_len": 26}}}, "blinkers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Say , what \u2019 s the matter with his blinkers ? They look awful funny", "context_len": 15}}}, "posed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Well , not much ; he just sorter knocked you , and I s \u2019 posed that was \u2019 cause he was sore on you", "context_len": 25}}}, "triple": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "Look a \u2019 the rotten old fishin \u2019 tackle I \u2019 ve got in that bo \u2019 t ; if I had money I \u2019 d buy an elegant jointed rod , a triple action reel , a silk line , and any amount of hooks and flies and baits", "context_len": 50}}}, "footwear": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Stone went down into the village to purchase a pair of shoes for Jerry , whose footwear was almost wholly gone to the uppers", "context_len": 24}}}, "uppers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "The soles of his boots were worn to pieces , and so long a time did he wear them without a change that the uppers stuck firmly to his legs ; and , though the boots were soaked in hot water , the skin came away when they were taken off", "context_len": 51}}}, "scudded": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "At the gate Davis paused an instant to glance back ; but Ben had disappeared , and Spotty scudded away into the gathering twilight", "context_len": 24}}}, "jones": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "JONES :", "context_len": 2}}}, "soughing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "A raw wind , chill and dank , came from the east , soughing fitfully through the bare limbs of the trees and sending fallen leaves scurrying along the ground", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "In the distance could be heard something like the soughing of a great wind", "context_len": 14}}}, "dazzles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "An exile from home , splendor dazzles in vain ; O ! give me my lowly thatch cottage again ; A charm from the skies seems to hallow us there , Which , seek through the world , is ne \u2019 er met with elsewhere", "context_len": 45}}}, "hallow": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "An exile from home , splendor dazzles in vain ; O ! give me my lowly thatch cottage again ; A charm from the skies seems to hallow us there , Which , seek through the world , is ne \u2019 er met with elsewhere", "context_len": 45}}}, "perverts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Fear , which frequently perverts the soundest judgment , had forced him , without reasoning or sober thought , into this flight by night", "context_len": 24}}}, "dampness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The dampness in the atmosphere added to the penetration of the chill , and Jerry \u2019 s teeth chattered", "context_len": 19}}}, "leering": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "The swift-falling darkness , the knowledge that danger was all about them , that insane enemies were skulking perhaps behind a tree , leering at them from branches overhead , crouching in ambush behind a concealing bush or shrub , at any moment to reach out a hairy arm -- all these things combined to fill them with terror", "context_len": 59}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The ugly old house leering at him with its open doorway tempted him , and he whipped in", "context_len": 18}}}, "cobwebby": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "High up in the peak of the barn the morning light streamed in through a broken , dusty , cobwebby window ; with the passing of the night the storm had passed also , and the new day was bright and fair", "context_len": 42}}}, "unclosed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Jerry awoke ; the sightless eyes unclosed and a faint smile crept over his face", "context_len": 15}}}, "beatings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Both lay perfectly still , scarcely breathing , but unable to repress the heavy beatings of their hearts", "context_len": 18}}}, "bluffer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Purty good bluffer , grinned Abel Hubbard", "context_len": 7}}}, "cumber": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "Mebbe \u2019 twas an oversight we wa \u2019 n \u2019 t give \u2019 orders to fetch ye , for you might serve as a witness against him ; but , having neither authority nor room , we won \u2019 t cumber ourselves with ye", "context_len": 44}}}, "slickness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "While avoiding or refusing to answer his questions , the two men had craftily sought to lead him into compromising statements ; failing in which , they disappointedly told each other that his attempt at slickness would do him no good", "context_len": 41}}}, "kindhearted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "He \u2019 s a good boy , an \u2019 a kindhearted boy", "context_len": 12}}}, "jailbird": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "He \u2019 s been up to things wuss \u2019 n that , and his father before him was a jailbird", "context_len": 20}}}, "pucker": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "You jest keep up your pucker , Ben , an \u2019 don \u2019 t lose courage ; for you \u2019 ve got some friends left , an \u2019 they \u2019 re goin \u2019 to do everything they can for y \u2019 u", "context_len": 42}}}, "reopened": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "He had eaten some of the beans and one of the doughnuts when Hubbard reopened the door on a crack and thrust in a pitcher of water , which he left standing upon the floor", "context_len": 35}}}, "pilfered": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "They were broken open and pilfered while football practice was in progress last night", "context_len": 14}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "At the gymnasium I made a close inspection of the pilfered lockers , being there to obtain a clew of some sort , a desire which was amply rewarded", "context_len": 29}}}, "purchasing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "In doing so , he went back to the cause of Jerry \u2019 s flight from the home of his dead uncle , explaining how the blind lad had been pursued even to Oakdale , and how while purchasing that pair of shoes Ben had learned that a man had arrived in the town and made inquiries for the fugitive", "context_len": 60}}}, "telephoning": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "He left this morning , after doing a lot of telephoning", "context_len": 11}}}, "optimism": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Their hands met again , and once more Eliot \u2019 s firm , friendly grip imparted some of his own optimism and strength", "context_len": 23}}}, "concisely": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "The clerk had done his part , the case had been stated , and Lawyer Frances , representing the prosecution , had made his opening , telling plainly and concisely what he would attempt to prove", "context_len": 36}}}, "chirography": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Your Honor , said the prosecuting attorney , the chirography is that of a person writing in great haste , and therefore somewhat difficult to read", "context_len": 26}}}, "apprehending": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "Pickle went on to tell how , urged by Bern Hayden and his father , he had set out at once to trace the fugitives , and had finally succeeded , through the discovery of the blind boy \u2019 s little dog , in apprehending Ben some miles beyond Barville", "context_len": 50}}}, "slick": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "I says to Constable Hubbard , says I , He \u2019 s a slick critter , an \u2019", "context_len": 18}}}, "rifled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "With the others he had gone into the gymnasium , where two lockers , his and Eliot \u2019 s , had been discovered broken open and rifled", "context_len": 27}}}, "misinformed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "And , if I am not misinformed , you had some trouble with him there , did you not ?", "context_len": 20}}}, "elusive": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "In endeavoring to do this he had been led a hard chase , failing more than once by the smallest margin in getting his hands upon the elusive boy , and in the end the pursuit had brought him to Oakdale", "context_len": 41}}}, "peel": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "To peel off my togs", "context_len": 5}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "She do n't know how , and I think there 's something the matter with her , '' said Bessie : and , stripping the peel from the fruit , she placed it once more in Dolly 's hand", "context_len": 39}}}, "analytical": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "Beneath the battery of wondering eyes turned upon him Sleuth bore himself proudly , for he felt that at last his hour had come the hour in which he would demonstrate to the confusion of those who had sneered at him that he really possessed the keen , penetrating , analytical mind of a great detective", "context_len": 56}}}, "underrated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "Indeed , there was an expression of dignity and sagacity in Piper \u2019 s face which surprised those who knew him best and led more than one to fancy it possible he had underrated the lad", "context_len": 36}}}, "sleuth": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "But , by a series of clever moves on the part of the young sleuth , the crime is solved and the diamond found in a most unusual hiding place", "context_len": 30}}}, "plaintiff": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "Within the locker of the plaintiff , Bernard Hayden , I discovered , attached to the end of a protruding nail , a shred of cloth apparently torn from the coat-sleeve of some person who had reached into that locker", "context_len": 40}}}, "appertaining": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 52, "context": "Being personally acquainted with the before-mentioned parties , what was more natural than that , on hearing that this heinous crime had been committed , I should become profoundly interested in the case and should resolve to give it my earnest attention with the determination of solving the deep , dark mystery appertaining thereunto ?", "context_len": 55}}}, "loot": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Upon my making further inquiries in a careless , offhand manner , Davis told me how the loot had been found hidden beneath the mattress two watches , two rings , and the exact sum of nine dollars and sixty-eight cents , including a five dollar bill and a two dollar bill", "context_len": 52}}}, "schoolmate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "I then proceeded to interview my fellow schoolmate , the plaintiff , Bernard Hayden ; but he likewise received me with extreme ungraciousness , informing me that I had better mind my own business", "context_len": 34}}}, "ungraciousness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "I then proceeded to interview my fellow schoolmate , the plaintiff , Bernard Hayden ; but he likewise received me with extreme ungraciousness , informing me that I had better mind my own business", "context_len": 34}}}, "defendant": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 47, "context": "As far as I could learn , proceeded Sleuth , not another person outside of those who were present at the time of the discovery of the swag knew exactly what sum of money had been found hidden beneath the straw mattress in the room of the defendant", "context_len": 48}}}, "withholding": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "To me it seemed very remarkable indeed that the latter by which , if you please , I refer to Bernard Hayden should impart such information to Spotty Davis , with whom he had never been on terms of close comradeship , while withholding the desired knowledge from me", "context_len": 49}}}, "offhand": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Upon my making further inquiries in a careless , offhand manner , Davis told me how the loot had been found hidden beneath the mattress two watches , two rings , and the exact sum of nine dollars and sixty-eight cents , including a five dollar bill and a two dollar bill", "context_len": 52}}}, "swag": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "As far as I could learn , proceeded Sleuth , not another person outside of those who were present at the time of the discovery of the swag knew exactly what sum of money had been found hidden beneath the straw mattress in the room of the defendant", "context_len": 48}}}, "discrepancy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "It is true that , by comparison of their losses , Bern Hayden and Roger Eliot had stated the amount of money stolen ; and here comes the discrepancy which set me at work upon a clew of vast importance", "context_len": 40}}}, "exhibits": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 46, "context": "It was indeed a kingly tree , thirteen feet and a half in circumference at the roots , bearing its enormous coronal of leaves in that symmetry of proportions which this tree ( seen nowhere in its perfection but in the North American forests ) sometimes exhibits", "context_len": 47}}}, "bloodhound": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "By this time , Your Honor , I was absolutely convinced that I was on the right trail , and thenceforth I shadowed the suspect with the persistence of a bloodhound , never once letting him escape from beneath my hawklike eye", "context_len": 42}}}, "insouciant": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "I was at his heels , lingering at a little distance in a careless , insouciant manner ; and from the open doorway of Mr", "context_len": 25}}}, "premium": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "The moment my eagle eye fell upon it I knew it was a coin on which there was a premium , as it bore the date of 1878", "context_len": 28}}}, "courtroom": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "Leaving the courtroom , Ben and Jerry were escorted by a triumphal procession all the way to Mrs", "context_len": 18}}}, "deductions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Never mind your deductions , young man , interrupted the justice", "context_len": 11}}}, "lated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "I don \u2019 t s \u2019 pose y \u2019 u have et anything since y \u2019 u was locked up ; but I cal \u2019 lated you \u2019 d git off , an \u2019 I \u2019 ve got Sadie tendin \u2019 a big roast , an \u2019 we \u2019 ll have a feed that \u2019 ll give y \u2019 u injunjesshun , which I guess y \u2019 u can stand once if Joel , my late departed , could endure it all his born days", "context_len": 85}}}, "perfected": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "It seems that your father , in those years while he worked so privately in his home , was engaged upon a very clever invention , which he had practically perfected at the time of his unfortunate arrest", "context_len": 38}}}, "exonerates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Ere he died , being satisfied beyond doubt that there was no hope for him , he made a confession which fully exonerates your father and clears his name of the stigma upon it", "context_len": 34}}}, "avarice": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 54, "context": "Lands to mercy ! Now ain \u2019 t that jest amazin \u2019 ! Here I \u2019 ve been entertainin \u2019 under my roof a couple of heirs to wealth ! Three thousand dollars ! Fifteen hundred dollars apiece ! Why , it puts y \u2019 u both beyond the touch of the tooth of avarice", "context_len": 55}}}, "booksellers": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 3, "context": "Several apprentices to booksellers with whom he made friends obligingly loaned him volumes from their masters \u2019 shelves", "context_len": 18}}}, "hayes": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "HAYES", "context_len": 1}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Hayes", "context_len": 1}}}, "drake": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "DRAKE", "context_len": 1}}}, "newsboy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` No , Jake , '' he answered with a smile , `` I did n't meet a newsboy", "context_len": 19}}}, "circumvented": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Ben here told me how she circumvented him at the Astor House over in York", "context_len": 15}}}, "shoemaker": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 35, "context": "And now for the major 's motive , for he had one , and he had artfully made use of Richmond to forward his plan : He was desirous of getting possession of the poor shoemaker 's house and land , having in view the purchase of the lot adjoining", "context_len": 50}}}, "patroness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "In this he was disappointed , but he had the good fortune to meet Miss Florence Douglas , by whom he was invited to accompany her to California as her escort , his expenses of course being paid by his patroness", "context_len": 41}}}, "affianced": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "He did not remain here long , but started for the mining-districts , still employed by Miss Douglas , in search of Richard Dewey , her affianced husband , whom her guardian had forbidden her to marry", "context_len": 37}}}, "jocosely": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Ki Sing , '' said Bradley , jocosely , `` I am afraid we ca n't carry your trunk with us", "context_len": 22}}}, "spraining": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` You wo n't catch a Chinaman spraining his ankle , '' said Bradley ; `` they 're too spry for that", "context_len": 22}}}, "queue": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "It may be explained here that Richard Dewey had at one time rescued Ki Sing from some rough companions who had made up their minds to cut off the Chinaman 's queue , thereby , in accordance with Chinese custom , preventing him ever returning to his native country", "context_len": 49}}}, "bestrode": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "The animal he bestrode , however , was very well-behaved , especially when he felt that his rider had the mastery over him", "context_len": 23}}}, "mustangs": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 13, "context": "They would have liked to improve the time by stealing away with the mustangs which they coveted , but even in this hour of public excitement they knew it would not be safe , and the act might arouse suspicion", "context_len": 40}}}, "bandits": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "They were themselves dressed in Mexican style , and bore a strong resemblance to bandits as we are apt to picture them", "context_len": 22}}}, "desperado": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "Mosely had been taken in by his confident tone , and knowing that he was himself a sham desperado , though a genuine thief and highwayman , had been made to feel uneasy while in Bradley 's company", "context_len": 38}}}, "riled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "I 'm a regular out-and-out desperado , I am , when I 'm riled", "context_len": 14}}}, "falsity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "It had not succeeded with Jake Bradley , who had enough knowledge of human nature to detect the falsity of Mosely 's pretensions and the sham character of his valor", "context_len": 30}}}, "braggadocio": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Bradley had paid Mosely in his own coin , and boasted of his prowess even more extravagantly than that braggadocio , claiming to have killed from seventy to eighty men in the course of his experience", "context_len": 36}}}, "chawing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "-- Well , heathen , I 'm so 's to be around , and would n't mind chawing up a dozen Chinamen", "context_len": 22}}}, "eliciting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Still , he resolved to push the inquiry , in the hope of eliciting some information that might be of value , for already a plan had come into his mind which was in accordance with his general character and reputation -- that of relieving the invalid of his hoard of gold-dust", "context_len": 52}}}, "corroborating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` I should say so , '' answered Hadley , whose respect for truth did not interfere with his corroborating in his usual style anything which his companion saw fit to assert", "context_len": 32}}}, "disposes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` That disposes of you , my friend , '' he said , mounting his mustang", "context_len": 16}}}, "bankers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` We 're bankers from 'Frisco , and we are going to take care of Dewey 's gold , as he ai n't in a situation to take care of it himself", "context_len": 32}}}, "tearer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "I 'm a regular tearer , I am", "context_len": 8}}}, "tauntingly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Perhaps you 'll insult a gentleman again , '' exclaimed Mosely tauntingly as he stood by and witnessed the ineffectual struggles of Tom 's victim , who had been taken at disadvantage", "context_len": 33}}}, "villainous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` I intend to express my opinion of you and your villainous conduct , '' said Dewey , undaunted , `` whatever you choose to call it", "context_len": 27}}}, "banker": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` So you are a banker , and your friend a thief ? I believe I have more respect for the thief , who openly avows his objects", "context_len": 28}}}, "avows": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` So you are a banker , and your friend a thief ? I believe I have more respect for the thief , who openly avows his objects", "context_len": 28}}}, "scamps": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` It would be just like them scamps to tie up a poor fellow like Ki Sing", "context_len": 17}}}, "blustered": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Are we goin ' to stand that , Tom ? '' blustered Mosely , whose courage was beginning to revive , as he had thus far only seen Bradley , and considered that the odds were two to one in his favor", "context_len": 43}}}, "disoblige": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` I am sorry to disoblige you , but I really could n't", "context_len": 13}}}, "pronouncing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Fliend come along -- cut stling , '' answered the Chinaman , pronouncing his words in Mongolian fashion", "context_len": 19}}}, "baboon": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Look here , you yellow baboon ! '' said Bill Mosely , angrily , `` you know what I mean", "context_len": 21}}}, "pedestrian": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` I do n't know what you mean , '' answered Mosely , determined to brazen it out and keep the horses if possible , for he was lazy and a pedestrian tramp would not have suited him very well", "context_len": 40}}}, "sentimentalist": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Jake Bradley had had rough experiences , and he was no soft-hearted sentimentalist , but he had a natural repugnance to taking the life of his fellow-creatures", "context_len": 27}}}, "repugnance": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "The fact that the cartridge belt had not been disturbed was probably due to a superstitious repugnance to touch a dead body for fear it would bring evil fortune", "context_len": 29}}}, "prospering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "How the old deacon would stare if he knew how I had been prospering at the mines ! I wish there was any way of sending part of this money home", "context_len": 31}}}, "securer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 61, "context": "The tame animal immediately started off at great speed , arching his neck and shaking his head , while the poor Chinaman , his bland smile succeeded by a look of extreme terror , was bounced up and down in the most unceremonious fashion , and would have been thrown off quickly but for the Mexican saddle , which is a securer seat than that used at the East", "context_len": 69}}}, "chinaman": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Chinaman", "context_len": 1}}}, "bronchial": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "One of the most brigandish-looking miners had been a clergyman in Western New York , who had been compelled by bronchial troubles to give up his parish , and , being poor , had wandered to the California mines in the hope of gathering a competence for the support of his family", "context_len": 52}}}, "conjured": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "The sight of his fresh young face and boyish figure recalled to many miners the sons whom they had left behind them , and helped to make more vivid the picture of home which their imaginations often conjured up , and they would have liked to have Ben join their company", "context_len": 51}}}, "architecturally": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "It was roughly put together , had but one story , was unpainted , and was altogether hardly equal , architecturally , to some of the huts which are to be found among the rocks at the upper end of Manhattan Island", "context_len": 42}}}, "scrupled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "It was considerably higher than the prices then asked at the best hotels in New York and Philadelphia ; but high prices prevailed in California , and no one scrupled to pay them", "context_len": 33}}}, "averaged": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "They averaged perhaps fifty dollars a week each , but out of this their expenses had to be paid , and these , on account of the high price of all articles of necessity , were rather heavy", "context_len": 38}}}, "amendment": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Perhaps we can buy them , '' suggested Mosely by way of amendment", "context_len": 14}}}, "impolitic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` We 're prospectin ' a little , '' answered Bill Mosely vaguely ; for there had been circumstances in his California career that made it impolitic to be too definite in his statements", "context_len": 34}}}, "condign": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "He was looked upon by all as a common and dangerous enemy , on whom any community was justified in visiting the most condign punishment", "context_len": 25}}}, "deviate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "It will be seen that the story did not entirely deviate from fact , and was very artfully framed to excite sympathy for the narrator and indignation against the perpetrators of the supposed outrage", "context_len": 34}}}, "artfully": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "And now for the major 's motive , for he had one , and he had artfully made use of Richmond to forward his plan : He was desirous of getting possession of the poor shoemaker 's house and land , having in view the purchase of the lot adjoining", "context_len": 50}}}, "prolific": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Tom Hadley , who had not the prolific imagination of his comrade , listened in open-mouthed wonder to the fanciful tale , but did not offer to corroborate it in his usual manner", "context_len": 33}}}, "brazenly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Of course you deny it , '' said Bill Mosely , brazenly persisting in his falsehood", "context_len": 17}}}, "persisting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Of course you deny it , '' said Bill Mosely , brazenly persisting in his falsehood", "context_len": 17}}}, "luckiest": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "In Chapter XVIII , a missing quotation mark has been added preceding `` We will make your share equal to that of the luckiest miner among us", "context_len": 27}}}, "unsupported": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Some of these men who were so near hanging us on the unsupported accusation of two men of whom they knew nothing were good , law-abiding citizens at home", "context_len": 29}}}, "extemporized": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "The two hunters went to view the bodies of the malefactors , who were still suspended from the extemporized gallows", "context_len": 20}}}, "mayhap": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "If you enjoy the howlin ' of wolves , mayhap you might like my singin '", "context_len": 16}}}, "dewey": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "Dewey", "context_len": 1}}}, "covetousness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "Both father and son were excessively fond of money , and the large amount of the fortune of the young lady , who stood to the father in the relation of ward , had excited the covetousness of both", "context_len": 39}}}, "pursuance": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "In pursuance of this agreement , Mr", "context_len": 7}}}, "avowal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Florence Douglas felt at first rather constrained to receive her guardian 's son with politeness , and this , being misinterpreted , led to an avowal of love", "context_len": 28}}}, "campbell": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 0, "context": "Campbell ! '' she ejaculated , in bewilderment", "context_len": 8}}}, "caprice": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` And nothing stands in the way but the caprice of a foolish girl ! I declare , father , it is too exasperating", "context_len": 24}}}, "saunders": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Saunders left the office , but presently returned", "context_len": 8}}}, "humbugging": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "If he is humbugging us , we can send him off", "context_len": 11}}}, "gambled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` I came with it to San Francisco , and gambled it away in one night", "context_len": 16}}}, "enrich": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` 'All that was required ' ? You know very well that you and he had conspired to put me in a mad-house if I would not agree to enrich you by giving you my hand", "context_len": 36}}}, "machinations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "It was not until then that I decided to leave your house and seek some place of refuge until time and the law should set me completely free from your family and their machinations", "context_len": 34}}}, "offish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` Very offish , Jones", "context_len": 5}}}, "civility": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "She hardly treated me with civility", "context_len": 6}}}, "sagaciously": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` That 's because of the other man , '' said Jones , sagaciously", "context_len": 14}}}, "unsound": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Her chief fear was that he would carry out his father 's threat and try to have her pronounced of unsound mind , in which case he could have her confined in an asylum", "context_len": 34}}}, "misjudging": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Poor as I was , I determined to come to you , even at the risk of your misjudging me", "context_len": 20}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` That 's as cheering a sight as I want to see ; and there was me a misjudging of my boy", "context_len": 22}}}, "mercenary": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "That the latter should be collecting these things for mercenary purposes with the design , when he should have enough , of shipping them overseas and selling them at fabulous prices , and then following them to live for the rest of his life in wealth and luxury amid the civilization whose value he knew , did not enter Bomba \u2019 s mind", "context_len": 63}}}, "outbid": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "I must outbid him", "context_len": 4}}}, "persecutor": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Meanwhile , though things looked dark for Florence and favorable for her persecutor , there was one circumstance that threatened failure to the latter 's plans", "context_len": 26}}}, "placate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "He looked so threatening that Orton Campbell thought it might be best to placate him , even at the expense of a small extra sum", "context_len": 25}}}, "offensively": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Bradshaw , offensively , putting her arms akimbo", "context_len": 8}}}, "circumvent": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` So this is his revenge ? I must go back to the city at once and circumvent him if I can", "context_len": 22}}}, "reckonings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Short reckonings make long friends", "context_len": 5}}}, "unblushingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` No , '' answered Campbell , unblushingly ; `` it was an agent of mine , who has deceived and betrayed me", "context_len": 23}}}, "eligibly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "They were eligibly located , and already had a commercial value , but were bestowed out of good-will , without a cent of compensation", "context_len": 24}}}, "creditably": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "It has already been mentioned that he could sing very creditably , and his talent was very often called into requisition in the evening", "context_len": 24}}}, "bouncer": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` Why , Ben ! '' exclaimed Bradley , in excitement , `` this is n't a rock ; it is a nugget , and a bouncer", "context_len": 27}}}, "exhilaration": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` How much is it worth ? '' demanded Ben , almost breathless with excitement and exhilaration", "context_len": 17}}}, "patronize": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "The good man thought he had come to order a new pair of shoes , and felt flattered that such a dashing man should have gone so far out of his way to patronize him", "context_len": 35}}}, "richmond": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "Richmond , '' he said , wiping his forehead with his red cotton handkerchief , `` about that money I 've had to pay", "context_len": 24}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Richmond the trader owed me a note for five hundred dollars , indorsed by Job Stanton , and as he did not pay it , I had to call on Stanton", "context_len": 31}}}, "stanton": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 0, "context": "Stanton if he would go with him and see the poor girl , and tell , if he could , what might be done for her", "context_len": 26}}}, "indorse": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Yes , '' continued Richmond , with gravity ; `` I want you to indorse my note for five hundred dollars", "context_len": 22}}}, "indorsing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The trader 's representation that indorsing the note was only a matter of form he had accepted as strictly true", "context_len": 20}}}, "indorses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "When a man indorses a note he knows that he may be called upon to pay , and of course has some plan for doing it", "context_len": 26}}}, "hypocritically": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` I do n't feel so , '' said the major , hypocritically", "context_len": 13}}}, "payments": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "I 've got two payments to make", "context_len": 7}}}, "debtor": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Another month passed , and Job made another visit to the store of his prosperous debtor", "context_len": 16}}}, "cancel": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` If you ca n't raise it , '' said the major , `` I am willing to cancel the note , give you two hundred and fifty dollars , and take a deed of the place", "context_len": 37}}}, "destitution": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "It is quite possible that he may end in poverty and destitution , and be forced at last to work for a living", "context_len": 23}}}, "swindled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Still , I shall advise Uncle Job to pay the mortgage , though he was swindled into agreeing to it", "context_len": 21}}}, "clients": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "He at first tried to bluster , then to temporize , but the lawyer was stern and threatened to exhaust the resources of the law in behalf of his clients", "context_len": 30}}}, "swindling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "With a crestfallen air John Richmond had to confess himself defeated in his mean attempt at swindling , for he had obtained Uncle Job 's indorsement with the deliberate intention of leaving him to pay the note , supposing that the old man would be too timid to do anything about it", "context_len": 52}}}, "indorsement": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "With a crestfallen air John Richmond had to confess himself defeated in his mean attempt at swindling , for he had obtained Uncle Job 's indorsement with the deliberate intention of leaving him to pay the note , supposing that the old man would be too timid to do anything about it", "context_len": 52}}}, "salutary": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "This he would regard as a misfortune , but it will probably be a blessing in disguise , for the necessity of honest labor is generally a salutary restraint", "context_len": 29}}}, "retailed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Doubtless , he has more than once retailed to wondering ears the account of his adventures and perils when he , as well as Ben , visited California `` in search of fortune", "context_len": 33}}}, "paul": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "PAUL THE PEDDLER", "context_len": 3}}}, "julius": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "JULIUS", "context_len": 1}}}, "gents": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "They are generally left to the `` young misses '' and `` gents '' who live in mortal fear of being found out to be what they feel themselves to be", "context_len": 31}}}, "finical": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "There is nothing finical or foppish about the conventions which Mr", "context_len": 11}}}, "foppish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "There is nothing finical or foppish about the conventions which Mr", "context_len": 11}}}, "codify": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Harcourt undertakes to codify and explain", "context_len": 6}}}, "cultured": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Society , '' thereby meaning well-bred and cultured men and women , has as much right to lay down rules to dress and conduct as any `` secret '' society has to insist upon ritual and ceremony", "context_len": 38}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "Harcourt 's book is a thoroughly sensible one and may be studied with profit by men who , not being to the manner born , desire to feel at ease among the cultured", "context_len": 33}}}, "boone": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Boone and Kenton Series", "context_len": 4}}}, "apostrophe": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "In Chapter V , an apostrophe following `` I do n't see anything that looks like a cabin , '' has been changed to a quotation mark", "context_len": 27}}}, "simeon": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "SIMEON", "context_len": 1}}}, "rebounded": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "She jumped -- but , to the horror of that anxious admiring throng below , her body struck against the projecting shop-sign , and rebounded , falling with terrific force on to the hard pavement below", "context_len": 36}}}, "commemorate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Beautiful windows have been erected at Red Cross Hall , Southwark , to commemorate her heroism ; but the best memorial is her own expression : `` I tried to do my best '' -- for this will live in the hearts of all who read of her self-devotion", "context_len": 49}}}, "herrings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Further search was made , and a hundred and fifty slaves were discovered packed as close as herrings in a barrel", "context_len": 21}}}, "unreliable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "They were lazy and mutinous ; the sentries went to sleep , the scouts were unreliable , they were full of complaints ; whilst round about him were the natives , ready to steal , maim , and murder whenever they could get an opportunity", "context_len": 45}}}, "maim": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "They were lazy and mutinous ; the sentries went to sleep , the scouts were unreliable , they were full of complaints ; whilst round about him were the natives , ready to steal , maim , and murder whenever they could get an opportunity", "context_len": 45}}}, "carriers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "All took the cows readily enough , but sixty-seven of the carriers did not appear at the time appointed", "context_len": 19}}}, "fireproof": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "They cut thick logs of wood , and planted them firmly in the ground , prepared fireproof rooms for the ammunition , and were in the course of a few days ready in case of emergency", "context_len": 36}}}, "emetics": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "At once Sir Samuel gave them mustard and water and other emetics , and they were soon better", "context_len": 18}}}, "lances": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Sir Samuel saw several lances pass close to his wife 's head , and he narrowly escaped being hit on various occasions", "context_len": 22}}}, "gasholders": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "The large gasholders , which are often a source of wonder to youthful minds as they rise and fall , are the places in which gas is stored for the use of our cities", "context_len": 34}}}, "gasholder": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The gasholder is placed in a tank of water , so that there is no waste of gas as the huge iron holder fills or empties", "context_len": 26}}}, "unscrewed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Then the labourer , regardless of the danger he was running , unscrewed his helmet , into which fresh air was being pumped , and , placing it quite near his fallen comrade , enabled him to get some of the air", "context_len": 42}}}, "hae": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` We 'll hae nane but Highland bonnets here ! '' and his troops rushed in after him like lions", "context_len": 20}}}, "demons": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 34, "context": "Before he could fit the arrow to the string a hideous chorus of shouts rent the air , and like magic the jungle was filled with men , men with the ferocious faces of demons , who rushed upon him , shouting and brandishing their sharp , murderous knives above their heads", "context_len": 52}}}, "bandsmen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Remember this : Whoever is wounded -- I do n't care what his rank is -- must lie where he falls till the bandsmen come to attend to him", "context_len": 29}}}, "hillock": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Sir Colin 's men , fierce and eager for the onset , would have dashed from behind the hillock where they were stationed , but for the stern voice commanding them to stand firm in their ranks", "context_len": 37}}}, "engulf": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 64, "context": "Startled by the red-coated Britishers rising up at the word of their leader , they broke and fled ; and the men of the 93rd , who , but a little before , had made up their minds to die where they stood , saw as in a dream their enemies scattered and broken ; and the cloud of horsemen which had threatened to engulf and annihilate them , make no effort to snatch the victory which seemed within their grasp", "context_len": 81}}}, "peerage": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "For his able conduct of the war he was warmly thanked by the Queen ; and at its conclusion was raised to the peerage , under the title of Lord Clyde", "context_len": 31}}}, "sandbags": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Cochrane however , with an immense quantity of sandbags , palisades , and barrels , made it pretty secure", "context_len": 19}}}, "cannonade": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "During the siege the Spanish flag was shot away whilst a heavy cannonade was going on ; but Cochrane , though the bullets were whistling about in every direction , calmly stepped down into the ditch , and rescued the flag", "context_len": 41}}}, "reforms": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "When he was not fighting his country 's battles at sea , he was besieging Parliament to bring about reforms in the Navy", "context_len": 23}}}, "electors": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The electors of Westminster , who had sent him to Parliament , never ceased to have faith in his truth and honour , and re-elected him when still in prison", "context_len": 30}}}, "velveteen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "First of all , he went into a cotton factory , and later to a velveteen factory ; then , having a taste for carpentering , he took to it as a trade , though he was at best but a rough unskilled workman , tramping about the country , and doing odd jobs wherever he could get them", "context_len": 59}}}, "carpentering": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "First of all , he went into a cotton factory , and later to a velveteen factory ; then , having a taste for carpentering , he took to it as a trade , though he was at best but a rough unskilled workman , tramping about the country , and doing odd jobs wherever he could get them", "context_len": 59}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Which wouldst thou prefer , carpentering or trying to persuade thy fellow-men to give up drinking , and to become teetotalers ? '' he asked", "context_len": 26}}}, "fustian": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Though he was dressed in fustian , and wore a workman 's apron , he spoke effectively , and his words went to the hearts of his hearers", "context_len": 28}}}, "audiences": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 16, "context": "He did not know that that voice , when in its prime , had thrilled great audiences that included emperors and kings and had given the singer a reputation as wide as the civilized world", "context_len": 35}}}, "lectured": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "He lectured far and wide ; and , though he met with much opposition , facts in the end prevailed", "context_len": 20}}}, "applicant": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Some of those on the platform viewed with distrust the gaunt , shabby , travel-stained applicant", "context_len": 16}}}, "teetotalism": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Joseph Livesey is a striking example of the benefits to health derived from teetotalism , as he lived to the good old age of ninety", "context_len": 25}}}, "mightier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Before long , indeed , one wave mightier than the rest lifted her bodily on to the sharp rocks and broke her in two", "context_len": 24}}}, "unwarrantable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Darling urged that the attempt was too perilous to be justified , and reproached Grace for endeavouring to persuade her father to run such unwarrantable risks", "context_len": 26}}}, "swamped": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Even if the boat was not at once swamped , two persons alone , and one of them only a girl , were insufficient for the work ; for , supposing they reached the wreck , they would probably be too exhausted to get back", "context_len": 45}}}, "billow": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Though every billow threatened to engulf the frail craft , yet it nevertheless rode through the mountainous waves and drew near the rock where the helpless men and women were standing face to face with death", "context_len": 36}}}, "leprosy": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "In the far-off Sandwich ( or Hawaiian ) Islands in the Pacific Ocean there are many lepers ; but the leprosy from which they suffer is of a more fatal kind than that which is spoken of in the Bible", "context_len": 40}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "The lepers of whom we read in the Bible were obliged to dwell alone outside the camp ; and even king Uzziah , when smitten with leprosy , mighty monarch though he was , had to give up his throne and dwell by himself to the end of his days", "context_len": 50}}}, "lepers": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 1, "context": "The lepers of whom we read in the Bible were obliged to dwell alone outside the camp ; and even king Uzziah , when smitten with leprosy , mighty monarch though he was , had to give up his throne and dwell by himself to the end of his days", "context_len": 50}}}, "leper": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "So as to prevent the spread of the disease , the lepers are sent to one of the smaller islands , where there is a leper village , in which those who are afflicted remain until their death", "context_len": 38}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "After about four years it usually attacks some vital organ , and the leper dies", "context_len": 15}}}, "shipload": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "When a shipload of these poor creatures leaves Honolulu for the little Isle of Molokai there is great wailing by the relatives of those sent away , for they know the parting is final", "context_len": 34}}}, "arctic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "One of the bravest arctic explorers was Sir John Franklin , who , after many wonderful adventures , finally died with his companions amid the frozen seas of the north", "context_len": 30}}}, "whaler": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "They were last seen by a whaler on the 26th of July that year , and then for years no word of their fate reached Great Britain", "context_len": 27}}}, "chronometers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Chronometers from Erebus and Terror", "context_len": 5}}}, "underlined": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "In a boat were discovered two skeletons ; and amongst other books a Bible , numerous passages in which were underlined , showing that these gallant men in their last hours had the comfort of God 's Word to support them when earthly hopes had passed away", "context_len": 47}}}, "shined": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "And it may be that a brave woman , into whose life the sunlight had shined , was stricken with grief and bowed down", "context_len": 24}}}, "balloonists": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "One of the most eminent balloonists of modern times , Mr", "context_len": 11}}}, "ascents": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "Papa consented , warning her , however , that she would find it harder work than she imagined to clamber up these steep ascents on her own two small feet", "context_len": 30}}}, "mercury": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` When at the height of 26,000 feet I could not see the fine column of the mercury in the tube ; then the fine divisions on the scale of the instrument became invisible", "context_len": 34}}}, "asphyxia": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "I thought I had been seized with asphyxia , and that I should experience no more , as death would come unless we speedily descended", "context_len": 25}}}, "overturn": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "Shortly after he was made commander of a force that was commissioned by the Emperor of China to put down a rebellion of the Taipings , of so dangerous a character that it threatened to overturn the monarchy", "context_len": 38}}}, "europeans": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Europeans were fighting both with him and with the rebels", "context_len": 10}}}, "gordon": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Gordon ! you will not let me be killed ''", "context_len": 10}}}, "workhouses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "He visited the hospitals and workhouses , and all the money he received he expended on the poor ; for he believed that having given his heart to God he had no right to keep anything for himself", "context_len": 38}}}, "camelback": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "He travelled on camelback thousands of miles", "context_len": 7}}}, "legions": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "But ere long it was found even beyond his powers ; for after sending off a portion of the Khartoum population in safety down the river , the Mahdi 's legions closed in upon him , and Khartoum was in a state of siege", "context_len": 44}}}, "victualling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "On the 10th of September , 1591 ( 31st August , old style ) , Lord Thomas Howard , with six of her Majesty 's ships , five victualling ships , a barque and two or three pinnaces , was at anchor near Flores , one of the westerly islands of the Azores , when Captain Middleton brought the news that the Spanish fleet was approaching", "context_len": 66}}}, "barque": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "On the 10th of September , 1591 ( 31st August , old style ) , Lord Thomas Howard , with six of her Majesty 's ships , five victualling ships , a barque and two or three pinnaces , was at anchor near Flores , one of the westerly islands of the Azores , when Captain Middleton brought the news that the Spanish fleet was approaching", "context_len": 66}}}, "unready": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The few ships at Lord Howard 's command were in a very unready state for fighting", "context_len": 16}}}, "galleons": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Ship after ship the whole night long their high-built galleons came ,", "context_len": 12}}}, "swooned": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "As he was being carried out of the ship he swooned , and reviving again desired the company to pray for him", "context_len": 22}}}, "disparity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Six small ships ill supplied with fighting men against fifty-three bigger ones filled with soldiers was too great a disparity of force to give even a hope of victory", "context_len": 29}}}, "veriest": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "At school Hannington was the veriest pickle , and was nicknamed `` Mad Jim ''", "context_len": 15}}}, "vacations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Both at Oxford and at Martinhoe , in North Devon , where he spent some time during the vacations , Hannington preserved his reputation for fun and love of adventure", "context_len": 30}}}, "ordination": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "From Norfolk Island , in 1870 , he wrote to his sister when he was holding an ordination : `` At such times as these , when one is specially engaged in solemn work , there is much heart searching ; and I can not tell you how my conscience accuses me of such systematic selfishness during many long years -- I mean I see how I was all along making self the centre , and neglecting all kinds of duties -- social and others -- in consequence ''", "context_len": 89}}}, "teetotaler": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "Pausing suddenly he remarked , `` If you please , Miss Weston , be you a teetotaler ? ''", "context_len": 19}}}, "revival": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "Not only was he a shining light in his own parish , but he also went about the country and assisted at revival missions , showing himself everywhere a bright and helpful minister of the Gospel", "context_len": 36}}}, "cleaves": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` My most bitter trial , '' he writes -- '' an agony that still cleaves to me -- was saying good-bye to the little ones", "context_len": 26}}}, "surmounting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "After surmounting many difficulties in his passage through Masai Land he had by October reached within a few days ' journey of Uganda ; but there , on the outskirts of the kingdom he sought to enter , a martyr 's death crowned his brief but earnest mission life", "context_len": 49}}}, "vermin": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "A terrible night , 1st with noisy , drunken guard , and 2nd with vermin which have found out my tent and swarm", "context_len": 23}}}, "excellency": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` Your excellency , '' said Sir Patrick Grant , presenting Havelock to Lord Canning , `` I have brought the man", "context_len": 22}}}, "quelling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "His instructions were that , `` after quelling all disturbances at Allahabad , he should not lose a moment in supporting Sir Henry Lawrence at Lucknow , and Sir Hugh Wheeler at Cawnpore ; and that he should take prompt measures for dispersing and utterly destroying all mutineers and insurgents ''", "context_len": 51}}}, "mutineers": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 47, "context": "His instructions were that , `` after quelling all disturbances at Allahabad , he should not lose a moment in supporting Sir Henry Lawrence at Lucknow , and Sir Hugh Wheeler at Cawnpore ; and that he should take prompt measures for dispersing and utterly destroying all mutineers and insurgents ''", "context_len": 51}}}, "insurgents": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "His instructions were that , `` after quelling all disturbances at Allahabad , he should not lose a moment in supporting Sir Henry Lawrence at Lucknow , and Sir Hugh Wheeler at Cawnpore ; and that he should take prompt measures for dispersing and utterly destroying all mutineers and insurgents ''", "context_len": 51}}}, "hairsbreadth": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "But their general rode out under fire of the guns , and , smiling as a cannon ball just missed him by a hairsbreadth , said : --", "context_len": 28}}}, "cawnpore": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Cawnpore was won ; but , alas ! the women and children had been slain whilst their countrymen had been fighting for their deliverance", "context_len": 24}}}, "extermination": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "For after advancing into Oude Havelock found that constant fighting , cholera , sunstroke and illness had so reduced his numbers that to go on would risk the extermination of his force", "context_len": 32}}}, "defrauding": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "It is only just over a hundred years since John Howard died ; yet in his day persons could be put to death for stealing a horse or a sheep , for robbing dwellings , for defrauding creditors , for forgery , for wounding deer , for killing or maiming cattle , for stealing goods to the value of five shillings , or even for cutting a band in a hop plantation", "context_len": 72}}}, "maiming": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "It is only just over a hundred years since John Howard died ; yet in his day persons could be put to death for stealing a horse or a sheep , for robbing dwellings , for defrauding creditors , for forgery , for wounding deer , for killing or maiming cattle , for stealing goods to the value of five shillings , or even for cutting a band in a hop plantation", "context_len": 72}}}, "visitations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "From the inquiries he made during the course of his visitations he was astonished to find that the gaolers received no salary , and that they lived on what they could make out of the prisoners", "context_len": 36}}}, "abuses": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "In the course of his travels the ship in which he was a passenger was attacked by pirates , and John Howard showed himself as brave in actual battle as he was in fighting abuses ; for he loaded the big gun with which the ship was armed nearly up to the muzzle with nails and spikes , and fired it into the pirate crew just in time to save himself and his companions from destruction", "context_len": 76}}}, "rioters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Two of the gaolers had been killed , and the rioters held possession of the building", "context_len": 16}}}, "empress": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "When Howard was in Russia the empress sent a message saying she desired to see him ; but he returned an answer that he was devoting his time to inspecting prisons , and had no leisure for visiting the palaces of rulers", "context_len": 42}}}, "debtors": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 46, "context": "He had a great dislike to any fuss being made about him personally ; and , though 1500 was subscribed during his life to erect a memorial , it was , at his earnest desire , either returned to the subscribers or spent in assisting poor debtors", "context_len": 47}}}, "lampblack": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 42, "context": "\u2019 Get a whole lot of pitch wood , and burn it in some tight thing , so as to keep in the smoke ; the black will stick to the sides , and you can scrape it off , as good lampblack as you can buy , and better than half of it", "context_len": 54}}}, "villager": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "About three o'clock a villager observing them approach called out a Sepoy guard of twenty-five men , who asked them all kinds of questions", "context_len": 24}}}, "creeper": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "Aunt Patty 's home was a pretty , snug cottage on the side of a hill ; its front covered with a beautiful trumpet creeper , which went climbing up to the very top of the many-cornered old chimney , and wreathing itself over the little porch and the bow window of the sitting-room , until the house looked like a quiet green nest", "context_len": 64}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "midstream": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Hardly had they reached midstream when Bangura 's rifle band , slipping over his arms , pinned them to his side", "context_len": 21}}}, "signatures": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "The little block in this page is a reproduction of Momo Bangura 's statement forwarded to the Colonial Office , duly witnessed by his companions ' signatures", "context_len": 27}}}, "weavers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "On the loom I was most industrious , working from early in the morning often till ten , and sometimes later , at night ; and she not only did all the house work , but wound the bobbins for three weavers -- myself , uncle , and grandfather ; and yet , with all this apparently hard lot , these were happy days", "context_len": 64}}}, "ringers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "In the church to which Joseph Livesey belonged the ringers and singers were hard drinkers , the gravedigger was a drunkard , and the parish clerk was often intoxicated !", "context_len": 30}}}, "singers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Yet the sound of the chant served him as a guide , and he went on and on , now coming closer to the singers , then seeming to draw away from them as the voices receded", "context_len": 37}}}, "drinkers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "In the church to which Joseph Livesey belonged the ringers and singers were hard drinkers , the gravedigger was a drunkard , and the parish clerk was often intoxicated !", "context_len": 30}}}, "autobiography": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Edward Clifford 's work ; of Elizabeth Gilbert , from the Life by Frances Martin ; and of George M\u00fcller , from the shilling autobiography he has written , which is worthy of the deepest attention", "context_len": 36}}}, "bobbins": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "On the loom I was most industrious , working from early in the morning often till ten , and sometimes later , at night ; and she not only did all the house work , but wound the bobbins for three weavers -- myself , uncle , and grandfather ; and yet , with all this apparently hard lot , these were happy days", "context_len": 64}}}, "eightpence": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "He had been ordered to take some cheese in the forenoon , so he bought a piece at about eightpence a pound ; and as he munched it came this thought : cheese wholesale cost but fivepence per pound ; would it not be possible to buy a piece wholesale and sell it to his friends , so that he too might have the benefit of getting it at this low price ?", "context_len": 73}}}, "mongering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "So he changed his trade : weaving gave place to cheese mongering ; and , after some very hard work and persevering efforts , he placed himself beyond the reach of poverty", "context_len": 32}}}, "facsimile": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 59, "context": "His first convert to total abstinence was a man named John King ; Livesey and he signed together ; and on 1st September , 1832 , at a meeting held at Preston , seven men -- '' the Seven Men of Preston , '' as they are called -- signed the pledge , of which the following is a facsimile : --", "context_len": 62}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Unwin , which is here reproduced in facsimile , it will be seen that Robert Moffat 's labours were not even then finished ; for up to the last he took the greatest interest in the missionary cause", "context_len": 38}}}, "pennyworth": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` There is more food in a pennyworth of bread , '' said Livesey , `` than in a gallon of ale '' ; and he proved it", "context_len": 28}}}, "unbolts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Then a woman 's hand quietly unbolts the door and receives the little wanderer back", "context_len": 15}}}, "abstainer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "He got on quickly at lessons , and became , like his father , a total abstainer for life", "context_len": 19}}}, "where'er": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "He studied the language of the tribes amongst whom he was ministering ; and soon the people were able to sing in their own tongue , `` There is a fountain filled with blood , '' `` Jesus shall reign where'er the sun , '' and other beautiful hymns which delight the hearts of those in our own land", "context_len": 59}}}, "helpmate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "She was a true helpmate , and in the trials and difficulties which beset him his way was made clearer and brighter by this good and loving woman", "context_len": 28}}}, "rhinoceros": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "`` Do you think , '' said Sechele , `` you can make my people believe by talking to them ? I can make them do nothing except by thrashing them , and if you like I shall call my headman , and with our whips of rhinoceros hide we will soon make them all believe together ! ''", "context_len": 59}}}, "livingstone": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Livingstone was deeply grieved about the capture of the children , but as to his own loss he merely says : `` The Boers by taking possession of all my goods have saved me the trouble of making a will ''", "context_len": 41}}}, "untrodden": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "In the course of these he traversed thousands of miles of country before untrodden by the feet of Europeans", "context_len": 19}}}, "milch": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": ", `` Knocked up quite , and remain -- recover -- sent to buy milch cows", "context_len": 16}}}, "implanted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "He had had but little schooling , and his master was one of those persons who thought the best way to get learning implanted in a boy 's mind was by forcing it into him at the point of the ruler", "context_len": 41}}}, "draper": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "So at about thirteen years of age George Moore began his business life as apprentice to a draper at Wigton", "context_len": 20}}}, "orphanages": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "In the year 1805 was born in Prussia George M\u00fcller , whose orphanages at Ashley Down , Bristol , may be regarded as one of the modern wonders of the world", "context_len": 31}}}, "expensively": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "When he was sixteen he went to Brunswick , and putting up at an hotel lived expensively , and had to part with his best clothes to pay the bill", "context_len": 30}}}, "donations": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "M\u00fcller , `` I have had cheques amounting even to 5000 , from individuals whose names I knew not before receiving their donations", "context_len": 23}}}, "cruet": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Other paragraphs in the report read thus : `` Received anonymously five large cheeses ; received a box of dessert knives and forks , a cruet , a silver soup ladle and a silver cup ; from Clifton , twelve tons house coals ; from Bedminster , a monster loaf , 200 lbs", "context_len": 53}}}, "brooches": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "A gold chain and watch-key , two gold brooches , and a pair of earrings were sent to Mr", "context_len": 19}}}, "earrings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "A gold chain and watch-key , two gold brooches , and a pair of earrings were sent to Mr", "context_len": 19}}}, "unsearchable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "M\u00fcller , with the following comment : `` My wife and I having , through the exceeding riches of God 's grace , been brought to the Lord Jesus , wish to lay aside the perishing gold of the world for the unsearchable riches of Christ , and send the enclosed for the support of the orphans ''", "context_len": 58}}}, "waugh": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Waugh remarked that `` thae twa lads were ower young to gang together , '' so they were separated", "context_len": 19}}}, "twa": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Waugh remarked that `` thae twa lads were ower young to gang together , '' so they were separated", "context_len": 19}}}, "baboons": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "The farmer , roused to indignation by such a request , said he would call in the dogs and baboons if Moffat wanted a congregation of that sort !", "context_len": 29}}}, "longfellow": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "LONGFELLOW", "context_len": 1}}}, "navies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "The English fleet had been for many days lying off the coast of Spain , eagerly waiting for the navies of France and Spain to leave their shelter in Cadiz harbour", "context_len": 31}}}, "misconduct": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` May the great God whom I worship grant to my country , and for the benefit of Europe in general , a great and glorious victory ; and may no misconduct in any one tarnish it , and may humanity after victory be the predominant feature in the British fleet ! For myself individually , I commit my life to Him that made me , and may His blessing alight on my endeavours for serving my country faithfully ! To Him I resign myself , and the just cause which is entrusted to me to defend", "context_len": 97}}}, "tarnish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` May the great God whom I worship grant to my country , and for the benefit of Europe in general , a great and glorious victory ; and may no misconduct in any one tarnish it , and may humanity after victory be the predominant feature in the British fleet ! For myself individually , I commit my life to Him that made me , and may His blessing alight on my endeavours for serving my country faithfully ! To Him I resign myself , and the just cause which is entrusted to me to defend", "context_len": 97}}}, "watchword": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Before the battle began Nelson made the signal which stirred every heart in the fleet on that day , and has since remained a watchword of the nation : --", "context_len": 30}}}, "harriet": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Harriet Attwood was born in Massachusetts , America , in the year 1793", "context_len": 13}}}, "falsehoods": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "I am very sorry for having done so many bad things -- I mean falsehoods -- and I heartily beg your pardon ; and Uncle Frank says that he thinks if I stay , in a month 's time Mr", "context_len": 40}}}, "birching": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "but I will not catch the birching", "context_len": 7}}}, "unbend": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "At another island , as he returned to the boat , he saw one of the natives draw a bow with the apparent intention of shooting him , and then unbend it at the entreaty of his comrades", "context_len": 38}}}, "besprinkle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "And thus full of faith he laboured on , telling the people of these scattered islands , which besprinkle the southern ocean like stars in the milky way , of the love of Christ", "context_len": 34}}}, "accuses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "From Norfolk Island , in 1870 , he wrote to his sister when he was holding an ordination : `` At such times as these , when one is specially engaged in solemn work , there is much heart searching ; and I can not tell you how my conscience accuses me of such systematic selfishness during many long years -- I mean I see how I was all along making self the centre , and neglecting all kinds of duties -- social and others -- in consequence ''", "context_len": 89}}}, "bowshot": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "About half an hour later the natives in the canoes , without the least warning , began shooting their arrows at the poor fellows in the boat , and ere it could be taken out of bowshot one of them was pierced with six arrows , and two of the others were also wounded", "context_len": 54}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "His game , therefore , could easily have got beyond bowshot before it would have been compelled to come to the surface for air", "context_len": 24}}}, "atkins": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Atkins stumbled in his speech , and then he and his companions knew the poison in his system was working", "context_len": 20}}}, "costermongers": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "The noble Knight of the Garter was not only interested in the costermongers themselves , but in their animals too", "context_len": 20}}}, "underfed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "At one time the costers had used their donkeys and ponies shamefully , had overworked and underfed them ; but gradually they were made to see how much better it was to treat their animals well", "context_len": 36}}}, "reporters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "The donkey was then led down the steps of the platform , and Lord Shaftesbury remarked , `` I hope the reporters of the press will state that , the donkey having vacated the chair , the place was taken by Lord Shaftesbury ''", "context_len": 44}}}, "unknowingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "She was one of those simple-minded humble Christians who , all unknowingly , plant in many minds the good seed which grows up and brings forth much fruit", "context_len": 28}}}, "deducted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "When the accident happened she had not completed her week 's work , so eighteenpence was deducted from her wages !", "context_len": 21}}}, "arabs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "To these schools , established in the poorest neighbourhoods of the metropolis , came the street arabs , the poor and abandoned , and received kindness and teaching , which comforted and civilised them", "context_len": 34}}}, "benefiting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "The outcasts who slept in doorways , under arches , and in all kinds of horrible and unhealthy places , were the objects of this good man 's care ; and ways were found of benefiting and starting afresh hundreds of lads who would otherwise have become thieves or vagabonds in the great city", "context_len": 54}}}, "universities": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "At the age of sixteen he greatly desired to proceed to one of the universities , and prepare for becoming a clergyman , but his parents being opposed to such a step he gave up the idea in deference to their wishes", "context_len": 42}}}, "overruling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "It was a great disappointment to him to do this -- yet he was able to write , `` It is my duty to acknowledge an overruling and directing Providence in all the very minutest things , by being in whatever state I am therewith content", "context_len": 46}}}, "forwarding": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "A great deal of the forwarding of newspapers was in those days done by coaches", "context_len": 15}}}, "bookstall": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Smith secured the entire bookstall rights on the London and North-Western Railway , much against his father 's advice", "context_len": 19}}}, "bookstalls": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "The vast improvement in the selection of books and the service of papers , however , induced other companies to desire to have a similar arrangement , till the chief portion of all the English railways came to be girdled by Smith 's bookstalls", "context_len": 44}}}, "hon": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Hon", "context_len": 1}}}, "primate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 51, "context": "`` As to Simeon , '' wrote Macaulay , `` if you knew what his authority and influence were , and how they extended from Cambridge to the most remote corners of England , you would allow that his real sway over the Church was far greater than that of any primate", "context_len": 52}}}, "sluggard": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "Later on in life , when he found it difficult to rise early in the morning , he resolved to give the servant half a crown every time he played the part of the sluggard", "context_len": 35}}}, "manuals": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "He at once set about reading devotional manuals , and sorrowed earnestly for his past sins", "context_len": 16}}}, "scapegoat": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "But in Passion Week the thought came to him that God had provided an Offering for him , on whose head he could lay his sins , just as the Jewish high priest laid the sins of the people on the head of the scapegoat", "context_len": 45}}}, "showily": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "He dressed showily , went to races , spent his Sundays carelessly", "context_len": 12}}}, "illegal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Besides beings rude and unmannerly , that was distinctly illegal ; but Simeon put up with the affront for the sake of peace", "context_len": 23}}}, "undergraduates": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "The heads of houses , the doctors , the masters of art , the bachelors , the undergraduates , the townsmen , all crowded to hear the venerable preacher", "context_len": 29}}}, "stereotyping": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` My parish sweetly harmonious , my whole works stereotyping in twenty-one volumes , and my ministry not altogether inefficient at the age of seventy-three", "context_len": 25}}}, "ministry": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` My parish sweetly harmonious , my whole works stereotyping in twenty-one volumes , and my ministry not altogether inefficient at the age of seventy-three", "context_len": 25}}}, "gusty": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The night was dark and gusty", "context_len": 6}}}, "entrenchment": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` Men of the 97th , follow me ! '' rang out his voice above the din of battle , and leaping the parapet of the entrenchment he charged the enemy down the ravine", "context_len": 34}}}, "cleaving": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "How true is the peace of mind that cleaving to Christ brings to a man ! There is nothing like it in this world", "context_len": 24}}}, "taming": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "At the Sunday school she showed so great a genius for taming unruly boys that the curate handed over to her the very worst of the youths , that she might `` lick them into shape ''", "context_len": 37}}}, "grenadiers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Passing one of the squares of grenadiers a shell fell among them , and the duke waited to see the result", "context_len": 21}}}, "instil": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` I do intend to be more particularly careful of the soul of this child that Thou hast so mercifully provided for than ever I have been , that I may do my endeavour to instil into his mind the principles of Thy true religion and virtue", "context_len": 47}}}, "regulate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "He also commenced to regulate the whole tone of his life", "context_len": 11}}}, "congregations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "It seems impossible to realise that one voice could reach such a multitude ; yet it is a fact that some of John Wesley 's open-air congregations consisted of over 20,000 persons", "context_len": 32}}}, "prizefighter": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Others took his part also -- one , fortunately , being a prizefighter", "context_len": 13}}}, "ives": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Ives in Cornwall there was a great uproar , but Wesley went amongst the mob and brought the chief mischiefmaker out", "context_len": 21}}}, "agitator": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Strange to say , the preacher received but one blow , and then he reasoned the case out with the agitator , and the man undertook to quiet his companions", "context_len": 30}}}, "vilest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Go on in the name of God and in the power of His might till even American slavery , the vilest that ever saw the sun , shall vanish away before it", "context_len": 32}}}, "bequests": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Amongst his few small gifts and bequests was `` 6 to be divided among the six poor men named by the assistant who shall carry my body to the grave ; for I particularly desire that there be no hearse , no coach , no escutcheon , no pomp ''", "context_len": 50}}}, "hearse": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "Amongst his few small gifts and bequests was `` 6 to be divided among the six poor men named by the assistant who shall carry my body to the grave ; for I particularly desire that there be no hearse , no coach , no escutcheon , no pomp ''", "context_len": 50}}}, "inquisition": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Indeed , when the king 's prime minister began to make investigation , he found the place so honey-combed by Christianity that he had to cease his inquisition , for fear of implicating chiefs , and upsetting society generally", "context_len": 39}}}, "implicating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "Indeed , when the king 's prime minister began to make investigation , he found the place so honey-combed by Christianity that he had to cease his inquisition , for fear of implicating chiefs , and upsetting society generally", "context_len": 39}}}, "carman": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "His father is a respectable carman , who , a year after little Johnnie 's birth , moved to 4 Church Terrace , Waterloo Road , Lambeth", "context_len": 27}}}, "headmaster": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "Davey , headmaster )", "context_len": 4}}}, "paving": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "In July , 1893 , a tiny child was playing in the middle of Stamford Street when a hansom cab came dashing along over the smooth wood paving", "context_len": 28}}}, "playgrounds": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "How many accidents to children are caused by the lamentable absence of open spaces and playgrounds ! 460 persons are yearly killed in the streets of London and over 2000 injured there , many of them being children playing in the only place they have to play in", "context_len": 48}}}, "lightermen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "The river police dragged for him , and the lightermen did all they could for some considerable time , but without success", "context_len": 22}}}, "lighterman": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "After fifteen minutes ' fruitless search , a lighterman suggested that the boy must be under the pier", "context_len": 18}}}, "postscript": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "POSTSCRIPT", "context_len": 1}}}, "agrarian": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Agrarian Tenures", "context_len": 2}}}, "arabian": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Arabian Nights Entertainments , Cassell 's Pictorial", "context_len": 7}}}, "shirley": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Shirley", "context_len": 1}}}, "kenilworth": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Kenilworth", "context_len": 1}}}, "ivanhoe": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Ivanhoe", "context_len": 1}}}, "pickwick": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Pickwick", "context_len": 1}}}, "rienzi": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Rienzi", "context_len": 1}}}, "british": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "British Battles on Land and Sea", "context_len": 6}}}, "logo": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "[ Illustration : Publisher logo ]", "context_len": 6}}}, "faraway": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "He thought longingly of those faraway cities described to him by the boy named Frank , the white boy , son of the woman with the golden hair , who had once kissed Bomba as though he had been her son", "context_len": 41}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Now the noise took on a deeper note , and with the humming were mingled discordant notes , rumbling notes , ominous notes , with an occasional crash as of faraway thunder", "context_len": 32}}}, "coffeehouses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Streets were unpaved and unlighted , but there was plenty of activity in the coffeehouses and taverns", "context_len": 17}}}, "versifier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "The verse writing was inspired by his father \u2019 s brother , Uncle Benjamin , a versifier himself , who appeared at varying intervals , usually staying as long as his welcome lasted", "context_len": 33}}}, "wick": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "After that he worked in his father \u2019 s soap and candle making shop , doing errands , dipping molds , cutting wick for candles", "context_len": 25}}}, "worthwhile": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "To improve their minds , Josiah often invited men of learning to dinner , encouraging them to discuss worthwhile matters", "context_len": 20}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Schwitz came to me and begged I 'd let him off and pretend he was not to be found , saying he would make it worthwhile to me", "context_len": 28}}}, "skills": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "He was a man of many skills , was handy with tools , played the violin , and sang hymns in a pleasing voice", "context_len": 24}}}, "repercussions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The wharf was a success but there were repercussions when the men found their stones missing", "context_len": 16}}}, "effortlessly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "To his delight , he found that if he floated on his back while holding the kite \u2019 s string , he was effortlessly drawn across the pond", "context_len": 28}}}, "wicks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "He had no enthusiasm for cutting candle wicks and often dreamed of going to sea as an older brother had done", "context_len": 21}}}, "sensing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Josiah Franklin , sensing his discontent , told him he could take his pick of other trades", "context_len": 17}}}, "bricklayers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "In turn , he took his son to watch the work of joiners , bricklayers , turners , and braziers", "context_len": 20}}}, "turners": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "In turn , he took his son to watch the work of joiners , bricklayers , turners , and braziers", "context_len": 20}}}, "braziers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "In turn , he took his son to watch the work of joiners , bricklayers , turners , and braziers", "context_len": 20}}}, "intellectuals": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "James Franklin was one of Boston \u2019 s young intellectuals , belonging to what the pious Cotton Mather called the Hell Fire Club , made up of clever young men like himself", "context_len": 32}}}, "techniques": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "He had reason to be pleased with how quickly his little brother mastered the techniques of a printer \u2019 s trade", "context_len": 21}}}, "sideline": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "As a sideline they printed patterns on linen , calico , and silk in good figures , very lively and durable colours", "context_len": 22}}}, "insufferably": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The Gazette was one of Boston \u2019 s two newspapers , both insufferably dull", "context_len": 14}}}, "inoculation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "The first issue of James Franklin \u2019 s New England Courant appeared August 7 , 1721 , during a smallpox epidemic and was devoted to opposing the new doubtful and dangerous practice of smallpox inoculation", "context_len": 35}}}, "boardinghouse": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "He had been taking his dinners with James and the workmen at a boardinghouse run by a Mrs", "context_len": 18}}}, "vegetarianism": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "One of the volumes he purchased at this time influenced him even more than Tryon and his vegetarianism", "context_len": 18}}}, "unequivocally": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Hitherto when Benjamin had an opinion , he stated it , as so many do , unequivocally as a fact", "context_len": 20}}}, "diffidence": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "From that time on , Benjamin used rarely such words as certainly or undoubtedly , but expressed his own ideas with seeming diffidence and modesty", "context_len": 25}}}, "wittily": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Dogood \u2019 s first letter , as well as subsequent ones in which she expressed herself , wittily and clearly , on such varied subjects as the folly of fashionable dress , the character of the so-called weaker sex , the ill effects of liquor , the inferior quality of New England poetry , the need of insurance for widows and old maids , the hypocrisy of certain pretenders to religion , and the uselessness of sending dullards to Harvard simply because their fathers could afford to pay their way", "context_len": 90}}}, "insurance": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 57, "context": "Dogood \u2019 s first letter , as well as subsequent ones in which she expressed herself , wittily and clearly , on such varied subjects as the folly of fashionable dress , the character of the so-called weaker sex , the ill effects of liquor , the inferior quality of New England poetry , the need of insurance for widows and old maids , the hypocrisy of certain pretenders to religion , and the uselessness of sending dullards to Harvard simply because their fathers could afford to pay their way", "context_len": 90}}}, "widows": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 59, "context": "Dogood \u2019 s first letter , as well as subsequent ones in which she expressed herself , wittily and clearly , on such varied subjects as the folly of fashionable dress , the character of the so-called weaker sex , the ill effects of liquor , the inferior quality of New England poetry , the need of insurance for widows and old maids , the hypocrisy of certain pretenders to religion , and the uselessness of sending dullards to Harvard simply because their fathers could afford to pay their way", "context_len": 90}}}, "dullards": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 77, "context": "Dogood \u2019 s first letter , as well as subsequent ones in which she expressed herself , wittily and clearly , on such varied subjects as the folly of fashionable dress , the character of the so-called weaker sex , the ill effects of liquor , the inferior quality of New England poetry , the need of insurance for widows and old maids , the hypocrisy of certain pretenders to religion , and the uselessness of sending dullards to Harvard simply because their fathers could afford to pay their way", "context_len": 90}}}, "controversial": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Not until her column had become the most controversial and the most popular in the paper , did James Franklin learn that his apprentice-brother was Mrs", "context_len": 26}}}, "creator": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Dogood \u2019 s creator", "context_len": 4}}}, "editorial": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Because of an editorial attack by one of his contributors on the Massachusetts governor , James was summoned before the City Council , sent to jail for a month , and released only when he agreed to make an abject apology", "context_len": 41}}}, "collins": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Collins persuaded the captain of a New York sloop to give him passage , telling a fantastic yarn about Benjamin being pursued by a young woman who wanted to marry him", "context_len": 31}}}, "businessmen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Streets , paved with cobblestones , were filled with elegantly attired English officials and wealthy businessmen", "context_len": 16}}}, "understandable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "It was understandable", "context_len": 3}}}, "regime": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Tryon \u2019 s vegetarian regime , never returning to it except for lack of funds", "context_len": 15}}}, "disrepair": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "His press was old and in disrepair with only one small and worn-out font ( set of type )", "context_len": 19}}}, "warmhearted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Debby was a warmhearted outspoken young lady , cheerful and quite pretty", "context_len": 12}}}, "overly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Nor was Benjamin overly ardent in his courtship", "context_len": 8}}}, "courtship": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Nor was Benjamin overly ardent in his courtship", "context_len": 8}}}, "heady": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "As Benjamin basked in this heady tribute , the governor and Colonel French launched into ways and means of setting him up in the printing business", "context_len": 26}}}, "fonts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "There he could pick out for himself his own press , type fonts , paper , and whatever else he needed for a printing shop", "context_len": 25}}}, "mailbag": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "He did not have the letters Keith had promised , but assured Benjamin they were safe in the captain \u2019 s mailbag", "context_len": 22}}}, "watt": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Watt", "context_len": 1}}}, "tutelage": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Under the tutelage of experienced workmen , he perfected his printing skills", "context_len": 12}}}, "prude": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Not that he was a prude", "context_len": 6}}}, "acclaim": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "He wrote a pamphlet called A Dissertation on Liberty and Necessity , Pleasure and Pain , which brought him some acclaim among London \u2019 s young intellectuals", "context_len": 27}}}, "asbestos": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "He presented an American curiosity , a purse of stone asbestos , to Sir Hans Sloane , secretary of the Royal Society , and almost met Sir Isaac Newton", "context_len": 29}}}, "coachmen": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 62, "context": "Bradford , coming in unexpectedly one day , actually found the old lady on her hands and knees , in a corner , playing the part of a horse eating hay from a manger ; while Franky , clothes-brush in hand , was , much to his own satisfaction , pretending to rub her down , making the hissing noise used by coachmen when they curry a horse , and positively refusing to allow his patient playfellow to rise", "context_len": 79}}}, "importing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "His Quaker friend Thomas Denham had offered him a position in his Philadelphia importing company", "context_len": 15}}}, "monstrously": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "He noted their glorious appearance in the water , their bodies of a bright green , mixed with a silver colour , and their tails of a shining golden yellow , and wondered at the vulgar error of the painters , who always represent this fish monstrously crooked and deformed", "context_len": 50}}}, "gulfweed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "From the Gulf Stream he fished out several branches of gulfweed and spent long hours studying a growth which he called vegetable animals , resembling shellfish and yet seeming part of the weed", "context_len": 33}}}, "shellfish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "From the Gulf Stream he fished out several branches of gulfweed and spent long hours studying a growth which he called vegetable animals , resembling shellfish and yet seeming part of the weed", "context_len": 33}}}, "idiosyncrasies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The idiosyncrasies of his fellow passengers also came under his scrutiny", "context_len": 11}}}, "confounds": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "From watching the men play drafts he concluded that if two persons equal in judgment play for a considerable sum , he that loves money will lose ; his anxiety for the success of the game confounds him", "context_len": 38}}}, "excommunicated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "after we had been long separated and excommunicated as it were from the rest of mankind", "context_len": 16}}}, "suffusion": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "I could not discern it so soon as the rest ; my eyes were dimmed with the suffusion of two small drops of joy", "context_len": 24}}}, "merchandising": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Under Thomas Denham he set himself to learning the intricacies of merchandising", "context_len": 12}}}, "executors": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "His executors took over his store , and Franklin was out of a job", "context_len": 14}}}, "mold": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "When types wore out , instead of sending an order to England for more , he devised a copper mold to cast new type , the first time this had been done in America", "context_len": 34}}}, "nagged": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "He complained that he was paying Franklin too much and nagged him incessantly", "context_len": 13}}}, "journeyman": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "That night a fellow journeyman named Hugh Meredith came to see him", "context_len": 12}}}, "scrivener": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "There were twelve members in all , including Hugh and two other printers , a shoemaker , a joiner , a scrivener , and others in modest trades", "context_len": 28}}}, "deteriorated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "His fortune had deteriorated", "context_len": 4}}}, "inks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "He used very white paper and very black inks and sometimes made decorative woodcuts to illustrate advertisements", "context_len": 17}}}, "outworked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "He hired a workman and took an apprentice , but outworked them both , staying in the shop from dawn to near midnight", "context_len": 23}}}, "slipshod": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "His rival , Andrew Bradford , printed an address from the Pennsylvania Assembly to the governor in a slipshod manner", "context_len": 20}}}, "reprinted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Franklin reprinted the same address elegantly , sending a copy to every Assembly member", "context_len": 14}}}, "pittance": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "That poor illiterate Keimer running a paper ? It lasted only until September 1729 when Keimer , head over heels in debt , sold it to Franklin for a pittance and departed to the Barbados , never to return", "context_len": 39}}}, "glazier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "A glazier \u2019 s family named Godfrey had been sharing his High Street house", "context_len": 14}}}, "throve": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "we throve together , and have ever mutually endeavor \u2019 d to make each other happy", "context_len": 16}}}, "bookshop": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "As there was no good bookshop in Philadelphia , the members pooled their own books and loaned them to each other", "context_len": 21}}}, "pooled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "As there was no good bookshop in Philadelphia , the members pooled their own books and loaned them to each other", "context_len": 21}}}, "communal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "This practice of communal sharing gave them so much pleasure that , at Franklin \u2019 s suggestion , they commenced a public library", "context_len": 23}}}, "sects": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Since all religious sects , in theory , preached the same , he never did see a reason to favor one of them above others", "context_len": 25}}}, "dons": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Seabrooke went to dine with `` the dons , '' caring not so much for the social pleasure as for the honor conferred upon him by the invitation ; Mr", "context_len": 30}}}, "percy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Percy opened his eyes in unfeigned astonishment", "context_len": 7}}}, "ransack": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 57, "context": "`` It shows what you are , '' answered Seabrooke , continuing his reproaches , instead of giving the straightforward answer which he considered unnecessary , `` that you have not the decent manliness to demand that which rightfully belonged to you because you were ashamed of your own folly and weakness , but must go and ransack in my quarters to find your money", "context_len": 65}}}, "interchanged": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Percy contradicted him flatly , and in language which left no doubt as to his opinion of his veracity , and very hard words were interchanged", "context_len": 26}}}, "merton": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Merton", "context_len": 1}}}, "drugging": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "What would he have said had he known how much worse the record might have been -- had all been revealed , had Seabrooke disclosed the drugging , the theft of his letter to his father , and the destruction , unintentional though it was , of the money ?", "context_len": 50}}}, "unintentional": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "What would he have said had he known how much worse the record might have been -- had all been revealed , had Seabrooke disclosed the drugging , the theft of his letter to his father , and the destruction , unintentional though it was , of the money ?", "context_len": 50}}}, "ringleader": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "But for some unknown reason Lewis Flagg , who was usually the ringleader in all such little amenities , held his peace and had nothing to say", "context_len": 27}}}, "amenities": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "But for some unknown reason Lewis Flagg , who was usually the ringleader in all such little amenities , held his peace and had nothing to say", "context_len": 27}}}, "commendatory": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "To the younger boys he addressed only a few commendatory words , praising them for their fair progress and general good conduct , and wishing them a very pleasant holiday", "context_len": 30}}}, "sneaky": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "But to those among them who had any sense of honor left , there was an added shame when this was presented anew to them , and as Percy afterwards said , he did `` feel uncommonly mean and sneaky", "context_len": 40}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` He 's a sneak , and I suspect he was doing something sneaky", "context_len": 14}}}, "restitution": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Was it possible that there was one among the circle who would do such a thing ? If so , let him make confession and restitution before he left to-day , and although he could not be suffered to return to the school , he might at least be spared the shame of confronting his schoolmates after discovery", "context_len": 58}}}, "banknote": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` What is it ? Percy Neville 's money ? '' asked Charlie , who , in common with every other boy in the school , knew the story of Percy 's lost banknote", "context_len": 34}}}, "inferences": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Quick-sighted and quick-witted , he drew his own inferences and attacked the boy from another quarter", "context_len": 16}}}, "cupidity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "But once in his possession , the note excited his cupidity and a strong desire to keep it", "context_len": 18}}}, "scot": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "Moffat enough to let her understand that he had unwittingly seen something he was not intended to see , and she , knowing enough of boys in general and of that senior class in particular , to be sure that Charlie would not go scot free , if the truth were known , hastened to comply with his request", "context_len": 59}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Stone , and he has gone home for mother and Lily , and the whole lot and scot of them ; they 're all coming to-morrow", "context_len": 26}}}, "scoundrelly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "The most careless and reckless among them were shocked at the enormity of the offence with which he stood charged , a theft of such magnitude , and then the scoundrelly attempt to make it appear that another had been guilty of it", "context_len": 43}}}, "muffs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Such a set of muffs , teachers and scholars , I never met", "context_len": 14}}}, "glorification": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 56, "context": "He returned to her the hundred-dollar note which had such a story attached to it , and in his turn had to hear from Lena her belief that the second sum sent for his relief had come from Hannah , and that the old nurse had sacrificed the gold which she had destined for her own glorification to his rescue from his predicament", "context_len": 63}}}, "uppish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "I can tell you he 's as uppish as the Grand Panjandrum himself about it , too ; says his sister is not an object of charity , and her father and brother are able to look after her", "context_len": 39}}}, "fiat": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "She did not reflect that , having a father and mother , it was not at all likely that her brother 's fiat would decide the matter for Gladys either one way or the other", "context_len": 35}}}, "chevalier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "He would really rather have borne some severe punishment at the hands of his parents or teacher than he would one contemptuous word or look from him who was regarded by all his young relations and friends as a chevalier sans peur et sans reproche", "context_len": 45}}}, "sans": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "unvarnished": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 53, "context": "He hesitated for a moment , not from any intention of refusing to give his uncle his confidence , or denying that he had been in trouble , but from a desire to frame his confession in the best manner possible ; but nothing came to his aid other than the plain , unvarnished truth ; nothing else , he felt , would serve his turn here with that steady , searching eye upon him ; and in a moment he had taken his resolve , and the whole shameful tale was poured into Colonel Rush 's ears", "context_len": 98}}}, "nurslings": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "She loved her and her children dearly , and would have done anything in the world for them , and if any one brought harm or trouble to her nurslings , she ruffled up her feathers like an old hen , and thought herself at liberty to do or say anything she pleased", "context_len": 53}}}, "forfeiting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "Of the kindly advice and admonition given to Percy by his uncle there is no need to speak further ; but it resulted in making Percy feel that he would do anything rather than again run the risk of forfeiting the good opinion which he now valued more than ever", "context_len": 50}}}, "obtrusive": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 67, "context": "Maggie and Bessie were almost as much at home in Colonel Rush 's house as they were in their own , and had they chosen to go in and out twenty times a day , they would always have been welcome ; and the young friends who accompanied them were about as much at their ease , although not one among the quintette would ever have been obtrusive or troublesome", "context_len": 70}}}, "bevy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Hallo ! what a bevy of birdlings ! But how comes it that you are not at Miss Ashton 's ? I have just left my Laura there , and she is in a state of frantic expectation over this composition prize the finest authoress among you is to gain this morning", "context_len": 53}}}, "spokeswoman": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` Oh , yes , sir , all of us , '' answered Lily Norris , always ready to be spokeswoman ; `` we are going to Miss Ashton 's in a few moments", "context_len": 34}}}, "unbiassed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "`` But , '' he said in conclusion , `` in comparing the compositions written at the commencement of the term of trial and those last submitted to Miss Ashton , I had , from my own unbiassed judgment , and before I had learned the choice of your teacher , decided that the one best entitled to the prize and the bestowal of this art education is Miss Bessie Bradford", "context_len": 71}}}, "award": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 53, "context": "Ashton , smiling at her , `` while giving all due credit to your sister Maggie 's compositions , which I have read with much pleasure , I still repeat that no little girl in the class has made such manifest improvement as yourself , and to you both your teacher and myself award the prize", "context_len": 56}}}, "providentially": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Do you all agree that since Lena Neville has been providentially prevented from continuing her efforts , and since she made so much improvement while she was able to enter the lists , that Bessie shall be permitted to resign this reward to her , and that she shall be the one to name the candidate for my trust ? ''", "context_len": 62}}}, "dissenting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Yes , sir ; yes , sir , '' came without one dissenting voice from the young group", "context_len": 19}}}, "girly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Well , we can clap , anyway , '' said Lily , `` that 's girly enough , '' and she forthwith set the example , which was speedily followed by the rest , Mr", "context_len": 36}}}, "mathews": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Mathews", "context_len": 1}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 0, "context": "MATHEWS", "context_len": 1}}}, "bessie": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Bessie might well say they were nice people in this house", "context_len": 11}}}, "cambridge": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "CAMBRIDGE : PRESS OF JOHN WILSON AND SON", "context_len": 8}}}, "heedlessly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Maggie thanked him for his thoughtful kindness when she saw the misfortune which had happened to Hafed ; for the little Persian , always anxious to please his `` Missys , '' had grasped too heedlessly the tempting branches , and was now wringing his fingers as he danced about , half laughing , half crying , and saying , --", "context_len": 61}}}, "tempers": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 11, "context": "And what hard work it was to struggle with the evil tempers and passions to which she had so long given way ; how difficult , when some trifle vexed her , to keep back the sharp and angry word , to put down the wish to bend everything to her own will , to learn of Him who was meek and lowly in heart !", "context_len": 66}}}, "pert": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "This he did five or six times in succession , to the great amusement and satisfaction of the little ones , who were beginning to hope he would go with them all the way to the house , when with a pert , defiant whisk of his bushy tail , he leaped down the bank , and was lost to sight in the thick trees of the ravine", "context_len": 68}}}, "bradford": {"freq": 72, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 72, "word_index": 0, "context": "Bradford that he did not know which he liked best , -- Bessie with her dainty , quiet , ladylike little ways , or Maggie with her half-shy , half-roguish manner , and love of fun and mischief", "context_len": 38}}}, "pantries": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "And a delightful house it was to make acquaintance with , -- full of all kinds of odd nooks and corners , with two or three steps here leading up to one room , two or three there going down to another ; queer little pantries and cupboards and crooked passages , and altogether unlike any other house the children had ever seen", "context_len": 63}}}, "almonds": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "They were particularly amused by the antics of one saucy rogue , who , not satisfied with the share which had fallen to him , crept under Bob 's arm , and actually began thrusting his nose into his pocket in search of more almonds", "context_len": 45}}}, "robs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` He is the greedy one of the lot , '' said Bob , `` and I have to watch him , or he eats his own share and then robs those that are weaker than himself , if he gets the chance", "context_len": 43}}}, "bunnies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "What `` ailed the fellows , '' was soon seen , for even as he spoke , Flossy , who had been left shut up in the house lest he should frighten the bunnies , came tearing round a great rock , and rushed to the foot of the tree , where he commenced a great barking", "context_len": 57}}}, "dasher": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "But Bessie found churning water in her own little churn at home , was a very different thing from trying to make the butter come with that heavy dasher ; she could scarcely stir it , and in a moment she was quite satisfied", "context_len": 44}}}, "rockaway": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "A book could be filled in telling all the children did in this charming place , of the drives they took in the great rockaway , of their rows upon the lake , of their walks in the lovely woods and glens , and even of one or two wild clambers over the higher rocks where the little girls had to be helped up and down , and Bessie often to be carried in the arms of papa or Uncle Ruthven", "context_len": 81}}}, "carryall": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "BRADFORD had brought from the city a famous rockaway , or carryall , large enough to hold all his own family and one or two persons beside ; light but strong , and just the thing for these mountain roads", "context_len": 40}}}, "dappled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "It was a bright , sunny afternoon , yet not too warm to be pleasant , the air was gay with the hum of bees and butterflies , the blue sky , dappled with fleecy clouds , was reflected in the clear water , mingled with the shadow of the rocks and trees ; swallows skimmed over the surface of the lake , chasing the myriads of insects which hummed in the summer air ; and as the carriage drove along the road which lay between the water and the great overhanging rocks , more than one fish was seen to dart swiftly away from the shady pool where he had been snugly lying till disturbed by the rumble of the wheels", "context_len": 122}}}, "greyhound": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "A great white cat peeped out from behind the geraniums which filled the window ; a greyhound lay upon the doormat , and beneath and about the porch hung several bird-cages , containing half a dozen canaries and two mocking-birds , while a donkey and a tame goat looked , the one over , the other between the bars of the fence which divided their little pasture ground from the neat garden", "context_len": 72}}}, "wis": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 53, "context": "She seized Frankie in her arms and covered him with kisses ; but that roguish young gentleman after exclaiming , `` Hallo , Patty ! '' would have nothing more to say to her , and struggled to be set free that he might run and see `` dat nanny-doat and dat pony wis long ears", "context_len": 56}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` Is he sorry wis me now ? I not do so any more , '' said Frankie", "context_len": 18}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "sumach": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "But at the side of the paddock ran one of the many streams in which this mountain country rejoiced , shadowed with a growth of elder , sumach , and other high bushes", "context_len": 33}}}, "nonesuch": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "grimaces": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "A wicked looking boy and girl they were too , and as Fred appeared they greeted him with grimaces and vulgar noises ; then as he darted at the boy , turned and ran", "context_len": 34}}}, "cos": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` He did it cos he had a mind to , '' she said , shaking herself free from Fred 's hold , `` and he 'll do it agin if he has a mind to", "context_len": 36}}}, "rakes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "They had a little garden house too , where they kept their spades , rakes and other tools , for each child had a plot of ground for its own , and every fall they had a flower and fruit show , when their father and mother gave prizes , not only for the best flowers and fruit , but also to those whose gardens had been neatly kept during the summer", "context_len": 72}}}, "zinnia": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "Why , last year dear little Katy took a prize and for what do you think ? Why , for a poor forlorn zinnia which she had nursed through the whole summer , and which bore but one scanty flower", "context_len": 40}}}, "fred": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Fred described the boy and girl , as nearly as he could , and Mr", "context_len": 15}}}, "dairies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "They live as they can , by robbing orchards , hen-roosts , dairies and cornfields during the summer ; picking up odd bits , and stealing whatever they can lay their fingers on in the winter , half starved and half frozen the most of the time", "context_len": 47}}}, "atoms": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "Well , will you believe it ? no sooner did she catch sight of them , and guess what they were after , than she just dashed the plate down on the rocks , smashing it to atoms , and ran like a deer", "context_len": 44}}}, "ungratefulness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` I never heard of such ungratefulness , and Mrs", "context_len": 10}}}, "hacked": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "And that 's the way ; they are both so sly and artful , they are seldom or never caught in the act ; so though when a melon patch or hen-roost is robbed , or some fine young trees are hacked to pieces , every one feels sure it was Lem or Dolly who did the mischief , yet it is difficult to prove it on them", "context_len": 68}}}, "thrashings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Lem has had more thrashings than any boy of his size that ever lived , I believe , but what 's the use ? It only makes him worse than ever", "context_len": 31}}}, "taunting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "They gathered about the captives , taunting and jeering at them and striking them with sticks until the guards intervened , not out of pity , but in order that the captives might be kept in good condition for the horrid festival that Nascanora was planning", "context_len": 46}}}, "pawing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` We were putting him on the sledge when I saw Sol , who had trotted back to the place where we found Owen , pawing away once more at the snow , snorting and sniffing as if he were displeased", "context_len": 41}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "I stood quite still , very much amused at the feeble pawing little hand of my young master , such a contrast to the steady firm one of the man", "context_len": 30}}}, "heliotropes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "Porter also kindly gave them such seeds as would do to plant at this late season ; and papa , who had driven down to the village with the Colonel and Uncle Ruthven , came back with a number of verbenas , heliotropes , geranium slips and other pretty things , which were set out in the new gardens", "context_len": 59}}}, "geranium": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 61, "context": "The tiny fences were pulled up , and scattered far and wide ; lady-slippers , mignonette , verbenas , and all the other simple flowers which had flourished so well , and given such pride and delight to the little gardeners , were rooted up and trampled into the earth ; and , worse than all , the beloved heliotrope and geranium were torn leaf from leaf and sprig from sprig , while their main stems had been twisted and bent , till no hope remained that even these could be revived", "context_len": 92}}}, "hinge": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "A square hole was cut for a window , but had neither glass nor casement ; and the door hung by one rusty hinge , which looked as if it might give way at any moment", "context_len": 36}}}, "columbine": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "The woods around were full of wild flowers , briar roses , delicate primroses , and the bright red columbine , and even here and there , a late anemone ; the little star-like flower , looking almost as if it had dropped from heaven , and wondered to find itself alone and solitary , so far away from its sister stars", "context_len": 62}}}, "anemone": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "The woods around were full of wild flowers , briar roses , delicate primroses , and the bright red columbine , and even here and there , a late anemone ; the little star-like flower , looking almost as if it had dropped from heaven , and wondered to find itself alone and solitary , so far away from its sister stars", "context_len": 62}}}, "jutted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "A lovely room it was indeed , carpeted with moss , curtained and shaded by the green trees which waved overhead , and furnished with seats made by one or two fallen stones on one side , on the other by a ledge of rock which jutted out at just such a height as to make a convenient bench for little people", "context_len": 62}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Colonel Rush saw now what he had not known before , -- a narrow ledge of rock , scarce six inches wide , jutted beyond the slope of gravel , and , on this , his sister 's form partly rested", "context_len": 41}}}, "frankie": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Frankie was there too , for he had begged to come , and had been allowed to do so on the promise that he would behave very well and sit still", "context_len": 31}}}, "chillers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Suffer 'ittle chillens -- dat is a pollywod , Fred -- suffer 'ittle chillers to tome unto me ; '' said Frankie", "context_len": 23}}}, "scrawls": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 48, "context": "Rush down to Bessie , were so taken up with it , that they were not thinking of Frankie , who for some time sat quiet between his little sisters , busy with the Colonel 's pencil-case and a piece of paper , on which he was making scrawls which he called `` pollywods", "context_len": 54}}}, "glens": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "A book could be filled in telling all the children did in this charming place , of the drives they took in the great rockaway , of their rows upon the lake , of their walks in the lovely woods and glens , and even of one or two wild clambers over the higher rocks where the little girls had to be helped up and down , and Bessie often to be carried in the arms of papa or Uncle Ruthven", "context_len": 81}}}, "clambers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "A book could be filled in telling all the children did in this charming place , of the drives they took in the great rockaway , of their rows upon the lake , of their walks in the lovely woods and glens , and even of one or two wild clambers over the higher rocks where the little girls had to be helped up and down , and Bessie often to be carried in the arms of papa or Uncle Ruthven", "context_len": 81}}}, "grandmamma": {"freq": 51, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` So he had said in the letter which came last night , and grandmamma had read it over many times , smiled over it , cried over it , and talked of the writer , until , if Maggie and Bessie had doubted the fact before , they must then have been quite convinced that no other children ever possessed such a wonderful uncle as this Uncle Ruthven of theirs", "context_len": 71}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` Oh , no ! '' sobbed the little girl , clinging about her grandmother 's neck , `` it is n't that , grandmamma ; I do n't care much if she did pull my hair ; but oh , I was so wicked ! I was in a passion again , and I was so bad ! I struck that man , I know I did", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Maggie wondered why grandmamma bit her lip , and why her eyebrows drew together in a frown , as if she were angry", "context_len": 23}}}, "althea": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "But these , after all , were not very great troubles , and were soon remedied ; for another bower was built beneath two tall althea bushes near the house , where no evil disposed person would dare to come ; and the boys took care not to leave their boats where they could be meddled with again", "context_len": 58}}}, "verbena": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The slips of geranium , heliotrope , and verbena had taken root , and were growing nicely , while lady-slippers , petunias , mignonette , and other seed plants had sprung up , and began to spread as if they meant to make up for lost time", "context_len": 47}}}, "petunias": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "The slips of geranium , heliotrope , and verbena had taken root , and were growing nicely , while lady-slippers , petunias , mignonette , and other seed plants had sprung up , and began to spread as if they meant to make up for lost time", "context_len": 47}}}, "pollywog": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 3, "context": "To have the pollywog for her own , or to have him any where near her , was the last thing Aunt Patty desired , for she had a great fear of snakes , toads , frogs and such creatures ; and now she started back in dismay as the sprawling thing was laid upon her handsome black silk dress", "context_len": 60}}}, "oo": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` MADDIE , '' said Frankie , running up to his sister the next morning with a pair of worsted reins in his hands , `` will oo fis my weins ? ''", "context_len": 33}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Dat sing oo are in , '' replied Frankie", "context_len": 10}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "pats": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "To go there each day with Fanny , and see her churn , or skim the rich cream from the milk , or roll the beautiful yellow butter into dainty little pats for the table ; or , to have the butter spaddles put into their own hands , and help Fanny in this last piece of work ; or sometimes even to pack the butter down into the great stone pots , -- all these were pleasures which never lost their charm", "context_len": 83}}}, "clefts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "But the boy still declared he knew nothing of any cup ; and , after hunting in vain for it among all the clefts of the `` Chief 's Head , '' they had to give up the search", "context_len": 39}}}, "uncombed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The water was dripping from her uncombed , tangled hair and poor rags ; her face was pale , and her bare arms and knees were cut and bleeding ; and , although the morning was clearing up close and warm , she shivered and drew herself together as if she were suffering from cold", "context_len": 55}}}, "birdies": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "Yes , spring was in the air , and the birdies knew it , though earth as yet showed but few signs of it", "context_len": 24}}}, "sunbonnet": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "John picked up a dirty old sunbonnet she used to wear , lying right in Bessie 's garden , and he says she was not at home when he went up with Lem last night", "context_len": 35}}}, "somersets": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 50, "context": "`` Papa , Uncle Ruthven , Aunt Bessie , and Aunt Annie are going with us , and we are going to have a grand corn-roasting up in the woods ; hurrah ! hurrah ! '' and Fred tossed his cap in the air , and turned two or three somersets on the grass , which Frankie immediately tried to imitate , but only succeeded in tumbling over on his side", "context_len": 71}}}, "cleansing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "The little knees and arms could be washed in the stream , and dried on the towel which had been brought ; but there was no way of cleansing the blackened clothes , and Bessie was distressed at the thought of passing the whole morning in such a condition", "context_len": 49}}}, "graceless": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` Did I ever hear such impudence ! '' exclaimed Starr , roused out of his usual stiffness ; `` well , you are the most graceless creature I ever did see", "context_len": 32}}}, "gi": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` Do n't you , now ? '' said Lem , looking up ; `` well , I thought may be you did n't when you gi ' me the money for the straw", "context_len": 34}}}, "snappishly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` No , I sha n't neither , I aint goin ' to stir , '' she answered snappishly , with one quick , suspicious glance at the children , and another towards the trunk of the old tree against which she leaned", "context_len": 43}}}, "sorriness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` And she said she was sorry about the gardens , '' said Bessie ; `` maybe it was her sorriness that made her tell where my cup was", "context_len": 29}}}, "channelled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Moreover , Lem had washed his face , for the first time perhaps in many weeks or months ; and , although he had left his cheeks all streaked and channelled , it was at least an attempt at something better , and , so far , even this was promising", "context_len": 51}}}, "plaything": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 42, "context": "Upon an overturned sheaf , sat mammy , her baby on her knee , the little one crowing and laughing , and shaking her dimpled hands , each of which grasped half a dozen ears of wheat , a new and wonderful plaything to baby 's eyes , as they bobbed their heads up and down with the motion", "context_len": 59}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "When we go to town , then my pet may have it , if you do n't want it ; and a nice plaything it will make for her then", "context_len": 30}}}, "locust": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 66, "context": "What a pleasant ride that was : out of the field where the bars had been let down ; past other fields ready , or nearly ready , for the harvesting ; pale green oats , and golden wheat , the white , sweet-scented buckwheat , and the tall Indian corn ; then through the orchard where a flock of sheep were feeding , past the locust grove , and then into the farmyard ; stopping at last between the open doors of the great barn !", "context_len": 87}}}, "ragamuffin": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` Why , you 're not offended with me , are you ? '' said John , `` and all along of that ragamuffin up there", "context_len": 26}}}, "prickers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Why , there are horrid prickers on it ! ''", "context_len": 11}}}, "mullein": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "So the kind nurse opened the paper containing the biscuits , and spread it on the flat stone on which the children sat ; next she pulled two broad mullein leaves , and put a handful of berries on each , and then having produced the drinking cup she always carried when the children went on an expedition , she asked John where she should find a stream , and one being near at hand as usual , the cup was soon filled and placed beside the other things", "context_len": 89}}}, "laps": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "It belonged to the little cousins down at the homestead , and was a great pet , and now it came rubbing its head against them , and putting its hoof into their laps , as if it were very glad to see some familiar faces", "context_len": 46}}}, "penitence": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` Listen , oh , listen , our Father all holy ! Humble and sorrowful , owning my sin , Hear me confess , in my penitence lowly , How in my weakness temptation came in", "context_len": 36}}}, "gentians": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Blue gentians , golden-rod , Michaelmas daisies , and the pretty , red partridge-berry grew all about , and the children soon had their hands full", "context_len": 26}}}, "agonizing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "Stanton , spite of his agonizing anxiety , wondered to see , unrolled the shawl from his own body , and fastened it about that of the senseless figure beside him ; then gave the word to raise her", "context_len": 39}}}, "moped": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "But there was no need to send the little girls away ; they were only too quiet , and moped about the house in a way that was quite melancholy to see", "context_len": 32}}}, "tidier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "He also tried to keep the old place a little tidier , and , when he knew that any of the family from the Lake House were coming there , would wash his face and hands ; and a comb having been brought by some of the ladies for Dolly 's use , he would draw it a few times through his tangled locks", "context_len": 64}}}, "squash": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 53, "context": "`` She made you take back old Miss Mapes ' handkercher , and made me go and tell Miss Jones she was sorry for unhookin ' her clothes-line and lettin ' down the clothes in the dirt ; and , oh ! do you think , there 's the biggest kind of a squash down in Todd 's cornfield , and I was a goin ' to get it for him , and Dol coaxed me not", "context_len": 77}}}, "scissor": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` Yes , '' said Dolly ; and then asked , `` Could you give me a nice bit of white paper and a scissor ? ''", "context_len": 27}}}, "fixes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` I know , '' said Lem ; `` she wants to fix up her posy , like the gardener fixes 'em up to the big farm", "context_len": 27}}}, "outlive": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "But she could not be satisfied without putting it about the flower with her own hands , while Lem held the pot for her ; and it was touching to see how the poor , wasted fingers fluttered feebly about the blossom that was to outlive her , -- touching it so tenderly , and folding the fringed paper about it with such care", "context_len": 64}}}, "marigold": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "says she had rather have Dolly 's little marigold than the prettiest prize that ever was , but I am afraid M", "context_len": 22}}}, "laborers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "They had a flag with them , which they waved and cheered whenever they passed a house or saw laborers at work in the fields ; and the people seemed to like it , and came running to see the fun , and waved and cheered in return , as good-naturedly as if they thought it was all done for their pleasure", "context_len": 62}}}, "lisping": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 60, "context": "What a delightful bustle there was when our friends arrived at the homestead , and the whole family came pouring out to receive them ! For the time , Maggie and Bessie forgot the little sore spot in their hearts which was caused by the thought that they had no share in that which brought them all together , until lisping little Katy Bradford , who was very fond of her young cousins , said , --", "context_len": 77}}}, "festooned": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "It was festooned with dahlias , chrysanthemums , and other bright-colored autumn flowers and leaves ; and , although the display upon the table might not have seemed very grand to less interested eyes , the children desired nothing better ; and it certainly did them great credit", "context_len": 48}}}, "exotic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "But there was more than one heart at that table for which those feeble , sickly plants had a value far beyond that of the richest and rarest exotic", "context_len": 29}}}, "cheep": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "overran": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` For our Maggie and Bessie , the dear little workers in the garden of the Lord , who tended the Christian plants of patience , kindness , and forbearance , till their lovely blossoms overran the evil weeds of malice and ill-will , and sowed the seeds of that which brought forth fruit for the glory of God", "context_len": 59}}}, "cheeps": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "Never was a birdie coaxed with more pretty names than was this one during the next ten minutes ; and he seemed to like them well , for , after answering with one or two more half-timid `` cheeps , '' he broke into a soft trill , which soon swelled into a clear , sweet song of joy", "context_len": 59}}}, "trill": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 46, "context": "Never was a birdie coaxed with more pretty names than was this one during the next ten minutes ; and he seemed to like them well , for , after answering with one or two more half-timid `` cheeps , '' he broke into a soft trill , which soon swelled into a clear , sweet song of joy", "context_len": 59}}}, "ecstasies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Maggie and Bessie were in ecstasies , and Cousin Alexander certainly had reason to think his kindness had given all the pleasure he intended it should", "context_len": 26}}}, "keepsake": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "' Oh , yes , you darling birdie ! do you want to say you know all about it ? Bessie , let 's think the canary is a kind of keepsake from Dolly , 'cause you know it seems as if it came by her , and mamma says it is of no use to take the marigolds to town , for they will be quite dead in a few weeks", "context_len": 72}}}, "pieced": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "The carpet , though somewhat worn , and pieced in more than one place , was well swept and tidy , and the stove and the kettle which sang merrily upon its top were polished till they shone", "context_len": 38}}}, "blindness": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "The younger children had gone through it lightly , Jennie escaping altogether ; but poor Willie had been nigh to death , and the terrible disease had left its mark in the blindness which was creeping upon him", "context_len": 38}}}, "tenderer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Granby 's voice always took a tenderer tone when she spoke of Willie", "context_len": 13}}}, "unsaid": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "What is said can not be unsaid ; and they will soon find out for themselves what the old lady is", "context_len": 21}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "You have been saying that which was better left unsaid , and your mother has done wrong in listening to you", "context_len": 21}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "The child is straightforward and honest , accustomed to speak as she thinks ; and if she has said what was better left unsaid , remember that you forced her to it", "context_len": 32}}}, "nursling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "And now came faithful old nurse to hear the good news and to have her share in the general family joy at the return of her first nursling , her beloved `` Master Ruthven", "context_len": 34}}}, "storing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` There was a large old barn standing at some distance from the house , used only for the storing of hay ; and as they found the sun too warm for play in the open air , Henry proposed they should go there and make some boats which later they might sail in the brook", "context_len": 56}}}, "summing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Maggie was rather vexed that she did not understand her without more words , but summing up all her courage , she said , --", "context_len": 25}}}, "spryer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Not but that she 's better than she was a spell back , and she 'd be spryer yet , I think , but for the trouble that 's weighin ' on her all the time , and hinders her getting well", "context_len": 42}}}, "hinders": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "Not but that she 's better than she was a spell back , and she 'd be spryer yet , I think , but for the trouble that 's weighin ' on her all the time , and hinders her getting well", "context_len": 42}}}, "aggravates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "If there 's a thing that aggravates me , it 's that , '' continued Mrs", "context_len": 16}}}, "calicoes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "So she 's wearin ' her summer calicoes yet , and even them are very short as to the skirts , and squeezed as to the waists , which ai n't good for a growin ' child", "context_len": 37}}}, "totes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "completest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` I think we 're the completest family that ever lived , '' said Maggie , looking around the room with great satisfaction , one evening when Colonel and Mrs", "context_len": 30}}}, "bookmaker": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` I 'll ask mamma for some paper , and I 'll write some every day till it 's done ; and then I 'll ask papa to take it to the bookmaker ; and when the book is made , we 'll sell it , and give the money to the poor", "context_len": 53}}}, "grandpapa": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 29, "context": "Now you know yourself that , although you took a great deal of pains , the hem was rather crooked and some of the stitches quite long , yet grandpapa was more pleased with that one than with the whole dozen which Aunt Helen hemmed , and which were beautifully done , because he knew that you had done the best you could , and that it was a great effort for you", "context_len": 73}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` But , mamma , '' said Maggie , `` we do n't want you to write it for us ; grandpapa will like it better if we do it all ourselves", "context_len": 32}}}, "muffling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "But it was no Santa Claus , and in spite of the muffling furs and the heavy beard , in spite of all the changes which ten long years of absence had made , the mother 's heart , and the mother 's eye knew her son , and rising from her seat with a low cry of joy , Mrs", "context_len": 61}}}, "ort": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "They had a travelling uncle who came home to them at last ; but he staid away ten years and did not come home even to see his mother , and I think he ort to do n't you ? But now he is come and has brought so many trunks and boxes with such lots and lots of things and kurositys in them that he is 'most like a Norz ' Ark only better , and his gret coat and cap are made of the bears ' skins he shot and he tells us about the tigers and lions and I do n't like it and Fred and Harry do and Bessie do n't too", "context_len": 116}}}, "frolics": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "Harry had a weak ankle , which in his boyish frolics he was constantly hurting , and now , having given it a slight sprain , he was laid up on the sofa", "context_len": 33}}}, "grieves": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "And it grieves Jesus when we feel naughty , and he saw my naughty heart to-night", "context_len": 16}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Solani grieves because he fears that harm may come to the stranger , he replied", "context_len": 15}}}, "brandished": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 108, "context": "`` Beware , woman ! '' shrieked Maggie , and Bessie , thinking it time for her to come to her sister 's aid , joined in the cry , `` Beware , woman ! '' while Franky , always ready to take part in any disturbance , struck at Aunt Patty with his whip , and shouted , `` 'Ware , woman ! '' and Hafed , knowing nothing but that this old lady had alarmed and distressed his young charge , and that it was his duty to protect them , raised his voice in a whoop of defiance , and snatching up the hearth-brush , brandished it in a threatening manner as he danced wildly about her", "context_len": 120}}}, "dervishes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "In the centre of the room stood an old lady who was a stranger to him , holding in her arms the screaming baby ; while around her danced his own little servant-boy , looking as if he might be one of the wild dervishes of his own country ; and in the crib stood his young nieces and Franky , all shouting , `` Beware , woman ! '' over and over again", "context_len": 74}}}, "wiz": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 83, "context": "Baby ceased her loud cries , and stretched out her dimpled arms to her uncle , with a beseeching whimper ; Hafed paused in his antics , and stood like a statue at sight of his master ; and the three other children all turned to him with exclamations of `` Oh , Uncle Ruthven ; we 're so glad ! '' and `` Please do n't leave us , '' from Maggie and Bessie ; and `` Make dat Patty be off wiz herself , '' from Franky", "context_len": 89}}}, "prattling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Poor Aunt Patty ! She heard the sweet , childish voices prattling about the house , ringing out so freely and joyfully in peals of merry laughter , or singing to simple music the pretty hymns and songs their dear mother and Mrs", "context_len": 43}}}, "roguishness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "In vain did the old woman question him ; he would give her no answer , only looking at her with roguishness dancing in every dimple on his chubby face ; and in vain did Jane search the halls and staircase", "context_len": 41}}}, "ky": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Do n't ky ; me dood now , '' lisped Franky , forgetting all his fear , and putting up his hand to wipe away her tears ; and from this minute Aunt Patty and Franky were the best of friends", "context_len": 42}}}, "lisped": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` Do n't ky ; me dood now , '' lisped Franky , forgetting all his fear , and putting up his hand to wipe away her tears ; and from this minute Aunt Patty and Franky were the best of friends", "context_len": 42}}}, "curry": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 65, "context": "Bradford , coming in unexpectedly one day , actually found the old lady on her hands and knees , in a corner , playing the part of a horse eating hay from a manger ; while Franky , clothes-brush in hand , was , much to his own satisfaction , pretending to rub her down , making the hissing noise used by coachmen when they curry a horse , and positively refusing to allow his patient playfellow to rise", "context_len": 79}}}, "practises": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "She does not repeat it in a parrot-like way , as many do , but she understands what it means , and practises it too , with her whole heart", "context_len": 30}}}, "stainless": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Yet , sorely tempted as he was , he never fell into sin , but returned to his Father 's heaven pure and stainless as he left it", "context_len": 28}}}, "meddles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Why , you do n't know how she meddles , and what things she says , even when she is not a bit mad", "context_len": 24}}}, "mends": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` It 's so nice ; it 's just like a fairy tale , when a good fairy comes and mends all the people that have been chopped to pieces , and makes them just as good as ever ; only this is true and that is not", "context_len": 48}}}, "unlatching": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Granby live here ? '' asked Jane , unlatching the gate", "context_len": 11}}}, "honeys": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Just at this moment nurse put her head in at the door with `` Come along , my honeys", "context_len": 19}}}, "completer": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "humped": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "She humped up her back and was cross , so Mrs", "context_len": 11}}}, "vexations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "We have all forgotten ourselves a little in the vexations of the morning", "context_len": 13}}}, "misspent": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 64, "context": "spend it in doing all she could for her Master 's service , in helping those of his children on whom he had laid pain and sorrows ? She had been wishing that she might be able to prove her love and gratitude for the great mercy that had been shown to her , that she might yet redeem the wasted years , the misspent life which lay behind her , and now when the Lord had given her the opportunity for which she had been longing , should she turn her back upon it , should she shut her ear to the cry of the needy , because to answer it would cost a sacrifice of her own wishes ? Should she bear the burdens of others", "context_len": 128}}}, "displeasing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "He is only afraid of displeasing his master ; he has neither love nor fear of One greater than that master , for he does not know there is such a wise and holy being", "context_len": 35}}}, "coo": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 54, "context": "It did Aunt Patty no harm , but a great deal of good , that long afternoon 's musing in the silent house , where no patter of children 's feet , nor any sound of young voices was heard ; for baby had gone to her grandmamma , so that even her soft coo and joyous crow were missing for some hours", "context_len": 63}}}, "dawson": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "Dawson", "context_len": 1}}}, "balusters": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Come and look over the balusters , but do n't let her see you , or else she 'll say , 'What are you staring at , child ? ' ''", "context_len": 31}}}, "tucks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` And it has such pretty frocks , all work and tucks and nice ribbons , '' said Jennie , determined not to be coaxed out of her envy and ill-humor , `` and our baby has to do with just a plain old slip with not a bit of trimming", "context_len": 51}}}, "chloroform": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "They had given the boy chloroform at the time , and then bound his eyes before he had recovered his senses", "context_len": 21}}}, "weakish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Rather weakish yet ; but when he can bear the light , and get out into the fresh air , it will do him good ; and I hope he 'll come round after a spell , now that his mind is at ease , and he 's had a sight of that he 'd set his heart on , even if we ca n't just follow out the doctor 's orders", "context_len": 72}}}, "moth": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Aunt Patty , you have laid up your treasure where moth and rust do not corrupt ; but surely it is bearing interest on earth", "context_len": 26}}}, "reservoir": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` Bessie , there 's such lots and lots of water out there ! You never saw so much , not even in the reservoir at the Central Park", "context_len": 29}}}, "fluted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Then there were a few which were fluted , which the children said were the prettiest of all", "context_len": 18}}}, "scup": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` I 'll show you the scup in a few minutes , as soon as I finish this job I 'm about", "context_len": 22}}}, "tot": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` What a funny little tot you are , '' said Walter", "context_len": 12}}}, "unsociable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "He was an ugly old fellow , and rather gruff and unsociable ; but , like some people , he was in reality better than he appeared", "context_len": 27}}}, "givers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Then it would have been hard to tell who were the most pleased , the givers or the receivers", "context_len": 19}}}, "receivers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Then it would have been hard to tell who were the most pleased , the givers or the receivers", "context_len": 19}}}, "anymore": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` No , I suppose not ; I guess it 's pretty naughty , and I wo n't make faces at her anymore", "context_len": 23}}}, "velocipede": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "In front of these was Tom 's place , -- an empty barrel turned upside-down for his desk , and Fred 's velocipede for his seat", "context_len": 26}}}, "pities": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "I suppose the reason is that the Bible is God 's own words , and he loved and pitied us so that he knew what we would want him to say , just as mother loves and pities me , and so knows what I like her to say", "context_len": 49}}}, "crowed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "Franky came inside that he might have his share , too ; and baby woke up , good-natured as she always was , and smiled and crowed at her father till he said he really thought she knew him , and was glad to see him", "context_len": 46}}}, "chatterbox": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Bessie had been taught that she must not interrupt when grown people were speaking ; so , though she was a little chatterbox when she had leave to talk , she knew when it was polite and proper for her to be quiet", "context_len": 43}}}, "yocks": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "I thinked about it when I was sitting on the yocks this morning , and I am going to ask mamma if it is yight", "context_len": 25}}}, "moans": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 61, "context": "Bessie woke many times , and as often as she did so , she found that nurse had not come to bed , and when she looked through the open door which led into her mother 's room , she saw either her father or mother walking up and down with the baby , trying to hush her pitiful cries and moans", "context_len": 62}}}, "turnovers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Bessie , I ca n't bear her if she does make us cookies and turnovers", "context_len": 15}}}, "cunninger": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "catchings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "All this was said with many sobs and tears and catchings of her breath , and grandmamma wished that Miss Adams could see the distress she had caused", "context_len": 28}}}, "cleanse": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` Just as I am , and waiting not To rid my soul of one dark blot , To thee , whose blood can cleanse each spot , O Lamb of God ! I come", "context_len": 35}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Just as I am thou wilt receive , Wilt welcome , pardon , cleanse , relieve , Because thy promise I believe , O Lamb of God ! I come", "context_len": 31}}}, "guava": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 39, "context": "`` Yes , ma'am , '' said Bessie , `` but I did n't know if I could eat all the toast , and I thought perhaps you only wanted me to eat just so much share of the guava as I eat a share of the toast ; so I eat that first to be sure", "context_len": 57}}}, "embroidering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "To-day Aunt Annie was embroidering a little piece of muslin , but she did not put it out of their sight , though she would answer no questions about it", "context_len": 30}}}, "clam": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "When the colonel found what they were about , he called for a couple of clam shells , and sent his man for a piece of wood and some twine ; with these he made a pair of scales , which Maggie and Bessie thought quite splendid", "context_len": 47}}}, "timothy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "They had sand for sugar , blades of timothy grass for corn , sea-weed for smoked beef and ham , and small pebbles for eggs , with larger ones for potatoes", "context_len": 31}}}, "yest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` Oh , no , '' answered Bessie , `` a great many are for papa and mamma , and the yest of the people I like", "context_len": 27}}}, "conundrum": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "She thought this a very fine joke ; and when , a moment after , her brothers came up to the house , she told them of Bessie 's `` conundrum", "context_len": 31}}}, "clams": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 16, "context": "First , a large , shallow hole was made in the sand , in which the clams were placed , standing on end ; a fire was built on top of them , and they were left until they were well roasted , when they were pulled out and eaten with bread and butter", "context_len": 54}}}, "chemise": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` I was counting , and there 's two ni'-gowns , and two chemise , and two everything , except only dresses , and there 's four of those , and they 're all marked like our things , -- 'Bessie , ' for yours , and 'Maggie ' for mine", "context_len": 51}}}, "perambulators": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "The new dolls went too , seated in the perambulators and snugly tucked in with the affghans , though it was such a warm day that when they reached the hotel , Bessie said she was `` yoasted", "context_len": 38}}}, "bodkin": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "This was a little brown morocco work-bag , lined with blue silk , and fitted up with scissors , thimble , bodkin , and several other things", "context_len": 27}}}, "mottoes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "She found her mother and Aunt Helen and Aunt Annie all making mottoes", "context_len": 13}}}, "covenant": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Yes ; the Lord told Noah he would make a covenant with him 'that the waters should no more become a flood to destroy the earth ; ' and he made the rainbow for a sign that his promise should stand sure", "context_len": 43}}}, "misfit": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "`` Yes , and Maggie was very good to him , '' said Bessie ; `` she put her new doll in the wagon , and let him pull it about the nursery , only we watched him all the time , 'cause he 's such a misfit", "context_len": 48}}}, "spencer": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` Leave my Bessie be ! Leave my Bessie be ! '' she screamed , flying upon the boy , and fastening with both her hands upon the arm with which he was tearing away the spencer and feeling for the pocket-book , while he held Bessie with the other", "context_len": 50}}}, "stealer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Why , I 'm afraid you 're a stealer ! Do n't you know -- ''", "context_len": 16}}}, "scud": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "How she did scud though ! I do n't like to see a woman act the way she does , and I ca n't quite forgive her about Carlo and Bessie ; but I do think there 's some good in her", "context_len": 42}}}, "dick": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` She is a queer dick , '' said Fred , `` pulling your hair , and tormenting you out of your life one time , and telling you secrets another", "context_len": 31}}}, "horace": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Horace was touched then , and her words took hold of him I could see , though he tried to seem impatient and vexed , and would not permit me to allude to them", "context_len": 34}}}, "almighty": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Throw away all others , and cast yourself upon the almighty arm which is stretched out to sustain and aid you", "context_len": 22}}}, "morris": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "MORRIS", "context_len": 1}}}, "ranking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "RANKING", "context_len": 1}}}, "william": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "WILLIAM", "context_len": 1}}}, "james": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "JAMES", "context_len": 1}}}, "typo": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Page 216 : `` affghan '' may be a typo for `` afghan", "context_len": 13}}}, "granddaughters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Hall ; `` but I should like to know what has become of two little granddaughters of mine , who went away to the sea-shore two months since", "context_len": 28}}}, "vultures": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 45, "context": "How Bomba met two white rubber hunters , Gillis and Dorn , and won their gratitude by saving their lives when their camp was attacked at night by jaguars ; how he trapped the cooanaradi when it pursued him ; how he drove off the vultures when they assailed his friends the monkeys ; how the latter came to his aid when the cabin was besieged by the headhunters -- these adventures are told in the first book of this series , entitled : Bomba the Jungle Boy ; or , The Old Naturalist \u2019 s Secret", "context_len": 97}}}, "machete": {"freq": 75, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 71, "word_index": 43, "context": "Bomba was all these in one now , as he fought with the Indian , straining backward and forward , resisting the pressure upon his knife arm , striving with all the power in him to drive downward the shining point of his machete , to sink it to the hilt in his enemy \u2019 s flesh", "context_len": 57}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "Then , when no doubt remained , he threw the reptile to the ground and with one stroke of his machete cut off its head", "context_len": 25}}}, "parrakeets": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "At the first blast of that fierce gale the parrakeets flew screaming to shelter", "context_len": 14}}}, "dens": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "Bomba thanked his stars that it was daylight and that most of the nocturnal prowlers had retired to their dens where they would sleep the day away in preparation for the next foray", "context_len": 33}}}, "vulture": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "What affected Bomba most was the sight of old Tatuc lying dead on the ground in a pool of blood , his teeth imbedded in the neck of the vulture that he had taken to death with him", "context_len": 38}}}, "volition": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Bomba was swept along almost without his own volition", "context_len": 9}}}, "overlapped": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "This was made of the leaves of the ubussu palm , placed so that they overlapped one another", "context_len": 18}}}, "inject": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "Once it was a scorpion that fell on the lad , and he was forced to act quickly to kill the creature , before it could inject into his veins the deadly poison of its bite", "context_len": 36}}}, "cairn": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "He finished his work by piling up a cairn of heavy stones , so that no marauding beast of the jungle should search out the resting place of his friend", "context_len": 30}}}, "intertwining": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "He avoided upflung roots and intertwining vines as though by instinct", "context_len": 11}}}, "agouti": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "He would gather something on the way to pay for them , perhaps a jaboty or agouti , or possibly some eggs of the forest tortoise , which were always acceptable to natives of the region", "context_len": 36}}}, "tortoise": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "He would gather something on the way to pay for them , perhaps a jaboty or agouti , or possibly some eggs of the forest tortoise , which were always acceptable to natives of the region", "context_len": 36}}}, "fixity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "As Casson had said , the savages were like children , as far as fixity of purpose was concerned", "context_len": 19}}}, "topaz": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "The sky above was turquoise and the air , washed clean by rain , was like topaz", "context_len": 17}}}, "trogons": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Then there were the trogons , motmots and kingfishers glowing with iridescent hues , flocks of scarlet macaws , flamingoes almost equally gorgeous , each standing on one long slender leg and basking in the sun ; herons , plover , toucans and scores of other curious birds that make the Amazon jungle the most wonderful natural aviary on earth", "context_len": 60}}}, "motmots": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Then there were the trogons , motmots and kingfishers glowing with iridescent hues , flocks of scarlet macaws , flamingoes almost equally gorgeous , each standing on one long slender leg and basking in the sun ; herons , plover , toucans and scores of other curious birds that make the Amazon jungle the most wonderful natural aviary on earth", "context_len": 60}}}, "kingfishers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Then there were the trogons , motmots and kingfishers glowing with iridescent hues , flocks of scarlet macaws , flamingoes almost equally gorgeous , each standing on one long slender leg and basking in the sun ; herons , plover , toucans and scores of other curious birds that make the Amazon jungle the most wonderful natural aviary on earth", "context_len": 60}}}, "iridescent": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Then there were the trogons , motmots and kingfishers glowing with iridescent hues , flocks of scarlet macaws , flamingoes almost equally gorgeous , each standing on one long slender leg and basking in the sun ; herons , plover , toucans and scores of other curious birds that make the Amazon jungle the most wonderful natural aviary on earth", "context_len": 60}}}, "macaws": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Then there were the trogons , motmots and kingfishers glowing with iridescent hues , flocks of scarlet macaws , flamingoes almost equally gorgeous , each standing on one long slender leg and basking in the sun ; herons , plover , toucans and scores of other curious birds that make the Amazon jungle the most wonderful natural aviary on earth", "context_len": 60}}}, "flamingoes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Then there were the trogons , motmots and kingfishers glowing with iridescent hues , flocks of scarlet macaws , flamingoes almost equally gorgeous , each standing on one long slender leg and basking in the sun ; herons , plover , toucans and scores of other curious birds that make the Amazon jungle the most wonderful natural aviary on earth", "context_len": 60}}}, "plover": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "Then there were the trogons , motmots and kingfishers glowing with iridescent hues , flocks of scarlet macaws , flamingoes almost equally gorgeous , each standing on one long slender leg and basking in the sun ; herons , plover , toucans and scores of other curious birds that make the Amazon jungle the most wonderful natural aviary on earth", "context_len": 60}}}, "toucans": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "Then there were the trogons , motmots and kingfishers glowing with iridescent hues , flocks of scarlet macaws , flamingoes almost equally gorgeous , each standing on one long slender leg and basking in the sun ; herons , plover , toucans and scores of other curious birds that make the Amazon jungle the most wonderful natural aviary on earth", "context_len": 60}}}, "aviary": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 57, "context": "Then there were the trogons , motmots and kingfishers glowing with iridescent hues , flocks of scarlet macaws , flamingoes almost equally gorgeous , each standing on one long slender leg and basking in the sun ; herons , plover , toucans and scores of other curious birds that make the Amazon jungle the most wonderful natural aviary on earth", "context_len": 60}}}, "mora": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "There was the giant mora tree , two hundred feet high , aglow with clusters of scarlet blossoms , feathery palms , the bright yellow trumpet flower with blooms so large that they were worn as hats by the Indian women and children , huge fuchsias with their purplish tubular bells , heliotrope , verbenas , orchids , glowing with all the colors of the rainbow", "context_len": 66}}}, "fuchsias": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "There was the giant mora tree , two hundred feet high , aglow with clusters of scarlet blossoms , feathery palms , the bright yellow trumpet flower with blooms so large that they were worn as hats by the Indian women and children , huge fuchsias with their purplish tubular bells , heliotrope , verbenas , orchids , glowing with all the colors of the rainbow", "context_len": 66}}}, "tubular": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "There was the giant mora tree , two hundred feet high , aglow with clusters of scarlet blossoms , feathery palms , the bright yellow trumpet flower with blooms so large that they were worn as hats by the Indian women and children , huge fuchsias with their purplish tubular bells , heliotrope , verbenas , orchids , glowing with all the colors of the rainbow", "context_len": 66}}}, "drabness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "The riot of color died on the borders of the swamp , and its place was taken by drabness and desolation", "context_len": 21}}}, "mazes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "Seeing the terror into which their priestess had been thrown , a strange wild singing rose from the native women as they wove in and out in fantastic mazes , evidently designed to ward off the evil portent", "context_len": 38}}}, "intersected": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Part of it was intersected with deep pools , in which he had to wade , sometimes to the waist", "context_len": 20}}}, "pathless": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "But Bomba knew the swamp as he knew the jungle , knew how to keep a reasonably straight course through the pathless waste and how to avoid the deeper and more dangerous parts", "context_len": 33}}}, "anacondas": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 47, "context": "How Bomba saved the camp of Gillis and Dorn , rubber hunters , from a night attack by jaguars -- how he trapped the deadly cooanaradi , the most dreaded serpent of the South American wilds , when it pursued him ; his adventures with alligators and anacondas ; the besieging of his cabin by the headhunters ; how his friends of the forest came to his aid when he was fearfully beset ; all this is narrated in the first volumes of this series , entitled : Bomba , the Jungle Boy ; or , The Old Naturalist \u2019 s Secret", "context_len": 102}}}, "anaconda": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 30, "context": "That hate dated back to the time he had been attacked by one of the reptiles and Casson had fired the rifle , which , though it frightened away the anaconda , had had such dire results to poor Casson himself", "context_len": 41}}}, "itched": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "His fingers itched for the comforting feel of his bow and arrow", "context_len": 12}}}, "somnolent": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "They were lazy and somnolent , scarcely aware of his presence", "context_len": 11}}}, "boa": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 36, "context": "Bomba threw the head from him with a sharp exclamation of disgust , mingled with relief , and stepped quickly to one side to escape the flailing of the writhing , twisting body of the headless boa constrictor", "context_len": 38}}}, "enfolding": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "He drew it forth from its sheath with his right hand and slashed furiously at the enfolding coils", "context_len": 18}}}, "slash": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "And at that instant a lucky slash went deep and severed the spinal cord", "context_len": 14}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "He was safe for the present and could smile grimly as they gnashed their tusks , those terrible tusks that were like so many knives and which could so easily slash him to bits", "context_len": 34}}}, "flailing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Bomba threw the head from him with a sharp exclamation of disgust , mingled with relief , and stepped quickly to one side to escape the flailing of the writhing , twisting body of the headless boa constrictor", "context_len": 38}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Flailing , sharp-thorned vines whipped about his head , stinging , half-blinding him", "context_len": 13}}}, "headless": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Bomba threw the head from him with a sharp exclamation of disgust , mingled with relief , and stepped quickly to one side to escape the flailing of the writhing , twisting body of the headless boa constrictor", "context_len": 38}}}, "constrictor": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 37, "context": "Bomba threw the head from him with a sharp exclamation of disgust , mingled with relief , and stepped quickly to one side to escape the flailing of the writhing , twisting body of the headless boa constrictor", "context_len": 38}}}, "glazed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "The horrible head , lying a little distance from the body , still gaped at him , though the malignant glitter of the eyes had been glazed by death", "context_len": 29}}}, "unchained": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Apart from these more spectacular demonstrations of the unchained forces of nature , there was a sense of gradual sinking that was sickening and ominous in the extreme", "context_len": 28}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Devering unchained him and led him toward the house", "context_len": 9}}}, "pellucid": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "If the man had loved the waterfall as Bomba did , his face would have lighted up and his eyes would have laughed , as Bomba had seen his own laugh one day when he had suddenly seen his face reflected in the pellucid waters of a lovely pool", "context_len": 49}}}, "ferreting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "Why had he forgotten for the moment Casson , poor old helpless Casson , left alone in the hut , an easy prey to the stealthy head-hunters if they should succeed in ferreting out his location", "context_len": 36}}}, "jaguar": {"freq": 69, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 69, "word_index": 44, "context": "He was fighting for two lives , his own and Casson \u2019 s , and , moreover , one of his fierce rages was upon him ; one of those wild bursts of fury that for the moment gave him the strength of the jaguar , the wile of the fox , the quickness of the snake", "context_len": 57}}}, "trussing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "He pounced upon it eagerly , and trussing it up with bush cord , swung it , still alive , across his shoulders", "context_len": 23}}}, "slaked": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "Farther back they had found a stream of clear , cool water , where they had slaked their thirst", "context_len": 19}}}, "slithering": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "It was the slithering of a snake through the underbrush , but a snake that , disturbed , was gliding away from the intruding boy", "context_len": 25}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Then he heard another sound , faint at first but gradually becoming more definite , a rustling as of some soft , long body slithering through the brush", "context_len": 28}}}, "enrage": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Caution told him it was wiser not to enrage such powerful foes", "context_len": 12}}}, "remorselessly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "He brooked no opposition , and swept every one who dared to dispute his rule remorselessly out of the way", "context_len": 20}}}, "eviction": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "But now they were growing fierce from this summary eviction", "context_len": 10}}}, "sinuously": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Their hair began to bristle and they commenced to creep forward , their bodies , still close to the ground , moving almost as sinuously as so many snakes", "context_len": 29}}}, "piston": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Teeth tearing , claws going like piston rods , the ferocious brutes fell to the ground and rolled over and over , growling , spitting , biting , each trying to get a strangle-hold on the other \u2019 s throat", "context_len": 40}}}, "duelists": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The boy circled about the duelists , seeking for an opportunity to help the friend who had so loyally helped him", "context_len": 21}}}, "loyally": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "The boy circled about the duelists , seeking for an opportunity to help the friend who had so loyally helped him", "context_len": 21}}}, "tapir": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 31, "context": "Then , as Bomba looked , the water was broken in several places by the horrid snouts and hideous jaws of other caymen , that had smelled the blood of the tapir and were hastening to have their share of the feast", "context_len": 42}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "And there was the tapir meat , which would make a fine meal for them both , roasted on a stout stick held over a blazing fire", "context_len": 27}}}, "unpropitious": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "He had a feeling , an instinct , that something unpropitious was in the air", "context_len": 15}}}, "stoically": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "The women and children of the tribe had gathered at one end of the maloca , and were looking on stoically at the scene", "context_len": 24}}}, "mildest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Palm leaves formed the roof of these rude abodes , wholly insufficient to shelter their owners from the mildest of tropical storms", "context_len": 22}}}, "inscrutable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "The chief himself was an old man , wizened and toothless , with an inscrutable expression in the small eyes he turned upon Bomba", "context_len": 24}}}, "scowls": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "All bore scowls similar to that of the scout who had led him to the maloca", "context_len": 16}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The scowls disappeared instantly from their faces and they echoed in chorus :", "context_len": 13}}}, "troublemakers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "And here was one of the troublemakers who had walked right into their hands", "context_len": 14}}}, "babble": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "The old medicine man broke out into a babble of words , sometimes almost beneath his breath , again rising to a shrill scream", "context_len": 24}}}, "intervention": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "A babble of excited exclamations broke out at this sudden intervention of the girl in Bomba \u2019 s behalf", "context_len": 19}}}, "shaman": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "Finally , when Bomba had begun to feel that he could not stand the strain much longer , the shaman opened his eyes , looked straight at the lad , and cried at the top of his cracked voice :", "context_len": 40}}}, "mauling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Bomba had stood the hauling and mauling without protest , though without much hope of any good coming from it", "context_len": 20}}}, "kaleidoscopic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The change was kaleidoscopic", "context_len": 4}}}, "sandaled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "As silently now as a panther he glided on , not a twig snapping beneath his sandaled feet", "context_len": 18}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Neram , bending lower , took one of Bomba \u2019 s sandaled feet and placed it on his neck as a sign that he was slave and Bomba master", "context_len": 29}}}, "outermost": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Bomba dropped on his stomach and wormed his way through the brush until he reached the outermost edge of the zone of light cast by the fire", "context_len": 27}}}, "incantation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Some sort of incantation had evidently been in progress , as was manifest from the presence of a kettle over the fire in which the medicine man had been stirring his horrid brew", "context_len": 33}}}, "outrace": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Setting off with strides like those of a deer , Bomba vowed that he would outrace the Indians", "context_len": 18}}}, "infuse": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "He made his way with desperate energy and all the speed he could infuse into his legs , pausing for no obstacle great or small", "context_len": 25}}}, "piranha": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "As he drew himself up , another piranha caught his foot in its vice-like jaws and hung on grimly , even after Bomba had drawn himself clear of the water", "context_len": 30}}}, "bloodcurdling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "And every instant he expected to hear the bloodcurdling war whoop of the savages as they rushed the cabin", "context_len": 19}}}, "playroom": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "This had been his playroom since his earliest childhood , and in its trunk Bomba had stored many of his treasures", "context_len": 21}}}, "catapulted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "The mad beast catapulted from the shoulders of Ashati and fell at the feet of Bomba", "context_len": 16}}}, "conjectural": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "How much Casson would be able to accomplish was of course conjectural , but there was a chance that even his feeble help might turn the scale of battle", "context_len": 29}}}, "twanged": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 13, "context": "The thud of the collision had come just as Bomba \u2019 s bow twanged", "context_len": 14}}}, "wizards": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "The spurts of flame and the roar of the weapon confirmed their conviction that the hut was the habitation of wizards", "context_len": 21}}}, "portholes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "He sprang to one of the portholes and sent out a loud , long , undulating cry that rang weirdly through the jungle", "context_len": 23}}}, "weirdly": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "Softly , weirdly , tenderly , there floated to the lad , as though from a great distance , the strain of that sweet melody , the song that Bartow \u2019 s wife had sung to Bonny", "context_len": 37}}}, "sensed": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 33, "context": "How he wished that Frank Parkhurst was with him to share the feast ! Before he had met with the white people he had been lonely , but he had not so keenly sensed his loneliness", "context_len": 36}}}, "demoralization": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "And their demoralization was complete when Polulu , with a tremendous roar , came bounding in upon them , his eyes glaring , his tail lashing , his paws striking out like flails", "context_len": 33}}}, "flails": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "And their demoralization was complete when Polulu , with a tremendous roar , came bounding in upon them , his eyes glaring , his tail lashing , his paws striking out like flails", "context_len": 33}}}, "deluged": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "It deluged the burning front wall until the blaze died out and great clouds of steam took its place", "context_len": 19}}}, "harmonica": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 9, "context": "One day , when he had been playing his harmonica and Doto , the monkey , and Kiki and Woowoo , the parrots , and others of their mates were gathered around him , there was a crashing in the bushes , and faithful Polulu , the giant puma , bounded into the clearing", "context_len": 54}}}, "adventuresome": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "This time the group starts out on a cruise simply for pleasure , but their adventuresome spirits lead them into the thick of things on a South Sea cannibal island", "context_len": 30}}}, "lightship": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Modern Pirates , with the ferocity of beasts , attack a lightship crew ; recounting the adventures that befall the survivors of that crew and RETRIBUTION", "context_len": 26}}}, "weirdest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Telling of a mutiny , and how two youngsters were unwillingly involved in one of the weirdest of treasure hunts , and THE GOLDEN FETISH", "context_len": 25}}}, "captures": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "Bob Dexter , while on a vacation , captures a band of criminals , and solves a mystery in which millions of dollars in gems and jewelry had been stolen", "context_len": 30}}}, "implications": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "A new group of stories for boys by a new author whose excellent air adventures are so realistically written and so up to the minute in all their implications as to win ready admiration from all readers", "context_len": 37}}}, "flyers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "The story of the rescue , after the flyers had been given up for lost , was a whale of a story", "context_len": 22}}}, "pg": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 0, "context": "pg 23 Changed : his memory of the way a similiar to : his memory of the way a similar", "context_len": 20}}}, "enfold": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "At any moment a boa constrictor might drop like a flash from a tree above and seek to enfold him in its crushing coils", "context_len": 24}}}, "headhunters": {"freq": 64, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 64, "word_index": 56, "context": "How Bomba saved the camp of Gillis and Dorn , rubber hunters , from a night attack by jaguars -- how he trapped the deadly cooanaradi , the most dreaded serpent of the South American wilds , when it pursued him ; his adventures with alligators and anacondas ; the besieging of his cabin by the headhunters ; how his friends of the forest came to his aid when he was fearfully beset ; all this is narrated in the first volumes of this series , entitled : Bomba , the Jungle Boy ; or , The Old Naturalist \u2019 s Secret", "context_len": 102}}}, "plumbing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "So it was with extreme caution that Bomba threaded his way through the jungle , his eyes darting from right to left , plumbing the recesses of every thicket , piercing the foliage of every tree", "context_len": 36}}}, "sharpness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "In his belt he carried a machete , a two-edged knife fully a foot in length and ground to an almost razor-like sharpness", "context_len": 23}}}, "jaguars": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 18, "context": "How Bomba saved the camp of Gillis and Dorn , rubber hunters , from a night attack by jaguars -- how he trapped the deadly cooanaradi , the most dreaded serpent of the South American wilds , when it pursued him ; his adventures with alligators and anacondas ; the besieging of his cabin by the headhunters ; how his friends of the forest came to his aid when he was fearfully beset ; all this is narrated in the first volumes of this series , entitled : Bomba , the Jungle Boy ; or , The Old Naturalist \u2019 s Secret", "context_len": 102}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Bomba \u2019 s own supplies were running low now , and he saw in the dead jaguars a chance to replenish them", "context_len": 22}}}, "interpreting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "There was another outbreak of sounds that would have been meaningless to anyone else , but which Bomba had no trouble in interpreting correctly", "context_len": 24}}}, "ferment": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "In spite of his eagerness to reach Casson , about whose condition he was in a ferment of anxiety , he must make a long circuit to avoid the region in which Doto had indicated that danger lay", "context_len": 38}}}, "monosyllables": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Shred by shred , in monosyllables and muttered exclamations , Bomba gathered , as he walked beside Sobrinini \u2019 s litter , the reason for her flight from the island", "context_len": 30}}}, "rudiments": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "He had started to give the boy the rudiments of an education", "context_len": 12}}}, "scaly": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "Beneath the scaly skin of the jaracara there was tremendous muscular power , and this was made more available when the reptile secured a purchase by wrapping its long body about the boy \u2019 s leg", "context_len": 36}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "But even as the thought came into his mind he caught sight of the scaly body and horrid head of an alligator between him and the shore", "context_len": 27}}}, "slavering": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "Gibbering and mouthing ferociously , froth slavering from its jaws , the huge ape sprang toward Bomba and the cowering slaves", "context_len": 21}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "So Bomba called to this slavering , hideous object in the language of the monkeys , trying to show that he was not an enemy", "context_len": 25}}}, "glaze": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "A glaze began to steal over the horrid eyes", "context_len": 9}}}, "eventuality": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "It was too much to hope , however , that sooner or later their attention would not be drawn toward the tree , and Bomba held himself prepared for that eventuality", "context_len": 31}}}, "shinned": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Turning to the nearest tree , Bomba shinned up it with the agility of a monkey and sought refuge in the thick foliage that hid him from sight , while yet permitting him to see what might be going on below", "context_len": 41}}}, "hacking": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "When he could , which was often , he jumped over the thickets instead of hacking his way through them , leaping into the air as lightly as a deer and landing softly on the other side", "context_len": 37}}}, "snags": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "But accidents , he had come to learn , were almost daily occurrences in the jungle , and he did not abate a jot of his vigilance , his keen eyes keeping on the lookout everywhere -- at the water for snags or alligators , at either shore for animal or human enemies , on the trees that overhung the stream for lurking anacondas", "context_len": 64}}}, "subconscious": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "But though always on the alert , his subconscious mind was busy with thoughts of his recent journey and of that which was to come", "context_len": 25}}}, "fruitlessly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Did Casson know her ? Would the mention of her name unlock the door of his memory , that door that he had tried so desperately but fruitlessly to open ?", "context_len": 31}}}, "assorted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "By the time Polulu , as Bomba named the puma , had fully recovered , a strong attachment had grown up between the oddly assorted pair", "context_len": 26}}}, "cudgeling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Father and mother , murmured Casson , and lapsed into silence , during which he seemed to be cudgeling his poor , disordered brain to make it yield up its secrets", "context_len": 31}}}, "preferably": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "He was after larger game , preferably a tapir , a creature as large as a calf , whose meat was succulent and nourishing", "context_len": 24}}}, "competing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "A little later Doto joined him together with other monkeys , so that he was soon the center of a group of birds and animals , all competing for a word or a pat of the hand", "context_len": 37}}}, "cayman": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 5, "context": "The deadly alligator , or cayman , infested all the waters of the jungle , and any daring person that ventured to cross knew that he might pay for the venture with his life", "context_len": 34}}}, "snouts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Then , as Bomba looked , the water was broken in several places by the horrid snouts and hideous jaws of other caymen , that had smelled the blood of the tapir and were hastening to have their share of the feast", "context_len": 42}}}, "gnashed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "He was safe for the present and could smile grimly as they gnashed their tusks , those terrible tusks that were like so many knives and which could so easily slash him to bits", "context_len": 34}}}, "discard": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "His immersion had been so sudden that he had not had time to discard them", "context_len": 15}}}, "behooved": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Soon the four-footed hunters of the jungle would themselves be abroad , and it behooved Bomba to make himself safe for the night", "context_len": 23}}}, "tinctured": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "As he lay there , waiting for sleep to come , his thoughts were tinctured somewhat with bitterness", "context_len": 18}}}, "intimates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Monkeys and parrots were his only intimates , except poor demented Casson and the squaw , Pipina", "context_len": 17}}}, "azure": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Was it thunder ? The sky was azure and the sun was shining with dazzling brilliance", "context_len": 16}}}, "communed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "So he communed with himself , though not with much conviction", "context_len": 11}}}, "halloo": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "He gave the loud halloo with which he was accustomed to announce his coming", "context_len": 14}}}, "derange": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "To keep on would be at least to delay attack and perhaps derange the plans of his pursuer", "context_len": 18}}}, "subterfuge": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "It was plain that the Indians considered this a subterfuge or a trap , and there was considerable parleying before Lodo finally announced that they would take him to the hut", "context_len": 31}}}, "parleying": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "It was plain that the Indians considered this a subterfuge or a trap , and there was considerable parleying before Lodo finally announced that they would take him to the hut", "context_len": 31}}}, "patterned": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The other bucks patterned their conduct on his and crowded around Bomba , muttering and growling like beasts of the jungle about to close in on a helpless prey", "context_len": 29}}}, "balefully": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "But they did not think of Grico ! said he of the split nose , his one eye gleaming balefully", "context_len": 20}}}, "powwows": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "But Bomba knew that this would take time and be the subject of innumerable powwows before the avengers would get fairly started", "context_len": 22}}}, "avengers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "But Bomba knew that this would take time and be the subject of innumerable powwows before the avengers would get fairly started", "context_len": 22}}}, "powwow": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "There was a great feast to celebrate the reunion , followed by a long powwow between Hondura , Grico and Lodo", "context_len": 21}}}, "onrush": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Then he left them to return with heavy and angry hearts to their despoiled maloca while he again sought out the path he had been pursuing when the onrush of Hondura \u2019 s enraged braves had put such an abrupt stop to his journey", "context_len": 44}}}, "despoiling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "For in his own mind Bomba did not doubt that the same hand that had directed the capture of Hondura had also ordered the despoiling and burning of the chief village of the Araos", "context_len": 34}}}, "onetime": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Bomba could but remember that on his onetime visit to the Araos tribe , when his life had been in danger from the suspicion of Hondura and his braves , it had been little Pirah who had stood between him and a horrible fate , and by her innocent friendship had changed enmity to confidence and trust", "context_len": 57}}}, "irrecoverably": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "But his treasures were gone , in all likelihood irrecoverably gone", "context_len": 11}}}, "peccary": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 26, "context": "Quick as thought , Bomba leaped for the limb of the tree nearest him , swinging his body clear of the ground just as the first peccary reached the spot where he had been", "context_len": 34}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The peccary nearest him lifted up its wicked , blunt-nosed head and sniffed the air", "context_len": 15}}}, "peccaries": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 19, "context": "As a rule , he gave the animals a wide berth , for nothing is more ferocious than the peccaries , whose murderous tusks , if they get to work , can tear a man into ribbons", "context_len": 37}}}, "tusks": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "As a rule , he gave the animals a wide berth , for nothing is more ferocious than the peccaries , whose murderous tusks , if they get to work , can tear a man into ribbons", "context_len": 37}}}, "obsessed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 54, "context": "Bomba wondered whether he would ever see that strange and wonderful city or others like it , wondered if he would always have to spend his life in the jungle , with none save Casson and a few natives for his friends , to none of whom he could speak of the longings that obsessed him , of the aspirations that seemed doomed forever to be thwarted", "context_len": 67}}}, "girded": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "He girded himself and resumed his journey , his heart heavy , but his body refreshed and strengthened by the hearty meal he had eaten", "context_len": 25}}}, "jangled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The wind tore through the jungle , shattering the ominous silence that had prevailed into jangled discords of sound", "context_len": 19}}}, "discords": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "The wind tore through the jungle , shattering the ominous silence that had prevailed into jangled discords of sound", "context_len": 19}}}, "tidal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Then , suddenly , as the tidal wave tops all other waves , came a gigantic burst of wind that bore great trees before it as though they had been toys , bending them , breaking them , uprooting them , whirling them about as in a fantastic dance", "context_len": 49}}}, "disembodied": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "He seemed to himself like a disembodied ghost", "context_len": 8}}}, "discouragement": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "This effort ended in a feeling of almost complete discouragement", "context_len": 10}}}, "torrential": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "The rain was still torrential , and the pool that had been for weeks only a muddy depression in the jungle floor was now filling with water", "context_len": 27}}}, "anguished": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "But in the lowering heavens to which Bomba lifted his anguished eyes there was no hope", "context_len": 16}}}, "confining": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The water crept higher , while Bomba slashed furiously at the confining branches", "context_len": 13}}}, "incubus": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "That dreadful incubus still held him securely", "context_len": 7}}}, "ridged": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The veins stood out on his neck , the great muscles in his shoulders were ridged like whipcords as he strained to throw the last of the imprisoning branches from his chest", "context_len": 32}}}, "cyclonic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "A great tree , torn loose by that last cyclonic burst of wind , had fallen , sweeping him along with its branches and imprisoning him in the pool", "context_len": 29}}}, "malign": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "If he had been superstitious , he might have thought that a malign fate had been following him ever since he set out on his journey", "context_len": 26}}}, "submergence": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "There was the loss of his revolver and harmonica , the enforced return to the hut when Hondura \u2019 s braves had come upon him , the further loss of his bow and arrows , his submergence in the pool when the tree had trapped him", "context_len": 46}}}, "strategic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "So he went higher , and higher , his eyes searching for a strategic position", "context_len": 15}}}, "sag": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Out on this Bomba crept , feeling it sag beneath his weight and not knowing but at any moment it might break and send him to the ground", "context_len": 28}}}, "switching": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "One alone lingered to cut some limbs from a hemlock bush , a whole armful of which he brought with him , and while the rest were passing the water from the beach , and pouring on the blazing wood-pile , he was switching out the flames , as they ran towards the beach , with a dexterity that showed he was no novice in fire-fighting", "context_len": 66}}}, "tensed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "Bomba \u2019 s fingers tensed about the handle of the machete and he shouted , hoping to disconcert the animal and perhaps make it lose its balance", "context_len": 27}}}, "disconcert": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Bomba \u2019 s fingers tensed about the handle of the machete and he shouted , hoping to disconcert the animal and perhaps make it lose its balance", "context_len": 27}}}, "kaleidoscope": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "The situation had changed with the quickness of a kaleidoscope", "context_len": 10}}}, "killers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The would-be killers had themselves been killed just at the moment that their victory had seemed assured", "context_len": 17}}}, "tropic": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "The tropic night had fallen now , but a full moon had risen , and by the light of it Bomba could be seen as he got to his feet and gained the bank", "context_len": 34}}}, "attribute": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "A native might enter at any minute and might attribute them to weakness , to fear at the situation in which he found himself", "context_len": 24}}}, "twanging": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "The bow of the jungle boy sang with a twanging sound as the arrow sped from it", "context_len": 17}}}, "jumble": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "But as time passed and they remained unmolested in their retreat , while the awful howling of the monkeys died down to a mere jumble of sound , they began to take courage and hope that all would still be well with them", "context_len": 43}}}, "outlaw": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Some native , perhaps , had become an outlaw from his tribe and had chosen the cave as his home", "context_len": 20}}}, "cabalistic": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "The natives scrambled to their knees and bowed their heads to the ground , making cabalistic signs and uttering entreaties either to the mountain or their gods", "context_len": 27}}}, "hollowing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "With the aid of Neram and Ashati , he cut down one of the smaller trees near the river bank and began the work of hollowing out a portion of the trunk in the form of a small canoe", "context_len": 39}}}, "slenderness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "A paddle was then fashioned from a sapling , that for all its slenderness was strong enough to breast the current of even that raging river", "context_len": 26}}}, "capsizing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "There was room for the three of them , and there would be less danger of capsizing if the canoe were well weighted down", "context_len": 24}}}, "righting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "On he went down the river , the canoe caught now by one current , now by another , sometimes dipping to one side at so sharp an angle that it seemed it must capsize , then righting itself and dancing on again over the frothing black water -- a frail barrier between Bomba and destruction", "context_len": 56}}}, "upstream": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "He found the island that was shaped like a finger , and worked his craft about the further end of it , heading upstream as the native had directed", "context_len": 29}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Scarcely daring to put these questions to himself , Bomba swam madly upstream , his one thought now of revenge", "context_len": 20}}}, "piranhas": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Dread of the piranhas , a dangerous , sharp-toothed fish , such as infest all the waters of the jungle , hurried Bomba \u2019 s steps so that he was not as cautious in his approach to Sobrinini as he would otherwise have been", "context_len": 44}}}, "infest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Dread of the piranhas , a dangerous , sharp-toothed fish , such as infest all the waters of the jungle , hurried Bomba \u2019 s steps so that he was not as cautious in his approach to Sobrinini as he would otherwise have been", "context_len": 44}}}, "lilting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "It was a lilting , rhythmic tune , and at the first notes of it the hideous reptiles surrounding Bomba began to sway to the sound and one after another slipped over the edge of the pit and slithered away into the darkness", "context_len": 43}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The lilting songs , even when sung by that cracked and ruined voice , struck a responsive chord in the boy and filled him with emotions that he could neither analyze nor understand", "context_len": 33}}}, "narrowness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Still shaken at the narrowness of his escape from a terrible death , the lad drew himself up beside Sobrinini", "context_len": 20}}}, "shriveled": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "She danced about Bomba in a hideous way , shaking her shriveled fists in the air and mouthing horribly", "context_len": 19}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "The prisoners passed through the streets of the village and their hearts sank , for they saw the human heads , shriveled and blackened by the sun , that were fastened on poles on the tops of the wigwams", "context_len": 39}}}, "unflinching": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Bomba stood unflinching", "context_len": 3}}}, "portent": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "Seeing the terror into which their priestess had been thrown , a strange wild singing rose from the native women as they wove in and out in fantastic mazes , evidently designed to ward off the evil portent", "context_len": 38}}}, "croaking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "Twice Bomba started to ask her what she meant by calling him Bartow and a ghost , but twice he was halted by a bony , shriveled finger on his lips and a croaking cry :", "context_len": 36}}}, "opera": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "At either end of this was a small compartment with rounded front , meant to represent an opera box , though this of course Bomba could not know", "context_len": 28}}}, "simulation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "As Bomba gazed about him , surprised and bewildered , Sobrinini left his side , and with a horrid simulation of youth skipped to the platform", "context_len": 26}}}, "simpering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Come ! she cried , as he hesitated , her simpering giving place to a terrible frown", "context_len": 17}}}, "simpered": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "But she simpered and smirked again when he approached , and , skipping lightly from the platform , forced him with a playfulness that Bomba found scarcely less terrifying than her wrath into one of the crazy chairs close to the stage", "context_len": 42}}}, "smirked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "But she simpered and smirked again when he approached , and , skipping lightly from the platform , forced him with a playfulness that Bomba found scarcely less terrifying than her wrath into one of the crazy chairs close to the stage", "context_len": 42}}}, "analyze": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "The lilting songs , even when sung by that cracked and ruined voice , struck a responsive chord in the boy and filled him with emotions that he could neither analyze nor understand", "context_len": 33}}}, "raucous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Suddenly Sobrinini paused , and fixing Bomba with a strange intent gaze , sang in a voice that had magically lost most of its raucous quality and for the moment had become the faint , sad echo of something that had been supremely beautiful -- sang a tender , haunting melody that touched some almost forgotten memory in Bomba \u2019 s heart and filled him with an exquisite pain", "context_len": 69}}}, "coquettishly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Ha , Bartow , like you Sobrinini in the dance that charmed all Paris ? she chirped , patting Bomba \u2019 s shoulder coquettishly", "context_len": 24}}}, "glinting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "But Sobrinini drew back from him , quick suspicion glinting in her eyes", "context_len": 13}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "He looked exceedingly dangerous , standing in all his splendid strength with the sunlight glinting on the red point of his upraised machete", "context_len": 23}}}, "intoning": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The natives were dancing , flinging their arms about wildly and intoning their weird chant , now harsh and loud , now soft like the sighing of the wind through the trees", "context_len": 32}}}, "glisten": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "But as the natives danced on , as tireless apparently as the ghosts that Bomba half-thought them , the mist began to clear and the sun struck through , causing the moisture upon the dark bodies of the dancers to glisten", "context_len": 41}}}, "uncoiling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "She halted in her dance , and the snakes , uncoiling themselves from about her arms and neck , as though they knew that their part in the frenzied performance was over , slithered off quietly into the long marsh grass and sought their lairs", "context_len": 45}}}, "lairs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "She halted in her dance , and the snakes , uncoiling themselves from about her arms and neck , as though they knew that their part in the frenzied performance was over , slithered off quietly into the long marsh grass and sought their lairs", "context_len": 45}}}, "untwined": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Come ! Come , dance with Sobrinini , the woman said in a wheedling voice , as she untwined her shriveled arms to grasp him by the hand", "context_len": 28}}}, "bleeds": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "See -- if you prick my flesh , it bleeds", "context_len": 10}}}, "placatingly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "I would have no quarrel with Sobrinini , he said placatingly", "context_len": 11}}}, "equaled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "And he doubted very much whether he equaled him in skill and agility", "context_len": 13}}}, "duel": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "And in the duel of eyes , Nascanora \u2019 s were the first to fall", "context_len": 15}}}, "agitatedly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Yes , yes , she whispered agitatedly", "context_len": 7}}}, "invocations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Bomba would have urged her to tell him then , but he was roughly hurried away , leaving Sobrinini to wring her withered hands and mutter invocations to her gods", "context_len": 30}}}, "slake": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Doubtless at this moment the chief was thinking of some fiendish mode of torture with which to slake his thirst for revenge", "context_len": 22}}}, "pitfalls": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "The savages were familiar with the rapids , and , evading the pitfalls of the river , rapidly neared the other shore", "context_len": 22}}}, "dissipate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The news helped to dissipate the gloom on the countenances of the slaves", "context_len": 13}}}, "thunderous": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "Fortunately , they had had a long rest while the war party under Nascanora had been making the raid on Snake Island , so they were in fairly good condition for the journey , and that the journey would not be a long one was now evident from the thunderous sound that grew ever louder and louder in their ears", "context_len": 60}}}, "besotted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "To the besotted minds of their captors the cataract was nothing , except that it signified that they were once more at home and now could revel in the torture of their victims", "context_len": 33}}}, "corralled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Into this the prisoners were corralled and left under the supervision of guards , while the others of the band dispersed to their homes", "context_len": 24}}}, "insincere": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "But how insincere those professions had been on the part of Ruspak and how deeply he had resented the affront to his dignity as a medicine man was evident now by the malignity in his eyes and the gloating smile on his lips", "context_len": 43}}}, "elicit": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "But all his recital of the horrid tortures that were preparing for Bomba failed to elicit a single word from the contemptuous captive , and Ruspak at last left him and went away , mumbling to himself and licking his lips in anticipation", "context_len": 43}}}, "claps": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Claps of thunder deafened their ears and vivid lightning flashes shot across the sky", "context_len": 14}}}, "rejecting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "All that time Bomba \u2019 s brain was at work thinking out plans of escape , rejecting one , seizing on another , and weighing the chances of all", "context_len": 29}}}, "passageways": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "They had joined the rabble that were now rushing from their huts and through the passageways between , only intent for the moment on getting away from the waters that threatened to overwhelm them", "context_len": 34}}}, "subside": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "It would be some time before the waters would subside so as to make pursuit possible", "context_len": 16}}}, "irresolution": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The pursuing party , after a little irresolution , gave up the chase and turned the prow of their boat in the direction of Snake Island", "context_len": 26}}}, "reconnoiter": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "He was about to drop on his hands and knees and creep through the brush to reconnoiter , when he heard a sound that made his heart leap with delight", "context_len": 30}}}, "protuberance": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Living on grass , and in the woods , with the milk from the house , he had not that protuberance of belly which swine reared in sties possess , and really merited Charlie \u2019 s encomium of being handsome ; he jumped up on his master and rubbed against his legs , with low grunts , expressive of satisfaction", "context_len": 60}}}, "litters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The grateful Hondura detailed eight of his men to make two litters on which they bore Casson and Sobrinini , who had reached the limit of their strength , to the cabin of Pipina", "context_len": 34}}}, "floodgates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "He hoped the meeting would unlock the floodgates of memories which they shared in common", "context_len": 15}}}, "fulfilling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "How Bomba followed it up and what exciting perils and adventures he met in fulfilling his task will be told in the next volume of this series , entitled : Bomba the Jungle Boy on Jaguar Island ; or , Adrift on the River of Mystery", "context_len": 46}}}, "cruises": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "Billy Smith , son of an eminent explorer , learns to pilot a seaplane aboard a Naval Air Station , and immediately fares forth upon a series of the most exciting cruises and mysterious quests by air , by land and by sea that have ever fallen to the lot of man", "context_len": 52}}}, "quests": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 34, "context": "Billy Smith , son of an eminent explorer , learns to pilot a seaplane aboard a Naval Air Station , and immediately fares forth upon a series of the most exciting cruises and mysterious quests by air , by land and by sea that have ever fallen to the lot of man", "context_len": 52}}}, "gangsters": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "Billy Smith and his pal , Nuky , with the aid of Billy \u2019 s father set a trap to catch desperate gangsters which they succeed in doing after many thrilling adventures", "context_len": 32}}}, "shanghaied": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "Billy shanghaied while on a search for a missing steamer and one passenger in particular , escapes in time to be of vast help , after all , and bring off a famous rescue in the South Sea Islands", "context_len": 39}}}, "brill": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "BRILL", "context_len": 1}}}, "cache": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "lath": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "LATH", "context_len": 1}}}, "geologist": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Four chums find a secret code stuck inside the binding of an old book written many years ago by a famous geologist", "context_len": 22}}}, "crackerjack": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Here we present three crackerjack stories of baseball , football , and basketball , written in the vernacular of the boy of to-day , full of action , suspense and thrills , in language every boy will understand , and which we know will be enthusiastically endorsed by all boys", "context_len": 50}}}, "endorsed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "Here we present three crackerjack stories of baseball , football , and basketball , written in the vernacular of the boy of to-day , full of action , suspense and thrills , in language every boy will understand , and which we know will be enthusiastically endorsed by all boys", "context_len": 50}}}, "rawboned": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Ralph Stanton , big , rawboned and serious , is a product of the backwoods and a crack rifle shot", "context_len": 20}}}, "dub": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Quick thinking and pluck bring him a scholarship to Clarkville School where he is branded grind and dub by classmates", "context_len": 20}}}, "secures": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "How his batting brings them first place in the League and how he secures his appointment to West Point make CRACKER STANTON an up-to-the-minute baseball story no lover of the game will want to put down until the last word is read", "context_len": 42}}}, "competitive": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "A corking story of football packed full of exciting action and good , clean competitive rivalry", "context_len": 16}}}, "climaxes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "The team \u2019 s subsequent adventures under the leadership of Captain Charlie Minor as he brings them back to the State College Gymnasium where the two last quarters of the Championship game are played next evening , climaxes twenty-four pulsating hours of adventure and basketball in the FIGHTING FIVE", "context_len": 49}}}, "pulsating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "The team \u2019 s subsequent adventures under the leadership of Captain Charlie Minor as he brings them back to the State College Gymnasium where the two last quarters of the Championship game are played next evening , climaxes twenty-four pulsating hours of adventure and basketball in the FIGHTING FIVE", "context_len": 49}}}, "dholes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "A huge clouded tiger , almost human , leads a pack of red dholes into Sorak \u2019 s country , and it takes all of Sorak \u2019 s ingenuity , and the aid of his friends to exterminate the pack", "context_len": 40}}}, "exterminate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "A huge clouded tiger , almost human , leads a pack of red dholes into Sorak \u2019 s country , and it takes all of Sorak \u2019 s ingenuity , and the aid of his friends to exterminate the pack", "context_len": 40}}}, "enthralled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Their exciting adventures will hold the reader enthralled until the last word", "context_len": 12}}}, "bootblack": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "A young newspaper man , whose brother is on the police force , becomes strangely involved in the mysterious killing of an Italian bootblack", "context_len": 24}}}, "duelist": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "He follows this up , watches a masked duelist and , with the help of a girl , catches the murderer who justifies his deed on the plea of Slow Vengeance", "context_len": 31}}}, "caddy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "A golf caddy who has a leaning toward amateur detective work , together with his younger cousin , are accidentally mixed up in the strange loss , or theft , of a valuable diamond , known as Green Fire", "context_len": 39}}}, "deepens": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "To clear his young cousin of suspicion , the older boy undertakes to solve the mystery which deepens when one man disappears and another is found murdered on the golf course", "context_len": 31}}}, "unmapped": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Donald Pierce is sent to solve the mystery of his father \u2019 s disappearance , into the unmapped barrens where King Stively weaves his web of wickedness , and rules a territory the size of a small empire with a ruthlessness and cunning that baffles the best of the Mounted Police", "context_len": 51}}}, "barrens": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Donald Pierce is sent to solve the mystery of his father \u2019 s disappearance , into the unmapped barrens where King Stively weaves his web of wickedness , and rules a territory the size of a small empire with a ruthlessness and cunning that baffles the best of the Mounted Police", "context_len": 51}}}, "ruthlessness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "Donald Pierce is sent to solve the mystery of his father \u2019 s disappearance , into the unmapped barrens where King Stively weaves his web of wickedness , and rules a territory the size of a small empire with a ruthlessness and cunning that baffles the best of the Mounted Police", "context_len": 51}}}, "frustrates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "With the aid of his friend , Jim MacPhail , Wade frustrates the outlaws , who try to trap the whole town behind the Pass of the Closing Door , and then races them to and across the breaking ice floes of the Yukon", "context_len": 44}}}, "floes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "With the aid of his friend , Jim MacPhail , Wade frustrates the outlaws , who try to trap the whole town behind the Pass of the Closing Door , and then races them to and across the breaking ice floes of the Yukon", "context_len": 44}}}, "standardized": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Inconsistencies in hyphenation have been standardized", "context_len": 6}}}, "deadlier": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "impeding": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Heavy ropes of creepers swung from the branches above the boy \u2019 s head and wrapped themselves about him , impeding his progress", "context_len": 23}}}, "evilly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Not more than a dozen yards away three Indians were creeping toward him , spear points lowered , glinting evilly !", "context_len": 21}}}, "litheness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "With the litheness of a deer he rose in the air , and with a series of successive bounds rapidly increased the distance between him and the enemy", "context_len": 28}}}, "hurdled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "He hurdled the bushes in great leaps while his heavier foes were forcing their way through them", "context_len": 17}}}, "machetes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "With their spears and machetes they viciously beat the brushwood to right and left , thrusting their weapons into every thicket , their anger growing as their efforts remained fruitless", "context_len": 30}}}, "eardrums": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "Over and over came those deafening roars that seemed to split his eardrums , and ever the rivers of lava swelled in volume", "context_len": 23}}}, "circumscribed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "All his life was circumscribed by the jungle", "context_len": 8}}}, "faun": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "So Bomba roamed the jungle like a young faun at the beginning of the world", "context_len": 15}}}, "undeniable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "But there was undeniable proof in his features , in his aquiline nose , his firm jaw , his brown hair and eyes , that he was of white blood", "context_len": 30}}}, "dourly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Above , the thunder rumbled dourly", "context_len": 6}}}, "wile": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "He was fighting for two lives , his own and Casson \u2019 s , and , moreover , one of his fierce rages was upon him ; one of those wild bursts of fury that for the moment gave him the strength of the jaguar , the wile of the fox , the quickness of the snake", "context_len": 57}}}, "forebode": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "His eyes and ears were alert for the slightest sight or sound that might forebode danger", "context_len": 16}}}, "yearnings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "All his yearnings were toward those of his own race", "context_len": 10}}}, "operatic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "He reviewed the strange behavior of the half-mad old woman , Sobrinini , she who had once been the operatic idol of Europe , she who had had kings at her feet", "context_len": 32}}}, "shears": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "Then the huge mouth opened , displaying the horrible array of teeth that , if they once closed on the lad , would bite him in half as easily as a pair of shears would snip a thread", "context_len": 38}}}, "serrated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "In its wild flounderings the end of its serrated tail caught Bomba on one of his legs and threw him farther out into the stream", "context_len": 25}}}, "scathe": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Again he repeated his stratagem , but this time not without scathe , for an arrow grazed his ankle", "context_len": 19}}}, "twang": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "There was a twang , a hideous yell , and the savage threw up his hands and fell headlong", "context_len": 19}}}, "luster": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "The moon , following close on the heels of the tropical storm , was now riding high in the heavens and shedding a soft luster over the jungle", "context_len": 28}}}, "inscrutably": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The squaw smiled inscrutably", "context_len": 4}}}, "singsong": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "The arrow is swift and the knife is sharp , murmured Neram in a singsong voice", "context_len": 16}}}, "caymans": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 26, "context": "Vain hope ! For in a little while he could see phosphorescent gleams in the water behind him that he knew marked the trails of the caymans", "context_len": 27}}}, "misstep": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The slightest misstep might mean disaster", "context_len": 6}}}, "teetered": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "For one horrible moment Bomba teetered over eternity", "context_len": 8}}}, "disarranging": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "But Pipina , in panic , was now squirming about in his arms and disarranging his calculations", "context_len": 17}}}, "bringer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Every thicket might harbor a bringer of death", "context_len": 8}}}, "tepee": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "The chief motioned them to seat themselves upon the cushions of rushes within his tepee , and presently food was brought to them which they devoured eagerly , for they had not eaten since noon of the day before", "context_len": 39}}}, "indulgently": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Hondura smiled indulgently upon the child and put a hand upon the dark hair", "context_len": 14}}}, "yearningly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "So he looked eagerly , yearningly , at the wrinkled face of the grizzled chieftain who spoke with such a calm air of assurance", "context_len": 24}}}, "fatalistic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "But he had the fatalistic philosophy born of his life in the jungle", "context_len": 13}}}, "revise": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "But there was something in the shape of it that made him revise his opinion , and he approached it with the caution that he always used when in the presence of something which he did not understand", "context_len": 38}}}, "grumblings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Flames licked at him hungrily , strange grumblings and roarings resounded about him , and yet he could make no move to escape", "context_len": 23}}}, "oozy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "It was imbedded in the thick oozy mud of a swamp", "context_len": 11}}}, "misgive": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Then did Bomba \u2019 s heart almost misgive him", "context_len": 9}}}, "gloatingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "And when , groaning , he let his head sink back , the mud sucked at it gloatingly", "context_len": 18}}}, "nub": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The jungle ! There lay the nub of his bitterness", "context_len": 10}}}, "markings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Not ten feet from him he saw a mass of coils that he recognized from the markings as that of the Brazilian rattlesnake , the jararaca", "context_len": 26}}}, "uncoiled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The snake uncoiled and crawled swiftly toward Bomba until it came within striking distance", "context_len": 14}}}, "conferences": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "He had often showed the knife to Doto in their conferences in the forest and boasted of its power", "context_len": 19}}}, "waveringly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Bomba tried a few steps and found that he could walk , though waveringly", "context_len": 14}}}, "easing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Where is it that Doto takes Bomba ? asked the lad , easing his bruised muscles as he paused to rest", "context_len": 21}}}, "glints": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Two yellow glints appeared before the mouth of the cave", "context_len": 10}}}, "soreness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "He was bent like an old man because of the soreness in his muscles , and one leg was lame where the cruel branches of the descending tree had bruised the tendons", "context_len": 32}}}, "gladiators": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "One of the gladiators of the night before !", "context_len": 9}}}, "flogging": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Always he hurried on , flogging himself to renewed effort , allowing only the most meager intervals for food and rest", "context_len": 21}}}, "unerringly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The arrow would have found its mark unerringly if the body of the jaguar had not been thrown out of the line of its flight", "context_len": 25}}}, "titanic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "It was a titanic struggle that was going on before his eyes", "context_len": 12}}}, "pinwheel": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "But the two beasts were whirling about like a giant pinwheel , first one on top and then the other , and Bomba did not dare use either arrow or machete for fear that he would wound or kill Polulu instead of the jaguar", "context_len": 44}}}, "avidly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "But though the beast drank avidly of the cool water , he would not touch the food", "context_len": 17}}}, "epicure": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Jaguar meat was not exactly the kind of food that would please an epicure , but it was eatable , and the opportunity could not be neglected", "context_len": 27}}}, "eatable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Jaguar meat was not exactly the kind of food that would please an epicure , but it was eatable , and the opportunity could not be neglected", "context_len": 27}}}, "doglike": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "He turned his head from Neram , and as his gaze fell upon Bomba such a look of rapturous delight and doglike devotion came into his eyes that the lad \u2019 s heart was touched", "context_len": 35}}}, "comings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Tell Bomba now , he commanded , where have been the comings and goings of Ashati and Neram since they left the cabin of the good squaw Pipina", "context_len": 28}}}, "skepticism": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "What things ? asked Bomba , with a tinge of skepticism in his tone", "context_len": 14}}}, "whimpers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Is Bomba a woman to listen to such things and tremble ? Shall he whimper as the monkey whimpers when he hears the roar of the jaguar ? No ! Bomba will go", "context_len": 33}}}, "recuperated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The sound sleep that he had enjoyed had recuperated him immensely", "context_len": 11}}}, "brilliance": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Was it thunder ? The sky was azure and the sun was shining with dazzling brilliance", "context_len": 16}}}, "hibernates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "The alligator , following a custom of its kind , had buried itself in the soft mud , on the same principle that a bear hibernates in winter", "context_len": 28}}}, "inundated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Probably a flood some time before had inundated the district and the alligator had come with it", "context_len": 17}}}, "withes": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "He also had a swing under the limbs , made of two long beech withes , that Joe Griffin had twisted for him ; and often , after supper , Sally would take her sewing , come up and swing with him ; and sometimes he would swing the pig , for he had made a basket that he could put into the swing", "context_len": 64}}}, "regale": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The monsters sank to the bottom to regale themselves", "context_len": 9}}}, "phosphorescence": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "But there were no more of those streaks of phosphorescence in evidence", "context_len": 12}}}, "unlashed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "He unlashed the raft from its mooring and with a hard push of his paddle sent it out from the shore , where it was promptly caught in the grip of the current", "context_len": 33}}}, "stud": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Now other lights , some faint , some bright , began to stud the surface of the stream", "context_len": 18}}}, "transcended": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "This transcended anything in his experience", "context_len": 6}}}, "puzzlement": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "That moment of puzzlement on the part of the reptile proved the boy \u2019 s salvation", "context_len": 16}}}, "clamping": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Clamping the jaguar in its great jaws , it went down under the surface", "context_len": 14}}}, "amity": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "But now , as steadily as the tremulous motion of the earth would permit , he stepped boldly out of his former hiding place with hands up and palms extended , as a token that he came in amity and goodwill", "context_len": 41}}}, "boles": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "The knots and boles of the trunks framed themselves into grimacing faces that seemed to be chuckling over the fate of the young invader who had come unbidden into their realm", "context_len": 31}}}, "grimacing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "The knots and boles of the trunks framed themselves into grimacing faces that seemed to be chuckling over the fate of the young invader who had come unbidden into their realm", "context_len": 31}}}, "tremors": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "In the vicinity of this , Bomba frequently felt slight tremors of the earth , one of them so pronounced that it nearly threw him from his feet", "context_len": 28}}}, "dweller": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "The jaguars that had given the island its name must abound in such numbers that no solitary dweller in the jungle would be safe", "context_len": 24}}}, "stealthiness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "With the speed and at the same time the stealthiness of a panther , Bomba glided to the biggest group of bowlders", "context_len": 22}}}, "bowlders": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "With the speed and at the same time the stealthiness of a panther , Bomba glided to the biggest group of bowlders", "context_len": 22}}}, "brutish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Their faces were savage , but not so brutish as those of the headhunters of Nascanora", "context_len": 16}}}, "unclothed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "They were unclothed , save for the customary breech clout", "context_len": 10}}}, "breech": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "They were unclothed , save for the customary breech clout", "context_len": 10}}}, "clout": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "They were unclothed , save for the customary breech clout", "context_len": 10}}}, "accentuate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "As though to accentuate this possibility , there came another shock more violent than the one before", "context_len": 17}}}, "ingratiating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Bomba is but a boy , he admitted , with an ingratiating smile", "context_len": 13}}}, "skeptically": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The stranger speaks big words , retorted Sunka skeptically", "context_len": 9}}}, "buzzard": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 24, "context": "Abino does not forget how the stranger slung the jaguar over his shoulder and how he shot his arrow through the neck of the buzzard on the top of the tall tree", "context_len": 32}}}, "preening": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "At a distance of two hundred feet was a high tree , on the topmost branch of which a buzzard was preening itself", "context_len": 23}}}, "counselor": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "Evidently it was forbidden ground except to the chosen few , such as Abino , who , as Bomba learned later , was Japazy \u2019 s chief counselor and who had charge of all matters pertaining to the tribe during his master \u2019 s absence", "context_len": 45}}}, "medallions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "There were statues of marble , tables cunningly carved and inlaid with gold and precious stones , curious medallions and intaglios , suits of armor and swords of the finest steel , a vast number of objects of art of all kinds scattered about in profusion", "context_len": 46}}}, "intaglios": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "There were statues of marble , tables cunningly carved and inlaid with gold and precious stones , curious medallions and intaglios , suits of armor and swords of the finest steel , a vast number of objects of art of all kinds scattered about in profusion", "context_len": 46}}}, "dependents": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Bomba could figure the half-breed sitting there , stern and commanding , clothed in gorgeous raiment the better to emphasize the distance between him and his dependents who bowed cringingly before him", "context_len": 32}}}, "lightnings": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "They will not make Japazy shoot lightnings from his eyes", "context_len": 10}}}, "repository": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Perhaps he was also the repository of Japazy \u2019 s secrets", "context_len": 11}}}, "moonrise": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "It was then within an hour of moonrise , and darkness was needed for the plan that he had in mind", "context_len": 21}}}, "lingeringly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Now he knew why Solani had looked at him so sadly and lingeringly , as one looks on the face of the dead", "context_len": 23}}}, "fathomed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Now he fathomed the meaning of that ominous phrase he had heard at the conference of the elders :", "context_len": 19}}}, "imperceptibly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Slowly , imperceptibly , Bomba \u2019 s right hand stole to his pouch", "context_len": 13}}}, "ostentatious": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Yes , replied Bomba , with an ostentatious yawn", "context_len": 9}}}, "belched": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "From the crater of the volcano a great mass of flame and smoke belched toward the sky", "context_len": 17}}}, "engulfing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "The earth was opening in great rents , which sometimes widened and at other times snapped shut , engulfing houses , trees and sometimes hapless human beings , whose screams of terror were dreadful to hear", "context_len": 36}}}, "spectacular": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Apart from these more spectacular demonstrations of the unchained forces of nature , there was a sense of gradual sinking that was sickening and ominous in the extreme", "context_len": 28}}}, "seared": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The hot blasts that came from them scorched his throat and lungs and seared his eyes", "context_len": 16}}}, "constrictors": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "There were hundreds of them , the jaguars which had given the island its name , boa constrictors , dragging their huge lengths through the brush , jararacas , cooanaradis", "context_len": 30}}}, "perplex": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "If problems vex , if cares perplex , O leave them all behind", "context_len": 13}}}, "straightens": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "I am a middle-aged pony and have seen enough of life to know that it does not pay to get stirred up about mysteries for invariably time straightens them all out", "context_len": 31}}}, "pasturing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "I bought this place from him and let it run into extra pasturing ground , but now the bears are bothering me and I wo n't keep any stock here till they get out", "context_len": 34}}}, "verandas": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 25, "context": "Cassowary was carrying food from the kitchen to the hen-house , Big Chief had gone to help clean the stables , Sojer was sweeping the verandas , Dovey was putting seeds and scraps on the wild bird tables about the lawn", "context_len": 41}}}, "silo": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "That was a silo to store green food for the cattle , but I could not tell him", "context_len": 18}}}, "democrat": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Then that awful drive in a democrat over rocks and rills and corduroy roads", "context_len": 14}}}, "ewes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` Is n't he a caution , '' he said , pointing to the old ram , who , after one terrified look at us two strangers , was leading the ewes in and out among the magnificent old trees scattered about the hillside", "context_len": 44}}}, "processing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "In amazement , I stopped my processing round the rock", "context_len": 10}}}, "catbird": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "Even a catbird sitting near by in a maple put his head on one side to listen and then turned to a young goldfinch who was about to fly in terror from him and motioned him to remain", "context_len": 38}}}, "goldfinch": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "Even a catbird sitting near by in a maple put his head on one side to listen and then turned to a young goldfinch who was about to fly in terror from him and motioned him to remain", "context_len": 38}}}, "loons": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` I ran my pony in a race , He leaped and bounded full of grace , The loons they called , they called to me , I answered them quite daft with glee", "context_len": 34}}}, "loonies": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "Echo ! loonies , '' and she began to shout ,", "context_len": 11}}}, "unstopped": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "How queer ! The average boy likes noise and he had better get his ears unstopped for the big man was coming up the hill", "context_len": 25}}}, "corduroys": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "He was a strapping middle-aged man in corduroys and cowboy hat with holes punched in it", "context_len": 16}}}, "nuzzled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "I nuzzled his coat buttons , then I followed his gaze , which was riveted on the boy", "context_len": 18}}}, "movies": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` Sometimes I think I 'd like to be the greatest home-run king in the world , and then when I see those high jumpers galloping over the roofs of houses in the movies I think I 'd like to be an actor", "context_len": 43}}}, "kidnapper": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` She does n't want that kidnapper to get me", "context_len": 10}}}, "rainfalls": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "My teacher used to tell me trees keep the soil moist and attract rainfalls", "context_len": 14}}}, "appal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Oh ! I do n't mind a few , sir , but these endless forests appal me", "context_len": 17}}}, "undresses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The maid undresses them and then the mother comes in and takes them in her arms and rocks and sings to them", "context_len": 22}}}, "bareback": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Both riders rode bareback", "context_len": 4}}}, "sprint": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Then did n't our riderless friend here sprint and win the race by a length", "context_len": 15}}}, "forbears": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "He is a wonderful combination of his gentle Scottish forbears and the fine Arabian stock", "context_len": 15}}}, "streamlets": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Little brownish streamlets ran across our track , and the boy was always jumping over or going on stepping stones to keep his feet from getting wet", "context_len": 27}}}, "abnormal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` Your life was abnormal", "context_len": 5}}}, "portages": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "There are three portages that will interest you -- come on , boy , I see a fresh track that I think is Lammie 's", "context_len": 25}}}, "ferny": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "There was not a sound from our footsteps for the trail was overgrown with a delicate ferny moss into which our feet sank noiselessly", "context_len": 24}}}, "curbstone": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Its roof was gone , its window eyes were broken , its doors were flapping and the well beside it had half the curbstone broken down", "context_len": 26}}}, "porcupine": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "My boy skipped past the dead porcupine and the broken chairs and dishes , and threw himself flat against a mildewed wall that he would have shrunk from had not his eyes been blinded by fear", "context_len": 36}}}, "quills": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "I looked over his shoulder and saw a porcupine shedding his quills for the last time", "context_len": 16}}}, "lopping": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "The bedrooms were deserted , the kitchen stove was lopping over , and the pots and pans were strewn about the floor", "context_len": 22}}}, "gamy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The cool north wind brought us a wild gamy smell", "context_len": 10}}}, "foal": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "Ponies and horses rarely cry out , they die without a sound , but we Shetlands are more like dogs than ponies and I am more dog-like than most Shetlands , for from the time I was a baby foal I have been like a brother to various human beings", "context_len": 50}}}, "cutest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "However , I have heard hunters say that the wolf is the cutest animal that roams the woods -- cuter even than the fox", "context_len": 24}}}, "roams": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "However , I have heard hunters say that the wolf is the cutest animal that roams the woods -- cuter even than the fox", "context_len": 24}}}, "legging": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "He was legging it for home , the most surprised animal in the Highlands of Ontario", "context_len": 16}}}, "mildewed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "My boy skipped past the dead porcupine and the broken chairs and dishes , and threw himself flat against a mildewed wall that he would have shrunk from had not his eyes been blinded by fear", "context_len": 36}}}, "gropingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "It was dreadful to see a boy suffer so much , and reaching out a hand he laid it gropingly on my head", "context_len": 23}}}, "crofters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 59, "context": "Certain tones in the man 's hearty voice reminded me of the boy 's sweet notes , and was n't there a queer suggestion of each other when I stood near them ? We Shetland ponies as I have said before are very close to human beings for our ancestors were literally brought up with the children in the crofters ' huts , and my mother has often told me that her mother had many a lick at the family platter and many a time her soft muzzle was buried in the children 's necks", "context_len": 95}}}, "tadpoles": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "About a quarter of a mile up this little stream was a place where some windfalls had partially dammed the water , forming a little pond , in which were myriads of frogs , tadpoles , polliwogs , and turtles of all sizes", "context_len": 43}}}, "infamous": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "warmness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "He is strangely fastidious about the warmness of it", "context_len": 9}}}, "habitude": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Those hens , '' said Bingi amiably , `` never forget their habitude of milk waiting and afterward drinking", "context_len": 20}}}, "seringa": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "Devering stood in the hall , and putting a hand on his nephew 's shoulder he came right across the room with him and out through the open French window , smiling at me as I bolted away under some seringa bushes", "context_len": 42}}}, "chipmunk": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` Well , if here is n't another beggar for crumbs , '' chattered an impudent little voice near me , and looking round I saw a chipmunk sitting on a sun dial and washing his face and paws for supper", "context_len": 41}}}, "juncos": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "I 'm always hungry , and there are about a dozen juncos , some hermit thrushes , a bluejay , a tame raccoon , a white rabbit , the cat and about a score of other creatures -- we 're the steadies but there are lots of chancers", "context_len": 48}}}, "bluejay": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "I 'm always hungry , and there are about a dozen juncos , some hermit thrushes , a bluejay , a tame raccoon , a white rabbit , the cat and about a score of other creatures -- we 're the steadies but there are lots of chancers", "context_len": 48}}}, "steadies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "I 'm always hungry , and there are about a dozen juncos , some hermit thrushes , a bluejay , a tame raccoon , a white rabbit , the cat and about a score of other creatures -- we 're the steadies but there are lots of chancers", "context_len": 48}}}, "chipmunks": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "I looked after him wondering what was the matter with his paws , and reflecting that although I do n't dislike chipmunks , I find them very disrespectful", "context_len": 28}}}, "junco": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Good gracious ! '' I said to the junco , `` what is that furry brown creature curled up and clinging to the skirt of her gown ? ''", "context_len": 30}}}, "showman": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "He had the showman fined heavily -- but , Pony , here comes the human mother-bird", "context_len": 16}}}, "matronly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Such a fine matronly young woman with a grave sweet face was coming out of the house", "context_len": 17}}}, "slides": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "When Grammie is not here , he follows her daughter about and slides along the floor holding on to her dress", "context_len": 21}}}, "cleanest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` He 's the cleanest animal on the place , '' said the junco", "context_len": 14}}}, "impudently": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Hold your tongue , '' said the jay impudently , and Biddy , turning away from him , said to me , `` You know ponies are not allowed down here at meal times", "context_len": 35}}}, "buries": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "She buries bones and meal , and these two come here every night for their after dinner feed", "context_len": 18}}}, "loping": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Not a bird peeped or stirred , except to glare at a big white rabbit who came loping easily down the hill from the wood , and went noiselessly under the veranda", "context_len": 32}}}, "flirting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Four plump fat birds had just settled themselves over us with much shaking of wings and flirting of tails , though they did not speak", "context_len": 25}}}, "specialty": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` That boy , '' said Biddy , who had asked permission to fly up to my back in order to get a better view of the table , `` that nice boy makes a specialty of robins , and often brings them up by hand when the parents are killed", "context_len": 51}}}, "cluck": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Hush ! do n't cackle nor cluck", "context_len": 7}}}, "shortcake": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "She motioned to Dallas to come beside her , and there was a plate of shortcake for him", "context_len": 18}}}, "clucked": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Biddy not liking my slippery back had shifted her position to a stick laid crosswise in the lilacs and she clucked in my ear , `` Those children love their pets", "context_len": 31}}}, "snuffled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Where 's my handkerchief ? I 've lost it , '' and he sniffed and snuffled and dried his face on my mane and with his coat sleeve", "context_len": 28}}}, "skulked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Knowing that I was supposed to be tied up , I kept at a discreet distance from him , and skulked behind shrubs until I reached my old hiding-place under the lilacs", "context_len": 32}}}, "regaled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "Biddy was gorging herself behind a tree trunk , the juncos and thrushes were eating seeds and cake crumbs scattered over the lawn , while the rabbit with a bland air surveyed the pleasant scenery and regaled himself on juicy lettuce leaves", "context_len": 42}}}, "miser": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` You little miser , '' she called after him", "context_len": 10}}}, "clotted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` It 's clotted milk on his wool", "context_len": 8}}}, "messed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "He sticks his head down in the pail to drink , and his wool gets messed up with the milk and then he lies down on the earth and it turns black", "context_len": 32}}}, "scuffed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Big Chief scuffed his way behind the circle of children and Jeanne , or Cassowary , as they called her , drew Merry-Eyes to the front", "context_len": 26}}}, "swap": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "Well , then , take mine ; I \u2019 ll go home in yours , and we will swap at the beach", "context_len": 22}}}, "popcorn": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` Pig-trot , '' and he twisted and turned , and the pigs followed him and really seemed to be enjoying themselves , for he kept throwing them popcorn , ends of cake , and candy , and as he pranced , he sang , `` Golden Dollars Rolling Down the Road , Roll , Golden Dollars , Roll", "context_len": 59}}}, "tagging": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Come on , race me to the barn , '' and down the road they all went , children and pigs , young Dallas and I tagging after", "context_len": 28}}}, "beloveds": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` Ah ! my beloveds , '' she exclaimed , `` I was forgetting them", "context_len": 15}}}, "piggies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Did their trotties feel sore after guarding piggies all day ? Come up to the house and Bingi will give us a foot-reviving supper for doggums", "context_len": 27}}}, "wean": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Do you suppose anyone could wean them away from their precious pigs ? They were brought up with them", "context_len": 20}}}, "piglets": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Talker says Foxy , after staring at the piglets , picked up a stick about the length of one of them and jumped through the hole with it in his mouth", "context_len": 31}}}, "piggy": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Finally he dropped the stick , went through the hole , seized a piggy and tried to go back through the hole with it", "context_len": 24}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "The moment Charlie heard the noise , he ran to the place , and began to call , Pig , pig ; and piggy replied by squealing with all his might", "context_len": 31}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "When Charlie returned , and saw Rover in such a condition , he came very near bursting into tears ; he knelt down by him , wiped the froth away from his mouth , and rubbed him , calling him good Rover ; but piggy gave no signs of life , except it could be perceived he breathed", "context_len": 58}}}, "loped": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Where are the other kids ? '' asked Dallas as he and Cassowary loped along after the two collies , who were in a great hurry", "context_len": 27}}}, "hydroplane": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Then the government has a lovely big hydroplane", "context_len": 8}}}, "filing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` Do the pigs sleep under the barn ? '' asked Dallas as he looked ahead and saw the Tamworth procession filing in under the big barn , which was painted green like the house", "context_len": 35}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Men are careless about filing the new ones and they worry a horse", "context_len": 13}}}, "troughs": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The big ones went to long troughs full of sour milk , the little fellows filed through a small door", "context_len": 20}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "In the first place they removed everything from the house to the beach ; then they hauled Charlie \u2019 s canoe up to the house , and filled it with water ; they also filled all the barrels , troughs , and tubs about the premises , and drove the cattle to the beach , lest the fire should run into the woods", "context_len": 63}}}, "chappies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Where are the young chappies going ? '' asked Dallas", "context_len": 11}}}, "cafeteria": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` You did n't attend to the cafeteria to-night ; Big Chief", "context_len": 12}}}, "stockmen": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "rooting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` To prevent their rooting", "context_len": 5}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Rover followed them round , rooting under the stumps for worms , and once in a while would shove his nose on a hot coal , which would make him run away squealing", "context_len": 33}}}, "crusher": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "Devering 's crusher had been at work , so I pricked up my hoofs and showed her what an Arabian-Shetland can do when on its mettle", "context_len": 26}}}, "roadbeds": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "The men either pile up the rocks or put them in a crusher to make good roadbeds", "context_len": 17}}}, "dallas": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Dallas shuddered", "context_len": 2}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Dallas by this time was sitting up and spitting out last mouthfuls of water , so his uncle saw he was in no danger", "context_len": 24}}}, "cassowary": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Cassowary went over the railing again to a corner where a shoat lay on a heap of clean straw", "context_len": 19}}}, "shoat": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Cassowary went over the railing again to a corner where a shoat lay on a heap of clean straw", "context_len": 19}}}, "lotion": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Because young Jaundice has bruised his shoulder and Guardie wants some lotion put on it", "context_len": 16}}}, "antiseptic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Your old tongue is as good as this antiseptic ; however , one must oblige a friend , if only a dog , '' and she washed the shoulder of the pig who took on great airs at having two dogs and a girl fussing over him while a pony and a boy looked on", "context_len": 56}}}, "mammas": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "They 're old enough to sleep alone and if they run to their mammas sometimes they get rolled on", "context_len": 19}}}, "undependable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "I 'm not so undependable , '' and she tossed her black head", "context_len": 13}}}, "bossing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "She was in a corner about this accusation of bossing , so she stopped talking , and ran a saucy tongue out at her brothers , then turning to Dallas she said , `` You do n't know what they 're talking about , Cousin", "context_len": 45}}}, "bickering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Nothing made me feel as much at home as this bickering between these young Canadians", "context_len": 15}}}, "superintending": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Talker was superintending the milking of the cows by patent milkers", "context_len": 11}}}, "milkers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "He took it out when we bought the milkers , but the cows fussed so that he had to put it back", "context_len": 22}}}, "kennel": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` Is he longing for the night to come ? Cassowary will let her boy loose , '' and taking a lead from the kennel , she fastened it to his collar", "context_len": 32}}}, "hikes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "He hikes to the woods the minute he 's free , and sometimes he goes 'way over the mountain", "context_len": 19}}}, "woodchucks": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "Thank you , Charlie ; and when we get learned we \u2019 ll come on here and give it to the squawks , and go on to Oak Island , and shoot squirrels and woodchucks , and save our powder and shot for sea-fowl", "context_len": 44}}}, "porcupines": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Then Dad goes on , 'Nor skunks , nor woodchucks , nor porcupines , nor beavers ' till he has all the animals of the woods", "context_len": 26}}}, "fastens": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "He only half wakes , fastens the snap to Drunkard 's collar , then goes to sleep again", "context_len": 18}}}, "tomboyish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "I found her a very amusing girl and very active and boyish with her short skirts and long legs , but not tomboyish", "context_len": 23}}}, "clucks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Faint clucks and hen whispers reached my ears , but when she turned on some bright lights several of the hens spoke to her quite amiably and distinctly , while a finely feathered Plymouth Rock rooster got off his perch and shaking his big wings came to put his beak in her hand", "context_len": 53}}}, "cocking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Then we must visit the Falls on the Merry-Tongue River that gives us energy to make things hum here -- Look at Daddy cocking his eye at you", "context_len": 28}}}, "doorpost": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "He leaned against the doorpost and little by little his young legs folded under him like tape , and he sank down , his head against the hard wood", "context_len": 29}}}, "highland": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` You 're highland plants", "context_len": 5}}}, "angering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Well , the boy suffered more particularly because the old aunt at intervals made silly attempts to kidnap him , thereby angering his father and making him keep the boy shut up with old servants", "context_len": 35}}}, "greenness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "We were rounding the head of the lake and high above us towered forbidding steep rock cliffs shorn of all greenness by a bush fire that had passed over and left them desolate", "context_len": 33}}}, "daft": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` I ran my pony in a race , He leaped and bounded full of grace , The loons they called , they called to me , I answered them quite daft with glee", "context_len": 34}}}, "pitchforks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Alas ! the master likes to lie , He does not know that Satan waits , And pitchforks boys to bye and bye , Who wander out from Truth 's dear gates", "context_len": 32}}}, "frontages": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "On one side of us was the lake , on the other the narrow frontages of the cottages and a cute little country store with a veranda to it", "context_len": 29}}}, "blocky": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` It 's queer , '' said Happy Harry , `` how the Old Country people run to a stocky , blocky pony", "context_len": 23}}}, "knits": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` He knits , '' said Cassowary to me", "context_len": 9}}}, "gambol": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Do n't I just ! Toss up your head now , pretend you 're a pup , and gambol down the road", "context_len": 23}}}, "grooming": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "When he finished grooming me he smiled as I went in and nosed over the hay in my rack", "context_len": 19}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "I went along too , feeling as light as a feather after my good grooming and vacuum cleaning", "context_len": 18}}}, "wildwood": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "I was glad I had been brought up to this wildwood place for this dear new young master , and I glanced toward the room where he was sleeping", "context_len": 29}}}, "birches": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 8, "context": "By this light they stripped bark from the birches , picked up pitch knots , and dragged dead branches , which , though wet on the outside , the fire was now hot enough to burn", "context_len": 36}}}, "bullhead": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "While I was drinking and playing with the water by blowing it through my nostrils , a bullhead came , and stared at me , then a full-grown bass gave me a very friendly wink", "context_len": 35}}}, "dee": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Cut , cut , ca , da , dee , '' they all said , and I put my head down to them in a kind manner , for to tell the truth there is just a little jealousy between us little ponies and the bigger horses", "context_len": 48}}}, "quadrupeds": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "We quadrupeds say a thing , and it 's over", "context_len": 10}}}, "biddies": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` So many dears , so many cares ; come on , my biddies , come on , come on", "context_len": 20}}}, "unhurried": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "As I stood listening to their hen talk dying away in the hollow , the cows , led by Princess Pat , came from the little barn , their walk unhurried , for cows do hate a fuss and flutter", "context_len": 40}}}, "yapped": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` You shall , '' yapped Guardie , `` the boss said so", "context_len": 13}}}, "loin": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "I never boasted before , but you make me , you runt of a pig , '' and he gave him a good nip on his loin", "context_len": 27}}}, "mowing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Picking up a mowing machine that stood in the yard , he carried it quite easily into a shed where other machines stood", "context_len": 23}}}, "pajama": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Big Chief was a sight , lying across his narrow bed , his pajama clad legs hanging down one side , his head the other", "context_len": 25}}}, "precipitousness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Of somewhat precipitousness thou , '' he said , `` but thy stomach is thy god", "context_len": 17}}}, "ramblings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "However in my ramblings I am forgetting the little house-mother who was building a great roaring fire in the living-room", "context_len": 20}}}, "ponying": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` I wondered why you stopped dogging or perhaps I should say ponying my footsteps -- -- Now we 'll play the rising tune , '' and going to the hall she took a bugle from a hook and began a lovely tune , first soft and low , then high and piercing", "context_len": 53}}}, "bathhouse": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` I -- I thought you said there was a bathhouse , '' remarked poor Dallas , `` with hot and cold water", "context_len": 23}}}, "sissies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Lots of bath-rooms , '' he cried , `` for sissies and old women", "context_len": 15}}}, "polo": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "Devering , `` I 'll keep the boy with me , '' and did n't she go diving off the end of the wharf and was soon playing water polo with her lively children", "context_len": 34}}}, "humors": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Your ugly humors are riding you to a fall , '' he said quietly", "context_len": 15}}}, "cereal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "By this time the cereal had been put on the table by Big Chief and the children were busily eating it and pouring on plenty of rich wonderful cream", "context_len": 29}}}, "screamer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Thpank the howlerths , '' cried little Big-Wig , who was the worst screamer of all", "context_len": 17}}}, "brassiest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` And you Big Chief , '' said Cassowary pertly , `` you with the brassiest voice of all , what say you ? ''", "context_len": 25}}}, "whisks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "Indians use the bare hand and arm -- utensils in common stable use among us whites are the metal curry-combs , bristle body-brushes , corn-brushes , rub rags , sponges , whisks and hoof picks", "context_len": 35}}}, "dandruff": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "Now here is a brush for you -- no , do n't rub your Prince the right way of the hair -- the reverse way to get out the dandruff and dry dirt", "context_len": 33}}}, "massage": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Then , as I am a racer , his uncle showed him how to massage me", "context_len": 16}}}, "housing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "I think myself there is too much housing of dumb animals", "context_len": 11}}}, "vacuum": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` A vacuum cleaner for horses , '' exclaimed Dallas after we had crossed to the horses ' side of the stable", "context_len": 22}}}, "skittish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "It was most unlikely that they would entrust their old bones to such skittish things as canoes", "context_len": 17}}}, "tortoiseshell": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Who was Sideways ? Of all things -- a common tortoiseshell cat", "context_len": 12}}}, "electrocuted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "One cat after another had been electrocuted on this farm for hunting birds , and this was a case of the survival of the fittest", "context_len": 25}}}, "survival": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "One cat after another had been electrocuted on this farm for hunting birds , and this was a case of the survival of the fittest", "context_len": 25}}}, "fittest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "One cat after another had been electrocuted on this farm for hunting birds , and this was a case of the survival of the fittest", "context_len": 25}}}, "frilly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Then she added , `` And Mother calls these frilly ankled things 'overettes , ' not 'overalls", "context_len": 17}}}, "welkin": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "They made the welkin ring when they heard this proposal , and smiling kindly on the little flock this patient man went on his way up to the house", "context_len": 29}}}, "surreys": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` Oh ! yes , he was for some years in Ohio , where one sees ponies hauling two or four persons in carts and small surreys", "context_len": 27}}}, "breeching": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` I understand about the bridle , collar , traces , breeching , hip-strap and so on , but how do you get harness and cart together ? ''", "context_len": 29}}}, "stumbler": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "If he were a stumbler down he would go", "context_len": 9}}}, "hah": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "squawked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Kay-auk , kay-ow , '' squawked the gull , then he gave a kind of groan", "context_len": 17}}}, "brayed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The gull brayed like a donkey , `` Ha , ha ha ! ''", "context_len": 14}}}, "trounced": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "I was out in my canoe Bluebird , and did n't I pick up that poor gull baby pretty quick as he lay flopping in the water , then I overhauled the boat , trounced the boy who was quite astonished to find that a girl had fists , swam to my canoe , paddled home , mothered Gullie who made his home with the hens , and revelled in bread and milk", "context_len": 73}}}, "sculpins": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "There were sculpins , lobsters , perch , cod , pollock , and once in a while a haddock , all living as socially together as could be", "context_len": 28}}}, "flounders": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "When I am fishing for flounders he will sit by me till I pull up a fish , and then he will swallow it in no time ; sometimes I say , Rover , you can \u2019 t have that ; it is for the house ; \u2019 and he will look so wishful I have to give it to him", "context_len": 61}}}, "bunkum": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "O , he had a bunkum time", "context_len": 7}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Well , here \u2019 s the tree to make a bunkum one , I tell you ; shall I cut it for you ?", "context_len": 24}}}, "coot": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "I can shoot a coot or a squirrel with an arrow , or any kind of sea-bird", "context_len": 17}}}, "raccoons": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "No , not one ; but there \u2019 s raccoons and squirrels", "context_len": 12}}}, "leach": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "I \u2019 ll set up the leach for you , said John ; and after the soap is made , if we have good luck , we \u2019 ll have a celebration , and make candy", "context_len": 36}}}, "quilting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "I want the fun of quilting it myself", "context_len": 8}}}, "chook": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "singeing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "That was well planned , Charlie , said Joe ; and I \u2019 ll forgive you for singeing me so", "context_len": 20}}}, "tamarinds": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "He brought home tamarinds , guava jelly , and other good things for Sally ; a hat made of palm-leaf for Ben , and some shells for Charlie", "context_len": 28}}}, "durability": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "As their object was neither beauty nor durability , only to serve the present occasion , they used all the cedar possible , that she might be the more buoyant", "context_len": 30}}}, "treenails": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "He could also drive treenails ; and when the men were not using the broad axe , would hew out small sticks with a skill that called forth many compliments from Uncle Isaac , who took great pains to show him , and found a most apt scholar", "context_len": 48}}}, "wales": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "They then put on two streaks of plank next the keel , to hold the ends of the timbers , and hung the wales", "context_len": 24}}}, "insured": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "It must be confessed she was a craft of magnificent distances , and probably could not have been insured at Lloyd \u2019 s", "context_len": 23}}}, "notches": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "When the men were hewing , he , with his narrow axe , could score in and beat off for them ( that is , cut notches in the timber , close together , and then split out the wood between ) , which very much facilitated the labor of hewing", "context_len": 51}}}, "facilitated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "When the men were hewing , he , with his narrow axe , could score in and beat off for them ( that is , cut notches in the timber , close together , and then split out the wood between ) , which very much facilitated the labor of hewing", "context_len": 51}}}, "hew": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 36, "context": "Charlie and Ben had been so fully occupied during the summer , they had not caught a single fish to dry for winter ; so Charlie now busied himself in fishing , while Ben continued to hew the timber for the barn , which was to be very large", "context_len": 49}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "We \u2019 ll hew it out , at any rate ; that \u2019 ll save him some work", "context_len": 18}}}, "breeches": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Is it the cloth for my breeches ?", "context_len": 8}}}, "mites": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "O , a little baby ! Whose is it ? Where did you get it ? What a wee bit of a thing ! what little mites of hands ! I wish it would wake up and open its eyes", "context_len": 40}}}, "fatten": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "It was not the custom in those days to put pigs in pens and keep them there ; they let them run about the door , and feed in the pasture with the cattle , only putting them up in the fall to fatten ; or when they bought a strange one in the spring , they shut him up till he got tame", "context_len": 64}}}, "clamming": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "I \u2019 ve named him Rover ; and don \u2019 t you think he knows when the tide is down just as well as I do ; then he goes to the beach , and digs clams with his nose ; he never goes a clamming at high water", "context_len": 49}}}, "sties": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Living on grass , and in the woods , with the milk from the house , he had not that protuberance of belly which swine reared in sties possess , and really merited Charlie \u2019 s encomium of being handsome ; he jumped up on his master and rubbed against his legs , with low grunts , expressive of satisfaction", "context_len": 60}}}, "piecing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "Here they fitted the rigging , and altered the ship \u2019 s sails into those of a schooner ; and so well versed were Captain Rhines and his son in all nautical matters , that , by dint of splicing and piecing , they managed to get all the standing rigging , and nearly all the running gear , out of the materials of the wreck", "context_len": 66}}}, "broody": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Charlie told his mother , and they put their heads together , and set every hen that was broody , insomuch that Ben complained that he could not get an egg to eat", "context_len": 33}}}, "eked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "He had to hire his oxen pastured away in the summer , as the island was so densely covered with wood that it afforded but little pasturage , which was eked out by falling maple trees for them to browse", "context_len": 40}}}, "sprouts": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 120, "context": "But a fire in a few hours will lick up every stick and leaf , except the large logs and stumps , burn up all the bushes , and the whole network of small roots that cover the ground , so that nothing will start for months , as it destroys all the seeds of the weeds and trees , of which the ground is full ; and if it is dry , and a thorough burn , will so roast the large stumps that very few of them will ever sprout again , -- while , as in Ben \u2019 s case , most of them are spruce , pine , or fir , that never throw up any sprouts from the roots", "context_len": 124}}}, "sprout": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 91, "context": "But a fire in a few hours will lick up every stick and leaf , except the large logs and stumps , burn up all the bushes , and the whole network of small roots that cover the ground , so that nothing will start for months , as it destroys all the seeds of the weeds and trees , of which the ground is full ; and if it is dry , and a thorough burn , will so roast the large stumps that very few of them will ever sprout again , -- while , as in Ben \u2019 s case , most of them are spruce , pine , or fir , that never throw up any sprouts from the roots", "context_len": 124}}}, "fosters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "There is then left a thick bed of ashes , which receives and fosters whatever is put into it", "context_len": 19}}}, "symmetry": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "This cleared land extended back on both sides of the brook for quite a distance , and was dotted over with elms ; and on a little knoll , about half way between the brook and the middle ridge , was an enormous rock maple , with that perfect symmetry of proportions which this noble tree often presents", "context_len": 58}}}, "slat": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "In an instant eight men , leaving the sails to slat in the wind , leaped into the water , and with buckets which they filled as they ran , came to the rescue", "context_len": 34}}}, "forsook": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "He it was , that , with a coolness that never forsook him , stopped to cut an armful of switches for himself and the rest", "context_len": 26}}}, "switches": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "He it was , that , with a coolness that never forsook him , stopped to cut an armful of switches for himself and the rest", "context_len": 26}}}, "fiddlestick": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Healing-salve ? fiddlestick ! I \u2019 ve seen Indians cut half to pieces , scalded , and burnt , and get well , and I never saw any salve among them", "context_len": 31}}}, "firebrands": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Charlie came to say , Henry wanted to know if they were going to live on firebrands , for he had been waiting with his supper two hours , and was almost starved", "context_len": 33}}}, "alders": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The willows and alders that fringed the brook were gone ; the trunks of the elms and that of the great maple scorched , and the grass all around the house black as a coal", "context_len": 35}}}, "smut": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "They could not sit down in a chair without smutting it all over ; and their faces were in streaks of white and black , where the perspiration ran down and washed away the smut", "context_len": 35}}}, "smutty": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "This smutty and laborious job being over , land fenced , and logs burned up , Ben sowed half of it with winter rye , reserving the other to plant with corn in the spring", "context_len": 35}}}, "soaks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "They arranged them with great care , that the joints might not all come in one place ; and frequently put in a stick of cedar to increase the buoyancy , as cedar , in addition to its lightness , soaks water very slowly", "context_len": 44}}}, "twitches": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The others laid the boards in twitches , and were all ready to hook the chain when Joe came for his boards ; and he hauled them into the vessel at a great rate , and dropped them just where Captain Rhines and Ben wanted them", "context_len": 46}}}, "hog": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "I can make a hog \u2019 s trough , and christen it a float , but to make one that will be stiff and light , and scull steady and true , there \u2019 s only one man round here can do it", "context_len": 43}}}, "sparred": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "I declare , Ben ! said Captain Rhines ; who would have thought she would go through the water so ; we \u2019 ve got her sparred just right , if we did do it by guess", "context_len": 37}}}, "calculates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Heavens ! said Joe Griffin ; the old man calculates on a long voyage , if he expects to burn all that", "context_len": 22}}}, "beets": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Produce was not carried there from all parts of the world in those days , as at present ; and a barrel or two of onions or beets would bring twenty-five or thirty dollars", "context_len": 34}}}, "alias": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "He was always in demand , notwithstanding this failing , -- the economical captains never hesitating to go one hand short when they had Flour ( alias James Peterson ) for cook , as he was always ready to lend a hand , and was worth three common men in bad weather", "context_len": 52}}}, "skillet": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Last Monday mornin \u2019 she break de skillet ; she kill my dog ; she put thistle under my horse \u2019 s tail when I goes to de store , so he fling me over his head -- most break my neck", "context_len": 42}}}, "manifesting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "As this was Tige \u2019 s method of manifesting the very acme of happiness , we are bound to suppose , with John , that he knew what was in store for him", "context_len": 33}}}, "smelts": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 1, "context": "The smelts going in to eat the bait , he gradually drew it up , and , when almost at the surface , gave a quick jerk ; but the water was so long filtering through the handkerchief that they all swam out", "context_len": 43}}}, "tinkers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "When the gates were open , and the tide from the sea flowed in , the fish -- smelts , tom-cod , and sometimes small mackerel , called tinkers , came with it", "context_len": 33}}}, "windfalls": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "About a quarter of a mile up this little stream was a place where some windfalls had partially dammed the water , forming a little pond , in which were myriads of frogs , tadpoles , polliwogs , and turtles of all sizes", "context_len": 43}}}, "polliwogs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "About a quarter of a mile up this little stream was a place where some windfalls had partially dammed the water , forming a little pond , in which were myriads of frogs , tadpoles , polliwogs , and turtles of all sizes", "context_len": 43}}}, "coronal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "It was indeed a kingly tree , thirteen feet and a half in circumference at the roots , bearing its enormous coronal of leaves in that symmetry of proportions which this tree ( seen nowhere in its perfection but in the North American forests ) sometimes exhibits", "context_len": 47}}}, "whitening": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "In the spring , at the time boys make whistles , Charlie had peeled the bark from some willow rods ( which he called whitening the sallies ) , and made a long , narrow basket", "context_len": 36}}}, "shingled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Going round to the north-west side of it , they found a building about seven feet high , and shingled on the roof and walls , with a tight-fitting door , having a wooden latch and hinges", "context_len": 37}}}, "planed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Opening the door , they saw that it had a regular frame , and was ceiled up with planed boards", "context_len": 20}}}, "fifes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "The first one opened was filled with all kinds of boys \u2019 playthings , which Charlie had made himself , -- whistles , fifes , and squirt-guns made of elder , and a ball", "context_len": 34}}}, "jingoes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "My jingoes ! I never saw a ball bounce like that", "context_len": 11}}}, "sturgeon": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "It \u2019 s wound on a sturgeon \u2019 s nose", "context_len": 10}}}, "squawks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Thank you , Charlie ; and when we get learned we \u2019 ll come on here and give it to the squawks , and go on to Oak Island , and shoot squirrels and woodchucks , and save our powder and shot for sea-fowl", "context_len": 44}}}, "gamekeepers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "It seems so wild and independent ; no dukes , and earls , and gamekeepers to watch you , but just go where you please , kill and eat", "context_len": 29}}}, "lolled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "They lolled on the grass a while , and at length Charlie proposed that they should go a fishing", "context_len": 19}}}, "fissure": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The tides being very high , the water had flowed up to the fissure in the ledge where the brook ran over", "context_len": 22}}}, "flippers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "We call them flippers , said Ben", "context_len": 7}}}, "membrane": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "He then showed them that there was a membrane between the toes of his feet , like a duck \u2019 s", "context_len": 21}}}, "gambrel": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "His hind legs were about as long as the thighs of a hog would be , if the legs were cut off at the gambrel joint", "context_len": 26}}}, "benumb": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "Ben also told them that the seals were so strong , that if you took hold of one of their paws when they were half dead , they would twist it out of your grip with such force and quickness as to benumb the fingers", "context_len": 45}}}, "lard": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The boys removed the skin from this mass of fat , like lard , which was quite a difficult operation for novices , and required a great deal of care , that they might not cut the skin , or leave the fat upon it", "context_len": 45}}}, "reefs": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "The cave was formed by two rocky points , and the bottom of it was , near the shore , a smooth granite ledge ; but across its mouth were ragged and broken reefs , two fathoms beneath the surface at the lowest tides", "context_len": 44}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "feathering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "At that moment the blade of John \u2019 s oar struck the ball ; feathering [ B ] his oar with a jerk , he sent it skipping over the water out of the eddy , where the wind drove it directly to the shore", "context_len": 45}}}, "edgewise": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "[ B ] Turning it edgewise", "context_len": 6}}}, "recovers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Youth soon recovers from fatigue ; and after lying an hour stretched at full length before a warm fire , they felt entirely rested", "context_len": 24}}}, "scythe": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "( We would say to our readers , that the scythe rifles in those days were not made as at present , by putting sand or emery upon wood , with cement ; but they scratched the wood and made it rough , then smeared it with tallow , and put fine sand on it , which adhered to the tallow and the scratches", "context_len": 64}}}, "emery": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "( We would say to our readers , that the scythe rifles in those days were not made as at present , by putting sand or emery upon wood , with cement ; but they scratched the wood and made it rough , then smeared it with tallow , and put fine sand on it , which adhered to the tallow and the scratches", "context_len": 64}}}, "huskings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "I drink it , said John , at huskings and raisings , and when father gives it to me", "context_len": 19}}}, "raisings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "I drink it , said John , at huskings and raisings , and when father gives it to me", "context_len": 19}}}, "withy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "It took a withy man to lay him on his back , or lift his load , I tell you", "context_len": 20}}}, "bores": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Charles , said Uncle Isaac , go to the house and bring up Ben \u2019 s big auger , that he bores yokes with", "context_len": 24}}}, "cod": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 59, "context": "Bring that to bear over the western pint of the Junk of Pork , at high-water mark ; then bring the north-west side of Smutty Nose , and the south-east side of Oak Island , just touching on to each other , and you \u2019 ll be on a kelp shoal , where there \u2019 s plenty of rock cod , and where it is so shallow that at low water you can see them bite", "context_len": 76}}}, "kelp": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 49, "context": "Bring that to bear over the western pint of the Junk of Pork , at high-water mark ; then bring the north-west side of Smutty Nose , and the south-east side of Oak Island , just touching on to each other , and you \u2019 ll be on a kelp shoal , where there \u2019 s plenty of rock cod , and where it is so shallow that at low water you can see them bite", "context_len": 76}}}, "menhaden": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "What shall we have for bait ? There are no menhaden in the bay", "context_len": 14}}}, "luff": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "halibut": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "It may be a halibut , said Charlie , taking hold of the line to help him", "context_len": 17}}}, "parboiled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Their hands , too , were sore and parboiled from the friction of the line and constant soaking in the water , especially those of John and Fred , who did not know how to take out the hook without putting their fingers into the fish \u2019 s mouth , and scratching and cutting them with his teeth and gills", "context_len": 60}}}, "pollock": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "There were sculpins , lobsters , perch , cod , pollock , and once in a while a haddock , all living as socially together as could be", "context_len": 28}}}, "haddock": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "There were sculpins , lobsters , perch , cod , pollock , and once in a while a haddock , all living as socially together as could be", "context_len": 28}}}, "cusk": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Sometimes a cusk would stray in among them , and a sea-nettle come drifting along just outside the kelp , his long feelers streaming a yard behind him", "context_len": 28}}}, "misshapen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "An ugly-looking , misshapen thing he was , with a black , dirty skin , like a sculpin , and called , from his lack of proportions , a lump-fish", "context_len": 30}}}, "sculpin": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "An ugly-looking , misshapen thing he was , with a black , dirty skin , like a sculpin , and called , from his lack of proportions , a lump-fish", "context_len": 30}}}, "mannish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Kind of mannish , said John", "context_len": 6}}}, "chowder": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "I guess , said Hannah Murch , you \u2019 ll enjoy yourselves better when you get that chowder , and that something else I am going to make", "context_len": 28}}}, "heron": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "What \u2019 s in that pot over the fire now ? said Fred , who was a dear lover of good cheer , and could eat as much as a heron", "context_len": 31}}}, "pare": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Sally , will you wash and pare the potatoes ? Mrs", "context_len": 11}}}, "sweetening": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "Rhines , won \u2019 t you be good enough to draw the tea ? Girls , put the dishes on the table ; you \u2019 ll find them in a tub in the cart ; and the pies are there , too , and the milk and sweetening", "context_len": 48}}}, "steers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "I \u2019 ll come and look at them once in the while , and , when they are cured , you can take your steers and cart and take them to the widow \u2019 s ; she is in no hurry for them , as the neighbors have given her all she needs for the present", "context_len": 56}}}, "bowlful": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Shouldn \u2019 t think you need say anything , growled Fred ; that \u2019 s the third bowlful you \u2019 ve eaten", "context_len": 22}}}, "germ": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Indeed , that was but the germ in a thoughtful , benevolent mind , of principles that were to be widely extended in after years", "context_len": 25}}}, "deviltry": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "I used to think there couldn \u2019 t be any good time without I was in some deviltry", "context_len": 18}}}, "brants": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "I know ; I \u2019 ve been there many a time to shoot brants", "context_len": 14}}}, "contrition": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "But so far from manifesting any contrition , Tige , all the time his master was talking to him , kept wagging his tail , and looking him in the face", "context_len": 31}}}, "plateful": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Well , Fred , said Sally ( when the pie had come upon the table , and he had despatched the first plateful ) , what do you think of my pie ?", "context_len": 33}}}, "obstructed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "Following the course of the brook for some distance , they came to where the soil changed to a stiff clay , and the brook was obstructed by an old beaver-dam , causing the water in many places to stand in little pools , in the bottom of which , and in the shelves of the rock which formed the bed of the brook , was a sediment of yellow mud , devoid of grit , and fine as flour", "context_len": 80}}}, "luffed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "Our young readers must bear in mind that these canoes could only go before the wind , or a little quartering , and therefore could not , like a boat , be luffed sharp into the wind , and beat out clear of danger ; hence the boys preferred to take the sail in , and trust to their oars , with which they could , if they saw a breaker , pull away from it", "context_len": 76}}}, "pyrites": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "It ran about twenty feet into the rock , which , being formed to a great extent of iron pyrites , had crumbled beneath the united forces of the frost and waves", "context_len": 32}}}, "predominates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Though boys are little given to sentiment , and the animal nature predominates , yet the scene was so singularly wild and beautiful , it was impossible they should not be impressed by it , which they manifested in their own fashion", "context_len": 42}}}, "silvered": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "A little on their left was the high , rocky bank of the cove , with its narrow strip of white sand , sheltered from the wind by the high bluff , on which the retiring wavelets gently rolled , silvered by the moonbeams", "context_len": 44}}}, "ebbs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Who cares ! said John ; we ain \u2019 t drowned , have got a fire , and can get our things when the tide ebbs", "context_len": 26}}}, "crotches": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The sail of the canoe , spread over a pole supported by crotches , made them a tent , and they were soon asleep", "context_len": 24}}}, "copperas": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "Just taste of that , said Fred , giving Charlie a piece of shelly , yellowish rock , who , putting it to his mouth , instantly spit it out , saying that it tasted like copperas", "context_len": 37}}}, "lozenge": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "O , Fred , look ! what are these ? and Charlie picked out from among the bones a double handful of little round things , about the size of a modern lozenge , with a hole in the centre", "context_len": 40}}}, "sediment": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 67, "context": "Following the course of the brook for some distance , they came to where the soil changed to a stiff clay , and the brook was obstructed by an old beaver-dam , causing the water in many places to stand in little pools , in the bottom of which , and in the shelves of the rock which formed the bed of the brook , was a sediment of yellow mud , devoid of grit , and fine as flour", "context_len": 80}}}, "sloops": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "In England they have boats with keels , masts , and sails , just like sloops and schooners ; they will sail on the wind , and beat to windward as well as the Perseverance", "context_len": 35}}}, "planked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "They have timbers , are planked up , and calked , just , for all the world , like little vessels ; and in some of them the planks are lapped over each other and nailed", "context_len": 36}}}, "calked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "They have timbers , are planked up , and calked , just , for all the world , like little vessels ; and in some of them the planks are lapped over each other and nailed", "context_len": 36}}}, "lapped": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "They have timbers , are planked up , and calked , just , for all the world , like little vessels ; and in some of them the planks are lapped over each other and nailed", "context_len": 36}}}, "oakum": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "Why not ? A barrel and a pail is tight , and there is no oakum in them", "context_len": 18}}}, "whetstone": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "After supper he produced Charlie \u2019 s broad-axe , with a good white-oak handle , and nicely ground ; he also gave him an excellent whetstone , which he told him came from the Gut of Canso", "context_len": 37}}}, "linseed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "I wouldn \u2019 t use that ; it is almost impossible to make it dry ; you can get linseed oil at the store", "context_len": 24}}}, "gripe": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Page 242 out of your gripe changed to out of your grip Page 267 Is \u2019 nt this good ? changed to Isn \u2019 t this good ?", "context_len": 28}}}}